window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-07-18T23:59:34.605043Z","count":211,"pages":[{"title":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","url":"/improving-your-thinking-and-analytical/","description":"Improving your thinking and analytical skills is less about becoming “cleverer” in the abstract and more about building habits that make your judgement...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Can You Train Better Judgment? Can You Train Better Judgment? Can You Train Better Judgment? Can You Train Better Judgment? Can You Train Better Judgment? Improving your thinking and analytical skills is less about becoming “cleverer” in the abstract and more about building habits that make your judgement... Improving your thinking and analytical skills is less about becoming “cleverer” in the abstract and more about building habits that make your judgement slower where it should be slower, clearer where it is muddled, and better tested against evidence. improving your thinking and analytical skills Introduction The good news is that analytical skill is trainable. The catch is that it does not usually improve just because someone reads more, argues more, or accumulates experience. People need structured practice : making reasoning visible, testing predictions, learning from errors, and using tools that reduce avoidable bias. [Sage Journals+2Bayes Business School] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think Criticallyby PC Abrami · 2015 · Cited by 2010 — This article summarizes the availa... What “better thinking” actually means Good thinking is not the same as being sceptical about everything. A useful working definition of critical thinking is purposeful, self regulated judgement: interpreting information, analysing arguments, evaluating evidence, drawing inferences, and explaining why a conclusion follows. That definition matters because it shifts the goal from “having strong opinions” to “being able to show the route from question to conclusion”. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think Criticallyby PC Abrami · 2015 · Cited by 2010 — This article summarizes the availa... Analytical thinking is the close cousin of that process. It asks: What is the problem? What do we know? What do we not know? Which explanation fits best? What would change my mind? In everyday life, that might mean comparing job offers, assessing a health claim, deciding whether a news story is reliable, or diagnosing why a project has stalled. A strong thinker usually combines three capacities: Cognitive skill: the ability to reason, compare, infer, estimate, categorise and detect contradictions. Metacognitive control: the ability to monitor one’s own understanding and notice when confidence is running ahead of evidence. Intellectual disposition: the willingness to seek reasons, consider alternatives, tolerate uncertainty and revise a view when the evidence changes. This combination is important because people can know reasoning rules yet fail to use them under pressure. The practical aim is therefore not just to learn concepts such as “confirmation bias” or “correlation is not causation”, but to build routines that make those concepts show up at the moment of decision. Why intuition is useful but unreliable on its own Much everyday thinking is fast, automatic and pattern based. That is not a flaw: fast judgement helps people recognise faces, read social cues, avoid hazards and make routine decisions without exhausting attention. Dual process theories describe this contrast between quicker intuitive processes and slower reflective processes, although researchers debate how sharply the two can really be separated. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCDual Process Theory: Embodied and Predictive PMCDual Process Theory: Embodied and Predictive The danger comes when a problem feels familiar but is actually different from the pattern your intuition has matched. A classic example is the Cognitive Reflection Test, designed to see whether people override an appealing but wrong first answer and do a little more reasoning. Later research cautions that the test measures several things, including numeracy and cognitive ability, not pure “reflection” alone; still, it captures a real problem: first impressions can be persuasive before they are justified. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A practical rule follows: do not try to suppress intuition entirely. Use it as a signal, then ask whether the situation deserves a second pass. Slow down especially when: the decision is costly or hard to reverse; the evidence is incomplete; the issue triggers strong emotion or identity; the first answer feels obvious but has not been tested; other informed people disagree; the result depends on probabilities, trade offs or causation. The skill is not “always think slowly”. It is knowing when speed is appropriate and when it is risky. Make your reasoning visible One of the most powerful ways to improve analytical thinking is to externalise it. Thoughts feel clearer inside the head than they often are on paper. When you write down a claim, its reasons, objections and assumptions , vague confidence becomes inspectable. Argument mapping is a useful example. It represents reasoning visually, often with boxes for claims and arrows showing support or objection. Research on argument mapping in critical thinking instruction suggests that it can help learners see the structure of arguments rather than merely react to their surface wording. [Reasoninglab+2University of Galway Research Repository] reasoninglab.com Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills You do not need specialist software to get the benefit. For any important conclusion, use a simple structure: 1. Conclusion: What exactly am I claiming? 2. Reasons: What supports it? 3. Evidence: What observations, data or sources support those reasons? 4. Assumptions: What must be true for this argument to work? 5. Objections: What is the strongest case against it? 6. Test: What evidence would weaken or change the conclusion? For example, “This project is failing because the team lacks discipline” may become less convincing once mapped. The evidence might show unclear goals, changing priorities, missing authority and poor feedback loops. The better analysis may be: the system is producing delay, not simply the people. Making reasoning visible helps separate a satisfying explanation from a well supported one. Practise metacognition: thinking about your thinking Metacognition means monitoring and directing your own thinking. In learning contexts, it includes recognising what you do and do not understand, choosing strategies, checking progress and adjusting when a method is not working. Educational research treats metacognition and self regulated learning as major contributors to better learning outcomes when they are taught clearly and connected to subject content. [EEF+2Digital Education Resource Archive] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk Open source on educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk. For analytical skill, metacognition turns into a set of self check questions: Before thinking: What kind of problem is this: factual, causal, moral, strategic, probabilistic or interpretive? During thinking: Am I explaining the evidence, or protecting my first answer? After thinking: What did I miss, and what would I do differently next time? The strongest metacognitive habit is calibration: matching confidence to evidence. A person with good calibration does not merely say “I am certain” or “I am unsure”; they can explain why. They distinguish between a well supported conclusion, a plausible guess, and a preference. A simple calibration practice is to record predictions. Write down what you expect, why, and how confident you are. Later, compare the outcome with the prediction. This turns judgement from a private feeling into a feedback loop. Deliberate Practice: Achieve Mastery in Anything Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Learn actively, not passively Analytical skill grows slowly when learning is passive. Reading, watching and listening can supply information, but they do not reliably show whether you can use it. Active learning asks people to discuss, solve, create, explain and apply ideas rather than merely receive them. [Center for Teaching Innovation] teaching.cornell.edu Center for Teaching Innovation Active Learning Center for Teaching Innovation Active Learning Retrieval practice is especially useful because it forces you to bring knowledge to mind rather than recognise it when it is shown to you. Feedback after retrieval helps correct errors and improve awareness of what has or has not been learned. Pooja K. Agarwal, Ph.D. [retrievalpractice.org] retrievalpractice.org Pooja K. Agarwal, Ph.D.Feedback and Metacognition Pooja K. Agarwal, Ph.D.Feedback and Metacognition To make learning analytical, replace “review” with tasks such as: explain the idea without notes; apply it to a new example; compare it with a rival idea; generate a counterexample; identify what evidence would support or weaken it; teach it in plain English to someone outside the field. This matters because many people mistake familiarity for understanding. A concept feels known because it has been seen before. Retrieval"},{"title":"Active Learning","url":"/active-learning/","description":"Analytical skill grows faster when people retrieve, explain, apply, and test ideas rather than merely reread them.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Active Learning Why Rereading Is Not Enough Active Learning Active Learning Why Rereading Is Not Enough Analytical skill grows faster when people retrieve, explain, apply, and test ideas rather than merely reread them. Analytical skill grows faster when people retrieve, explain, apply, and test ideas rather than merely reread them. policy intervention Familiarity versus understanding Tasks that force use Active learning at work improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning Introduction Rereading feels productive because it makes ideas look familiar. Active learning is more demanding because it asks a person to retrieve, explain , apply, test, or correct an idea before they feel fully ready. That difficulty is the point. For improving thinking and analytical skills, passive review mainly strengthens recognition: “I have seen this before.” Active learning strengthens usable understanding: “I can recall it, explain it, use it in a new case, and notice where my reasoning breaks.” The practical shift is simple but uncomfortable: replace some review time with tasks that force use. Instead of rereading notes on argument quality, close the notes and reconstruct the argument. Instead of highlighting a chapter on bias, predict where the bias would appear in a real decision. Instead of watching someone solve a problem, solve a similar one, compare your route, and correct the gap. This matters because research on practice testing, retrieval practice , self explanation and active classrooms repeatedly finds that learners often remember and transfer more when they actively generate answers than when they merely re expose themselves to material. [PubMed+2Sage Journals] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov taking memory tests improves long term retentionby HL Roediger · 2006 · Cited by 4881 — Taking a memory test not only assesses what... Familiarity Is Not the Same as Understanding The main trap in passive review is not laziness. It is miscalibration. When a text becomes easier to read, the learner often mistakes processing fluency for mastery. The page feels clear because the eyes move through it smoothly, not necessarily because the knowledge can be retrieved or used. This is why rereading can produce confidence at exactly the moment when a more useful question would be: “Could I explain this without looking?” The classic evidence comes from research on the “testing effect”. In Roediger and Karpicke’s 2006 study, students learned prose passages and either restudied them or practised recalling them. Restudying could help immediate performance, but retrieval practice produced stronger long term retention, showing that a test can be more than a measurement device: it can be a learning event. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov taking memory tests improves long term retentionby HL Roediger · 2006 · Cited by 4881 — Taking a memory test not only assesses what... A later review by Dunlosky and colleagues assessed common learning techniques and gave high utility ratings to practice testing and distributed practice, while rereading was judged less powerful as a general purpose learning strategy. The reason is not that rereading never helps. It can help with initial exposure, clarification and checking. The problem is that rereading alone gives too little evidence about whether knowledge is available under pressure, after delay, or in a changed context. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Improving Students' Learning With Effective...Practice testing and distributed practice received high utility assessments because... For analytical skill, this distinction is especially important. A person may recognise a definition of confirmation bias, causal reasoning, opportunity cost, or base rate neglect when reading it. That does not mean they will use the concept when evaluating a news claim, diagnosing a failed project, or comparing explanations. Active learning narrows that gap by turning knowledge from something recognised into something callable. What Active Learning Forces the Mind to Do Active learning is not just “doing activities”. It is useful when the activity makes the learner perform part of the thinking that later judgement will require. The task should create a small but meaningful demand: recall the idea, organise it, explain why it applies, compare it with an alternative, or use it to solve a new problem. Retrieval practice is the clearest example. Instead of asking, “Have I reviewed this?”, it asks, “Can I bring this back without help?” A 2017 meta analysis of practice testing found that students who took practice tests often outperformed learners in non testing conditions, including restudying. The value comes partly from strengthening memory and partly from revealing gaps that passive review can hide. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsRethinking the Use of Tests: A Meta Analysis of Practice...by OO Adesope · 2017 · Cited by 913 — Research demonstrates that... Self explanation adds another layer. It asks learners to explain steps, causes, assumptions or connections to themselves rather than merely accepting an explanation supplied by someone else. Research on self explanation has found benefits for understanding because learners generate links between new material and prior knowledge, notice missing steps and correct misconceptions. [Andy Matuschak] andymatuschak.org Chi et al 1994 Eliciting self explanations improves understanding Chi et al 1994 Eliciting self explanations improves understanding Application then tests whether understanding survives contact with variation. Karpicke and Blunt’s 2011 work compared retrieval practice with elaborative studying through concept mapping and found that practising retrieval produced greater gains in meaningful learning. The result was striking because concept mapping looks deeper than testing, yet the act of reconstructing knowledge from memory helped learners perform better on later measures of understanding. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For thinking skills, these mechanisms matter because analysis is not a spectator skill. You improve by doing the moves: stating the claim, identifying the evidence, naming the assumption, testing an implication, and revising the answer. Tasks That Force Use The best active learning tasks are short, concrete and slightly uncomfortable. They should not become elaborate rituals that replace learning with administration. A useful test is whether the task produces evidence of understanding that rereading would not reveal. Close book recall. After reading a section, shut the source and write the three most important points from memory. Then reopen the source and correct omissions. This is simple retrieval practice, and it quickly shows the difference between recognition and recall. Explain it as a causal chain. For analytical material, do not merely define a concept. Explain how it works. For example: “Why does survivorship bias lead to bad business advice?” A good answer should move from sample selection, to missing failures, to distorted conclusions. Use a fresh example. After learning a reasoning principle, apply it to a case not used in the original lesson. If the concept is “correlation is not causation”, test it against a health headline, workplace metric or social trend. Transfer is where shallow familiarity often fails. Predict before checking. Before reading an analysis, make a short prediction: what would I expect the data to show, which explanation seems most likely, or what objection might appear? Prediction turns reading into an active comparison between expectation and evidence. Write the wrong answer and diagnose it. Deliberately sketch a plausible but flawed interpretation, then name the flaw. This is especially useful for analytical skill because many real errors are not absurd; they are attractive, fluent and incomplete. Teach the idea briefly. A two minute explanation to a colleague, study partner or imagined audience forces selection, sequencing and clarity. If the explanation depends on repeating a memorised sentence, the understanding is probably still brittle. These tasks work best when they are low stakes. The aim is not to make every learning moment feel like an exam. It is to create frequent moments where the learner has to bring knowledge into use before the final decision, essay, meeting, test or project. Active Recall vs Passive Learning What the Research Says Channel: Decroly Education Centre Open on YouTube Active Learning at Work In the workplace, passive review often takes the form of reading a policy, watching a training module, sitting through a briefing, or scanning a slide deck. Those formats can transmit information, but they do not prove that people can use the information when a real problem appears. Active learning turns workplace learning into rehearsal for judgement. A manager learning better decision making might replace a reread of “decision bias” notes with a pre mortem: “Assume this"},{"title":"Recall","url":"/recall/","description":"Closing the source and reconstructing ideas from memory exposes the gap between familiar-looking notes and usable knowledge.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Active Learning","level":3,"text":"Recall Can You Explain It Without Looking? Recall Recall Can You Explain It Without Looking? Closing the source and reconstructing ideas from memory exposes the gap between familiar looking notes and usable knowledge. Closing the source and reconstructing ideas from memory exposes the gap between familiar looking notes and usable knowledge. policy intervention Why rereading feels easier than recall A simple close book recall routine How to correct gaps without overstudying improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning recall Introduction One of the quickest ways to discover whether you genuinely understand an idea is to close the book, hide your notes, and explain it from memory. This simple habit replaces the comforting feeling of recognition with the harder but more useful test of retrieval. Within active learning , close book recall is valuable not because it feels efficient, but because it exposes the difference between information that merely looks familiar and knowledge that is available when you need to think, solve problems, or make decisions. Research on retrieval practice consistently shows that attempting to recall information strengthens long term learning more effectively than repeatedly rereading it, while also revealing misunderstandings that passive review often conceals. [psychnet.wustl.edu] psychnet.wustl.edu Roediger Karpicke 2006 PPS The Power of Testing Memoryby HL Roediger III · Cited by 3302 — The experiment involves three phases— study, retrieval practice, and fina... Why rereading feels easier than recall Rereading creates a powerful illusion. On the second or third pass through a page, the material feels increasingly clear because the brain recognises words, sentences and diagrams it has seen before. That sense of ease is called processing fluency . Unfortunately, fluent reading is not reliable evidence that the knowledge can be retrieved later or applied in a different situation. [Structural Learning] structural learning.com fluency illusions students think they know Structural LearningFluency Illusions: Why Students Think They Know More29 Dec 2025 — Fluency illusions cause students to overestimate the... Close book recall deliberately removes that fluency. Once the notes disappear, the learner must reconstruct the idea without external support. If the explanation falls apart, that is not failure—it is accurate feedback . The exercise measures what is stored in memory rather than what is visible on the page. This distinction matters because analytical thinking depends on retrieval rather than recognition. When evaluating an argument, diagnosing a problem or explaining a concept to someone else, there are no highlighted notes available. The relevant ideas must be recalled, organised and connected in real time. Classic experiments by Henry Roediger and Jeffrey Karpicke demonstrated this difference. Students who repeatedly studied passages often performed well immediately afterwards, but those who spent more time recalling the material retained substantially more after a delay. The act of retrieval functioned as part of learning, not merely as an assessment of learning. [psychnet.wustl.edu] psychnet.wustl.edu Roediger Karpicke 2006 PPS The Power of Testing Memoryby HL Roediger III · Cited by 3302 — The experiment involves three phases— study, retrieval practice, and fina... A simple close book recall routine The goal is not to reproduce every sentence perfectly. The goal is to reconstruct the important ideas from memory, identify what is missing, then repair only those gaps. A practical routine looks like this: 1. Study briefly. Read or watch the material until you understand its main ideas. 2. Remove the source completely. Close the book, minimise the document or put the notes away. 3. Reconstruct from memory. Write, sketch or explain aloud everything you remember without checking. 4. Compare with the original. Look for omissions, incorrect relationships and inaccurate details rather than small wording differences. 5. Repair the gaps. Restudy only the missing or mistaken parts. 6. Repeat later. Return after several hours or days and perform another close book recall rather than another uninterrupted reread. Notice that most of the time is spent attempting retrieval, not reviewing. The comparison stage is essential because it prevents errors from becoming permanent while keeping review tightly focused. For conceptual material, a useful prompt is, \"Could I teach this to someone with no notes?\" For analytical subjects, ask, \"Could I explain why this method works and when it would fail?\" These questions require reconstruction instead of recognition. The Testing Effect: The Science of How To Actually Remember Channel: Dr. Erik Albinson Learning & Performance Coach · Views: 740 · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube What successful recall actually tells you Being able to retrieve an idea without looking demonstrates more than memorisation. It suggests that you can: identify the central concepts without prompts; organise ideas into a coherent explanation; distinguish important points from supporting detail; recognise where your understanding becomes uncertain. Equally important is what unsuccessful recall reveals. Learners often discover that they remember definitions but cannot explain relationships, recall examples but not underlying principles, or remember conclusions without the reasoning that produced them. These are precisely the weaknesses that passive review tends to hide. Because retrieval exposes these weaknesses, it improves calibration —the match between confidence and actual understanding. Studies of student learning repeatedly show that learners tend to overestimate their mastery after rereading but judge their knowledge more accurately after attempting retrieval. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Do students practise retrieval when they study on their own?Repeated testing enhances learning more than repeated reading, wh... How to correct gaps without overstudying The purpose of close book recall is not to create endless testing . It is to make review efficient. When comparing your reconstruction with the source, separate mistakes into three categories: Missing ideas: concepts you forgot entirely. Incomplete explanations: ideas you remembered but could not fully explain. Misconceptions: explanations that were confidently wrong. Each category deserves a different response. Missing ideas usually require another brief review followed by another recall attempt. Incomplete explanations often improve by writing a simpler explanation in your own words and testing yourself again after a short delay. Misconceptions deserve the most attention because incorrect mental models are harder to replace than missing facts. Correct them immediately, then perform another recall attempt without looking. Avoid rereading the entire chapter simply because one paragraph caused difficulty. Retrieval practice is most efficient when correction is targeted rather than global. Active Recall vs Passive Learning What the Research Says Channel: Decroly Education Centre Open on YouTube Why struggling is often a good sign Many learners abandon close book recall because it feels slower and more frustrating than rereading. That discomfort is expected. Robert and Elizabeth Bjork describe retrieval as a \"desirable difficulty\": a challenge that reduces short term performance but improves long term learning when it remains achievable. The effort required to reconstruct information appears to strengthen later access to that information. [Bjork Lab] bjorklab.psych.ucla.edu EBjork RBjork 2011 ▻ How did you get interested...Read more... The important qualification is that the difficulty must be productive rather than overwhelming. If nothing can be recalled, the learner probably needs another brief exposure before trying again. If almost everything can be recalled effortlessly every time, the task may no longer be demanding enough to reveal remaining weaknesses. The most productive point lies between those extremes: retrieval should require genuine mental effort while remaining largely successful. Active Recall: Why the Best Study Method Feels Like the Worst Channel: Glasp Open on YouTube Common mistakes that reduce the value of close book recall Several habits weaken the method without learners noticing. Checking too quickly. Looking back after the first hesitation prevents genuine retrieval. Using recognition as evidence. Saying \"I knew that\" after reading the answer is not the same as producing it independently. Copying instead of reconstructing. Rewriting notes while looking at them provides little information about understanding. Only recalling facts. Analytical subjects also require explaining causes, assumptions , comparisons and applications. Stopping after one successful attempt. Knowledge that is retrievable today may not remain available after several days unless retrieval is repeated. These"},{"title":"Transfer","url":"/transfer-621cf2/","description":"Applying a concept to a new case tests whether understanding survives beyond the example that first taught it.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Active Learning","level":3,"text":"Transfer Can the Idea Survive a New Example? Transfer Transfer Can the Idea Survive a New Example? Applying a concept to a new case tests whether understanding survives beyond the example that first taught it. Applying a concept to a new case tests whether understanding survives beyond the example that first taught it. population context Why familiar examples can hide shallow learning How to choose a fresh case Checking whether transfer really happened improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning transfer Introduction The strongest sign that you truly understand an idea is not that you can recognise it in the example that taught it, but that you can use it successfully in a different situation. This ability, known as transfer , is central to improving analytical thinking. Active learning becomes far more valuable when it asks you to apply a concept to a fresh case rather than simply repeat a familiar one. Research on retrieval practice , analogical reasoning and learning transfer consistently shows that changing the context forces learners to rely on underlying principles instead of surface features, making knowledge more flexible and more likely to be used in real decisions. [PMC+2pdf.retrievalpractice.org] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Far transfer of retrieval practice benefits: rule based learning...by B Opitz · 2024 · Cited by 4 — Benefits of self testing for lear... Within an active learning routine, introducing fresh examples is therefore not an optional extra. It is the test that reveals whether your reasoning has become portable. Why familiar examples can hide shallow learning A learner who repeatedly studies the same example often becomes highly fluent at recalling its details. That fluency can create the illusion of understanding even when the underlying reasoning has not been generalised. Imagine learning confirmation bias by reading only about investment decisions. After several reviews, you may easily identify confirmation bias in stock market examples. But if you fail to notice the same pattern in medical choices, hiring decisions or online debates, the concept has remained tied to one setting instead of becoming a general reasoning tool. This distinction mirrors the difference between near transfer and far transfer : Near transfer means applying an idea to a very similar situation. Far transfer means recognising the same underlying principle despite substantial changes in context, subject or appearance. [TeacherToolkit] teachertoolkit.co.uk The science of effective learning with spacing and retrieval...by SK Carpenter · Cited by 289 — Simple transfer is sometim... Far transfer is more difficult because people naturally focus on obvious surface similarities. Effective analytical thinkers instead search for deeper structural relationships. How to choose a fresh case The goal is not simply to make examples different. The new example should preserve the same reasoning structure while changing the surrounding details. Useful ways to create fresh examples include: Switch domains. Apply an economic principle to environmental policy, or a scientific reasoning method to evaluating news reports. Reverse the perspective. Instead of identifying flaws in someone else's argument, analyse one of your own previous decisions. Change the scale. Move from individual behaviour to organisations, or from international events to everyday situations. Alter the evidence. Replace numerical data with written testimony, diagrams or competing explanations while asking the same analytical question. For example: Original learning exampleFresh transfer exampleIdentify survivorship bias in successful start up stories.Look for survivorship bias when judging exercise programmes promoted by successful athletes.Evaluate correlation versus causation in health research.Evaluate correlation versus causation in crime statistics reported in the media.Estimate opportunity cost when choosing investments.Estimate opportunity cost when deciding how to spend limited study time before an exam. In every case, the principle stays constant while the context changes. Transfer of Learning Channel: The Learning Scientists Open on YouTube Why analogy helps transfer One of the most effective ways to promote transfer is through analogical reasoning —finding the same relational structure beneath different situations. Rather than asking, \"Have I seen this example before?\", analogical reasoning asks, \"What pattern is shared between these apparently different problems?\" For instance: Negotiating a business contract and dividing household responsibilities both involve balancing competing interests rather than maximising one side's gain. Debugging computer code and diagnosing a mechanical fault both require generating competing hypotheses and eliminating them with evidence. Evaluating a historical source and assessing a viral social media claim both involve judging reliability, bias and supporting evidence. Research shows that comparing multiple examples encourages learners to extract the underlying schema—the abstract structure that can later be applied elsewhere—instead of memorising isolated cases. Self explanation further strengthens this process because learners actively describe why the situations are equivalent rather than merely noticing that they look similar. [DigitalCommons+2PMC] digitalcommons.sacredheart.edu Analogical Reasoning: A Process for Fostering Learning...by TE Speicher · 2009 · Cited by 18 — This study showed the value... Fresh examples that strengthen analytical thinking Many reasoning skills can be deliberately transferred across unrelated contexts. Evaluating evidence Instead of analysing only scientific studies: compare eyewitness testimony, company earnings reports, historical documents, opinion polls. The core questions remain: What is the evidence? How reliable is it? What alternative explanations exist? Recognising cognitive biases Instead of studying confirmation bias only in politics: examine product reviews, sporting decisions, workplace disagreements, personal financial choices. Bias becomes easier to recognise because it is detached from one emotionally familiar setting. Building causal explanations Rather than repeatedly explaining why one business succeeded, explain : why a city reduced traffic congestion, why an ecological system changed, why a software project failed, why an educational intervention worked. Different facts, same reasoning process. Episode 28: All About Part Whole Systems Channel: The Cabrera Lab Podcast Open on YouTube Checking whether transfer really happened Using a fresh example is only valuable if it genuinely requires new reasoning rather than simple pattern matching. A practical self check includes three questions: 1. Could I identify the relevant principle without being told? 2. Can I explain why this new case shares the same structure as the original one? 3. Can I predict what should happen before checking the answer? If you need to see the original worked example before solving the new case, transfer has probably not yet occurred. Retrieval practice can also be adapted specifically for transfer. Instead of recalling isolated facts, retrieve broad principles and deliberately apply them to unfamiliar scenarios. Reviews of retrieval practice research indicate that such approaches improve not only retention but also the ability to apply knowledge beyond the original learning context, although transfer tends to be stronger when practice requires explanation and flexible use rather than simple recall . [pdf.retrievalpractice.org+2PMC] pdf.retrievalpractice.org Transfer Guide RETRIEVAL PRACTICE AND TRANSFER OF LEARNING:2020 · Cited by 20 — Based on rigorous scientific research, here we share three retrieval pra... Common mistakes when creating new examples Changing examples is not enough if the learner can still rely on superficial similarities. Frequent pitfalls include: changing only names while leaving every other feature identical; practising only within one subject area; selecting examples whose solutions are already memorised; focusing on facts instead of identifying the governing principle; assuming that successful recall automatically means successful transfer. Research on learning transfer suggests that far transfer rarely emerges automatically. Learners benefit when instructors or self directed learners explicitly compare multiple cases, highlight common relationships and encourage explanation of why one principle applies across different situations. [TeacherToolkit+2DigitalCommons] teachertoolkit.co.uk The science of effective learning with spacing and retrieval...by SK Carpenter · Cited by 289 — Simple transfer is sometim... If You Have Too Many Interests, You Are Probably Built For The 'M Shaped' Future Channel: PsychologyAboutYou Open on YouTube Making transfer a regular habit A simple way to build transfer into active learning is to finish every study session with one unfamiliar application"},{"title":"Testing","url":"/testing/","description":"Practice tests improve thinking because answering from memory strengthens recall and reveals mistakes before they matter.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Active Learning","level":3,"text":"Testing Why Tests Can Teach Better Than Review Testing Testing Why Tests Can Teach Better Than Review Practice tests improve thinking because answering from memory strengthens recall and reveals mistakes before they matter. Practice tests improve thinking because answering from memory strengthens recall and reveals mistakes before they matter. dataset evidence The testing effect in plain language Why retrieval changes long term memory How to use low stakes practice tests improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning testing Introduction Practice testing is more than a way of checking what you know. Decades of research show that attempting to recall information from memory is itself a powerful learning activity. Rather than simply revealing whether learning has happened, a well designed practice test helps create learning by strengthening memory, exposing gaps in understanding, and making knowledge easier to use later. This is why practice testing sits at the heart of active learning . Instead of asking, “Have I seen this before?”, it asks the more demanding question, “Can I produce it without looking?” That shift from recognition to retrieval is one of the most reliable ways to improve long term retention and support analytical thinking. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov taking memory tests improves long term retentionby HL Roediger · 2006 · Cited by 4857 — Taking a memory test not only assesses what... The testing effect in plain language The testing effect refers to the finding that retrieving information from memory improves later learning more than simply reviewing the same material. A learner who closes their notes and answers questions from memory often remembers the material better days or weeks later than someone who spends the same amount of time rereading. One of the most influential demonstrations came from research by Henry Roediger and Jeffrey Karpicke. Students studied short prose passages and either repeatedly reread them or practised recalling them. Although rereading sometimes produced better immediate performance, the advantage disappeared over longer delays. After a week, learners who had spent more time retrieving the information remembered substantially more than those who had mainly restudied it. The study helped establish that a test can function as a learning event rather than merely an assessment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov taking memory tests improves long term retentionby HL Roediger · 2006 · Cited by 4857 — Taking a memory test not only assesses what... The finding has since been replicated across many types of material, including vocabulary, scientific concepts, history, medicine and classroom learning. Reviews of the evidence consistently identify practice testing as one of the highest utility study techniques available when the goal is durable learning rather than short lived familiarity . [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Testing Improves Performance as Well as Assesses Learningby CW Polack · 2022 · Cited by 46 — Taking a test of previously studied mater... Henry Roediger Retrieval Practice to Enhance Learning and Retention Channel: Montana State University Open on YouTube Why retrieval changes long term memory Retrieval practice is effective because it makes the learner reconstruct knowledge rather than merely recognise it. That reconstruction appears to strengthen the memory itself while also improving the ability to locate and use the information later. Several mechanisms are thought to contribute: Memory becomes more accessible. Successfully recalling an idea strengthens the pathways needed to retrieve it again in the future. Knowledge becomes better organised. Retrieval encourages learners to connect ideas instead of storing isolated facts, making later reasoning easier. Weaknesses become visible. Failed retrieval attempts reveal exactly what has not yet been learned, allowing targeted correction instead of false confidence. Future learning improves. Testing previously learned material can reduce interference from earlier information and make it easier to absorb new material that follows. Researchers describe this as the \"forward effect\" of testing. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Retrieval practice enhances new learning: the forward effect of...by B Pastötter · 2014 · Cited by 276 — The review discusses current... These mechanisms matter for analytical thinking because analysis depends on being able to call relevant concepts into working memory when evaluating evidence, comparing explanations or solving unfamiliar problems. Knowledge that can only be recognised on a page is far less useful than knowledge that can be retrieved when needed. Practice tests measure understanding differently from rereading Rereading often creates an illusion of mastery because familiar material feels easier to process each time it is encountered. Practice testing provides a more demanding and more informative signal. For example, someone studying logical fallacies may recognise examples while reading a textbook yet struggle to identify those same fallacies in a newspaper article without prompts. A retrieval exercise immediately exposes this gap. The temporary discomfort of failing to recall is valuable because it identifies where further learning is needed before those weaknesses appear in an examination or real world decision. In this way, practice tests serve two purposes simultaneously: they strengthen memory and improve self assessment. Instead of relying on feelings of familiarity, learners obtain direct evidence of what they can actually produce independently. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Testing Improves Performance as Well as Assesses Learningby CW Polack · 2022 · Cited by 46 — Taking a test of previously studied mater... How to pronounce RETRIEVAL in British English Channel: English with Collins Dictionary Open on YouTube Does testing only improve memory, or does it improve thinking? Practice testing is primarily a memory intervention, but its benefits extend beyond simple recall when the questions require meaningful retrieval. Research has found that retrieval practice can improve the transfer of learning—the ability to apply previously learned knowledge in new situations—particularly when practice questions resemble the kinds of reasoning learners will later perform. Explaining causes, comparing alternatives, interpreting evidence and solving novel problems all require retrieving and reorganising knowledge rather than repeating isolated facts. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Testing effect Testing effect The implication is important for improving analytical skills. If practice tests ask only for memorised definitions, they mainly strengthen factual recall. If they require learners to justify conclusions, critique arguments or apply concepts to unfamiliar cases, retrieval practice also strengthens the mental operations involved in analysis. How to use low stakes practice tests Practice testing works best when the goal is learning rather than judgement. Low stakes quizzes reduce anxiety while preserving the benefits of retrieval. Effective approaches include: 1. Answer before checking notes. Even unsuccessful retrieval attempts can improve later learning when followed by correction. 2. Use short answer questions before multiple choice. Producing an answer from memory generally requires more retrieval than recognising one from a list. 3. Check feedback immediately after attempting recall. Corrective feedback prevents errors from becoming reinforced while consolidating accurate knowledge. 4. Repeat retrieval over time. Revisiting material after delays strengthens long term retention more effectively than massed practice in one session. How To study EFFECTIVELY For Exams Scientifically Proven Techniques Channel: Noyan Muini Open on YouTube 5. Mix old and new topics. Returning to earlier material encourages flexible retrieval and reduces forgetting across longer periods. PMC+2PMC Importantly, practice tests do not need to resemble formal examinations. Flashcards requiring free recall, self generated questions, explaining a concept aloud, writing everything remembered on a blank sheet of paper, or solving new problems without reference materials all function as forms of retrieval practice. Common misunderstandings One misconception is that practice tests are useful only for preparing for examinations. In reality, their greatest value is often during learning itself, when mistakes still have time to be corrected. Another misunderstanding is that retrieval practice should replace studying altogether. It does not. Learners still need an initial opportunity to understand new material. Retrieval becomes valuable once there is something meaningful to recall. Many successful study routines therefore alternate between brief study periods and repeated retrieval attempts rather than separating learning and testing into different stages. Finally, people sometimes believe that struggling to remember means learning has"},{"title":"Predict","url":"/predict/","description":"Making a prediction before checking the answer turns passive reading into a comparison between expectation and evidence.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Active Learning","level":3,"text":"Predict Predict First, Then Let Evidence Correct You Predict Predict Predict First, Then Let Evidence Correct You Making a prediction before checking the answer turns passive reading into a comparison between expectation and evidence. Making a prediction before checking the answer turns passive reading into a comparison between expectation and evidence. policy intervention Why prediction makes reading active How to write useful pre reading predictions What to do when evidence disagrees improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning predict Introduction One of the simplest ways to turn passive reading into active thinking is to make a prediction before reading an analysis. Rather than immediately accepting an author's explanation, you first commit yourself to an expectation: what you think the conclusion will be, which evidence will matter most, or how the argument is likely to unfold. Once you read the analysis, you compare your prediction with the evidence instead of merely recognising familiar ideas. This approach matters because it exposes assumptions that would otherwise remain hidden. A prediction creates a record of your current mental model. Whether the prediction proves right or wrong, the comparison that follows helps you understand not only the topic but also how your own reasoning works. Research on prediction, pretesting, and errorful generation suggests that attempting an answer before receiving instruction can improve later learning, even when the initial answer is incorrect. [PMC+2Springer Nature Link] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredicting as a learning strategy NIHby G Brod · 2021 · Cited by 77 — This article attempts to delineate the procedural and mechanistic characteristics of predicting... Why prediction makes reading active Reading an expert analysis is often deceptively easy. Because the finished argument is coherent, readers can mistake agreement with understanding. Prediction interrupts this passive process by forcing the reader to generate an explanation before seeing the author's. Instead of asking, \"Do I understand this analysis?\", prediction asks, \"What do I think explains this?\" That subtle shift changes the role of the reader from observer to participant. Several cognitive processes appear to contribute: Activation of prior knowledge. Predicting requires recalling relevant ideas before new information arrives, making it easier to connect incoming evidence with existing knowledge. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredicting as a learning strategy NIHby G Brod · 2021 · Cited by 77 — This article attempts to delineate the procedural and mechanistic characteristics of predicting... Attention to diagnostic evidence. Once a prediction exists, readers naturally notice which pieces of evidence confirm or contradict it. Metacognitive calibration. Comparing prediction with reality reveals whether confidence matched accuracy, improving judgement about one's own understanding over time. Deeper encoding. Generating an answer—even an incomplete one—often produces stronger memory than simply receiving the answer because the learner actively constructs rather than passively receives information. [PMC+2OSF] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredicting as a learning strategy NIHby G Brod · 2021 · Cited by 77 — This article attempts to delineate the procedural and mechanistic characteristics of predicting... The goal is not to predict correctly. The goal is to create something that can later be evaluated. 0202: Active Reading and Note Taking: How to Activate Your Reading Channel: Columbia University Center for Veteran Transition and Integration Open on YouTube How to write useful pre reading predictions Effective predictions are brief, specific, and testable. They should make your current reasoning visible without attempting to reproduce the entire forthcoming analysis. Before opening an article or expert explanation, write one or more statements such as: \"I think the main cause will be...\" \"The strongest evidence will probably come from...\" \"I expect explanation A to be stronger than explanation B because...\" \"If I had to recommend a decision now, I would choose...\" Keep predictions concise—usually one to three sentences. Long speculative essays become difficult to compare against the actual analysis. Good predictions also include a reason. Instead of writing: \"I think the policy failed.\" write: \"I think the policy failed because it created incentives that people could easily avoid.\" The explanation exposes the assumptions that can later be confirmed, refined, or rejected. What to do when evidence disagrees The most valuable outcome is often discovering that your prediction was wrong. Many learners instinctively treat an incorrect prediction as failure. Research suggests the opposite can occur. Attempting an answer before instruction—even unsuccessfully—can improve later learning because the subsequent correction becomes more meaningful. This phenomenon has been demonstrated across a variety of materials including text, lectures, and factual learning, although the size of the benefit depends on factors such as the type of material, feedback , and task design. [Springer Nature Link+2PMC] link.springer.com Springer Nature LinkPrequestioning and Pretesting Effects: a Review of Empirical...by SC Pan · 2023 · Cited by 104 — In this review, we... When your prediction differs from the evidence, avoid simply replacing the old answer with the new one. Instead, ask three questions: 1. Which assumption turned out to be incorrect? 2. What evidence did I overlook or underestimate? 3. Would my original reasoning work in any similar situations, or was the underlying model flawed? These questions transform disagreement into an opportunity for model revision rather than mere correction. For example, if you predicted that a company's falling profits resulted from declining demand, but the analysis shows that supply chain disruptions were the dominant cause, the important lesson is not merely the correct answer. It is recognising which cues led you to favour the wrong explanation and why those cues were insufficient. if you've never written an essay for fun, you're making yourself dumber Channel: erin meryl study Open on YouTube Common mistakes that reduce the benefit Prediction is effective only when it genuinely commits you to an expectation before seeing the answer. Several habits weaken the exercise: Making vague predictions. Statements that could fit almost any outcome (\"There will be several factors\") cannot meaningfully be evaluated. Changing predictions while reading. Revising your guess halfway through removes the comparison that drives learning. Treating wrong predictions as embarrassing. Incorrect predictions often generate the strongest opportunities for conceptual change when followed by clear feedback. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredicting as a learning strategy NIHby G Brod · 2021 · Cited by 77 — This article attempts to delineate the procedural and mechanistic characteristics of predicting... Skipping reflection after reading. Without explicitly comparing prediction and evidence, much of the metacognitive value is lost. Another subtle mistake is predicting what the author will say based on style or reputation instead of predicting what you genuinely believe. The exercise is designed to reveal your own reasoning, not your ability to anticipate another person's conclusions. Limits and when to use the strategy carefully Prediction is a useful learning tool, but it is not universally beneficial. Research generally supports prediction and pretesting for promoting later learning, yet the effects vary across subjects, learner characteristics, and instructional designs. Some studies have found limited or even negative effects in particular contexts, especially when predictions become overly speculative, when readers lack enough background knowledge to generate meaningful expectations, or when misconceptions are left uncorrected. Prompt, accurate feedback is therefore an important part of the process. [Springer Nature Link+2EasyChair] link.springer.com Springer Nature LinkPrequestioning and Pretesting Effects: a Review of Empirical...by SC Pan · 2023 · Cited by 104 — In this review, we... Prediction is therefore best used when: the learner possesses at least some relevant prior knowledge; corrective evidence follows soon afterwards; the comparison between prediction and evidence is explicit rather than assumed. For complete beginners, predictions should focus on broad mechanisms or expectations rather than precise technical details that cannot yet be reasoned about. Turning prediction into a lasting thinking habit Within active learning , prediction works because it creates a visible record of thought before evidence arrives. Instead of judging an analysis only by whether it seems convincing, you also examine how your own expectations changed. A simple routine is enough: 1. Read only the title or question. 2. Write a short"},{"title":"Explain","url":"/explain/","description":"Explaining each step in your own words makes hidden assumptions, weak links and misconceptions easier to notice.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Active Learning","level":3,"text":"Explain The Missing Step You Notice When Explaining Explain Explain The Missing Step You Notice When Explaining Explaining each step in your own words makes hidden assumptions, weak links and misconceptions easier to notice. Explaining each step in your own words makes hidden assumptions, weak links and misconceptions easier to notice. mechanism What self explanation asks the mind to do How explanations reveal weak links Prompts for analytical self explanation improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning explain Introduction Self explanation is the practice of explaining an idea, argument or solution to yourself in your own words rather than simply rereading or accepting it. Within active learning , its distinctive value is not that it helps you speak more fluently, but that it exposes reasoning that was previously invisible. When you try to explain why each step follows from the previous one, hidden assumptions , missing evidence and weak logical connections become much easier to detect. Research over several decades has shown that learners who actively generate explanations often develop deeper understanding and transfer their knowledge more effectively than learners who merely study the same material. [Andy Matuschak+2ResearchGate] andymatuschak.org Chi et al 1994 Eliciting self explanations improves understanding Andy MatuschakEliciting Self Explanations Improves Understandingby MTH CHI · 1994 · Cited by 4299 — Here, we explore the generality of th... Unlike passive review, self explanation makes your thinking inspectable. Instead of asking, “Do I recognise this?”, it asks, “Can I justify why this step is true?” That small change shifts attention from familiarity to reasoning quality, making it particularly valuable for improving analytical skills. What self explanation asks the mind to do A good self explanation is not a paraphrase of what was read. It is an attempt to reconstruct why the information makes sense. When people explain a worked example or an argument to themselves, they typically have to: [dictionary.cambridge.org] dictionary.cambridge.org English meaning Cambridge Dictionaryrelating to all parts of a nation or to a nation as a whole rather than to any part of it: Nation... identify the goal of each step; connect the current step with previous knowledge; explain why one conclusion follows from another; notice where information has been assumed rather than demonstrated; recognise uncertainty instead of filling gaps with familiarity. This process is constructive rather than repetitive. Instead of repeating information already supplied, the learner generates additional reasoning that is absent from the original material. Research comparing self explanation with simple paraphrasing suggests that much of its benefit comes from this active generation rather than merely spending longer with the material. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The effect of self explanation on robust learningSelf explaining is a domain independent learning strategy that general... This is why explaining a proof, a statistical argument or a scientific mechanism often feels unexpectedly difficult. The difficulty signals that your mind is trying to build the missing logical connections rather than simply recognising sentences it has already seen. How explanations reveal weak links Reasoning failures often remain hidden until someone tries to explain them. Consider a simple chain of reasoning: Self Explanation: Help Students Connect and Deepen Learning Channel: IRIS Connect Open on YouTube 1. The evidence supports claim A. 2. Therefore claim B is probably true. 3. Therefore policy C should be adopted. While reading, this sequence may appear perfectly coherent. During self explanation, however, several questions naturally arise: Why does A support B? Are there alternative explanations for the evidence? What assumptions connect B with C? Could a different policy follow from the same evidence? These questions expose what psychologists sometimes describe as \"knowledge gaps\"—places where the learner believed they understood but had never actually constructed the intermediate reasoning. Studies of successful learners solving worked examples found that they spontaneously generated explanations connecting solution steps, whereas less successful learners tended to focus on surface features without explaining why the procedures worked. [Andy Matuschak] andymatuschak.org Chi et al 1994 Eliciting self explanations improves understanding Andy MatuschakEliciting Self Explanations Improves Understandingby MTH CHI · 1994 · Cited by 4299 — Here, we explore the generality of th... In analytical work, these missing links often take familiar forms: Hidden assumptions: accepting an unstated premise without noticing it. Missing causal links: describing what happened without explaining why. Unjustified generalisations: moving from one example to a broad rule. Unsupported conclusions: reaching a claim that exceeds the available evidence. Confused definitions: using important terms consistently without being able to define them precisely. Because self explanation forces each transition to be justified, these weaknesses become visible before they become habits. Why generating explanations matters more than rereading One important finding from research is that the act of producing an explanation appears to contribute independently to learning. Researchers have compared learners who simply reread worked examples with learners who explain each solution step. Others have compared complete examples with examples that intentionally omit intermediate reasoning. Across many studies, learners who actively generated missing explanations typically achieved stronger conceptual understanding and transferred their knowledge more effectively to new problems. [ResearchGate+2eScholarship] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The effect of self explanation on robust learningSelf explaining is a domain independent learning strategy that general... This distinction matters because understanding is not merely possessing information. It is possessing enough connected reasoning to reconstruct an answer in unfamiliar situations. An incomplete explanation therefore becomes useful rather than frustrating. Instead of treating the missing step as an inconvenience, self explanation treats it as an opportunity to build the reasoning yourself. Prompts for analytical self explanation The quality of self explanation depends less on length than on the questions being asked. Prompts that focus attention on reasoning are consistently more effective than prompts encouraging simple summary. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICSelf explanation prompts explained April 7, 2022 — by K McGinn · 2019 · Cited by 3 — In this article Kelly, Laura and Julie explain the benefits of using self explanation p... Published: April 7, 2022 Useful prompts include: Why is this step necessary rather than merely convenient? What evidence supports this conclusion? Which assumption is being used here? How would I justify this to someone who disagreed? Could the same evidence support another explanation? What would have to be false for this reasoning to fail? Which previous idea does this step depend upon? Can I explain this without repeating the author's wording? These prompts encourage inspection of reasoning rather than memorisation of conclusions. When reviewing a worked example, another productive question is: [dictionary.cambridge.org] dictionary.cambridge.org English meaning Cambridge Dictionaryrelating to all parts of a nation or to a nation as a whole rather than to any part of it: Nation... \"If this line were removed, could I reconstruct it?\" If the answer is no, that missing reasoning deserves attention before moving on. How to Use the Feynman Technique Study Tips How to Study Channel: Socratica Open on YouTube A practical example Imagine reading an article claiming that a new policy reduced traffic congestion. Passive review might produce the impression that the argument is convincing. A self explanation proceeds differently: [researchgate.net] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The effect of self explanation on robust learningSelf explaining is a domain independent learning strategy that general... \"The policy reduced congestion because drivers changed routes.\" \"How do we know drivers changed routes?\" \"The article cites traffic counts.\" \"Could traffic simply have fallen overall?\" \"Did anything else happen during the same period?\" \"Would the conclusion still hold without assuming comparable weather and travel demand?\" Nothing has been memorised differently. Instead, the reasoning itself has been examined. Missing assumptions become explicit, and uncertainty becomes easier to recognise. The same approach applies to mathematics, programming, scientific papers, legal reasoning and everyday decision making because the mechanism is the same: every conclusion depends on intermediate steps that can"},{"title":"Work Rehearsal","url":"/work-rehearsal/","description":"Workplace learning becomes more useful when teams practise the judgments they will later need under real pressure.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Active Learning","level":3,"text":"Work Rehearsal Training That Rehearses the Real Decision Work Rehearsal Work Rehearsal Training That Rehearses the Real Decision Workplace learning becomes more useful when teams practise the judgments they will later need under real pressure. Workplace learning becomes more useful when teams practise the judgments they will later need under real pressure. population context Why briefings do not prove usable skill Premortems, incident reviews and case classification Designing practice around real workplace decisions improving your thinking and analytical skills active learning work rehearsal Introduction Active learning becomes genuinely valuable at work when it rehearses the decisions employees will later have to make under real constraints. Reading procedures, attending briefings and completing compliance modules can increase familiarity , but they do not prove that people can recognise a situation, weigh competing evidence and act appropriately when time is limited, information is incomplete or consequences are significant. The strongest workplace learning therefore shifts from information delivery to structured decision rehearsal: practising the judgement itself rather than merely reviewing the rules. Research from organisational psychology, naturalistic decision making and simulation based learning consistently shows that realistic practice, combined with structured reflection, improves the transfer of knowledge into performance more effectively than passive instruction alone. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Naturalistic Decision Making in After Action Review Meetingsby R Reiter Palmon · 2014 · Cited by 71 — The purpose of this study was to... Why briefings do not prove usable skill Many organisations still equate training with exposure to information. Employees attend presentations, read policy documents or complete online modules, then are assumed to be prepared. The weakness is that recognising information is different from applying it in an ambiguous situation. Real workplace decisions rarely resemble textbook examples. A project manager must decide whether a schedule slip justifies escalation. A customer service adviser must distinguish between a difficult client and a genuine safeguarding concern. A cybersecurity analyst must decide whether unusual network activity represents a harmless anomaly or the beginning of an attack. None of these judgements depends solely on remembering facts. They require recognising patterns, weighing uncertainty and anticipating consequences. Research on naturalistic decision making emerged precisely because laboratory decision models often failed to explain how experienced professionals actually work. Studies of firefighters, military commanders, emergency clinicians and other experts found that they frequently identify workable options by recognising familiar patterns and mentally simulating likely outcomes instead of comparing long lists of alternatives. Such expertise develops through repeated exposure to realistic cases rather than repeated exposure to written information. [ResearchGate+2Sage Journals] researchgate.net Investigations of Naturalistic Decision Making and the...March 1, 1996 — 25 Nov 2015 — This monograph reviews 3 years of res... Published: March 1, 1996 This helps explain why workplace learning often disappoints. Employees may pass a knowledge assessment immediately after training yet struggle months later because they never rehearsed recognising when the knowledge should be applied. Why realistic rehearsal changes judgement Decision rehearsal works because it strengthens several abilities simultaneously. Pattern recognition: learners begin noticing cues that distinguish routine situations from exceptional ones. Mental simulation: they practise imagining how different actions might unfold before committing to one. Calibration: they discover where confidence exceeds competence because scenarios expose mistakes safely. Shared judgement: teams develop common expectations about what good decisions look like under pressure. Simulation based professional education repeatedly finds that learners benefit from making realistic decisions, receiving immediate feedback and repeating challenging situations until sound judgement becomes more automatic. The value lies not simply in \"learning by doing\" but in practising the same cognitive work required during real operations. Springer Nature Link+2Northumbria University Research Portal [link.springer.com] link.springer.com Springer Nature LinkSimulation based training in professional education: learning...by C Sellberg · 2026 · Cited by 6 — Simulations prov... Importantly, the rehearsal should preserve uncertainty. If every exercise has one obvious correct answer, participants learn to identify the trainer's preferred response rather than improving real judgement. Re Broadcast Using PreMortem Analysis to Make Better Decisions with Gary A. Klein Channel: Red Team Thinking Open on YouTube Premortems, incident reviews and case classification Premortems: rehearsing failure before it happens One of the simplest ways to rehearse judgement is the premortem, developed by Gary Klein. Instead of asking, \"What could go wrong?\", participants imagine that a project has already failed and independently write down the reasons why. This subtle change matters because it reduces social pressure to appear optimistic and encourages people to voice concerns they might otherwise suppress. Klein linked the technique to research on \"prospective hindsight\", which found that imagining an event has already occurred improves people's ability to identify plausible causes. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org He is the author Harvard Business ReviewPerforming a Project PremortemSeptember 1, 2007 — Gary Klein (gary@decisionmaking.com) is the chief scientist of K... Published: September 1, 2007 Premortems do more than generate risk lists. They rehearse an important workplace judgement: identifying weak signals before commitment makes changing course politically difficult. Incident reviews that improve future decisions Post incident reviews often become exercises in assigning blame or documenting compliance. Effective reviews instead reconstruct the decisions that participants faced using only the information available at the time. The goal is not to ask why people failed to predict an outcome that is now obvious, but how the situation appeared before the result was known. This distinction reduces hindsight bias and produces lessons that transfer to future situations. Research on After Action Reviews (AARs) shows that structured discussion of what happened, why it happened and what should change can improve future decision making, particularly when participants openly discuss reasoning rather than merely listing procedural errors. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Naturalistic Decision Making in After Action Review Meetingsby R Reiter Palmon · 2014 · Cited by 71 — The purpose of this study was to... Case classification builds better recognition Another overlooked form of rehearsal involves comparing multiple cases instead of studying each individually. For example, fraud investigators might classify previous incidents by behavioural warning signs rather than by financial loss. Engineers may group equipment failures according to underlying mechanisms instead of component type. HR teams might sort employee relations cases by patterns of evidence rather than legal category. Classification forces learners to identify diagnostic features that separate superficially similar situations. This mirrors real work, where the first challenge is often recognising which kind of problem is actually present. Designing practice around real workplace decisions Effective workplace rehearsal begins by identifying the decisions that genuinely determine organisational performance rather than the information employees are expected to remember. A useful design process typically follows this sequence: 1. Identify high value decisions. Focus on recurring judgements with meaningful consequences, such as approving expenditure, diagnosing faults, responding to complaints or escalating risks. 2. Collect authentic cases. Use real incidents, anonymised where necessary, instead of invented examples whenever possible. 3. Preserve ambiguity. Include incomplete information, conflicting evidence and realistic time constraints. 4. Require explicit reasoning. Ask participants to explain not only what they chose but why. 5. Provide expert comparison. Feedback should compare reasoning processes, not simply reveal the correct answer. 6. Repeat with variation. Similar decisions presented in different contexts strengthen transferable judgement rather than memorised responses. This design reflects deliberate practice principles: repeated performance of clearly defined tasks with immediate feedback and opportunities for correction rather than passive review alone. [Northumbria University Research Portal]"},{"title":"Alternatives","url":"/alternatives/","description":"A strong explanation is not just plausible; it has survived comparison with serious alternatives.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Alternatives What Else Could Explain This? Alternatives Alternatives What Else Could Explain This? A strong explanation is not just plausible; it has survived comparison with serious alternatives. A strong explanation is not just plausible; it has survived comparison with serious alternatives. mechanism Generate rival explanations Compare fit with evidence Avoid satisfying stories improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives Introduction A strong explanation is not simply the first story that makes sense. It is the explanation that still looks good after being compared with serious rivals. In practical thinking, this means asking: what else could explain this evidence, and what evidence would separate the possibilities? The habit matters because coherent stories are easy to build after the fact. People often stop once they have a plausible account, then collect supporting details around it. Better analysis deliberately generates alternatives, looks for evidence that would count against each one, and resists the comfort of a tidy conclusion when several explanations remain live. Research on confirmation bias, “consider the opposite” interventions, scientific hypothesis testing , intelligence analysis, forensic reasoning and diagnostic error all points to the same mechanism: judgement improves when explanations compete against each other rather than being defended one at a time. [PubMed+2Springer Nature Link] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1165 — Individuals who are induced to co... Why the first coherent story is often too easy to believe The mind is good at turning scattered evidence into a narrative. That ability is useful: it helps us infer motives, diagnose everyday problems, and act before every uncertainty is resolved. The danger is that plausibility can arrive before comparison. Once a story explains several facts, it can feel stronger than it really is, even if another explanation would explain the same facts just as well. This is the central warning behind “inference to the best explanation”: the aim is to infer the hypothesis that best explains the evidence, not merely one that explains it. Contemporary overviews of the idea stress that explanatory virtues such as simplicity, scope and unification are useful but contested; philosophers still debate whether these virtues reliably track truth. That caveat is important for everyday reasoning. A simpler story may be cleaner, but a messier rival may fit the evidence better. [Springer Nature Link] link.springer.com Springer Nature LinkInference to the Best Explanation: An Overview Springer Nature Link... A familiar example is a delayed reply to an important message. One explanation is that the recipient is annoyed. But rival explanations are immediately available: they are travelling, busy, ill, avoiding all messages, unsure how to answer, or never saw it. The evidence “no reply” fits all of them. The explanation becomes stronger only when more diagnostic evidence appears: a pattern of avoidance, a later apology, a read receipt, a mutual friend’s update, or a reply to someone else on the same topic. The practical point is not to become paralysed by endless possibilities. It is to notice when a fact is compatible with a favoured story but not selective enough to support it over alternatives. Generate rival explanations before judging the evidence Rival hypotheses are not objections added at the end. They should be created early, before a preferred explanation hardens into the default. T. C. Chamberlin’s method of multiple working hypotheses, later discussed in modern scientific method debates, was designed to avoid the “ruling theory” problem: once a single hypothesis becomes personally or professionally attractive, the thinker may unconsciously arrange the evidence around it. [Auburn University] auburn.edu University The Method of Multiple Working Hypotheses Author(s 1964). Platt wrote: \"This charming paper deserves to be reprinted.\" Several readers, having...Read more... A useful rival explanation has three features. It is plausible enough to deserve testing, different enough to make distinct predictions, and specific enough that evidence could count against it. “Anything is possible” is not a useful alternative. “The sales drop came from a tracking error rather than lower demand” is useful because it points to different evidence: analytics logs, payment data, customer traffic, refund rates, and comparisons with unaffected channels. Good alternative generation often starts with a small set of prompts: Different cause: What other mechanism could produce the same observation? Different actor: Who else could have caused or influenced the outcome? Different timescale: Is this a temporary fluctuation rather than a trend? Different measurement: Could the evidence reflect how the data was collected? Different base rate: Is this event common enough that no special cause is needed? Different incentive: Who benefits from one interpretation being accepted? Different missing fact: What fact, if discovered, would make the current story collapse? The value of these questions is that they change the task from defending an answer to exploring a space of possibilities. That shift is especially important in ambiguous problems, where the available facts often fit several accounts at once. Compare fit, not just support The most common mistake in judging explanations is to ask only, “Does this evidence support my hypothesis?” A better question is, “Does this evidence support my hypothesis more than the alternatives?” This difference is the heart of serious evidential reasoning. Richards Heuer’s work on Analysis of Competing Hypotheses, developed for intelligence analysis, makes this point sharply. The method asks analysts to list plausible hypotheses, compare each piece of evidence against each hypothesis, and pay special attention to disconfirming evidence . Heuer argues that the most probable hypothesis is often the one with the least evidence against it, not the one with the most evidence in favour of it. [IALEIA] ialeia.org Psychology of Intelligence Analysis Psychology of Intelligence Analysis That principle is powerful because much evidence is non diagnostic. Suppose a company launches a new product and revenue rises. The increase is consistent with “the launch worked”, but it may also be consistent with seasonality, a competitor’s outage, a pricing change, a one off bulk purchase, a reporting change, or an advertising campaign that began earlier. The evidence matters most when it separates these explanations. For example, if new customers came mainly from the product’s target segment and began converting after launch exposure, the product explanation gains strength. If revenue rose across unrelated products and regions at the same time, the product story weakens. A compact comparison can prevent premature certainty: Evidence questionFavoured explanationRival explanationWhat would discriminate?Does the evidence uniquely fit one story?“The new training improved performance.”“The task got easier.”Compare with a control group or unchanged task.Does timing line up?“The policy caused the change.”“The trend had already begun.”Look at pre policy trend and comparable cases.Is the measure trustworthy?“Complaints fell.”“Reporting became harder.”Check independent measures, not just complaint counts.What evidence is missing?“The person intended harm.”“They made a mistake under pressure.”Look for prior warnings, incentives, communications, and context. This style of comparison does not require formal mathematics. It requires treating evidence as a filter. The best evidence is not the most vivid evidence; it is the evidence that rules out live alternatives. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): A Structured Analytic Technique (SAT) for FinCrime Channel: Atlas Analytics: Intelligence Analysis Open on YouTube Look for disconfirmation without pretending certainty is easy Many people think “being objective” means looking for more evidence. Often it means looking for a different kind of evidence: evidence that could show the explanation is wrong. The classic Wason rule discovery task illustrates the problem. Participants given the sequence “2 4 6” often tested examples that matched their current rule rather than examples that might falsify it. Recent research using a related task found that prompting people to think in opposites nearly doubled success and reduced repeated testing of the same hypothesis. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The older “consider the opposite” research in social judgement found a similar corrective effect. Lord, Lepper and Preston argued that several biases arise from failing to consider possibilities at odds with the current belief, and their experiments found that inducing people to consider the opposite reduced biased assimilation and biased hypothesis testing."},{"title":"Accident Chains","url":"/accident-chains/","description":"Serious accident inquiries show why visible first causes often give way to chains of mechanical, human, organizational, and environmental factors.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Alternatives","level":3,"text":"Accident Chains Why First Causes Miss the Chain Accident Chains Accident Chains Why First Causes Miss the Chain Serious accident inquiries show why visible first causes often give way to chains of mechanical, human, organizational, and environmental factors. Serious accident inquiries show why visible first causes often give way to chains of mechanical, human, organizational, and environmental factors. event time window Why early accident stories over focus on visible causes How fact gathering widens the explanation set What everyday analysis can learn from causal chains improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives accident chains Introduction Serious accident investigations are among the clearest demonstrations that the first explanation is rarely the best one. In the immediate aftermath of a disaster, attention often centres on the last visible event: a pilot's mistake, a broken component, a missed warning, or a poor decision. Yet formal investigations repeatedly show that catastrophic outcomes usually emerge from a chain of interacting causes rather than a single failure. Mechanical faults, human judgement, organisational policies, environmental conditions, training, communication, and chance often combine in ways that only become clear after systematic evidence gathering. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Understanding the “Swiss Cheese Model” and Its Application...by DA Wiegmann · 2022 · Cited by 204 — The Swiss Cheese Model is commonl... For anyone trying to improve analytical thinking, accident investigations offer a practical lesson: do not stop when you find a plausible cause. Keep asking what allowed that cause to exist, what alternative explanations fit the evidence, and what combination of factors made the outcome possible. Why early accident stories over focus on visible causes The first public account of an accident almost always relies on what is immediately observable. A crashed aircraft may appear to have fallen because a pilot made an error. A factory explosion may seem to result from one valve being left open. A train collision may initially be blamed on a signal passed at danger. These explanations are attractive because they identify an obvious event near the end of the causal sequence. They provide a simple story, identify responsibility quickly, and satisfy the natural desire for closure. Professional investigators deliberately resist this temptation. Their task is not simply to identify what happened last but to reconstruct the entire sequence that made the final event possible. The question changes from \"Who made the mistake?\" to \"Why did the system allow this mistake to become catastrophic?\" [Skybrary] skybrary.aero James Reason HF ModelThe Swiss Cheese model of accident causation, originally proposed by James Reason, likens human system defen... This distinction matters because many different causal chains can produce the same visible outcome. A driver crossing into the wrong lane could reflect fatigue, medical incapacity, poor road design, misleading signage, vehicle failure, distraction, or several of these together. Treating the first apparent cause as the only cause closes off rival explanations before they have been tested. How fact gathering widens the explanation set Formal accident inquiries expand the explanation space by collecting evidence from many independent sources before settling on conclusions. Rather than relying on eyewitness accounts alone, investigators compare multiple forms of evidence, including: Physical damage patterns. Electronic recordings and sensor data. Maintenance records. Weather observations. Training histories. Operating procedures. Communication transcripts. Organisational documentation. Human factors evidence such as fatigue or workload. Each new category of evidence may eliminate some hypotheses while strengthening others. This process illustrates an important reasoning principle: evidence should discriminate between competing explanations rather than merely fit one preferred story. If several hypotheses explain the same observation equally well, investigators seek new evidence that would be expected under one explanation but not another. The Swiss Cheese Model Explained: Why Disasters Really Happen Channel: HSEstudy Open on YouTube From single mistakes to causal chains Modern safety investigation increasingly views accidents as products of interacting systems rather than isolated failures. James Reason's influential \"Swiss Cheese Model\" illustrates this idea. Instead of imagining a single broken safeguard, it depicts multiple defensive layers, each containing weaknesses or \"holes\". Most hazards are stopped by one or more barriers. Catastrophe occurs only when weaknesses temporarily align across several layers, allowing an accident trajectory to pass through the entire system. [PMC+2EUROCONTROL] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Understanding the “Swiss Cheese Model” and Its Application...by DA Wiegmann · 2022 · Cited by 204 — The Swiss Cheese Model is commonl... In this framework investigators distinguish between: Active failures : immediate unsafe actions with direct consequences, such as an incorrect control input. Latent conditions : organisational decisions, maintenance practices, equipment design, staffing levels, training deficiencies, or cultural problems that remain hidden until combined with active failures. The practical consequence is profound. Removing one operator or replacing one component may prevent a repeat of one accident while leaving the underlying vulnerabilities untouched. Case study: the Tenerife airport disaster The 1977 runway collision at Tenerife remains one of the clearest examples of why first causes rarely tell the whole story. An immediate description might simply state that a captain began take off without clearance. The official investigation indeed identified the unauthorised take off as the fundamental triggering event. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Tenerife airport disaster Tenerife airport disaster However, the broader investigation uncovered a much richer causal chain involving: Heavy fog reducing visibility. Congestion after another airport closed. Unusual taxi procedures. Radio communication ambiguities. Simultaneous transmissions that obscured messages. Crew expectations and assumptions . Time pressure associated with operational constraints. None of these factors alone guaranteed disaster. Together they created conditions in which one mistaken interpretation became fatal. The investigation therefore transformed a seemingly simple explanation into a network of interacting contributors rather than a single act of negligence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Tenerife airport disaster Tenerife airport disaster Case study: the Challenger disaster The loss of the space shuttle Challenger in 1986 is often remembered as an engineering failure involving damaged O rings in unusually cold weather. Subsequent investigations revealed that the physical failure itself was only one link in a longer chain. Engineering concerns had been raised before launch, uncertainty was interpreted differently by managers and engineers, communication between organisational levels broke down, and schedule pressures influenced decision making. The lesson extends beyond engineering. The visible technical failure became understandable only after examining organisational judgement, information flow, and institutional incentives. Modern safety literature frequently uses Challenger as an example of latent organisational failures combining with immediate technical causes. [NHS Wales Performance and Improvement] performanceandimprovement.nhs.wales Wales Performance and Improvement Reasons Swiss Cheese Model NHS Wales Performance and ImprovementReasons Swiss Cheese ModelAugust 5, 2024 — The Swiss cheese model is a complex linear safety model o... Published: August 5, 2024 Rival explanations are not obstacles—they improve investigations A well run investigation actively entertains competing explanations rather than defending the first plausible one. For example, investigators may initially consider several hypotheses simultaneously: Mechanical failure. Human error. Environmental conditions. Design defects. Maintenance shortcomings. Communication failures. Deliberate interference. Each remains provisional until evidence progressively excludes alternatives. Importantly, these explanations are not necessarily mutually exclusive. One of the biggest lessons from accident analysis is that multiple rival explanations often survive because each contributes part of the overall causal chain. The final explanation therefore becomes layered rather than singular. This differs from everyday debates, where people often assume that if one cause is identified, all others become irrelevant. Root Cause: Understanding Why Incidents Happen Fundamentals of Accident Investigation Part 3 Channel: Real Practical Safety Open on YouTube What everyday analysis can learn from causal chains The habits used by"},{"title":"Weak Evidence","url":"/weak-evidence/","description":"A fact can fit your favorite story without doing much to rule out serious rivals.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Alternatives","level":3,"text":"Weak Evidence Does This Evidence Really Pick a Side? Weak Evidence Weak Evidence Does This Evidence Really Pick a Side? A fact can fit your favorite story without doing much to rule out serious rivals. A fact can fit your favorite story without doing much to rule out serious rivals. mechanism Compatible evidence versus selective evidence Why vivid facts can be non diagnostic How to ask what would separate the explanations improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives weak evidence Introduction A piece of evidence does not become strong simply because it matches your preferred explanation. The key question is whether it matches that explanation better than the serious alternatives . If the same observation would be expected under several competing explanations, it provides little help in deciding between them. Such evidence is often described as non diagnostic : it is compatible with multiple hypotheses and therefore does little to distinguish which one is most likely. [Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy] iep.utm.edu Internet Encyclopedia of PhilosophyEvidenceEvidence is taken to be what confirms or refutes scientific theories, and thereby constitutes... This distinction is central to good reasoning. Many thinking errors arise because people ask only, \"Does this fit my idea?\" rather than, \"Would I have expected this even if my idea were wrong?\" The second question shifts attention from evidence that merely supports a story to evidence that actually discriminates between competing explanations. Compatible Evidence Versus Selective Evidence A useful way to think about evidence is to separate compatibility from selectivity . Compatible evidence is simply evidence that does not contradict an explanation. Many different explanations can be compatible with the same fact. Selective evidence is evidence that one explanation predicts substantially better than its rivals. It changes the balance between explanations because it would be noticeably less expected if another explanation were true. Philosophers of science and epistemologists emphasise that evidence earns its value by helping rational observers decide between competing accounts of the world, not merely by fitting one of them. [Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy] iep.utm.edu Internet Encyclopedia of PhilosophyEvidenceEvidence is taken to be what confirms or refutes scientific theories, and thereby constitutes... Consider a simple example: Observation: \"The garden is wet.\" Explanation A: It rained overnight. Explanation B: The sprinkler system ran. Explanation C: Someone washed the driveway. A wet garden is compatible with all three explanations. Until additional evidence appears, the observation itself does not pick a side. Now add another observation: The weather station recorded no rain. Neighbours report hearing sprinklers at 6 a.m. These new facts are more selective because they favour one explanation over the others. The important lesson is that evidence should be judged comparatively, not in isolation. Presenting Competing Hypotheses in the Introduction Channel: Scientific Writing with Karen L. McKee Open on YouTube Why Vivid Facts Can Be Non Diagnostic People naturally give extra weight to evidence that is memorable, emotional or unusually detailed. Unfortunately, vividness and diagnostic value are different qualities. Suppose someone believes that a colleague is deliberately avoiding them. They notice: delayed email replies, brief conversations, cancelled meetings. Each observation feels persuasive because it fits the story. Yet each is equally compatible with many ordinary alternatives such as workload, illness, travel or competing priorities. The vividness of the observations can create an illusion of strong support even though none of them distinguishes among the rival explanations . Psychological research on confirmation bias notes that people frequently evaluate only the probability of seeing evidence if their preferred hypothesis were true , while neglecting the equally important question of how likely the same evidence would be if competing hypotheses were true. This tendency has been called pseudodiagnosticity because apparently supportive evidence turns out not to discriminate between alternatives. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu nickerson Confirmation Bias UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — An observation that is... The Mechanism: Why Compatible Evidence Feels More Convincing Than It Is The mistake usually follows a predictable sequence. 1. A preferred explanation comes to mind. 2. Evidence consistent with that explanation is noticed. 3. The mind treats consistency as confirmation. 4. Alternative explanations receive little attention. 5. Confidence increases despite little change in the actual balance of evidence. The critical missing comparison is: How surprising would this observation be if another explanation were correct? If the answer is \"not very surprising\", then the evidence contributes little. This is closely related to the idea of likelihood in scientific and statistical reasoning. Evidence is informative when it is substantially more probable under one explanation than another. When the probabilities are similar across explanations, the evidence has little discriminating power. [UC San Diego Pages+2TheNNT] pages.ucsd.edu nickerson Confirmation Bias UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — An observation that is... How to Ask What Would Separate the Explanations A practical way to avoid over interpreting compatible evidence is to ask what observation would genuinely distinguish the competing explanations. Instead of asking: What evidence supports my explanation? [core.ac.uk] core.ac.uk vidence supports—promises epistemological riches; it has struck many philosophers as...Read more... Ask: What evidence would look different if another explanation were true? Examples include: Competing explanationsWeak evidenceMore selective evidenceWebsite sales fell because demand dropped vs tracking software failedSales dashboard shows lower revenuePayment records, server logs and independent transaction countsFriend is angry vs simply busyNo reply to a messageEvidence they are actively responding to others while consistently ignoring you over timeMachine has a faulty motor vs low batteryDevice stopped workingVoltage tests, motor diagnostics or replacement battery results Notice that the stronger evidence is not necessarily more dramatic. It is simply more capable of separating one explanation from another. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): A Structured Analytic Technique (SAT) for FinCrime Channel: Atlas Analytics: Intelligence Analysis Open on YouTube Scientific Thinking Relies on Discriminating Evidence Scientific investigation deliberately seeks observations that competing hypotheses predict differently. The classic method of multiple working hypotheses , proposed by T. C. Chamberlin, encourages researchers to develop several plausible explanations before collecting evidence. The goal is to avoid becoming attached to one \"ruling theory\" and instead search for observations capable of distinguishing among alternatives. [Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution+2railsback.org] whoi.edu Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution The Method of Multiple Working Hypotheses Woods Hole Oceanographic InstitutionThe Method of Multiple Working HypothesesJanuary 23, 2002 — by TC Chamberlin · Cited by 856 — The wor... Published: January 23, 2002 This approach changes the purpose of evidence collection. Instead of asking: Can I find facts consistent with my hypothesis? Researchers ask: Which experiment would produce different outcomes depending on which explanation is true? That shift dramatically improves the quality of inference because it prioritises diagnostic evidence over merely compatible evidence. Everyday Warning Signs That Evidence Is Weak Several patterns suggest that apparently persuasive evidence may not actually distinguish explanations: The evidence could easily occur under several reasonable explanations. Only one explanation has been examined in detail. The supporting facts are all individually unsurprising. No one has identified what evidence would count against the preferred explanation. Confidence grows faster than the evidence becomes more selective. None of these guarantees that the preferred explanation is wrong. They simply indicate that the current evidence may not justify choosing it over serious rivals. A Useful Habit of Mind Whenever a fact seems to support a conclusion, pause before asking whether it is convincing. Instead, ask three questions: 1. Which alternative explanations also predict this observation? 2. Would I expect this evidence almost as often if one of those alternatives were true? 3. What observation would genuinely separate these explanations? These questions transform the"},{"title":"Competing Hypotheses","url":"/competing-hypotheses/","description":"Listing hypotheses side by side helps reveal which explanation has the least serious evidence against it.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Alternatives","level":3,"text":"Competing Hypotheses Put Your Explanations in Competition Competing Hypotheses Competing Hypotheses Put Your Explanations in Competition Listing hypotheses side by side helps reveal which explanation has the least serious evidence against it. Listing hypotheses side by side helps reveal which explanation has the least serious evidence against it. policy intervention How side by side comparison changes the question Why disconfirming evidence gets special weight A simple matrix for everyday judgment calls improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives competing hypotheses Introduction Everyday decisions often feel as though they require choosing between a “right” explanation and a “wrong” one. In practice , the better question is: which explanation survives comparison with the least serious evidence against it? Putting plausible explanations into direct competition changes the way we think. Instead of asking, “Can I find evidence for my preferred story?”, we ask, “Which explanation best accounts for all the evidence, especially the awkward facts?” This approach reduces the risk that the first convincing narrative becomes the default simply because it appeared first. Research in decision making, intelligence analysis and cognitive psychology consistently shows that explicitly comparing rival hypotheses helps counter confirmation bias, although structured techniques are most effective when used thoughtfully rather than mechanically. [PMC+2Strathprints] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of task structure and confirmation bias in alternative...by MK Dhami · 2024 · Cited by 2 — We empirically examined the effect... How side by side comparison changes the question Most people evaluate one explanation at a time. Suppose a friend cancels dinner at short notice. A first reaction might be, \"They no longer value the friendship.\" Once that story forms, new information is unconsciously interpreted through it. A competing hypotheses approach reframes the problem by placing several plausible explanations side by side: ObservationLost interestGenuine emergencyWork overloadMiscommunicationCancelled at the last minute✓✓✓✓Suggested another date✗✓✓✓Similar pattern over months✓✗✓✗Apologised promptlyWeakensFitsFitsFits The important shift is that each new piece of evidence is compared across every explanation , rather than being added only to the favourite one. Richards Heuer's Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH), originally developed for intelligence analysis, deliberately encourages analysts to work across a matrix in this way because it makes alternative explanations harder to ignore. [Pherson] pherson.org This paper identifies three different approaches to Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH) and four major steps in the intelligence analy... For everyday decisions, the same principle applies whether deciding why sales have fallen, why a household appliance stopped working, or why someone behaved unexpectedly. The aim is not exhaustive analysis but disciplined comparison. Why disconfirming evidence gets special weight Supporting evidence is often less informative than people assume because several explanations can usually accommodate it. Imagine receiving only one observation: \"The customer has stopped ordering.\" This observation could fit many stories: the customer found a cheaper supplier; the customer is temporarily reducing spending; an administrative error prevented orders; the customer has changed business priorities. By itself, the observation discriminates poorly between explanations. Now consider a different observation: \"The customer continues buying the same products from competitors.\" That single fact strongly weakens some explanations while strengthening others. It is therefore more diagnostic . One of the central ideas behind competing hypothesis analysis is that evidence capable of ruling explanations out deserves more attention than evidence that merely confirms what several explanations already predict . The process therefore asks analysts to look first for inconsistencies rather than accumulating supporting examples. [Pherson] pherson.org This paper identifies three different approaches to Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH) and four major steps in the intelligence analy... This is psychologically difficult because people naturally search for confirming information. Experimental research examining ACH has found that structured comparison can influence how people organise evidence and reduce some aspects of confirmation bias, although improvements are not uniform and depend on how carefully the method is applied. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of task structure and confirmation bias in alternative...by MK Dhami · 2024 · Cited by 2 — We empirically examined the effect... Intelligence Analysis Skills: An Example of the Analysis of Competing Hypotheses Channel: Brian Urlacher Open on YouTube A simple matrix for everyday judgment calls A full intelligence style ACH exercise is unnecessary for most personal decisions. A lightweight comparison matrix usually captures most of the benefit. 1. State the question clearly. \"Why did website traffic fall this week?\" \"Why am I feeling unusually tired?\" \"Why was my proposal rejected?\" 2. List three to five plausible explanations. Avoid creating one favourite explanation and several implausible alternatives. 3. List observations separately from interpretations. For example: traffic fell by 30%; advertising budget was unchanged; competitors launched promotions; website response times increased. 4. Compare each observation against every explanation. Ask: Does this fit? Does it contradict? Is it irrelevant? 5. Focus on the explanation with the fewest important conflicts. A hypothesis should survive because competing explanations fit the evidence less well—not because it accumulated the largest pile of confirming facts. This process usually takes only a few minutes but forces slower, more deliberate reasoning. Common mistakes when comparing explanations Several habits weaken the value of competing hypothesis analysis. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Analysis of competing hypotheses Analysis of competing hypothesesThe analysis of competing hypotheses (ACH) is a methodology for evaluating multiple competing hypothes... Treating every imaginable possibility as equally plausible. The goal is not unlimited scepticism. Implausible explanations need not occupy equal attention simply because they are conceivable. Counting evidence instead of weighing it. Ten weak observations rarely outweigh one highly diagnostic fact. Ignoring assumptions . Many explanations depend on hidden assumptions such as \"the measurements are accurate\" or \"people acted rationally\". Listing these assumptions often reveals where uncertainty actually lies. Stopping too early. Once one explanation looks promising, it is tempting to stop searching. Maintaining at least one credible rival prevents premature closure . Failing to update. Competing hypotheses are provisional. New evidence should change relative confidence rather than forcing an all or nothing reversal. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): A Structured Analytic Technique (SAT) for FinCrime Channel: Atlas Analytics: Intelligence Analysis Open on YouTube Everyday examples where competition improves judgement The approach is particularly useful whenever several ordinary explanations fit the same observation. Health decisions. Before attributing fatigue to stress , compare sleep quality, illness, medication, nutrition and workload. A structured comparison encourages gathering information that distinguishes between these possibilities rather than reinforcing an initial assumption. Workplace problems. If a project is behind schedule, compare inadequate planning, changing client requirements, staffing shortages and technical failures instead of blaming the first visible cause. Personal relationships. Delayed replies, changed behaviour or cancelled plans usually admit multiple reasonable explanations. Explicit comparison reduces unnecessary conflict driven by overconfident interpretation. Financial decisions. Before assuming an investment performed well because of personal skill, compare broader market movements, sector trends and chance. Rival explanations help avoid overconfidence after successful outcomes. In each case, the practical benefit is less about finding certainty than about making the most defensible judgement from incomplete information. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): Finding Plausible Answers Channel: Adam Goss Open on YouTube Using the method without becoming indecisive A common concern is that considering alternatives will delay decisions indefinitely. In practice, competing hypothesis analysis works best when bounded. Useful stopping rules include: compare only the strongest three to five explanations; seek evidence that would genuinely distinguish between them; decide once additional evidence is unlikely to change the ranking substantially;"},{"title":"Late Replies","url":"/late-replies/","description":"A late message can feel personal, but the same silence may come from travel, stress, uncertainty, illness, or simple oversight.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Alternatives","level":3,"text":"Late Replies What Else Could Silence Mean? Late Replies Late Replies What Else Could Silence Mean? A late message can feel personal, but the same silence may come from travel, stress, uncertainty, illness, or simple oversight. A late message can feel personal, but the same silence may come from travel, stress, uncertainty, illness, or simple oversight. population context Why no reply supports many stories Signals that make avoidance more likely How to avoid over reading weak social evidence improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives late replies Introduction A delayed reply often feels emotionally meaningful because the absence of information invites the mind to fill in the gaps. It is easy to conclude that someone is angry, losing interest, or deliberately ignoring you. Yet a single observation—silence—usually fits many different explanations. From the perspective of good reasoning, the question is not whether your preferred explanation is possible , but whether the available evidence distinguishes it from realistic alternatives. This is a practical example of analytical thinking. Instead of treating \"no reply\" as evidence for one story, ask what other stories predict the same observation, what evidence would separate them, and whether you have enough information to justify a confident conclusion. This approach reduces unnecessary anxiety while producing more accurate judgements. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...Confirmation bias is perhaps the best known and most widely accepted n... Why no reply supports many stories Silence is unusually poor evidence because it is compatible with numerous everyday situations. Before interpreting motives, it helps to recognise how many ordinary circumstances produce the same outward behaviour. Common rival explanations include: The message was overlooked among many notifications. The person read it but intended to reply later and forgot. They are travelling, working, studying, or caring for someone. They are ill, exhausted, or dealing with a personal problem. They need time to think before answering. They feel uncertain how to respond. They are reducing phone use generally. They are intentionally avoiding this particular conversation. The important point is that every one of these explanations predicts the same initial observation: no immediate response. Until additional evidence appears, the observation itself does little to distinguish between them. This illustrates a broader principle in reasoning. Evidence is strongest when it is diagnostic —that is, when it is much more likely under one explanation than its rivals. A delayed reply, on its own, is rarely diagnostic. Why the mind jumps to personal explanations People naturally search for causes when something unexpected happens. In social situations, however, we often overestimate personal motives and underestimate situational influences. Psychologists describe this tendency as part of attribution bias and the fundamental attribution error: observers commonly explain behaviour by referring to personality or intention while overlooking circumstances that may be invisible to them. [The Oxford Review OR Briefings+2iMotions] oxford review.com The Oxford Review OR BriefingsAttribution Bias Definition and ExplanationMarch 14, 2024 — 14 Mar 2024 — Attribution bias is a psychological phenomenon th... Published: March 14, 2024 For example: \"They have not replied because they do not respect me.\" \"They must be angry.\" \"They are deliberately ignoring me.\" Each may eventually prove correct, but none follows logically from silence alone. The problem becomes worse when emotions are involved. If you already fear rejection, you are more likely to notice information supporting that fear while discounting competing explanations. This is a form of confirmation bias: once an initial interpretation forms, people tend to interpret new information in ways that reinforce it rather than challenge it. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...Confirmation bias is perhaps the best known and most widely accepted n... Abduction (Inference to the Best Explanation) Channel: Carneades.org Open on YouTube Signals that make avoidance more likely Although one delayed reply is weak evidence , patterns over time can become more informative. Evidence becomes more consistent with deliberate avoidance when several indicators occur together rather than in isolation. Examples include: Repeatedly ignoring direct questions while responding normally to unrelated conversations. Consistent delays only with one particular person. Multiple reminders receiving no acknowledgement over an extended period. Replies that repeatedly avoid the topic rather than merely arriving late. Explicit statements about not wishing to continue communication. A long term pattern of withdrawal across similar interactions. Even then, these remain probabilistic signals rather than proof. Personal crises, mental health difficulties, burnout, family emergencies, or changing priorities can produce remarkably similar behavioural patterns. Good judgement therefore weighs the entire context rather than treating any single cue as decisive. Weak evidence versus stronger evidence One useful way to improve reasoning is to rank observations by how much they actually discriminate between explanations. Weak evidence One unanswered message. A reply taking longer than expected. Seeing someone online without receiving a response. Changes from one day to the next. These observations fit many competing explanations. Stronger evidence Consistent behavioural patterns across weeks or months. Direct communication about boundaries or reduced contact. Behaviour repeated across multiple situations. Independent information that supports one explanation over another. Analytical thinking improves when confidence rises only as evidence becomes more selective. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): A Structured Analytic Technique (SAT) for FinCrime Channel: Atlas Analytics: Intelligence Analysis Open on YouTube How to avoid over reading weak social evidence When interpreting silence, replacing automatic conclusions with structured questions helps reduce error. Instead of asking: \"Why are they ignoring me?\" Ask: What are at least three plausible explanations? Which explanation best predicts everything I currently know? What evidence would make me change my mind ? Am I treating missing information as if it were positive evidence? Would I interpret the same delay differently if someone else experienced it? This shifts thinking from defending an initial story to comparing competing hypotheses. Another practical habit is to distinguish between facts and interpretations . For example: Fact: \"My message has not been answered for two days.\" Interpretation: \"They no longer value the friendship.\" Keeping these separate makes it easier to revise beliefs as new evidence appears. Practical examples Imagine three people who all fail to reply for 48 hours. Person A later apologises and explains they were travelling with poor reception. Person B replies thoughtfully after saying they needed time to think about a difficult question. Person C repeatedly ignores messages over several months while remaining active with everyone else and eventually says they do not wish to continue the relationship. All three produced the same initial observation—silence—but the later evidence strongly separates the explanations. Judging all three cases identically from the first delayed reply would have been an avoidable reasoning error. The analytical lesson Delayed replies are an excellent reminder that everyday evidence is often ambiguous. Human judgement improves when we resist treating a single observation as confirmation of the explanation that feels most emotionally compelling. The most reliable approach is to generate realistic rival explanations, wait for evidence that distinguishes between them, and allow confidence to grow only when the evidence becomes genuinely selective. This habit not only reduces mistaken relationship judgements but also strengthens analytical thinking in many other areas where incomplete information can support several competing stories. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): Finding Plausible Answers Channel: Adam Goss Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Else Could Silence Mean?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman Explains why people jump to conclusions from limited evidence and how to evaluate alternative explanations. See on Amazon Book The Art of Thinking Clearly By Rolf Dobelli Directly addresses mistaken inferences, overinterpretation, and reasoning under uncertainty. See on Amazon Book Super Thinking By Gabriel Weinberg, Lauren McCann Introduces mental"},{"title":"A Sudden Fall In Sales","url":"/a-sudden-fall-in-sales/","description":"A revenue change is easier to interpret when analytics, payment data, traffic, refunds, and channel comparisons tell the same story.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Alternatives","level":3,"text":"A Sudden Fall In Sales Is Demand Down or Data Broken? A Sudden Fall In Sales A Sudden Fall In Sales Is Demand Down or Data Broken? A revenue change is easier to interpret when analytics, payment data, traffic, refunds, and channel comparisons tell the same story. A revenue change is easier to interpret when analytics, payment data, traffic, refunds, and channel comparisons tell the same story. dataset evidence Demand explanations versus measurement explanations Checks that separate tracking errors from customer behavior Why unaffected channels make useful comparisons improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives sales drops Introduction A sudden fall in sales is tempting to interpret as evidence that customers no longer want a product. However, one of the most common rival explanations is that the business has stopped measuring sales correctly rather than stopped making them. Tracking failures, payment reporting problems, changes to analytics implementations, privacy restrictions, or broken checkout instrumentation can all make healthy demand appear to have collapsed. Conversely, genuine demand declines usually leave a consistent pattern across multiple independent data sources. Good analytical thinking therefore asks a simple question before explaining why sales fell: is demand actually down, or is the data broken? The strongest conclusion comes from comparing independent measurements rather than trusting a single dashboard. Revenue changes become much more convincing when analytics, payment systems, order databases, website traffic, refunds, and unaffected sales channels all point in the same direction. Demand explanations versus measurement explanations The same headline metric—a 25% drop in reported sales, for example—can arise from very different mechanisms. A genuine demand decline reflects changes in customer behaviour. Possible causes include weaker consumer confidence, increased competition, seasonal effects, pricing changes, stock shortages, poorer marketing performance, or declining product appeal. These explanations predict observable changes in customer activity, such as fewer visitors, lower conversion rates, reduced repeat purchases, or falling order volumes across multiple reporting systems. A measurement problem instead affects what the business records rather than what customers actually do. Examples include: Broken purchase or checkout tracking after a website update. Incorrect tag configuration following a migration to a new analytics platform. Missing ecommerce events in analytics software. [analyticsmania.com] analyticsmania.com missing google analytics transactions Analytics ManiaMissing Google Analytics 4 Transactions? Here are the...16 Dec 2025 — Learn how to identify and fix missing Google Analyt... Cross domain tracking failures that disconnect sessions from completed purchases. Payment processor reporting delays. Data filtering mistakes or duplicate exclusions. Consent management or browser privacy changes reducing measurable activity without reducing actual sales. [Simo Ahava's blog+2Analytics Mania] simoahava.com Simo Ahava's blog Google Analytics 4: Ecommerce Guide For Google Tag Simo Ahava's blogGoogle Analytics 4: Ecommerce Guide For Google Tag...October 22, 2020 — 22 Oct 2020 — Guide to implementing Google Anal... Published: October 22, 2020 These rival explanations generate different predictions. If demand has genuinely weakened, multiple independent systems should agree. If only one measurement system shows the decline while accounting, fulfilment, or payment records remain stable, the measurement explanation becomes much more plausible. Checks that separate tracking errors from customer behaviour Rather than asking whether analytics \"looks wrong\", it is more useful to compare evidence that was collected independently. Compare financial records with analytics Accounting systems, payment processors and order management databases are usually generated independently from marketing analytics. If analytics reports a 30% sales decline but: payment processor revenue is unchanged, order numbers remain stable, warehouse shipments continue at normal levels, then the evidence suggests a tracking failure rather than collapsing demand. Conversely, if every operational system reports fewer completed orders, the demand explanation becomes much stronger. Examine the conversion funnel Tracking failures rarely affect every customer interaction equally. For example: product page views remain unchanged, add to cart events remain unchanged, checkout initiation remains unchanged, purchase events suddenly collapse. This pattern is suspicious because customer behaviour would have to change dramatically at exactly one technical step. A broken purchase event, missing confirmation page, or failed analytics tag often explains such discontinuities better than a sudden shift in consumer preferences. Google recommends implementing ecommerce events consistently throughout the purchase journey because missing purchase events can distort reported revenue even when transactions succeed. [Simo Ahava's blog] simoahava.com Simo Ahava's blog Google Analytics 4: Ecommerce Guide For Google Tag Simo Ahava's blogGoogle Analytics 4: Ecommerce Guide For Google Tag...October 22, 2020 — 22 Oct 2020 — Guide to implementing Google Anal... Published: October 22, 2020 Compare order counts with traffic Demand declines usually affect multiple ratios simultaneously. Examples include: fewer visitors and fewer sales, stable visitors but worse conversion, stable conversion but less qualified traffic. A tracking problem often produces implausible combinations, such as: identical traffic, identical checkout behaviour, identical payment revenue, sharply lower reported purchases. When only one metric changes, the measurement hypothesis deserves serious consideration. Look for abrupt breaks Customer demand usually changes gradually unless an identifiable external event occurs. Measurement failures often begin at precise moments: immediately after deploying a new website, after changing tag management settings, following analytics migration, after installing a consent platform, after altering payment integrations. A sharp discontinuity that coincides with a technical release is stronger evidence of instrumentation failure than changing customer preferences. Why unaffected channels make useful comparisons Independent comparison groups are among the strongest tools for distinguishing rival explanations. Suppose an online retailer sells through: its own website, a marketplace, physical stores, a mobile application . If only website analytics reports a collapse while marketplace sales, shop sales and mobile purchases remain stable, then the evidence points towards website specific measurement or implementation issues rather than an economy wide fall in demand. Similarly, businesses operating across countries or brands can compare regions unaffected by recent technical changes. If only one region experiences the apparent decline immediately after a local website update, the technical explanation becomes considerably more credible. This logic resembles a scientific control group. Unaffected channels help separate changes in customer behaviour from changes in measurement. Common tracking failures that imitate falling sales Several well documented implementation problems can create the appearance of declining revenue. Broken purchase events are among the most common. If the final purchase event fails to fire, customers complete orders successfully but analytics never records them. Cross domain tracking failures occur when customers leave the main website for a payment provider or checkout domain and return without preserving session information. Completed purchases may then become disconnected from their original customer journey. [SE Ranking] seranking.com ga4 ecommerce guide SE RankingGA4 Ecommerce Guide for 202522 Feb 2024 — Learn how to set up GA4 ecommerce tracking to collect the most valuable insights on c... Incorrect data layer implementations can prevent transaction details from reaching analytics even though orders are processed correctly. GA4 implementations depend on correctly structured purchase events and parameters; errors in these implementations frequently produce incomplete or missing revenue reporting. [Simo Ahava's blog] simoahava.com Simo Ahava's blog Google Analytics 4: Ecommerce Guide For Google Tag Simo Ahava's blogGoogle Analytics 4: Ecommerce Guide For Google Tag...October 22, 2020 — 22 Oct 2020 — Guide to implementing Google Anal... Published: October 22, 2020 Browser privacy protections, consent choices and ad blockers also reduce observable tracking. Modern analytics systems increasingly acknowledge that client side measurement cannot capture every transaction perfectly, meaning discrepancies between analytics and backend financial systems are expected within reasonable limits. [Analytics Mania] analyticsmania.com"},{"title":"Control Groups","url":"/control-groups/","description":"Performance gains are not strong evidence for training until rival explanations such as easier tasks or changing conditions are tested.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Alternatives","level":3,"text":"Control Groups Did Training Work, or Did the Task Change? Control Groups Control Groups Did Training Work, or Did the Task Change? Performance gains are not strong evidence for training until rival explanations such as easier tasks or changing conditions are tested. Performance gains are not strong evidence for training until rival explanations such as easier tasks or changing conditions are tested. policy intervention Why before and after gains can mislead Control groups and unchanged tasks as separators What to compare before crediting an intervention improving your thinking and analytical skills alternatives control groups Introduction When people perform better after training, it is tempting to conclude that the training caused the improvement. However, a simple before and after comparison cannot distinguish between genuine learning and other explanations. Participants may have become familiar with the task, the second assessment may have been easier, external conditions may have changed, or merely taking part in the study may have influenced behaviour. Control groups exist to separate these competing explanations. By comparing people who received an intervention with similar people who did not, researchers can ask a more precise question: did the intervention produce an improvement beyond what would have happened anyway? This comparison is one of the strongest practical safeguards against mistaking an easier task or changing circumstances for evidence that training worked. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Randomized controlled trial Randomized controlled trial Why before and after gains can mislead A rise in performance between two tests is real, but its cause is often uncertain. Several rival explanations can produce apparent improvement even when the intervention has little or no effect. Common alternatives include: Practice effects. People often perform better simply because they have seen the task before and understand its format. Task differences. The second version of a test may inadvertently be easier, even if intended to measure the same skill. Changing conditions. Better equipment, different instructions, reduced stress , or more favourable timing can all improve performance independently of training. Regression to the mean. Individuals selected because of unusually poor or unusually good initial performance often move closer to their typical level on later testing, even without intervention. [American Journal of Clinical Nutrition] ajcn.nutrition.org American Journal of Clinical NutritionBest (but oft forgotten) practices: identifying and accounting for...by DM Thomas · 2020 · Cited b... Suppose a company introduces a new reasoning course and employees score higher on a follow up assessment. If the second assessment contains simpler questions or participants already know what to expect, the improvement may exaggerate—or even completely explain —the apparent success of the course. Looking only at the trained group cannot distinguish these possibilities. This illustrates an important principle in analytical thinking: evidence becomes stronger when it rules out plausible rival explanations, not merely when it is consistent with a preferred story. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): A Structured Analytic Technique (SAT) for FinCrime Channel: Atlas Analytics: Intelligence Analysis Open on YouTube Control groups and unchanged tasks as separators A control group provides a benchmark for what would have happened without the intervention. Ideally, both groups experience the same testing schedule, environmental conditions and measurement methods, with the intervention itself being the principal difference between them. Random assignment strengthens this comparison by making the groups similar in both measured and unmeasured characteristics before the study begins. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Randomized controlled trial Randomized controlled trial Consider two groups taking the same reasoning assessment twice: The training group completes a six week programme. The control group continues normal activities. Both groups sit equivalent assessments under the same conditions. If both groups improve by roughly the same amount, practice, familiarity or easier testing become more convincing explanations than the training itself. If the trained group improves substantially more than the control group, the intervention becomes a more credible explanation because the shared influences have largely been accounted for. The key comparison is therefore the difference in improvement , not simply whether improvement occurred. Why control groups sometimes improve as well An important misconception is that control groups should remain unchanged. In reality, they often improve for entirely understandable reasons. Research across behavioural and health interventions has repeatedly found meaningful gains among control participants. In some studies, simply measuring behaviour, asking repeated questions, or increasing participants' awareness appears to influence subsequent performance. This phenomenon overlaps with measurement reactivity and the broader family of effects sometimes described under the Hawthorne effect. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Systematic review of the Hawthorne effect: New concepts are...by J McCambridge · 2014 · Cited by 3787 — This study aims to (1) elucid... For example, systematic reviews of physical activity interventions have found that many control groups increased their reported activity levels despite receiving no substantial exercise programme. Factors such as repeated measurement, self monitoring and participant awareness contributed to these improvements. Without a comparison group, researchers could easily have attributed these gains to an intervention that participants never actually received. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Explanations have been...Read more... Rather than undermining the value of control groups, these findings demonstrate why they are indispensable. They reveal background improvements that would otherwise be mistaken for treatment effects. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH): Finding Plausible Answers Channel: Adam Goss Open on YouTube What to compare before crediting an intervention When evaluating whether training genuinely worked, several comparisons are more informative than the headline \"scores went up\". Prioritise questions such as: Did both groups complete equivalent tasks under comparable conditions? Were both groups tested at the same times? Did the control group also improve? If so, by how much? Was the intervention group's improvement substantially larger than the control group's? Could any remaining differences be explained by unequal starting points, participant drop out or changes in measurement? These questions encourage attention to the quality of the comparison rather than the size of the apparent gain alone. Where randomised control groups are impractical, well designed comparison groups, matched cohorts or quasi experimental designs can still provide stronger evidence than isolated before and after measurements because they retain the central logic of comparing change against a credible counterfactual. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. A practical habit for better analytical thinking Outside formal experiments, few decisions involve perfect control groups. Nevertheless, the underlying reasoning remains valuable. Whenever someone claims that an intervention worked, ask what happened to an equivalent group that did not receive it. If no comparison exists, consider what background changes could plausibly explain the result instead: Was everyone improving over time? Did the task become easier? Were expectations different on the second attempt? Did measurement itself influence behaviour? Thinking in these terms shifts attention from impressive looking gains to diagnostic evidence. The question is no longer, \"Did performance improve?\" but rather, \"Did it improve more than we would reasonably expect without the intervention?\" That distinction is central to evaluating rival explanations and avoiding the common mistake of crediting training for improvements that arose because the task, the measurement or the surrounding conditions changed instead. Abduction (Inference to the Best Explanation) Channel: Carneades.org Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Did Training Work, or Did the Task Change?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman Explains why people misattribute causes and overinterpret apparent improvements, reinforcing the need for proper comparisons. See on Amazon Book Bad Science By Ben Goldacre Uses accessible examples to explain why control groups and fair comparisons matter when evaluating interventions. See on Amazon Book The Art of Statistics By David Spiegelhalter Covers evidence, uncertainty, and how to distinguish genuine effects from"},{"title":"Argument Maps","url":"/argument-maps/","description":"Argument maps turn conclusions, reasons, objections, and assumptions into a visible structure that can be tested.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Argument Maps Can Mapping an Argument Improve It? Argument Maps Argument Maps Can Mapping an Argument Improve It? Argument maps turn conclusions, reasons, objections, and assumptions into a visible structure that can be tested. Argument maps turn conclusions, reasons, objections, and assumptions into a visible structure that can be tested. policy intervention How argument maps work What maps reveal Simple no software templates improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps Introduction Argument mapping improves reasoning by turning a chain of thought into something you can inspect. Instead of holding a vague position in your head, you place the main conclusion, supporting reasons, objections, replies and hidden assumptions into a visible structure. That structure makes it easier to ask: which claim is actually being defended, which reasons support it, where does the evidence stop, and what would have to be true for the conclusion to follow? Argument mapping is therefore less a decorative study technique than a practical intervention for clearer thinking: it slows the rush from impression to verdict and gives reasoning a shape that can be tested. The evidence is not that drawing boxes magically makes people smarter. The stronger claim is more precise: structured practice in mapping arguments, especially with feedback , can improve critical thinking performance more reliably than many loose “think harder” approaches. Studies and reviews of argument mapping instruction report benefits for evaluation, comprehension and reflective judgement, while also noting that effects depend on practice intensity, task design and the kinds of arguments being mapped. [Research Repository+2AJET] researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie Research RepositoryAn evaluation of argument mapping as a method...by CP Dwyer · 2012 · Cited by 238 — Results are discussed in light of... How argument maps work An argument map is a diagram of reasoning, not a general brainstorm. Its basic unit is a claim: a statement that can be accepted, rejected, questioned or supported. The map then shows how claims relate to one another. A top level conclusion might be supported by several independent reasons; a reason might itself need evidence; an objection might attack either the conclusion, a supporting claim, or the link between them. This is why argument maps are different from mind maps. Mind maps are useful for association and memory, while argument maps are built to expose inferential structure: what is supposed to support what, and whether the support is strong enough. [Springer] link.springer.com Concept mapping, mind mapping and argument mappingby M Davies · 2011 · Cited by 1536 — This paper offers an outline of the variou... A simple map starts with the conclusion. For example: “The council should pedestrianise this street.” Below it might sit reasons such as “traffic injuries would probably fall”, “local air quality would improve”, and “nearby shops may gain footfall”. Each of those reasons then needs its own support: collision data, pollution readings, evidence from comparable streets, or survey results from affected businesses. Objections sit alongside the reasons: “disabled access may become harder”, “traffic may be displaced into neighbouring streets”, or “delivery costs may rise”. The map makes the debate more honest because it stops a general pro or con mood from standing in for a tested argument. Good maps usually distinguish four things: Conclusion: the claim the argument is trying to establish. Reasons: claims offered in support of that conclusion. Objections: claims that weaken the conclusion, a reason, or an inference. Assumptions: unstated claims that must be true for the reasoning to work. The assumption layer is often where the most useful thinking happens. In ordinary prose, people skip steps because the missing premise feels obvious to them. In an argument map, the gap becomes visible. If someone argues that “ remote work should continue because productivity has not fallen”, the hidden assumption may be that productivity is the main criterion for deciding the policy. A map allows that assumption to be examined rather than smuggled into the conclusion. What maps reveal that prose can hide The chief value of argument mapping is that it separates the psychological force of an argument from its logical structure. A confident paragraph can sound persuasive because it moves quickly, uses vivid examples, or mixes several claims together. A map interrupts that flow. It asks each claim to stand in its own box and show its relation to the rest of the argument. That visibility helps reveal several common reasoning problems. A conclusion may have many words around it but only one real reason supporting it. A reason may look strong until the map shows that it depends on an untested assumption. An objection may appear to refute a whole argument when it only weakens one branch. Conversely, a small looking objection may be devastating if it attacks a crucial inference rather than a peripheral detail. Researchers and teachers of argument mapping often stress this “externalisation” effect: the map reduces the burden on working memory. Instead of trying to remember every strand of a debate at once, the thinker can inspect the structure on the page. Tim van Gelder’s work on critical thinking instruction argues that students improve faster when they practise reasoning in a visible, feedback rich form rather than relying only on conventional discussion or essay writing. [University of Bath Personal Homepages] people.bath.ac.uk Open source on bath.ac.uk. A second benefit is that maps encourage fairer disagreement. In a heated discussion, people often respond to tone, identity, or the weakest version of the other side. A map asks a more disciplined question: “Which exact claim are you objecting to?” That can reveal whether the disagreement is about evidence, values, definitions, risk tolerance, or an unstated assumption. The result is not automatic consensus, but a cleaner disagreement. Why mapping can improve analytical skill Argument mapping works best when treated as deliberate practice. The learner repeatedly breaks arguments into claims, tests support relations, identifies missing assumptions, receives feedback, and revises the map. That matters because critical thinking is not simply a set of definitions. People may know the names of fallacies yet fail to notice weak reasoning in real time. Mapping gives them a repeatable action: locate the conclusion, identify the reasons, test the links, add objections, and revise. Several studies have examined argument mapping in higher education. Charles Twardy reported that computer based argument mapping produced large gains in critical thinking courses compared with typical gains from other methods, though the study belongs to a specific instructional context rather than a universal guarantee. [University of Southampton Web Archive] web archive.southampton.ac.uk Open source on southampton.ac.uk. Dwyer, Hogan and Stewart evaluated argument mapping in e learning environments and found that argument mapping groups could outperform controls on aspects of critical thinking performance, while later work also cautioned that short interventions may produce smaller or less stable effects than semester length practice. [Research Repository] researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie Research RepositoryAn evaluation of argument mapping as a method...by CP Dwyer · 2012 · Cited by 238 — Results are discussed in light of... The pattern across this evidence is useful for implementation. Argument mapping is strongest when it is not a one off worksheet. It needs repeated practice, examples of good maps, feedback on errors, and tasks that require genuine evaluation rather than merely copying statements into boxes. A 2009 study of web based argument mapping exercises with automated feedback reported a 0.45 standard deviation improvement from pre test to post test in a single undergraduate critical thinking course, describing the result as modest but encouraging. [AJET] ajet.org.au Improving critical thinking using web based argument...by S Butchart · 2009 · Cited by 136 — In this paper we describe a simple soft... The mechanism is also plausible. Mapping forces a person to perform the operations that good reasoning requires: distinguish a claim from evidence, decide whether two premises work independently or jointly, check whether an objection attacks a premise or an inference, and make missing assumptions explicit. These are small cognitive moves, but repeated practice makes them more available when the thinker later reads an article, writes a proposal, analyses a policy, or evaluates a disputed claim. A simple map for everyday decisions Argument maps do not have to be software heavy. A useful beginner template can be done on paper or in a plain document. The aim is not to produce a beautiful chart; it is to make the reasoning"},{"title":"Practice","url":"/practice/","description":"The strongest evidence for argument mapping comes from repeated practice with feedback, not from drawing one diagram once.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Argument Maps","level":3,"text":"Practice Why One Argument Map Is Not Enough Practice Practice Why One Argument Map Is Not Enough The strongest evidence for argument mapping comes from repeated practice with feedback, not from drawing one diagram once. The strongest evidence for argument mapping comes from repeated practice with feedback, not from drawing one diagram once. dataset evidence What deliberate practice adds to mapping Evidence from higher education studies Why short interventions may have weaker effects improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps practice Introduction The strongest evidence for argument mapping does not come from students drawing a single diagram and immediately becoming better thinkers. Instead, the most consistent findings point to a combination of repeated practice, increasingly challenging mapping tasks, and timely feedback . In higher education, argument mapping appears to work best when it is treated as a skill to be trained rather than a one off classroom activity. Studies that report meaningful gains in critical thinking typically involve students constructing many maps over time, receiving guidance on mistakes , and revising their work rather than simply learning what an argument map looks like. [AJET+2ThinkerU] ajet.org.au Improving critical thinking using web based argument...by S Butchart · 2009 · Cited by 135 — In this paper we describe a simple soft... This matters because argument mapping is fundamentally a performance skill. Like learning statistics, writing, or chess analysis, understanding the technique is not the same as becoming proficient at it. Research on argument mapping instruction repeatedly highlights practice intensity and feedback quality as key conditions for improvement. [web archive.southampton.ac.uk+2ResearchGate] web archive.southampton.ac.uk Argument Maps Improve Critical Thinkingby CR Twardy · Cited by 356 — Practice is clearly important: argument mapping without practice wou... What Deliberate Practice Adds to Mapping A recurring theme in the argument mapping literature is the idea of deliberate practice : structured, repeated exercises focused on specific reasoning skills, combined with feedback that helps learners identify and correct errors. Researchers associated with the development of argument mapping pedagogy have argued that reasoning improves when students repeatedly analyse arguments, identify premises and conclusions, expose hidden assumptions , and refine their maps in response to criticism. [ThinkerU+2Reasoninglab] thinkeru.org Argument mapping. Argument...Read more... The rationale is straightforward. Novices often struggle with several tasks at once: Distinguishing conclusions from supporting reasons. Identifying linked versus independent premises. Recognising unstated assumptions. Evaluating objections and rebuttals. Judging whether evidence actually supports a claim. A single mapping exercise may reveal these difficulties, but repeated exercises allow students to encounter the same reasoning patterns across different contexts. Over time, identifying argumentative structure becomes less effortful, freeing attention for deeper evaluation. Researchers have linked this process to broader theories of expertise development, where improvement comes from many cycles of practice, feedback, and correction rather than exposure alone. [ResearchGate+2ThinkerU] researchgate.net Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills19 Feb 2026 — Provide multiple, progressive argument mapping activi... Importantly, argument mapping is not simply visual note taking. The educational value appears to come from repeatedly practising inferential judgement: deciding exactly how claims support or challenge one another. The map is the vehicle for that practice, not the endpoint. [researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie] researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie An evaluation of argument mapping as a method...by CP Dwyer · 2012 · Cited by 238 — Though research suggests that CT training can improv... Argument Mapping: Advanced Tips and Tricks Channel: Tanya Hall · Views: 1.4K · Uploaded: February 2020 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Evidence from Higher Education Studies Several influential studies in universities have found that argument mapping interventions can produce measurable gains in critical thinking performance, particularly when students engage in sustained mapping activities over a semester. One widely cited study by Butchart and colleagues examined a web based argument mapping system that provided automated, real time feedback as students constructed maps. Students completed repeated mapping exercises throughout a university critical thinking course. The researchers reported an improvement of approximately 0.45 standard deviations on a standardised critical thinking measure between pre test and post test. The authors argued that the combination of repeated practice opportunities and immediate feedback was central to the intervention's effectiveness. [AJET] ajet.org.au AJETImproving critical thinking using web based argument May 9, 2009 — by S Butchart · 2009 · Cited by 136 — In this paper we describe a simple software system that allows students to practise t... Published: May 9, 2009 Research by Dwyer, Hogan and Stewart similarly found that argument mapping training could outperform alternative instructional approaches in promoting critical thinking skills. Their work emphasised that learners benefited from active construction and revision of maps rather than passive exposure to argumentative structures. The studies also connected argument mapping to improvements in comprehension and memory, suggesting that repeated engagement with argument structure helps learners organise complex information more effectively. [researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie+2eprints.teachingandlearning.ie] researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie An evaluation of argument mapping as a method...by CP Dwyer · 2012 · Cited by 238 — Though research suggests that CT training can improv... More recent higher education research has continued to support the importance of iterative engagement. In a study of argument map supported online debates, students participated in a sequence of mapping activities over several weeks. Researchers observed gradual improvements in the depth of critical thinking across successive debates, and teacher feedback was identified as an important contributor to those gains. Rather than showing a dramatic change after one exercise, the results suggested cumulative improvement through repeated cycles of performance and review. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Exploring the Effects of Argument Map Supported Online...by X Chen · 2022 · Cited by 36 — The current study aimed to explore the effe... Systematic reviews of argument mapping in postsecondary education have reached a similar conclusion. While findings vary across contexts and disciplines, stronger outcomes are typically associated with instructional designs that integrate mapping into sustained coursework rather than isolated demonstrations. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com teaching argumentation (van Gelder Wiley Online LibraryArgument Mapping in Higher Education: A Systematic...15 Oct 2025 — This systematic review examines research on the u... Why Feedback Matters as Much as Practice Practice alone is not always enough. A student can repeatedly produce flawed maps and simply reinforce misunderstandings. For this reason, feedback occupies a central place in many successful argument mapping programmes. Feedback can take several forms: Instructor feedback , where teachers comment on structure, missing premises, or weak evidential links. Peer feedback , where students critique one another's maps. Automated feedback , where software identifies structural errors or provides guidance during construction. Model comparison , where learners compare their maps against expert versions. The common feature is that feedback makes reasoning visible and correctable. Students can see exactly where their interpretation differs from an accepted analysis and adjust accordingly. [AJET+2ResearchGate] ajet.org.au AJETImproving critical thinking using web based argument May 9, 2009 — by S Butchart · 2009 · Cited by 136 — In this paper we describe a simple software system that allows students to practise t... Published: May 9, 2009 The role of feedback is particularly important because argument mapping requires skills that are often invisible in ordinary classroom discussion. A student may arrive at the correct conclusion while misunderstanding the structure of the argument. Mapping exposes those hidden weaknesses, and feedback helps learners address them before they become habits. [researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie] researchrepository.universityofgalway.ie An evaluation of argument mapping as a method...by CP Dwyer · 2012 · Cited by 238 — Though research suggests that CT training can improv... Technology has been"},{"title":"Map Types","url":"/map-types/","description":"Mind maps help association, but argument maps show which claims support, weaken, or depend on each other.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Argument Maps","level":3,"text":"Map Types Why Argument Maps Are Not Mind Maps Map Types Map Types Why Argument Maps Are Not Mind Maps Mind maps help association, but argument maps show which claims support, weaken, or depend on each other. Mind maps help association, but argument maps show which claims support, weaken, or depend on each other. policy intervention Association maps versus inferential maps When a brainstorm becomes misleading Choosing the right map for a reasoning task improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps map types Introduction When people want to organise their thoughts, they often reach for a diagram. The problem is that not all diagrams do the same job. A mind map helps generate, collect and connect ideas. An argument map helps evaluate whether a conclusion is actually supported by reasons and evidence. Confusing the two can create a dangerous illusion: a page full of connected ideas may look rigorous even when the reasoning is weak. For improving analytical thinking, the distinction matters because reasoning depends not just on having ideas, but on understanding which claims support, weaken or depend upon other claims. Researchers and argument mapping practitioners consistently distinguish mind maps as tools for association and brainstorming, while argument maps are designed to reveal inferential structure—the logic of an argument itself. [Reasoninglab+2The Open University] reasoninglab.com Concept Mapping, Mind Mapping and Argument Mapping However, in mind mapping, any idea can be connected to any other. Free form, spontaneous...Read more... Association Maps Versus Inferential Maps At first glance, mind maps and argument maps can appear similar. Both use boxes, nodes, branches and visual connections. The crucial difference lies in what those connections mean. In a mind map, a link usually means that two concepts are related in some way. The relationship may be causal, thematic, chronological, personal or simply associative. A branch from “urban transport” to “cycling”, “pollution” and “commuting” does not tell the reader whether one idea proves another. It merely indicates a connection. Mind maps are intentionally flexible and encourage free form exploration. Any idea can potentially connect to any other idea. [Reasoninglab] reasoninglab.com Concept Mapping, Mind Mapping and Argument Mapping However, in mind mapping, any idea can be connected to any other. Free form, spontaneous...Read more... In an argument map, connections carry a much stricter meaning. Nodes represent claims rather than general concepts, and links indicate support, objection or dependency relationships. A branch is not saying “these ideas are related”; it is saying “this claim is offered as a reason for that claim” or “this objection weakens that conclusion”. [The Open University+2Wikipedia] open.edu The Open University3.1 Mind and argument mapsIn an argument map, the nodes represent claims and the connections represent support and opp... Consider a policy question: Should a city pedestrianise its high street? A mind map might include: Retail activity Air quality Tourism Parking Public transport Accessibility This is useful for identifying relevant topics. An argument map asks different questions: Does improved air quality support pedestrianisation? What evidence supports that claim? Does reduced parking weaken the proposal? Is there evidence that retail activity increases or decreases after pedestrianisation? The map becomes a structure of reasoning rather than a catalogue of ideas. Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w/ Nate Otey Channel: The Stoa · Views: 3.5K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube When a Brainstorm Becomes Misleading Mind maps are excellent tools for creativity, memory and exploration. Problems arise when people use them to evaluate arguments. A common mistake is assuming that a large number of branches indicates a strong case. In reality, ten associated ideas may provide less support for a conclusion than one carefully evidenced reason. Because mind maps do not require explicit support relationships, they can make a weak argument appear comprehensive. [Reasoninglab] reasoninglab.com Concept Mapping, Mind Mapping and Argument Mapping However, in mind mapping, any idea can be connected to any other. Free form, spontaneous...Read more... Imagine a debate about remote working. A mind map might produce branches such as: Productivity Employee satisfaction Office costs Team culture Recruitment Environmental impact The map successfully broadens the discussion. However, it does not reveal whether productivity increased, whether employee satisfaction matters more than culture, or how these considerations combine into a final recommendation. An argument map forces those questions into the open. Each claim must justify its place. Unsupported assertions become visible. Hidden assumptions become easier to identify. The visual structure prevents a discussion from drifting between evidence, opinion and unrelated observations without distinction. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Argument map Argument map This is one reason argument mapping has attracted attention in critical thinking education. The discipline of specifying support and objection relationships trains people to separate relevance from proof. Research on argument mapping instruction has found improvements in critical thinking performance and argument evaluation, suggesting that the structured examination of reasoning provides benefits beyond simple information organisation. University of Southampton Web Archive+2Research Repository [web archive.southampton.ac.uk] web archive.southampton.ac.uk University of Southampton Web ArchiveArgument Maps Improve Critical Thinkingby CR Twardy · Cited by 356 — Computer based argument mapping... Why Reasoning Needs Different Visual Rules Historically, mind mapping became popular through creativity and memory techniques that emphasised free association, imagery and non linear thinking. Argument mapping emerged from traditions in logic, critical thinking and argumentation theory, where the primary concern was analysing how conclusions follow from premises. [Reasoninglab] reasoninglab.com Concept Mapping, Mind Mapping and Argument Mapping However, in mind mapping, any idea can be connected to any other. Free form, spontaneous...Read more... These origins explain why the two methods use different visual rules. Mind maps generally encourage: [reasoninglab.com] reasoninglab.com Concept Mapping, Mind Mapping and Argument Mapping However, in mind mapping, any idea can be connected to any other. Free form, spontaneous...Read more... Rapid idea generation Exploration of possibilities Flexible categorisation Memory and recall Argument maps generally require: [reasoninglab.com] reasoninglab.com Concept Mapping, Mind Mapping and Argument Mapping However, in mind mapping, any idea can be connected to any other. Free form, spontaneous...Read more... Explicit claims Identifiable conclusions Evidence and reasons Counterarguments and objections Clear inferential links The stricter structure is not a limitation. It is what makes argument maps useful for evaluating reasoning. If every connection is allowed to mean anything, it becomes difficult to determine whether a conclusion is justified. [The Open University+2Argunet] open.edu The Open University3.1 Mind and argument mapsIn an argument map, the nodes represent claims and the connections represent support and opp... How to make and use argument maps : Avoid endless discussions Channel: Decision making and more · Views: 24.6K · Uploaded: January 2014 · Length: 22 minutes Open on YouTube Choosing the Right Map for a Reasoning Task The most effective thinkers often use both tools, but at different stages. Use a Mind Map When the Goal Is Exploration A mind map is usually the better choice when: Starting a new topic Generating possibilities Identifying relevant factors Collecting information Discovering connections For example, before writing an essay on artificial intelligence regulation, a mind map can help identify themes such as innovation, safety, employment, privacy and competition. At this stage, breadth matters more than judgement. Use an Argument Map When the Goal Is Evaluation An argument map becomes valuable when questions shift from “What should we consider?” to “What should we believe?” It is particularly useful when: Comparing competing explanations Evaluating policy proposals Testing assumptions Preparing debates Writing persuasive essays Making complex decisions At this stage, the key issue is no longer idea generation. It is determining whether reasons genuinely support a conclusion. [Wikipedia+2UAB Digital Document Repository] Wikipedia Argument map Argument map Critical Thinking with Argument Maps Channel: Argumentful · Views: 8.5K · Uploaded: July 2020 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube A Practical Sequence Many experienced users follow a progression: 1. Brainstorm"},{"title":"Decision Claims","url":"/decision-claims/","description":"Starting with a complete decision claim keeps an argument map focused on what is actually being defended.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Argument Maps","level":3,"text":"Decision Claims What Is Your Map Really Trying to Prove? Decision Claims Decision Claims What Is Your Map Really Trying to Prove? Starting with a complete decision claim keeps an argument map focused on what is actually being defended. Starting with a complete decision claim keeps an argument map focused on what is actually being defended. policy intervention Turning topics into testable claims Finding the real top level conclusion Using job offers and policy choices as examples improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps decision claims Introduction When people say they are “mapping a decision”, they often begin with a topic: job offer , working from home , should we introduce a new policy? The problem is that a topic is not yet an argument. An argument map becomes useful only when it is organised around a claim that could be accepted, rejected, questioned, or supported. In practice , the most effective maps for everyday decisions are often conclusion first maps: they start by stating exactly what is being defended and then work backwards to examine the reasons, evidence, objections, and assumptions . Argument mapping guidance consistently places identification of the main claim or conclusion at the centre of the mapping process because the structure of the rest of the map depends on it. [The Open University+2The Open University] open.edu First, try to find the main claim. The main claim is the overall point or conclusion that the author of the text is making.Read more... For improving thinking and analytical skills, this seemingly small change matters. A clear conclusion prevents a map from becoming a collection of disconnected considerations. It forces the question: what, exactly, am I trying to prove? What Is Your Map Really Trying to Prove? Many reasoning failures occur before any evidence is examined. The mistake is not weak support but an unclear target. Consider the difference between these starting points: “ Remote work ” “Job offer” “City transport” “I should accept the offer from Company A.” “Our team should continue remote work three days per week.” “The council should introduce a bus priority scheme.” The first group names subjects. The second group states conclusions. Only the second group can serve as the top node of an argument map because only they make a definite claim. Argument mapping methods therefore emphasise identifying the main claim before building the rest of the structure. [The Open University+2Reasons] open.edu First, try to find the main claim. The main claim is the overall point or conclusion that the author of the text is making.Read more... A useful test is whether someone could reasonably respond “yes”, “no”, or “I'm not convinced”. If they can, the statement is probably specific enough to function as a conclusion. Starting with a conclusion also reduces a common analytical error: endlessly collecting information without knowing what decision the information is supposed to support. Evidence gains meaning only relative to a claim. Turning Topics into Testable Claims A practical way to build conclusion first maps is to convert broad topics into explicit decision statements. Instead of mapping: “Buying a house” Map: [ddd.uab.cat] ddd.uab.cat cat Argument map map Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia5 Jul 2016 — An argument map typically includes the key components of the argument, traditionally c... “I should buy the house on Oak Street this year.” Instead of mapping: “Public transport” Map: [ddd.uab.cat] ddd.uab.cat cat Argument map map Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia5 Jul 2016 — An argument map typically includes the key components of the argument, traditionally c... “The city should expand the tram network into the northern district.” Instead of mapping: “Career options” Map: [ddd.uab.cat] ddd.uab.cat cat Argument map map Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia5 Jul 2016 — An argument map typically includes the key components of the argument, traditionally c... “I should move into product management within the next twelve months.” The more concrete the claim, the easier it becomes to identify relevant support and irrelevant distractions. This approach mirrors evidence based decision making practices, which focus on evaluating evidence in relation to a specific claim, assumption, or hypothesis rather than treating information gathering as an end in itself. [CIPD] cipd.org Evidence based practice for effective decision making Evidence based practice for effective decision makingMay 19, 2025 — Evidence based practice is an approach for improving decision mak... Published: May 19, 2025 A conclusion first map therefore acts as a filter. Every reason, statistic, anecdote, or objection must answer a single question: does this help establish or weaken the conclusion? Critical Thinking with Argument Maps Channel: Argumentful · Views: 8.5K · Uploaded: July 2020 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Finding the Real Top Level Conclusion One of the most valuable skills in argument mapping is distinguishing the final conclusion from supporting conclusions. In many everyday decisions, people accidentally place a supporting claim at the top of the map. For example: “The salary is higher.” “The commute is shorter.” “The company has better promotion prospects.” These are not the decision. They are reasons. The actual conclusion might be: “I should accept Job Offer A.” Argument mapping literature frequently distinguishes between the main conclusion and intermediate conclusions. Intermediate conclusions function both as conclusions and as premises: they are supported by evidence and then go on to support a higher level claim. [Humanities LibreTexts+3LSAT Demon+3PastPaperHero] lsatdemon.com argument parts and indicators LSAT DemonArgument Parts and Indicators6 Mar 2022 — An intermediate conclusion is a conclusion that supports the main conclusion. Think o... A simple diagnostic question helps identify the true top level conclusion: If all these reasons were proven true, what decision would follow? The answer is usually the main conclusion. For example: Evidence: Salary is 20% higher. Evidence: Career progression appears faster. Intermediate conclusion: Offer A provides stronger long term career value. Main conclusion: I should accept Offer A. Without separating these levels, maps become confused. A person may spend hours debating salary data while never clearly stating the decision the salary is supposed to justify. Using Job Offers as a Conclusion First Example Imagine choosing between two jobs. A topic first approach often produces a list: Salary Culture Location Flexibility Benefits The list is useful but lacks direction. A conclusion first map begins with: “I should accept the offer from Company A.” The next level contains supporting and opposing reasons. Supporting reasons: Total compensation is higher. Promotion opportunities appear stronger. The role develops skills aligned with long term goals. Objections: Longer commute. Greater workload uncertainty. Smaller professional network in the new organisation. Each branch can then be tested independently. Notice what changes. The discussion is no longer about whether salary is “good”. It is about whether salary contributes enough support to justify the specific conclusion. This keeps analytical effort focused on the actual decision rather than on isolated factors. Mapping Arguments Channel: Adam Rosenfeld · Views: 5.3K · Uploaded: January 2017 · Length: 29 minutes Open on YouTube Using Policy Choices as a Conclusion First Example The same principle works for public or organisational decisions. A policy discussion often starts with a vague subject such as “school uniforms” or “congestion charging”. Participants then talk past one another because different people are defending different conclusions. A conclusion first map begins with a statement such as: “The school should introduce a uniform policy next year.” Supporters and critics can now attach reasons to the same claim. Support might include: Reduced visible economic differences among pupils. Simpler dress code enforcement. Objections might include: Additional costs for families. Reduced student autonomy. The advantage is not that disagreement disappears. Rather, the disagreement becomes easier to locate. Participants can identify whether they dispute the evidence, the assumptions, or the conclusion itself. Argument mapping approaches used for analysing real world decisions similarly organise reasons around explicit conclusions so that support and objections can be examined systematically. [Insight Assessment+2Open University] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment Mapping Decisions and Arguments Insight AssessmentMapping Decisions and Arguments Insight AssessmentThe twenty example maps included begin with a configuration illustr... Common Mistakes When Building Decision Claims Even when people try to start with a conclusion, several predictable problems appear. Conclusions that are too vague A claim"},{"title":"Evidence Gaps","url":"/evidence-gaps/","description":"Mapping a choice reveals which facts still need checking before confidence is justified.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Argument Maps","level":3,"text":"Evidence Gaps What Your Decision Map Says to Check Next Evidence Gaps Evidence Gaps What Your Decision Map Says to Check Next Mapping a choice reveals which facts still need checking before confidence is justified. Mapping a choice reveals which facts still need checking before confidence is justified. population context Marking unsupported reasons Separating confidence from missing evidence Using job offer checks as a practical example improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps evidence gaps Introduction A personal decision map is most useful not when it confirms what you already think, but when it reveals what you do not yet know. In argument mapping, a decision often appears stronger once its reasons are written down. Yet the real value comes from identifying unsupported claims , hidden assumptions , and missing facts that must be checked before confidence is justified. A visible map can expose the gap between “I feel ready to decide” and “I have enough evidence to decide well”. Research on argument mapping consistently shows that making reasoning explicit improves critical thinking because it forces claims and supporting evidence into a structure that can be examined rather than merely felt. At the same time, studies of confidence and decision making show that people frequently become confident before gathering all the information that would improve judgement. The combination makes evidence gaps especially important: they are the places where additional checking could materially change a decision. [University of Southampton Web Archive+2AJET] web archive.southampton.ac.uk University of Southampton Web ArchiveArgument Maps Improve Critical Thinkingby CR Twardy · Cited by 356 — Computer based argument mapping... What Your Decision Map Says to Check Next When people map a decision, they often discover that some reasons are supported by evidence while others are merely beliefs, impressions, or expectations. The map becomes a guide to future information gathering. Consider a simple decision: “I should buy this house.” Supporting reasons might include affordability, location, investment potential, and quality of life. Once these reasons are placed on a map, each can be inspected separately. Some branches may already have evidence, such as mortgage calculations. Others may depend on assumptions, such as expected future property values or neighbourhood development plans. The practical question becomes: which branch contains the largest uncertainty? Instead of gathering information randomly, the map highlights the evidence gaps most likely to affect the conclusion. This mirrors a broader principle from evidence based decision making: high quality decisions depend on evidence that is relevant, reliable, and appropriately reflects uncertainty. Missing information is not evenly distributed across a decision. Some gaps are trivial; others sit beneath a crucial supporting reason. [QCMR] qcmr 1.itrcweb.org QCMR3 Evidence Based DecisionsToday — Evidence fit for decision making is relevant, reliable, based on appropriate data and judgment, and... Marking Unsupported Reasons A useful decision map distinguishes between supported reasons and unsupported reasons. Unsupported reasons are not necessarily wrong. They are simply claims for which adequate evidence has not yet been collected. Common examples include: Predictions about future outcomes . Assumptions about costs or risks. Judgements based on limited experience. Generalisations from a small number of examples. Information heard second hand but not verified. One practical technique is to label each reason according to its evidence status: StatusMeaningVerifiedSupported by direct evidence or reliable sourcesPartially supportedSome evidence exists but important questions remainUnverifiedNo meaningful evidence has been checkedContradictedAvailable evidence weakens the claim This prevents all reasons from appearing equally strong simply because they occupy similar positions in the diagram. Argument mapping research emphasises that the process of linking reasons to evidence improves evaluation because it makes support relationships visible. A reason that looked persuasive in conversation can appear surprisingly weak once the supporting evidence is absent from the map. University of Southampton Web Archive+2Research Repository [web archive.southampton.ac.uk] web archive.southampton.ac.uk University of Southampton Web ArchiveArgument Maps Improve Critical Thinkingby CR Twardy · Cited by 356 — Computer based argument mapping... Critical Thinking with Argument Maps Channel: Argumentful · Views: 8.5K · Uploaded: July 2020 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Hidden assumptions often create the largest gaps Many evidence gaps are not missing facts but unstated assumptions. For example: “This role will advance my career.” “The cheaper option will be good enough.” “The risk is unlikely to occur.” “I can adapt to the longer commute.” Each statement may be functioning as a support for the decision without being explicitly recognised. Once written into the map, the assumption becomes a claim that can be investigated rather than silently accepted. Research on argument mapping highlights the importance of exposing these hidden premises because they frequently carry much of the argumentative weight despite receiving little scrutiny. [UAB Digital Document Repository] ddd.uab.cat Digital Document Repository Argument map UAB Digital Document RepositoryArgument map Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia5 Jul 2016 — The purpose of mapping is to uncover the logic... The Easiest Way to Improve Critical Thinking Channel: Argumentful · Views: 959 · Uploaded: November 2020 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Separating Confidence from Missing Evidence One of the most valuable lessons from decision mapping is that confidence and evidence are not the same thing. People often feel increasingly confident as they construct a coherent story around a decision. However, psychological research shows that confidence can become disconnected from accuracy. Studies of calibration—the relationship between confidence and correctness—find that better decisions emerge when confidence is aligned with the quality of available evidence rather than with the strength of a person's intuition or narrative. [Emerald Publishing+2Alexandria] emerald.com Improving decision making through visual knowledge Emerald PublishingImproving decision making through visual knowledge calibration29 Mar 2023 — calibration helps to close the gap between... A decision map helps separate these two questions: 1. How confident am I? 2. What evidence supports that confidence? If confidence is high but several critical branches remain unsupported, the map provides a warning signal. The appropriate response is not necessarily to delay the decision indefinitely. Instead, it is to identify which missing facts would most improve confidence calibration. Research on information seeking under uncertainty suggests that confidence can influence whether people search for additional information, yet people do not always seek available evidence even when uncertainty remains. Visualising evidence gaps makes those opportunities harder to ignore. [Oxford University Research Archive] ora.ox.ac.uk ford University Research ArchiveConfidence and information seeking in decision making under...by L Wei · 2021 · Cited by 1 — This thes... Not every gap deserves equal attention A common mistake is trying to eliminate all uncertainty. Decision maps work better when they prioritise high impact gaps. Ask: Would resolving this uncertainty change my choice? Does this claim support a central branch or a peripheral one? Is reliable evidence reasonably available? What is the cost of being wrong? A missing fact about a minor feature may not justify further investigation. A missing fact about a major financial obligation or legal risk probably does. The goal is not perfect information. It is sufficient evidence for the importance and consequences of the decision. Argument Mapping: Advanced Tips and Tricks Channel: Tanya Hall · Views: 1.4K · Uploaded: February 2020 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Using Job Offer Checks as a Practical Example Job offers provide a useful example because people often feel positive about an opportunity before fully examining the details. Imagine the conclusion in a decision map is: “I should accept this job offer.” [indeed.com] indeed.com job offer considerations 18 Important Considerations Before Accepting a Job Offer4 May 2026 — You should seriously assess a job offer before accepting it, w... Published: May 2026 Supporting reasons might include: [indeed.com] indeed.com job offer considerations 18 Important Considerations Before Accepting a Job Offer4 May 2026 — You should seriously assess a job offer before accepting it, w... Published: May 2026 Higher salary. Better career progression. More interesting work. Flexible working arrangements. Strong"},{"title":"Assumptions","url":"/assumptions/","description":"Argument maps expose the unstated premises that often decide whether a conclusion really follows.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Argument Maps","level":3,"text":"Assumptions The Missing Premise That Carries the Argument Assumptions Assumptions The Missing Premise That Carries the Argument Argument maps expose the unstated premises that often decide whether a conclusion really follows. Argument maps expose the unstated premises that often decide whether a conclusion really follows. mechanism Why assumptions vanish in ordinary prose How to spot a smuggled criterion Testing assumptions before accepting the conclusion improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps assumptions Introduction Many arguments succeed or fail on a claim that is never actually stated. In argument mapping, this hidden claim is often called an unstated premise, implicit premise, assumption, or warrant: the missing link that connects a reason to a conclusion. When that link is weak, controversial, or false, the entire argument can collapse even if the visible evidence is accurate. The practical value of argument mapping is that it forces these invisible links into the open, where they can be examined rather than quietly accepted. Research on argument mapping and critical thinking consistently treats the identification of unstated assumptions as a core skill because it reveals what an argument truly depends upon. [Wikipedia+2IU Pressbooks] Wikipedia Argument map Argument map The Missing Premise That Carries the Argument An argument rarely moves directly from evidence to conclusion. Usually there is an intermediate belief that tells us why the evidence matters. Consider: Productivity has not fallen since remote work was introduced. Therefore, remote work should continue. At first glance, the reasoning appears complete. Yet the conclusion only follows if we add a hidden assumption such as: “Productivity is the main criterion for deciding the policy.” Once that assumption is visible, the discussion changes. Someone might agree with the productivity evidence while disagreeing that productivity should outweigh employee wellbeing, collaboration, security concerns, or organisational culture. The disagreement was never really about productivity; it was about the unstated criterion. [readlite.in+2AdmitStudio] readlite.in Authors rarely spell out every step of their reasoning — they assume...Read more... This is why hidden assumptions matter so much in argument maps. They often carry more argumentative weight than the visible premises. A map makes this visible by showing that the conclusion depends not only on stated reasons but also on an inferential bridge connecting them. [Wikipedia+2UMass People] Wikipedia Argument map Argument map Why Assumptions Vanish in Ordinary Prose People usually do not hide assumptions deliberately. Most disappear because they seem obvious to the speaker. Shared background beliefs In everyday conversation, repeating every premise would be exhausting. Speakers assume that listeners share common knowledge, cultural expectations, or background values. As a result, crucial premises remain implicit. Philosophers have long referred to such arguments as enthymemes—arguments with unstated premises. [UMass People+2Humanities LibreTexts] people.umass.edu 3. Validity and Connecting Premises. Recognizing a certain kind of implicit premise is at the heart of...Read more... For example: [admitstudio.com] admitstudio.com Identifying Unstated Assumptions: Finding the Hidden...Feb 18, 2026 — Unstated assumptions are premises an author takes for g... “Cloning humans is wrong because it is unnatural.” [philosophy.hku.hk] philosophy.hku.hk Philosophy@HKU[A07] Hidden AssumptionsWhen people give arguments sometimes certain assumptions are left implicit. Example: Cloning human... The reasoning only works if another premise is added: “Whatever is unnatural is wrong.” Without that additional claim, the conclusion does not logically follow. [Philosophy@HKU] philosophy.hku.hk Philosophy@HKU[A07] Hidden AssumptionsWhen people give arguments sometimes certain assumptions are left implicit. Example: Cloning human... Compression for persuasion Arguments are often more persuasive when they move quickly. A speaker may jump from evidence to conclusion because the audience mentally fills in the missing step. The result feels intuitive and compelling even when the omitted premise would be controversial if stated directly. [PrepAiro] prepairo.ai Critical Reasoning Strategies for GRE Arguments &...23 Dec 2025 — The Assumptions: The unstated beliefs that connect premises... Hidden value judgements Many assumptions are not factual claims at all. They are value judgements about what should count. For example: [admitstudio.com] admitstudio.com Identifying Unstated Assumptions: Finding the Hidden...Feb 18, 2026 — Unstated assumptions are premises an author takes for g... Crime rates fell after a surveillance programme was introduced. Therefore, the programme should continue. The hidden premise is not merely causal. It may be: “Reducing crime justifies the privacy costs of surveillance.” Once stated, the real debate becomes visible. Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w/ Nate Otey Channel: The Stoa · Views: 3.5K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube How to Spot a Smuggled Criterion One of the most important kinds of hidden assumption is the smuggled criterion: an unstated standard that determines success, failure, rightness, or importance. Arguments frequently appear to be about facts when they are actually about criteria. Consider the claim: University tuition should increase because it will generate more revenue. The unstated criterion might be: “Generating more revenue is the primary goal that should guide tuition policy.” A critic could accept every factual claim while rejecting that criterion. When mapping arguments, ask: [philosophy.hku.hk] philosophy.hku.hk Philosophy@HKU[A07] Hidden AssumptionsWhen people give arguments sometimes certain assumptions are left implicit. Example: Cloning human... 1. Why does this reason matter? 2. What standard is being used to judge the conclusion? 3. Would the argument still work if someone accepted the facts but rejected that standard? If the answer to the third question is “no”, you have probably found a hidden assumption. Many public disagreements persist because participants argue about evidence while actually disagreeing about criteria. One side prioritises efficiency; another prioritises fairness. One side values growth; another values sustainability. The visible dispute hides a deeper assumption level conflict. [Condor] condor.depaul.edu A Guide To Critical ThinkingContrasting Assumptions. If two sides are arguing from different assumptions, it is very effective to f... When One Assumption Reverses the Conclusion Some assumptions are so central that changing them completely reverses the argument. [readlite.in] readlite.in find assumptions in argument The Assumption Hunter: Uncover Hidden Premises9 Feb 2026 — An assumption is what an argument treats as true without proving or stating. I... Consider: A new medicine extends life expectancy by six months. Therefore, it should be publicly funded. Possible hidden assumptions include: [readlite.in] readlite.in Authors rarely spell out every step of their reasoning — they assume...Read more... Extending life expectancy is worth the cost. The healthcare budget can absorb the expense. Resources would not produce greater benefits elsewhere. Alter those assumptions and the conclusion may change even though the evidence remains identical. This illustrates a key insight from argument mapping: evidence rarely determines a conclusion by itself. Conclusions emerge from evidence plus assumptions. The same evidence can support different conclusions when different assumptions are supplied. [IU Pressbooks+2Informal Logic] iu.pressbooks.pub Arguments IV – Hidden Premises – Phil P102 Critical...A hidden premise is a claim that is logically relied upon as a premis... Argument Basics: How to Spot Hidden Assumptions (Warrants) Channel: Effective Writing Center Open on YouTube Testing Assumptions Before Accepting the Conclusion Finding assumptions is only the first step. The next task is evaluating them. Make the assumption explicit Rewrite the argument so the hidden premise becomes visible. Instead of: “The campaign increased sales, so it was successful.” Write: “The campaign increased sales.” “Increasing sales is the primary measure of campaign success.” “Therefore, the campaign was successful.” The hidden premise can now be assessed directly. [PrepAiro] prepairo.ai Critical Reasoning Strategies for GRE Arguments &...23 Dec 2025 — The Assumptions: The unstated beliefs that connect premises... Ask whether the assumption is necessary Remove the assumption and see whether the conclusion still follows. If the reasoning breaks, the assumption is genuinely carrying argumentative weight rather than merely providing background context. [Humanities LibreTexts] human.libretexts.org 2.09: Uncovering Implicit Premises ities"},{"title":"Objections","url":"/objections-b6eac6/","description":"A good map shows whether an objection weakens one branch, a hidden inference, or the entire claim.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Argument Maps","level":3,"text":"Objections Where Does the Objection Actually Land? Objections Objections Where Does the Objection Actually Land? A good map shows whether an objection weakens one branch, a hidden inference, or the entire claim. A good map shows whether an objection weakens one branch, a hidden inference, or the entire claim. mechanism Objections to conclusions, reasons, and links Why small objections can matter more than they look Mapping cleaner disagreement in heated debates improving your thinking and analytical skills argument maps objections Introduction In argument mapping, the most useful question about an objection is often not whether it is true, but where it lands. An objection can target a supporting reason, challenge the connection between a reason and a conclusion, or attack the conclusion directly. These are different moves, and they have different consequences for the strength of an argument. A good map makes those differences visible by showing exactly which part of the reasoning is under pressure . Research on argument mapping emphasises that evaluating an argument involves assessing both the quality of premises and the strength of the inferences connecting them to conclusions, not merely judging the final claim in isolation. [AJET] ajet.org.au Improving critical thinking using web based argument...by S Butchart · 2009 · Cited by 136 — There are two aspects to argument evalu... When disagreements remain vague, people often talk past one another. One person may be attacking evidence, another may be challenging an assumption, and a third may be rejecting the conclusion outright. Mapping objections to their precise targets turns a general dispute into a specific analytical task. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Argument map January 25, 2026 — Argument maps are commonly used in the context of teaching and applying critical thinking. The purpose of mapping is t... Published: January 25, 2026 Objections to Conclusions, Reasons, and Links Argument maps are valuable because they separate three distinct targets of criticism. Objections to a Reason A reason objection argues that one of the supporting claims is weak, false, incomplete, or poorly evidenced. Consider the claim: Conclusion: \"The council should pedestrianise the street.\" Reason: \"Pedestrianisation increases local business activity.\" An objection to the reason might be: \"The business data comes from a very different type of neighbourhood.\" The critic is not yet denying the conclusion. They are challenging one supporting branch. If the argument has several independent reasons, the conclusion may still survive even if this branch fails. This is why maps are useful: they reveal whether a criticism removes a single support beam or threatens the whole structure. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Argument Mapping with Reason!AbleArgument mappings are box and line diagrams that represent the arguments and evidence... Objections to the Link A link objection accepts the reason but disputes the inference. Using the same example: Reason: \"Pedestrianisation increases local business activity.\" Conclusion: \"Therefore the street should be pedestrianised.\" A link objection could be: \"Economic activity alone is not enough to justify the policy.\" Notice the difference. The critic may fully accept the evidence about business activity. What they reject is the step from that evidence to the policy recommendation. Argumentation researchers often describe these hidden connecting assumptions as warrants or inferential bridges. Many arguments fail not because their reasons are false, but because the link between reason and conclusion is insufficient or relies on an unstated assumption. [arXiv] arxiv.org The Argument Reasoning Comprehension Task: Identification and Reconstruction of Implicit WarrantsAugust 4, 2017... Published: August 4, 2017 Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w/ Nate Otey Channel: The Stoa · Views: 3.5K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube Objections to the Conclusion A conclusion objection directly disputes the final claim. For example: Conclusion: \"The street should be pedestrianised.\" Objection: \"The street should remain open to vehicles.\" This type of objection presents a rival position. However, it is often less informative than objections aimed at reasons or links. Simply denying a conclusion does not explain why the conclusion is wrong. Effective critical thinking usually requires identifying the specific premises or inferences that fail. [LogicCheck] logiccheck.net evaluating an argument map Evaluating an Argument Map1 Sept 2020 — A map is extremely useful for evaluating an argument because it gives you a precise, sy... Why Small Objections Can Matter More Than They Look People often overestimate objections aimed at visible evidence and underestimate objections aimed at inferential links. A seemingly minor challenge to a hidden assumption can undermine an entire argument. Consider: Reason: \"Remote workers are equally productive.\" Conclusion: \" Remote work should remain permanent.\" The argument may rely on an unstated assumption: \"Productivity is the main criterion for deciding workplace policy.\" An objection that attacks this assumption may be more damaging than disputes about productivity statistics. If decision makers care equally about mentoring, collaboration, security, or employee wellbeing, then the original inference becomes much weaker. Argument mapping makes these hidden dependencies visible. Rather than arguing endlessly about productivity figures, participants can identify the real point of disagreement. [YouTube] youtube.com Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w...\"Argument mapping is a visual method of displaying how reasons work to s... This is one reason mapping often improves critical thinking performance. The process encourages evaluation of inferential structure rather than attention only to surface claims. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Argument Maps Improve Critical ThinkingComputer based argument mapping greatly enhances student critical think ing, mo... The Difference Between Local Damage and Structural Damage A useful habit is to ask whether an objection causes local damage or structural damage. Local damage affects one branch of support. Example: Reason A supports the conclusion. Reason B supports the conclusion. Reason C supports the conclusion. If an objection defeats Reason B, Reasons A and C may still provide substantial support. Structural damage affects a central inference or assumption shared by multiple branches. For example: Several reasons support the idea that a policy is beneficial. All depend on the assumption that benefits should outweigh costs. An objection to that decision rule affects every branch simultaneously. Maps help distinguish these situations because the visual structure shows whether a criticism attacks a leaf of the tree or the trunk itself. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Argument Mapping with Reason!AbleArgument mappings are box and line diagrams that represent the arguments and evidence... Argument Mapping: Advanced Tips and Tricks Channel: Tanya Hall · Views: 1.4K · Uploaded: February 2020 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Mapping Cleaner Disagreement in Heated Debates Many public disagreements become unproductive because participants target different parts of the argument without recognising it. A debate about artificial intelligence, housing policy, education, or climate action often contains multiple simultaneous disputes: Disagreement about facts. Disagreement about causal relationships. Disagreement about values. Disagreement about what follows from the evidence. Without a map, these disputes blur together. With a map, participants can identify questions such as: Is the evidence reliable? Does the evidence actually support the claim? [thetenuretrack.com] thetenuretrack.com Mapping Your Legal Arguments by Etienne Toussaint31 May 2025 — This mapping reveals how different types of evidence and reasoning work together to support the central... Published: May 2025 Is a hidden assumption doing the work? Are we disagreeing about values rather than facts? Research and educational practice in argument mapping repeatedly highlight that visualising claims, objections, and inferential relations helps make reasoning explicit and easier to evaluate. Rather than treating an argument as one large block, the map turns disagreement into a set of smaller, testable questions. [eCampusOntario+2Psychology Today] ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub See Figure 1, for an example...Read more... A Practical Test for Any Objection When encountering an objection, ask three questions: 1. Does it challenge a reason? If accepted, one piece of support becomes weaker. 2. Does it challenge the link? If accepted, the reason may no longer justify the conclusion. 3. Does it challenge the conclusion itself? If accepted, a competing position replaces the"},{"title":"Change Mind","url":"/change-mind/","description":"Asking what evidence would change your conclusion protects against reasoning that cannot be tested.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Change Mind What Evidence Would Change Your Mind? Change Mind Change Mind What Evidence Would Change Your Mind? Asking what evidence would change your conclusion protects against reasoning that cannot be tested. Asking what evidence would change your conclusion protects against reasoning that cannot be tested. policy intervention Testable conclusions Disconfirming evidence Decision review triggers improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind Introduction “What would change my mind?” is a small question with a large effect: it turns a conclusion from something you defend into something you can test. In practical thinking, the point is not to become endlessly doubtful or to abandon convictions at the first objection. It is to name, in advance, the evidence, observation, result, or failed prediction that would make you revise your view. That protects you from reasoning that cannot lose. This question matters because people naturally search for, notice, and trust evidence that fits what they already believe. Psychologists call this confirmation bias: the tendency to give more weight to belief consistent information and less weight to evidence that challenges it. A “change my mind” question interrupts that pattern by forcing the decision maker to specify what would count against the current conclusion, not only what would support it. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabConfirmation BiasConfirmation bias describes our underlying tendency to notice, focus on, and provide greater credence to... Used well, it becomes a practical intervention for better judgement: define the claim, identify disconfirming evidence , set review triggers , and make belief revision feel like progress rather than defeat. Why this question improves thinking The question works because it changes the structure of the reasoning task. Instead of asking, “Can I find reasons I am right?”, it asks, “What would I expect to see if I were wrong?” That is a different cognitive move. Peter Wason’s classic 2 4 6 rule discovery experiment illustrates the problem. Participants were shown the sequence 2, 4, 6 and asked to discover the rule by proposing other number triples. Many tested examples that fitted their first guess, such as more even numbers increasing by two, rather than examples that could disprove it. The actual rule was broader: ascending numbers. Wason interpreted the pattern as a preference for confirmation over falsification. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu On the failure to eliminate hypotheses in a conceptual task On the failure to eliminate hypotheses in a conceptual task The lesson is not that people are foolish. It is that a confirming test often feels informative even when it is weak. If you believe a project is on track, asking team members to list reasons it might succeed will probably produce plausible answers. Asking what evidence would show it is not on track is more useful: missed milestones, rising defect rates, user drop off, unresolved dependencies, budget burn, or repeated “temporary” workarounds. In this sense, “What would change my mind?” is a testability device. It pushes a belief towards the same discipline that makes a scientific claim useful: a claim should forbid something, or at least expose itself to possible contradiction. Philosophers and scientists debate the limits of falsifiability as a full theory of knowledge, but the everyday version remains valuable: a view that cannot name any possible contrary evidence is not being seriously tested. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Turn conclusions into testable claims The first implementation step is to translate a broad judgement into a claim that could be checked. “This strategy is good” is too vague. “This strategy will reduce customer churn among small business users within two quarters without raising support costs” is much easier to test. A useful change my mind question usually has three parts : Current conclusion: What do I currently believe or plan to do? Decision relevant assumption: What must be true for that conclusion to hold? Revision condition: What evidence would make me lower confidence, change course, or stop? For example, a team might believe: “We should launch the new onboarding flow to all users.” The change my mind version would be: “What evidence would make us delay or narrow the launch?” Good answers might include a statistically meaningful fall in activation among new users, a spike in support tickets from a specific segment, poor performance on mobile, or failed accessibility checks. This is stronger than a general instruction to “be open minded”. Research on actively open minded thinking defines it in behavioural terms: considering alternative opinions, being sensitive to contradictory evidence, delaying premature closure , and recognising that one’s judgement can be wrong. The practical question gives those ideals a place to operate inside a real decision. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement The most important design rule is specificity. A trigger such as “if the data looks bad” leaves too much room for motivated interpretation. A trigger such as “if week four retention falls by more than five percentage points for the treatment group, or if support tickets rise by more than 15 per cent for two consecutive weeks” is harder to explain away after the fact. Look for disconfirming evidence, not just more evidence More information does not automatically improve judgement. If the search is biased, more information may simply give a person more material with which to defend their existing position. That is why the question should be aimed at disconfirming evidence: the kind of finding that would make the current view less likely, not merely less comfortable. Disconfirming evidence can take several forms: A failed prediction: “We expected referrals to rise after the campaign, but they did not.” A missing mechanism: “The proposed cause does not appear in the cases where it should appear.” A counterexample: “The policy works in one group but fails in the group it was mainly meant to help.” A better rival explanation : “The outcome can be explained by seasonality, not by our intervention.” A revealed trade off: “The plan improves speed but damages quality enough to erase the benefit.” The value of disconfirming evidence is clear in forecasting research. Studies of expert forecasting and the Good Judgment Project found that better forecasters update their beliefs more often as evidence changes, and that actively open minded thinking is among the traits associated with better performance. The key habit is not dramatic reversal; it is incremental updating when the world gives new information. [Stanford University] stanford.edu Open source on stanford.edu. That distinction matters. Changing your mind does not have to mean flipping from “yes” to “no”. It can mean moving from 80 per cent confidence to 60 per cent, narrowing the scope of a claim, postponing a decision, running a smaller trial, or replacing one explanation with a better one. A good change my mind question allows partial revision. The Most Common Cognitive Bias Channel: Veritasium Open on YouTube Use the question before the decision hardens The best time to ask “What would change my mind?” is before social, financial, or identity commitments make revision painful. Once a public position has been taken, a budget has been approved, or a team has invested months of effort, contrary evidence becomes easier to reinterpret as noise. This is why the question pairs well with a premortem. Gary Klein’s premortem method asks a team to imagine that a project has already failed and then work backwards to explain why. Klein argued that this makes it safer for knowledgeable dissenters to voice concerns during planning, when those concerns can still change the plan. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org Harvard Business Review Performing a Project Premortem Harvard Business Review Performing a Project Premortem A change my mind version of a premortem is especially practical: 1. Assume the decision has failed. 2. List the earliest signs that failure was becoming likely. 3. Convert those signs into review triggers. 4. Decide in advance what action each trigger will cause. For a hiring decision, a trigger might be: “If the work sample test contradicts the interview impression, we will give the work sample more weight.” For a product launch, it might be: “If early adopters use the feature once but do not return, we will treat excitement as curiosity rather than demand.” For a policy intervention, it might be: “If the programme helps the easiest to reach group but not the target group, we will redesign the delivery model rather than declare success.” This turns dissent from a personality conflict into a planning tool. The question is no longer “Who is being negative?” but “Which"},{"title":"Review Triggers","url":"/review-triggers/","description":"Predefined review triggers help teams revisit decisions without treating every setback as a crisis.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Change Mind","level":3,"text":"Review Triggers How Review Triggers Make Changing Course Easier Review Triggers Review Triggers How Review Triggers Make Changing Course Easier Predefined review triggers help teams revisit decisions without treating every setback as a crisis. Predefined review triggers help teams revisit decisions without treating every setback as a crisis. policy intervention Choosing meaningful metrics Setting thresholds and time windows Assigning review actions and owners improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind review triggers Introduction A decision review trigger is a pre agreed rule that specifies when a decision must be revisited and what kind of evidence could justify changing course. Within the broader practice of asking “What would change my mind ?”, review triggers turn an abstract commitment to open mindedness into an operational governance process. Rather than waiting until a project has clearly failed—or reacting to every temporary setback—they create predictable moments for reassessment based on evidence instead of emotion. This matters because people and organisations often become trapped by previous investments of time, money, effort or reputation. Research on escalation of commitment shows that decision makers frequently continue supporting a struggling course of action even after receiving negative feedback , partly because abandoning it feels like admitting failure rather than making a better forward looking decision. [PMC+2Project Management Institute] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A New Inaction Effect Perspective on the Sunk Cost Fallacyby G Feldman · 2018 · Cited by 66 — Escalation of commitment situations invo... How review triggers reduce commitment traps The central purpose of a review trigger is to separate evaluating the original decision from evaluating today's best option . Without predefined triggers, reviews often happen only when someone becomes concerned enough to challenge the existing plan. By then, discussions can become personal. Team members may defend earlier decisions because they made them, because others expect consistency, or because substantial resources have already been invested. A review trigger changes the conversation. Instead of asking: \"Should we admit we were wrong?\" the agreed question becomes: \"Has the agreed review condition been met?\" This shift reduces self justification and makes changing course appear as following the process rather than abandoning a commitment. Organisational research has repeatedly found that external oversight and separating initial decisions from later review decisions can reduce escalation of commitment because they weaken personal ownership of the original choice. [Northwestern Psychology] psychology.northwestern.edu Psychology Promoting De Escalation of Commitment: A Regulatory Northwestern PsychologyPromoting De Escalation of Commitment: A Regulatory ...January 5, 2011 — by DC Molden · 2011 · Cited by 117 — Sol... Published: January 5, 2011 Choosing meaningful metrics Review triggers are only useful if they rely on measures that genuinely reflect whether the underlying assumptions remain true. Good review metrics have three characteristics: They relate directly to the decision's expected outcomes . They are observable rather than subjective. They can deteriorate before complete failure becomes obvious. For example, a team launching a new software feature might agree in advance to review the rollout if: weekly active usage remains below an agreed target; customer support requests exceed a defined level; critical defects remain unresolved beyond a specified period; or infrastructure costs exceed expected limits without corresponding user growth. The important feature is that these indicators were chosen before knowing whether the decision would succeed. That reduces the temptation to redefine success after disappointing results. Poor review metrics often measure effort rather than progress. Statements such as \"the team is working hard\" or \"we've already invested six months\" describe commitment, not evidence that continuing is worthwhile. What is the Escalation of Commitment? Psychology Facts Channel: Biased Knowledge Open on YouTube Setting thresholds and time windows A useful trigger combines what will be measured with how much change is sufficient to require a review and when that judgement will be made. Thresholds prevent constant second guessing while avoiding indefinite persistence. Examples include: customer retention falls below 80% for two consecutive months; project costs exceed the approved budget by 15%; recruitment targets remain unmet after one quarter; a policy fails to achieve its minimum outcome by the first annual evaluation. Time windows are equally important. Reviewing too early may mistake normal variation for failure. Waiting too long increases the risk of additional sunk costs . Different decisions naturally require different review periods: operational experiments may justify weekly reviews; product launches may need monthly or quarterly reviews; strategic investments often require milestone based reviews rather than continuous reconsideration. Combining thresholds with minimum observation periods helps distinguish genuine trends from random fluctuations. Instead of reacting to one disappointing week, the organisation asks whether performance has crossed the agreed boundary over the agreed period. Assigning review actions and owners A trigger should specify not only when a review occurs but also who conducts it and what happens next . Without predefined ownership, review meetings are easily postponed or become exercises in defending existing plans. A practical governance rule normally specifies: the individual or committee responsible for initiating the review; the evidence to be gathered before discussion; who has authority to recommend continuation, modification or termination; whether additional approval is needed before significant new resources are committed. Many organisations deliberately separate implementation from review. The people responsible for delivering a project possess valuable operational knowledge, but an independent reviewer or steering group may be less vulnerable to self justification because their own reputation is less closely tied to the original decision. Research on de escalation strategies consistently identifies independent oversight and divided responsibility as mechanisms that reduce commitment bias. [Northwestern Psychology] psychology.northwestern.edu Psychology Promoting De Escalation of Commitment: A Regulatory Northwestern PsychologyPromoting De Escalation of Commitment: A Regulatory ...January 5, 2011 — by DC Molden · 2011 · Cited by 117 — Sol... Published: January 5, 2011 Sunk Cost Fallacy: Not Knowing When It’s Time to Stop Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Reviews should create options, not only binary choices One misconception is that crossing a review threshold means a project automatically stops. More often, the review creates a structured choice among several future actions, for example: continue unchanged because assumptions still hold; continue with revised scope; pause while key uncertainties are resolved; conduct another limited experiment; terminate the initiative and redirect resources. This keeps the review focused on future expected value rather than on judging past decisions. The objective is not to prove that someone was wrong but to determine which option now offers the strongest evidence supported path forward. Common design mistakes Even well intentioned review systems can fail if the triggers are poorly designed. Frequent problems include: Vague triggers. \"Review if things are not going well\" leaves too much room for interpretation. Moving thresholds. Lowering standards after disappointing results removes the discipline that review triggers are intended to create. Too many indicators. Excessive metrics allow decision makers to ignore the few that genuinely matter. Ignoring opportunity costs. Reviews should ask not only whether the current project can still succeed, but whether available alternatives now offer greater expected value. Treating reviews as blame sessions. If every review becomes an investigation into personal responsibility, people become less willing to surface negative information early. Research on escalation of commitment suggests that reputational concerns can themselves encourage persistence. People may continue with weak decisions because consistency is interpreted as trustworthiness or reliability. Effective governance therefore makes evidence based revision appear as responsible decision making rather than personal failure. [CHARLES DORISON] charlesdorison.com CHARLES DORISONJournal of Experimental Psychology: Generalby CA Dorison · 2021 · Cited by 37 — Escalation of commitment—the tendency to r... The Tool That Stops You From Holding Losers Too Long Annie Duke Channel: William Green Open on YouTube Making \"What would change my mind?\" operational The"},{"title":"Disconfirming Evidence","url":"/disconfirming-evidence/","description":"The strongest test of a belief often comes from evidence that could prove it wrong.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Change Mind","level":3,"text":"Disconfirming Evidence Why Contradictory Evidence Is More Useful Than More Proof Disconfirming Evidence Disconfirming Evidence Why Contradictory Evidence Is More Useful Than More Proof The strongest test of a belief often comes from evidence that could prove it wrong. The strongest test of a belief often comes from evidence that could prove it wrong. mechanism Types of disconfirming evidence Finding rival explanations Updating confidence without overreacting improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind disconfirming evidence Introduction Asking “What would change my mind ?” is only useful if you genuinely look for evidence that could reduce your confidence rather than simply collecting more reasons to believe you are already correct. Once a belief has enough supporting evidence to be plausible, additional confirming examples often add surprisingly little information. By contrast, a single piece of reliable contradictory evidence can reveal that an assumption is incomplete, a causal explanation is wrong, or the belief only holds under limited conditions. This does not mean treating every objection as decisive. It means deliberately searching for evidence that has the power to prove your current explanation wrong, because such evidence is usually more informative than another example that merely fits what you already expected. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Why contradictory evidence is more useful than more proof One of the most common reasoning mistakes is assuming that confidence should grow simply because more supporting evidence has been collected. In reality, evidence has unequal value. Suppose you believe that a new training programme improves employee performance. Ten managers who already favour the programme may report positive experiences. Those reports increase confidence only slightly because they are exactly what your hypothesis predicted. However, discovering that comparable teams using the programme consistently perform worse under carefully measured conditions would force a much deeper rethink. The contradictory finding tests the belief in a way that repeated agreement cannot. This principle explains why psychologists distinguish between confirmation and diagnostic testing . Confirming evidence often tells you what you already expected to observe. Disconfirming evidence asks a different question: what would I see if my explanation were mistaken? Evidence that can answer that question carries much greater informational value. [Wikipedia+2lakens.github.io] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Types of disconfirming evidence Not every disagreement counts as meaningful contradictory evidence. The most useful forms directly challenge the assumptions on which the belief depends. Common examples include: Failed predictions. A belief predicts that a specific outcome should occur, but it repeatedly does not. Reliable counterexamples. Even one well established case can show that a supposedly universal claim is false. Competing explanations. Another hypothesis explains the same observations equally well or better. Boundary cases. The belief works only under narrow conditions rather than generally. Independent replication failures. Other investigators cannot reproduce the expected results despite following comparable methods. The key question is whether the evidence would genuinely lower your confidence if it turned out to be true. If the answer is no, then the evidence is not functioning as a real test. Confirmation Bias: The Reason Smart People Are Usually the Most Wrong Channel: ThinkLessPsy Open on YouTube Finding rival explanations instead of defending one explanation People naturally explain new information in ways that preserve existing beliefs. This tendency, often called confirmation bias or myside bias, affects what information we search for, how we interpret ambiguous evidence, and even what we remember later. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias A stronger approach is to generate competing explanations before collecting more evidence. Instead of asking: \"What evidence shows my conclusion is correct?\" ask questions such as: What else could explain these observations? What prediction differs between the two explanations? What observation would favour the alternative instead? If I had to argue against my own position, what would be my strongest point? For example, if customer satisfaction rises after launching a new website, the obvious explanation is that the redesign worked. A rival explanation might be seasonal demand, a pricing change, or improved customer support. The next step is not to defend either explanation but to identify evidence capable of distinguishing between them. This shifts reasoning away from advocacy and towards discrimination between competing hypotheses. Lessons from the classic Wason experiments Psychologist Peter Wason demonstrated this problem in his famous rule discovery experiments. Participants were shown the sequence 2, 4, 6 and asked to discover the underlying rule by proposing additional number triples. Most quickly guessed a specific rule—for example, \"numbers increasing by two\"—and then tested more examples that fit that idea, such as 8, 10, 12 . Those examples almost always received confirmation, reinforcing the incorrect hypothesis. Far fewer participants proposed triples designed to prove themselves wrong, such as 3, 5, 4 or 8, 6, 4 . Those disconfirming tests would have revealed much sooner that the actual rule was simply any ascending sequence . [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Peter Cathcart Wason Peter Cathcart Wason The lesson extends well beyond laboratory puzzles. People often design \"tests\" that can only produce supporting evidence. A better test deliberately creates the possibility of discovering that the current explanation is mistaken. Updating confidence without overreacting Seeking contradictory evidence does not mean abandoning a belief whenever criticism appears. Instead, think of confidence as something that moves gradually. Useful questions include: Is the contradictory evidence reliable? Does it directly challenge a core assumption or only a minor detail? Could measurement error explain the apparent contradiction? Does the evidence repeat across independent observations? Does the alternative explanation now account for more of the facts? This approach avoids two opposite mistakes: Ignoring contradictory evidence , which leads to unwarranted certainty. Overreacting to isolated anomalies , which creates instability and constant opinion changes. Good judgement means allowing confidence to increase or decrease in proportion to the quality of the evidence, not simply the quantity. this is how to improve your critical thinking according to neuroscience THE ONLY VIDEO THAT WORKS Channel: erin meryl study Open on YouTube Practical ways to search for disconfirming evidence The habit becomes easier if it is built into decision making before a conclusion feels psychologically committed. Useful practices include: Write down one observation that would genuinely change your mind before gathering additional evidence. Assign someone to argue for the strongest competing explanation rather than simply criticising details. Search specifically for studies, examples or datasets that contradict your preferred conclusion. Compare predictions from multiple explanations before deciding which evidence to collect. Periodically ask whether new evidence would still count if it supported the opposite conclusion. These habits make it less likely that evidence collection becomes an exercise in self validation. A useful shift in mindset Collecting more supporting evidence often increases confidence without substantially increasing understanding. Looking for evidence that could prove a belief wrong does the opposite: it exposes hidden assumptions, distinguishes between competing explanations, and reveals where confidence is genuinely deserved. Within the broader practice of asking \"What would change my mind?\", this is the central mechanism. The goal is not to become sceptical of everything, but to make beliefs answerable to evidence capable of changing them. That is what turns a belief from something merely defended into something genuinely tested. [Wikipedia+2PMC] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias The Black Swan Video 3: \"Why Your Brain Lies: The Narrative Fallacy\" Channel: Economista del Código Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Contradictory Evidence Is More Useful Than More Proof. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman Explains confirmation bias and diagnostic evidence. See on Amazon Book The Scout Mindset By Julia Galef Centers on seeking evidence that could prove you wrong. See on Amazon Book Superforecasting By Philip Tetlock, Dan Gardner Demonstrates updating beliefs from"},{"title":"Confidence Updates","url":"/confidence-updates/","description":"Evidence often justifies smaller confidence shifts rather than dramatic reversals.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Change Mind","level":3,"text":"Confidence Updates Why Changing Your Mind Can Mean Changing Your Confidence Confidence Updates Confidence Updates Why Changing Your Mind Can Mean Changing Your Confidence Evidence often justifies smaller confidence shifts rather than dramatic reversals. Evidence often justifies smaller confidence shifts rather than dramatic reversals. mechanism Confidence as a spectrum When partial updates are enough Avoiding all or nothing thinking improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind confidence updates Introduction One of the most useful answers to the question “What would change my mind ?” is: “It would not necessarily make me abandon my view—it would make me less confident in it.” Good reasoning is rarely a choice between complete certainty and complete reversal. Instead, new evidence often justifies moving from, for example, 90% confidence to 75%, or from “very likely” to “more uncertain”. This kind of gradual updating makes beliefs more responsive to reality while avoiding the instability of changing positions with every new piece of information. Research in psychology, decision science, and Bayesian reasoning consistently suggests that treating confidence as something that can be adjusted in degrees leads to better calibrated judgement and reduces the risk of both dogmatism and overreaction. [PMC+2P.J. Healy] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement by KE Stanovich · 2023 · Cited by 133 — Actively open minded thinking (AOT) is measured by items that tap the willingness to consider... Confidence as a spectrum People often describe beliefs as though they were switches: either true or false, accepted or rejected. In practice , most important decisions involve uncertainty. Whether assessing an investment, evaluating scientific evidence, or deciding between competing explanations, the more realistic question is not \"Do I believe this?\" but \"How strongly do I believe it?\" Thinking in terms of confidence creates room for evidence to have proportional effects. A single contradictory study, one disappointing business result, or an unexpected observation may reduce confidence without eliminating the overall case. Likewise, one supportive finding may strengthen a view without proving it beyond doubt. This idea is closely related to Bayesian thinking. In Bayesian reasoning, beliefs begin with a prior level of confidence and are updated as new evidence arrives, producing a revised—or posterior—level of confidence. The important practical lesson is not that everyone must perform probability calculations, but that evidence should shift beliefs by an amount roughly proportional to its strength and reliability rather than triggering automatic reversals. [astrowizici.st+2HEC Paris] astrowizici.st Bayesian Updating in Practice Andy Casey2 Mar 2026 — The Bayesian view describes how probable it is some event will take place, and we update our beliefs about the wor... A useful mental scale might look like this: 95–99%: Extraordinary confidence; only very strong contrary evidence would justify a major revision. 70–90%: Strong confidence but genuine openness to adjustment. 50–70%: Tentative judgement where additional evidence could move confidence substantially. Below 50%: The alternative explanation appears more plausible. The exact numbers matter less than recognising that confidence has many possible positions between certainty and rejection. Gentle Bayesian updating David Manley EA Student Summit 2020 Channel: Effective Altruism Open on YouTube When partial updates are enough Many decisions involve evidence that is informative without being decisive. Expecting every new fact either to confirm everything or overturn everything creates distorted thinking. Partial updates are appropriate when: The new evidence is credible but limited in scope. Different pieces of evidence point in different directions. Existing evidence remains substantial despite new objections. The new information changes the estimated likelihood rather than the underlying possibility. Consider a product launch. A company expects strong customer adoption. After the first month, uptake is weaker than expected, but customer satisfaction among early users is excellent. The rational response is rarely \"The launch will definitely fail\" or \"Nothing has changed.\" Instead, confidence in rapid growth may decrease while confidence in long term potential remains relatively high. Similarly, when evaluating a scientific claim, one failed replication should usually reduce confidence rather than eliminate belief, particularly if multiple earlier studies remain supportive. Conversely, one successful replication should increase confidence without implying that every remaining uncertainty has disappeared. Updating confidence in this way prevents a common mistake: treating every new observation as if it deserved equal weight regardless of its quality or relevance. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIReviewing the evidence: heuristics and biases NIHby L Bojke · 2021 · Cited by 3 — This chapter reviewed evidence relating to the psychological biases of greatest relevance to S... Avoiding all or nothing thinking All or nothing thinking encourages unnecessary certainty. Once people feel committed to an extreme position, any concession can seem like admitting complete error. This creates psychological resistance to learning. A confidence based approach changes the incentives. Lowering confidence from 90% to 75% is not a failure. It is evidence that the belief is responding appropriately to new information. This also makes discussions more productive. Instead of asking: \"Were you wrong?\" people can ask: \"How much should this evidence change your confidence?\" That question is usually easier to answer honestly because it allows proportionate responses instead of forcing binary choices. Research on actively open minded thinking describes this willingness to consider contrary evidence and delay premature certainty as a characteristic associated with better reasoning and more accurate judgement. Importantly, open mindedness does not require abandoning beliefs easily; it requires allowing evidence to influence confidence appropriately. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement by KE Stanovich · 2023 · Cited by 133 — Actively open minded thinking (AOT) is measured by items that tap the willingness to consider... The Metacognitive Underpinnings of Confirmation Bias and its Political Consequences Channel: CenterforBrainHealth Open on YouTube Why overconfidence makes updating harder Confidence itself influences how people process new information. Experimental neuroscience has shown that when individuals are highly confident in an initial judgement, they become more likely to process supporting information while giving less weight to contradictory evidence. High confidence therefore does more than reflect beliefs—it can actively make future belief revision less likely by changing how subsequent evidence is interpreted. [Nature] nature.com Confidence drives a neural confirmation biasby M Rollwage · 2020 · Cited by 269 — We show that holding high confidence in a decisio... This helps explain why confidence should itself be treated as something worth monitoring. Questions such as these can help: How certain was I before seeing this evidence? Is my confidence justified by the quality of the evidence, or simply by familiarity ? Would someone who disagreed reasonably lower my confidence after seeing this information? These questions separate confidence from commitment. They encourage calibration rather than stubbornness. Practical ways to update confidence Confidence updating works best when it is deliberate rather than intuitive. A simple process is: 1. State your current confidence. Even rough estimates such as \"about 80% confident\" create a baseline. 2. Evaluate the new evidence. Consider its reliability, independence, and relevance rather than whether it supports your preferred conclusion. 3. Adjust proportionally. Strong evidence deserves larger shifts than weak or ambiguous evidence. 4. Look for accumulation. Several modest pieces of evidence may together justify a substantial update, even if none would do so individually. This approach also reduces emotional attachment to particular conclusions. Instead of defending an identity or a position, you are continually refining an estimate. The power of Bayesian reasoning BBC Ideas Channel: BBC Ideas Open on YouTube The goal is calibration, not constant doubt Updating confidence should not become permanent indecision. Good judgement aims for calibration: confidence that matches the available evidence as closely as possible. Someone who never changes confidence is likely to ignore new information. Someone who dramatically changes position after every new observation is equally unlikely to reach consistently good conclusions. The most reliable thinkers tend to occupy the middle ground, making many"},{"title":"Premortems","url":"/premortems/","description":"Imagining a future failure helps identify early warning signs before a decision becomes difficult to reverse.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Change Mind","level":3,"text":"Premortems Can a Premortem Reveal When You Should Reconsider? Premortems Premortems Can a Premortem Reveal When You Should Reconsider? Imagining a future failure helps identify early warning signs before a decision becomes difficult to reverse. Imagining a future failure helps identify early warning signs before a decision becomes difficult to reverse. policy intervention Imagining the failed outcome Turning warning signs into triggers Using premortems without creating pessimism improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind premortems Introduction A premortem is one of the most practical ways to answer the question, “What would change my mind ?” Instead of asking whether a plan seems sensible, a premortem asks participants to imagine that the decision has already failed and then explain why. That shift in perspective makes it easier to identify the observations that should cause a team—or an individual—to reconsider before a failure becomes irreversible. Rather than treating belief revision as an emotional reaction to bad news, a premortem turns it into a planned part of decision making. Research on “prospective hindsight”, the psychological principle behind premortems, suggests that imagining a future outcome as certain helps people generate more concrete explanations for what could go wrong, making blind spots easier to detect. [homepages.se.edu] homepages.se.edu Performing a Project Premortem by Gary Klein July 18, 2011 — By making it safe for dissenters who are knowledgeable about the undertaking and worried about its weaknesses to speak up... Published: July 18, 2011 Within a broader practice of asking “What would change my mind?”, the distinctive value of a premortem is that it converts imagined causes of failure into predefined signals for changing course. The exercise is therefore not merely about listing risks. It is about deciding in advance which warning signs deserve action. Imagining the failed outcome The premortem technique was introduced by cognitive psychologist Gary Klein as an alternative to conventional risk reviews. Instead of asking, “What might go wrong?”, participants are told to imagine that the project has already failed spectacularly. They then work backwards to explain the failure as though it were a historical fact. This framing reduces the social pressure to defend the existing plan and gives people permission to voice concerns that might otherwise remain unspoken. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org Harvard Business Review Performing a Project Premortem Harvard Business ReviewPerforming a Project PremortemSeptember 1, 2007 — Reprint: F0709A In a premortem, team members assume that the pro... Published: September 1, 2007 This seemingly small change matters because ordinary planning meetings often reward confidence. Team members may hesitate to challenge a proposal that appears to have broad support, particularly when senior decision makers are present. By assuming failure has already happened, the discussion shifts from criticism of colleagues to diagnosis of an imagined event. That makes dissent feel less personal and more constructive. [McKinsey & Company] mckinsey.com bias busters premortems being smart at the start McKinsey & CompanyBias Busters: Premortems: Being smart at the start3 Apr 2019 — Despite their best intentions, executives fall prey to c... For someone trying to improve their thinking, the key insight is that a premortem is not an exercise in predicting the future. It is an exercise in expanding the range of possibilities considered before committing to a course of action. Turning warning signs into change my mind triggers A premortem becomes especially valuable when its outputs are translated into explicit reconsideration criteria. Many teams stop after generating a list of possible failure causes. A stronger approach is to ask a second question for every plausible failure: If this cause were beginning to happen in real life, what would we actually observe? Those observations become practical \"change my mind\" signals. For example, imagine a software team concludes during a premortem that its launch failed because customers found onboarding confusing. Instead of merely recording \"poor onboarding\" as a risk, the team could define measurable triggers such as: activation rates falling below a predetermined threshold support requests about account setup doubling over two consecutive weeks usability testing repeatedly exposing the same navigation problem abandonment rates rising after a specific onboarding screen None of these observations proves the strategy has failed. However, each is a pre agreed signal that the original assumptions deserve re examination. The same principle applies outside project management: Career decisions: \"If I still have no meaningful opportunities after six months despite following the plan, I will reconsider the strategy.\" Investment decisions: \"If earnings consistently diverge from the assumptions that justified the investment, I will review whether the original thesis still holds.\" Health goals: \"If objective progress stalls despite following the programme as planned, I will reassess rather than simply trying harder.\" The important feature is that the trigger is specified before emotions, sunk costs and commitment begin to distort judgement. How to Use a Premortem to Stress Test Your Strategy in Stage 4 Design Thinking (APPLY) Channel: Create & Innovate with Andrew & Gaia Grant Open on YouTube Why prospective hindsight works Premortems rely on a psychological effect known as prospective hindsight. Research by Deborah Mitchell, Jay Russo and Nancy Pennington found that imagining a future event as already having occurred helped participants generate substantially more explanations for why that outcome happened than conventional forecasting approaches. Gary Klein later drew on this work when developing the premortem technique for organisational decision making. [homepages.se.edu] homepages.se.edu Performing a Project Premortem by Gary Klein July 18, 2011 — By making it safe for dissenters who are knowledgeable about the undertaking and worried about its weaknesses to speak up... Published: July 18, 2011 Several mechanisms appear to contribute: Reduced overconfidence . Imagining failure makes optimistic assumptions easier to question. More complete causal thinking. People search for interacting causes rather than isolated problems. Greater psychological safety. Team members gain permission to express reservations without appearing obstructive. Attention to leading indicators. Thinking backwards naturally encourages discussion of the early events that would precede major failure. These mechanisms align closely with the purpose of \"What would change my mind?\" questions. Both practices encourage people to define conditions under which confidence should decrease instead of merely collecting reasons to remain confident. [McKinsey & Company] mckinsey.com bias busters premortems being smart at the start McKinsey & CompanyBias Busters: Premortems: Being smart at the start3 Apr 2019 — Despite their best intentions, executives fall prey to c... Designing effective reconsideration triggers Not every imagined failure should become a change my mind signal. Useful triggers share several characteristics. They are observable. A trigger should depend on something that can actually be noticed or measured, not on a vague feeling that \"things seem wrong\". They appear early. Waiting until complete failure occurs defeats the purpose. Good triggers identify developments while meaningful alternatives still exist. They relate to critical assumptions. The strongest triggers monitor the beliefs that justified the decision in the first place. If those assumptions weaken, the decision itself deserves review. They specify a response. A trigger should not automatically require abandoning the project. Instead, it should initiate a predefined review: gather more evidence, reduce confidence, pause implementation or reconsider the decision. For example: Weak triggerStrong trigger\"If morale seems lower.\"\"If employee turnover exceeds our expected range for two consecutive quarters.\"\"If customers complain.\"\"If customer retention drops below the threshold assumed in our business case.\"\"If the market changes.\"\"If a competing technology achieves widespread adoption before our launch.\" The stronger version makes belief revision operational rather than aspirational. How to prevent product failure — Anu Jagga Narang (AT&T) Channel: Mind the Product Open on YouTube Using premortems without creating pessimism A common misconception is that premortems encourage excessive negativity. In practice, the opposite is often true. Their purpose is not to convince people that failure is inevitable, but to increase confidence that important risks have been examined honestly before resources are committed. [Forbes] forbes.com how to improve your strategic planning through a premortem analysis How To"},{"title":"Revision Rules","url":"/revision-rules/","description":"Clear revision conditions make it harder to ignore evidence that challenges your current view.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Change Mind","level":3,"text":"Revision Rules What Would Actually Make You Change Your Mind? Revision Rules Revision Rules What Would Actually Make You Change Your Mind? Clear revision conditions make it harder to ignore evidence that challenges your current view. Clear revision conditions make it harder to ignore evidence that challenges your current view. mechanism Turn beliefs into testable claims Write specific revision conditions Avoid vague escape clauses improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind revision rules Introduction The question “What would actually make me change my mind ?” only improves decisions if the answer is specific enough to force a genuine revision. A vague promise to “follow the evidence” leaves plenty of room to reinterpret disappointing results, postpone action, or invent exceptions after the fact. By contrast, writing explicit revision conditions before new evidence arrives makes beliefs testable, reduces confirmation bias, and creates a clear standard for when to update your judgement rather than defend it. Research on confirmation bias and actively open minded thinking consistently shows that people are better at finding evidence for what they already believe than at recognising when they should revise those beliefs. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement by KE Stanovich · 2023 · Cited by 133 — Actively open minded thinking (AOT) is measured by items that tap the willingness to consider... The goal is not to make yourself change your mind easily. It is to decide in advance what evidence would be strong enough to justify doing so. Turn beliefs into claims that can succeed or fail A revision condition only works if it is attached to a concrete claim. Broad opinions such as \"this project is a good idea\" or \"this investment will work out\" cannot be meaningfully tested because they do not predict anything specific. Instead, identify the assumption that matters most and express it as an observable claim. For example: \"This marketing campaign will increase qualified leads by at least 20% within three months.\" \"The new onboarding process will reduce customer drop off without increasing support requests.\" \"Hiring another engineer will shorten release cycles by at least one week.\" Each statement creates the possibility of being wrong. That is essential. A claim that cannot fail cannot guide better decisions. This reflects the broader idea that useful reasoning depends on exposing beliefs to potential contradiction rather than continually finding confirming examples. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabConfirmation BiasConfirmation bias describes our underlying tendency to notice, focus on, and provide greater credence to... The Tool That Stops You From Holding Losers Too Long Annie Duke Channel: William Green Open on YouTube What makes a revision condition strong? A useful revision condition answers a simple question: \"If this happened, would I genuinely change my decision?\" Strong revision conditions usually contain four elements: 1. A measurable indicator. Define exactly what will be observed, such as revenue, error rates, customer retention, completion times or test results . 2. A threshold. Specify how much evidence is required. \"Sales fall below forecast by 15%\" is stronger than \"sales disappoint.\" 3. A timeframe. Decide when the evidence will be assessed. Without a deadline, disappointing evidence can always be dismissed as temporary. 4. A predetermined response. State what revision means. You might reduce confidence, run another experiment, delay a launch, abandon a proposal or adopt an alternative explanation. Notice that the revision condition is separate from the evidence itself. The evidence arrives later; the standard for interpreting it is set beforehand. Cognitive Bias: The Invisible Decision Maker Channel: Medical Centric Podcast Open on YouTube Write specific revision conditions Weak revision rules usually sound reasonable but provide unlimited room for escape. Weak conditionStrong condition\"If things go badly.\"\"If customer retention drops below 85% after eight weeks.\"\"If experts disagree.\"\"If two independent systematic reviews conclude the treatment has no meaningful effect.\"\"If users dislike it.\"\"If fewer than 70% of test users complete the task unaided.\"\"If costs become too high.\"\"If total implementation costs exceed the approved budget by more than 10%.\" The stronger version removes ambiguity. When the evidence arrives, the question is no longer \"How do I feel about these results?\" but \"Did the agreed condition occur?\" This approach also reduces the temptation to reinterpret evidence after becoming emotionally invested in a decision. Avoid vague escape clauses Many revision conditions fail because they quietly include loopholes that allow the original belief to survive almost any outcome. Common escape clauses include: \"Unless the situation is unusually complicated.\" \"The evidence wasn't representative.\" \"We just need more time.\" \"The timing was unfortunate.\" \"The data collection was imperfect.\" Sometimes these objections are valid. The problem is introducing them only after unfavourable evidence appears. A better approach is to specify acceptable exceptions beforehand. For example: Define which data sources will count. Agree which external events would invalidate the test. Decide in advance how much uncertainty is acceptable. State how many independent observations are required before changing course. This prevents standards from shifting every time evidence becomes uncomfortable. Separate confidence from commitment Changing your mind is not an all or nothing event. Many decisions improve when revision conditions change confidence rather than immediately reversing a conclusion. For example: One failed experiment may reduce confidence from 80% to 60%. Repeated failures across independent tests may justify abandoning the idea altogether. Strong supporting evidence may increase confidence without eliminating uncertainty. Thinking in terms of confidence encourages gradual belief updating instead of treating every decision as either complete victory or total defeat. Research on belief revision shows that people naturally tend to update too conservatively, giving insufficient weight to genuinely informative new evidence. Explicit revision rules help counter that tendency. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Conservatism (belief revision Conservatism (belief revision Use implementation rather than intention Simply intending to be open minded is often ineffective because confirmation bias influences how evidence is searched for, interpreted and remembered. People frequently believe they are evaluating information fairly while unconsciously favouring evidence consistent with existing views. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabConfirmation BiasConfirmation bias describes our underlying tendency to notice, focus on, and provide greater credence to... Writing revision conditions transforms an abstract value into a behavioural commitment. Instead of saying: \"I'll reconsider if convincing evidence appears.\" Write: \"If the next two quarterly reviews both miss the target by more than 10%, despite normal market conditions, I will pause further investment until an alternative explanation has been evaluated.\" The second statement creates accountability because it specifies both the trigger and the response. Confirmation Bias Definition & Examples (3 Minute Explanation) Channel: Helpful Professor Explains! Open on YouTube A practical template For important decisions, a simple template is often sufficient: Current belief: What do I currently think is true? Critical assumption: What has to be true for this belief to hold? Evidence that would reduce my confidence: What specific observation would count against it? Revision threshold: How much contrary evidence is enough? Decision change: What action will I take if that threshold is reached? Completing these questions before the outcome is known makes it substantially harder to ignore evidence simply because it is inconvenient. Why this improves thinking Well written revision conditions change the structure of decision making. Instead of debating every new piece of information from scratch, they establish a fair standard before personal preferences, sunk costs and identity become entangled with the outcome. The result is not perfect objectivity. It is a practical safeguard against moving the goalposts after evidence arrives. By turning beliefs into testable claims, defining explicit revision thresholds and eliminating vague escape clauses, you make changing your mind a disciplined response to evidence rather than an emotional concession. Research on actively open minded thinking suggests that this willingness to seek, consider and act on contradictory evidence is a measurable thinking disposition associated with better judgement across many domains. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and"},{"title":"Wason 2 4 6","url":"/wason-2-4-6/","description":"A classic psychology experiment shows why people often test ideas by confirming rather than challenging them.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Change Mind","level":3,"text":"Wason 2 4 6 What the 2 4 6 Experiment Teaches About Better Decisions Wason 2 4 6 Wason 2 4 6 What the 2 4 6 Experiment Teaches About Better Decisions A classic psychology experiment shows why people often test ideas by confirming rather than challenging them. A classic psychology experiment shows why people often test ideas by confirming rather than challenging them. dataset evidence How the experiment worked Why confirming tests mislead Applying the lesson to real choices improving your thinking and analytical skills change mind wason 2 4 6 Introduction Peter Wason's famous 2 4 6 experiment remains one of the clearest demonstrations of why people often fail to test their own beliefs effectively. Rather than deliberately looking for evidence that could prove their initial idea wrong, most participants searched for examples that confirmed it. The lesson reaches far beyond laboratory psychology. In everyday decisions—whether choosing investments, evaluating a medical concern, hiring someone, or deciding whether a relationship is working—the same tendency can leave people increasingly confident in incorrect conclusions because they ask the wrong questions. Wason's experiment shows that better thinking depends not only on gathering more evidence, but on actively seeking evidence that could change your mind. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu wason qjep1960 On the failure to eliminate hypotheses in a conceptual taskby PC Wason · Cited by 3767 — This investigation examines the extent to which... How the experiment worked In the original 1960 study, participants were shown the number sequence 2, 4, 6 and told that it followed a rule known only to the experimenter. Their task was to discover that rule by proposing additional triples of numbers. After each proposal, the experimenter simply replied whether it conformed to the rule or not. Participants could test as many examples as they wished before stating their answer. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu wason qjep1960 On the failure to eliminate hypotheses in a conceptual taskby PC Wason · Cited by 3767 — This investigation examines the extent to which... Many participants quickly formed a specific hypothesis such as: even numbers numbers increasing by two regularly spaced ascending sequences They then generated more examples such as 8, 10, 12 or 20, 22, 24 . Because these examples were accepted, participants became increasingly convinced their hypothesis was correct. The actual rule, however, was far broader: Any three numbers in ascending order. Sequences such as 3, 5, 100 , 1, 2, 9 , or 14, 15, 16 also satisfied the rule. A sequence like 5, 4, 3 or 7, 7, 8 would have been rejected. The striking feature was not that participants guessed incorrectly. It was that relatively few deliberately tested examples that might have disproved their own theory. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu wason qjep1960 On the failure to eliminate hypotheses in a conceptual taskby PC Wason · Cited by 3767 — This investigation examines the extent to which... Why confirming tests mislead The experiment illustrates an important distinction between confirming a favourite explanation and challenging it. Suppose someone believes the rule is \"even numbers increasing by two.\" Testing 12, 14, 16 feels informative because it succeeds. Yet success reveals very little. That sequence would fit both the narrow hypothesis (\"increase by two\") and the broader true rule (\"ascending numbers\"). It cannot distinguish between the competing explanations. A much stronger test would be something like: 3, 5, 7 1, 4, 9 2, 3, 100 If any of these were accepted, the original hypothesis would immediately need revision. The key insight is that evidence supporting multiple explanations is usually weak evidence . Progress comes from finding observations that separate one explanation from another. Wason described this contrast as the difference between seeking only confirming cases and attempting to eliminate competing hypotheses. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu wason qjep1960 On the failure to eliminate hypotheses in a conceptual taskby PC Wason · Cited by 3767 — This investigation examines the extent to which... Later researchers have argued that the phenomenon is more nuanced than a simple desire to confirm beliefs. Some suggest people naturally generate \"positive tests\" because they appear informative or are easier to imagine. Even so, the practical recommendation remains largely unchanged: deliberately search for cases your preferred explanation predicts should fail. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Generation of Hypotheses in Wason's 2–4–6 TaskThere are currently two competing interpretations of hypothesis testing in Waso... Unit 5.4: The Wason 2 4 6 Task Channel: Fred Cummins Open on YouTube Applying the lesson to real choices The value of the 2 4 6 experiment lies in recognising the same pattern in ordinary decisions. Career decisions Imagine believing: \"This new job is clearly the right move.\" A confirming approach asks: What makes this company attractive? Why am I excited? A Wason style test asks: [researchgate.net] researchgate.net 324150467 Confirmation Bias and the 2 4 6 Game How do we Test our Perspectives Confirmation Bias and the 2 4 6 Game: How do we Test...This class activity builds on Peter Cathcart Wason's (1960) 2 4 6 Hypothesis Rule... What evidence would show this role is a poor fit? What information would make me decline the offer? Which current employees might tell a different story? Those questions are more likely to uncover hidden risks. Personal relationships Someone who believes a relationship is healthy may notice affectionate moments while overlooking recurring patterns of avoidance or unresolved conflict. A better question becomes: What behaviour would convince me that my current view is mistaken? Rather than collecting pleasant examples, the focus shifts to identifying evidence that genuinely discriminates between alternative interpretations. Financial decisions An investor convinced that a company is undervalued can easily accumulate confirming news articles and optimistic analyst reports. A stronger approach is to ask: Which financial results would invalidate my investment thesis? What would competitors need to achieve to prove me wrong? Which assumptions about future growth are most vulnerable? This changes investing from defending a position to testing one. The Reasoning Test Psychologists Still Can't Explain Channel: The Rest Is Science Open on YouTube Everyday planning Even routine choices benefit from disconfirming tests. Instead of asking: \"Does my plan seem reasonable?\" ask: What would cause this plan to fail? What assumptions have I never checked? What result would make me change course immediately? These questions are practical versions of \"What would change my mind ?\" What later research adds Wason's experiment became one of the most influential studies in the psychology of reasoning and helped launch decades of research into hypothesis testing and confirmation bias. Researchers have refined, criticised and extended its interpretation rather than simply accepting the original explanation. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineReasoning, biases and dual processes: The lasting impact...by JSBT Evans · 2016 · Cited by 82 — Wason (1960) publ... Several findings are especially relevant: People often use a positive test strategy , examining cases where they expect the property to occur, even when this is not the best way to distinguish between competing hypotheses. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Generation of Hypotheses in Wason's 2–4–6 TaskThere are currently two competing interpretations of hypothesis testing in Waso... Performance improves when people are encouraged to think in contrasts or opposites rather than searching for supporting examples alone. Recent experiments have shown that prompting participants to generate contrasting possibilities substantially increases the proportion who discover the correct rule. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by E Branchini · 2023 · Cited by 3 — With reference to Wason's 2 4 6 rule discovery task, this study investigated the effects of a sim... The experiment remains an active research tool because it captures an important feature of scientific reasoning: useful hypotheses expose themselves to potential failure instead of merely accumulating confirming observations. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineReasoning, biases and dual processes: The lasting impact...by JSBT Evans · 2016 · Cited by 82 — Wason (1960) publ... These developments do not weaken Wason's central lesson. They clarify why the error occurs and suggest practical ways to reduce it. A simple habit for better decisions The 2 4 6 experiment can be translated into a straightforward thinking routine whenever an important judgement is at stake. How Logical Are You? (Psychology of Reasoning)"},{"title":"Claims","url":"/claims/","description":"Good analysis depends on separating what is being claimed from the evidence and assumptions that supposedly support it.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Claims What Is the Claim Really Based On? Claims Claims What Is the Claim Really Based On? Good analysis depends on separating what is being claimed from the evidence and assumptions that supposedly support it. Good analysis depends on separating what is being claimed from the evidence and assumptions that supposedly support it. mechanism Spot the conclusion Separate evidence from opinion Find hidden assumptions improving your thinking and analytical skills claims Introduction Everyday arguments become clearer when you separate three things: the claim someone wants you to accept, the evidence offered for it, and the assumptions that connect the evidence to the claim. A claim says “this is true”, “this is likely”, or “we should do this”. Evidence is the material meant to support it: data, examples, testimony, documents, observations, or expert findings. Assumptions are the often unstated links: “this example is typical”, “this source is reliable”, “this cause really produced that effect”, or “this value matters more than that one”. This matters because weak arguments often look strong when these parts are blended together. A confident opinion may borrow the language of evidence. A striking example may be treated as proof. A true fact may be used to support a conclusion it does not actually justify. Learning to ask “What exactly is being claimed, what supports it, and what has to be assumed?” is one of the most practical analytical habits a person can build. It helps in work decisions, health claims , news stories, online debates, and personal disagreements. Spot the Conclusion Before Judging the Argument The first move is to find the conclusion. In argument analysis, the conclusion is the point being argued for, while premises are the reasons or evidence offered in support of it. This sounds simple, but ordinary conversation rarely arrives in neat “premise, premise, conclusion” form. People tell stories, vent, imply, exaggerate, change scope, or present a recommendation before giving reasons. Open critical thinking guides use the same basic distinction: an argument is a set of statements in which reasons or premises are meant to support a conclusion. [Open Oklahoma State Library] open.library.okstate.edu Arguments. Arguments are a set of statements (premises and conclusion). The premises provide evidence, reasons, and grounds for the concl... A conclusion is not always the loudest sentence. In “The team missed the deadline again, the brief changed twice, and we need a better project manager”, the conclusion is probably “we need a better project manager”. The missed deadline and changed brief are reasons, but they do not automatically prove that the project manager is the cause. The argument also assumes that management, rather than staffing, planning, client behaviour, or workload, is the decisive factor. A useful test is to ask: What does the speaker want me to believe, decide, or do? That reveals the claim. Then ask: What are they giving me as support? That reveals the evidence. Finally ask: What has to be true for that support to justify the claim? That reveals the assumptions. Consider four everyday versions of the same structure: SituationClaimEvidence offeredHidden assumptionWork“This software is slowing the team down.”“Three people complained this week.”Those complaints are representative, and the software is the main cause of delay.News“This policy is failing.”“A bad outcome happened after it was introduced.”The timing shows causation , not coincidence or another cause.Health“This supplement works.”“My friend felt better after taking it.”The improvement was caused by the supplement rather than recovery, placebo effect, lifestyle changes, or chance.Relationships“They do not respect my time.”“They replied late twice.”Late replies reliably indicate disrespect rather than busyness, stress, or different communication habits. The point is not to dismiss the claim. Sometimes the claim is right. The point is to make the route from claim to support visible before accepting it. Separate Evidence From Opinion Evidence and opinion are not enemies. Good judgement often needs both. Evidence helps answer “What is true or likely?” Opinion, values, and priorities help answer “What matters?” The problem comes when a value judgement is presented as if it were a factual finding, or when a factual claim is supported only by personal confidence. A statement such as “The new policy is unfair” may contain two layers. One layer is factual: who is affected, what changed, what outcomes followed, and whether comparable people are treated differently. The other layer is evaluative: what counts as fair. If the conversation treats “unfair” as self evident, people may argue past each other. One person is disputing the facts; another is disputing the values. Evidence also varies in strength. A single anecdote can be useful as a clue, especially when it points to a problem worth investigating, but it is usually weak evidence for a broad claim. A pattern across many cases is stronger. A well designed study is stronger still for certain kinds of causal claims. In health research, for example, evidence based medicine uses explicit ways to grade evidence, while also warning that flawed evidence should be acknowledged rather than hidden. The Oxford Centre for Evidence Based Medicine says evidence levels are intended to make the process of finding appropriate evidence “feasible” and its results explicit, not to replace judgement. [cebm.ox.ac.uk] cebm.ox.ac.uk oxford centre for evidence based medicine levels of evidence march 2009 oxford centre for evidence based medicine levels of evidence march 2009 Published: march 2009 The same distinction matters outside medicine. If someone says, “Remote work makes people less productive”, different kinds of evidence would support different versions of the claim. Productivity data across teams would support one version. A manager’s impression would support another, weaker version. A story about one employee would support only a narrow claim unless there is reason to think the case is typical. A practical way to test evidence is to classify it: Anecdote: “This happened to me” or “I know someone who…” Observation: “I saw this pattern repeatedly.” Documented example: “Here is a record, message, policy, or timestamp.” Data pattern: “Here are numbers across many cases.” Expert interpretation: “Someone with relevant expertise has assessed the evidence.” Causal study or structured comparison: “The design helps test whether one thing produced another.” None of these is automatically useless or decisive. The quality depends on the claim. A screenshot may be excellent evidence that a message was sent, but poor evidence that a person had bad intentions. A randomised controlled trial may be powerful evidence for a treatment effect, but not the right tool for every ethical, historical, or personal question. Find the Assumption Doing the Work An assumption is what an argument takes for granted. In Stephen Toulmin’s influential model of argument, the basic parts include a claim, grounds or data, and a warrant. The warrant is the connecting principle that explains why the evidence supports the claim. Educational summaries of Toulmin’s model commonly define the warrant as the assumption on which the move from evidence to claim depends. [Blinn College] blinn.edu Open source on blinn.edu. This is why assumptions are so important: they are often where the real argument lives. The visible evidence may be true, but the assumption may be weak. Take the claim: “We should cancel the meeting because only four people accepted the invitation.” The evidence may be accurate. But the argument assumes that attendance numbers determine meeting value, that the people who declined are essential, and that the topic can wait. If the four attendees are the only decision makers needed, cancellation may be a mistake. Hidden assumptions often fall into a few everyday types: Typicality assumptions. One example is treated as representative. “One customer hated the new design, so users will reject it.” Causation assumptions. One event is treated as the cause of another. “Sales fell after the price change, so the price change caused the fall.” Source assumptions. A person, outlet, or organisation is treated as reliable enough for the claim. “A senior colleague said it, so it must be right.” Value assumptions. A conclusion depends on what should matter most. “The cheapest option is best” assumes cost outranks durability, safety, convenience, or fairness. Definition assumptions. The argument depends on a contested word. “This is success” may mean profit, learning, speed, wellbeing, reputation, or something else. Risk assumptions. The argument assumes a level of uncertainty is acceptable. “It will probably be fine” may be reasonable for a low stakes choice and careless for a high stakes one. The most revealing question is often: Could someone accept the evidence but"},{"title":"Anecdotes","url":"/anecdotes/","description":"A single vivid story can be a useful clue, but it rarely proves a broad claim unless it is typical and well connected.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Claims","level":3,"text":"Anecdotes When Does One Example Actually Prove Anything? Anecdotes Anecdotes When Does One Example Actually Prove Anything? A single vivid story can be a useful clue, but it rarely proves a broad claim unless it is typical and well connected. A single vivid story can be a useful clue, but it rarely proves a broad claim unless it is typical and well connected. dataset evidence What an anecdote can and cannot show How typicality assumptions sneak in Questions that turn a story into better evidence improving your thinking and analytical skills claims anecdotes Introduction Anecdotes are personal stories, individual experiences, or isolated examples used as evidence. They can be valuable because they make abstract issues concrete, reveal possibilities that statistics may miss, and sometimes point to problems worth investigating. However, a single story rarely proves a broad claim such as “this product works”, “young people no longer do X”, or “working from home reduces productivity”. The central question is not whether the anecdote is true. It is whether the anecdote is typical enough to justify a wider conclusion. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence Improving analytical thinking requires separating the truth of a story from the strength of the inference drawn from it. One person’s experience may be completely genuine and still be poor evidence for a claim about thousands or millions of people. The challenge is learning when an anecdote is a useful clue and when it is being asked to carry more weight than it can reasonably support. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence What an Anecdote Can and Cannot Show An anecdote can establish that something happened. If a colleague describes a software failure, that story is evidence that at least one user experienced a problem. If a friend reports an unexpected side effect from a treatment, that account is evidence that the experience occurred. Anecdotes can also generate hypotheses by drawing attention to patterns that deserve further investigation. In medicine, individual case reports have sometimes helped identify genuine drug side effects before larger studies were conducted. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence What an anecdote usually cannot show on its own is frequency, typicality, or causation . Consider three common claims : “This diet works because my neighbour lost weight.” “Customer service is terrible because one employee was rude.” “ Remote work harms teamwork because our department struggled.” Each story may be accurate. Yet the broader claim requires additional information. How many people had similar experiences? How many had different experiences? Were there other factors involved? Without answers to those questions, the leap from one case to a general conclusion remains weak. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence Research on persuasion shows that anecdotal evidence often has a strong psychological impact even when broader statistical information is available. People are frequently influenced by vivid personal stories because narratives are easier to understand and remember than abstract numbers. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com The analysis...Read more... A useful rule is: an anecdote can show possibility, but broad claims usually require evidence of prevalence and representativeness. What is Anecdotal Evidence? (Easiest Explanation) Channel: Helpful Professor Explains! · Views: 1.8K · Uploaded: March 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 59 seconds Open on YouTube How Typicality Assumptions Sneak In The hidden assumption behind many anecdotal arguments is that the example is representative of a larger group. Imagine someone says: “I know a university graduate who cannot find a job, so degrees are not worth it.” The conclusion depends not on the existence of that graduate but on an unstated assumption that the graduate's experience reflects the typical outcome. If the example is unusual, the argument weakens considerably. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence This problem appears in everyday discussions because people naturally generalise from limited experience. Psychologists have documented related cognitive shortcuts, including the availability heuristic: the tendency to judge how common or important something is based on how easily examples come to mind. Vivid, recent, emotional, or memorable cases often feel more representative than they really are. [The Decision Lab+2Simply Psychology] thedecisionlab.com The Decision Lab Availability Heuristic The Decision LabAvailability Heuristic The Decision...The availability heuristic describes our tendency to think that whatever is easi... For example: After hearing a dramatic story about a burglary, people may overestimate how common burglaries are. After meeting one exceptionally difficult customer from a particular profession, someone may form a stereotype about the entire profession. After reading a viral story about a product failure, consumers may assume the problem is widespread. In each case, the memorable example becomes a substitute for broader evidence. The ease with which the example is recalled creates an impression of frequency or importance. [The Decision Lab+2globalfactchecking.com] thedecisionlab.com The Decision Lab Availability Heuristic The Decision LabAvailability Heuristic The Decision...The availability heuristic describes our tendency to think that whatever is easi... The key analytical question is therefore not “Can I think of an example?” but “How typical is this example compared with the wider population?” Why Vivid Stories Feel More Convincing Than Data Human beings are natural storytellers. A single detailed narrative often creates a stronger emotional response than a table of percentages. A story has characters, causes, consequences, and concrete details. Statistics often appear abstract and impersonal. As a result, people may remember one compelling account long after forgetting a larger dataset. Researchers studying anecdotal and statistical evidence have repeatedly found that narratives can exert a substantial influence on beliefs and decisions. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com The analysis...Read more... This does not mean stories are useless. In many situations, stories provide context that numbers alone cannot supply. A hospital report may show a percentage of patients affected by a problem, while individual patient accounts reveal what the experience actually felt like. The mistake occurs when vividness is confused with evidential strength. A dramatic example may be emotionally powerful precisely because it is unusual. Exceptional events are often more memorable than ordinary ones, which means the stories that spread most widely are not necessarily the stories that best represent reality. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence When evaluating a claim, it helps to ask whether the example is memorable because it is common or memorable because it is rare. Questions That Turn a Story into Better Evidence Instead of dismissing anecdotes outright, analytical thinking asks better questions. What is Anecdotal Evidence? (Easy Explanation) Channel: Social Science Explainer · Views: 658 · Uploaded: February 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 34 seconds Open on YouTube How many similar cases exist? One story can be a starting point. Multiple independent stories pointing in the same direction are more informative. Even then, it matters how those cases were gathered. Ten complaints collected from a self selected online group may not represent the broader population. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence Is there a known comparison group? Suppose someone says, “Everyone I know who uses this method succeeds.” A useful response is: compared with whom? Without a comparison group, it is difficult to judge whether the outcome is unusual or expected. Could the example have been cherry picked? People naturally select stories that support their position. A persuasive speaker may highlight one striking success or failure while ignoring dozens of less dramatic cases. The existence of a compelling example does not tell us how many contrary examples were left out. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anecdotal evidence Anecdotal evidence Is there a plausible alternative explanation? If someone improved after taking a supplement, was the supplement responsible? Could natural recovery, lifestyle changes, coincidence, or expectation effects explain the outcome? Personal experiences often contain many interacting factors, making causal conclusions difficult from a single case. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Anecdotes impact medical decisions even when presented...by EN Line · 2024 · Cited by 9 — We found that reading anecdotes for either... Does broader evidence support the story? The strongest position is often a combination of anecdotal and systematic evidence. A personal story becomes more"},{"title":"Late Replies","url":"/late-replies-d270d9/","description":"Late messages may feel personal, but the argument from delay to disrespect depends on assumptions about intent, norms, and context.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Claims","level":3,"text":"Late Replies Do Late Replies Really Show Disrespect? Late Replies Late Replies Do Late Replies Really Show Disrespect? Late messages may feel personal, but the argument from delay to disrespect depends on assumptions about intent, norms, and context. Late messages may feel personal, but the argument from delay to disrespect depends on assumptions about intent, norms, and context. population context The claim hidden inside the complaint Alternative explanations for the same evidence How to test intent without overreading improving your thinking and analytical skills claims late replies Introduction A late reply can feel like evidence of something important. Many people move quickly from “they took eight hours to answer” to “they do not care”, “they are ignoring me”, or “they do not respect me”. Yet the observation and the conclusion are not the same thing. The gap between them is filled by assumptions about intent, priorities, social norms, and the relationship itself. For anyone trying to improve their thinking and analytical skills, late replies provide a useful everyday example of how people often convert ambiguous evidence into confident claims . The delay is real. The meaning of the delay is usually much less certain. Research on attribution, communication expectations, and digital messaging suggests that people frequently overestimate what response times reveal about another person's character or feelings while underestimating situational explanations. [Psychology Today+2Psych Safety] psychologytoday.com attribution theory and why your relationships hinge on it Psychology TodayAttribution Theory—And Why Your Relationships Hinge on ItMar 17, 2024 — The fundamental attribution error (FAE) describes... The Claim Hidden Inside the Complaint When someone says, “You always reply late,” they may appear to be describing a fact. In many cases, however, the complaint contains a second, hidden claim: Observation: You replied late. Hidden conclusion: You do not value me, respect me, or care enough. The reasoning often remains unstated because the emotional conclusion feels obvious to the person making it. Yet analytically, several assumptions must be true before the conclusion follows: The person saw the message promptly. They were free to respond. They understood a quick reply was expected. They chose not to respond. That choice reflected their feelings toward the sender. Each step adds uncertainty. A delay may support the conclusion, but it does not prove it. This pattern reflects a well studied cognitive tendency known as the fundamental attribution error : people often explain others' behaviour in terms of personality, character, or intentions while overlooking situational factors. In relationships, this can transform a delayed message into evidence of disrespect when the actual cause may be workload, distraction, stress , competing obligations, or different communication habits. textbooks.whatcom.edu+3Psychology Today+3Psych Safety [psychologytoday.com] psychologytoday.com attribution theory and why your relationships hinge on it Psychology TodayAttribution Theory—And Why Your Relationships Hinge on ItMar 17, 2024 — The fundamental attribution error (FAE) describes... A useful comparison highlights the asymmetry. If you reply late, you may think, “I was busy.” If someone else replies late, you may think, “They do not care.” The same behaviour receives different explanations depending on who performed it. Why Delays Feel More Meaningful Than They Often Are Text messaging creates conditions that encourage overinterpretation. Unlike face to face conversation, messaging removes tone of voice, facial expressions, and immediate context. The recipient often sees only one piece of evidence: elapsed time. Humans naturally try to fill in missing information, and negative interpretations can emerge surprisingly quickly. Research on hostile attribution bias shows that people sometimes infer hostile intent from ambiguous behaviour even when the evidence is unclear. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Open source on tandfonline.com. Modern communication also creates expectations that did not exist in earlier forms of contact. Because messages arrive instantly, many people assume responses should be nearly instant as well. Yet studies of romantic and developing relationships show that expectations about messaging speed vary considerably between individuals and couples. What one person sees as normal may seem dismissive to another. [thejsms.org+2Psychology Today] thejsms.org What to expect when you are textingby C Guest · 2024 — This study qualitatively investigates expectations of direct mobile messaging (via... This mismatch creates a common analytical mistake: treating a personal expectation as a universal rule. For example: Person A believes replies within an hour are normal. Person B sees texting as asynchronous and replies when convenient. Person A interprets delays as disrespect. Person B experiences no disrespectful intention at all. The evidence is identical. The interpretations differ because the underlying assumptions differ. Attribution Theory—And Why Your Relationships Hinge on It Channel: Psychology with Dr. Jessica Open on YouTube Alternative Explanations for the Same Evidence A delayed reply can be consistent with many explanations at once. Good reasoning requires considering more than the first explanation that comes to mind. Imagine a message answered six hours later. Possible explanations include: The recipient was working or studying. They read the message and intended to reply later. They were mentally exhausted. They wanted to give a thoughtful response. They were handling family obligations. They have different texting habits. They were intentionally avoiding the conversation. They were losing interest in the relationship. Notice that only some of these explanations involve disrespect. This does not mean disrespect is impossible. It means the evidence alone rarely identifies which explanation is correct. Research on messaging behaviour reinforces this point. Studies have found that communication frequency and responsiveness can relate to relationship satisfaction, but the meaning of messaging behaviour depends heavily on context, expectations, and the nature of the relationship rather than on a simple rule that “slow replies equal low interest”. [Verywell Mind+3PMC+3PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCLong distance texting: Text messaging is linked with higher Some studies have found that more frequent texting is linked to greater relationship satisfaction (Luo & Tuney, 2015), ratings of part... The analytical question is therefore not, “Could this delay mean disrespect?” but rather, “How many plausible explanations fit the available evidence?” The more plausible alternatives exist, the weaker the case for any single interpretation. When the Pattern Matters More Than the Delay One late reply tells very little. Patterns are often more informative than isolated incidents. Suppose someone: Frequently ignores direct questions. Replies quickly to others but rarely to you. Makes promises to respond and repeatedly fails to do so. Shows similar disregard across multiple forms of interaction. At that point, the argument becomes stronger because it relies on a broader set of observations rather than a single ambiguous event. Even then, the strongest claim may still be narrower than people initially assume. Compare these conclusions: 1. “They replied late.” 2. “They are inconsistent communicators.” 3. “They prioritise communication with me less than I would like.” 4. “They do not respect me.” The first three require progressively more evidence but remain relatively close to observable behaviour. The fourth jumps from behaviour to character and intention. Analytically, the larger the jump, the more assumptions are required. What is the Fundamental Attribution Error? (Easy Explanation) Channel: Helpful Professor Explains! Open on YouTube How to Test Intent Without Overreading The best way to reduce uncertainty is to gather better evidence rather than constructing elaborate stories from limited evidence. Ask About Expectations Many conflicts arise because expectations were never discussed. Research on messaging expectations in romantic relationships suggests that people develop informal rules about speed, frequency, and transparency, but those rules are often assumed rather than explicitly negotiated. [thejsms.org] thejsms.org What to expect when you are textingby C Guest · 2024 — This study qualitatively investigates expectations of direct mobile messaging (via... A question such as “What does normal communication look like for you during a busy day?” often produces more useful information than analysing response times. Compare Words and Behaviour Over Time Single incidents are noisy. Patterns are more reliable. Instead of focusing on one delayed message, look at broader behaviour: Do they initiate conversations? Do they follow"},{"title":"Policy Timing","url":"/policy-timing/","description":"A bad outcome after a policy change is not enough to show the policy failed unless competing causes are ruled out.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Claims","level":3,"text":"Policy Timing Did the Policy Fail, or Just Come First? Policy Timing Policy Timing Did the Policy Fail, or Just Come First? A bad outcome after a policy change is not enough to show the policy failed unless competing causes are ruled out. A bad outcome after a policy change is not enough to show the policy failed unless competing causes are ruled out. policy intervention Why timing feels persuasive Coincidence, background trends, and rival causes Evidence that strengthens a policy failure claim improving your thinking and analytical skills claims policy timing Introduction When a policy is introduced and a bad outcome follows, many people instinctively connect the two. A rise in crime after a policing reform, higher prices after a regulation, or worse exam results after an education change can all seem like straightforward evidence that the policy failed. The timing feels convincing because the policy came first and the problem came second. Yet timing alone is rarely enough to establish cause and effect. In everyday policy debates, one of the most common reasoning errors is to treat a before and after sequence as proof that the policy produced the outcome. To judge whether a policy really failed, we must ask a more demanding question: what would likely have happened if the policy had not been introduced? Answering that question requires considering background trends, rival explanations , and the mechanism by which the policy is supposed to affect outcomes . Modern policy evaluation methods are built around precisely this challenge. [What Works Growth] whatworksgrowth.org using impact evaluation evidence in policymaking What Works GrowthUsing impact evaluation evidence in policymaking30 Jan 2025 — Impact evaluation evidence helps us understand which polic... Did the Policy Fail, or Just Come First? Before and after arguments have a simple structure: A policy was introduced. An outcome occurred afterwards. Therefore, the policy caused the outcome. The problem is that many things change over time. Economies expand and contract, populations age, technologies spread, weather varies, and social behaviour shifts. Any of these factors may influence the outcome being discussed. Imagine a city introduces a new transport policy in January and traffic congestion worsens by June. The worsening congestion could be caused by the policy. But it could also reflect population growth, major roadworks, changes in commuting patterns, or a broader regional trend. The fact that congestion increased after the policy does not by itself distinguish among these possibilities. Critical thinking therefore requires separating temporal order from causal evidence. A cause must come before its effect, but not everything that comes before an event is its cause. Why Timing Feels Persuasive Humans are naturally sensitive to sequences of events. Detecting patterns is often useful, and in daily life many genuine causes do precede their effects. If a person touches a hot stove and immediately feels pain, timing is an important clue. Policy interventions, however, occur in complex systems involving thousands or millions of people. Multiple influences operate simultaneously, and effects may emerge gradually rather than instantly. This complexity makes simple before and after reasoning much less reliable. Timing becomes especially persuasive when: The outcome is emotionally charged. The policy was controversial before implementation. The outcome matches prior expectations. The policy change was highly visible while other influences were less obvious. In public debate, a dramatic event shortly after a policy change often receives disproportionate attention. A single negative outcome may be presented as proof of failure even when longer term data point in a different direction. This tendency illustrates a broader analytical lesson: evidence that fits a preferred story often feels stronger than it really is. Impact Evaluation: Simplest Explanation Ever impactevaluation evaluation Channel: M&E Guru · Views: 8.9K · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Coincidence, Background Trends, and Rival Causes The strongest challenge to a simple before and after claim is the existence of alternative explanations. Background trends can mislead Suppose unemployment rises after a government introduces a job training programme. Critics may claim the programme failed. Yet if unemployment was already rising before the programme began, or if a national recession affected many regions simultaneously, the increase may tell us little about the programme itself. Policy analysts therefore examine trends before the intervention. If the outcome was already moving in the same direction, attributing the change solely to the new policy becomes much harder. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicHow to do (or not to do) … Assessing the impact of a policy...by M Lagarde · 2012 · Cited by 386 — The objective of this art... Other events may occur at the same time Policies rarely operate in isolation. A tax change may coincide with shifts in consumer confidence. A public health policy may overlap with seasonal disease patterns. An education reform may occur alongside curriculum changes, teacher shortages, or demographic shifts. These competing influences are often called confounders or rival causes because they can create the appearance of a policy effect even when the policy itself played little or no role. Researchers studying policy impacts devote substantial effort to identifying and controlling for such factors. [evidenceforaction.org+2PMC] evidenceforaction.org A Brief Discussion on Time Varying Confounding in...1 Feb 2021 — Confounding is a major threat to the validity of all observational stud... Some outcomes fluctuate naturally Many social indicators rise and fall over time even without intervention. Crime rates, inflation, employment, and health outcomes often show natural variation. If observers focus only on two points in time—before the policy and after the policy—they may mistake ordinary fluctuation for evidence of success or failure. Looking at longer trends helps reveal whether an apparent change is unusual or simply part of an ongoing pattern. Evidence That Strengthens a Policy Failure Claim A before and after comparison is not useless. It can provide an important clue. The question is what additional evidence makes the claim more convincing. A plausible mechanism A stronger argument explains how the policy could realistically produce the outcome. For example, if a regulation increases compliance costs for businesses, and those costs are expected to raise prices, then subsequent price increases fit a clear causal pathway. The presence of a believable mechanism does not prove the claim, but it makes it more credible than a mere coincidence in timing. Difference in Differences method (DID) Policy impact evaluation Channel: Joint Research Centre (JRC) · Views: 48.4K · Uploaded: December 2017 · Length: 1 minute 51 seconds Open on YouTube Comparison with a credible counterfactual The central challenge in policy evaluation is determining what would have happened without the policy. Because we cannot observe both realities at once, analysts seek comparison groups or alternative benchmarks. One widely used approach compares changes in an affected group with changes in a similar unaffected group. If outcomes worsen only where the policy was implemented while remaining stable elsewhere, the case for causation becomes stronger. This family of methods, often called difference in differences analysis, is designed specifically to move beyond simple before and after comparisons. [PMC+2Lippincott Journals] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Advances in Difference in differences Methods for Policy...by G Wang · 2024 · Cited by 121 — Difference in differences (DiD) is a pow... Multiple observations over time A stronger assessment uses many observations before and after implementation rather than a single snapshot. If a policy is followed by a clear and sustained break from a long established trend, that pattern provides more persuasive evidence than one isolated post policy data point. Researchers often use interrupted time series designs to examine whether a trend changed noticeably after a policy was introduced. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicHow to do (or not to do) … Assessing the impact of a policy...by M Lagarde · 2012 · Cited by 386 — The objective of this art... Ruling out rival explanations Confidence increases when alternative causes are investigated and found insufficient. Suppose a policy is blamed for rising prices. Analysts would ask: Did similar price increases occur in places without the policy? Were there supply shocks affecting the entire market? Did input costs change at the same time? Did broader economic conditions explain the increase? The more thoroughly competing explanations are examined, the stronger a causal claim becomes. [PMC+2RAND]"},{"title":"Supplements","url":"/supplements-ac33a5/","description":"Feeling better after taking a supplement can matter personally, but it does not by itself prove the supplement caused the improvement.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Claims","level":3,"text":"Supplements Did the Supplement Work, or Did You Recover? Supplements Supplements Did the Supplement Work, or Did You Recover? Feeling better after taking a supplement can matter personally, but it does not by itself prove the supplement caused the improvement. Feeling better after taking a supplement can matter personally, but it does not by itself prove the supplement caused the improvement. mechanism Personal improvement versus causal proof Placebo, chance, and lifestyle changes What stronger health evidence looks like improving your thinking and analytical skills claims supplements Introduction Many people have a sincere story about a supplement that seemed to work. Someone starts taking a vitamin, herbal product, probiotic, or mineral and soon feels better. That experience matters. The person really may have improved. The analytical question is different: did the supplement cause the improvement? This distinction is central to evaluating health claims . A personal experience can show that two events happened together—taking a supplement and feeling better—but it cannot by itself establish that one caused the other. Symptoms often change naturally, people make multiple lifestyle changes at once, and expectations can influence how they feel. To improve reasoning about health claims, it helps to separate personal improvement from evidence of causation . Stronger conclusions require stronger evidence. [NCCIH] nccih.nih.gov NCCIHUsing Dietary Supplements Wisely NCCIH Information on using dietary supplements wisely, including safety, regulations, research, and resources... Personal Improvement Versus Causal Proof Imagine someone says, “I started taking a supplement for fatigue and within two weeks I had much more energy.” The statement may be completely accurate. The person genuinely felt better. What the story does not automatically tell us is why they felt better. Several explanations may fit the same observation: The supplement caused the improvement. The condition would have improved anyway. Better sleep, diet, exercise, or reduced stress produced the change. Symptoms fluctuated naturally and happened to improve after the supplement was started. Expectations about the supplement altered the perception of symptoms. The key analytical mistake is moving from “I improved after taking it” to “I improved because of it” without examining alternatives. This is a version of a common reasoning error known as confusing sequence with causation. Many illnesses and symptoms are temporary. People often seek supplements when they feel especially bad, which means improvement may occur simply because symptoms tend to return toward a more typical level over time. In everyday life, this pattern can make ineffective treatments appear effective. [Our World in Data] ourworldindata.org randomized controlled trials Our World in DataWhy randomized controlled trials matter and the...by S Dattani · 2022 · Cited by 2 — Randomized controlled trials are a... Anecdotes are therefore useful as clues but weak as proof. They can suggest a hypothesis worth testing, but they do not by themselves show that the supplement works for most people or even that it worked for the individual reporting the experience. [Federal Trade Commission] ftc.gov health products compliance guidance Federal Trade CommissionHealth Products Compliance GuidanceDec 20, 2022 — This document provides guidance from FTC staff on how to ensure... How Placebo Effects Work to Change Our Biology & Psychology Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube Placebo, Chance, and Lifestyle Changes Why feeling better does not always identify the cause Health outcomes are influenced by many factors simultaneously. A person who begins taking a supplement may also: Start sleeping more consistently. Exercise more. Eat differently. Reduce alcohol consumption. Recover from an infection or stressful period. Pay closer attention to their health in general. When several changes occur together, identifying the true cause becomes difficult. The supplement may receive credit for improvements produced by something else. This problem is especially common with symptoms that naturally fluctuate, such as fatigue, pain, digestive discomfort, stress, or sleep quality. A good week arriving after a new supplement can create a compelling but potentially misleading story. [NCCIH] nccih.nih.gov NCCIHUsing Dietary Supplements Wisely NCCIH Information on using dietary supplements wisely, including safety, regulations, research, and resources... The placebo effect is real, but it is not proof The placebo effect is sometimes misunderstood as “imaginary improvement”. Research shows that placebo responses can involve genuine changes in symptom experience and measurable biological processes, particularly for symptoms influenced by perception, expectation, and brain activity. [PMC+2NCBI] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPlacebo Effects: Neurological Mechanisms Inducing by SK Niazi · 2024 · Cited by 13 — The placebo effect can induce physiological or clinical neurological and organic responses despite... For example, studies have found placebo related improvements in pain, fatigue, anxiety, and other subjective symptoms. Brain imaging research suggests that expectations can influence neurotransmitter systems and alter how symptoms are experienced. [Harvard Health+2MDPI] health.harvard.edu Health The power of the placebo effect Instead, placebos work on symptoms modulated by the brain, like the perception of pain.Read more... This creates an important analytical challenge. If a person feels better after taking a supplement, the improvement is real to them. However, the existence of a real improvement does not demonstrate that the supplement's active ingredients produced it. A supplement can appear successful because: The supplement contains an effective ingredient. The placebo response contributed to symptom relief. Natural recovery occurred. Several factors worked together. The personal experience alone cannot distinguish among these possibilities. [Harvard Health+2NCBI] health.harvard.edu Health The power of the placebo effect Instead, placebos work on symptoms modulated by the brain, like the perception of pain.Read more... Medical Misinformation: How to Spot Myths & Trust the Science Ep. 8 (ft. Dr. Nontokozo Yusuff) Channel: Atlantia Clinical Trials Open on YouTube Chance can create convincing stories Humans are skilled at noticing patterns but less skilled at recognising coincidence. If thousands of people try a supplement, some will improve soon afterwards by chance alone. Those people are much more likely to tell their stories than people who noticed no difference. Over time, positive stories accumulate and can create the impression of strong effectiveness even when the overall evidence is weak. This does not mean positive reports are false. It means that isolated success stories are expected to occur even when a treatment has little or no effect. Determining whether the supplement truly helps requires looking beyond the most memorable examples. [Federal Trade Commission] ftc.gov health products compliance guidance Federal Trade CommissionHealth Products Compliance GuidanceDec 20, 2022 — This document provides guidance from FTC staff on how to ensure... Vitamin C Does Nothing for the Common Cold Channel: Medscape Open on YouTube What Stronger Health Evidence Looks Like If anecdotes cannot reliably establish causation, what can? The strongest evidence usually comes from controlled studies designed to compare outcomes between people who receive a supplement and people who receive a placebo or comparison treatment under similar conditions. Randomisation helps ensure that the groups differ mainly in the treatment being tested rather than in other factors. [Our World in Data] ourworldindata.org randomized controlled trials Our World in DataWhy randomized controlled trials matter and the...by S Dattani · 2022 · Cited by 2 — Randomized controlled trials are a... A useful progression of evidence looks like this: Type of evidenceWhat it can tell usIndividual anecdoteSomething happened to one personMultiple testimonialsA pattern may exist, but biases remainObservational studiesAssociations can be identifiedRandomised controlled trialsStronger evidence about cause and effectSystematic reviews of many trialsThe most reliable overall picture No single study is perfect, and even randomised controlled trials have limitations. However, they are designed specifically to answer the causal question that anecdotes cannot answer: what would have happened if similar people had not taken the supplement? [Our World in Data+2PMC] ourworldindata.org randomized controlled trials Our World in DataWhy randomized controlled trials matter and the...by S Dattani · 2022 · Cited by 2 — Randomized controlled trials are a... This matters because evidence for supplements varies enormously. Some supplements have substantial research behind specific uses, while others are"},{"title":"Warrants","url":"/warrants/","description":"The warrant is the quiet bridge between evidence and claim, and finding it often reveals where the disagreement really sits.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Claims","level":3,"text":"Warrants The Hidden Bridge in Every Argument Warrants Warrants The Hidden Bridge in Every Argument The warrant is the quiet bridge between evidence and claim, and finding it often reveals where the disagreement really sits. The warrant is the quiet bridge between evidence and claim, and finding it often reveals where the disagreement really sits. mechanism Claim, grounds, and warrant in plain language How to spot the assumption doing the work Testing whether the bridge is strong enough improving your thinking and analytical skills claims warrants Introduction When people disagree, they often think they are arguing about facts. In many cases, however, the real disagreement sits elsewhere: in the hidden assumption that connects those facts to a conclusion. Stephen Toulmin, the philosopher who developed the Toulmin model of argumentation in the 1950s, called this connecting assumption the warrant . The warrant is the reason the evidence is supposed to support the claim. Without it, facts and conclusions simply sit side by side. [Purdue OWL] owl.purdue.edu OWLToulmin Argument Purdue OWLToulmin Argument Purdue OWLThe Toulmin method is a style of argumentation that breaks arguments down into six component parts... For anyone trying to improve their thinking and analytical skills, warrants matter because they reveal the often unspoken logic inside everyday arguments. Once the warrant becomes visible, it is easier to understand why reasonable people can look at the same evidence and reach different conclusions. The debate is often not about the evidence itself but about whether the bridge from evidence to claim is justified. [Purdue OWL] owl.purdue.edu OWLOrganizing Your Argument Purdue OWLOrganizing Your Argument Purdue OWLWarrant (Bridge): In this section, you explain why or how your data supports the claim. As... The Hidden Bridge in Every Argument Claim, grounds, and warrant in plain language Toulmin's model identifies three core elements present in ordinary arguments: [youtube.com] youtube.com Toulmin Model of Argumentation The Toulmin Method of Argumentation THUNK... Claim : what someone wants you to believe. Grounds (or data): the evidence offered in support. Warrant : the assumption or principle that explains why the evidence supports the claim. [Purdue OWL+2mhcc.pressbooks.pub] owl.purdue.edu OWLToulmin Argument Purdue OWLToulmin Argument Purdue OWLThe Toulmin method is a style of argumentation that breaks arguments down into six component parts... A simple example shows the difference: Claim: \"This restaurant is worth visiting.\" Grounds: \"It is always busy on weekends.\" Warrant: \"Restaurants that attract many customers are usually good.\" The evidence alone does not force the conclusion. The conclusion becomes plausible only if the listener accepts the warrant. If someone instead believes that popularity often reflects marketing rather than quality, they may reject the conclusion while accepting the evidence. This is why Toulmin described warrants as the mechanism that authorises movement from grounds to claims . They are the hidden rules of reasoning that make an argument seem sensible. [Argument Centered Education] argumentcenterededucation.com our adaptation of the toulmin model of argument Argument Centered EducationOur Adaptation of the Toulmin Model of ArgumentJul 17, 2019 — In The Uses of Argument, he says that a warrant... Why warrants are often invisible In everyday conversation, people rarely state their warrants directly. They assume listeners already share them. Writing guides based on Toulmin's work consistently note that warrants are frequently implicit rather than explicitly stated. [purdueglobalwriting.center] purdueglobalwriting.center The Toulmin Model of Argument PodcastThe warrant is the assumption, or belief, the writer has in mind when forming an argument. The under... Consider these examples: GroundsClaimLikely warrant\"She has twenty years of experience.\"\"She should lead the project.\"Experience usually improves judgement and leadership.\"The policy was introduced last year, and costs increased.\"\"The policy caused the increase.\"Events that occur before a change are likely responsible for it.\"Several friends recommend the film.\"\"The film is probably good.\"Trusted recommendations are useful indicators of quality. In each case, the warrant quietly does the heavy lifting. Without it, the grounds do not automatically support the claim. The Toulmin Model of Argumentation: Claims, Data, and Warrants, Oh My! Logic Series Academy ... Channel: Academy 4 Social Civics · Views: 12.9K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube How to Spot the Assumption Doing the Work One of the most useful analytical habits is learning to uncover the warrant beneath an argument. A practical technique is to ask: \"Why does that evidence make this conclusion reasonable?\" The answer usually exposes the warrant. Imagine someone says: \"Three employees resigned this month, so morale must be poor.\" The grounds are clear: three resignations. The claim is that morale is poor. The hidden warrant is something like: \"When multiple employees leave in a short period, low morale is usually a significant cause.\" Now the discussion becomes more precise. Instead of arguing vaguely about the conclusion, people can examine the assumption itself. Perhaps resignations were caused by higher salaries elsewhere. Perhaps a restructuring programme was announced. The real issue is whether the warrant fits the situation. This is one reason Toulmin's model remains influential in argument analysis. By separating claims, grounds, and warrants, it allows each part to be evaluated independently. [Blinn College] blinn.edu Blinn CollegeToulmin ArgumentIdentify each argument's claims, data, and warrants. Look for qualifiers, rebuttals, and backing for the war... Where disagreements often really live Many public and personal disagreements turn out to be warrant disputes. For example: Health discussions : Two people may agree that someone improved after taking a supplement. One accepts the warrant that improvement indicates effectiveness; the other questions whether recovery, placebo effects, or other factors explain the change. Workplace debates : Colleagues may agree that a team missed deadlines. They disagree about whether missed deadlines indicate poor management or unrealistic workload expectations. News and politics : People may accept the same facts but disagree over whether those facts demonstrate success, failure, fairness, or responsibility. The evidence is not always the source of conflict. Often the conflict lies in different assumptions about what the evidence means. Testing Whether the Bridge Is Strong Enough Recognising a warrant is only the first step. The next question is whether it is a good warrant. Toulmin Model of Argumentation Channel: Amateur Logician · Views: 409 · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Ask whether the warrant is generally true A strong warrant usually connects evidence and conclusion through a principle that works in many comparable situations. For example: Grounds: \"The bridge inspection found structural damage.\" Claim: \"The bridge should be repaired.\" Warrant: \"Structural damage creates safety risks.\" This warrant is broadly reliable. Compare it with: [blinn.edu] blinn.edu Blinn CollegeToulmin ArgumentIdentify each argument's claims, data, and warrants. Look for qualifiers, rebuttals, and backing for the war... Grounds: \"The bridge inspection found structural damage.\" Claim: \"The city council is incompetent.\" Warrant: \"Infrastructure problems always result from incompetence.\" That warrant is much more questionable because alternative explanations are easy to imagine. Look for exceptions Toulmin emphasised that arguments should be tested against possible objections and exceptions. A warrant that collapses when a few obvious exceptions appear is usually weak. [Open Oco] open.ocolearnok.org Open OcoModels of Argumentation – First Year English CompositionToulmin emphasizes first identifying all of the potential objections to t... Consider: Grounds: \"The company is profitable.\" Claim: \"Employees must be happy.\" Warrant: \"Profitable companies treat employees well.\" Many exceptions immediately come to mind. Profitability and employee satisfaction are related in some circumstances but not reliably enough to support a confident conclusion. Check whether the warrant matches the strength of the claim A common reasoning error occurs when a modest warrant is used to support a sweeping conclusion. For example: Grounds: \"Two customers complained.\" Claim: \"The product is a failure.\" Warrant: \"Customer complaints indicate product quality.\" Even if the warrant has some truth, it does not justify such a broad claim. The evidence and warrant support only a much weaker conclusion, such as \"the product may have issues worth investigating\". Strong reasoning requires proportionality between grounds, warrant, and claim."},{"title":"Work Complaints","url":"/work-complaints/","description":"Three frustrated colleagues may reveal a real software problem, or they may reflect a narrow slice of the team's experience.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Claims","level":3,"text":"Work Complaints Are Three Complaints Enough to Blame the Software? Work Complaints Work Complaints Are Three Complaints Enough to Blame the Software? Three frustrated colleagues may reveal a real software problem, or they may reflect a narrow slice of the team's experience. Three frustrated colleagues may reveal a real software problem, or they may reflect a narrow slice of the team's experience. population context Separating user frustration from system failure Checking whether complaints are representative Evidence that can confirm or weaken the claim improving your thinking and analytical skills claims work complaints Introduction Three colleagues complaining about a piece of software may be the first sign of a genuine problem. They may also represent only a small, unusually frustrated part of the workforce. The key analytical question is not whether the complaints are sincere, but whether they are representative. In workplace discussions, people often move quickly from “several employees are unhappy” to “the system is broken”. That leap contains an assumption: that the people speaking up reflect the experience of the wider group. Sometimes they do. Sometimes they do not. Improving your thinking requires separating the existence of complaints from the strength of the evidence those complaints provide. A handful of complaints can be enough to justify investigation, but not necessarily enough to justify a broad conclusion about software, managers, policies, or processes. [DavidsonMorris Solicitors] davidsonmorris.com SolicitorsEmployee Surveys: HR Strategy, Risk and Action 2026February 7, 2026 — 7 Feb 2026 — Survey data provides an evi... Published: February 7, 2026 Are Three Complaints Enough to Blame the Software? Imagine a team of fifty people using a new project management tool. Three employees report that it is slow, confusing, and causing delays. A manager concludes that the software is harming productivity. The complaints are real evidence. They should not be dismissed. However, the conclusion depends on information that has not yet been gathered. Are the three employees typical users? Are they among the heaviest users? Are they new to the tool? Are others experiencing the same problems but remaining silent? Or are most employees using it successfully? The analytical mistake is treating a small number of observations as if they automatically describe the whole population. In this case, the relevant population is everyone affected by the software, not merely the people who complained. A useful distinction is: Complaint evidence : employees report difficulties. Population evidence : information showing how widespread those difficulties are. Causal evidence : information showing that the software, rather than another factor, is producing the difficulties. The existence of complaints establishes the first point. It does not automatically establish the second or third. Separating User Frustration from System Failure Complaints often reveal genuine friction. The challenge is determining whether the friction reflects a system wide problem or a local experience. Consider several possibilities: The software crashes regularly for most users. The software works well, but a particular team lacks training. The software is slow only on older hardware. The software performs adequately, but a recent update introduced a temporary bug. The software is unpopular because it changed established work habits. Each scenario can produce frustrated employees. Yet the appropriate response differs dramatically. This is why organisations increasingly rely on broader employee feedback systems, surveys, and performance measures rather than isolated anecdotes . Survey data can provide a wider evidential base and reduce dependence on individual stories when assessing organisational issues. [DavidsonMorris Solicitors] davidsonmorris.com SolicitorsEmployee Surveys: HR Strategy, Risk and Action 2026February 7, 2026 — 7 Feb 2026 — Survey data provides an evi... Published: February 7, 2026 A complaint is therefore often best treated as a clue rather than a verdict. It identifies where to look. It does not necessarily tell you what you will find. What is Anecdotal Evidence? (Easiest Explanation) Channel: Helpful Professor Explains! · Views: 1.8K · Uploaded: March 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 59 seconds Open on YouTube Checking Whether Complaints Are Representative The central question is simple: do the complaints reflect the wider workforce? Who Is Speaking? People who experience strong dissatisfaction are often more motivated to speak up than people who are merely content. As a result, the visible pool of complaints may not match the underlying distribution of experiences. Researchers studying surveys and employee feedback have long recognised that participation can be affected by people's attitudes and experiences, creating the possibility that vocal respondents differ systematically from less vocal employees. [arXiv] arxiv.org How many federal employees are not satisfied? Using response times to estimate population proportions under the survey variable caus... When evaluating workplace complaints, ask: How many people are affected? How many people were asked? How many chose to respond? Are certain groups overrepresented among complainants? Three complaints from three users out of three contacted employees suggest something very different from three complaints out of three hundred users. Who Is Missing? Analytical errors often arise from paying attention only to available information. Psychologists describe the availability heuristic as a tendency to judge situations based on examples that come easily to mind. Vivid complaints are memorable and therefore risk appearing more common than they really are. [The Decision Lab+2Ness Labs] thedecisionlab.com The Decision Lab Availability Heuristic The Decision LabAvailability Heuristic The Decision...The availability heuristic describes our tendency to use information that comes... In a workplace context, managers may repeatedly hear from the same dissatisfied employees and gradually conclude that everyone feels the same way. Yet the silent majority may have a different experience. A useful habit is to ask: Who has not been heard from yet? Are the Complainants Typical Users? Representativeness depends not only on numbers but also on relevance. Suppose three complaints come from: The most experienced users. The newest employees. One department. Remote workers only. Employees using specialised features. [arxiv.org] arxiv.org How many federal employees are not satisfied? Using response times to estimate population proportions under the survey variable caus... Each group may encounter issues that others rarely see. The question is not whether their experiences are valid. The question is whether those experiences can reasonably be generalised to everyone else. Anecdotal Fallacy: A Data Point of One Is Not Evidence Channel: Beyond UX Design · Views: 36 · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Evidence That Can Confirm or Weaken the Claim When someone claims that software, a manager, or a process is causing problems, additional evidence can either strengthen or weaken that conclusion. Evidence That Strengthens the Claim The claim becomes more persuasive when multiple forms of evidence point in the same direction: Employee surveys show widespread dissatisfaction. Help desk tickets reveal recurring issues. Usage data shows frequent errors or workarounds. Productivity measures decline after implementation. Complaints emerge independently from different teams. When separate sources converge, confidence increases because the conclusion no longer relies on a small set of anecdotes. Organisations frequently use employee surveys precisely because they allow comparisons across teams, locations, and time periods rather than relying solely on individual reports. [Gallup.com+2Culture Amp] gallup.com s Q12 Employee Engagement Survey Gallup's Q12 Employee Engagement SurveyOctober 26, 2021 — Use the Gallup Q12 survey to engage employees and improve performance. Drive cr... Published: October 26, 2021 Evidence That Weakens the Claim The claim becomes less convincing when broader information tells a different story: Most users report satisfaction. Performance measures remain stable or improve. Problems are concentrated in one small group. Alternative explanations fit the facts better. The complaints disappear after training or configuration changes. In these situations, the complaints may still reveal a real issue, but not necessarily the broad failure originally alleged. Combining Different Sources Research in other complaint heavy environments has shown the value of combining multiple reporting channels. Studies comparing different forms of reporting have found that separate sources often reveal different aspects of the same problem and can produce a more complete picture when considered together. [Sage Journals]"},{"title":"Calibration","url":"/calibration/","description":"Recording predictions with confidence levels turns judgment into a feedback loop instead of a private feeling.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Calibration Is Your Confidence Matched to Evidence? Calibration Calibration Is Your Confidence Matched to Evidence? Recording predictions with confidence levels turns judgment into a feedback loop instead of a private feeling. Recording predictions with confidence levels turns judgment into a feedback loop instead of a private feeling. policy intervention Why calibration matters How prediction journals work Learning from misses improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration Introduction Confidence calibration is the practice of checking whether your certainty matches reality. If you say ten things are 70% likely, roughly seven should happen over time. A prediction journal makes that test possible: before an outcome is known, you write down the claim, the probability you assign to it, the evidence behind it, and the date when it will be checked. That small habit turns judgement from a private feeling into a feedback loop . This matters because memory is a poor scorekeeper. People often remember the broad direction of their past views, but not the exact confidence they felt, the alternatives they dismissed, or the conditions under which they said they would change their mind. Calibration does not require you to become cautious about everything. The aim is sharper confidence: low when the evidence is thin, higher when the evidence is strong, and revisable when new information arrives. Forecasting research, especially the Good Judgment Project, suggests that probabilistic prediction, feedback , practice and clear scoring can improve judgement in real world questions, not just in classroom puzzles. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good Judgment The impact of training and practice on judgmental accuracy Good JudgmentThe impact of training and practice on judgmental accuracy...September 30, 2016 — by W Chang · 2016 · Cited by 153 — Althou... Published: September 30, 2016 Why calibration matters A person can be knowledgeable and still miscalibrated. The issue is not simply whether a conclusion is right or wrong; it is whether the stated confidence was appropriate. A 55% judgement that turns out wrong may have been reasonable. A 95% judgement that turns out wrong deserves much closer review, because the error was not only in the prediction but in the strength of belief attached to it. Calibration is easiest to understand through weather forecasts. If a forecaster says there is a 70% chance of rain on many similar days, rain should occur on about 70% of those days. The same idea applies to personal and professional judgement: “I am 80% sure this project will ship by Friday”, “There is a 30% chance this client renews”, or “I am 65% confident this news report will be corrected within a week”. The Brier score, originally developed for probability forecasts, measures the gap between predicted probabilities and actual outcomes; lower scores mean better probabilistic accuracy. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score The practical value is that calibration separates three things people often blur together: Accuracy: did the thing happen? Confidence: how strongly did you expect it? Learning signal: what should change in your future judgement? Without calibration, a person can protect almost any self image. A failed 80% prediction becomes “I only said it was likely, not certain”. A successful 55% prediction becomes “I knew it”. Written probabilities make that escape harder. They also make improvement less moralistic. Instead of asking “Am I good at judgement?”, you ask “Where am I overconfident, underconfident, vague, or slow to update?” Research on overconfidence is more nuanced than the slogan “people are always overconfident”. Reviews of probability calibration show that miscalibration varies by task difficulty, knowledge and evidence quality. People may be overconfident on hard questions yet underconfident on easier ones, which means the useful intervention is not blanket humility but better matching between evidence and certainty. [Warrington College of Business] bear.warrington.ufl.edu Open source on ufl.edu. How prediction journals work A prediction journal is a simple record of claims before reality has had a chance to make them look obvious. It can be a spreadsheet, notebook, database, forecasting platform or shared team log. The format matters less than the discipline: the prediction must be specific enough to resolve, probabilistic enough to reveal confidence, and reviewed often enough to create feedback. A useful entry normally includes five parts: 1. A clear question. “Will the supplier deliver the parts by 5 pm on 30 June 2026?” is better than “Will the supplier be reliable?” 2. A probability. Use numbers, not vague words such as “probably” or “unlikely”. A 60% forecast and an 85% forecast should not be treated as the same belief. 3. The evidence and assumptions . Record the base rate, current signals, reasons for doubt, and any key dependency. 4. A resolution rule. State what will count as yes, no, partial, or unresolved. 5. A review date. Decide when the prediction will be scored or revisited. The Good Judgment Project is the strongest modern demonstration of this style of judgement training at scale. In a multi year geopolitical forecasting tournament, forecasters made probabilistic predictions on real world questions, updated them as new information arrived, and were scored using measures such as the Brier score. Research from the project found that brief probability training improved forecasting accuracy, with one study reporting Brier score improvements of 6% to 11% over a control condition after training lasting less than an hour. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good Judgment The impact of training and practice on judgmental accuracy Good JudgmentThe impact of training and practice on judgmental accuracy...September 30, 2016 — by W Chang · 2016 · Cited by 153 — Althou... Published: September 30, 2016 The point is not that everyone needs a formal tournament. The transferable lesson is that judgement improves when forecasts are explicit, repeated, scored, and compared against outcomes. Good Judgment describes its evidence based process as combining talent spotting, training, teamwork and aggregation; for an individual or small team, the most accessible parts are training, practice, and careful review. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Open source on goodjudgment.com. A prediction journal also changes the emotional texture of thinking. When a belief stays private, being wrong can feel like a threat. When predictions are logged routinely, wrong forecasts become data. A journal makes it normal to say, “I gave this 75%, it failed, and here is what I missed.” That is a better learning environment than one in which only confident sounding conclusions are rewarded. 'Superforecasting': The people that predict the future – BBC REEL Channel: BBC Global Open on YouTube What to predict The best journal questions sit in the middle zone: uncertain enough to teach you something, but concrete enough to resolve. Very obvious predictions teach little. Extremely vague predictions cannot be scored. The useful targets are decisions and recurring judgement clusters where better calibration would change behaviour. Good candidates include: Project delivery: whether a task will finish by a date, exceed budget, or need rework. Hiring and management: whether a candidate will accept, whether a team member will hit a milestone, or whether a meeting will produce a decision. Research and analysis: whether a source will be confirmed, whether a claim will survive checking, or whether an initial explanation will remain the best one. Personal planning: whether a habit will be maintained, whether a trip will stay within budget, or whether a deadline estimate is realistic. External events: public questions in politics, economics, technology, sport or policy, where outcome criteria are available. For improving analytical skill, repeated small predictions are usually more valuable than rare dramatic ones. A person who makes two major forecasts a year gets little feedback. A person who makes ten small forecasts a week can begin to see patterns: perhaps they are too optimistic about delivery dates, too deferential to confident colleagues, too slow to update after contrary evidence, or too inclined to put everything in the safe 55% to 65% range. Forecasting platforms such as Metaculus and Good Judgment Open show how this can work in public settings: questions are stated in advance, probabilities can be updated, and track records can be compared over time. Their broader value for a private prediction journal is cultural as much as technical: they normalise the idea that good judgement is not a single impressive call, but a long run record of probabilistic claims. [metaculus.com] metaculus.com comparing forecasting track records for ai benchmarking and beyond comparing forecasting track records for ai benchmarking and beyond Learning from misses The"},{"title":"Brier Scores","url":"/brier-scores/","description":"Brier scores give prediction journals a simple way to compare stated probabilities with what actually happened.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Calibration","level":3,"text":"Brier Scores A Simple Scorecard for Forecasting Skill Brier Scores Brier Scores A Simple Scorecard for Forecasting Skill Brier scores give prediction journals a simple way to compare stated probabilities with what actually happened. Brier scores give prediction journals a simple way to compare stated probabilities with what actually happened. dataset evidence What the Brier score measures How lower scores reflect better probability forecasts Limits of scoring small personal samples improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration brier scores Introduction A prediction journal becomes much more valuable when it includes a consistent way to score forecasts after the outcome is known. The Brier score is one of the simplest and most widely used methods for doing this. Instead of asking only whether a prediction was right or wrong, it measures how close your stated probability was to reality. That makes it especially useful for improving judgement: a confident mistake is penalised more heavily than a cautious one, while a well calibrated probability is rewarded even if certainty was impossible. Originally developed for weather forecasting, the Brier score is now widely used across forecasting research because it encourages honest probability estimates rather than exaggerated confidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score Within a personal prediction journal, the score provides a running record of forecasting skill. Rather than remembering a few memorable successes or failures, you accumulate evidence about whether your confidence levels actually match the world. What the Brier score measures For a binary prediction—an event either happens or it does not—the Brier score is the average squared difference between the probability you assigned and the outcome that occurred. Outcomes are coded as either 1 (the event happened) or 0 (it did not). For a single prediction: Forecast 90%, event occurs → score = (0.9 − 1)² = 0.01 Forecast 90%, event does not occur → score = (0.9 − 0)² = 0.81 Forecast 50%, regardless of outcome → score = 0.25 The score ranges from 0 (perfect) to 1 (worst possible under the modern binary formulation). Lower scores are always better. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score The squared error design matters because it reflects the cost of overconfidence . Predicting an event with 99% confidence that then fails is treated as a much larger error than assigning 60% confidence and being wrong. Likewise, assigning 40% to an event that does happen is not considered disastrous because you acknowledged substantial uncertainty from the outset. This property makes the Brier score a proper scoring rule : over many forecasts, the best strategy is to record your genuine beliefs rather than systematically inflating or hedging probabilities . [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicProbabilistic Forecasts, Calibration and Sharpnessby T Gneiting · 2007 · Cited by 2513 — Like all proper scoring rules for bi... Bias Variance Trade off Explained Channel: DataMListic · Views: 6.6K · Uploaded: February 2023 · Length: 4 minutes 47 seconds Open on YouTube How lower scores reflect better probability forecasts A personal prediction journal is not trying to reward boldness or caution. It is trying to reward probabilities that correspond well with reality. Imagine two people each making 100 forecasts. Forecaster A often predicts 95% confidence, but many of those predictions fail. Forecaster B usually predicts between 60% and 80%, matching uncertainty more realistically. Forecaster A may appear impressive because of occasional highly confident successes, yet repeated overconfidence will produce a worse average Brier score. Forecaster B is likely to score better because the stated probabilities more closely reflect actual frequencies. Over many predictions, the score naturally combines two desirable qualities: Calibration: Do events predicted at roughly 70% actually occur about 70% of the time? Resolution (or sharpness): Are you willing to move away from 50% when the evidence genuinely supports stronger probabilities? Forecasting research emphasises that excellent forecasters are not simply cautious. They distinguish situations where evidence justifies high confidence from those where uncertainty remains. Proper scoring rules such as the Brier score reward this combination of good calibration and informative forecasts. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicProbabilistic Forecasts, Calibration and Sharpnessby T Gneiting · 2007 · Cited by 2513 — Like all proper scoring rules for bi... For a prediction journal, this means that improvement is visible even before overall accuracy changes dramatically. You may still experience many incorrect predictions, but your confidence can become progressively better matched to reality. Using the score in a personal prediction journal The mechanics are straightforward. For every prediction: 1. Record a probability before the outcome is known. 2. Wait until the event resolves using a predefined resolution rule. 3. Calculate the Brier score for that prediction. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score 4. Average scores across many predictions. A spreadsheet can automate the calculation using the formula: (Predicted probability − Actual outcome)² where the outcome is 1 if the event occurred and 0 otherwise. The average is more informative than individual scores. A single unfortunate forecast tells little about forecasting skill, but dozens or hundreds begin to reveal consistent patterns. Reviewing the highest scoring (worst) forecasts is particularly valuable. Questions worth asking include: Was I consistently too confident? Did I ignore important base rates? Did I update too slowly when new evidence appeared? Did I use probabilities like 95% or 99% too freely? This shifts post mortems away from simply asking whether a prediction succeeded and towards understanding whether the confidence level itself was justified. Measuring Accuracy: the Brier Score Channel: Gerard Rothfus Open on YouTube Limits of scoring small personal samples Although the Brier score is mathematically simple, interpreting it requires caution when the number of forecasts is small. With only twenty or thirty predictions, randomness can dominate. A few surprising outcomes may substantially change your average score even if your judgement has not changed much. This is particularly true for rare events, where meaningful comparisons often require much larger datasets before differences in forecasting skill become statistically reliable. Meteorological verification research has shown that evaluating rare event forecasts may require very large sample sizes, especially when distinguishing between already strong forecasters. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score Several practical limitations follow. Small samples are noisy. Avoid drawing strong conclusions from only a few months of predictions. Rare events distort comparisons. If you mostly forecast unusual events, average scores may fluctuate considerably. Question difficulty matters. A journal dominated by extremely hard predictions will naturally produce different scores from one containing routine operational forecasts. Because of these issues, comparing your own scores over time is usually more informative than comparing them with someone else's unless both people forecast similar questions over similar periods. What the score does not tell you A low Brier score does not mean every prediction was correct.( endnote 1 \"Endnote 1\") [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score Likewise, a high score does not necessarily mean your reasoning was poor. Unexpected events happen, and a well justified 70% prediction will still be wrong about three times out of ten. The score also cannot explain why a forecast succeeded or failed. A prediction journal should therefore preserve the reasoning behind each probability. Reviewing assumptions alongside the numerical score often reveals recurring judgement errors—such as neglecting base rates, relying on vivid anecdotes , or failing to update after new information—that the score alone cannot diagnose. Researchers have also pointed out that the Brier score reflects not only forecasting quality but, in some settings, characteristics of the underlying data and outcome distribution. More recent methodological work therefore recommends interpreting scores alongside the context in which predictions were made rather than treating a single number as a complete measure of forecasting ability. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov On misconceptions about the Brier score in binary prediction...by L Hoessly · 2026 · Cited by 19 — The Brier score was shown to refle... Why Brier scores strengthen deliberate thinking The greatest value of the Brier score is not mathematical precision but behavioural feedback . It converts vague memories into measurable evidence. Over months or years, a prediction journal stops being a collection of anecdotes"},{"title":"Good Judgment","url":"/good-judgment/","description":"The Good Judgment Project shows how explicit probabilities, feedback, updating, and scoring can improve real-world judgement.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Calibration","level":3,"text":"Good Judgment What Superforecasting Teaches Everyday Thinkers Good Judgment Good Judgment What Superforecasting Teaches Everyday Thinkers The Good Judgment Project shows how explicit probabilities, feedback, updating, and scoring can improve real world judgement. The Good Judgment Project shows how explicit probabilities, feedback, updating, and scoring can improve real world judgement. policy intervention Why forecasting tournaments matter Training, updating, teamwork, and aggregation What individuals can borrow without a tournament improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration good judgment Introduction The Good Judgment Project showed that better judgement is not mainly about having exceptional intelligence or privileged information. Instead, it comes from making explicit probabilistic forecasts, updating them as evidence changes, and learning systematically from feedback . Originally developed as part of a large forecasting tournament for the US intelligence community, the project demonstrated that ordinary people could substantially improve their predictive accuracy through training, disciplined thinking, teamwork, and careful measurement. These lessons translate surprisingly well to everyday decisions, from planning projects and assessing business risks to judging whether a news story will develop as expected. Rather than trying to predict everything perfectly, the project teaches people how to become better calibrated: more confident when the evidence is strong, less certain when it is weak, and quicker to revise beliefs when reality changes. [Stanford University+2iarpa.gov] web.stanford.edu Intelligence Community sponsored a series of forecasting tournaments designed to explore the best... The Good Judgment Project (GJP). In... Why forecasting tournaments matter The Good Judgment Project emerged from the Intelligence Advanced Research Projects Activity (IARPA) Aggregative Contingent Estimation (ACE) programme, which asked competing research teams to improve geopolitical forecasting. Participants answered hundreds of clearly defined questions such as whether a country would hold an election by a particular date or whether a political agreement would be reached within a specified period. Every forecast received a numerical probability and was later scored against reality. [iarpa.gov] iarpa.gov ACEThe goal of the ACE Program is to dramatically enhance the accuracy, precision, and timeliness of intelligence forecasts for a broad r... This structure matters because it removes many of the excuses that normally protect poor judgement. Forecasts must be: specific enough to be resolved; probabilistic rather than vague; made before outcomes are known; scored objectively after the event. Unlike ordinary opinions, forecasts cannot quietly evolve in memory after the fact. The continual cycle of prediction, outcome and feedback allows genuine improvement. Perhaps the project's most important finding was that forecasting ability varied far more than many researchers expected. Some participants consistently outperformed both experts and other volunteers over multiple years. These \"superforecasters\" were not simply lucky. Their advantage persisted across different questions and forecasting seasons, suggesting that better judgement reflected repeatable habits rather than isolated successes. [Stanford University+2Wharton Faculty Platform] web.stanford.edu Intelligence Community sponsored a series of forecasting tournaments designed to explore the best... The Good Judgment Project (GJP). In... Training, updating, teamwork, and aggregation The Good Judgment Project identified several practical behaviours that repeatedly distinguished stronger forecasters. None require participation in a forecasting tournament. Think in probabilities, not certainties Everyday conversations encourage words such as \"probably\", \"unlikely\" or \"almost certain\". Superforecasters instead attached explicit numerical probabilities to claims . Doing so forces clearer thinking. Saying there is a 65% chance that a supplier will deliver on time communicates much more information than simply saying the delivery is \"likely\". It also makes later evaluation possible. Repeated use of numerical probabilities gradually improves calibration because forecasters discover whether their 70% predictions really occur about seven times out of ten. [Stanford University] web.stanford.edu Intelligence Community sponsored a series of forecasting tournaments designed to explore the best... The Good Judgment Project (GJP). In... Update beliefs frequently One of the strongest findings from the project was that successful forecasters revised their estimates more often than weaker participants. Updating did not mean constantly changing one's mind for trivial reasons. Instead, it meant responding proportionately to genuinely new evidence. Rather than treating an initial prediction as something to defend, superforecasters viewed it as a temporary estimate that should improve over time. This contrasts with a common psychological tendency to anchor on first impressions and adjust too little afterwards. [Stanford University+2Good Judgment] web.stanford.edu Intelligence Community sponsored a series of forecasting tournaments designed to explore the best... The Good Judgment Project (GJP). In... Why Intelligent People Are Wrong More Often Than Chimps Philip Tetlock Channel: ohxpsy Open on YouTube Break difficult questions into smaller ones Large uncertain questions become easier when divided into manageable components. Instead of asking: \"Will this new product succeed?\" a forecaster might separately estimate: whether development finishes on schedule; whether regulators approve it; whether competitors launch alternatives; whether customer demand reaches a specified level. Thinking through each component often produces better overall judgement than relying on intuition about the whole problem. This \"outside view plus inside view\" style of reasoning became a recurring theme in the project's training. [Corporate Research Forum] crforum.co.uk Intelligence Advanced Research Projects Activity tournament.Read more rporate Research ForumSuperforecasting: How to Upgrade Your Company's...by PJH Schoemaker · 2016 · Cited by 71 — The Good Judgment Pro... Use base rates before unique stories Superforecasters typically began with historical frequencies before considering special circumstances. Suppose someone wants to estimate whether a software project will finish on time. A useful first question is not \"How motivated is this team?\" but \"How often do comparable software projects finish on schedule?\" Starting with similar past cases helps prevent compelling narratives from overwhelming statistical reality. Individual details still matter, but they are interpreted relative to an evidence based baseline rather than replacing it altogether. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentTen Commandments for Aspiring SuperforecastersThese commandments describe behaviors that have been “experimentally demonstra... Learn collaboratively without seeking consensus An interesting finding from the Good Judgment Project was that carefully designed teamwork improved forecasting. The most successful teams did not simply vote or compromise. Members challenged one another's assumptions, shared evidence and independently revised their probabilities. Diversity of perspective proved valuable when combined with respectful disagreement and independent judgement. Aggregation algorithms then combined many individual forecasts, usually producing predictions that outperformed most single forecasters. This illustrates a refined version of the \"wisdom of crowds\": crowds become especially effective when individuals think independently and update from shared information rather than merely copying one another. [Stanford University+2Wharton Faculty Platform] web.stanford.edu Intelligence Community sponsored a series of forecasting tournaments designed to explore the best... The Good Judgment Project (GJP). In... What individuals can borrow without a tournament Most people do not need sophisticated software or hundreds of forecasting questions to benefit from these ideas. The habits can be incorporated into an ordinary prediction journal. Useful questions include: Will this project finish before the deadline? Will inflation fall below a stated figure by year end? Will I complete this course within six months? Will this customer renew their contract? Each prediction should include: a precise resolution criterion; a numerical probability; key assumptions; the date for review. After the outcome becomes known, compare the prediction with reality rather than with memory. Over time, patterns emerge. Some people discover they consistently assign probabilities that are too high. Others realise they update too slowly when new evidence arrives. These recurring errors are far more useful than isolated mistakes because they reveal systematic"},{"title":"Miss Reviews","url":"/miss-reviews/","description":"Missed forecasts are most useful when the review separates bad evidence, bad framing, slow updates, and reasonable uncertainty.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Calibration","level":3,"text":"Miss Reviews When a Wrong Forecast Was Still Reasonable Miss Reviews Miss Reviews When a Wrong Forecast Was Still Reasonable Missed forecasts are most useful when the review separates bad evidence, bad framing, slow updates, and reasonable uncertainty. Missed forecasts are most useful when the review separates bad evidence, bad framing, slow updates, and reasonable uncertainty. mechanism Why wrong does not always mean badly judged Five failure types to look for in reviews How to avoid punishing good uncertainty improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration miss reviews Introduction Reviewing a failed prediction is one of the fastest ways to improve judgement—provided the review asks whether the reasoning was sound at the time rather than whether the outcome happened to be favourable. Hindsight bias, often described as the \"I knew it all along\" effect, makes past events appear more predictable than they really were. Once the outcome is known, people unconsciously rewrite their memory of what they expected, making good decisions look obvious and reasonable uncertainty look like incompetence. [The Decision Lab+2Wikipedia] thedecisionlab.com The Decision Lab Hindsight Bias Also called the “knew it all along” effect...Read more... Within a prediction journal, the purpose of reviewing missed forecasts is therefore not to punish every incorrect prediction. It is to identify whether the forecast failed because the evidence was poor, the question was framed badly, important information arrived later, or because an unlikely event simply occurred. That distinction is essential for improving confidence calibration rather than merely becoming more cautious. Why wrong does not always mean badly judged A prediction should be evaluated against the information that existed when it was made, not against information revealed afterwards. This principle is sometimes called evaluating the process rather than the outcome. Consider two forecasts: A prediction given a 70% probability fails. A prediction given a 99% probability fails. The first may represent perfectly reasonable judgement. If many independent 70% predictions are made, roughly three out of ten should fail. The second deserves much closer examination because the stated confidence implied that very little uncertainty remained. This distinction is closely related to the difference between accuracy and calibration . Good calibration accepts that uncertain events will sometimes go the \"wrong\" way. Poor calibration usually appears as confidence that was too high, too low, or insufficiently responsive to new evidence. Research from forecasting tournaments such as the Good Judgment Project consistently emphasises continual feedback and recalibration rather than judging forecasters solely by isolated successes or failures. [Wharton Faculty Platform+2Good Judgment] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu 2015 superforecasters In this article, we describe the best performing strategy of the winning research program: the GJP. To preempt confusion, we... The opposite mistake is outcome bias : judging a decision purely because the result was good or bad. A poor decision can produce a lucky success, while an excellent decision can end with an unfavourable outcome simply because uncertainty was genuine. Daniel Kahneman has repeatedly argued that outcome bias systematically rewards luck and punishes sound reasoning. [Air Force] af.mil nobel laureate gives important decision making tips Air ForceNobel Laureate gives important decision making tips23 Apr 2013 — This leads to another bias he called the \"outcome bias.\" This m... Five failure types to look for in reviews Instead of asking only \"Was I wrong?\", a prediction review becomes much more useful when it classifies why the forecast missed. 1. Bad evidence Sometimes the available information genuinely pointed in the wrong direction because sources were unreliable, incomplete or misunderstood. Questions to ask include: Which evidence turned out to be misleading? Did I rely on a single source instead of independent confirmation? Were stronger base rates available but ignored? The lesson is usually to improve information gathering rather than to reduce confidence across every future prediction. 2. Bad framing Many forecasting errors begin before any evidence is collected because the question itself was poorly specified. Examples include: predicting something too vaguely to resolve clearly; combining several independent events into one prediction; failing to define success before the outcome. Prediction journals work best when every forecast has explicit resolution criteria written in advance, preventing later reinterpretation. [Review] How to Decide: Simple Tools for Making Better Choices (Annie Duke) Summarized Channel: 9Natree Open on YouTube 3. Slow updating Some forecasts start reasonably but remain unchanged after meaningful new information appears. Review questions include: When did important evidence become available? Did I notice it promptly? If I noticed it, why did my probability remain unchanged? Research on high performing forecasters consistently finds that they revise probabilities incrementally as evidence accumulates rather than treating forecasts as positions to defend. [Good Judgment+2Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentGood Judgment: See the future sooner with SuperforecastingReports that Superforecasters were 30% more accurate than intellig... 4. Overconfidence or underconfidence The prediction itself may have pointed in roughly the correct direction while the assigned confidence was poorly matched to reality. Examples include: assigning 95% confidence where 70% was more appropriate; repeatedly using probabilities clustered around 50% because of excessive caution; treating complex events as virtually certain. Modern calibration research suggests that confidence errors vary across tasks rather than always taking the form of overconfidence . On difficult questions people often become too certain, while on easier questions they may even become underconfident. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryThe effect of calibration training on...Sep 7, 2024 — Results indicate that the training shifted analyst bias toward... 5. Reasonable uncertainty Some forecasts simply lose because low probability events happen. If an event assigned a 20% probability occurs, the prediction is not automatically poor. Across many similar predictions, one in five such events should happen eventually. The review should therefore ask: Was the event genuinely foreseeable? Was its probability underestimated? Or did a legitimate tail event simply occur? Treating every surprise as evidence of incompetence eventually encourages unrealistic certainty or excessive timidity—both forms of poor calibration. How hindsight bias distorts reviews [Hindsight bias] Wikipedia Hindsight bias does more than create embarrassment. It changes memory itself. Researchers distinguish several related effects: Memory distortion: remembering that you assigned a probability closer to the true outcome than you actually did. Inevitability: believing the outcome \"had to happen.\" Foreseeability: believing you personally could easily have predicted it. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Hindsight bias Hindsight bias These distortions interfere directly with learning because they erase the gap between what was genuinely known beforehand and what became obvious afterwards. This is one reason written prediction journals outperform retrospective reflection alone. A dated record fixes the original evidence, confidence level and assumptions before memory begins reconstructing them. The Hindsight Bias: Action The Illusion of Predictability Channel: Explified Labs Open on YouTube Conducting a review without rewriting history A useful review follows the timeline rather than the outcome. A practical sequence is: 1. Read the original prediction exactly as written. 2. Ignore the eventual outcome initially. 3. Reconstruct what information was genuinely available at that time. 4. Identify which assumptions proved correct and which failed. 5. Ask whether a different probability—not merely a different answer—would have been more appropriate. 6. Record one concrete change to future forecasting practice . Notice that the final step concerns improving the forecasting process rather than defending or condemning the original judgement. Questions that produce better learning Certain review questions encourage calibration, while others mainly encourage self criticism. Helpful questions include: Which assumption contributed most to the error? Which evidence deserved greater weight? Which evidence did I overweight? What information would genuinely have changed my mind at the time? Did I confuse confidence with conviction? If I faced the identical evidence tomorrow, would I assign a different probability? Less useful questions include: \"How could I have missed something so obvious?\" \"Why didn't I know the"},{"title":"Probability Words","url":"/probability-words/","description":"Numbers make confidence testable in a way that words like likely, doubtful, or probably cannot.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Calibration","level":3,"text":"Probability Words Why Probably Is Not Precise Enough Probability Words Probability Words Why Probably Is Not Precise Enough Numbers make confidence testable in a way that words like likely, doubtful, or probably cannot. Numbers make confidence testable in a way that words like likely, doubtful, or probably cannot. mechanism Why vague confidence words hide disagreement How to choose practical probability ranges When 60 percent and 85 percent mean different beliefs improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration probability words Introduction Confidence calibration improves when you stop saying “probably”, “unlikely” or “I’m fairly sure” and instead assign explicit probabilities . Numbers force you to state how strongly you believe a claim and make it possible to compare that belief with what eventually happens. If you record that an event has a 70% chance of occurring, you can later ask whether events you rated around 70% actually happened about seven times out of ten. Vague language cannot be tested in the same way because different people often attach very different meanings to the same words. Research in psychology, forecasting and intelligence analysis consistently shows that numerical probabilities reduce ambiguity, improve communication and create better feedback for learning. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Words or Numbers? Communicating Probability in...July 23, 2020 — 8 Apr 2020 — We review the benefits and drawbacks of this approach, drawing on psychologi... Published: July 23, 2020 Within a prediction journal, replacing verbal confidence with numbers is one of the simplest changes that produces measurable improvement. It turns confidence from a feeling into something that can be evaluated, criticised and refined over time. Why vague confidence words hide disagreement Words that sound precise are often interpreted very differently by different people. If two colleagues both describe a project as \"likely\", one may mean there is roughly a 60% chance of success while the other imagines something closer to 90%. They appear to agree while holding substantially different beliefs. Studies of linguistic probability repeatedly find wide variation in how people interpret common expressions such as \"likely\", \"possible\" and \"unlikely\". Context matters as well. The same word can receive different numerical interpretations depending on whether it refers to a medical diagnosis, a business forecast or an intelligence assessment. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. This ambiguity creates several practical problems. False agreement. People think they share the same expectation when they do not. Difficult accountability. After the outcome is known, almost any verbal prediction can be reinterpreted. Poor feedback. You cannot easily evaluate whether \"probably\" was appropriately confident because no measurable prediction was ever recorded. Hidden overconfidence . Strong emotional conviction can hide behind soft language that sounds appropriately cautious. Numerical probabilities expose these differences immediately. Saying \"I think there is a 62% chance\" communicates a noticeably different judgement from saying \"I think there is an 88% chance\", even though both might otherwise have been described simply as \"likely\". Bias Variance Trade off Explained Channel: DataMListic · Views: 6.6K · Uploaded: February 2023 · Length: 4 minutes 47 seconds Open on YouTube How to choose practical probability ranges The goal is not to pretend you can estimate every probability with scientific precision. Instead, probabilities should express the strength of your current belief as honestly as possible. Many experienced forecasters avoid assigning arbitrary decimal precision unless they genuinely believe it is meaningful. Practical ranges often communicate uncertainty more faithfully than pretending to distinguish between, for example, 73% and 74%. A useful working scale is: ProbabilityPractical interpretation50%Evidence is balanced; either outcome seems equally plausible.60%Slightly more likely than not, but substantial doubt remains.70%A clear favourite, though failure is still common.80%Strong expectation with meaningful uncertainty remaining.90%Very confident, reserved for unusually strong evidence.95% or aboveShould be used sparingly because errors become especially informative. Some people prefer fixed probability bands such as 5%, 20%, 40%, 60%, 80% and 95%. Others use increments of five percentage points. The exact system matters less than using it consistently enough that your forecasts can later be compared with reality. When evidence is weak, saying \"I don't know\" often corresponds to assigning a probability near 50%, not because both outcomes are equally attractive, but because your available information does not justify greater confidence. When 60% and 85% mean different beliefs The difference between 60% and 85% is much larger than it first appears. A 60% prediction says that failure is expected almost two times in five. Someone hearing that forecast should prepare seriously for both outcomes. An 85% prediction implies failure should occur only about once in every six or seven similar cases. Acting as though these two probabilities express the same confidence leads to poor decisions. Imagine three project managers estimating whether a software release will meet its deadline. Manager A: 60% Manager B: 75% Manager C: 90% All three might casually say the release will \"probably\" ship on time. Yet their expectations are dramatically different. Manager C is effectively saying that missing the deadline would be an unusual surprise, while Manager A expects delays to remain entirely plausible. Recording numerical probabilities makes these differences visible before events unfold instead of allowing everyone to remember having held the \"correct\" opinion afterwards. Words of estimative probability Channel: WikiAudio Open on YouTube Why prediction journals benefit from numbers A prediction journal works because it creates repeated opportunities for calibration. Suppose you record one hundred forecasts over a year. Predictions around 60% should succeed roughly six times out of ten. Predictions around 80% should succeed roughly eight times out of ten. Predictions around 95% should almost always succeed. If your 90% predictions fail one third of the time, you are systematically overconfident. If your 60% predictions succeed almost every time, you may be underconfident. Numerical forecasts reveal these patterns in a way verbal forecasts never can. This is also why scoring methods such as the Brier score evaluate probabilistic forecasts rather than simple right or wrong answers. They reward assigning probabilities that accurately reflect real uncertainty instead of expressing excessive certainty. [Harvard App Extension] appext.hks.harvard.edu 1964 1994 found that 96 percent of key judgments expressed probability in ways that...Read more... Lessons from fields that depend on uncertainty Several professions have adopted numerical probability systems because misunderstanding uncertainty can have serious consequences. Intelligence organisations have spent decades studying \"words of estimative probability\" after discovering that policymakers interpreted expressions such as \"likely\" and \"probable\" very differently. Numerous organisations now pair verbal expressions with numerical ranges or encourage direct numerical estimates to reduce misunderstanding. [Gwern+2Sage Journals] gwern.net Verbal Probability Expressions In National Intelligence Verbal Probability Expressions In National Intelligence...May 12, 2008 — 2 May 2008 — Sherman Kent's classic work “Words of Estimat... Published: May 12, 2008 Climate science follows a similar approach. Reports by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) attach explicit probability ranges to phrases such as \"very likely\" or \"likely\" so that readers share a common interpretation rather than relying on personal intuition . [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Words of estimative probability Words of estimative probability These examples illustrate a broader principle: whenever important decisions depend on uncertain information, standardised probabilities communicate more reliably than ordinary language alone. Practical habits that improve probability estimates Using numerical probabilities becomes easier with regular practice . Before writing a forecast, ask what probability would genuinely surprise you if the opposite occurred. Avoid defaulting to 50% simply because estimating feels difficult; use it only when evidence is genuinely balanced or insufficient. Reserve probabilities above about 95% for situations where you would be genuinely shocked to be wrong. Review resolved predictions in groups rather than individually so you can detect systematic overconfidence or underconfidence. When discussing forecasts with others, ask them to state their own numerical estimate before"},{"title":"Scorable Questions","url":"/scorable-questions/","description":"A good prediction journal starts with questions that are specific enough to resolve and narrow enough to teach you something.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Calibration","level":3,"text":"Scorable Questions Can Your Forecast Actually Be Scored? Scorable Questions Scorable Questions Can Your Forecast Actually Be Scored? A good prediction journal starts with questions that are specific enough to resolve and narrow enough to teach you something. A good prediction journal starts with questions that are specific enough to resolve and narrow enough to teach you something. mechanism What makes a prediction resolvable Turning vague claims into clear questions Common framing mistakes that ruin feedback improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration scorable questions Introduction A prediction journal only teaches you anything if your forecasts can later be judged against reality. That means every forecast should answer a question with a clear resolution, a defined deadline, and an outcome that independent observers would agree on. Vague predictions such as \"the economy will struggle\" or \"this project will probably succeed\" may feel insightful, but they generate little useful feedback because there is no objective way to determine whether they were correct. Research from forecasting tournaments led by Philip Tetlock and colleagues consistently relies on carefully specified, resolvable questions because accurate scoring is the foundation of calibration and improvement. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu 2015 superforecasters Wharton Faculty Platform2015 superforecasters.pdf Wharton Faculty PlatformMay 18, 2015 — by B Mellers · 2015 · Cited by 332 — Brier s... Published: May 18, 2015 Writing scorable questions is therefore not administrative detail. It is the mechanism that turns intuition into measurable performance. The clearer the question, the easier it becomes to compare your confidence with reality, identify patterns of overconfidence or underconfidence, and improve future judgement. What makes a prediction resolvable? A forecast is resolvable when a future observer can answer it without debating what the predictor \"really meant\". Good forecasting platforms and research projects impose strict resolution criteria for exactly this reason. [Commoncog] commoncog.com How Do You Evaluate Your Own Predictions? How Do You Evaluate Your Own Predictions?December 16, 2019 — 17 Dec 2019 — This post provides a comprehensive summary of the tec... Published: December 16, 2019 A well written forecast normally contains five elements: A single outcome. Ask one question at a time rather than combining several claims. A precise deadline. State exactly when the forecast stops being updated and when it will be judged. An observable criterion. The answer should depend on publicly observable facts or clearly defined evidence. Binary resolution whenever possible. \"Yes\" or \"No\" questions are much easier to score than subjective assessments. A stable resolution source. Decide in advance which source will determine the outcome if there is disagreement. For example: Poor question Will artificial intelligence transform education? Problems: No deadline. \"Transform\" is undefined. Multiple interpretations are possible. Scorable version Will at least 30% of secondary schools in England report using generative AI tools in regular classroom instruction by 31 December 2028, according to the annual Department for Education survey? Even if the prediction proves difficult, everyone knows how it will be resolved. Turning vague claims into clear questions Many forecasting errors begin before probabilities are assigned. The question itself is too broad to generate meaningful feedback. A useful way to rewrite forecasts is to identify four components: Vague statementBetter forecasting questionInflation will stay high.Will annual UK CPI inflation exceed 4% in the Office for National Statistics release for December 2026?The company will do well.Will Company X report positive year on year revenue growth in its FY2027 annual report?This product launch will succeed.Will Product X sell more than one million units worldwide within six months of release?The negotiations will fail.Will the parties publicly announce that negotiations have ended without a signed agreement before 1 October 2026? Notice that each revision introduces measurable thresholds, a deadline, and an identifiable source. This approach also forces you to think about what evidence would genuinely change your mind. If you cannot describe what success or failure looks like, you are probably forecasting an impression rather than an event. Introducing a New Forecast Question Type: Conditional Pairs Channel: Metaculus Open on YouTube Choose questions that teach you something Not every scorable question is equally valuable. Forecasting research suggests concentrating on questions that are uncertain but answerable rather than either obvious or effectively unknowable. Tetlock describes this as working in the \"Goldilocks zone\": difficult enough that judgement matters, but not so uncertain that evidence cannot discriminate between better and worse forecasts. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentTen Commandments for Aspiring SuperforecastersThese commandments describe behaviors that have been “experimentally demonstra... Questions are especially useful when they: involve decisions you regularly make; repeat often enough to build a track record; depend on evidence you can observe before resolution; encourage updating rather than one off guessing. For example, someone managing software projects might repeatedly forecast delivery dates, customer renewals, or hiring outcomes . After dozens of such forecasts, patterns become visible that would never emerge from isolated predictions. Define the resolution rule before making the forecast One of the easiest ways to corrupt a prediction journal is to change the scoring rules after the outcome is known. Write the resolution criteria before assigning a probability. A simple template is: Question: Will Event X occur? Resolution date: 30 September 2027. Resolution source: Organisation Y's official report. Resolves Yes if: Conditions A and B are both satisfied. Resolves No if: The deadline passes without those conditions. Resolves Void if: The specified report is permanently cancelled. Precommitting to these rules prevents hindsight bias and motivated reinterpretation. Forecasting tournaments use similar procedures because disagreements about wording can otherwise overwhelm meaningful evaluation. [Commoncog] commoncog.com How Do You Evaluate Your Own Predictions? How Do You Evaluate Your Own Predictions?December 16, 2019 — 17 Dec 2019 — This post provides a comprehensive summary of the tec... Published: December 16, 2019 Common framing mistakes that ruin feedback Several recurring mistakes make forecasts difficult or impossible to score. Bundling multiple predictions together Questions like: Will inflation fall, unemployment remain low, and interest rates be cut? contain three separate forecasts. One may be correct while the others fail. Instead, create three independent questions. Using subjective language Words such as: successful major significant dramatic soon reliable usually require interpretation. Replace them with measurable quantities, dates, or explicit thresholds. 371 :: Improve Your Decision Making with Your Own Brier Score Channel: Bradley Hartmann & Co. Open on YouTube Leaving the deadline open Predictions without end dates eventually become impossible to falsify. \"Bitcoin will exceed $200,000.\" Compared with: \"Will Bitcoin close above US$200,000 on any trading day before 31 December 2028?\" The second version can actually be resolved. Allowing moving goalposts Avoid writing questions that permit changing definitions after new information appears. For example: The project counts as successful if management seems happy. Instead, specify measurable criteria before the project finishes. Predicting trends rather than events Forecasts like: Public opinion will shift. are difficult to score. Convert them into measurable outcomes: Will support for Policy X exceed 55% in the next nationally representative YouGov poll conducted before 1 June 2027? Build questions that work with probabilistic scoring Scorable questions also make statistical evaluation possible. Measures such as the Brier score compare the probability you assigned with the actual outcome. These scoring rules reward honest probability estimates rather than exaggerated confidence. However, they only work when each forecast has a clearly defined outcome that eventually resolves as true or false. [Wikipedia+2Corporate Research Forum] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score For example: 80% probability that an event occurs. Event happens. Forecast receives a relatively good score. If the same event fails to occur, the high confidence prediction receives a much worse score, providing useful feedback about calibration rather than simply whether the prediction was right. Without unambiguous questions, that feedback loop breaks down. How to Enter What a Winning Forecast Actually Looks"},{"title":"What To Predict","url":"/what-to-predict/","description":"The most useful journal entries are concrete, repeated predictions about decisions where better calibration would change behavior.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Calibration","level":3,"text":"What To Predict The Predictions Worth Writing Down What To Predict What To Predict The Predictions Worth Writing Down The most useful journal entries are concrete, repeated predictions about decisions where better calibration would change behavior. The most useful journal entries are concrete, repeated predictions about decisions where better calibration would change behavior. population context Why repeated small forecasts beat rare dramatic ones Work, research, planning, and external event examples How to avoid obvious or unscorable questions improving your thinking and analytical skills calibration what to predict Introduction A prediction journal improves judgement only if it captures the kinds of forecasts that can change future decisions. The most valuable entries are not dramatic guesses about elections, pandemics or technological breakthroughs. They are repeated, concrete forecasts about situations you encounter often enough to learn from: project deadlines, hiring decisions, research hypotheses, customer behaviour, negotiations or personal planning. Repetition creates feedback , and feedback allows calibration—bringing your confidence into better alignment with reality. Research on forecasting consistently shows that improvement comes from making clear probabilistic predictions, reviewing outcomes and updating beliefs, not from occasionally being right about spectacular events. [Good Judgment+2Wiley Online Library] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... The Predictions Worth Writing Down A useful prediction journal asks one question repeatedly: \"If I become better at forecasting this type of event, will I make better decisions?\" Many possible predictions fail this test. Guessing tomorrow's lottery numbers, celebrity news or isolated political surprises may be entertaining, but they rarely improve the choices you make. By contrast, forecasting recurring decisions lets you discover systematic patterns in your thinking. Good prediction topics usually have four characteristics: They occur regularly enough to generate many observations. The outcome can be resolved objectively. The prediction influences an actual decision. Similar situations arise again, allowing lessons to transfer. Forecasting researchers emphasise that skill develops through repeated practice with feedback rather than isolated one off judgements. Forecasting tournaments intentionally present participants with many questions because calibration improves through accumulation rather than memorable anecdotes . [Good Judgment+2PMC] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... Why Repeated Small Forecasts Beat Rare Dramatic Ones Large, unusual events create poor learning environments. They happen infrequently, involve many uncontrollable variables and often cannot be compared with previous experiences. Even if your prediction turns out correct, you may not know whether it reflected genuine insight or luck. Small recurring forecasts provide much richer feedback. Instead of asking: \"Will artificial general intelligence arrive before 2035?\" consider asking: \"Will this machine learning experiment improve validation accuracy by at least 2%?\" \"Will the reviewer request major revisions?\" \"Will this feature reduce support tickets within one month?\" Instead of: \"Will the economy enter recession next year?\" consider: \"Will our sales pipeline convert at least 18% of qualified leads this quarter?\" \"Will procurement costs exceed our budget by more than 5%?\" After dozens of similar predictions, patterns emerge. You may discover that you are consistently overconfident about deadlines, overly pessimistic about customer adoption or too optimistic when evaluating your own ideas. Those discoveries directly improve future planning. Philip Tetlock's work distinguishes forecasting skill from isolated prediction success. Reliable judgement comes from repeated probabilistic estimation, continual updating and disciplined evaluation rather than occasional impressive forecasts. [Wharton Faculty Platform+2econtalk.org] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu Wharton Faculty Platform Expert In the long run (...Read more... Expert Political Judgment: How Good Is It? How Can We Know? Philip Tetlock Talks at Google Channel: Talks at Google Open on YouTube Focus on Decisions You Actually Control Prediction journals become more valuable when forecasts support governance of your own work rather than passive observation. Useful categories include: Project management Will this milestone finish before Friday? Will integration testing uncover more than five critical bugs? Will the supplier deliver within the agreed window? These forecasts improve scheduling, contingency planning and resource allocation. [ Research ] researchgate.net PDF) Superforecasting: The Art and Science of Prediction 5 Jul 2016 — This is an excellent book to read. It is not only informative, as it should be for a book on forecasting, but it is highly e... Researchers can forecast whether an experiment will replicate, whether data collection will finish on time or whether a hypothesis will survive analysis. Recording confidence before seeing results reduces hindsight bias and encourages honest evaluation. Professional judgement Managers can estimate: whether a candidate will accept an offer, whether a proposal will be approved, whether a client will renew, whether estimated effort will exceed actual effort. Because these decisions recur frequently, forecasting exposes persistent biases. Personal planning Examples include: finishing a report before a deadline, maintaining an exercise routine for four weeks, completing reading goals, keeping travel within budget. Although these predictions concern personal behaviour, they remain measurable and directly affect future planning. Include External Events That Affect Decisions Not every useful forecast concerns your own actions. External events deserve attention when they influence meaningful decisions. Examples include: Will inflation exceed a threshold that changes investment strategy? Will a regulator publish new guidance before contract negotiations? Will a competitor release a product before your planned launch? Will interest rates remain unchanged before refinancing? These questions connect outside uncertainty to concrete choices. Their purpose is not to demonstrate world knowledge but to improve decision timing, contingency planning and resource allocation. Forecasting organisations often focus on clearly defined external events because they produce objective feedback while encouraging careful probability estimates instead of binary certainty. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... Sharpening Judgment: Treating Decisions as Forecasts Kingbridge Insights Series Episode 6 Channel: The Kingbridge Centre Open on YouTube How to Avoid Questions That Teach You Nothing Many prediction journals gradually become collections of interesting trivia. Several warning signs indicate that a question is unlikely to improve judgement. Avoid questions that are: [gjopen.com] gjopen.com t by 100 so that your probabilities range between 0 (0...Read more... Too vague. \"Will this project succeed?\" leaves too much room for reinterpretation. Impossible to resolve. \"Will people eventually appreciate this idea?\" has no obvious endpoint. Dependent on hidden definitions. Everyone should know exactly what counts as success or failure. Extremely rare. Learning from a prediction that resolves once every decade is painfully slow. Pure entertainment. If the answer would not change future behaviour, it adds little value. A good forecasting question specifies: [goodjudgment.com] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... the event, the deadline, the resolution rule, the probability, and the information available when the prediction was made. Clear definitions make later evaluation fair and prevent subconscious reinterpretation after the outcome is known. Forecasting practice consistently stresses explicit time horizons and operational definitions because ambiguity weakens learning. [Summrize] summrize.com Superforecasting by Philip Tetlock Book Superforecasting by Philip Tetlock Book SummarySpecify clear time horizons and definitions for all forecast questions; Track pred... Build Families of Similar Forecasts Individual predictions matter less than collections of related ones. Instead of recording one forecast about software delivery, build a category: release dates, bug counts, estimation accuracy, customer adoption, post release defects. Likewise, a"},{"title":"Confirmation Bias","url":"/confirmation-bias/","description":"Confirmation bias makes people seek support for a first answer instead of seriously testing alternatives.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Confirmation Bias Are You Protecting Your First Answer? Confirmation Bias Confirmation Bias Are You Protecting Your First Answer? Confirmation bias makes people seek support for a first answer instead of seriously testing alternatives. Confirmation bias makes people seek support for a first answer instead of seriously testing alternatives. mechanism How confirmation bias appears Common decision settings Counterevidence habits improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias Introduction Confirmation bias in personal decisions is the habit of protecting an early answer instead of seriously testing whether it is right. It appears when you search for information that supports what you already want to do, trust friendly evidence more than awkward evidence, or explain away warning signs because changing course would feel embarrassing, costly or socially difficult. In the wider project of improving your thinking and analytical skills , this matters because many poor decisions do not begin with ignorance. They begin with a plausible first impression that becomes emotionally easier to defend than to examine. The core risk is not that people deliberately lie to themselves. It is that ordinary reasoning can become selective: attention, memory, interpretation and information search all start bending towards the option that already feels acceptable. Nickerson’s influential review describes confirmation bias as seeking or interpreting evidence in ways that are partial to existing beliefs, expectations or a current hypothesis, and notes that it appears across practical contexts rather than only in laboratory puzzles. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu nickerson Confirmation Bias UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12282 — Confirmation bias, as t... How Confirmation Bias Appears Confirmation bias is easiest to spot in other people’s opinions, but it is most useful to notice in your own decisions before they harden. A person thinking about accepting a job may over read every good sign about the employer and treat bad Glassdoor reviews as written by unusually bitter former staff. Someone considering a relationship may interpret inconsiderate behaviour as stress , banter or a one off because they already want the relationship to work. An investor may keep reading bullish commentary after buying a stock and avoid analysts who challenge the original thesis. The bias can operate through several small moves rather than one dramatic mistake: Selective search: looking for “why this is a good idea” instead of “when does this fail?” Selective interpretation: treating supporting evidence as realistic and opposing evidence as exaggerated, irrelevant or biased. Selective memory: remembering the examples that fit the preferred story while forgetting near misses and inconvenient exceptions. Selective standards: demanding stronger proof from the alternative than from the favourite option. The classic lesson from confirmation bias research is that people often do not test a favoured hypothesis against the strongest alternative. Klayman and Ha’s work on hypothesis testing argues that people frequently use a “positive test strategy”: they examine cases where they expect their hypothesis to hold. This is not always irrational, because in many real situations it can be informative. But it becomes dangerous when a person only checks whether their favourite story can be supported, rather than asking what observation would distinguish it from a rival explanation . [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu According to this strategy, you.Read more UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — We propose that many phenom ena of human hypoth... Published: October 7, 2004 That distinction matters for personal decisions. “Can I find reasons to do this?” is a much easier question than “Is this still the best option after comparing the strongest reasons not to do it?” The first question protects momentum. The second question tests judgement. Why the First Answer Becomes Protected A first answer often arrives with a feeling attached to it. It may promise relief, status, belonging, certainty or escape from an unpleasant trade off. Once that feeling is in place, counterevidence can become psychologically costly. It does not merely challenge an option; it threatens the story that made the option attractive. This is where confirmation bias overlaps with motivated reasoning. In practical terms, motivated reasoning means that the preferred conclusion affects how strictly evidence is judged. Nickerson’s review notes that people may treat evidence in biased ways when they are motivated to defend beliefs they want to maintain, while also stressing that this need not be deliberate or conscious. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu nickerson Confirmation Bias UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12282 — Confirmation bias, as t... A useful concrete example comes from Lord, Ross and Lepper’s 1979 study on biased assimilation. Participants with opposing views on capital punishment reviewed mixed evidence. The study is widely cited because people tended to accept evidence that supported their prior view more readily and scrutinise opposing evidence more harshly, leaving some participants more convinced of their original position after seeing the same mixed material. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Frank Baumgartner Biased Assimilation and Attitude Polarization: The Effects Frank Baumgartner Biased Assimilation and Attitude Polarization: The Effects The personal decision version is quieter but familiar. After choosing a direction, the mind starts acting less like a judge and more like a defence lawyer. It asks, “How can this still be true?” rather than “What would I believe if I had not already chosen?” That shift is especially likely when the decision is tied to identity: being a loyal friend, a clever investor, a good parent, a resilient employee, a romantic optimist, or someone who does not “give up”. Common Decision Settings Confirmation bias is not equally dangerous in every choice. It matters most when the preferred answer is emotionally or socially easier than the more accurate one. Routine decisions usually do not need elaborate debiasing . High stakes, ambiguous and identity laden decisions do. Career and work choices Career decisions create ideal conditions for confirmation bias because evidence is incomplete and the stakes feel personal. Before accepting a role, staying in a failing project or backing a strategy, people can become attached to the version of themselves implied by the decision: ambitious, loyal, brave, indispensable or finally ready for change. In organisations, this can become collective. Kahneman, Lovallo and Sibony argue that major strategic choices are vulnerable to biases including confirmation bias, and recommend decision processes that force reviewers to look for contrary evidence before commitment. [DenkProducties] denkproducties.nl Denk Productiesdaniel kahneman hbr before you make that big decision Denk Productiesdaniel kahneman hbr before you make that big decision The same principle applies at a personal scale. A jobseeker who only asks “Why is this a great opportunity?” is likely to miss the sharper question: “What would make me regret accepting this six months from now?” Health decisions Health choices can be especially vulnerable because fear and hope both distort evidence. A patient may search for stories that confirm a feared diagnosis, or for testimonials that support a treatment they already want to believe in. Clinicians are also vulnerable: medical bias reviews describe confirmation bias as one factor in diagnostic error, where early impressions can shape the interpretation of later symptoms or tests. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical lesson is not “distrust doctors” or “distrust yourself”. It is to separate the preferred explanation from the diagnostic question. In health decisions, useful counterevidence habits include asking what else could explain the symptoms, what finding would change the plan, and whether a second opinion is warranted when the decision is serious, uncertain or irreversible. 12 Cognitive Biases Explained How to Think Better and More Logically Removing Bias Channel: Practical Psychology Open on YouTube Money and investing Personal finance gives confirmation bias endless material because markets produce constant commentary. After buying an asset, a person can choose a media diet that validates the purchase: optimistic forecasts, like minded forums, selective charts and confident narratives. Investment education sources commonly warn that confirmation bias can lead investors to favour information that supports existing views and ignore contradictory data. [Investopedia] investopedia.com How Confirmation Bias"},{"title":"Health Doubt","url":"/health-doubt/","description":"Fear or hope can make one explanation feel obvious, so serious health decisions need clear counterevidence questions and second-opinion triggers.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Confirmation Bias","level":3,"text":"Health Doubt When Symptoms Seem to Confirm Your Fear Health Doubt Health Doubt When Symptoms Seem to Confirm Your Fear Fear or hope can make one explanation feel obvious, so serious health decisions need clear counterevidence questions and second opinion triggers. Fear or hope can make one explanation feel obvious, so serious health decisions need clear counterevidence questions and second opinion triggers. population context How early diagnoses shape later evidence Questions that reopen the differential When uncertainty justifies another opinion improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias health doubt Introduction When a new symptom appears, the mind naturally searches for a story that explains it. That first explanation may be frightening (“this must be cancer”), reassuring (“it's only stress ”), or simply familiar (“I always get migraines”). The danger is not having an initial idea—it is becoming too confident in it before the evidence justifies that confidence. This is confirmation bias applied to health: once a diagnosis feels right, people often notice information that supports it while discounting signs that point elsewhere. The result can be unnecessary anxiety, delayed treatment, or persistence with an incorrect explanation even as new evidence emerges. Research on diagnostic reasoning shows that both patients and clinicians are vulnerable to cognitive shortcuts, particularly when working under uncertainty or time pressure . PMC+2Royal College of Physicians of Edinburgh [pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive biases in diagnosis and decision making during...by CS Webster · 2021 · Cited by 86 — Cognitive bias is typically defined a... Within the broader topic of confirmation bias in personal decisions, health is especially important because symptoms are emotionally charged. Fear can make serious diseases seem more likely than they are, while hope can make reassuring explanations seem more convincing than the evidence supports. How an early diagnosis shapes later evidence An initial diagnosis often becomes an anchor for everything that follows. Once a person thinks, \"I know what this is,\" new symptoms are interpreted through that lens. A headache becomes proof of a brain tumour after reading alarming stories online, or persistent fatigue is repeatedly attributed to work stress despite new warning signs. Psychologists describe this as a combination of confirmation bias, anchoring and premature closure . Anchoring occurs when early information receives disproportionate weight. Premature closure happens when the search for alternative explanations stops too soon. Confirmation bias then reinforces the process by favouring evidence that supports the original conclusion while overlooking conflicting information. These biases are well documented in medical decision making and are recognised contributors to diagnostic error. PMC+2Royal College of Physicians of Edinburgh [pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive biases in diagnosis and decision making during...by CS Webster · 2021 · Cited by 86 — Cognitive bias is typically defined a... This pattern affects patients as well as clinicians. A patient who strongly believes they have one condition may selectively report symptoms that fit their theory or search online using highly specific terms that reinforce it. Equally, a clinician who forms an early impression may unconsciously ask questions that confirm rather than challenge that impression, especially when consultations are brief or information arrives in a particular order. [American Medical Association+2catalogofbias.org] ama assn.org Learn with the AMA about strategies to neutralize them American Medical Association4 widespread cognitive biases and how doctors can...4 Feb 2021 — Cognitive biases can cause diagnostic and t... Why fear and reassurance both distort judgement Confirmation bias does not only make people imagine the worst. It can also make them dismiss genuine problems. Fear driven bias may involve: interpreting common symptoms as evidence of rare illnesses; giving excessive weight to dramatic personal stories found online; treating every new sensation as further confirmation of a feared diagnosis. Reassurance driven bias works in the opposite direction: assuming symptoms are \"just getting older\"; repeatedly attributing recurring symptoms to stress without reconsideration; ignoring changes because they conflict with a preferred explanation. Neither direction reflects objective reasoning. Instead, emotional preferences influence how evidence is collected and interpreted. Research on motivated reasoning suggests that people tend to apply lower standards of evidence to conclusions they want to believe and higher standards to conclusions they would rather avoid. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive biases in diagnosis and decision making during...by CS Webster · 2021 · Cited by 86 — Cognitive bias is typically defined a... Cognitive Biases Doctors Should Watch Out For Pitfalls in Decision Making Channel: Dr Ollie Burton Open on YouTube Questions that reopen the differential A useful safeguard against confirmation bias is deliberately reopening the differential diagnosis—the list of plausible alternative explanations rather than assuming the first answer is correct. Instead of asking, \"What confirms my diagnosis?\", ask questions such as: What findings would make this explanation less likely? What common conditions could produce the same symptoms? Have any new symptoms appeared that no longer fit my original explanation? If someone else presented exactly these symptoms, would I reach the same conclusion? Am I relying mainly on internet anecdotes rather than clinical evidence? These questions do not require medical expertise. Their purpose is to interrupt the natural tendency to defend an initial conclusion and instead encourage genuine comparison between competing explanations. Medical education increasingly teaches similar reflective strategies. Studies suggest that structured reflection—actively considering alternative diagnoses and examining evidence against one's first impression—can improve diagnostic reasoning, particularly in difficult cases. American Medical Association+2Royal College of Physicians of Edinburgh [ama assn.org] ama assn.org Learn with the AMA about strategies to neutralize them American Medical Association4 widespread cognitive biases and how doctors can...4 Feb 2021 — Cognitive biases can cause diagnostic and t... How online information can strengthen false certainty Searching the internet for symptoms is not inherently harmful, but confirmation bias changes how people search. Someone worried about a neurological disease may repeatedly search combinations of symptoms that assume the feared illness is present. Search engines and social media can then present increasingly similar material, creating the impression that many independent sources support the same conclusion. Another problem is selective reading. People often: stop searching once they find a diagnosis that matches their fears or hopes; ignore information about how common alternative conditions are; pay greater attention to memorable personal stories than to population level evidence. Recent research on online self diagnosis also highlights how exposure to emotionally resonant health content can increase availability bias, making dramatic illnesses feel more probable simply because they are easier to remember. Confirmation bias then reinforces those impressions. [arXiv] arxiv.org Mining Evidence about Your Symptoms: Mitigating Availability Bias in Online Self DiagnosisJanuary 25, 2025... Published: January 25, 2025 Understanding and Preventing Cognitive Errors in Healthcare Channel: Marjorie Stiegler MD Open on YouTube When uncertainty justifies another opinion Seeking another medical opinion should not be viewed as rejecting professional expertise. It is often an appropriate response when genuine uncertainty remains. Situations where another opinion deserves consideration include: symptoms persist despite treatment based on the original diagnosis; important symptoms remain unexplained; different test results appear inconsistent; treatment carries significant risks or irreversible consequences; the diagnosis depends largely on subjective interpretation rather than objective findings; multiple reasonable diagnoses remain possible. Second opinions can reduce the influence of what researchers call previous opinion bias, where later clinicians are unintentionally influenced by earlier diagnostic labels. One practical recommendation is that the consulting clinician first evaluates the patient with as little leading information as practical before reviewing previous conclusions, allowing a more independent assessment. [catalogofbias.org] catalogofbias.org Previous opinion biasPrevious opinion bias describes how the diagnostic process may be influenced by an investigator who is aware of prev..."},{"title":"Job Regret","url":"/job-regret/","description":"Before accepting or staying in a role, the regret question forces hidden warnings into view before career momentum takes over.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Confirmation Bias","level":3,"text":"Job Regret What Would Make This Job Regrettable? Job Regret Job Regret What Would Make This Job Regrettable? Before accepting or staying in a role, the regret question forces hidden warnings into view before career momentum takes over. Before accepting or staying in a role, the regret question forces hidden warnings into view before career momentum takes over. population context Why career choices invite confirmation bias Signals jobseekers explain away A six month regret precheck improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias job regret Introduction Choosing whether to accept, reject or remain in a job is rarely a purely rational exercise. Career decisions affect income, identity , relationships, status and future opportunities, making them especially vulnerable to confirmation bias. Once a role starts to feel like \"the right move\", many people unconsciously begin collecting reasons to justify that conclusion while discounting warning signs. The regret question is a practical way to interrupt that process: If I look back six months after taking or staying in this job, what would make me wish I had decided differently? This question does not ask whether the decision will succeed. Instead, it deliberately searches for the circumstances that would prove your current enthusiasm misplaced. By shifting attention from reasons to believe to reasons to doubt, it helps expose evidence that confirmation bias would otherwise explain away. Research on judgment and decision making consistently shows that actively seeking disconfirming evidence improves the quality of important decisions compared with simply accumulating supporting arguments. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Why career choices invite confirmation bias Career decisions combine uncertainty with strong emotions. A new role may promise a higher salary, greater prestige, escape from a difficult manager or the feeling of finally moving forward. Those emotional rewards create an incentive to defend the preferred option before enough evidence has been gathered. Unlike many everyday decisions, the consequences also unfold slowly. Poor management, unrealistic workloads or limited development opportunities may not become obvious until months after joining. Because there is no immediate feedback, it is easy to mistake optimism for good judgment. Confirmation bias often appears through selective information gathering. Someone excited about a new employer may spend hours reading positive employee stories while dismissing repeated complaints as isolated grievances. During interviews they may interpret vague promises about progression as evidence of a strong career path because those promises fit the story they already hope is true. This reflects the broader finding that people naturally favour information consistent with an existing hypothesis unless they deliberately test competing explanations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Signals jobseekers explain away Many regretted career decisions are not caused by hidden information. They arise because visible warning signs were repeatedly reinterpreted in a favourable way. Common examples include: High staff turnover. \"The company is just growing quickly.\" Interviewers contradict each other. \"Everyone simply has a different perspective.\" Unclear reporting lines. \"They are probably flexible rather than disorganised.\" Repeated delays in recruitment. \"They must be very thorough.\" Pressure to accept immediately. \"They really value me.\" Poor work life balance described as passion. \"This is just what ambitious companies are like.\" None of these signals automatically means a role is unsuitable. The problem is the asymmetry in evaluation. Positive information is accepted at face value, while negative information requires extraordinary proof before it changes the preferred conclusion. The same pattern appears when deciding whether to remain in an existing role. Employees often reinterpret recurring frustrations as temporary because leaving would require admitting that years of investment may not produce the hoped for outcome. Confirmation bias can therefore reinforce both unnecessary job changes and unnecessarily prolonged stays. [JobCannon] jobcannon.io 8 Cognitive Biases That Derail Career Decisions (and How...April 6, 2026 — 6 Apr 2026 — Confirmation bias, sunk cost fallacy, a... Published: April 6, 2026 Using Regret Minimization to Make Better Decisions: Psychology of Regrets Channel: Jackson Kerchis Open on YouTube What the regret question changes The regret question deliberately reverses the usual direction of thinking. Instead of asking: \"Why is this a good opportunity?\" it asks: \"What future experience would make me wish I had recognised today's warning signs?\" That subtle shift matters because it encourages diagnostic rather than supportive evidence. For example, someone considering a management role might identify future regrets such as: discovering that promised autonomy never existed; spending most days dealing with office politics instead of meaningful work; accepting a salary increase that came with unsustainable hours; joining a team where experienced colleagues leave within months; finding that advancement depends more on internal alliances than performance. Once these possible regrets have been written down, each becomes a factual question that can be investigated before accepting the offer rather than rationalised afterwards. A six month regret precheck A useful way to counter confirmation bias is to imagine yourself exactly six months into the future. Assume the decision has turned out badly. Write a brief explanation of why. Then separate genuine risks from imagined fears. Ask questions such as: 1. What specific event made me regret accepting or staying? 2. Was that risk already visible before I decided? 3. What evidence could I gather today that would increase or decrease that concern? 4. Whom could I ask who has little incentive to sell me this role? 5. What answer would genuinely change my mind ? This approach resembles the \"pre mortem\" technique developed in organisational psychology, where decision makers imagine that a project has already failed and work backwards to identify plausible causes. Research suggests this helps reveal overlooked risks because it temporarily gives people permission to think critically about an option they otherwise feel committed to supporting. [Ahead App] ahead app.com Ahead AppSelf Awareness in Decision Making: Why Knowing Your Blind...This article introduces two game changing techniques—the pre mortem... The value lies not in predicting every possible problem but in identifying assumptions that deserve testing before commitment. A Decision Making Framework That Removes Regret Channel: Real Syllabus Open on YouTube Distinguishing realistic regret from anxious overthinking The regret question is not an invitation to imagine every unlikely disaster. Helpful regret questions focus on evidence that could realistically have been examined beforehand. Examples include management quality, workload expectations, turnover, role clarity, promotion practices and organisational culture. Less helpful questions focus on highly unpredictable events outside anyone's reasonable foresight, such as an unexpected recession or a sudden industry wide collapse. A useful distinction is: Preventable regret: \"I ignored evidence that was available.\" Unavoidable disappointment: \"Events changed in ways nobody could reasonably predict .\" The aim is to reduce preventable regret, not eliminate uncertainty. Practical habits that reduce confirmation bias before accepting a role The regret question becomes much more effective when paired with deliberate evidence gathering. Rather than asking only people who accepted similar roles successfully, seek conversations with people who left within a year. Compare interview promises with publicly available information about organisational performance and turnover. Ask interviewers what typically causes new hires to struggle rather than what makes people successful. Request concrete examples instead of general assurances. Another useful habit is to define in advance what evidence would make you reject the offer. If no possible answer could change your decision, then the decision has already become emotional rather than analytical. Research on debiasing consistently finds that structured decision processes outperform relying solely on intuition in high stakes choices because they encourage people to search for information that challenges rather than merely confirms their preferred conclusion. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. The goal is better judgment, not perfect certainty No decision framework can guarantee that a new job will prove rewarding. Organisations change, managers leave and priorities shift. The value of the regret question lies elsewhere. It transforms confirmation bias from an invisible"},{"title":"First Answer","url":"/first-answer/","description":"A promising first answer can turn into a protected story when relief, identity, or social cost makes contrary evidence feel threatening.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Confirmation Bias","level":3,"text":"First Answer Why the First Answer Starts Winning First Answer First Answer Why the First Answer Starts Winning A promising first answer can turn into a protected story when relief, identity, or social cost makes contrary evidence feel threatening. A promising first answer can turn into a protected story when relief, identity, or social cost makes contrary evidence feel threatening. mechanism The emotional pull of an early answer How identity raises the cost of changing course Questions that loosen a protected conclusion improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias first answer Introduction A first impression is often only a starting point, yet it can quickly become the default explanation that shapes everything that follows. In personal decisions, the problem is rarely that the first answer is always wrong. The problem is that once an early explanation provides relief, confidence or a sense of direction, the mind begins treating it as something to protect rather than something to test. New information is no longer evaluated on equal terms. Instead, it is filtered through the original judgement, making contradictory evidence feel less convincing than it would have been before the first conclusion formed. This mechanism helps explain why confirmation bias is so persistent in everyday choices, from relationships and careers to investments and health decisions. Research on confirmation bias, motivated reasoning and first impression effects shows that emotional rewards, identity concerns and selective information processing work together to make early conclusions unusually resistant to change. UC San Diego Pages+2The Decision Lab [pages.ucsd.edu] pages.ucsd.edu As already noted, this is not to.Read more UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12272 — People may treat eviden... Why the First Answer Starts Winning The first plausible answer offers more than information. It reduces uncertainty. When people face an ambiguous decision, uncertainty is mentally demanding. A convincing early explanation provides immediate psychological benefits: it simplifies a complex situation, creates a feeling of understanding and allows action to begin. Once these benefits are experienced, abandoning the first answer means giving up certainty and returning to ambiguity. This helps explain why confirmation bias often begins before anyone deliberately searches for supporting evidence. The preferred explanation becomes the reference point against which later information is judged. Rather than asking, \"Which explanation best fits all the evidence?\", people increasingly ask, often without noticing, \"Does this new evidence fit what I already think?\" Research on confirmation bias consistently finds that people selectively seek, interpret and remember information that supports existing beliefs or decisions. This tendency becomes stronger when the issue is emotionally important or connected to desired outcomes . [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu As already noted, this is not to.Read more UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12272 — People may treat eviden... The emotional pull of an early answer Early conclusions often become attractive because they solve an emotional problem before they solve an intellectual one. For example: A promising job offer reduces anxiety about an uncertain future. A new relationship satisfies hopes for companionship. An investment idea promises financial success. A medical explanation reduces fear by providing certainty. In each case, the first answer delivers emotional relief alongside factual interpretation. Later contradictory information therefore threatens not only the conclusion itself but also the emotional comfort attached to it. Psychologists describe part of this process as motivated reasoning: reasoning influenced by preferred outcomes rather than purely by evidence. Importantly, this usually happens outside conscious awareness. People rarely decide to ignore evidence. Instead, supporting evidence simply feels more reasonable, while opposing evidence feels unusually weak, unfair or exceptional. [UC San Diego Pages+2Psychology Today] pages.ucsd.edu As already noted, this is not to.Read more UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12272 — People may treat eviden... Confirmation Bias: Your Brain is So Judgmental Heidi Grant Halvorson Big Think Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube How Identity Raises the Cost of Changing Course As time passes, an early judgement often becomes woven into how people see themselves. A decision may begin as a practical choice but gradually acquire social and personal meaning: \"I chose this career because I'm independent.\" \"I'm good at spotting talented people.\" \"I'm someone who trusts my instincts.\" \"People already know I've committed to this.\" At this stage, evidence no longer threatens only the decision. It threatens competence, consistency or reputation. This increases the psychological cost of changing one's mind. Admitting the original judgement was incomplete can feel like admitting personal failure, even when changing course would objectively improve the outcome. Researchers studying motivated reasoning argue that people often evaluate evidence differently depending on whether it supports or threatens valued beliefs or identities. Information inconsistent with those identities tends to receive closer scrutiny, while supportive information is accepted more readily. The result is not necessarily stronger reasoning but asymmetric reasoning. [UC San Diego Pages+2Psychology Today] pages.ucsd.edu As already noted, this is not to.Read more UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12272 — People may treat eviden... A practical example is someone who buys a house after quickly becoming convinced it is perfect. Once family and friends have heard enthusiastic descriptions, changing their mind becomes socially expensive. Problems discovered during inspections may be dismissed as minor because abandoning the purchase now also means explaining the reversal. Why Contradictory Evidence Often Fails People sometimes assume that stronger evidence naturally changes minds. In reality, later evidence must compete with an interpretation that has already been organised around the first answer. Several mechanisms reinforce this pattern: Selective attention: supporting facts attract more notice than conflicting ones. Selective interpretation: ambiguous information is interpreted in ways that preserve the existing story. Selective memory: confirming experiences become easier to remember. Unequal standards of proof: evidence against the preferred conclusion is expected to meet a much higher standard before being accepted. These processes interact. A person who already believes a business opportunity is excellent may notice encouraging customer reviews, discount warnings as isolated complaints, remember successful examples and demand overwhelming proof before reconsidering. This creates the impression that the conclusion has survived rigorous testing when, in reality, much of the testing has been uneven. Research on first impression bias similarly shows that initial information often receives disproportionate weight, influencing how subsequent information is interpreted rather than simply being combined with it. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabThe First Impression BiasThe first impression bias describes how we make quick judgements about others that are often sub... Anchoring Bias – Why First Impressions Stick (Even When They’re Wrong) Channel: @mindBlowing 893 Open on YouTube Questions That Loosen a Protected Conclusion The goal is not to distrust every first impression. Many first impressions are accurate enough to serve as useful starting points. The challenge is preventing them from becoming protected conclusions. Several questions can interrupt this process: What evidence would genuinely change my mind ? If no realistic evidence qualifies, the belief may already be insulated from testing. Would I judge the same evidence differently if it supported the opposite conclusion? This exposes unequal standards of proof. Am I defending the decision or defending my identity? Distinguishing the two reduces unnecessary commitment. What information did I avoid because I expected it to be uncomfortable? The answer often reveals missing evidence. If I had not already committed time, money or reputation, would I still make the same choice today? This helps separate current evidence from past investment. These questions work because they redirect attention away from defending the original answer and back towards comparing competing explanations. Recognising the Mechanism Before It Hardens The earliest stage of confirmation bias is"},{"title":"Red Flags","url":"/red-flags/","description":"Generosity becomes risky when repeated behavior would look obvious in a friend's relationship but complicated in your own.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Confirmation Bias","level":3,"text":"Red Flags Is This Patience or Evidence Avoidance? Red Flags Red Flags Is This Patience or Evidence Avoidance? Generosity becomes risky when repeated behavior would look obvious in a friend's relationship but complicated in your own. Generosity becomes risky when repeated behavior would look obvious in a friend's relationship but complicated in your own. population context How hope protects a relationship story The close friend comparison test Patterns that deserve clearer standards improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias red flags Introduction When a relationship matters, generosity is usually a strength. Most healthy relationships require people to give each other the benefit of the doubt, forgive occasional mistakes and recognise that everyone behaves badly sometimes. The difficulty is knowing when generosity has stopped being a virtue and has become a way of protecting a preferred story. Confirmation bias can quietly turn patience into evidence avoidance: each new warning sign is explained away so that the relationship still fits the hopeful narrative already formed. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 The practical question is not whether people should be charitable. It is whether they apply the same standards to their own relationship that they would apply if the same pattern were happening to someone they cared about. That distinction helps separate reasonable optimism from selective interpretation. How hope protects a relationship story Most relationship concerns do not arrive as dramatic revelations. They emerge through a sequence of ambiguous events: a cancelled plan, an angry outburst, repeated dishonesty, a promise that is not kept, or a pattern of emotional withdrawal. Each incident has a plausible explanation on its own. Because relationships involve trust and emotional investment, people often interpret these events in the most favourable light. Someone who wants the relationship to succeed may naturally think: \"They were under unusual stress .\" \"Everyone has flaws.\" \"This isn't who they really are.\" \"Things will improve once work calms down.\" None of these explanations is necessarily wrong. The problem arises when the explanations become automatic regardless of the evidence. Confirmation bias encourages people to collect reasons that preserve the preferred conclusion—\"this relationship is fundamentally healthy\"—while giving less attention to observations that point elsewhere. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 Importantly, charitable interpretation should become more confident when behaviour improves. If explanations remain unchanged even after repeated disappointments, the interpretation has stopped responding to evidence and has become a defence of the existing belief. The Attachment Theory: How Childhood Affects Life Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube The close friend comparison test One practical way to interrupt confirmation bias is to compare your judgement with the advice you would give a close friend. Imagine someone you care about describing the following pattern over several months: Their partner repeatedly lies about small matters. Promises are often broken. Difficult conversations end with blame or contempt. Apologies rarely lead to lasting behavioural change. Would you advise your friend to be more patient, or would you begin to question the relationship itself? This comparison works because psychological distance reduces the emotional pressure to defend the existing relationship. When judging a friend's situation, there is less investment in preserving hope, less fear of loneliness and less temptation to justify sunk emotional costs. The standards that suddenly seem obvious from the outside can reveal how confirmation bias has been operating from the inside. The exercise should not become a rule that every disagreement is a \"red flag\". Instead, it asks whether identical evidence receives different interpretations solely because one case is personally important. Predicting Divorce Contempt, Stonewalling, Vagal Tone & Gottman Wisdom Channel: The Gottman Institute Open on YouTube Patterns deserve more weight than explanations People often overvalue explanations and undervalue repetition. An isolated incident can usually be understood in several ways. A repeated pattern is much harder to dismiss. For example: One forgotten anniversary may reflect distraction. Five years of consistently forgotten important occasions suggest a stable pattern of priorities. Similarly: One angry argument after exceptional stress may not reveal much. Repeated contempt, ridicule or humiliation across ordinary disagreements tells a different story. Research on long term relationships supports paying attention to enduring interaction patterns rather than isolated episodes. The work of the Gottman Institute identifies recurring communication behaviours—notably criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling—as especially damaging when they become established features of conflict rather than occasional lapses. Contempt, in particular, has consistently emerged as one of the strongest predictors of relationship breakdown. [The Gottman Institute] gottman.com the four horsemen recognizing criticism contempt defensiveness and stonewalling The Gottman InstituteThe Four Horsemen: Criticism, Contempt, Defensiveness...10 May 2026 — Identify the Four Horsemen in your conflict d... Published: May 2026 The analytical lesson is simple: explanations should be judged against the stability of behaviour. If the explanation changes every time while the behaviour stays the same, the behaviour deserves greater weight. When generosity becomes evidence avoidance Healthy relationships involve assuming good intentions until evidence suggests otherwise. Evidence avoidance begins when the standard for changing one's view keeps moving. Common warning signs include: [pacificcoasttherapy.com] pacificcoasttherapy.com the four horsemen warning signs your relationship needs help The Four Horsemen: Signs Your Relationship Needs Help30 May 2026 — The Four Horsemen are four distinct behaviors that erode trust, emotio... Published: May 2026 Every concern is treated as an isolated exception despite obvious repetition. Promised changes are accepted without checking whether behaviour actually changes. Friends or family repeatedly notice patterns that the person in the relationship dismisses. Clear standards gradually become weaker because enforcing them might threaten the relationship. New information is interpreted differently depending on whether it supports staying together. These are examples of selective interpretation rather than deliberate self deception. People are often genuinely convinced that each explanation is reasonable, even while overlooking the cumulative picture. Confirmation bias operates through many small interpretive choices rather than one conscious decision to ignore reality. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 Attachment, vulnerability and over interpreting red flags Avoiding confirmation bias does not mean becoming suspicious of every imperfection. [britannica.com] britannica.com confirmation bias Definition, Examples, Psychology, & Facts11 Jun 2026 — Confirmation bias, people's tendency to process information by looking for, or int... Attachment research shows that people differ in how they perceive closeness, rejection and security. Individuals who are especially anxious about abandonment may detect danger too readily, while others may tolerate unhealthy behaviour for too long in order to preserve connection. Attachment patterns influence how people interpret relationship events, but they do not determine whether a particular relationship is healthy or unhealthy. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCContributions of Attachment Theory and Research NIHby J Cassidy · 2013 · Cited by 792 — In this article we focus on the documented antecedents and consequences of individual diffe... That distinction matters because \"red flag\" language is easily misused. A single awkward conversation, a personality difference or occasional conflict is not, by itself, reliable evidence of an unhealthy relationship. Likewise, attachment theory should not be used as a shortcut explanation for every disagreement. Good judgement depends on examining"},{"title":"Rival Test","url":"/rival-test/","description":"A useful test does not just ask whether your favorite explanation can survive; it asks what would separate it from a serious rival.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Confirmation Bias","level":3,"text":"Rival Test The Test Your Favorite Story Needs Rival Test Rival Test The Test Your Favorite Story Needs A useful test does not just ask whether your favorite explanation can survive; it asks what would separate it from a serious rival. A useful test does not just ask whether your favorite explanation can survive; it asks what would separate it from a serious rival. dataset evidence What positive testing gets right Where positive testing misleads decisions How to compare rival explanations improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias rival test Introduction Positive testing is one of the most misunderstood ideas in research on confirmation bias. It is often described as a flaw in reasoning, but the evidence shows something more subtle. People naturally test a hypothesis by looking for situations where they expect it to be true. This \"positive test strategy\" is often efficient because it focuses attention on cases that are likely to be informative. The problem arises when that strategy becomes the only strategy, especially in personal decisions where a preferred explanation has emotional appeal. Instead of asking whether a favourite story can gather supporting evidence, good reasoning asks what evidence would distinguish it from the strongest competing explanation. That shift—from supporting one story to comparing rival stories—is one of the most effective ways to reduce confirmation bias. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — Next, we show how the positive test strategy pro... Published: October 7, 2004 What positive testing gets right Early discussions of confirmation bias often gave the impression that people irrationally searched only for confirming evidence. Joshua Klayman and Young Won Ha challenged that interpretation by arguing that many experiments were better explained by a general positive test strategy than by a simple desire to confirm beliefs. Rather than asking questions that could produce either answer with equal probability, people tend to examine situations where the hypothesised property is expected to appear. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — Next, we show how the positive test strategy pro... Published: October 7, 2004 This strategy is not inherently irrational. In many everyday situations it is an efficient heuristic—a mental shortcut that reduces the enormous number of possible tests. If you suspect that a plant dies because it receives too little water, looking at under watered plants is often more informative than randomly examining every plant regardless of watering. Similarly, if you believe a particular study method improves exam performance, comparing people who actually used that method can be a sensible place to start. Klayman and Ha argued that under common real world conditions, positive testing often provides useful information because the hypotheses people investigate are usually quite specific rather than broad. In those circumstances, checking where the predicted effect should appear is often a reasonable first move. The mistake is assuming that a useful heuristic is automatically a complete method for reaching sound conclusions. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — Next, we show how the positive test strategy pro... Published: October 7, 2004 Where positive testing misleads decisions The weakness of positive testing appears when several plausible explanations could account for the same observations. Supporting evidence for one explanation is often perfectly compatible with another. Suppose someone believes: \"My manager criticised my presentation because they dislike me.\" Looking only for confirming observations may reveal that the manager was curt during the meeting or failed to smile afterwards. Those observations fit the hypothesis. However, they may also fit alternative explanations: The manager was under unusual time pressure . The presentation genuinely contained weaknesses. The manager behaves similarly with everyone. The organisation has recently tightened performance expectations. Each additional confirming observation may increase confidence without actually increasing the ability to distinguish between these possibilities. This illustrates an important principle in hypothesis testing: evidence has greatest value when it is diagnostic , meaning it changes the balance between competing explanations rather than merely fitting one of them. Many observations support multiple stories simultaneously. They feel persuasive because they are consistent with the preferred explanation, not because they uniquely identify it. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — Next, we show how the positive test strategy pro... Published: October 7, 2004 Personal decisions are especially vulnerable because preferred explanations often satisfy emotional needs. A hopeful investor wants a company to be undervalued. Someone considering a relationship wants awkward behaviour to be temporary. Someone choosing between jobs wants reassuring signs that justify an exciting opportunity. Positive testing then becomes selective information gathering rather than genuine evaluation. CONFIRMATION BIAS: Why we only ask \"yes\" questions? Channel: Ava Katushka Open on YouTube The rival explanation test The rival explanation test asks a different question: What evidence would separate my preferred explanation from the strongest alternative? This shifts attention from accumulating supportive evidence to comparing competing models of reality. Instead of asking: \"Can I find evidence that supports my view?\" you ask: \"What would I expect to observe if my explanation were wrong but the best alternative were correct?\" The distinction is subtle but powerful. Imagine deciding whether a freelance business is succeeding because of excellent marketing. A positive test looks for: [pages.ucsd.edu] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — Next, we show how the positive test strategy pro... Published: October 7, 2004 increasing enquiries, positive customer comments, growing website traffic. A rival explanation test asks whether these same observations could equally be explained by: seasonal demand, temporary market shortages, unusually favourable economic conditions, referral effects independent of marketing. The next useful question becomes: \"What evidence would differ if marketing really were the main cause?\" Perhaps new customers could be asked how they found the business. Conversion rates could be compared before and after specific campaigns. Different marketing channels could be tested separately. Those observations discriminate between explanations rather than merely supporting one. Why comparing rivals improves judgement Psychological research suggests that people often generate and evaluate a single working hypothesis rather than several simultaneously. Once attention centres on one explanation, information search naturally follows that direction. This creates an illusion that evidence is becoming increasingly convincing when, in reality, alternatives have simply received less attention. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego PagesConfirmation, Discontinuation, and Information in...October 7, 2004 — Next, we show how the positive test strategy pro... Published: October 7, 2004 Comparing rival explanations improves judgement for several reasons. It changes the standard of evidence. Instead of asking whether evidence is consistent with a favourite explanation, you ask whether it is more consistent with one explanation than another. It exposes missing information. Rival explanations often reveal variables that have not yet been measured or observed. It reduces motivated interpretation. When several explanations are evaluated together, it becomes harder to excuse weaknesses in only one preferred account while demanding unusually high standards from alternatives. It encourages prediction rather than storytelling. Competing explanations often generate different expectations about future events. Predictions can later be checked against reality. Six Principles of Scientific Thinking in Psychology Channel: Daniel Storage Open on YouTube How to compare rival explanations The rival explanation test does not require formal statistical training. It can be incorporated into ordinary decisions through a structured comparison. 1. State the preferred explanation clearly. Avoid vague impressions such as \"this feels right.\" 2. Generate at least one serious alternative. The goal is not to invent absurd possibilities but plausible competitors. 3. Ask what each explanation predicts. Different explanations should imply different"},{"title":"Search Trap","url":"/search-trap/","description":"Searching only for reasons to proceed can make a decision feel researched while hiding the strongest reasons it might fail.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Confirmation Bias","level":3,"text":"Search Trap When Research Becomes a Search Trap Search Trap Search Trap When Research Becomes a Search Trap Searching only for reasons to proceed can make a decision feel researched while hiding the strongest reasons it might fail. Searching only for reasons to proceed can make a decision feel researched while hiding the strongest reasons it might fail. mechanism Supportive searches versus failure searches Why one sided research feels convincing Search prompts that uncover contrary evidence improving your thinking and analytical skills confirmation bias search trap Introduction Researching an important personal decision can feel rigorous simply because it involves many searches, reviews and conversations. Yet the quantity of information gathered is often less important than the direction of the search. A common trap is selective search : looking mainly for evidence that supports the option you already favour while overlooking information that could change your mind. The result is not necessarily deliberate self deception. Rather, the research process gradually becomes a way of defending an emerging preference instead of testing whether it deserves to survive. Psychological research identifies this as one of the most practical forms of confirmation bias because it shapes what information reaches you before you ever evaluate it. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 In everyday choices—whether buying a home, accepting a job, starting a business or choosing a university—the difference between asking \"Why is this a good idea?\" and \"Under what conditions does this become a bad idea?\" can determine whether research improves judgement or merely reinforces enthusiasm. When Research Becomes a Search Trap Selective search happens before evidence is weighed. It begins with the questions people ask. Someone excited about moving to a new city may search for \"best neighbourhoods\", \"things to do\", and \"why people love living there\". A prospective employee may spend hours reading positive company stories while dismissing critical accounts as isolated complaints. A buyer considering an expensive product may repeatedly search for positive reviews while avoiding discussions of reliability problems. None of these searches is unreasonable on its own. The problem arises when they become the entire research strategy. Raymond Nickerson's review of confirmation bias distinguishes between impartially gathering evidence to reach a conclusion and unintentionally building a case for a conclusion that already feels attractive. In personal decisions, selective search often reflects the latter without people recognising that they are doing it. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 Supportive Searches Versus Failure Searches The easiest way to recognise selective search is to compare two different styles of information gathering. Supportive searchFailure search\"Why should I take this job?\"\"Why do people regret taking this job?\"\"Is this neighbourhood good?\"\"What problems do long term residents report?\"\"Which reviews recommend this product?\"\"What failures appear after two years of ownership?\"\"Why is this investment promising?\"\"What assumptions would have to be wrong for this investment to lose value?\" Supportive searches ask whether an appealing option has advantages. Failure searches ask how the option breaks down, who is dissatisfied, or under what circumstances it performs poorly. Both forms of search have value. The danger comes from relying almost entirely on the first. Research by Joshua Klayman and Young Won Ha helps explain why. They argued that people naturally adopt a positive test strategy , looking at situations where their current idea is expected to succeed. This strategy is often efficient, but it becomes misleading when it is used alone because it fails to distinguish between an idea that merely sounds plausible and one that survives serious attempts to disprove it. [Stats.org.uk] stats.org.uk Confirmation, Disconfirmation, and Information in Hypothesis...by J Klayman · Cited by 3328 — This strategy is not equivalent to confirm... Confirmation Bias in Trading: Why Your Brain Lies to You (And How to Fix It) Channel: Trader Mindset Open on YouTube Why One Sided Research Feels Convincing Selective search creates an illusion of confidence through several reinforcing mechanisms. First, every confirming result makes the preferred option appear more common than it really is. Search engines, recommendation systems and social media can quickly produce long sequences of agreeing information once your initial queries indicate a preference. This is partly a consequence of selective exposure: people naturally gravitate towards information consistent with their current beliefs. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Second, finding confirming evidence feels productive. Every favourable review, encouraging anecdote or expert endorsement creates the impression that the case is becoming stronger, even if little genuinely diagnostic evidence has been collected. Third, contrary information often requires more effort to interpret. Negative experiences may involve unusual circumstances, conflicting explanations or uncomfortable trade offs . Because these require additional thinking, it is psychologically easier to continue collecting simple confirming examples. The outcome is that confidence increases faster than understanding. Search Prompts That Uncover Contrary Evidence The most effective safeguard is not avoiding supportive searches but deliberately balancing them with questions designed to expose weaknesses. Useful prompts include: \"Why do some people regret this decision?\" \"Who should not make this choice?\" \"What assumptions am I making that might be false?\" \"If this turns out badly, what will probably have caused it?\" \"What evidence would genuinely change my mind ?\" \"What do critics agree on, even if supporters disagree?\" \"What happened to people who made a similar decision five years ago?\" These questions are valuable because they search for information capable of changing the conclusion rather than merely enlarging the supporting file. Notice that these prompts seek diagnostic evidence —information that can separate a good decision from a poor one—not simply additional opinions. A Practical Example Imagine someone deciding whether to leave a secure job for a start up. A selective search strategy might include: success stories from founders; articles about rapid career growth; testimonials from enthusiastic employees; salary comparisons. A balanced strategy would deliberately add: employee turnover rates; reasons former staff left; financial stability indicators; accounts from people whose move damaged their career; warning signs that distinguish successful start ups from unsuccessful ones. The second approach does not make failure more likely. It simply produces a more realistic picture before commitment. Confirmation Bias How Your Brain Tricks You into Believing What You Want to Believe Channel: Elevate Psychology Open on YouTube How to Build a Better Research Habit One practical way to resist selective search is to separate research into two distinct phases. First, gather reasons why the option could succeed. This ensures legitimate benefits are not ignored. Then deliberately switch goals. Pretend your task is to persuade a cautious friend not to make the same decision. Search specifically for the strongest objections, credible failure cases and situations where the choice performs badly. A few additional habits strengthen this process: Set a target ratio, such as reading one substantial critical source for every supportive one. Search for long term experiences rather than first impressions. Look for independent evidence instead of multiple articles repeating the same claim. Write down the conditions that would make you reject the option before continuing your research. Delay making the final decision until both the supportive and contrary evidence have been reviewed together. These techniques reduce the chance that research becomes advocacy for an option already chosen emotionally. The Goal Is Better Testing, Not Permanent Doubt The purpose of contrary searching is not to become cynical or endlessly indecisive. Every decision eventually requires commitment. The aim is simpler: ensure that confidence comes from surviving meaningful challenges rather than from avoiding them. Well researched personal decisions are rarely those with the greatest volume of supporting information."},{"title":"Causation","url":"/causation/","description":"Analytical thinking improves when people ask whether evidence really supports a cause or only shows a pattern.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Causation Does the Evidence Show a Cause? Causation Causation Does the Evidence Show a Cause? Analytical thinking improves when people ask whether evidence really supports a cause or only shows a pattern. Analytical thinking improves when people ask whether evidence really supports a cause or only shows a pattern. mechanism Patterns versus causes Rival explanations What would change the conclusion improving your thinking and analytical skills causation Introduction A pattern is not the same as a cause. Two things can rise and fall together because one causes the other, because the second causes the first, because both are driven by a third factor, because the data have been grouped in a misleading way, or because coincidence has been mistaken for meaning. Good analytical thinking therefore asks a sharper question than “Are these things linked?” It asks: “What mechanism would make this link causal, and what rival explanations would also fit the evidence?” This matters because causal claims appear everywhere: a headline says a food “reduces risk”, a manager says a new policy “improved productivity”, a business dashboard shows that customers who use one feature spend more, or a person concludes that a habit changed their mood. Some of those claims may be right. The error is not noticing a correlation; it is stopping there. Modern causal inference treats causal conclusions as claims about what would happen under an intervention, not merely as descriptions of what happened alongside what. Observational evidence can be useful, but it is vulnerable to confounding, selection bias and measurement problems unless those rival explanations are actively tested. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by G Hammerton · 2021 · Cited by 394 — The goal of much observational research is to identify risk factors that have a causal effect o... Patterns versus causes A correlation says that two variables move together in some way. A causal claim says that changing one would change the other, through some mechanism. The difference is small in wording and large in consequence. “People who sleep very little have higher mortality” is a pattern. “Sleeping too little causes higher mortality” is a stronger claim. To accept the second, the reader needs more than a graph: they need a plausible pathway, careful comparison groups, and reasons to think the association is not mainly due to illness, stress , work patterns, age, socioeconomic factors, or measurement error. The classic warning is often summarised as “correlation does not imply causation”, but the phrase is sometimes misunderstood. It does not mean correlations are useless. Correlations are often the first clue that something deserves investigation. The point is that correlation alone is not enough to identify direction, mechanism or intervention value. A correlation may suggest a causal hypothesis; it does not by itself prove one. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Correlation does not imply causation Correlation does not imply causation A simple example is the relationship between ice cream sales and drownings. In warmer periods, both may increase, but it would be a mistake to infer that buying ice cream causes drowning. A better explanation is that hot weather increases both swimming and ice cream consumption. In that case, temperature is a common cause: it creates a relationship between two variables that are not directly causing each other. Forecasting models may still use ice cream sales to predict drownings, but prediction is not the same as explanation. [OTexts: Online, open access textbooks] otexts.com Online, open access textbooks7.8 Correlation, causation and forecastingThe model can give reasonable forecasts, not because ice c... This distinction is especially important in dashboards and everyday decision making. A business may observe that customers who attend onboarding sessions spend more money later. That could mean onboarding increases spending. It could also mean that already motivated customers are more likely to attend onboarding and more likely to spend. A school may find that pupils using a revision app get higher marks. That could mean the app helps, or that conscientious pupils both use the app and study more in other ways. In each case, the practical question is not “Is there an association?” but “Would changing the suspected cause actually change the outcome?” Rival explanations A good causal thinker does not merely object that “there could be other factors”. They name the rival explanations clearly enough that they can be checked. The most common alternatives are direction reversal, confounding, selection effects, measurement problems, chance, and misleading aggregation. Reverse causation occurs when the apparent effect may actually be the cause. A person might say that social isolation causes poor health, but poor health can also make people more isolated. A company might say that using an advanced feature causes customers to stay subscribed, but loyal customers may be the ones most willing to learn the advanced feature. The proposed arrow can point the other way, or both ways at once. Confounding occurs when a third factor influences both the proposed cause and the outcome. In observational research, confounders can distort the estimated association so that the apparent effect is too large, too small, or even in the wrong direction. Health, income, education, baseline motivation and prior risk are common confounders because they influence many later outcomes and many exposures people choose or experience. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by G Hammerton · 2021 · Cited by 394 — The goal of much observational research is to identify risk factors that have a causal effect o... Selection effects occur when the people or cases being compared are not comparable because of how they entered the dataset. Suppose a workplace compares productivity among employees who voluntarily use a new tool with those who do not. The users may differ before the tool is introduced: they may be more engaged, more comfortable with technology, or working in teams where managers encourage experimentation. The difference in outcomes may partly reflect who selected into the group, not what the tool did. Measurement problems occur when the variables do not capture what the claim says they capture. A study may measure “screen time” by self report, “wellbeing” with a short survey, or “performance” through a narrow metric that employees can game. Even if the numbers correlate, the causal story may be about the measurement process rather than the real world concept the reader cares about. Chance and multiple comparisons matter because people often inspect many possible relationships and remember the striking ones. In a large enough dataset, some variables will appear to move together by accident. This does not make statistics useless, but it does mean that surprising patterns need replication, pre specified tests, or independent confirmation before they should be treated as explanations. Misleading aggregation can make a pattern appear at one level and disappear or reverse at another. Simpson’s paradox is the famous case: a relationship in a combined dataset can reverse when the data are split into meaningful subgroups. The University of California, Berkeley graduate admissions example is widely used because the overall admission rate appeared lower for women, yet department level analysis showed that application patterns across more and less competitive departments changed the interpretation. [Philosophy at HKU] philosophy.hku.hk Open source on hku.hk. The mechanism test The word “mechanism” is the bridge between a pattern and a plausible cause. A mechanism explains how the cause would produce the effect. Without it, a causal claim is often just a decorated correlation. A useful mechanism does three things. First, it identifies the pathway: what happens after the cause and before the outcome? Second, it makes timing matter: the cause should come before the effect, and the relevant intermediate changes should appear in the expected order. Third, it creates testable expectations: if the mechanism is real, certain related patterns should appear, and others should not. Take the claim that a new meeting format improves project delivery. The mechanism might be that shorter meetings force clearer ownership, which reduces duplicated work, which speeds delivery. That is much stronger than simply saying “teams using the format delivered faster”. It suggests what to inspect: Were responsibilities clearer? Did duplicated work fall? Did the effect appear first in teams that previously suffered from unclear ownership? Did teams that adopted the format only in name show the same improvement? In epidemiology, Austin Bradford Hill’s 1965 discussion of association and causation remains influential because it treated causality as a judgement built from several considerations rather than one magic test. His famous viewpoints include strength of association,"},{"title":"Berkeley Data","url":"/berkeley-data/","description":"The Berkeley admissions example shows how an overall pattern can change when the data are split into meaningful groups.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Causation","level":3,"text":"Berkeley Data When the Combined Data Tells the Wrong Story Berkeley Data Berkeley Data When the Combined Data Tells the Wrong Story The Berkeley admissions example shows how an overall pattern can change when the data are split into meaningful groups. The Berkeley admissions example shows how an overall pattern can change when the data are split into meaningful groups. dataset evidence What the overall admissions pattern seemed to show Why department level data changed the interpretation How to spot aggregation traps in other claims improving your thinking and analytical skills causation berkeley data Introduction The University of California, Berkeley graduate admissions data from 1973 is the best known real world example of Simpson's paradox: a situation in which an overall statistical pattern reverses after the data are divided into meaningful groups. At first glance, the combined admissions figures appeared to show that women were admitted at much lower rates than men. However, when researchers examined admissions department by department, that apparent pattern largely disappeared and, in several cases, even reversed. The lesson is not that discrimination was impossible, but that aggregate statistics alone can produce misleading causal stories when important differences between subgroups are ignored. This case has become a cornerstone of statistical thinking because it demonstrates why careful analysis must ask what is being combined before drawing conclusions about cause, fairness, or bias. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sex bias in graduate admissions: data from berkeleyby PJ Bickel · 1975 · Cited by 977 — Examination of aggregate data on graduate a... What the overall admissions pattern seemed to show The controversy began with graduate admissions for the autumn of 1973. Across all departments combined, approximately 44% of male applicants were admitted compared with about 35% of female applicants. Viewed only at this level, the figures appeared to provide strong evidence that women faced systematic discrimination in admissions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sex bias in graduate admissions: data from berkeleyby PJ Bickel · 1975 · Cited by 977 — Examination of aggregate data on graduate a... This aggregate result was especially persuasive because: the difference was large enough that it was unlikely to be explained by random chance alone; the data covered thousands of applicants rather than a small sample; and the comparison seemed straightforward: compare overall admission rates by gender. Had the analysis stopped there, many readers would reasonably have concluded that Berkeley's admissions process discriminated broadly against women. Are University Admissions Biased? Simpson's Paradox Part 2 Channel: minutephysics Open on YouTube Why department level data changed the interpretation The key insight came when statisticians analysed admissions separately for individual departments rather than pooling them together. Graduate departments made admissions decisions independently, and departments varied enormously in how selective they were. Researchers found two important facts: Women disproportionately applied to departments with very low admission rates. Men disproportionately applied to departments with much higher admission rates. Because of this difference in application patterns, combining every department into one overall statistic mixed together applicants facing very different admission odds. Once departments were examined individually, most showed little evidence of gender bias in admissions decisions, and some actually admitted women at slightly higher rates than comparable male applicants. The original paper concluded that the aggregate pattern suggesting bias against women was misleading because it ignored differences between departments. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sex bias in graduate admissions: data from berkeleyby PJ Bickel · 1975 · Cited by 977 — Examination of aggregate data on graduate a... The six largest departments illustrate the effect particularly well. In several departments, women had admission rates comparable with or higher than men's, yet the overall total still showed women being admitted less frequently because many female applicants had concentrated in highly competitive programmes with very low acceptance rates. [Vincent Arel Bundock's GitHub Projects] vincentarelbundock.github.io Vincent Arel Bundock's GitHub ProjectsR: Student Admissions at UC BerkeleyThis data set is frequently used for illustrating Simpson's par... This reversal is exactly what Simpson's paradox describes: a trend that appears in the combined data but changes—or even reverses—after accounting for a relevant grouping variable. Simpson's Paradox Channel: minutephysics Open on YouTube Why this is not simply \"proof there was no discrimination\" The Berkeley example is often oversimplified into the claim that \"there was no discrimination.\" That is not what the original research established. The authors argued that the evidence did not support widespread department level discrimination against women in admissions decisions themselves. They found relatively few departments with statistically significant departures from expected admissions rates, with roughly similar numbers appearing to favour women as to favour men. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sex bias in graduate admissions: data from berkeleyby PJ Bickel · 1975 · Cited by 977 — Examination of aggregate data on graduate a... However, the study did not claim that every aspect of graduate education was free from gender bias. Many other factors could influence who chose to apply to particular departments, including: differences in career expectations; barriers affecting earlier educational choices; departmental reputations; social and cultural influences on subject selection. The paradox concerns how the admissions data should be interpreted statistically. It does not settle every broader question about gender equality in higher education. What the Berkeley data teaches about causal thinking The Berkeley admissions case illustrates an essential principle for better analytical reasoning: before explaining a statistical association, ask whether different populations have been merged together. The overall admission rate depended on two separate processes: 1. Which departments applicants chose. 2. How each department selected applicants. If department choice differs systematically between groups, then the overall admission rate reflects both processes simultaneously. Treating the combined figure as though it measured only admissions decisions mistakes correlation for explanation. From a causal perspective, department choice acted as an important confounding variable. Ignoring it created an apparent relationship between gender and admissions that was largely explained by differences in where applicants applied. Modern causal analysis uses this example to show why identifying relevant causal structure is more important than relying on simple averages. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Simpson's paradox Simpson's paradox How to spot aggregation traps in other claims The Berkeley example provides several practical questions that help identify when aggregated statistics may be misleading. Ask whether meaningful subgroups exist. Different schools, hospitals, regions, customer segments or departments may operate under very different conditions. Check whether the groups have different baseline rates. Simpson's paradox requires substantial differences between subgroup outcomes . If every subgroup behaves similarly, aggregation is less likely to reverse the pattern. Look for unequal group membership. If one population disproportionately enters easier or harder categories—as women did by applying more often to highly selective departments—the combined average can become misleading. Compare both the pooled and separated data. Neither view is automatically correct. The appropriate level of analysis depends on the causal question being asked. For example, a company might conclude that one sales team performs better overall. After separating results by territory difficulty, each team member could actually outperform their counterpart within comparable markets. Similarly, a medical treatment may appear superior overall because it was given more often to patients with milder illnesses, not because it was intrinsically more effective. How statistics can be misleading Mark Liddell Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Why the Berkeley case remains influential More than fifty years after the original study, the Berkeley admissions data continues to appear in statistics, economics, epidemiology, machine learning and fairness research because it captures a surprisingly common analytical mistake in a single dataset. Its lasting importance is not that it produced a clever mathematical curiosity. Rather, it demonstrates a practical habit of mind: whenever a striking overall pattern appears, ask whether"},{"title":"Hidden Causes","url":"/hidden-causes/","description":"The ice cream and drowning example shows how a hidden third factor can make a harmless pattern look causal.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Causation","level":3,"text":"Hidden Causes When a Third Factor Creates the Pattern Hidden Causes Hidden Causes When a Third Factor Creates the Pattern The ice cream and drowning example shows how a hidden third factor can make a harmless pattern look causal. The ice cream and drowning example shows how a hidden third factor can make a harmless pattern look causal. mechanism Why the obvious causal story is wrong How common causes create misleading links Questions that expose hidden third factors improving your thinking and analytical skills causation hidden causes Introduction The relationship between ice cream sales and drowning deaths is one of the best known examples of why correlation alone cannot establish causation . The two variables often rise and fall together, yet there is no credible mechanism by which buying or eating ice cream causes people to drown. Instead, both are influenced by a shared background factor: warm weather. Higher temperatures encourage people to buy more ice cream while also increasing swimming, boating and other water activities, which raise the opportunity for drowning incidents. [OTexts: Online, open access textbooks] otexts.com They are both caused by a third variable (temperature).Read more Online, open access textbooks7.8 Correlation, causation and forecastingSo the two variables (ice cream sales and drownings) are c... This example is memorable because it demonstrates a common analytical trap. When two trends move together, the real explanation may be a hidden third factor rather than a direct cause and effect relationship. Learning to recognise this pattern is an essential part of improving critical thinking and evaluating everyday claims . When the obvious causal story is wrong At first glance, the data appear persuasive. Plot monthly ice cream sales alongside monthly drowning incidents and both usually peak during the summer before falling again in colder months. A simple statistical analysis may reveal a strong positive correlation. [OTexts: Online, open access textbooks] otexts.com They are both caused by a third variable (temperature).Read more Online, open access textbooks7.8 Correlation, causation and forecastingSo the two variables (ice cream sales and drownings) are c... The mistake is to leap from \"these variables are associated\" to \"one causes the other\". If ice cream really caused drownings, then increasing ice cream consumption while holding everything else constant should increase drowning risk. There is no plausible biological or behavioural mechanism supporting such a claim, and the association disappears once the underlying seasonal conditions are taken into account. [Discover Magazine] discovermagazine.com Discover MagazineStatistics: When Confounding Variables Are Out of Control2 Apr 2016 — The ice cream/drownings correlation would cease to... The lesson extends far beyond this example. A striking statistical relationship may be genuine, but the explanation behind it may be completely different from the most obvious story. Correlation vs Causation Explained: Why Patterns Can Mislead Us Channel: Sprouts · Views: 10.0K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 4 minutes 51 seconds Open on YouTube How common causes create misleading links The key mechanism is confounding , where a third variable influences both the supposed cause and the observed outcome. In this example: Higher temperatures increase demand for cold foods such as ice cream. The same higher temperatures encourage more people to visit beaches, lakes and swimming pools. More people entering the water creates more opportunities for accidental drowning. Temperature therefore acts as a common cause of both variables. Ice cream sales and drowning incidents become statistically linked even though neither directly produces the other. [OTexts: Online, open access textbooks] otexts.com They are both caused by a third variable (temperature).Read more Online, open access textbooks7.8 Correlation, causation and forecastingSo the two variables (ice cream sales and drownings) are c... This is sometimes called a spurious relationship : the observed association is real, but interpreting it as direct causation is incorrect because the common cause has not been accounted for. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spurious relationship Spurious relationship An important subtlety is that the correlation may still have predictive value. If unusually high ice cream sales reliably occur on very hot days, they might help forecast periods when drowning risk is also elevated. That does not mean ice cream is the mechanism causing drownings; it is acting as an indirect indicator of conditions associated with greater water exposure. [OTexts: Online, open access textbooks] otexts.com They are both caused by a third variable (temperature).Read more Online, open access textbooks7.8 Correlation, causation and forecastingSo the two variables (ice cream sales and drownings) are c... Why this example remains so useful The ice cream example has survived for decades because it strips away emotional or political distractions and lets the reasoning stand on its own. Readers immediately recognise that: the correlation exists; the proposed causal explanation sounds absurd; a better explanation naturally emerges once the hidden variable is considered. This makes the example an effective teaching tool for introducing confounding, hidden variables and causal reasoning in statistics, epidemiology, economics and data science. It has also been used in educational activities that ask students to identify the unseen variable before revealing temperature as the common cause. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Because the example is so intuitive, it encourages people to apply the same scepticism to less obvious situations where the hidden factor is much harder to detect. How Ice Cream Kills! Correlation vs. Causation Channel: DecisionSkills Open on YouTube Questions that expose hidden third factors Whenever two variables appear strongly related, a few simple questions help uncover possible confounders. Could both variables be responding to the same external condition? Season, weather, economic conditions, age, income, education or underlying health frequently influence multiple outcomes at once. Does a believable causal mechanism exist? A statistical pattern is more convincing when supported by an explanation of how one variable could physically, biologically or socially influence the other. What happens if the suspected confounder is held constant? If the relationship weakens or disappears after accounting for temperature, season or another common cause, then the original association was probably driven largely by that factor rather than by a direct causal effect. [Discover Magazine] discovermagazine.com Discover MagazineStatistics: When Confounding Variables Are Out of Control2 Apr 2016 — The ice cream/drownings correlation would cease to... Would an intervention change the outcome? If people were randomly encouraged to buy more ice cream without changing weather or swimming behaviour, there is no reason to expect drowning rates to increase. Thinking in terms of interventions helps distinguish prediction from genuine causation. [OTexts: Online, open access textbooks] otexts.com They are both caused by a third variable (temperature).Read more Online, open access textbooks7.8 Correlation, causation and forecastingSo the two variables (ice cream sales and drownings) are c... The broader thinking habit The ice cream and drowning example teaches a habit that applies far beyond statistics. Many everyday claims—about health, education, business performance or public policy—can appear convincing because two measures move together over time. Before accepting a causal explanation, ask whether both outcomes could simply be responding to the same underlying influence. That small shift in thinking changes the question from \"What caused this?\" to \"What else could explain both observations?\" In analytical reasoning, that search for hidden common causes is often the difference between a persuasive story and a sound explanation. Confounding Variables Explained: Why Correlation is NOT Causation Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When a Third Factor Creates the Pattern. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Art of Statistics By David Spiegelhalter Explains correlation, causation, confounding variables, and how to interpret evidence. See on Amazon Book Calling Bullshit By Carl T. Bergstrom, Jevin Darwin West Shows how misleading correlations and hidden assumptions produce false conclusions. See on Amazon Book The Book of Why By Judea Pearl, Dana Mackenzie Directly addresses causal inference, confounding, and distinguishing association from cause. See on Amazon Book How to Lie with Statistics By Darrell Huff Introduces common statistical pitfalls, including mistaken interpretations of relationships in"},{"title":"Onboarding Bias","url":"/onboarding-bias/","description":"Customers who attend onboarding may spend more because they were already more motivated, not because onboarding changed them.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Causation","level":3,"text":"Onboarding Bias Did Onboarding Help, or Did Motivated Customers Join? Onboarding Bias Onboarding Bias Did Onboarding Help, or Did Motivated Customers Join? Customers who attend onboarding may spend more because they were already more motivated, not because onboarding changed them. Customers who attend onboarding may spend more because they were already more motivated, not because onboarding changed them. population context Why voluntary users are not a fair comparison Signals that selection bias is driving the result Better tests for onboarding impact improving your thinking and analytical skills causation onboarding bias Introduction Customers who attend onboarding sessions often spend more money than those who do not. At first glance, this appears to show that onboarding increases customer value. However, this conclusion can be misleading because the people who choose to attend onboarding are frequently different from those who do not. They may already be more motivated, have larger budgets, be implementing the product for more important business needs, or simply be more willing to invest time in learning it. In other words, the observed difference may reflect selection bias rather than the effect of onboarding itself. Recognising this distinction is an important analytical skill. Rather than asking whether onboarding attendees spend more, the better question is: Would those same customers have spent more even if they had never attended onboarding? Until that question is addressed, a correlation should not be treated as evidence of causation . Research on causal inference consistently identifies self selection as a major threat to drawing reliable conclusions from observational business data. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Making Stronger Causal Inferences ResearchGate(PDF) Making Stronger Causal InferencesMay 17, 2018 — We develop competing hypotheses about the relationship between high per... Published: May 17, 2018 Why voluntary users are not a fair comparison Voluntary onboarding creates two groups that differ before the onboarding session begins. Customers who sign up for onboarding are often those who: have already decided to make significant use of the product; are implementing it across larger teams; have executive support or larger budgets; are more engaged during the trial or purchasing process; are willing to invest time because they expect substantial value. Customers who skip onboarding may include casual users, organisations with limited resources, or experienced users who believe they need little assistance. These pre existing differences influence future spending regardless of whether onboarding is effective. Suppose a software company finds that onboarding attendees spend twice as much during their first year. That observation alone cannot distinguish between two very different explanations: onboarding taught customers how to use advanced features, increasing purchases; or customers planning major deployments were always more likely to attend onboarding and spend more. Both explanations fit the same dashboard. Without additional evidence, the data cannot determine which is correct. This is a classic example of selection bias , where the characteristics influencing participation are also related to the outcome being measured. Studies across business and organisational research show that failing to account for selection effects can substantially exaggerate or even reverse apparent treatment effects. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Making Stronger Causal Inferences ResearchGate(PDF) Making Stronger Causal InferencesMay 17, 2018 — We develop competing hypotheses about the relationship between high per... Published: May 17, 2018 Best Tips To Avoid Biases in Business Intelligence Google Career Certificates Channel: Google Career Certificates Open on YouTube Signals that selection bias is driving the result Several warning signs suggest that higher spending among onboarding participants may reflect customer differences rather than onboarding success. Attendance is voluntary. When customers decide for themselves whether to participate, motivation becomes part of the comparison. Highly motivated customers tend to engage with many product resources, not only onboarding. High value customers receive more encouragement. Customer success teams frequently prioritise larger accounts for personalised onboarding. If premium customers are deliberately offered more support, higher future revenue is partly built into the process. Pre onboarding differences already exist. If onboarding participants already have higher contract values, larger organisations, more active users, or greater feature usage before onboarding begins, comparing later spending without adjustment is misleading. Engagement moves together. Customers who attend onboarding often also open more emails, contact support, complete setup tasks, and participate in webinars. Onboarding may simply be one visible part of a broader pattern of engagement. The effect appears immediately. If attendees already have higher spending commitments before the onboarding session occurs, the apparent benefit cannot reasonably be attributed to training received afterwards. Looking for these patterns helps analysts identify situations where selection is likely influencing the results. Better tests for onboarding impact The strongest evidence comes from comparing customers who are similar before onboarding but differ only in whether they receive it. Several approaches are commonly used. Randomised experiments The most reliable approach is to randomly assign eligible customers to different onboarding experiences. Randomisation makes the groups similar on average before the intervention, allowing later differences in spending to be interpreted more confidently as causal effects. Natural experiments Sometimes operational constraints effectively create random variation. For example, customers might receive onboarding only when consultant capacity is available or because of scheduling differences unrelated to customer quality. Such situations can sometimes approximate experimental conditions if carefully evaluated. Matched comparisons When experiments are impossible, analysts can compare customers with similar characteristics, including: company size; initial purchase value; industry; product usage before onboarding; acquisition channel; customer tenure. Methods such as propensity score matching or covariate balancing attempt to reduce differences between groups before estimating onboarding effects. While these techniques cannot eliminate hidden differences, they usually provide more credible estimates than simple before and after comparisons. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Making Stronger Causal Inferences ResearchGate(PDF) Making Stronger Causal InferencesMay 17, 2018 — We develop competing hypotheses about the relationship between high per... Published: May 17, 2018 Regression with appropriate controls Statistical models can adjust for measurable differences such as account size or initial engagement. However, they only account for variables that are actually measured. Unobserved factors—such as customer enthusiasm or internal organisational priorities—may still bias estimates. Tackling Selection Bias in Marketing Models Channel: JigyasaAnalytics Open on YouTube A practical dashboard example Imagine a software company reports: Onboarding attendees spend an average of £9,000 during their first year. Non attendees spend an average of £5,000. The immediate conclusion might be that onboarding increases spending by £4,000. A closer investigation finds that onboarding attendees also: purchased larger initial licences; invited more users during the first week; completed setup more quickly; were more likely to request enterprise features before onboarding. Those findings suggest that onboarding attendees already represented stronger customers before the programme started. After matching customers with similar initial characteristics, the estimated difference might fall substantially. Perhaps onboarding still improves spending—but by hundreds of pounds rather than thousands. In some cases, the apparent advantage may disappear entirely once selection bias is addressed. The key lesson is not that onboarding is ineffective. Rather, it is that the raw comparison was answering the wrong question. Thinking more carefully about business metrics Business dashboards often encourage quick causal stories because they display simple comparisons between customer groups. Yet these groups frequently differ long before a product feature, marketing campaign, or customer success programme is introduced. When evaluating onboarding results, useful questions include: Were customers choosing whether to participate? Were high value customers more likely to receive invitations? Did attendees already differ before onboarding? What evidence rules out pre existing"},{"title":"Fair Tests","url":"/fair-tests/","description":"Random assignment helps test causal claims by making groups more comparable than ordinary observation usually allows.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Causation","level":3,"text":"Fair Tests Why Randomised Trials Make Causal Claims Stronger Fair Tests Fair Tests Why Randomised Trials Make Causal Claims Stronger Random assignment helps test causal claims by making groups more comparable than ordinary observation usually allows. Random assignment helps test causal claims by making groups more comparable than ordinary observation usually allows. policy intervention What random assignment fixes What randomisation still cannot guarantee When observational evidence remains useful improving your thinking and analytical skills causation fair tests Introduction Randomised trials are among the strongest tools for testing whether an intervention genuinely causes an outcome rather than merely being associated with it. Their key advantage is not that they eliminate every source of error, but that they create comparison groups that are, on average, similar before the intervention begins. By assigning participants to groups by chance rather than by choice, randomised trials greatly reduce selection bias and confounding, making it more credible that differences observed afterwards are due to the intervention itself rather than pre existing differences between participants. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Randomized controlled trial Randomized controlled trial Within the broader challenge of distinguishing correlation from causation , randomised trials provide a practical way to ask the counterfactual question: what would have happened if otherwise similar people had received a different treatment or policy? While no study design is perfect, careful randomisation usually provides a fairer comparison than ordinary observation alone. What random assignment fixes The central problem in causal reasoning is that people who receive an intervention are often different from those who do not. Patients choosing a new treatment may be healthier or more motivated. Schools adopting a new teaching method may already have stronger leadership. Businesses that introduce a new policy may differ from those that do not in ways that are difficult to measure. Random assignment tackles this problem by making allocation unpredictable. Because neither researchers nor participants determine who receives the intervention, both known and unknown characteristics are expected to be balanced between groups when the sample is sufficiently large. Any remaining differences are largely due to chance rather than systematic bias. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Randomized controlled trial Randomized controlled trial This balancing has several important consequences: It reduces confounding. Factors such as age, motivation, income or previous health are less likely to be concentrated in one group. It limits selection bias. Researchers cannot consciously or unconsciously steer particular participants into preferred groups when allocation is properly concealed. It simplifies causal interpretation. If groups begin similarly and receive different interventions, later differences are more plausibly attributed to the intervention itself. For example, imagine evaluating a new job training programme. If participation is voluntary, those who enrol may already be more motivated to find work. Higher employment afterwards could reflect motivation rather than programme quality. Random allocation makes motivation, on average, equally common in both groups, giving a much fairer test of the programme's effect. Why fair comparison is more important than perfect prediction Randomisation does not guarantee that every individual receives the treatment best suited to them. Instead, it produces an unbiased estimate of the intervention's average effect across the study population. This distinction matters because causal questions concern interventions rather than prediction. A retailer might accurately predict which customers will spend more, but prediction alone does not reveal whether sending a voucher causes additional spending. Randomly assigning vouchers allows researchers to estimate the effect of the voucher itself rather than the characteristics of customers who happened to receive one. The logic is similar across medicine, education, economics and public policy: the goal is to compare like with like as closely as possible before asking whether changing one factor changes the outcome. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Randomized experiment Randomized experiment Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs) Channel: UNICEF Innocenti Open on YouTube What randomisation still cannot guarantee Randomisation is powerful, but it is not magic. A well designed trial can still produce misleading results if other problems remain. Small samples can produce chance imbalances Random assignment balances groups on average, not perfectly in every trial. With small samples, one group may still end up older, sicker or more experienced simply through chance. Larger studies reduce this risk because random variation averages out over many participants. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Randomized controlled trial Randomized controlled trial Bias after randomisation Even when allocation is fair, later stages of the study can introduce bias. Examples include: participants dropping out at different rates; researchers or participants knowing treatment assignments and changing behaviour accordingly; outcomes being measured differently across groups; selective reporting of favourable results. For these reasons, high quality trials often use allocation concealment, blinding where feasible, pre registered protocols and standardised outcome measures. Reporting standards such as CONSORT exist to improve transparency and help readers judge trial quality. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCHow to Conduct a Randomized Controlled Trial CONSORT = Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials. RCT = randomized controlled trial. Formulating the...Read more... Average effects may hide important variation A trial typically estimates the average treatment effect. Some participants may benefit greatly, others may experience little effect, and some may even be harmed. Understanding which groups respond differently often requires additional analysis and sometimes further studies. A statistically significant average effect does not mean every individual should expect the same outcome. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov CONSORT 2010, 15. Baseline data. [Retrieved November...Read more... Results may not generalise People who volunteer for trials are not always representative of the wider population. Clinical trials may exclude older adults or people with multiple medical conditions. Educational trials may involve unusually motivated schools. Consequently, a trial can have excellent internal validity—showing that the intervention caused the observed effect within the study—while leaving open questions about whether the same effect will occur elsewhere. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov CONSORT 2010, 15. Baseline data. [Retrieved November...Read more... Observational Studies Versus Experiments Channel: Nicole Hamilton Open on YouTube When observational evidence remains useful Randomised trials are not always possible. Some questions cannot be randomised ethically. Researchers cannot randomly assign people to smoke, breathe polluted air or experience poverty. Other interventions may be too expensive, too disruptive or too slow to study experimentally. Observational studies therefore remain essential for many important questions. They are particularly valuable when: randomisation would be unethical; rare or long term outcomes are being studied; researchers need evidence from large, representative populations; early signals are needed before trials can be organised. Modern observational research uses techniques such as matching, statistical adjustment and causal modelling to reduce confounding, but these methods cannot guarantee that every relevant difference has been accounted for. The possibility of unmeasured confounders remains an important limitation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Increasingly, researchers combine evidence from both approaches. Randomised trials provide highly credible estimates of causal effects under controlled conditions, while observational studies help assess how well those findings apply to broader populations and real world settings. Statistical methods can integrate both sources to improve estimates while preserving the strengths of randomisation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Causal Inference Methods for Combining Randomized Trials...by B Colnet · 2024 · Cited by 298 — In this paper, we review the growing l... 1.4 What Does Imply Causation? Randomized Control Trials Channel: Brady Neal Causal Inference Open on YouTube Using randomised evidence as a critical thinker When evaluating a causal claim based on a randomised trial, ask a few focused questions rather than assuming the label \"randomised\" settles the matter: Were participants genuinely assigned at random? Was allocation concealed so researchers could not influence group assignment? Were both"},{"title":"App Scores","url":"/app-scores/","description":"Higher scores among revision app users may reflect study habits and motivation as much as the app itself.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Causation","level":3,"text":"App Scores Does the App Raise Scores, or Track Motivation? App Scores App Scores Does the App Raise Scores, or Track Motivation? Higher scores among revision app users may reflect study habits and motivation as much as the app itself. Higher scores among revision app users may reflect study habits and motivation as much as the app itself. population context The difference between app use and app effect Confounders in student performance data What stronger education evidence would compare improving your thinking and analytical skills causation app scores Introduction Students who use revision apps often achieve higher exam scores than students who do not. At first glance, this seems to show that the app improves learning. However, this is a classic example of why analytical thinking distinguishes correlation from causation . The students who voluntarily download, configure and consistently use revision tools may already differ from other students in important ways. They may be more motivated, more organised, more interested in high achievement, or have stronger study habits before they ever open the app. Unless those differences are accounted for, higher scores among app users cannot automatically be credited to the software itself. Research on educational technology repeatedly highlights this challenge, while also showing that some app features—such as spaced repetition and retrieval practice —can genuinely improve learning when tested under stronger research designs. Journal of Learning Analytics+2bera journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com [learning analytics.info] learning analytics.info Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education...Read more... The difference between app use and app effect Imagine a school finds that pupils using a revision app score 10% higher on average than those who do not. That observation alone answers only one question: who uses the app? It does not answer the more important question: what would have happened if the same pupils had not used it? The distinction matters because educational technology is rarely assigned at random. Students usually choose whether to install an app, how often to use it, and whether to continue after the novelty wears off. Those decisions are themselves influenced by characteristics that also affect academic performance. A motivated student is more likely to: begin revision earlier; complete optional homework; seek extra resources; persist when topics become difficult; and use a revision app consistently. Each of these behaviours can raise examination performance independently of the app. If motivation explains both app use and higher marks, motivation becomes a confounding factor rather than the app being the sole cause of better results. This is precisely the kind of selection bias that causal inference seeks to identify and control. Journal of Learning Analytics+2University of Colorado Boulder [learning analytics.info] learning analytics.info Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education...Read more... Why motivation is such a powerful confounder Motivation is difficult to observe directly, yet it influences almost every stage of learning. Students who are intrinsically motivated often study more frequently, monitor their own understanding, ask for feedback , and revise over longer periods rather than relying on last minute cramming. Those same students are also more likely to experiment with new educational tools. This creates a misleading pattern: App use increases because motivated students seek effective resources. Exam scores increase because motivated students study more effectively overall. The app appears responsible , even if much of the advantage comes from the students themselves. The same issue appears in many educational settings. Students who attend optional revision sessions, visit office hours or complete practice quizzes usually outperform their peers, but attendance itself may simply indicate stronger engagement rather than being the entire reason for improved outcomes . Without careful comparison, it is impossible to separate the effect of the intervention from the characteristics of the students choosing it. [Journal of Learning Analytics+2PMC] learning analytics.info Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education...Read more... Spaced repetition in learning theory Channel: Osmosis from Elsevier Open on YouTube Confounders in student performance data Motivation is only one possible explanation. Several other factors may create the appearance that a revision app is highly effective. Prior attainment. Students who already perform well may adopt sophisticated study tools earlier than others. Self regulated learning. Learners who routinely plan study sessions, monitor progress and review mistakes often use educational apps more consistently. Time spent studying. App users may simply spend more total time revising, regardless of whether the app itself is especially effective. Socioeconomic factors. Access to reliable devices, internet connections and quiet study environments can influence both app usage and examination results. Teacher encouragement. Schools or teachers who actively recommend particular revision platforms may also provide better overall academic support. These variables can overlap, making simple comparisons between users and non users increasingly unreliable. Learning analytics researchers have repeatedly warned that observational educational data can easily confuse engagement with intervention effects if these competing explanations are ignored. [Journal of Learning Analytics] learning analytics.info Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education...Read more... When higher scores may genuinely reflect app benefits Recognising confounding does not mean revision apps are ineffective. Many incorporate learning techniques with strong independent evidence. One widely supported example is spaced repetition , where material is reviewed at increasing intervals instead of repeatedly in one sitting. Another is retrieval practice , where learners actively recall information through testing rather than simply rereading notes. Both approaches have extensive cognitive psychology evidence supporting improved long term retention. If a revision app successfully encourages students to use these evidence based techniques more consistently than they otherwise would, then the app can have a genuine causal effect. In that case, the mechanism is not \"using an app\" in itself, but the learning behaviours the app promotes. Studies evaluating spaced repetition systems have found improved knowledge retention and examination performance under controlled or quasi experimental conditions, although results vary by subject, implementation and learner population. [PMC+2ResearchGate] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Implementation of a spaced repetition approach to enhance...by K Vagha · 2025 · Cited by 5 — In conclusion, this study highlights the... This distinction illustrates an important analytical habit: instead of asking whether technology works, ask which specific features change learning behaviour and through what mechanism . What stronger education evidence would compare The strongest evidence tries to estimate what would have happened if otherwise similar students had revised differently. Several research designs are more convincing than simple observational comparisons. Randomised controlled trials , where students are randomly assigned to use an app or an alternative revision method. Quasi experiments , where naturally occurring differences approximate random assignment. Matched comparison studies , where researchers compare students with similar prior attainment, motivation and background characteristics. Longitudinal studies , which measure performance before and after introducing the app while accounting for earlier achievement. Even these methods have limitations, but they substantially reduce the risk of attributing existing differences between students to the technology itself. Modern educational and causal inference research increasingly emphasises these approaches because straightforward comparisons of users and non users often exaggerate intervention effects. Journal of Learning Analytics+2Life Sciences Education [learning analytics.info] learning analytics.info Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education...Read more... Clinical Research and Statistics Introduction To Biases and How We Can Identify Them Channel: Whiteboard Medicine Emergency And Critical Care Open on YouTube A practical way to interpret claims about revision apps When you encounter claims such as \"students using this revision app scored 15% higher\", treat them as the beginning of an investigation rather than the end. Ask questions such as: Were students randomly assigned to use the app? Did users already have higher grades before using it? Were motivation and study time measured? Which learning techniques does the app actually encourage? Would equally motivated students using another revision method achieve"},{"title":"Sleep Claims","url":"/sleep-claims/","description":"Sleep and mortality claims need timing, pathways, and rival explanations before a correlation becomes credible advice.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Causation","level":3,"text":"Sleep Claims Does Short Sleep Cause Risk, or Signal It? Sleep Claims Sleep Claims Does Short Sleep Cause Risk, or Signal It? Sleep and mortality claims need timing, pathways, and rival explanations before a correlation becomes credible advice. Sleep and mortality claims need timing, pathways, and rival explanations before a correlation becomes credible advice. mechanism Why sleep duration is not automatically a cause Illness, stress, work, and measurement as rivals What a plausible sleep mechanism would predict improving your thinking and analytical skills causation sleep claims Introduction Headlines often claim that “sleeping less increases your risk of dying”, but the mechanism test asks a more demanding question: if short sleep truly causes earlier death, what biological pathways should connect the two, and are those pathways a better explanation than the alternatives? Observational studies consistently find that people reporting very short sleep, and often very long sleep, have higher mortality rates. However, an association is not enough to justify advice. Illness can reduce or extend sleep, chronic stress can disrupt it, shift work can alter both sleep and health, and self reported sleep is often imprecise. The strongest conclusion is therefore more nuanced than many headlines suggest: inadequate sleep is biologically plausible as a contributor to disease, but mortality associations must be interpreted alongside competing explanations and evidence from studies designed to test causation . [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Duration and All Cause Mortality: A Systematic Review...by FP Cappuccio · 2010 · Cited by 2672 — Both short and long duration o... Why sleep duration is not automatically a cause Large prospective studies have repeatedly found a relationship between habitual sleep duration and all cause mortality, often described as a U shaped curve: people reporting both very short and very long sleep appear to have higher death rates than those sleeping around seven to eight hours. Meta analyses involving more than a million participants have confirmed that this pattern is remarkably consistent across many populations. [PMC+2AHA Journals] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Duration and All Cause Mortality: A Systematic Review...by FP Cappuccio · 2010 · Cited by 2672 — Both short and long duration o... Yet consistency alone does not establish causation. A mechanism test asks whether changing sleep duration itself would change mortality risk. That requires separating sleep from everything that accompanies it. Several rival explanations deserve serious consideration: Reverse causation. Undiagnosed heart disease, cancer, neurodegenerative disease, depression, chronic pain and inflammatory illnesses can alter sleep years before diagnosis. In this case, poor health causes unusual sleep patterns rather than the reverse. Confounding. Socioeconomic disadvantage, smoking, obesity, alcohol use, mental illness, stressful work, and shift schedules all influence both sleep and long term health. Measurement error. Most large studies rely on people estimating their usual sleep duration. Self reported sleep often differs substantially from objectively measured sleep, making apparent dose response relationships less reliable. Selection effects. Individuals who consistently sleep unusually long may represent a population already burdened by chronic disease, disability or frailty rather than a group harmed by sleep itself. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCSleep duration and all cause mortality: a critical review by LM Kurina · 2013 · Cited by 215 — A robust association exists between sleep duration and mortality risk; specifically, that mortali... These alternatives matter because they predict many of the same statistical patterns as a genuine causal effect. Distinguishing correlation from causation: minimising bias and confounding in observational studies Channel: Gemma Sharp Open on YouTube Illness, stress, work and measurement as rival explanations Long sleep illustrates why mechanism testing is essential. If simply sleeping longer damaged the body, researchers would expect a reasonably direct biological pathway explaining why nine or ten hours in bed causes increased mortality across diverse diseases. Evidence for such a pathway remains weak. Instead, long sleep frequently accompanies conditions such as chronic inflammatory disease, neurological disorders, depression, obstructive sleep apnoea, medication effects or general frailty. These conditions may increase the need for sleep, fragment sleep quality, or increase time spent in bed without increasing restorative sleep. Under this explanation, long sleep functions mainly as a marker of poor health rather than its cause. Critical reviews have argued that reverse causation and residual confounding may explain much of the observed association. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCSleep duration and all cause mortality: a critical review by LM Kurina · 2013 · Cited by 215 — A robust association exists between sleep duration and mortality risk; specifically, that mortali... Short sleep presents a stronger causal case, but rival explanations remain important. Modern lifestyles that produce chronic sleep restriction often include long working hours, irregular schedules, psychological stress, shift work and unhealthy behaviours. Any of these may independently increase cardiovascular risk. Measurement adds another complication. Someone reporting \"five hours of sleep\" may actually spend seven hours in bed with frequent awakenings, while another person objectively sleeping six hours may feel fully rested. Sleep quality, timing and regularity may therefore matter as much as duration alone. Recent objective studies suggest that irregular sleep schedules can predict mortality independently of average sleep length, indicating that duration alone may be an incomplete exposure variable. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicSleep regularity is a stronger predictor of mortality risk than...by DP Windred · 2024 · Cited by 297 — Abnormally short and... What a plausible sleep mechanism would predict A convincing causal mechanism should generate predictions that extend beyond a statistical association. For short sleep, several biologically plausible pathways exist. Experimental sleep restriction can produce measurable changes in: blood pressure regulation; glucose metabolism and insulin sensitivity; appetite regulating hormones; inflammatory signalling; immune function; sympathetic nervous system activity. These changes provide credible routes through which chronic sleep deprivation could contribute to cardiovascular disease, diabetes and related illnesses over many years. If these mechanisms are genuinely causal, populations experiencing sustained sleep deprivation should eventually show higher rates of diseases linked to these pathways, even after careful adjustment for confounding. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Duration and All Cause Mortality: A Systematic Review...by FP Cappuccio · 2010 · Cited by 2672 — Both short and long duration o... The mechanism test therefore makes predictions beyond mortality itself. Intermediate outcomes—such as hypertension, impaired glucose control or increased inflammatory markers—should also appear, and interventions that successfully improve chronic sleep should eventually reduce at least some downstream disease risk if sleep is an important causal factor. For long sleep, the mechanism test is less convincing. Proposed biological explanations remain comparatively speculative, whereas explanations based on underlying illness naturally predict why prolonged sleep correlates with many different causes of death. Dr. Matt Walker: The Biology of Sleep & Your Unique Sleep Needs Huberman Lab Guest Series Channel: Andrew Huberman Open on YouTube What newer causal methods add Researchers increasingly use Mendelian randomisation, which exploits naturally occurring genetic variants associated with sleep traits to reduce confounding and reverse causation. These studies paint a more nuanced picture than observational research alone. Evidence increasingly supports a causal contribution of genetically predicted short sleep to several cardiovascular and metabolic outcomes, although effect sizes are generally modest and not every disease shows consistent results. By contrast, evidence that genetically predicted long sleep directly causes poor health is considerably weaker. In many analyses, the observational association between long sleep and mortality is stronger than the corresponding genetic evidence, supporting the idea that long sleep often signals underlying illness rather than causing it. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Associations between sleep duration and cardiovascular...by S Wang · 2022 · Cited by 80 — Equitable quality reviews showed that both... Even these studies have limitations. Mendelian randomisation depends on assumptions about genetic instruments and cannot fully"},{"title":"Decision Stakes","url":"/decision-stakes/","description":"The amount of analysis should match the cost, uncertainty, and reversibility of the decision.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Decision Stakes How Much Thinking Is Enough? Decision Stakes Decision Stakes How Much Thinking Is Enough? The amount of analysis should match the cost, uncertainty, and reversibility of the decision. The amount of analysis should match the cost, uncertainty, and reversibility of the decision. mechanism Costly decisions Reversible choices Decision speed rules improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes Introduction Decision stakes and reversibility checks answer a practical question at the heart of better thinking: how much analysis is enough for this decision? A useful thinker does not treat every choice as a courtroom trial. They slow down when the cost of being wrong is high, when consequences are hard to undo, when uncertainty is large, or when other people will bear the risk. They move faster when the downside is limited, the decision can be reversed, and the best learning will come from trying. Amazon’s well known “one way door” and “two way door” distinction captures this logic: some decisions deserve careful, consultative deliberation because they are consequential and nearly irreversible, while many others should use a lighter process because they can be corrected later. [s2.q4cdn.com] s2.q4cdn.com 2015 Letter to Shareholders But most decisions aren't like that – they are changeable, reversible – they're two way doors. If you've made...Read more... This matters because overthinking is not the same as careful thinking. Analysis has a cost: it consumes attention, delays learning, may invite decision fatigue, and can cause missed opportunities. The skill is not simply “think more”, but “match the thinking to the stakes”. A reversibility check gives that skill a simple trigger: before analysing harder, ask what would happen if the decision turned out badly, how quickly you would know, and how easily you could change course. The Core Mechanism A decision stakes check separates two questions that people often blur together: “Is this important?” and “Is this recoverable?” A decision can feel emotionally important but still be reversible, such as choosing a draft title, trying a study method for a week, or testing a new meeting format. Another decision may feel routine but be hard to undo, such as signing a long lease, publishing a public accusation, deleting a database, or accepting a job that requires relocation. The mechanism is simple: allocate thinking according to expected downside, uncertainty, reversibility, and learning speed. UK Health and Safety Executive guidance expresses the same broad logic in workplace risk terms: identify what could cause harm, decide how likely and serious that harm could be, take action to eliminate or control the risk, then review the controls. [HSE] hse.gov.uk Managing risks and risk assessment at work: Overview24 Mar 2025 — 1. Overview · identify what could cause injury or illness in your bu... The same pattern works for personal and analytical decisions: name the harm, estimate seriousness, reduce exposure, and set a review point. A useful stakes check asks: Cost: What is the realistic downside if this is wrong? Reversibility: Can the decision be undone, paused, refunded, edited, cancelled, or corrected? Detection speed: How soon would I know it was going badly? Correction cost: What would it take to reverse or repair the damage? Affected parties: Who else bears the consequences? Information value: Would more research materially change the decision, or merely make me feel safer? These questions prevent two opposite mistakes . The first is rushing a one way door decision because the first answer feels obvious. The second is treating every two way door choice as though it needs complete certainty. Costly Decisions Need Friction High stakes decisions deserve deliberate friction: extra time, outside perspectives, written assumptions , and checks for failure modes. NASA’s risk informed decision making handbook describes major decisions as requiring alternatives, uncertainty analysis, performance measures, and a “technical basis for deliberation” so that decision makers can compare options in light of known uncertainties and values. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov Technical Reports Server NASA Risk Informed Decision Making Handbook Technical Reports Server NASA Risk Informed Decision Making Handbook That is more process than everyday life usually needs, but the principle transfers well: when the downside is serious, make the reasoning visible before acting. For a person improving their thinking, “costly” does not only mean financially expensive. It can include reputational damage, health and safety risk, lost time, relationship consequences, legal exposure, or path dependence. Path dependence means that one choice changes the set of future choices available: a degree, mortgage, business partnership, migration decision, or public commitment may narrow later options even if it is not literally irreversible. The practical move is to add friction only where it buys protection. For a costly decision, a good process might include a written decision note: what you believe, what you are assuming, what you would regret, what would change your mind, and when you will review the result. NIST’s risk guidance is aimed at organisations, but its idea of “risk framing” is directly relevant: good risk decisions make assumptions, constraints, tolerance, priorities, and trade offs explicit before assessment and response. [NIST Publications] nvlpubs.nist.gov Publications NIST SP 800 39, Managing Information Security Risk Publications NIST SP 800 39, Managing Information Security Risk One effective friction device is the premortem. Gary Klein’s premortem method asks a team to imagine that a project has failed and then generate plausible reasons why, using “prospective hindsight” before the decision is final. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org Harvard Business Review Performing a Project Premortem Harvard Business Review Performing a Project Premortem For individual thinking, the same technique can be scaled down: “It is six months from now and this decision was a mistake. What most likely went wrong?” This is especially useful when enthusiasm, social pressure , or sunk effort makes the downside feel impolite to mention. Reversible Choices Should Move Faster Reversible decisions are not trivial; they are decisions where the cost of being wrong is limited because the decision can be changed. Amazon’s 2015 shareholder letter called these “two way doors”: if the decision proves suboptimal, you can reopen the door and go back through. [s2.q4cdn.com] s2.q4cdn.com 2015 Letter to Shareholders But most decisions aren't like that – they are changeable, reversible – they're two way doors. If you've made...Read more... That framing is useful because it shifts the question from “Am I certain?” to “Can I safely learn by acting?” This is the heart of decision speed. A reversible choice often benefits from a small experiment more than from prolonged speculation. Trying a new note taking system for two weeks, testing a landing page, inviting feedback on a rough outline, or switching a routine meeting to asynchronous updates can produce real evidence faster than private analysis. In these cases, action is not the enemy of thinking; it is a way to gather better information. The 2016 Amazon shareholder letter makes the velocity rule explicit: do not use a one size fits all process, because many decisions are reversible and can use a lighter process; it also suggests that many decisions should be made with roughly 70% of the information one wishes one had, because waiting for much more can be slow. [Amazon News] aboutamazon.com 2016 letter to shareholders Speed matters in business – plus a high velocity decision ...Read more The exact percentage is not a scientific law, but the managerial principle is sound: if you can correct quickly, speed itself has value. This does not mean “act carelessly”. It means build reversibility into the action. A reversible decision is safer when it has a stop loss rule, a review date, a small initial scale, and a clear signal of failure. For example, instead of “change the whole team’s workflow permanently”, try “test the workflow with one project for three weeks, then keep it only if cycle time improves and complaints do not rise”. The decision becomes faster because the downside has been bounded. How to Decide Faster Than 99% of People Without Overthinking (The Two Way Door Framework Explained) Channel: Value Catcher Open on YouTube The Hidden Cost of Treating Every Choice as High Stakes Overanalysis can become a reasoning error in its own right. Research on decision fatigue has examined how repeated choices can impair later self control, persistence, and decision quality; a 2008 paper by Vohs and colleagues found that making many choices led to worse subsequent self control behaviours such as reduced stamina and more procrastination. [Csom Assets] assets.csom.umn.edu Csom"},{"title":"Paralysis","url":"/paralysis/","description":"Overthinking small reversible choices can waste attention that would be better saved for decisions with real downside.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Decision Stakes","level":3,"text":"Paralysis Why Small Choices Can Steal Big Attention Paralysis Paralysis Why Small Choices Can Steal Big Attention Overthinking small reversible choices can waste attention that would be better saved for decisions with real downside. Overthinking small reversible choices can waste attention that would be better saved for decisions with real downside. population context When more comparison stops helping Decision fatigue and attention costs Simple stopping rules for low risk choices improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes paralysis Introduction Analysis paralysis in low stakes decisions occurs when people devote disproportionate time and mental effort to choices that are inexpensive to reverse and carry little real downside. Rather than improving outcomes , this extra analysis often consumes attention that would be more valuable when facing genuinely consequential decisions. A central idea in improving thinking is therefore to match the depth of analysis to the stakes: if a choice is easy to undo, it is usually better to make a reasonable decision quickly, learn from the result, and move on. Research on choice overload, decision making and cognitive effort supports this principle. While careful thinking is valuable for irreversible or high risk choices, the evidence suggests that endlessly comparing options in low risk situations can reduce satisfaction, delay action and contribute to mental fatigue without producing meaningfully better results. [UW Faculty] faculty.washington.edu UW FacultyWhen Choice is Demotivating: Can One Desire Too Much...by SS Iyengar · 2000 · Cited by 6813 — Current psychological theory and... When More Comparison Stops Helping People often assume that additional comparison always leads to better decisions. In reality, the value of gathering more information falls rapidly once the remaining options are all \"good enough\". At that point, each additional review, comparison or search is increasingly likely to produce diminishing returns. This pattern appears in many everyday decisions: Choosing between several highly rated restaurants. Comparing dozens of nearly identical headphones within the same price range. Spending hours selecting a notebook, study app or desk lamp. Rewriting an email subject line repeatedly before sending it. In each case, the opportunity cost may exceed the expected benefit. The extra hour spent comparing products or refining a minor decision is an hour unavailable for work, learning, relationships or higher value planning. Psychologists distinguish between maximisers , who seek the objectively best option through extensive comparison, and satisficers , who choose the first option that adequately meets their criteria. Although maximising can improve outcomes when differences between options are substantial, research consistently finds that satisficing often produces similar objective results while reducing stress , regret and decision fatigue for routine choices. [Wikipedia+2meinpodcast.de] Wikipedia The Paradox of Choice The Paradox of Choice How to Stop Overthinking Decisions and Overcome Analysis Paralysis Channel: Therapy in a Nutshell Open on YouTube Why Small Decisions Consume So Much Attention Low stakes choices can feel surprisingly difficult because the mind often treats uncertainty itself as a problem to eliminate. Several psychological mechanisms reinforce this tendency. Choice overload. When many acceptable alternatives exist, comparing them becomes mentally demanding. Rather than feeling liberated by more options, people may delay deciding altogether or abandon the decision. The well known jam experiment by psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper illustrated this effect: shoppers were more attracted to a larger display but were substantially less likely to make a purchase than shoppers offered a smaller selection. Although later research has shown that choice overload depends on context rather than occurring universally, the broader finding—that excessive options can impair decision making under certain conditions—has been replicated across many settings. [UW Faculty+2PMC] faculty.washington.edu UW FacultyWhen Choice is Demotivating: Can One Desire Too Much...by SS Iyengar · 2000 · Cited by 6813 — Current psychological theory and... Fear of regret. With many similar options, people imagine the possibility that another alternative might have been slightly better. This anticipated regret encourages further searching, even when additional information is unlikely to change the practical outcome. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabChoice Overload BiasChoice overload, or \"overchoice\", is a phenomena stating our tendency to have difficulty making a cho... Perfectionism. Some individuals implicitly treat every decision as a test of judgement. The desire to avoid even tiny mistakes makes reversible choices feel far more consequential than they really are. These mechanisms are especially powerful in modern environments where online reviews, rankings and comparison tools make it possible to continue searching almost indefinitely. Decision Fatigue and the Hidden Cost of Tiny Choices The cost of overthinking a small decision is not limited to the time spent making it. Every prolonged comparison consumes working memory, attention and self control that could otherwise support more valuable thinking later in the day. Researchers describe decision making as drawing on limited cognitive resources. Although the precise mechanisms behind decision fatigue remain debated, there is broad agreement that repeated, effortful choices increase subjective mental fatigue and can reduce people's willingness to engage in subsequent demanding decisions. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabChoice Overload BiasChoice overload, or \"overchoice\", is a phenomena stating our tendency to have difficulty making a cho... For example, imagine someone spending forty minutes comparing different coffee machines before work. Even if they eventually identify the objectively \"best\" model, they may begin the rest of the day with reduced mental energy for tasks that genuinely require careful reasoning, such as planning a project, negotiating with colleagues or reviewing important financial information. The hidden cost therefore lies not merely in the minutes spent deciding, but in the attention that those minutes permanently consume. Why Reversible Decisions Benefit From Action A reversible decision creates an opportunity that prolonged analysis cannot: learning from experience. Consider several examples: Trying a new note taking application for one week. Ordering from a different café. Testing a revised meeting agenda. Rearranging a workspace. In each case, direct experience quickly generates information that speculation cannot provide. If the experiment fails, the decision can usually be reversed at modest cost. This highlights an important distinction between prediction and feedback . Endless comparison relies on increasingly uncertain predictions about which option will prove best. Acting instead creates real evidence about what actually works in practice . For low risk decisions, fast feedback often has greater value than increasingly detailed prediction. The Paradox of Choice, Barry Schwartz Channel: Claremont McKenna College Open on YouTube Simple Stopping Rules for Low Risk Choices The goal is not to become careless. Instead, it is to stop analysing once additional effort is unlikely to improve the outcome. Useful stopping rules include: Define success before comparing. Decide on two or three essential requirements in advance, then stop searching once an option satisfies them. Limit comparison time. Allocate a fixed period—perhaps ten or fifteen minutes—for decisions with little downside. Ignore tiny differences. If competing options differ only marginally in price, ratings or features, treat them as effectively equivalent. Choose, then evaluate later. Instead of asking whether this is the perfect option, ask whether it is good enough to test. Review reversibility explicitly. Before continuing to research, ask: \"If this turns out to be the wrong choice, how difficult would it be to change?\" These rules deliberately shift attention from finding certainty to managing risk. A Common Misunderstanding Advice to avoid analysis paralysis is sometimes interpreted as encouragement to think less. That is not the lesson. The evidence does not suggest that quick decisions are universally better. Rather, it suggests that the value of additional analysis depends on the structure of the decision itself. Some decisions genuinely deserve extended deliberation because they involve irreversible commitments, substantial uncertainty or serious consequences. Others do not. The challenge is calibration. Treating every decision as equally important wastes cognitive resources, while treating every decision as trivial invites preventable mistakes. The most effective thinkers"},{"title":"One Way Doors","url":"/one-way-doors/","description":"Some choices deserve extra friction because the damage is serious, hard to undo, or likely to reshape later options.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Decision Stakes","level":3,"text":"One Way Doors Which Decisions Deserve Slower Thinking? One Way Doors One Way Doors Which Decisions Deserve Slower Thinking? Some choices deserve extra friction because the damage is serious, hard to undo, or likely to reshape later options. Some choices deserve extra friction because the damage is serious, hard to undo, or likely to reshape later options. mechanism What makes a decision hard to reverse Realistic costs beyond money A slower process checklist before acting improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes one way doors Introduction Some decisions deserve deliberate, slower thinking because they function like one way doors : once you pass through them, reversing course is difficult, expensive, or impossible. The challenge is not simply that they are important, but that a mistake can permanently reshape your future options. Choosing to sign a long term contract, relocate for a job, publicly commit to a position, or enter a business partnership can create consequences that persist long after the initial decision. Improving your thinking means learning to distinguish these high commitment choices from the many everyday decisions that are easily corrected. Rather than applying maximum analysis to everything, reserve your deepest judgement for decisions where recovery is slow, costly, or incomplete. This approach improves both decision quality and decision speed by concentrating effort where it matters most. [Amazon News] aboutamazon.com 2016 letter to shareholders Many decisions are reversible, two way doors. Those decisions can use a light ...Read more What Makes a Decision Hard to Reverse? A decision becomes a one way door not because it is emotionally significant, but because reversing it requires substantial cost or leaves lasting consequences even after reversal. Several features make a decision genuinely difficult to undo: Irrecoverable commitments. Time, money, reputation, legal obligations or relationships become locked in. Path dependence. One choice changes the set of future choices available. Accepting one opportunity may permanently close several others. Delayed feedback . You may not discover whether the decision was good until months or years later, making rapid correction impossible. High switching costs. Undoing the decision demands major financial, emotional or organisational effort. Effects on other people. Decisions affecting employees, family members, customers or communities become harder to reverse because they create wider commitments. Amazon popularised the distinction between \"Type 1\" (one way) and \"Type 2\" (two way) decisions for precisely this reason. One way decisions require careful deliberation because mistakes cannot simply be corrected through quick iteration, while two way decisions benefit from experimentation and rapid learning. [Amazon News] aboutamazon.com 2016 letter to shareholders Many decisions are reversible, two way doors. Those decisions can use a light ...Read more Importantly, reversibility is rarely absolute. Many decisions can technically be undone, but only after significant losses. Leaving an unsuitable job is possible; recovering the years spent building specialised experience may not be. Ending a business partnership is possible; repairing damaged trust or recovering legal costs may not be. The Real Costs Extend Beyond Money People often evaluate irreversible decisions primarily through financial cost. In practice , the most important losses are frequently less visible. Opportunity costs Every major commitment crowds out alternatives. Committing to one university, one career path or one market strategy means giving up other possibilities that cannot all be explored simultaneously. The cost is not simply what you spend, but what you never get the chance to pursue. Reputation Public actions create expectations that are difficult to erase. Publishing incorrect information, making public accusations or repeatedly breaking commitments can leave reputational effects long after the original event. Unlike financial losses, reputation often recovers slowly and unevenly. Relationships Marriage, business partnerships, hiring senior executives or dismissing trusted colleagues reshape social systems rather than isolated transactions. Even when relationships formally end, the practical consequences often continue through networks, trust and future collaboration. Time Perhaps the least appreciated irreversible resource is time. Money can sometimes be earned again. Years invested in the wrong direction cannot be recovered, especially when the decision delays acquiring experience elsewhere. Jeff Bezos’s explain his famous one way door and two way door decision making Channel: Finance & Tech Career Advice Open on YouTube Why Instinct Alone Is Often Not Enough Human intuition performs well in familiar environments with rapid feedback. It performs less reliably when decisions involve uncertainty, delayed consequences and rare events. One way door decisions frequently combine exactly these conditions: little previous experience incomplete information emotional pressure delayed outcomes large consequences. This creates fertile conditions for common thinking errors. People may: overweight recent experiences; become overly confident in their predictions; underestimate future regret; confuse commitment with certainty; mistake urgency for importance. Because feedback arrives slowly, these mistakes often remain hidden until reversing course becomes extremely expensive. Slowing down introduces deliberate checks that compensate for these predictable weaknesses rather than attempting to eliminate uncertainty altogether. A Slower Process Checklist Before Acting Slower judgement does not mean endless analysis. It means introducing structured friction before crossing a one way door. A practical checklist includes the following questions. 1. What exactly becomes difficult to undo? Be specific. Rather than asking whether the decision is \"important\", ask: What commitment becomes permanent? What future options disappear? What obligations begin immediately? Writing these answers often reveals hidden commitments that emotions overlook. 2. What is the worst realistic outcome? Avoid imagining either catastrophe or perfection. Instead ask: What would failure actually look like? Could I recover? How long would recovery take? Who else would bear the consequences? Thinking in concrete scenarios produces more reliable judgement than relying on vague feelings. Jeff Bezos explains one way door decisions and two way door decisions Channel: Startup Archive Open on YouTube 3. Which assumptions must be true? List the few assumptions on which the decision depends. For example: the new employer remains financially stable; projected demand actually exists; the partnership remains cooperative; the legal environment does not materially change. Identifying assumptions makes it easier to test them before committing. 4. Can I reduce the irreversibility? Many apparently one way decisions can be redesigned into smaller, reversible steps. Examples include: accepting a short trial before signing a long contract; piloting a product with limited customers before a national launch; renting before buying property; testing a collaboration before forming a permanent partnership. Reducing commitment often preserves valuable learning while limiting downside. 5. Have I sought disagreement? For one way door decisions, consultation is valuable not because groups are always wiser, but because independent perspectives expose assumptions you cannot easily see yourself. The goal is not unanimous agreement but stronger reasoning. Amazon's guidance emphasises deliberate consultation for Type 1 decisions precisely because the cost of unnoticed errors is unusually high. [Amazon News] aboutamazon.com 2016 letter to shareholders Many decisions are reversible, two way doors. Those decisions can use a light ...Read more Practical Examples of One Way Door Decisions The concept applies across personal and professional life. DecisionWhy slower judgement helpsAccepting a role requiring international relocationChanges career path, relationships and living arrangements simultaneously.Choosing a business co founderCreates long term legal, financial and interpersonal commitments.Selling a family businessOwnership, customer relationships and strategic control may never return.Publicly adopting an irreversible organisational strategyEmployees, investors and customers begin adapting immediately.Undergoing major irreversible medical treatmentBenefits and risks may extend throughout the remainder of life. By contrast, changing meeting formats, testing pricing, trying new software or experimenting with a study routine are often much closer to two way doors because they can usually be adjusted quickly after learning from experience. [Amazon News] aboutamazon.com 2016 letter to shareholders Many decisions are reversible, two way doors. Those decisions can"},{"title":"Premortems","url":"/premortems-5ab680/","description":"A premortem helps people notice plausible reasons a decision could fail before commitment makes them harder to discuss.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Decision Stakes","level":3,"text":"Premortems Imagine Failure Before It Happens Premortems Premortems Imagine Failure Before It Happens A premortem helps people notice plausible reasons a decision could fail before commitment makes them harder to discuss. A premortem helps people notice plausible reasons a decision could fail before commitment makes them harder to discuss. policy intervention How prospective hindsight works Questions that reveal hidden risks When a premortem is worth the time improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes premortems Introduction A premortem is a structured way to imagine that an important decision or project has already failed and then work backwards to explain why. Unlike a postmortem, which analyses failure after the damage has been done, a premortem aims to uncover weak assumptions , overlooked risks and hidden dependencies before commitment makes them harder to question. It is particularly valuable for high stakes or difficult to reverse decisions, where discovering a critical flaw early is far cheaper than correcting it later. Within a broader approach to decision stakes and reversibility, a premortem provides deliberate friction at exactly the point where optimism and momentum are most likely to distort judgement. Rather than asking, \"What could go wrong?\", it asks a more psychologically powerful question: \"It is one year from now and this decision has failed badly. What happened?\" Research on this form of \"prospective hindsight\" suggests that it improves people's ability to identify plausible causes of future outcomes by roughly 30%, making it a practical tool for spotting costly failure modes before they become reality. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Performing a Project Premortem identify reasons for future outcomes. by 30%. We have used prospective hind . sight to devise a method called a premortem. which helps p... How prospective hindsight works Most planning begins from an assumption of success. Teams naturally defend their preferred option, discount contrary evidence and become attached to the effort already invested. These tendencies contribute to well known thinking errors such as optimism bias, overconfidence and the planning fallacy. A premortem changes the mental task. Instead of predicting whether a project will succeed, participants accept—temporarily—that it has already failed. Their job is no longer to criticise colleagues or argue against the proposal but to explain an established outcome. This subtle change reduces the social cost of expressing doubts because people are not challenging the project itself; they are diagnosing an imagined failure. Gary Klein introduced the technique specifically to encourage knowledgeable dissent that would otherwise remain unspoken. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org performing a project premortem Harvard Business ReviewPerforming a Project PremortemReprint: F0709A In a premortem, team members assume that the project they are planni... The psychological mechanism is known as prospective hindsight . Rather than reasoning forwards through uncertain possibilities, participants mentally \"look back\" from a future failure. This tends to generate richer explanations, surface uncomfortable possibilities and reveal interactions between small risks that ordinary planning often overlooks. [homepages.se.edu] homepages.se.edu increases the ability to correctly identify reasons for future.Read more... Importantly, the exercise is not an attempt to predict the future with certainty. Instead, it broadens the range of plausible futures that decision makers actively consider before committing resources. Questions that reveal hidden risks The quality of a premortem depends less on imagination than on asking questions that expose fragile assumptions. Useful prompts include: What assumption proved false? Which belief looked obvious today but turned out to be wrong? What warning signs did we ignore? Were there early indicators that everyone discounted? What dependency failed? Did success rely on a supplier, regulator, technology or partner behaving exactly as expected? What changed externally? Could market conditions, legislation, competitors or public opinion have shifted? Where were estimates too optimistic? Were costs, timelines or adoption rates unrealistic? Who had information that never reached the decision maker? Did organisational hierarchy or group pressure suppress concerns? Which small problem cascaded? Many expensive failures arise from several manageable issues combining rather than one catastrophic mistake. These questions help distinguish superficial risks (\"the schedule slipped\") from underlying mechanisms (\"the schedule assumed specialist staff would remain available, but competing priorities removed them\"). A particularly valuable follow up asks which imagined failures would have been visible before commitment if only someone had looked. Those are often the highest value risks because they are still preventable. How to Use a Premortem to Stress Test Your Strategy in Stage 4 Design Thinking (APPLY) Channel: Create & Innovate with Andrew & Gaia Grant Open on YouTube Turning imagined failures into better decisions A premortem is valuable only if it changes the decision rather than merely producing a list of worries. After generating possible failure modes, they should be grouped according to both likelihood and consequence . Some require redesigning the plan entirely, while others simply justify contingency measures or additional monitoring. Typical responses include: strengthening assumptions with additional evidence; running a small pilot before committing fully; adding checkpoints where the decision can be revisited; assigning explicit ownership for major risks; identifying objective indicators that would trigger a change of course; abandoning options whose downside remains unacceptable even after mitigation. This approach preserves one of the central ideas of good decision making: analysis should improve choices rather than merely increase confidence. Common failure modes that premortems expose Although every important decision has unique risks, premortems frequently uncover recurring patterns. Hidden dependency chains. Success depends on several independent events all occurring together, yet planning assumes each will happen without examining their combined fragility. Single points of failure. One individual, supplier, approval or technology quietly becomes indispensable. Planning fallacy. Teams systematically underestimate time, complexity and coordination costs despite experience suggesting otherwise. Escalation of commitment. Early investments make people reluctant to reconsider even when evidence deteriorates. Behavioural research identifies escalation of commitment as one of the major biases affecting project outcomes. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Top Ten Behavioral Biases in Project Management: An Overview Top Ten Behavioral Biases in Project Management: An OverviewJanuary 24, 2022... Published: January 24, 2022 Communication breakdowns. Critical information exists somewhere in the organisation but never reaches those making the decision. Unclear ownership. Everyone assumes someone else is responsible for monitoring an important risk. Recognising these recurring failure modes makes future premortems faster because participants begin with patterns that have repeatedly caused expensive mistakes elsewhere. The Power of Pre mortems: How to Spot Problems Before They Happen Channel: ShadowBox Training Open on YouTube When a premortem is worth the time Not every decision deserves a formal premortem. Running one before choosing a restaurant or drafting an email simply adds unnecessary friction. A premortem becomes worthwhile when several of the following conditions apply: the decision is expensive or difficult to reverse; failure would significantly affect other people; uncertainty is high despite available information; success depends on multiple interacting assumptions; organisational pressure encourages agreement; reputational, legal or safety consequences could be substantial. Examples include launching a major product, relocating a business, making a significant investment, introducing organisational change or adopting critical new technology. For low cost, reversible decisions, rapid experimentation usually provides better learning than extensive hypothetical analysis. Pitfalls and limitations A premortem is not immune to misuse. One common mistake is treating every imagined problem as equally likely. The objective is to improve judgement, not accumulate endless speculative risks. Once obvious hazards have been identified, further brainstorming often produces diminishing returns. Another mistake is confusing pessimism with realism. Effective premortems identify plausible failure mechanisms supported by evidence or experience rather than dramatic worst case scenarios. The exercise can also fail if organisational culture discourages honesty."},{"title":"Shared Risk","url":"/shared-risk/","description":"A decision deserves more care when other people bear the risk, even if it feels easy or routine to the decision-maker.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Decision Stakes","level":3,"text":"Shared Risk Who Pays If This Goes Wrong? Shared Risk Shared Risk Who Pays If This Goes Wrong? A decision deserves more care when other people bear the risk, even if it feels easy or routine to the decision maker. A decision deserves more care when other people bear the risk, even if it feels easy or routine to the decision maker. population context Affected parties as a stakes signal Borrowing risk control logic from safety practice How to add consultation without creating gridlock improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes shared risk Introduction Good decision making is not only about protecting yourself. A choice that feels low risk from your own perspective may expose other people to costs, inconvenience, danger, or long term commitments they did not choose. Within the broader practice of matching analysis to decision stakes , one of the strongest signals that a decision deserves greater care is that its consequences will be shared. A simple shift in perspective changes the quality of thinking: instead of asking only, “What happens if I am wrong?”, ask, “Who else pays if I am wrong?” This question often reveals hidden risks, identifies people who should be consulted before acting, and encourages safeguards that reduce harm without unnecessarily slowing every decision. Who Pays If This Goes Wrong? Many poor decisions arise because the decision maker experiences different incentives from the people who bear the consequences. Economists sometimes describe this as a principal–agent problem, but the practical idea is straightforward: the person choosing is not always the person carrying the risk. Common examples include: A manager adopting a new workflow that increases employees' workload. A software engineer deploying a change that customers cannot easily undo. A parent making a financial commitment that affects the entire household. A project leader accepting an ambitious deadline that colleagues must meet. A local authority approving development that permanently changes a neighbourhood. These decisions differ from purely personal choices because the downside is distributed across other people. That makes the decision effectively higher stakes, even if it feels routine to the person making it. The UK Health and Safety Executive (HSE) places this idea at the centre of practical risk assessment by requiring organisations to identify not only hazards but also who might be harmed and how . The first step is therefore not merely identifying a problem but identifying the people exposed to its consequences. [HSE] hse.gov.uk risk HSEManaging risks and risk assessment at work: Overview24 Mar 2025 — As an employer, you must make a 'suitable and sufficient assessment'... Mastering Risk Assessment: Strategic Decision Making for Success! Channel: Critical Thinking Secrets Open on YouTube Affected Parties Are a Signal That More Thinking Is Needed Not every decision involving others requires lengthy deliberation. The important question is whether other people become involuntary participants in the risk. Useful questions include: Who gains the benefit? Who bears the cost if things fail? Who cannot easily opt out? Who lacks information that I already possess? Who will have to repair the damage if my assumptions prove wrong? These questions often reveal risks that ordinary cost benefit thinking misses. For example, selecting a new internal software tool may appear inexpensive because the purchase price is low. However, if hundreds of employees must learn new systems, experience temporary productivity losses, or migrate years of work, the true cost is distributed rather than visible on a budget sheet. Similarly, a physician choosing among treatments, a school changing safeguarding procedures, or a company altering privacy settings all make decisions whose consequences fall partly on people with less control over the outcome. The broader the circle of affected people, the stronger the argument for slowing down and testing assumptions before acting. What Is the Principal Agent Problem? Channel: Marginal Revolution University Open on YouTube Borrowing Risk Control Logic from Safety Practice Safety critical industries have long recognised that decision makers must account for people beyond themselves. Although everyday choices rarely require formal risk analysis, several principles transfer remarkably well. The HSE's risk assessment framework asks organisations to: [youtube.com] youtube.com Risk Assessment 5 Steps QUICKLY! Stakeholder Engagement in Risk Management Decisions Exclusive Lesson... Risk Assessment 5 Steps QUICKLY! 😅 Channel: Safeti Your Trusted Health and Safety Partner Open on YouTube 1. Identify hazards. 2. Decide who might be harmed. 3. Evaluate the likelihood and severity of harm. 4. Introduce proportionate controls. 5. Review decisions when circumstances change. HSE Applied to everyday judgement, the same structure becomes: Risk control questionEveryday equivalentWhat could go wrong?What assumptions am I making?Who might be harmed?Who bears the downside besides me?How serious is the harm?Is inconvenience, financial loss, or lasting damage possible?What controls reduce risk?Can I test, phase, or limit the decision first?When should I review?How will I know if correction is needed? Notice that none of these steps require certainty. They simply make hidden consequences visible before action. Consultation Without Creating Gridlock A common mistake is assuming that involving affected people means every decision must become a committee decision. Effective consultation is not unanimous agreement; it is improving information before commitment. Consultation is most valuable when other people possess information that the decision maker lacks, when they understand operational realities, or when they will experience the consequences directly. A practical approach is to scale consultation with the stakes. Low shared risk ... Inform people. Invite brief feedback . Decide quickly. Moderate shared risk ... Consult those directly affected. Test assumptions. Adjust obvious problems before implementation. High shared risk ... Seek perspectives from multiple affected groups. Document important assumptions. Explore alternatives. Create monitoring and review points before irreversible commitment. Health and safety guidance similarly distinguishes consultation from surrendering responsibility. Decision makers remain accountable, but consulting affected people often produces better risk identification because those doing the work frequently understand practical hazards that senior leaders overlook. Digital Education Resource Archive Practical Ways to Reduce Shared Risk When a decision affects others, several techniques reduce potential harm without imposing excessive bureaucracy. Start with reversible trials If possible, introduce a pilot rather than a permanent change. Examples include: running a new policy in one department; testing software with a small user group; introducing temporary scheduling changes before permanent adoption. Reversible experiments generate information while limiting the number of people exposed if assumptions prove wrong. Separate confidence from evidence Decision makers often mistake familiarity for safety. A routine decision may deserve fresh scrutiny if: circumstances have changed; new groups are affected; the scale is much larger than before; previous success depended on conditions that no longer exist. Look for silent stakeholders People affected most are not always those represented in meetings. Consider: future employees; customers; contractors; neighbours; vulnerable users; people with limited bargaining power. Good governance asks not only who is speaking, but also whose interests are absent. Ask who performs the recovery When failures occur, someone usually pays for correction. If the answer is \"someone else\", the original decision deserves additional scrutiny because the costs have been shifted rather than eliminated. Common Failure Modes Several recurring thinking errors increase shared risk. Assuming consent from silence. People may remain quiet because they lack authority, not because they agree. Overweighting personal benefit. Decision makers naturally notice advantages they receive more readily than costs imposed on others. Treating routine decisions as harmless. Repeated small decisions can accumulate into substantial burdens for staff, customers or communities. Ignoring unequal vulnerability. The same mistake affects different people differently. A minor inconvenience for one person may create major hardship for another. Confusing speed with effectiveness. Faster decisions are valuable only when they do not create larger downstream costs through avoidable failures and rework. A Simple Shared Risk Check Before committing to a decision that affects other people, ask: 1. Who besides me experiences the downside? 2. Can those people reasonably avoid the consequences? 3. Have I consulted the people"},{"title":"Two Way Tests","url":"/two-way-tests/","description":"Reversible choices often improve faster through small experiments than through long private debate.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Decision Stakes","level":3,"text":"Two Way Tests When Is Acting the Better Analysis? Two Way Tests Two Way Tests When Is Acting the Better Analysis? Reversible choices often improve faster through small experiments than through long private debate. Reversible choices often improve faster through small experiments than through long private debate. mechanism How reversibility changes the decision standard Small trials that limit downside Review dates, stop loss rules, and exit ramps improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes two way tests Introduction Many decisions feel more important than they really are because we imagine making them once and living with them forever. In practice , a large share of everyday choices are \"two way door\" decisions: they can be reversed, adjusted, or abandoned before they cause lasting harm. The better question is often not \"What is the perfect answer?\" but \"Can I test this safely?\" When a choice is genuinely reversible, small experiments usually produce better information than extended private debate. Jeff Bezos popularised this distinction by arguing that organisations become slow when they apply heavyweight decision processes to choices that can easily be changed later. [Inc.com] inc.com Amazon Founder Jeff Bezos: This Is How Successful People...Dec 3, 2018 — Some decisions are consequential and irreversible or nearly irr... This does not mean acting carelessly. A reversible decision still deserves thought. The difference is that the standard shifts from seeking certainty to designing a test that limits downside while generating reliable feedback . How reversibility changes the decision standard A two way door decision is not defined by being unimportant. It is defined by being recoverable. If you can reverse the choice quickly, cheaply, and without causing major harm to yourself or others, then the value of learning by doing often exceeds the value of collecting more information before acting. [The Uncertainty Project] theuncertaintyproject.org The Uncertainty ProjectDecision TypesType 2: Also known as two way door decisions, these are reversible and have short term consequences... This changes what counts as \"good analysis\". Instead of trying to predict every outcome in advance, the aim becomes to answer three practical questions: How easily can I undo this? How quickly will I know whether it is working? What is the maximum cost if the experiment fails? If the answers are \"easy\", \"soon\", and \"small\", then delaying for perfect confidence usually has diminishing returns. The experiment itself becomes part of the analysis rather than something that happens after analysis is complete. This is the mechanism that allows faster learning without increasing overall risk. [Leading Sapiens] leadingsapiens.com Leading Sapiens How Bezos Thinks Those decisions can use a light weight process. For those, so what if you're wrong? Second, most...Read more... How Great Executives Make Faster Decisions (The 70% Rule) Channel: Mark Murphy Open on YouTube Small trials that limit downside The safest two way door decisions are designed as experiments rather than commitments. Instead of asking whether an idea will work forever, ask whether it is worth testing for a limited period. Common examples include: Trying a different study schedule for one week instead of redesigning an entire semester. Running a pilot meeting format before changing how a whole organisation collaborates. Testing a software tool with one team rather than migrating every department. Taking a short online course before committing to a long qualification. Renting or borrowing equipment before buying expensive versions. Each example narrows the possible loss while preserving the opportunity to learn. A useful way to structure these trials is to reduce one or more dimensions of exposure: Time: test for a week instead of indefinitely. Scale: involve one project instead of every project. Money: begin with a free trial or low cost option. Commitment: avoid contracts that remove the ability to exit. Visibility: test privately before making a public commitment where appropriate. These adjustments do not eliminate uncertainty. They make uncertainty affordable. Jeff Bezos’s explain his famous one way door and two way door decision making Channel: Finance & Tech Career Advice Open on YouTube Why action often produces better evidence than reflection People frequently overestimate how much additional thinking will improve a decision. Many questions cannot be answered by reasoning alone because the missing information only appears after action. For example: You cannot know whether a productivity system suits you until you use it consistently. You cannot accurately judge whether a new workflow improves collaboration until people work with it. You cannot reliably predict whether an exercise routine fits your lifestyle without attempting it. Behavioural research has long shown that people are poor at forecasting many future preferences and experiences. Direct experience often corrects assumptions that seemed convincing beforehand. Well designed experiments therefore produce evidence that is both more relevant and more personalised than abstract speculation. The key insight is that action is not the opposite of analysis. In reversible situations, action is often the fastest way to gather higher quality evidence. Review dates, stop loss rules, and exit ramps Two way door decisions work best when the exit is planned before the experiment begins. Without predefined review points, temporary experiments easily become accidental long term commitments. Three simple safeguards improve almost every reversible test. Set a review date Choose a specific date before starting. Instead of saying, \"I'll see how it goes,\" decide: after one week, after ten customer conversations, after completing three practice sessions, after one monthly billing cycle. A fixed review point prevents both premature abandonment and endless drift. Define a stop loss rule Investors use stop loss rules to limit losses before emotions take over. The same idea works in everyday decisions. Examples include: stop if costs exceed a set budget; stop if the experiment consistently interferes with sleep or health; stop if agreed performance measures decline for several review periods. Predetermined limits reduce the temptation to continue simply because time or effort has already been invested. Why the Best Startup Founders Prioritize Action Over Certainty Channel: The Tech Leader's Playbook Open on YouTube Build an exit ramp A decision is more reversible when leaving has already been considered. Examples include: keeping backups before changing software; choosing monthly subscriptions rather than long contracts; maintaining previous procedures until the replacement has proved itself; documenting changes so they can be reversed quickly. The easier it is to return, the more confidently small experiments can be attempted. Common mistakes when judging reversibility People often misclassify decisions because they focus on one aspect of reversibility while ignoring others. Frequent errors include: Ignoring hidden switching costs. A free trial may still require weeks of setup and retraining. Confusing embarrassment with irreversibility. Many decisions feel permanent only because changing your mind is uncomfortable. Overlooking effects on others. A decision that is reversible for one person may impose significant costs on colleagues, customers, or family. Waiting for complete certainty. By the time nearly all uncertainty has disappeared, the opportunity to learn quickly may already have been lost. Bezos has argued that many decisions can be made with roughly 70% of the information desired, provided organisations are good at recognising and correcting mistakes rapidly. [Leading Sapiens] leadingsapiens.com Leading Sapiens How Bezos Thinks Those decisions can use a light weight process. For those, so what if you're wrong? Second, most...Read more... Recognising these pitfalls helps preserve the central advantage of two way door decisions: rapid learning with bounded risk. Turning more decisions into safe experiments Sometimes a choice appears irreversible only because it has been framed too broadly. Breaking it into smaller steps can transform a one way commitment into a sequence of reversible tests. Instead of deciding whether to \"change careers\", someone might first shadow professionals, complete a short project, freelance part time, or take evening training. Rather than deciding whether to \"launch a business\", they might first interview potential customers, build a prototype, or test demand with a limited offering. This approach does not remove uncertainty. It changes the structure of uncertainty. Each successful experiment increases confidence while each unsuccessful one limits the cost of being wrong. The practical lesson is straightforward: when consequences are genuinely reversible, the highest quality analysis often comes from"},{"title":"Decision Notes","url":"/decision-notes/","description":"A short decision note can capture assumptions, tradeoffs, regret risks, and review points before a hard-to-reverse choice is made.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Decision Stakes","level":3,"text":"Decision Notes Write the Reasoning Before You Commit Decision Notes Decision Notes Write the Reasoning Before You Commit A short decision note can capture assumptions, tradeoffs, regret risks, and review points before a hard to reverse choice is made. A short decision note can capture assumptions, tradeoffs, regret risks, and review points before a hard to reverse choice is made. policy intervention What a decision note should record Assumptions, constraints, and tradeoffs How review points turn judgment into learning improving your thinking and analytical skills decision stakes decision notes Introduction A written decision note is a short record created before committing to a high stakes or difficult to reverse choice. Its purpose is not to predict the future perfectly, but to capture what you currently believe, why you believe it, what could prove you wrong, and how you will judge the outcome later. This simple practice separates the quality of the decision from the quality of the eventual outcome, making hindsight bias less likely and future learning more reliable. Research on judgement and decision making consistently shows that people reconstruct their past reasoning after outcomes become known, making contemporaneous records especially valuable for improving future judgement. [Noba+2Wikipedia] nobaproject.com NobaJudgment and Decision MakingThis module provides an overview of decision making and includes discussion of many of the common biases... Within a broader framework of checking decision stakes and reversibility, a decision note adds deliberate friction only where it is worthwhile. Instead of trying to eliminate uncertainty, it makes uncertainty visible. Write the Reasoning Before You Commit High stakes decisions often involve large consequences, uncertain evidence, conflicting objectives, and irreversible commitments. Examples include accepting a job requiring relocation, making a major investment, entering a business partnership, signing a long term contract, undertaking a significant medical choice, or making a public organisational decision. Without a written record, memory tends to simplify events into a story that feels inevitable. After learning the outcome, people frequently believe they \"knew it all along\", even when their original confidence or assumptions were very different. Recording the reasoning before acting provides a benchmark against which later reflection can be measured, reducing hindsight distortion and encouraging more accurate self evaluation. [Noba] nobaproject.com NobaJudgment and Decision MakingThis module provides an overview of decision making and includes discussion of many of the common biases... A useful decision note is intentionally brief. The goal is clarity rather than documentation for its own sake. What a Decision Note Should Record A practical decision note usually fits on one page. It should answer the questions that future you will no longer remember accurately. Record: The decision being made. State exactly what is being decided and by when. The options considered. Include the principal alternatives rather than only the chosen path. The objective. What problem is the decision intended to solve? The expected benefits. What outcomes are you hoping to achieve? The principal risks. What are the most plausible ways the decision could fail? Your confidence. Estimate how confident you are, preferably numerically rather than using vague language. Information gaps. Identify what remains uncertain and why you are deciding despite incomplete information. What would change your mind. Specify evidence that would have led you to choose differently. The emphasis is on recording reasoning, not producing exhaustive analysis. If the note becomes lengthy enough that it discourages use, it loses much of its practical value. Assumptions, Constraints, and Trade offs The most valuable part of a decision note is often not the conclusion but the assumptions that support it. Assumptions Every important decision depends on beliefs about facts that may later prove incorrect. Writing assumptions explicitly makes them easier to challenge before commitment. Examples include: expected market conditions estimated project costs likely behaviour of other people health prognosis future availability of resources legal or regulatory stability When assumptions remain implicit, they are difficult for others—or even your future self—to examine critically. How To Use Obsidian for Decision Making 2026 (Step By Step) Channel: Emma's Productivity Lab Open on YouTube Constraints Good decisions are made within real limits rather than ideal conditions. Record constraints such as: budget available time legal obligations ethical commitments organisational policies family or personal responsibilities Making constraints explicit prevents later criticism based on options that were never realistically available. Trade offs High quality decisions rarely maximise every objective simultaneously. A useful note states the trade offs directly: choosing speed over completeness accepting higher cost for lower risk sacrificing short term gains for long term flexibility prioritising reliability over innovation Explicit trade offs reduce the temptation to pretend that one option was simply \"best\" in every respect. Decision quality frameworks similarly emphasise clear values, feasible alternatives, reliable information, and logically consistent reasoning rather than judging decisions solely by eventual outcomes. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Decision quality Decision quality Record the Main Sources of Regret Risk One practical addition is to ask: \"If this turns out badly, what will I most likely wish I had considered?\" Potential regret risks include: ignoring contradictory evidence relying too heavily on optimistic forecasts underestimating implementation difficulty overlooking impacts on other people assuming conditions will remain stable This exercise encourages consideration of failure modes before commitment rather than after disappointment. Importantly, regret risk is different from predicting failure. It identifies where additional thinking or information could genuinely improve the decision instead of merely increasing confidence. How to Make High Stakes Decisions with Only 10% Information ft Hiten Shah (@hitenshow) Channel: Life of Luba Open on YouTube How Review Points Turn Judgement into Learning A decision note becomes far more valuable when paired with predetermined review points. Instead of asking only whether the decision \"worked\", schedule specific future questions: After one month, have the expected indicators appeared? After six months, which assumptions proved incorrect? Were warning signs visible but ignored? Did success or failure result from skill, luck, or external events? Separating these reviews from the emotional aftermath improves calibration—the ability to judge uncertainty accurately over time. Good calibration develops by comparing predicted outcomes with actual results across many decisions rather than evaluating isolated successes or failures. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersThe Impact of Cognitive Biases on Professionals' Decision...by V Berthet · 2022 · Cited by 305 — The author reviewed the resear... Avoid Judging Decisions Only by Outcomes A fortunate outcome does not necessarily imply a good decision. Likewise, a poor outcome does not automatically mean the reasoning was flawed. For example: An investment based on careful research may lose value because of an unforeseen geopolitical event. A poorly researched investment may generate profits simply because markets moved favourably. Evaluating only the outcome encourages outcome bias —the tendency to judge decisions by results rather than by the quality of reasoning available at the time. Written decision notes preserve the original evidence base, making later evaluation substantially fairer. [Noba] nobaproject.com NobaJudgment and Decision MakingThis module provides an overview of decision making and includes discussion of many of the common biases... Keep the Process Lightweight Decision notes should scale with the stakes. For routine, easily reversible decisions, a written note is unnecessary. For one way door decisions, however, even ten minutes spent writing can expose hidden assumptions, reveal missing alternatives, or prompt a final conversation with someone who sees the problem differently. A concise structure often works well: 1. Decision. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Decision quality Decision quality 2. Objective. 3. Alternatives considered. 4. Key assumptions. 5. Constraints. 6. Trade offs accepted. 7. Biggest risks. 8. Confidence estimate. 9. Evidence that would change the decision. 10. Review date and success criteria. The value lies less in producing documentation than in slowing thought just enough to improve judgement where mistakes are expensive and difficult to reverse. Building a Mental Model Toolbox with Shane Parrish Channel: The Science Of"},{"title":"Deliberate Practice","url":"/deliberate-practice/","description":"Better judgment comes from targeted practice with feedback, not simply from years of unexamined experience.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Deliberate Practice Why Experience Alone Does Not Fix Thinking Deliberate Practice Deliberate Practice Why Experience Alone Does Not Fix Thinking Better judgment comes from targeted practice with feedback, not simply from years of unexamined experience. Better judgment comes from targeted practice with feedback, not simply from years of unexamined experience. policy intervention What deliberate practice changes Reasoning subskills to train Feedback and adjustment loops improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice Introduction Experience alone does not reliably fix poor thinking. People can spend years making decisions, reading arguments, joining debates or analysing problems while quietly rehearsing the same shortcuts, blind spots and overconfidence . Deliberate practice changes that pattern by turning reasoning into a trainable performance: isolate a subskill, attempt a hard but manageable task, get feedback , revise the method, and repeat until the improved habit becomes easier to use under pressure. In research on expertise, deliberate practice is usually defined by focused tasks, immediate feedback, opportunities to evaluate errors, and repeated refinement rather than by the raw number of hours spent “doing” an activity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Deliberate practice and acquisition of expert performanceby KA Ericsson · 2008 · Cited by 2927 — DP also involves the provision of... For thinking and analytical skills, this matters because the goal is not to become more argumentative or more confident. The goal is to become more corrigible: better at noticing when a conclusion is weak, when evidence is missing, when confidence is inflated, and when a problem needs a different reasoning strategy. Studies of critical thinking instruction, argument mapping, debiasing and forecasting all point towards the same practical lesson: reasoning improves most when practice is explicit, repeated, feedback rich and tied to visible performance rather than left as a vague aspiration. [Sage Journals+2City Research Online] journals.sagepub.com First, the evidence on...Read more... What deliberate practice changes The central shift is from “I thought about it” to “I practised a specific move and checked whether it worked”. In ordinary experience, feedback is often delayed, noisy or absent. A manager may make a hiring decision and never know whether a rejected candidate would have been better. A student may write an essay, receive a grade, and still not know which inference failed. A clinician, analyst or investor may be rewarded by luck and punished despite good reasoning. Without clean feedback, repeated exposure can strengthen habits that feel fluent but are not accurate. Deliberate practice tries to make reasoning observable. Instead of treating judgement as a private mental event, it asks for an external product: a probability estimate, an argument map, a written explanation of assumptions, a comparison of rival hypotheses , a prediction log, or a worked solution with error notes. This gives the learner and coach something to inspect. The Ericsson tradition emphasises practice that targets a defined weakness, is effortful, and includes feedback and repeated attempts; later debates caution that deliberate practice is not the only source of expert performance, but the training design principle remains valuable for skills where performance can be measured and corrected. [PubMed+2PMC] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Deliberate practice and acquisition of expert performanceby KA Ericsson · 2008 · Cited by 2927 — DP also involves the provision of... A useful example comes from science education. In work on teaching quantitative critical thinking in physics labs, students were repeatedly asked to make decisions based on data, compare datasets with models, and improve methods in response to evidence. After the prompts were gradually withdrawn, students in the experimental condition were far more likely than students in traditional labs to spontaneously improve experimental methods and explain limitations in a physical model. The important point is not that physics labs are the only route to better reasoning; it is that the course made the target reasoning move visible, practised it repeatedly, and faded support only after students had internalised the move. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Teaching Critical Thinking arXiv Teaching Critical Thinking The same logic applies outside classrooms. A person trying to improve judgement should not merely “read more about bias”. They should choose one reasoning behaviour at a time: estimating uncertainty, distinguishing evidence from interpretation, identifying the weakest premise, generating alternative explanations, or updating a belief after new information. Then they need a task where success and failure can be seen. Reasoning subskills to train Deliberate practice works best when “better thinking” is broken into components. Critical thinking is often described as purposeful, self regulated judgement involving interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference and explanation. That definition is helpful because each part can become a practice target rather than an abstract virtue. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Argument reconstruction is one of the most trainable subskills. A learner takes a short article, memo, speech or essay and identifies the main conclusion, supporting reasons, hidden assumptions and objections. Argument mapping makes this visual, using boxes and arrows to show how claims depend on one another. Studies and reviews of argument mapping suggest that it can improve critical thinking because it forces the structure of reasoning into the open: a weak link can no longer hide inside polished prose. [ThinkerU+2Springer Nature Link] thinkeru.org Open source on thinkeru.org. Evidence evaluation is another core target. The practical task is to ask what kind of evidence would actually support the claim. A correlation, anecdote, expert opinion, controlled study and base rate statistic do not carry the same weight. Deliberate practice here might involve ranking evidence quality, spotting missing comparison groups, or rewriting a claim so it matches the strength of the evidence. Alternative generation trains the habit of asking, “What else could explain this?” This is especially important when a first explanation feels obvious. A person may assume a colleague is careless when the real cause is unclear instructions, workload, incentives or missing tools. Practising rival explanations reduces the chance that confidence attaches too quickly to the first plausible story. Calibration is the skill of matching confidence to accuracy. Instead of saying “I’m sure”, the learner gives a probability or confidence interval, later checks the outcome, and adjusts. Forecasting tournaments and calibration exercises are valuable because they create repeated, scoreable judgements. The Good Judgment Project became influential precisely because it treated forecasting as a trainable judgement skill, combining probability training, practice, feedback, updating and aggregation. [AI Impacts] aiimpacts.org Open source on aiimpacts.org. Error diagnosis turns mistakes into material for improvement. The question is not just “Was I wrong?” but “What kind of wrong was this?” Common categories include misread evidence, ignored base rates, vague definitions, motivated interpretation, poor numeracy, overconfidence, and failure to consider alternatives. A short error log can reveal that a person’s recurring weakness is not “bad thinking” in general but, for example, jumping from anecdotes to general claims. Why feedback has to be specific Feedback is the difference between practice and repetition. But not all feedback is useful. “Good argument”, “weak analysis” or “be more critical” gives too little information to change behaviour. Deliberate practice needs feedback that points to the move that failed and what a better move would look like. In critical thinking instruction, broad exposure is less reliable than explicit teaching plus practice. A major meta analysis of critical thinking interventions found positive average effects, but also substantial variation across studies, which is exactly what one would expect if “critical thinking” courses differ in whether they teach skills directly, require practice, and provide feedback. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com First, the evidence on...Read more... Argument mapping shows why specificity matters. A learner can be told that an essay is unconvincing and still not know what to do. But if a map shows that the conclusion rests on an unsupported assumption, or that a reason supports a different conclusion from the one claimed, the learner can revise a precise part of the reasoning. That is closer to how a musician corrects a phrase or a chess player reviews a tactical mistake: the error is local enough to practise. Feedback also needs a credible standard. In many"},{"title":"Alternatives","url":"/alternatives-0a2107/","description":"Generating rival explanations slows the jump from a plausible first story to a confident but poorly tested conclusion.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Deliberate Practice","level":3,"text":"Alternatives What else could explain this? Alternatives Alternatives What else could explain this? Generating rival explanations slows the jump from a plausible first story to a confident but poorly tested conclusion. Generating rival explanations slows the jump from a plausible first story to a confident but poorly tested conclusion. mechanism Why the first plausible story feels convincing Alternative generation drills for everyday cases How to compare rival explanations fairly improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice alternatives Introduction One of the most effective reasoning habits is deceptively simple: before becoming confident in your first explanation, deliberately ask what else could explain the same facts. This practice slows the natural tendency to settle on the first story that feels coherent and instead forces a comparison between competing possibilities. It is not about becoming indecisive. It is about making confidence depend on how well an explanation survives comparison with plausible alternatives rather than on how quickly it comes to mind. Within deliberate practice for reasoning, generating alternative explanations is a trainable skill rather than a personality trait. Research in psychology, judgement and decision making, and diagnostic reasoning consistently shows that people become less vulnerable to confirmation bias and premature closure when they actively generate and test rival hypotheses instead of merely searching for evidence supporting their initial idea. [PubMed+2UC San Diego Pages] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov In two separate but conceptually parallel Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... Why the first plausible story feels convincing Humans naturally seek coherence. Faced with incomplete information, the brain rapidly constructs a narrative that links the available facts. Once this narrative exists, it becomes the reference point against which new information is interpreted. This tendency is closely related to confirmation bias: people preferentially notice, interpret and remember information that supports their existing explanation while overlooking evidence that would favour a different one. The process is usually unconscious rather than deliberate. [Encyclopedia Britannica+2UC San Diego Pages] britannica.com Encyclopedia BritannicaConfirmation bias Definition, Examples, Psychology, & FactsAugust 1, 2016 — 11 Jun 2026 — Confirmation bias, peo... Published: August 1, 2016 Several mechanisms reinforce this effect: Fluency is mistaken for accuracy. A story that is easy to imagine often feels more likely than it really is. Early commitment shapes later search. Once an explanation is accepted, people begin collecting supporting evidence instead of asking whether another explanation would fit better. Confidence changes information gathering. Experimental work shows that higher confidence in an initial judgement increases selective information sampling, making people more likely to seek confirming evidence and less likely to search broadly. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Humans actively sample evidence to support prior beliefs PMCby P Kaanders · 2022 · Cited by 68 — Accordingly, we predict that confir... The danger is not that the first explanation is always wrong. Often it is correct. The problem is that confidence becomes attached to arrival order rather than evidential strength. Why deliberately generating alternatives improves reasoning Generating alternative explanations changes the task from asking, \"Can I explain this?\" to asking, \"Which explanation explains it best?\" This subtle shift produces several improvements. First, it exposes hidden assumptions . An explanation that seemed inevitable often depends on assumptions that only become visible when another possibility is considered. Second, it changes how evidence is evaluated. Instead of asking whether evidence fits a favourite explanation, the reasoner asks whether the evidence discriminates between competing explanations. Third, it weakens motivated reasoning. Researchers Charles Lord, Mark Lepper and Elizabeth Preston found that simply instructing people to \"consider the opposite\" reduced several forms of biased judgement more effectively than merely telling participants to be objective or fair. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov In two separate but conceptually parallel Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... Importantly, the goal is not to generate endless possibilities. A few genuinely plausible rivals are usually enough to reveal whether the first explanation deserves confidence. Alternative generation drills for everyday cases Like any reasoning skill, producing rival explanations improves with repetition. The \"three explanations\" exercise Whenever a conclusion feels obvious, pause before accepting it. Write down: How generative AI Is changing clinical reasoning in medical education DDx by Sketchy Channel: Sketchy Learning Open on YouTube 1. Your current explanation. 2. A second explanation that could also fit the evidence. 3. A third explanation that initially seems less likely but remains plausible. For example: Observation: A colleague did not reply to an email. Instead of stopping at: \"They're ignoring me.\" Also generate: \"They have not seen the email.\" \"They are waiting for information before replying.\" \"The message was unclear or went to spam.\" The exercise is valuable even when the original explanation eventually proves correct because it trains the habit of separating plausibility from certainty. Reverse your preferred conclusion Suppose you believe: \"Project X failed because the team lacked competence.\" Ask instead: \"If that explanation were false, what would I expect to see?\" Possible alternatives might include: unclear objectives unrealistic deadlines inadequate resources changing customer requirements This mirrors the experimentally supported \"consider the opposite\" strategy, which encourages active search for disconfirming possibilities rather than passive acceptance of a favoured interpretation. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov In two separate but conceptually parallel Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... Separate observations from explanations Many reasoning mistakes arise because explanations are mistaken for observations. Instead of writing: \"The customer was angry.\" Separate the layers: Observed facts Raised voice. Short responses. Cancelled meeting. Possible explanations Angry. Under severe time pressure. Distracted by another problem. Frustrated with a different issue entirely. This makes it easier to compare hypotheses fairly. Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w/ Nate Otey Channel: The Stoa · Views: 3.5K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube How to compare rival explanations fairly Generating alternatives is only the first step. They also need to be evaluated consistently. Useful questions include: Which explanation accounts for the greatest number of observed facts? Which requires the fewest unsupported assumptions? Which makes predictions that could actually be tested? Which explanation would most surprise me if new evidence appeared? What evidence would clearly distinguish between these possibilities? Notice that these questions compare explanations directly rather than asking whether one explanation is merely possible. A useful practical habit is to score each explanation against the same evidence instead of evaluating only your preferred one. This reduces the temptation to apply stricter standards to competing ideas than to your own. Lessons from diagnostic reasoning Medicine provides one of the clearest examples of why alternative explanations matter. Clinicians often generate several possible diagnoses early in an assessment rather than committing immediately to the first condition that matches a patient's symptoms . The subsequent consultation is then used to collect evidence that distinguishes among these competing hypotheses. This hypothetico deductive approach is widely recognised in research on clinical reasoning. While experienced practitioners may recognise familiar patterns quickly, they still improve diagnostic accuracy by considering differential diagnoses rather than relying solely on the first plausible interpretation. [PMC+2Brighton University CRIS] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Models of clinical reasoning with a focus on general practiceby S YAZDANI · 2017 · Cited by 105 — Six models of clinical reasoning wer... The lesson transfers well beyond medicine. Whether evaluating financial decisions, workplace conflicts, technical failures or news stories, the question is not simply \"What explains this?\" but \"What competing explanations deserve consideration before I"},{"title":"Argument Maps","url":"/argument-maps-d17ee5/","description":"Argument maps turn messy prose into visible claims, reasons and assumptions so weak links can be fixed instead of vaguely criticised.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Deliberate Practice","level":3,"text":"Argument Maps Can argument maps expose weak reasoning? Argument Maps Argument Maps Can argument maps expose weak reasoning? Argument maps turn messy prose into visible claims, reasons and assumptions so weak links can be fixed instead of vaguely criticised. Argument maps turn messy prose into visible claims, reasons and assumptions so weak links can be fixed instead of vaguely criticised. policy intervention How argument maps turn prose into structure Finding hidden assumptions and unsupported links Practice drills with short articles and memos improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice argument maps Introduction Argument maps are one of the most effective tools for finding weak premises because they convert a flowing piece of prose into a visible network of claims, reasons and assumptions. Once every supporting statement has to occupy its own place on a map, vague persuasion becomes explicit reasoning. Weak evidence , missing links, hidden assumptions and unsupported leaps are much harder to overlook. Research on argument mapping suggests that, when used as part of deliberate practice with feedback, it can produce larger improvements in critical thinking than many traditional discussion based approaches. [ResearchGate+2ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Argument Maps Improve Critical ThinkingComputer based argument mapping greatly enhances student critical think ing, mo... For someone trying to improve analytical thinking, the value is practical rather than philosophical. Instead of asking the general question, \"Is this argument convincing?\", an argument map asks several narrower questions: What exactly is the conclusion? Which premises support it? Which premises depend on each other? Which assumptions have been left unstated? Those questions expose the weakest parts of the reasoning before they become habits. How argument maps turn prose into structure Ordinary writing unfolds one sentence after another, but reasoning is rarely linear. Authors move between evidence, examples, assumptions and conclusions without always signalling the logical relationships. Argument maps replace that sequence with a diagram in which each box contains a single claim and arrows indicate which claims support or attack others. This makes the inferential structure—not merely the wording—visible. [U Sask LibGuides+2reasons.io] libguides.usask.ca U Sask Lib Guides Critical Thinking Tutorial: Argument Mapping U Sask LibGuidesCritical Thinking Tutorial: Argument Mapping Research Guides8 Jan 2026 — An argument map is a visual representation of... A well constructed map normally separates: the main conclusion direct supporting premises intermediate conclusions that support later claims objections and counterarguments rebuttals implicit assumptions added where the argument would otherwise fail This separation matters because people often criticise arguments at the wrong level. They attack a conclusion when the real weakness lies several steps earlier in an unsupported intermediate claim. Consider a short example: \"The council should ban cars from the city centre because air pollution is harmful.\" In prose this sounds straightforward. Mapping quickly reveals several missing links: Air pollution in this city is caused substantially by cars. A ban would reduce pollution enough to matter. The benefits outweigh the economic and practical costs. No less restrictive policy would achieve similar results. None of these assumptions automatically follows from the original sentence. Once they are visible, each becomes something that can be tested with evidence rather than accepted implicitly. Finding hidden assumptions and unsupported links Many arguments fail not because the stated premises are false, but because essential premises were never stated. Logicians often describe such incomplete arguments as enthymemes —arguments that rely on an unstated assumption. Argument mapping encourages learners to reconstruct these hidden premises explicitly before evaluating the reasoning. Recent research in computational argumentation has also focused on identifying implicit premises because automated reasoning systems cannot reliably assess an argument until these missing steps have been represented. [arXiv] arxiv.org Understanding Enthymemes in Argument Maps: Bridging Argument Mining and Logic based ArgumentationAugust 16, 2024... Published: August 16, 2024 Several common warning signs emerge once an argument has been mapped. Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w/ Nate Otey Channel: The Stoa · Views: 3.5K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube A premise supports nothing Sometimes writers include facts that appear impressive but contribute nothing to the conclusion. For example: Company revenue increased. The CEO graduated from a prestigious university. Therefore the company is well managed. After mapping, the educational background simply hangs disconnected unless an additional premise explains why it is relevant. A conclusion rests on only one fragile premise Some conclusions depend entirely on a single uncertain claim. For example: Social media caused lower exam scores. Therefore schools should prohibit student phone use. If the causal premise is poorly supported, the whole argument becomes vulnerable. The map identifies exactly where further evidence is needed. Independent reasons are confused with linked reasons Argument maps distinguish between premises that work independently and premises that only support the conclusion together. For example: All medicines with ingredient X reduce symptoms . Drug A contains ingredient X. Neither premise alone supports the conclusion. Together they do. Students frequently separate linked premises incorrectly, creating the illusion of multiple reasons when there is really only one combined inference. Studies of argument mapping instruction note that visualising these relationships helps instructors diagnose such errors more precisely. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Argument Maps Improve Critical ThinkingComputer based argument mapping greatly enhances student critical think ing, mo... Hidden value judgements Many policy arguments rely on unstated values rather than hidden facts. For example: Public parks should remain undeveloped. Therefore housing should not be built there. The missing premise might be: Preserving public green space is more important than increasing housing supply in this case. Making value assumptions explicit improves disagreement because participants can discuss the real point of contention instead of arguing about secondary facts. Charting the Thicket: Using Argument Mapping to Explore Controversial Topics Channel: International Studies Association (ISA) Open on YouTube Why weak premises often survive in fluent writing Fluent prose can disguise poor reasoning because readers naturally fill in missing steps. Psychologists refer to this tendency as generating coherence: readers unconsciously complete incomplete explanations if the overall story feels plausible. Argument maps interrupt this process by forcing every supporting claim to be represented individually. If a reader cannot draw an arrow from one statement to another without inventing extra information, the argument contains a missing premise. This explains why people often feel an argument is persuasive yet struggle to explain exactly why. The persuasive impression comes from narrative flow, while the logical support remains incomplete. Mapping therefore separates two different questions: Does this sound convincing? Does each conclusion genuinely follow from its stated support? Those questions often produce different answers. Practice drills with short articles and memos Argument mapping improves through repeated, focused exercises rather than occasional use. The goal is not producing beautiful diagrams but repeatedly identifying where reasoning depends on assumptions that deserve inspection. Useful drills include: 1. Map a newspaper editorial. Restrict yourself to the main conclusion and no more than six supporting claims. Then identify one unstated assumption that must be true for the argument to succeed. 2. Map a workplace memo. Managers frequently justify recommendations with mixed evidence, predictions and priorities. Separate factual premises from value judgements before evaluating either. 3. Compare two competing opinion pieces. Construct separate maps before deciding which is stronger. Avoid comparing conclusions until each structure has been analysed independently. 4. Delete one premise. Remove each premise in turn and ask whether the conclusion still follows. Premises whose removal collapses the argument deserve the closest scrutiny. 5. Strengthen the weakest link. Rather than criticising the argument immediately, ask what additional evidence would make its weakest premise more credible. This develops charitable interpretation alongside critical evaluation. A"},{"title":"Calibration","url":"/calibration-5b6c7f/","description":"Calibration practice teaches people to attach numbers to uncertainty, check outcomes, and notice when confidence outruns accuracy.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Deliberate Practice","level":3,"text":"Calibration How sure should you really be? Calibration Calibration How sure should you really be? Calibration practice teaches people to attach numbers to uncertainty, check outcomes, and notice when confidence outruns accuracy. Calibration practice teaches people to attach numbers to uncertainty, check outcomes, and notice when confidence outruns accuracy. mechanism Why confidence feels clearer than accuracy Using probabilities, intervals and outcome checks Keeping a calibration log without overcomplicating it improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice calibration Introduction Calibration is the habit of matching your confidence to the strength of the evidence rather than to how convincing an idea feels. Someone who is well calibrated is not necessarily more knowledgeable than everyone else; they are simply better at saying “I am about 70% sure” when they are right about 70% of the time, and reserving near certainty for genuinely exceptional cases. This makes calibration one of the most practical reasoning skills to train because it converts vague confidence into something measurable and improvable. Research from psychology, forecasting and decision science consistently shows that structured feedback on probability judgements can reduce overconfidence and improve the quality of decisions, even with relatively short training programmes. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryCalibration training for improving probabilistic judgments...by R Gruetzemacher · 2024 · Cited by 4 — We describe an... Within deliberate practice , calibration is valuable because it creates a feedback loop . Instead of asking only whether you were correct, you also ask whether your level of confidence deserved to be that high. Over time, this helps separate genuine expertise from the illusion of certainty. Why confidence feels clearer than accuracy Human intuition naturally produces feelings of certainty, but those feelings are not direct measurements of truth. Familiarity, fluency, strong narratives and recent experiences can all make an answer feel more convincing than the available evidence justifies. As a result, people often display overprecision —being too certain that a particular answer is correct—even when their overall knowledge is reasonable. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Overconfidence effect Overconfidence effect A useful distinction is between accuracy and calibration . Accuracy asks whether the answer was correct. Calibration asks whether the stated confidence matched the long run frequency of being correct. Imagine answering 100 questions while claiming 90% confidence each time. Perfect calibration means approximately 90 of those answers should be correct. If only 70 are correct, confidence has outrun reality. Conversely, consistently being correct 90% of the time while claiming only 60% confidence indicates underconfidence rather than good judgement. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Calibrated probability assessment Calibrated probability assessment This distinction matters because many important decisions involve uncertainty that cannot be eliminated. Better judgement often comes not from becoming certain, but from expressing uncertainty honestly enough that later feedback can improve future decisions. Using probabilities, intervals and outcome checks Calibration drills work because they force uncertainty into the open. Instead of recording only conclusions, they require explicit numerical estimates that can later be checked. Assign probabilities instead of certainty A simple exercise is to replace statements like \"I'm sure\" or \"I think probably\" with numerical probabilities . For example: \"I am 60% confident this meeting will be cancelled.\" \"I am 80% confident this diagnosis is correct.\" \"There is a 30% chance this project finishes before Friday.\" The exact number is less important than committing to one before the outcome is known. Once the result arrives, compare prediction with reality. Over dozens or hundreds of predictions, patterns emerge. Some people discover that every 90% judgement behaves like an actual 70% judgement. Others find that they avoid expressing high confidence even when evidence supports it. Both patterns become visible only when confidence is quantified. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Calibrated probability assessment Calibrated probability assessment Charting the Thicket: Using Argument Mapping to Explore Controversial Topics Channel: International Studies Association (ISA) Open on YouTube Practise with confidence intervals Not every judgement is yes or no. Many involve estimating quantities. Instead of predicting a single value, estimate a range that you believe has a specified chance of containing the truth. Examples include: \"I am 90% confident the population lies between X and Y.\" \"I am 90% confident this task will take between three and six hours.\" \"I am 90% confident the answer falls within this numerical range.\" Most people make these intervals far too narrow. Studies repeatedly find that intervals intended to capture the correct answer 90% of the time often succeed only about half the time, revealing substantial overconfidence. A practical correction is to widen intervals until observed success approaches the intended confidence level. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Overconfidence effect Overconfidence effect Separate judgement from outcome One incorrect prediction does not necessarily indicate poor reasoning, just as one lucky success does not prove good judgement. Suppose you assign a 20% chance that a supplier misses a deadline. If the supplier does miss it, your prediction was not \"wrong\"; a one in five event should occur occasionally. Likewise, assigning 99% confidence to an outcome that fails reveals a serious calibration problem even if similar predictions often succeed. Calibration therefore evaluates many predictions together rather than judging isolated successes or failures. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score Keeping a calibration log without overcomplicating it An effective calibration log can remain remarkably simple. The goal is consistency rather than elaborate record keeping. For each prediction, record: ItemExampleDate25 JunePrediction\"Client approves proposal this week.\"Confidence70%Reason\"Strong prior relationship; minor revisions outstanding.\"OutcomeApproved after four daysReflectionConfidence slightly high because I ignored scheduling delays. After accumulating around 30–50 predictions, review them by confidence level. For example: Confidence statedActual success rate60%58%70%73%80%66%90%74% The objective is not perfection but convergence. If your 90% predictions repeatedly succeed only about three quarters of the time, reserve 90% confidence for much stronger evidence. Forecasting practitioners often recommend reviewing predictions in batches rather than emotionally reacting to individual mistakes. This encourages learning from statistical patterns rather than memorable anecdotes . [Commoncog] commoncog.com how do you evaluate your own predictions How Do You Evaluate Your Own Predictions?Dec 17, 2019 — This post provides a comprehensive summary of the technique that Tetlock... Calibration drills that target overconfidence Different drills expose different forms of misplaced certainty. The \"Could I be wrong?\" drill Before finalising an answer, ask: What evidence would change my mind? What probability would I assign to the strongest alternative? What assumption am I treating as certain without checking? The aim is not endless doubt but preventing unwarranted certainty from becoming invisible. Model Calibration Brier Score Explained Channel: DataMListic Open on YouTube The outside view adjustment After making an initial estimate, compare it with similar past situations. Instead of asking only, \"How will this project unfold?\" also ask, \"How long have comparable projects actually taken?\" External reference points frequently reveal that initial confidence relied too heavily on the unique story rather than historical evidence. Forced probability bins Restrict yourself to confidence categories such as: 55% 65% 75% 85% 95% This discourages meaningless precision such as claiming 83.7% confidence while still requiring genuine distinctions between weak, moderate and strong evidence. Delayed answer drill When possible, write down a prediction before gathering additional opinions or seeing the eventual result. This prevents hindsight from rewriting your memory of how certain you really were. Hindsight bias often makes people remember having been \"obviously right\" even when their original judgement was much less confident. Measuring improvement without chasing perfect scores Calibration improves through repeated prediction, feedback and adjustment rather than through occasional dramatic insights. One common evaluation method is the Brier score , which measures how closely probability estimates match actual outcomes. Unlike simple right or wrong scoring, it rewards honest"},{"title":"Error Logs","url":"/error-logs/","description":"An error log turns being wrong into useful data by naming the kind of mistake and the correction to practise next.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Deliberate Practice","level":3,"text":"Error Logs What kind of wrong was it? Error Logs Error Logs What kind of wrong was it? An error log turns being wrong into useful data by naming the kind of mistake and the correction to practise next. An error log turns being wrong into useful data by naming the kind of mistake and the correction to practise next. policy intervention Turning mistakes into categories instead of shame Common reasoning error patterns to track Designing the next practice task from the log improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice error logs Introduction An error log is a structured record of reasoning mistakes that turns being wrong into usable feedback . Instead of asking, “Did I get the answer right?”, it asks, “What kind of wrong was this, why did it happen, and what should I practise next?” That shift is important because reasoning rarely improves through repetition alone. Improvement depends on recognising recurring failure patterns, monitoring one's own thinking, and deliberately targeting weak points with new practice. Research on self regulated learning consistently shows that planning, monitoring and evaluating one's own performance are central to developing higher level thinking skills. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... Within deliberate practice , an error log is therefore not a diary of failures. It is a diagnostic tool. Its purpose is to convert isolated mistakes into categories that reveal stable habits, allowing each future exercise to address a specific reasoning weakness rather than hoping that experience alone will produce improvement. Turning mistakes into categories instead of shame People often treat errors as evidence of ability. Deliberate practice treats them as evidence about process. Suppose two people reach the same incorrect conclusion. One misunderstood the evidence, while the other ignored an alternative explanation because they became overconfident after recognising a familiar pattern. Although the outcome is identical, the training need is completely different. An effective error log separates these cases instead of recording only that the answer was \"wrong\". The key principle is that every entry should identify the reasoning failure , not merely the incorrect conclusion. Useful questions include: What assumption proved false? Which step first departed from good reasoning? What evidence should have changed my mind? Was the mistake due to missing knowledge or poor reasoning with available knowledge? Would I make this mistake again under similar conditions? These questions encourage metacognitive regulation—the ability to monitor and evaluate one's own thinking—which educational research identifies as a core component of effective self regulated learning. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... The emotional effect also matters. When errors become classified observations rather than personal failures, they become easier to inspect honestly. The goal is diagnosis, not self judgement. What kind of wrong was it? A useful reasoning error log contains relatively few categories that are broad enough to reveal patterns but specific enough to suggest a corrective exercise. Common categories include: Error categoryDiagnostic questionTypical next practiceMisread the evidenceDid I misunderstand what the information actually showed?Summarise evidence before drawing conclusions.Unsupported assumptionWhat did I treat as true without justification?Explicitly list assumptions before solving similar problems.Missing alternative explanationsDid I stop after the first plausible answer?Force yourself to generate at least three competing explanations.OverconfidenceHow certain was I before checking?Record confidence estimates before receiving feedback.Weak causal reasoningDid I mistake correlation or sequence for causation?Compare rival causal models using the same evidence.Incomplete searchDid I stop gathering information too early?Practise structured evidence searches with stopping rules.Arithmetic or procedural slipWas the reasoning sound but execution careless?Slow down verification rather than relearning concepts.Ambiguous interpretationDid multiple meanings exist that I failed to distinguish?Restate the problem in different words before answering. These categories are examples rather than fixed standards. The important feature is consistency. If the same label appears repeatedly, the learner has identified a trainable weakness instead of treating each mistake as unrelated. Why Every GMAT Taker Needs an Error Log (And How to Use It) 🤔 GMATErrorLog GMATTips Channel: Target Test Prep GMAT Open on YouTube Common reasoning error patterns worth tracking Over time, an error log becomes valuable because repeated entries expose habits that are difficult to notice in individual exercises. Several recurring patterns deserve particular attention. Premature closure. The learner accepts the first explanation that appears satisfactory instead of continuing to search for alternatives. This often produces confident but fragile conclusions. Evidence neglect. Information that contradicts an initial judgement is overlooked, discounted or forgotten. The error frequently appears when someone forms an opinion before carefully examining available evidence. Confidence calibration errors. The confidence assigned to a conclusion does not match its actual reliability. Recording confidence before receiving feedback makes these errors visible and allows calibration to improve over repeated practice. Strategy mismatch. The learner applies an otherwise useful reasoning method to the wrong type of problem—for example, relying on intuition where systematic analysis is required, or over analysing a task that depends on recognising a familiar pattern. Transfer failures. A reasoning strategy works in one context but is not recognised as applicable elsewhere. Logging both successful and unsuccessful transfers helps identify missing generalisation rather than missing knowledge. Rather than treating these as isolated mistakes, the log asks whether one pattern repeatedly dominates performance. That recurring pattern becomes the highest priority target for deliberate practice. Recording confidence makes mistakes more informative An unusually powerful addition to an error log is a confidence rating made before checking the answer. This separates two very different situations: low confidence followed by an incorrect answer, suggesting recognised uncertainty; high confidence followed by an incorrect answer, revealing a misconception or serious calibration problem. Research on the hypercorrection effect shows that high confidence errors are often especially memorable once corrected because the conflict between expectation and reality captures attention. However, this benefit depends on receiving accurate corrective feedback rather than simply discovering that an answer was wrong. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Hypercorrection (psychology Hypercorrection (psychology For reasoning practice, the lesson is practical: do not record only whether you were correct. Record how certain you were before checking. A simple confidence scale from 0–100% is often sufficient to reveal systematic overconfidence or excessive caution across many exercises. Cognition How Your Mind Can Amaze and Betray You: Crash Course Psychology 15 Channel: CrashCourse Open on YouTube Designing the next practice task from the log An error log only becomes deliberate practice if it changes what happens next. Each category should lead directly to a targeted exercise rather than another general problem set. For example: Repeated unsupported assumptions → complete exercises where every assumption must be written before solving. Repeated failure to consider alternatives → require three competing explanations before selecting one. Frequent evidence neglect → practise distinguishing observations from interpretations using short articles or case studies. Persistent overconfidence → estimate probabilities before revealing answers and review calibration weekly. Frequent procedural slips → introduce deliberate verification checklists rather than additional conceptual study. This principle mirrors broader research on deliberate practice: feedback is useful only when it identifies the next performance target rather than merely evaluating past performance. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... Using AI without weakening the error log Generative AI can provide rapid feedback, suggest alternative arguments or identify overlooked evidence. However, if it supplies corrections before the learner has analysed the mistake independently,"},{"title":"Faded Prompts","url":"/faded-prompts/","description":"Faded prompts help learners practise a reasoning move repeatedly until they can use it without step-by-step support.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Deliberate Practice","level":3,"text":"Faded Prompts When should reasoning prompts disappear? Faded Prompts Faded Prompts When should reasoning prompts disappear? Faded prompts help learners practise a reasoning move repeatedly until they can use it without step by step support. Faded prompts help learners practise a reasoning move repeatedly until they can use it without step by step support. policy intervention How prompts guide a target reasoning move Why support should fade after repeated practice Lessons from data rich classroom tasks improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice faded prompts Introduction Reasoning prompts are most valuable when they are temporary. The aim is not to create learners who always depend on checklists , sentence starters or guided questions, but to help them internalise a specific reasoning habit until they can apply it independently. This instructional approach is commonly called fading : support is deliberately reduced as competence increases rather than remaining permanently available. Research across cognitive apprenticeship, worked example instruction and classroom science education shows that gradual withdrawal of guidance can produce more durable reasoning skills than either leaving learners unsupported from the beginning or providing permanent scaffolding. American Federation of Teachers+2mr barton maths [aft.org] aft.org American Federation of TeachersCognitive Apprenticeshipby A Collins · Cited by 3468 — There are four important aspects of traditional app... Within deliberate practice for reasoning, faded prompts occupy a distinctive role. They are not simply a motivational technique or a way to make tasks easier. Instead, they are a policy for transferring responsibility from the instructor to the learner while preserving the quality of the target reasoning move. How prompts guide a target reasoning move A reasoning prompt directs attention towards a particular mental action that experts perform automatically. Instead of asking learners merely to produce an answer, prompts encourage behaviours such as: identifying assumptions before reaching a conclusion; comparing competing explanations; explaining why evidence supports one interpretation rather than another; estimating uncertainty instead of expressing unwarranted certainty; checking whether alternative evidence would change the conclusion. Initially these prompts make invisible thinking visible. Learners repeatedly perform the same reasoning move in a structured way until the move itself becomes familiar. The educational model of cognitive apprenticeship describes this progression through modelling, coaching, scaffolding and finally fading. Early instruction provides explicit support because novices often lack the mental structures needed to organise complex reasoning. As learners improve, assistance becomes less detailed and less frequent so that responsibility shifts towards independent performance. [American Federation of Teachers] aft.org American Federation of TeachersCognitive Apprenticeshipby A Collins · Cited by 3468 — There are four important aspects of traditional app... Research on worked examples follows the same principle. Rather than asking beginners to solve every problem independently, instructors first demonstrate complete reasoning processes. Later examples deliberately omit increasing numbers of solution steps, requiring learners to complete the missing reasoning themselves. Self explanation prompts are often incorporated during this transition because explaining missing steps helps learners build transferable mental models rather than memorising procedures. [mr barton maths] mrbartonmaths.com Fading out and Prompts Reflection is also...Read more... Vygotsky's Theory of Cognitive Development in Social Relationships Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Why support should fade after repeated practice Permanent prompts can unintentionally become a crutch. If learners always receive the same checklist before analysing evidence, they may learn to follow the checklist only when it is visibly present instead of developing an internal habit. Fading addresses this problem by gradually increasing cognitive responsibility while keeping the task achievable. Typical progression includes: 1. Full guidance: every reasoning step is explicitly prompted. 2. Partial guidance: some prompts disappear while key decision points remain. 3. Minimal guidance: only occasional reminders are provided. 4. Independent performance: learners perform the reasoning move without external prompts. This sequence balances two competing risks. Removing support too early may overload working memory and encourage guessing. Leaving support in place for too long can create dependence and reduce transfer to new situations. Cognitive load theory therefore recommends adapting instructional guidance to learners' developing expertise rather than treating scaffolds as permanent features of instruction. [mr barton maths+2PMC] mrbartonmaths.com Fading out and Prompts Reflection is also...Read more... A related finding is the expertise reversal effect . Instructional supports that help novices can become unnecessary or even distracting once learners possess sufficient knowledge. At that stage, simplified prompts may consume attention without contributing additional understanding, making gradual withdrawal an important part of effective instructional design. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Worked example effect Worked example effect Lessons from data rich classroom tasks One of the clearest demonstrations comes from undergraduate physics laboratories designed to strengthen scientific reasoning rather than merely complete experiments. Students were repeatedly prompted to compare data with theoretical models, identify discrepancies, improve experimental methods and justify revisions. These prompts were not intended to remain throughout the course. Instead, instructors progressively removed them after repeated practice . When later completing laboratory work with far less guidance, students who had experienced faded prompting were substantially more likely than students in conventional laboratory courses to: improve their experimental methods without being instructed to do so; evaluate whether evidence supported theoretical models; explain limitations of those models using their own reasoning. The important outcome was not simply better laboratory technique. Students had begun initiating the target reasoning behaviour spontaneously rather than waiting for instructions. This illustrates the central purpose of faded prompts: transferring ownership of the reasoning process from teacher to learner. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NIHby ND Martin · 2018 · Cited by 109 — Our study explores the interplay between support embedded in instructional materials and sc... Similar patterns appear beyond laboratory science. Studies of worked examples consistently show that learners transition more successfully from observing expert reasoning to solving problems independently when instructional steps are gradually removed instead of disappearing all at once. Self explanation during this fading process further strengthens conceptual understanding by requiring learners to reconstruct missing reasoning rather than copy it mechanically. [mr barton maths] mrbartonmaths.com Fading out and Prompts Reflection is also...Read more... Cognitive Apprenticeship Channel: Michael Mittag Open on YouTube Historical development of the fading idea The concept emerged prominently within cognitive apprenticeship during the late 1980s. Rather than viewing expertise as knowledge that could simply be transmitted through lectures, researchers argued that complex thinking should be taught similarly to traditional apprenticeships. Experts first demonstrate the craft, then coach learners through increasingly difficult performances while reducing assistance over time. Fading therefore became a defining feature rather than an optional extra. It distinguishes genuine scaffolding from permanent instructional support because the ultimate objective is independent competence. [American Federation of Teachers] aft.org American Federation of TeachersCognitive Apprenticeshipby A Collins · Cited by 3468 — There are four important aspects of traditional app... During the following decades, cognitive load theory and worked example research supplied complementary evidence. Investigators found that carefully designed fading procedures reduced unnecessary mental effort for novices while encouraging progressively greater learner responsibility. Rather than representing competing theories, cognitive apprenticeship and worked example research converged on the same instructional policy: guidance should evolve as learners develop. [mr barton maths] mrbartonmaths.com Fading out and Prompts Reflection is also...Read more... YouTube Teaching Strategies: Cognitive Load Theory Teaching Strategies: Cognitive Load Theory Open on YouTube What successful fading looks like in"},{"title":"Prediction Logs","url":"/prediction-logs/","description":"Prediction logs make reasoning practice measurable by recording forecasts, evidence, updates and results in one place.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Deliberate Practice","level":3,"text":"Prediction Logs Why prediction logs make thinking measurable Prediction Logs Prediction Logs Why prediction logs make thinking measurable Prediction logs make reasoning practice measurable by recording forecasts, evidence, updates and results in one place. Prediction logs make reasoning practice measurable by recording forecasts, evidence, updates and results in one place. dataset evidence What to record before the outcome is known How scoring reveals recurring judgement errors Using updates as practice rather than embarrassment improving your thinking and analytical skills deliberate practice prediction logs Introduction Prediction logs turn reasoning into something that can be measured instead of merely remembered. Rather than asking whether a decision “felt sensible”, they require you to state a forecast before the outcome is known, explain why you believe it, record your confidence as a probability, and later compare that judgement with reality. This creates a permanent record of how well your thinking matches the world rather than your memory of it. Within deliberate practice for reasoning, prediction logs are valuable because they generate repeated, scoreable judgements. They expose patterns that are otherwise difficult to notice, such as chronic overconfidence , excessive caution, reluctance to update beliefs, or relying on weak evidence. Research from forecasting tournaments, including the Good Judgment Project, has shown that keeping score and repeatedly refining probabilistic judgements can improve forecasting performance over time. [Good Judgment+2AI Impacts] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingProven methods. Powerful results. Good Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate for... What to record before the outcome is known The usefulness of a prediction log depends far more on what is written before an outcome is known than on commentary afterwards. Once people know the result, hindsight bias makes previous beliefs seem more obvious than they really were. A practical prediction entry usually includes: The prediction itself. Phrase it so that it will clearly be true or false. Ambiguous predictions cannot be scored consistently. A probability estimate. Instead of \"probably\", assign a number such as 30%, 65% or 90%. Probability estimates reveal calibration—whether events assigned a 70% chance really happen about seven times in ten. A deadline. Specify exactly when the prediction will be judged. The evidence available now. Separate observations from interpretations. Record the facts you relied upon rather than reconstructing them later. Alternative explanations. Briefly note what would make the opposite outcome plausible. This discourages tunnel vision. Conditions for revision. State what new evidence would cause you to increase or decrease your confidence before the deadline. For example, instead of writing, \"I think sales will improve\", a prediction log might record: \"There is a 65% probability that quarterly sales will exceed last year's equivalent quarter. Confidence is based on current order volume and announced product launches. If two major customers cancel before month end, confidence falls below 50%.\" The important feature is that every part of the reasoning is timestamped before reality provides the answer. Model Calibration Brier Score Explained Channel: DataMListic Open on YouTube How scoring reveals recurring judgement errors Prediction logs become useful only when forecasts are scored systematically rather than remembered selectively. Forecasting research commonly uses the Brier score , a proper scoring rule that compares predicted probabilities with actual outcomes. Lower scores indicate better probabilistic forecasting because they reward both accuracy and well calibrated confidence. Proper scoring rules encourage honest probability estimates rather than exaggerated certainty designed to appear impressive. [Wikipedia+2scattered thoughts.net] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score Over dozens or hundreds of predictions, scores begin to reveal stable reasoning habits rather than isolated mistakes. Typical patterns include: Overconfidence. Events predicted with 90% confidence occur far less frequently than expected. Underconfidence. Correct forecasts are consistently assigned probabilities that are too low. Poor discrimination. Almost every forecast receives similar probabilities, regardless of evidence quality. Failure to distinguish evidence strength. Weak and strong cases receive nearly identical confidence levels. Calibration is especially informative. Suppose every forecast assigned an 80% probability is later examined. If only about half actually occur, confidence is inflated. If nearly all occur, confidence may be too conservative. Good calibration means long run frequencies match stated probabilities, not that every individual prediction succeeds. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov 4: A Brier On misconceptions about the Brier score in binary prediction...by L Hoessly · 2026 · Cited by 18 — Calibration refers to how well pre... It is also important not to overinterpret a single score. Researchers emphasise that the Brier score combines several aspects of forecast quality and is influenced by the difficulty of the prediction task. It should therefore be used to compare repeated forecasts in similar settings rather than treated as a universal measure of intelligence or reasoning ability. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov On misconceptions about the Brier score in binary...by L Hoessly · 2026 · Cited by 19 — The Brier score was shown to reflect not o... Using updates as practice rather than embarrassment Many people resist recording prediction updates because changing one's mind can feel like admitting failure. Forecasting research suggests the opposite. Good forecasters revise beliefs whenever meaningful new evidence appears. The goal is not consistency with previous opinions but increasing correspondence with reality. Forecasts are therefore living judgements rather than declarations of loyalty to an initial view. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingProven methods. Powerful results. Good Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate for... A useful update records three things: what new evidence appeared; how much confidence changed; why the evidence justified that change. For instance: DateProbabilityReason2 June45%Initial evidence mixed10 June62%New economic data stronger than expected18 June54%Competitor announcement weakens original case Reviewing these updates later often exposes recurring habits. Some people hardly update until the answer is obvious. Others swing dramatically after every headline. Both patterns become visible only when changes are logged over time. The objective is not to minimise revisions but to make them evidence based. A prediction log rewards thoughtful updating because every revision itself becomes observable practice in reasoning under uncertainty. Superforecasting Philip Tetlock Channel: The Long Now Foundation Open on YouTube What prediction logs teach that memory cannot Human memory edits past beliefs surprisingly well. After outcomes become known, people frequently remember having been \"almost certain\" of whatever happened, even when earlier confidence was much lower. This hindsight bias makes informal self assessment unreliable. Prediction logs bypass this problem because they preserve: original confidence levels; original reasoning; the evidence available at the time; every subsequent revision. Instead of debating whether a decision was \"reasonable\", the learner can inspect exactly what information was available and whether confidence was proportional to that evidence. Over time, this archive becomes a personalised dataset of thinking errors. Unlike general advice about cognitive biases, it identifies the biases that repeatedly affect one individual's judgements. Building a useful prediction log The most effective logs favour many small predictions over a few dramatic ones. Frequent forecasts provide enough observations to detect patterns and shorten the feedback cycle. Suitable practice questions include: Will this project finish by its stated deadline? Will a published study replicate? Will a policy proposal pass within six months? Will an investment thesis outperform a benchmark over a specified period? Will a software feature be completed by the next release? The questions matter less than their clarity. Good prediction questions have an observable outcome, a defined resolution date and a probability estimate recorded in advance. Consistency also matters more than complexity. A simple spreadsheet or notebook that captures forecasts, evidence, updates and outcomes is usually sufficient. The cumulative record—not any single prediction—is what gradually improves judgement. As forecasting researchers often note, the discipline of keeping score distinguishes genuine learning from the comforting illusion"},{"title":"Pressure","url":"/pressure/","description":"Strong feelings and identity threats can make evidence harder to evaluate fairly.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Pressure When Emotion Distorts Reasoning Pressure Pressure When Emotion Distorts Reasoning Strong feelings and identity threats can make evidence harder to evaluate fairly. Strong feelings and identity threats can make evidence harder to evaluate fairly. population context Emotional triggers Identity protective thinking Cooling off checks improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure Introduction Strong feelings do not simply “get in the way” of thinking; they often decide which facts feel relevant, which sources feel trustworthy, and which conclusions feel safe to consider. Under pressure, the problem is sharper: anger, fear, humiliation, loyalty and defensiveness can narrow attention just when careful judgement needs room. Identity makes this harder still. When a claim threatens someone’s group, values, reputation or self image, evidence can be treated less like information and more like a social test. Research on motivated reasoning, identity protective cognition, affect, stress and misinformation points to a practical lesson: better reasoning is not just about knowing logic, but about recognising when emotion has changed the task from “What is true?” to “What protects me, my side, or my story?” [Frank Baumgartner+2ndg.asc.upenn.edu] unc.edu Frank Baumgartner The Case for Motivated Reasoning Frank BaumgartnerThe Case for Motivated ReasoningOctober 6, 2004 — by Z Kunda · 1990 · Cited by 13553 — In this article I explore the pos... Why emotional pressure changes the question Emotion can improve judgement when it draws attention to real stakes: fear can flag danger, anger can register injustice, disgust can warn of contamination, and anxiety can make people check details. The mistake is to treat emotion as either pure wisdom or pure noise. A major review of emotion and decision making argues that emotions are “potent, pervasive, predictable” influences on choices, sometimes helpful and sometimes harmful, depending on whether the feeling fits the actual problem. [SciSpace] scispace.com Sci Space Emotion and Decision Making Emotion and Decision MakingAbstract. A revolution in the science of emotion has emerged in recent decades, with the potential to... The risk begins when a feeling becomes a shortcut for evidence. The “affect heuristic” describes how people use the positive or negative feeling attached to a person, policy, product or claim as a fast guide to its risk and benefit. If something feels good, its risks may seem smaller; if it feels threatening, its benefits may disappear from view. This is efficient in simple situations, but unreliable when the issue is complex, politicised or unfamiliar. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Frontiers The Affect Heuristic and Risk Perception – Stability Across Frontiers The Affect Heuristic and Risk Perception – Stability Across A useful example is public risk perception. People rarely evaluate a hazard by calmly listing probabilities , uncertainties and trade offs . They often start with a gut tag: safe, dangerous, corrupt, natural, artificial, elite, ordinary, familiar, alien. Once that tag is attached, later facts may be interpreted through it. The same evidence can feel reassuring to one person and manipulative to another, not because the evidence has changed, but because the emotional meaning of the topic has changed. For analytical thinking, this means the first self check is not “Am I emotional?” Everyone is. The better question is: “What is this emotion making obvious, and what is it making hard to see?” Emotional triggers that distort evidence Emotional triggers matter because they do not merely add intensity; they change attention, memory and standards of proof. A person may demand impossibly strong evidence for a claim they dislike while accepting weak evidence for a claim that relieves anxiety or confirms blame. That is not always conscious dishonesty. Motivated reasoning often works through ordinary mental operations: searching memory, judging credibility, spotting flaws, and deciding when enough evidence is enough. Kunda’s classic review described motivation as shaping reasoning through a biased set of cognitive processes rather than through simple wishful thinking. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Frank Baumgartner The Case for Motivated Reasoning Frank BaumgartnerThe Case for Motivated ReasoningOctober 6, 2004 — by Z Kunda · 1990 · Cited by 13553 — In this article I explore the pos... Three triggers are especially important in reasoning under pressure. Threat narrows the field. When people feel attacked, rushed or unsafe, they often prioritise immediate defence over open evaluation. Neuroscience reviews of stress find that even mild uncontrollable stress can impair prefrontal cortex functions involved in working memory, flexible thinking and top down control. In everyday terms, the mind has less spare capacity for holding competing possibilities at once. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCStress signalling pathways that impair prefrontal cortex by AFT Arnsten · 2009 · Cited by 5202 — The Review first describes how exposure to even mild uncontrollable stress can rapidly impair... Anger can increase certainty. Anger often carries the appraisal that someone is responsible and that action is justified. That can be useful when responsibility is clear, but it can also make blame feel like proof. In organisational decision making, researchers warn that anger and other high certainty emotions can push people towards action before they have thought enough, making cooling off periods valuable for major decisions. [jenniferlerner.com] jenniferlerner.com Emotion in organizational judgment and decision making Emotion in organizational judgment and decision making Humiliation and status threat make revision costly. If changing your mind means admitting that your group was wrong, your profession missed something, your public statement was premature, or your opponent had a point, the evidence is no longer just evidence. It becomes a demand for social or personal loss. This is where intellectual humility becomes hardest: not when a topic is abstract, but when the conclusion touches pride, belonging or reputation. Identity protective thinking Identity protective thinking is motivated reasoning aimed at preserving a valued identity. The identity might be political, religious, professional, national, moral, class based, scientific, sceptical, activist, entrepreneurial or personal. The core pattern is the same: claims aligned with the identity receive easier passage; claims that threaten it face heavier inspection. Dan Kahan’s work on identity protective cognition has been especially influential in showing how people can use reasoning ability not to escape bias, but to defend identity consistent conclusions more skilfully. [ndg.asc.upenn.edu] ndg.asc.upenn.edu Ideology motivated reasoning Ideology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2114 — When in dividuals display identity protectiv... This is why “just give people more facts” often disappoints. On identity loaded questions, people may not be short of information. They may be defending a social position. Kahan’s research on ideology, motivated reasoning and cognitive reflection challenged the simple idea that polarisation is mainly caused by people relying too little on analytical thought. In some contexts, more cognitively reflective or numerate people were better at reaching identity congenial interpretations of evidence. [ndg.asc.upenn.edu] ndg.asc.upenn.edu Ideology motivated reasoning Ideology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2114 — When in dividuals display identity protectiv... This does not mean intelligence makes people irrational. It means reasoning has more than one possible goal. One goal is accuracy: forming the belief best supported by evidence. Another is identity protection: maintaining standing with people whose respect, trust or shared worldview matters. From the inside, identity protective reasoning can feel responsible: “I am not being biased; I am refusing to be fooled by hostile people.” The danger is that the same scepticism is not applied evenly. A practical sign of identity protective thinking is asymmetry. Ask: Would the same evidence impress me if it supported the other side? Would I accept this source if it criticised my group? Am I treating a single bad argument from opponents as proof that their whole case is false? Am I more interested in whether a claim is useful to my side than whether it is accurate? Does changing my mind feel like betrayal rather than correction? These questions are uncomfortable because they move the issue from abstract reasoning to social risk. That discomfort is often the point at which better thinking begins. You're a hypocrite. That's the best news you'll hear today Channel: Owen Fitzpatrick Open on YouTube Pressure turns reasoning into self defence Under pressure, people have less time, less working memory"},{"title":"Gut Feelings","url":"/gut-feelings/","description":"Gut feelings can flag real stakes, but they can also make risks, benefits, and sources seem clearer than they really are.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Pressure","level":3,"text":"Gut Feelings When gut feelings start judging the facts Gut Feelings Gut Feelings When gut feelings start judging the facts Gut feelings can flag real stakes, but they can also make risks, benefits, and sources seem clearer than they really are. Gut feelings can flag real stakes, but they can also make risks, benefits, and sources seem clearer than they really are. mechanism How the affect heuristic changes perceived risk and benefit Why complex or politicised claims are especially vulnerable Self checks for separating emotional salience from evidence improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure gut feelings Introduction Gut feelings are not random. They often compress past experience, rapid pattern recognition and emotional reactions into a fast judgement that can be genuinely useful when time is short or information is incomplete. The problem arises when that emotional impression is treated as evidence rather than as a signal to investigate further. This mental shortcut is known as the affect heuristic : people use their immediate positive or negative feelings about a person, idea, technology or event to judge its risks, benefits and credibility, often before they consciously examine the facts. [Warrington College of Business] bear.warrington.ufl.edu slovic affect heuristic 2002 Judgments of risk and benefit...Read more... For improving analytical thinking, the key lesson is not to distrust intuition altogether. Instead, it is to recognise when emotion has shifted from highlighting what deserves attention to silently deciding what must be true. This distinction becomes especially important when issues are urgent, unfamiliar, morally charged or politically contested. How the affect heuristic changes perceived risk and benefit The affect heuristic is one of the best established findings in research on judgement under uncertainty. Rather than estimating risks and benefits independently, people often derive both from a single emotional impression. If something feels attractive, familiar or morally desirable, its benefits tend to seem larger and its risks smaller. If it feels threatening or repellent, the opposite happens. [UCLA Anderson School of Management] anderson.ucla.edu Fin Alh Slovic John Affect Heur00 UCLA Anderson School of ManagementThe AАect Heuristic in Judgments of Risks and Bene®tsby ML FINUCANE · 2000 · Cited by 4654 — According... This creates a characteristic pattern: A favourable emotional reaction leads people to underestimate possible harms. An unfavourable emotional reaction leads them to overlook genuine advantages. Confidence in these judgements often exceeds the amount of supporting evidence. Experimental research by Melissa Finucane, Paul Slovic and colleagues demonstrated that altering participants' emotional evaluation of technologies or activities systematically changed both their perceived risks and their perceived benefits, even when the underlying facts remained unchanged. Time pressure strengthened this effect, suggesting that emotional shortcuts become more influential when careful analysis is harder. [Stanford University] stanford.edu We propose that this relationship occurs...Read more... Importantly, this is not simply \"being emotional\". The shortcut operates quickly and often outside awareness. People usually experience the resulting judgement as obvious or intuitive rather than as emotionally driven. Why gut feelings often feel like evidence The brain continuously attaches emotional value to experiences. These positive and negative associations form what researchers describe as an \"affect pool\" that can be consulted far faster than conscious reasoning. When a new judgement is needed, the emotional tag is often retrieved before probabilities , statistics or competing explanations. [Warrington College of Business] bear.warrington.ufl.edu slovic affect heuristic 2002 Judgments of risk and benefit...Read more... This makes emotional impressions efficient because they reduce mental effort. Instead of asking: How reliable is the evidence? What are the uncertainties? What alternative explanations exist? the mind unconsciously substitutes an easier question: \"How do I feel about this?\" Psychologists call this a heuristic because it is a practical rule of thumb rather than a formal reasoning process. In many everyday situations it works surprisingly well, particularly when someone has accumulated genuine expertise through repeated experience. However, it becomes much less reliable when the situation is novel, highly technical or deliberately designed to provoke emotion. [BehavioralEconomics.com The BE Hub] behavioraleconomics.com affect heuristic The BE HubAffect heuristic4 Dec 2024 — The affect heuristic represents a reliance on good or bad feelings experienced in relation to a st... Masterclass Part 1: Psychology of Risk: Risk Perceptions Channel: IPUR NUS Open on YouTube Why complex or politicised claims are especially vulnerable The affect heuristic becomes particularly influential when people lack direct knowledge. Complex issues such as climate policy, artificial intelligence, public health, nuclear energy or biotechnology contain technical uncertainties that are difficult to evaluate directly. Emotional impressions therefore become an attractive substitute for detailed analysis. Several factors increase reliance on affect: Limited expertise. Without a clear understanding of the subject, emotional reactions fill informational gaps. Information overload. Large volumes of conflicting evidence encourage simpler judgement strategies. Identity relevance. When a topic is associated with political, cultural or social identity, emotional responses become intertwined with belonging. Urgency. Perceived crises encourage rapid decisions rather than deliberate evaluation. In these situations, people may interpret identical evidence very differently because they begin with different emotional meanings attached to the issue rather than different factual knowledge. The same study, graph or expert statement can therefore appear reassuring to one audience and deeply suspicious to another. [Sage Journals+2MPG.PuRe] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Risk Perception and Affect Risk as feelings refers to individuals' instinctive and intuitive reactions to danger.Read more... This helps explain why simply providing more information often fails to resolve disagreements. The disagreement is frequently rooted in differing emotional interpretations rather than missing facts alone. Emotional salience changes what seems credible The affect heuristic influences more than risk perception. It also changes how people judge sources. A report that supports an emotionally comfortable conclusion may feel balanced, objective and trustworthy before its methodology has been examined. Conversely, evidence that challenges an emotionally significant belief may immediately appear biased, selective or politically motivated. This does not mean people consciously reject evidence. Instead, emotional impressions influence several earlier stages of reasoning: which information attracts attention; which experts appear trustworthy; how much uncertainty seems acceptable; when enough evidence feels sufficient. Because these judgements occur rapidly, people often experience them as rational evaluations rather than emotional responses. Dr. Paul Slovic: How Risk and Emotions Affect Decision Making Channel: The Franklin Institute Open on YouTube When gut feelings help rather than hinder The existence of the affect heuristic does not imply that intuition should be ignored. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu slovic affect heuristic 2002 Judgments of risk and benefit...Read more... Experienced firefighters, emergency clinicians, pilots and other experts often make remarkably accurate rapid judgements because their intuitions reflect thousands of previous encounters. Their gut feelings frequently represent compressed learning rather than arbitrary emotion. The important distinction is whether intuition comes from relevant experience or from emotional association alone . Useful intuition typically has several characteristics: it develops through repeated feedback ; it operates within a familiar environment; it can often be explained after reflection; it remains open to correction when contradictory evidence appears. By contrast, intuitive certainty about unfamiliar scientific, political or statistical questions deserves greater caution because emotional impressions may be doing most of the work. Self checks for separating emotional salience from evidence The goal is not to eliminate emotion but to prevent emotional importance from becoming mistaken for factual strength. When a claim immediately feels obviously true or obviously false, it can help to pause and ask: 1. What exactly triggered my reaction? Was it the evidence itself, the language, the speaker or the wider topic? 2. Would I judge this evidence the same way if it"},{"title":"Anger","url":"/anger/","description":"Anger can reveal injustice, but it can also make responsibility feel proven before the evidence has been tested.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Pressure","level":3,"text":"Anger When anger turns blame into proof Anger Anger When anger turns blame into proof Anger can reveal injustice, but it can also make responsibility feel proven before the evidence has been tested. Anger can reveal injustice, but it can also make responsibility feel proven before the evidence has been tested. mechanism How anger raises certainty and action readiness Why blame can outrun evidence in disputes and crises Cooling off rules for high stakes judgments improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure anger Introduction Anger is often treated as the enemy of good judgement, but that is too simple. Anger can be an accurate response to injustice, deception or genuine harm. It can focus attention on problems that would otherwise be ignored and motivate people to challenge abuse or defend others. The danger arises when anger changes not only what feels important, but also what feels already proven. Instead of encouraging investigation, it can create a powerful sense that responsibility has already been established and that further evidence is merely confirmation. This shift matters because many of the most consequential decisions—disciplinary actions, legal disputes, political conflicts, workplace investigations and personal confrontations—require separating justified moral concern from justified factual certainty. Research on emotion and judgement shows that anger is associated with unusually high feelings of certainty, personal control and readiness for action, making it distinct from many other negative emotions. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Lerner 2001 FearAngerRisk S. Lerner & D. Keltner, 2000), the authors predicted and found that fear and anger have opposite effects on risk How anger raises certainty and action readiness Not all negative emotions influence thinking in the same way. Fear tends to highlight uncertainty and encourage caution. Anger, by contrast, is commonly associated with appraisals that someone is responsible, the situation is understandable and action is both possible and justified. This combination makes anger especially influential in shaping confidence rather than merely increasing emotional intensity. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Lerner 2001 FearAngerRisk S. Lerner & D. Keltner, 2000), the authors predicted and found that fear and anger have opposite effects on risk The appraisal tendency framework, developed by psychologists Jennifer Lerner and colleagues, explains why. Rather than treating emotions as simply positive or negative, the framework argues that each emotion carries characteristic patterns of appraisal. Anger is linked with: high perceived certainty about what happened; strong beliefs that events are under human control rather than due to chance; a tendency to identify responsible agents; increased confidence that decisive action is appropriate. These appraisals often persist even when the anger originated elsewhere. Someone angered by one event may unconsciously carry that mindset into judging an unrelated dispute or risk. [jenniferlerner.com] jenniferlerner.com The appraisal tendency framework Jennifer Lernerby S Han · Cited by 1101 — Lerner and Keltner (2000, 2001) proposed the Appraisal Tendency. Framework (ATF) as a basis for... This helps explain why angry people often express unusually high confidence despite possessing no more information than calmer observers. The feeling of certainty is itself part of the emotional state rather than independent evidence that the conclusion is correct. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryAppraisal Tendencies Shape Anger's Influence on Cognitionby JS Lerner · 2006 · Cited by 1571 — Lerner and Keltner (20... Brené Brown on Blame Channel: The RSA Open on YouTube Why blame can outrun evidence in disputes and crises When people become angry, they often shift from asking \"What happened?\" to asking \"Who caused this?\" That transition can be useful if responsibility is genuinely clear, but it also creates predictable reasoning risks. One well studied pattern is a stronger tendency to explain behaviour by stable personal character rather than circumstances. Instead of asking whether stress , confusion, poor information or organisational failures contributed, the angry observer becomes more likely to conclude that the person involved is dishonest, malicious or incompetent. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Lerner 2001 FearAngerRisk S. Lerner & D. Keltner, 2000), the authors predicted and found that fear and anger have opposite effects on risk Several mechanisms reinforce this process. Ambiguity feels smaller than it really is. When evidence is incomplete, anger can make uncertainty feel temporary rather than fundamental. Missing information is interpreted as a gap that will eventually support the initial judgement instead of a reason to suspend judgement. [jenniferlerner.com] jenniferlerner.com The appraisal tendency framework Jennifer Lernerby S Han · Cited by 1101 — Lerner and Keltner (2000, 2001) proposed the Appraisal Tendency. Framework (ATF) as a basis for... Contradictory evidence receives greater suspicion. Information inconsistent with the emerging blame narrative may be dismissed as excuses, manipulation or bias before it has been evaluated on its merits. This does not require deliberate bad faith; the emotional state changes how credibility is assessed. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryAppraisal Tendencies Shape Anger's Influence on Cognitionby JS Lerner · 2006 · Cited by 1571 — Lerner and Keltner (20... Action begins to feel morally urgent. Anger naturally prepares people to intervene rather than observe. That urgency can reduce willingness to tolerate the delays needed for careful investigation, creating pressure to act before the factual picture has stabilised. [jenniferlerner.com] jenniferlerner.com The appraisal tendency framework Jennifer Lernerby S Han · Cited by 1101 — Lerner and Keltner (2000, 2001) proposed the Appraisal Tendency. Framework (ATF) as a basis for... These tendencies help explain why public controversies often escalate rapidly after emotionally charged events. Early narratives may become psychologically compelling long before investigators have reconstructed timelines or verified competing accounts. How Anger Affects Thinking Anger Management Channel: Howcast Open on YouTube Why confidence is not the same as evidence One of the most misleading features of anger is that it changes subjective certainty. People frequently experience the feeling that \"it is obvious\" who is responsible. Subjective certainty, however, is not a reliable indicator of evidential strength. In psychology, confidence and accuracy are related only imperfectly. Emotional states can increase confidence without improving the underlying quality of reasoning. Anger is particularly important because, unlike fear or sadness, it tends to increase optimism about one's own judgement and willingness to accept risk. Studies comparing anger and fear found that angry participants judged uncertain situations differently despite having identical information. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Lerner 2001 FearAngerRisk S. Lerner & D. Keltner, 2000), the authors predicted and found that fear and anger have opposite effects on risk This distinction is practically important because investigations frequently begin under emotionally charged conditions: allegations after accidents; workplace misconduct claims ; political scandals; social media controversies; emergency response failures. In each case, anger may correctly signal that something serious deserves attention while simultaneously overstating how much is already known. How anger interacts with group conflict Anger rarely remains confined to judging individuals. In conflicts between groups, it can reshape perceptions of entire categories of people. Once an out group becomes associated with blame, observers often generalise beyond the specific individuals involved. New information is filtered through existing expectations, making ambiguous behaviour appear intentionally hostile while benevolent behaviour receives less attention or greater scepticism. These effects contribute to stereotyping, reduced trust and escalating cycles of mutual accusation. [Frank Baumgartner] unc.edu Lerner 2001 FearAngerRisk S. Lerner & D. Keltner, 2000), the authors predicted and found that fear and anger have opposite effects on risk This helps explain why prolonged conflicts often become increasingly resistant to factual correction. Each new incident is interpreted through an already established blame framework rather than evaluated independently. When anger improves judgement Recognising these risks does not require treating anger as irrational. Anger often identifies genuine moral problems before detached analysis does. Many historical reforms began because people refused to remain emotionally neutral about injustice, corruption or abuse. In everyday life, anger can motivate"},{"title":"Identity","url":"/identity/","description":"On identity-loaded issues, people may use reasoning skill to defend belonging rather than to update beliefs evenly.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Pressure","level":3,"text":"Identity Why smart people defend their side better Identity Identity Why smart people defend their side better On identity loaded issues, people may use reasoning skill to defend belonging rather than to update beliefs evenly. On identity loaded issues, people may use reasoning skill to defend belonging rather than to update beliefs evenly. mechanism How identity changes source trust and standards of proof Why more information does not always reduce polarisation Questions that reveal asymmetric scepticism improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure identity Introduction Identity protective cognition describes a pattern in which people use their reasoning abilities not only to discover what is true, but also to protect their standing within groups that matter to them. On issues tied to politics, culture, profession, religion or other social identities, changing one's mind can carry social costs: it may signal disloyalty, threaten status or weaken a sense of belonging. As a result, evidence is often evaluated through the question, \"What does accepting this claim say about people like me?\" rather than simply, \"Is this claim supported?\" Research suggests that this process is usually unconscious rather than deliberately dishonest, making it particularly difficult to recognise in ourselves. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentIdeology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2068 — When i... Understanding this mechanism is important for improving analytical thinking because it explains why intelligent, well informed people can reach sharply different conclusions from the same evidence. It also helps explain why simply supplying more facts often fails to resolve polarised disagreements. Why smart people defend their side better Identity protective cognition is a specific form of motivated reasoning. Rather than reasoning impartially and then forming an opinion, people often begin with commitments linked to their social identity and use their analytical abilities to defend those commitments. The stronger a person's reasoning skills, the better they may become at finding counterarguments, questioning inconvenient studies and constructing coherent justifications for their existing position. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentIdeology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2068 — When i... This finding challenges the common assumption that political or cultural disagreement mainly reflects ignorance. Several studies associated with Dan Kahan's cultural cognition research found that greater scientific literacy or numeracy did not necessarily reduce disagreement on politically charged topics. In some circumstances, people with stronger analytical skills became more polarised because they were better equipped to interpret ambiguous evidence in ways that supported their group's position. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentIdeology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2068 — When i... This does not mean reasoning is harmful. Instead, it suggests that reasoning serves multiple goals simultaneously: Accuracy goals , where the objective is to reach the most justified conclusion. Identity goals , where the objective is to preserve belonging, reputation or consistency with valued groups. When these goals conflict, identity goals can quietly shape how evidence is interpreted without the person experiencing themselves as biased. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentIdeology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2068 — When i... What is STEM identity? Dan Kahan Channel: Center for Advancement of Informal Science Education Open on YouTube How identity changes source trust and standards of proof Identity protective cognition rarely works by making people reject every inconvenient fact outright. More often, it changes how people judge the credibility of evidence. Three related shifts commonly appear. Trust becomes identity sensitive. Information coming from trusted members of one's own community is treated as more credible, while identical information from an opposing group attracts greater suspicion. Expertise itself may be evaluated differently depending on whether the expert appears aligned with or opposed to one's identity. [The Cultural Cognition Project] culturalcognition.net The Cultural Cognition Project Motivated reasoning & its cognates The Cultural Cognition ProjectMotivated reasoning & its cognates Cultural Cognition15 May 2013 — Identity protective cognition, like ot... Published: May 2013 Standards of proof become asymmetric. Weak evidence may be accepted when it supports one's preferred conclusion, while exceptionally strong evidence is demanded before accepting an opposing claim. This asymmetry often feels completely reasonable to the person applying it because each judgement is made separately rather than side by side. [The Cultural Cognition Project] culturalcognition.net The Cultural Cognition Project Motivated reasoning & its cognates The Cultural Cognition ProjectMotivated reasoning & its cognates Cultural Cognition15 May 2013 — Identity protective cognition, like ot... Published: May 2013 Ambiguity is interpreted selectively. Complex or mixed evidence usually leaves room for interpretation. Identity protective reasoning directs attention towards interpretations that preserve valued beliefs while identifying weaknesses in conflicting interpretations. The result is not fabricated evidence but unequal scrutiny. [The Cultural Cognition Project] culturalcognition.net The Cultural Cognition Project Motivated reasoning & its cognates The Cultural Cognition ProjectMotivated reasoning & its cognates Cultural Cognition15 May 2013 — Identity protective cognition, like ot... Published: May 2013 These shifts help explain why disagreements often persist even after participants have examined the same reports, statistics or expert testimony. Why more information does not always reduce polarisation A common assumption is that disagreement exists because people lack sufficient information. Research on identity protective cognition suggests the picture is more complicated. Additional evidence only improves agreement if people are motivated primarily by accuracy. When evidence threatens important identities, additional information may instead provide more material for selective interpretation. Better informed individuals can become more sophisticated defenders of their original position rather than more open minded evaluators. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentIdeology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2068 — When i... This helps explain why public disputes over subjects such as climate policy, firearms, public health or immigration sometimes persist despite enormous amounts of available evidence. Participants are not merely disagreeing about facts; they are often disagreeing within different social worlds where accepting certain facts carries symbolic meaning about loyalty, morality or group membership. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com It argues that misinformation has the greatest power to mislead.Read more... However, the evidence is not entirely one sided. Some research suggests that presenting information in ways that reduce identity threat—for example by emphasising broad scientific agreement rather than framing issues as victories for one political camp—can lessen defensive processing in some contexts. Other studies have found more mixed results, indicating that no communication strategy reliably eliminates motivated reasoning across all issues. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Gateway belief model Gateway belief model The practical lesson is that information alone is often insufficient when identity is at stake. Dan Kahan: How cultural values shape beliefs and perceptions of risk Channel: spitfirestrategies Open on YouTube Questions that reveal asymmetric scepticism Identity protective cognition is easiest to notice when identical standards are applied to competing claims . The following questions can expose hidden asymmetries in one's own reasoning. Would I evaluate this evidence differently if it supported the opposite conclusion? Am I questioning the evidence itself, or only the side presenting it? How much evidence would realistically persuade me that I am mistaken? Have I looked for the strongest argument against my current view, or only its weakest version? Would I trust this expert if they reached the opposite conclusion on another issue? Am I holding competing claims to identical standards of proof? These questions do not guarantee objectivity, but they help"},{"title":"Misinformation","url":"/misinformation/","description":"Misinformation spreads more easily when urgency, repetition, outrage, and identity cues make checking feel like delay.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Pressure","level":3,"text":"Misinformation How outrage makes false claims feel urgent Misinformation Misinformation How outrage makes false claims feel urgent Misinformation spreads more easily when urgency, repetition, outrage, and identity cues make checking feel like delay. Misinformation spreads more easily when urgency, repetition, outrage, and identity cues make checking feel like delay. policy intervention Why emotional arousal increases sharing and reduces checking How identity cues and repetition shape belief Prebunking tactics for recognising manipulation early improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure misinformation Introduction False and misleading claims often spread not because people have carefully evaluated them, but because the message changes the emotional conditions under which judgement takes place. Urgency, outrage and identity cues can make verification feel like an obstacle rather than a safeguard. Instead of asking, “Is this true?”, people are nudged towards asking, “Should I act now?” or “What does this say about my side?” For anyone seeking to improve analytical thinking, recognising these emotional tactics is as important as checking facts. Research in psychology and communication consistently shows that emotional manipulation can increase willingness to share content, reduce careful scrutiny and make repeated claims feel more familiar and therefore more believable. [SDM Lab] sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk SDM Lab Countering misinformation through psychological inoculation SDM LabCountering misinformation through psychological inoculationMarch 2, 2024 — by S van der Linden · 2024 · Cited by 72 — In this chap... Published: March 2, 2024 Why emotional arousal increases sharing and reduces checking Misinformation frequently succeeds by altering attention rather than by presenting compelling evidence. Messages framed as emergencies—\"before they delete this\", \"share immediately\", or \"they don't want you to know\"—create a sense that delay carries a cost. Under those conditions, the perceived value of rapid action can outweigh the perceived value of verification. This works because heightened emotional arousal changes how people allocate mental effort. Anger narrows attention towards blame and action, while fear prioritises rapid responses to possible threats. In social media environments, these emotional states combine with platforms that reward speed and engagement. A post that provokes outrage is more likely to attract comments, shares and algorithmic amplification before careful correction has time to spread. [SDM Lab] sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk SDM Lab Countering misinformation through psychological inoculation SDM LabCountering misinformation through psychological inoculationMarch 2, 2024 — by S van der Linden · 2024 · Cited by 72 — In this chap... Published: March 2, 2024 Several recurring tactics exploit this pattern: Artificial urgency. Claims insist that action must happen immediately, discouraging fact checking because checking is framed as dangerous delay. Moral outrage. Stories portray obvious villains and innocent victims, encouraging emotional certainty before evidence is examined. Threat amplification. Small risks are presented as imminent catastrophes, making caution appear irresponsible. Exclusive insider framing. Audiences are told they possess hidden knowledge unavailable to ordinary people, making scepticism feel like betrayal rather than critical thinking. These techniques are not confined to one ideology or topic. They appear in health misinformation, election rumours, financial scams, conspiracy theories and fabricated breaking news because they exploit general features of human decision making rather than particular political beliefs. [SDM Lab] sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk SDM Lab Countering misinformation through psychological inoculation SDM LabCountering misinformation through psychological inoculationMarch 2, 2024 — by S van der Linden · 2024 · Cited by 72 — In this chap... Published: March 2, 2024 Why People Believe Misinformation: The Psychology of Fear Beyond the Books Channel: The Open University · Views: 500 · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube How identity cues and repetition shape belief Emotional manipulation rarely relies on urgency alone. Many misleading messages also signal group identity. Rather than presenting evidence, they imply that accepting or rejecting a claim demonstrates loyalty. Identity cues often include phrases such as \"real patriots know\", \"people like us understand\", or \"only the corrupt deny this\". These statements subtly redefine agreement as membership. Rejecting the claim can therefore feel like rejecting one's community rather than evaluating information. Repetition strengthens this effect. Psychological research has long shown that repeated statements tend to feel more familiar, and familiarity can be mistaken for accuracy, especially when people are distracted or emotionally engaged. Repetition across multiple accounts, screenshots or reposts can create the impression of widespread independent confirmation even when every version traces back to the same unsupported source. [SDM Lab] sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk SDM Lab Countering misinformation through psychological inoculation SDM LabCountering misinformation through psychological inoculationMarch 2, 2024 — by S van der Linden · 2024 · Cited by 72 — In this chap... Published: March 2, 2024 Identity and repetition also reinforce each other: Repeated exposure normalises emotionally charged narratives. Seeing trusted members of one's own group repeat a claim increases perceived credibility. Public agreement creates social pressure to conform before independent evaluation. Contradictory evidence may then feel socially costly, encouraging motivated reasoning rather than open investigation. The important analytical point is that social agreement is not itself evidence. Large numbers of people can sincerely repeat inaccurate information, particularly when repetition is driven by emotional engagement rather than independent verification. Why outrage often feels like evidence One reason outrage is persuasive is that it changes the standards people use when judging claims. Strong emotional reactions can create an intuitive sense that \"something this shocking must be true.\" This is a form of emotional reasoning rather than evidential reasoning. Instead of evaluating the quality of sources, people use the intensity of their reaction as an indirect measure of credibility. Misleading content often reinforces this by combining several persuasive signals: emotionally vivid anecdotes instead of representative evidence; selective images or video clips stripped of context; claims that institutions are deliberately hiding the truth; demands for immediate sharing before verification becomes possible. None of these techniques proves a claim is false. Genuine emergencies and genuine scandals also produce strong emotions. The key distinction is whether emotional intensity replaces evidence rather than directing attention towards evidence. An analytical reader therefore separates two questions: How \"rage bait\" manipulates emotions for online engagement Channel: CBS Mornings Open on YouTube 1. Does this deserve an emotional response? 2. Has the evidence earned that response? Keeping those questions separate makes manipulation substantially more difficult. Stopping the spread of misinformation, with Sander van der Linden, PhD Speaking of Psychology Channel: American Psychological Association Open on YouTube Prebunking tactics for recognising manipulation early Traditional fact checking happens after misinformation has already been encountered. Increasing evidence suggests that prebunking —warning people about common manipulation techniques before exposure—can make them more resistant to later misinformation. Rather than teaching individual facts, prebunking teaches recognition of recurring persuasive strategies. [SDM Lab+2Taylor & Francis] sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk SDM Lab Countering misinformation through psychological inoculation SDM LabCountering misinformation through psychological inoculationMarch 2, 2024 — by S van der Linden · 2024 · Cited by 72 — In this chap... Published: March 2, 2024 Research led by social psychologists has found that exposing people to weakened examples of misleading techniques can improve their ability to recognise manipulation across different topics. Interactive approaches, including educational games that let participants practise identifying tactics such as impersonation, emotional manipulation and conspiracy framing, have shown measurable improvements in resilience to misinformation. [Taylor & Francis+2SDM Lab] taylorfrancis.com psychological inoculation fake news sander van der linden jon roozenbeek Taylor & FrancisPsychological Inoculation Against Fake News 11by S van der Linden · 2020 · Cited by 165 — We provide empirical evidence... A practical prebunking routine asks readers to pause before"},{"title":"Status Threat","url":"/status-threat/","description":"Admitting error becomes harder when revision feels like public loss, professional embarrassment, or betrayal of a group.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Pressure","level":3,"text":"Status Threat Why changing your mind can feel unsafe Status Threat Status Threat Why changing your mind can feel unsafe Admitting error becomes harder when revision feels like public loss, professional embarrassment, or betrayal of a group. Admitting error becomes harder when revision feels like public loss, professional embarrassment, or betrayal of a group. population context How pride and reputation raise the cost of revision Why public commitments harden weak conclusions Ways to make correction less humiliating improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure status threat Introduction Changing your mind is often presented as a purely intellectual exercise: examine the evidence, update your beliefs, and move on. In practice , it rarely feels that simple. Revising a view can threaten reputation, professional credibility, friendships, political identity or social standing. When admitting error feels like admitting weakness, the emotional cost of being wrong can outweigh the intellectual benefit of being right. Research on identity protective cognition, social status and humiliation suggests that resistance to evidence is frequently driven less by ignorance than by the perceived social consequences of changing position. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com identity affirming rather than identity threatening. Effectively... It demands in addition the protection of the science communication... For anyone seeking to improve analytical thinking, this matters because the obstacle is often not a lack of information but a feeling of danger. If changing your mind seems likely to bring embarrassment, exclusion or loss of status, reasoning can quietly shift from asking \"What is true?\" to asking \"What can I safely admit?\" How pride and reputation raise the cost of revision People live within networks where opinions communicate more than beliefs. They also signal competence, loyalty, confidence and reliability. In many situations, admitting a mistake risks being interpreted not merely as correcting one fact but as revealing poor judgement. The cost varies by context: A manager fears appearing incompetent after supporting a failed strategy. A scientist worries about professional reputation after publicly defending a hypothesis. A politician fears accusations of inconsistency. A member of a close knit community worries that accepting contrary evidence could look like disloyalty. These concerns are often rational from a social perspective. Human beings depend on reputation for trust, influence and cooperation. The result is that evidence becomes entangled with status. Researchers studying identity protective cognition argue that people selectively evaluate evidence in ways that protect membership within valued groups. On polarised issues, individuals with greater analytical ability are not necessarily less biased. Instead, they may become better at constructing arguments that defend their group's position because the social penalty for disagreement outweighs the personal benefit of accuracy. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com identity affirming rather than identity threatening. Effectively... It demands in addition the protection of the science communication... This helps explain why intelligent people can sincerely resist strong evidence. Their reasoning is not simply serving truth seeking; it is simultaneously protecting relationships, identity and social standing. Why public commitments harden weak conclusions A belief expressed privately is easier to revise than one announced before an audience. Public commitments create several additional pressures. First, they become part of a person's public identity. Others begin to associate them with a particular position, making later revision more conspicuous. Second, consistency itself has social value. Reliable people are generally expected to keep their word. Unfortunately, this desirable norm can spill over into situations where changing one's mind would actually demonstrate intellectual honesty. Third, public disagreement often invites competition rather than inquiry. Once people have defended a position in debates, meetings or online discussions, admitting error may feel like handing victory to an opponent rather than improving understanding. This is especially visible on social media, where old statements remain searchable and corrections are highly visible. A mistaken prediction that might once have faded from memory can instead become permanent evidence against someone's competence. Public visibility therefore increases the emotional stakes of revision. The consequence is that weak conclusions may become more firmly defended after they have been publicly declared than they were before any evidence was presented. \"Why you think you're right — even if you're wrong\" Julia Galef at TEDx Channel: EcoworldReactor Open on YouTube Humiliation changes the psychology of evidence Humiliation differs from simple embarrassment. Embarrassment is usually temporary and concerns a specific mistake. Humiliation involves a perceived lowering of social rank or dignity in front of others. It carries a stronger sense that one's value or status has been damaged. Psychological research links humiliation with defensive responses, anger , withdrawal and heightened sensitivity to further criticism. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Losing trust in the world: Humiliation and its consequencesby P Leask · 2013 · Cited by 74 — I argue that psychotherapists risk pathol... When correction is experienced as humiliation, several shifts in thinking become more likely: criticism is interpreted as personal attack rather than informational feedback ; counter evidence is scrutinised much more harshly than supporting evidence; neutral observers are seen as taking sides; admitting uncertainty feels increasingly unsafe; preserving dignity becomes a higher priority than discovering accuracy. Under these conditions, reasoning serves emotional self protection. This helps explain why direct factual correction sometimes produces surprisingly little change. The information itself may be convincing, but accepting it carries a social meaning that feels intolerable. Status threats inside groups Groups often reward loyalty more visibly than independent judgement. Whether the setting is political, religious, professional or organisational, members quickly learn which opinions earn respect and which create friction. When disagreement threatens acceptance, individuals may privately recognise problems while publicly defending the prevailing view. Identity protective cognition research suggests that this process is not necessarily conscious deception. Instead, people naturally find identity consistent evidence more persuasive and identity threatening evidence easier to dismiss. The motivation is to avoid social costs rather than simply to avoid truth. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com identity affirming rather than identity threatening. Effectively... It demands in addition the protection of the science communication... This produces a subtle paradox. The stronger the community, the more valuable membership becomes. The more valuable membership becomes, the greater the psychological incentive to avoid beliefs that could jeopardise it. Consequently, groups with strong internal trust can also become environments where changing one's mind becomes unusually difficult. Identity Protective Cognition: Why We Ignore Facts That Threaten Our Identity Channel: Rational Ruminations Open on YouTube Why experts are not immune Professional expertise creates additional status pressures. Experts invest years developing specialised knowledge and public credibility. Admitting mistakes may therefore appear more expensive than it does for novices. Academic researchers, executives, physicians and technical specialists all face incentives that can unintentionally discourage revision: previous publications or presentations become part of professional identity; career advancement may reward confidence more visibly than uncertainty; competitors may exploit visible mistakes; followers or clients often expect certainty. Ironically, expertise increases both the capacity to evaluate evidence and the reputational cost of abandoning previous conclusions. The healthiest professional cultures therefore distinguish between incompetence and updating. In science, for example, theories are expected to change as better evidence emerges. Although individuals may still experience reputational concerns, the ideal remains that revision reflects progress rather than failure. Ways to make correction less humiliating Because status threats distort reasoning, improving analytical thinking requires reducing the emotional cost of being wrong. Several practical approaches help. Normalise revision rather than celebrate certainty. Teams that praise careful updating instead of unwavering confidence reduce the status penalty attached to correction. Separate identity from ideas. Viewing beliefs as working models rather than personal"},{"title":"Stress","url":"/stress/","description":"Stress can shrink the mental space needed to compare explanations, delay closure, and notice contradictions.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Pressure","level":3,"text":"Stress Why pressure makes first answers feel final Stress Stress Why pressure makes first answers feel final Stress can shrink the mental space needed to compare explanations, delay closure, and notice contradictions. Stress can shrink the mental space needed to compare explanations, delay closure, and notice contradictions. mechanism What stress does to working memory and cognitive control Why threat favours defence, habit, and quick closure Practical pauses that restore room for analysis improving your thinking and analytical skills pressure stress Introduction When people are under threat or intense pressure , reasoning does not usually fail because intelligence disappears. It becomes harder because the mental systems that support flexible thinking are diverted towards immediate survival. The result is a narrower field of attention, reduced ability to hold several possibilities in mind at once, and a stronger urge to settle quickly on the first explanation that seems to fit. This is why stress often makes initial judgements feel unusually certain, even when the available evidence is incomplete. For anyone trying to improve analytical thinking, the practical lesson is not that emotions must be eliminated. Rather, it is to recognise when stress has reduced the mental space needed to compare alternatives, notice contradictions, and revise an early conclusion. Research from cognitive psychology, neuroscience and stress physiology consistently shows that acute stress weakens working memory and cognitive flexibility—the executive functions that make careful reasoning possible. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe Effects of Acute Stress on Core Executive Functions by GS Shields · 2016 · Cited by 1254 — We found that stress impaired working memory and cognitive flexibility, whereas it had nuanced... What stress does to working memory and cognitive control Flexible reasoning depends heavily on working memory: the limited mental workspace used to hold information temporarily while comparing explanations, testing assumptions and following chains of logic. When this workspace shrinks, thinking becomes less exploratory and more linear. Acute stress changes how the brain allocates its resources. Elevated stress hormones and increased release of neurotransmitters such as noradrenaline and dopamine help prepare the body for rapid action, but they also reduce the efficiency of prefrontal cortex networks that normally support planning, working memory and cognitive control. Instead of carefully coordinating competing ideas, the brain becomes more likely to rely on simpler, well established responses. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCStress signalling pathways that impair prefrontal cortex by AFT Arnsten · 2009 · Cited by 4959 — The Review first describes how exposure to even mild uncontrollable stress can rapidly impair... Large reviews of experimental studies find that acute stress reliably impairs two executive functions that matter directly for reasoning: Working memory , making it harder to keep multiple pieces of evidence active simultaneously. Cognitive flexibility , reducing the ability to switch perspectives, update beliefs or abandon an unsuccessful strategy. The effects on inhibition are more mixed, suggesting that stress does not simply make people impulsive. Rather, it changes which forms of control are preserved and which are weakened. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe Effects of Acute Stress on Core Executive Functions by GS Shields · 2016 · Cited by 1254 — We found that stress impaired working memory and cognitive flexibility, whereas it had nuanced... This explains a common experience during high pressure discussions. A person may genuinely struggle to remember qualifying information, alternative explanations or earlier uncertainties—not because they have disappeared, but because the cognitive workspace needed to manipulate them has become overloaded. Neuroscience of Workplace Stress: Ways to Calm Your Brain Fast Channel: Sharon McLaughlin MD Open on YouTube Why pressure makes first answers feel final Stress changes not only how much information can be processed, but also what the brain treats as worth processing. Under threat, uncertainty itself becomes uncomfortable. Continuing to compare competing explanations consumes mental resources at precisely the moment when the brain is prioritising rapid action. Closing the question quickly often feels safer than remaining undecided. Several mechanisms reinforce this tendency. Attention narrows. Threat increases focus on information that appears immediately relevant to the perceived danger, while peripheral details receive less processing. This can improve reaction speed in emergencies but reduces the chance of noticing evidence that challenges an initial interpretation. [Nature] nature.com Neurocognitive effects of stress: a metaparadigm perspectiveby EJ Kim · 2023 · Cited by 161 — Stress has been found to impair learn... Habit becomes more attractive. When executive control weakens, previously learned routines and familiar explanations become easier to retrieve than careful analysis. Existing beliefs therefore feel more compelling under stress, even if they are not objectively better supported. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Prefrontal cortex executive processes affected by stress in...by M Girotti · 2017 · Cited by 262 — We will review the effects of stre... Updating becomes harder. Flexible thinking requires replacing an old interpretation with a better one. Stress reduces this capacity, increasing perseveration—the tendency to continue applying the same strategy despite evidence that it is no longer appropriate. [Nature] nature.com Even though an individual's perception of their chronic...Read more... Together these effects help explain why people under pressure often become more certain before they become more accurate. Why threat favours defence, habit and quick closure Not all stressful situations have the same psychological meaning. A deadline, physical danger, public criticism and an attack on personal identity all activate overlapping but not identical responses. When a situation is interpreted as threatening, defensive goals begin to compete with truth seeking goals. Cognitive resources increasingly support questions such as: How do I protect myself? Who is responsible? What action should I take immediately? Which explanation reduces uncertainty fastest? These questions are often adaptive during genuine emergencies. If a cyclist swerves into traffic, hesitation is dangerous. Quick pattern recognition and habitual responses save time. The problem arises when the same threat oriented mode is activated during tasks that require careful analysis rather than immediate action. Complex policy debates, scientific questions, workplace disagreements or personal conflicts benefit from sustained uncertainty while evidence is evaluated. Stress biases the mind in the opposite direction. Research on resilience and executive function suggests that threat states shift cognition towards reliable, well practised responses while limiting exploration and flexibility. In effect, the brain trades adaptability for speed and predictability. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersMental Resilience and Coping With Stressby IS Palamarchuk · 2021 · Cited by 100 — In other words, “cold” executive functioning i... Decision Making When You’re Overwhelmed: What To Do Channel: Abby Medcalf PhD Open on YouTube Acute and chronic stress do not affect reasoning in the same way Short bursts of stress and long term stress deserve separate consideration. Acute stress produces rapid, often reversible changes in executive function. Once the stress response subsides, cognitive flexibility frequently returns. Laboratory studies occasionally find small improvements in specific tasks—particularly when the stress is manageable, perceived as controllable, or closely matched to the task's demands. For example, some experiments have reported improved set shifting under particular conditions, although these effects are not the dominant pattern and diminish when people experience the stressor as uncontrollable. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineFull article: Acute stressor effects on cognitive flexibilityby RL Gabrys · 2019 · Cited by 62 — These data indica... Chronic stress is different. Repeated activation of stress systems is associated with longer lasting changes in prefrontal cortex function and structure, alongside persistent difficulties with working memory, attention and flexible behaviour. Rather than temporarily narrowing thought, prolonged stress can gradually make rigid thinking more likely across many situations. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCEffects of chronic stress on cognitive function by M Girotti · 2024 · Cited by 86 — In this review, we will present an overview of the cognitive domains affected by stress with a spe... This distinction matters because the best"},{"title":"Feedback","url":"/feedback/","description":"Feedback turns reasoning from a one-off performance into a process that can improve after errors.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Feedback How Do You Learn From Wrong Calls? Feedback Feedback How Do You Learn From Wrong Calls? Feedback turns reasoning from a one off performance into a process that can improve after errors. Feedback turns reasoning from a one off performance into a process that can improve after errors. policy intervention Why feedback matters Error review routines Avoiding hindsight excuses improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback Introduction Feedback loops improve judgement by turning a decision into a learning cycle: make the call, record the reasoning, observe what happened, compare outcome with expectation, and change the next decision process. Without that loop, experience can simply reinforce confidence. With it, errors become data. The core lesson from research on learning, forecasting, team reviews and hindsight bias is that feedback works best when it is specific, timely enough to be useful, tied to the original expectation, and focused on the reasoning process rather than on blame or embarrassment. Error alone is not enough; error followed by corrective feedback is what tends to produce learning, especially when people were confident in the wrong answer. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Learning from Errorsby J Metcalfe · 2017 · Cited by 899 — Experimental investigations indicate that errorful learning followed by c... This matters for improving thinking and analytical skills because many important judgements do not produce clean, immediate signals. A doctor may not hear about a missed diagnosis, a manager may confuse a lucky success with a sound decision, and a political forecaster may remember vague impressions rather than exact probabilities . Better judgement therefore depends on deliberately designing feedback: write down the prediction, define what would count as success or failure, review the result, and protect the review from hindsight excuses. Why Feedback Matters Feedback is the difference between merely having experience and learning from experience. Daniel Kahneman and Gary Klein’s influential discussion of intuitive expertise argued that skilled intuition is most trustworthy in environments with valid cues and opportunities for rapid, unambiguous feedback. Chess, firefighting and some clinical pattern recognition tasks can offer repeated cues and correction; many strategic, financial, political and organisational decisions do not. In those weaker learning environments, people may feel experienced while receiving little reliable evidence about whether their judgement is improving. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The practical implication is uncomfortable: confidence is not proof of learning. A person can make hundreds of decisions and still learn the wrong lessons if outcomes are noisy, delayed, selectively observed or interpreted defensively. This is why feedback loops should capture not just “what happened” but “what did we expect, how confident were we, and why?” The loop creates a record against which judgement can be tested rather than reconstructed. Research on learning from errors supports this point. Janet Metcalfe’s review of experimental work found that making mistakes can aid learning when followed by corrective feedback, and that high confidence errors can be especially memorable once corrected. The sting of being wrong is not the mechanism by itself; the useful part is the contrast between the mistaken belief and the corrective information. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Learning from Errorsby J Metcalfe · 2017 · Cited by 899 — Experimental investigations indicate that errorful learning followed by c... Feedback also needs good design. Kluger and DeNisi’s major meta analysis of feedback interventions found that feedback improved performance on average, but more than a third of interventions reduced performance. One reason is that feedback can shift attention away from the task and towards ego, self defence or social comparison. For judgement improvement, the safest rule is to make feedback about the decision model: information quality, assumptions, probability estimates, alternatives considered and triggers for revision. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) The Effects of Feedback Interventions on Performance Research Gate(PDF) The Effects of Feedback Interventions on Performance What a Good Judgement Loop Looks Like A useful judgement loop has four parts: a forecast, a decision record, an outcome check and a process review. Each part protects against a different failure. First, the judgement must be stated clearly enough to be wrong. “This project feels risky” is too vague to teach much later. “There is a 40% chance the supplier misses the August deadline” is much better, because it can be compared with reality. Probability estimates are not just for professional forecasters; they force a thinker to separate “possible”, “likely” and “almost certain”. Second, the reasoning must be recorded before the outcome is known. This can be a short decision journal: the options, the chosen path, the evidence available, the main assumptions, the confidence level, and what would change the decision. A record matters because memory is a poor audit trail. Once the result is known, people tend to remember the past as more predictable than it was. Hindsight bias includes memory distortion, changed beliefs about likelihood and inflated belief in one’s own foresight. [Carlson School of Management] carlsonschool.umn.edu vohs et al 2012 hindsight bias vohs et al 2012 hindsight bias Third, the outcome must be checked against the original expectation. This is where many informal feedback loops fail. People review only dramatic failures, forget quiet near misses, or stop at “it worked” and “it didn’t”. A better review distinguishes four cases: Decision processGood outcomeBad outcomeGood processRepeat the process, but do not assume perfectionTreat as possible bad luck; check whether any warning signs were missedBad processTreat as good luck; do not reward the reasoningDiagnose the error and change the routine This distinction helps avoid outcome bias: the tendency to judge a decision mainly by its result rather than by the quality of the reasoning given what was knowable at the time. A good bet can lose, and a reckless bet can win. Feedback improves judgement only when it can separate luck from skill. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com Open source on thedecisionlab.com. Finally, the loop must change behaviour. A review that produces no new threshold, checklist, question, trigger or habit is not a feedback loop; it is a conversation about the past. The smallest useful output is a rule for the next similar case: “Before approving a project plan, ask which assumption would make the timeline fail,” or “For any forecast above 80%, write down the strongest reason it might be wrong.” Error Review Routines The most effective error review routines are simple enough to use repeatedly. They do not require a long post mortem for every decision. They require a stable habit of capturing judgement before reality rewrites the story. A compact routine can work like this: 1. Before the decision: write the call, confidence level and top three reasons. 2. Before acting: name one plausible alternative explanation or failure route. 3. After the outcome: compare the result with the original expectation. 4. During review: ask whether the error came from missing information, weak reasoning, poor process, bad incentives, or unavoidable uncertainty. 5. Before the next decision: change one practical rule, checklist item or trigger. After action reviews are a team version of this loop. They are commonly framed around a small set of questions: what was supposed to happen, what actually happened, why was there a difference, and what should change next time? BetterEvaluation describes an after action review as a simple method for assessing organisational performance by bringing a team together to discuss a task, while guidance for humanitarian and operational settings stresses that an AAR is not a complaint session or a full evaluation report but a learning opportunity after successes and setbacks. [Better Evaluation] betterevaluation.org Open source on betterevaluation.org. The evidence suggests that structured reviews can improve team performance when they are implemented well. A study of after action review meetings notes that AARs can be used to reduce errors and cites evidence that team effectiveness can improve when teams conduct them. Patient safety discussions have also treated AARs as a way to improve individual and team learning after clinical events, though the quality of the review depends heavily on psychological safety, relevant facts and follow through. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For individual judgement, the equivalent is a lightweight decision journal. It is most useful for decisions that are important, repeated or uncertain: hiring, investments, medical"},{"title":"Team Reviews","url":"/team-reviews/","description":"A good after-action review asks what was expected, what happened, why it differed and what should change next time.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Feedback","level":3,"text":"Team Reviews When Team Reviews Actually Improve Judgement Team Reviews Team Reviews When Team Reviews Actually Improve Judgement A good after action review asks what was expected, what happened, why it differed and what should change next time. A good after action review asks what was expected, what happened, why it differed and what should change next time. policy intervention The four questions that keep reviews focused Why blame ruins feedback How teams turn lessons into routines improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback team reviews Introduction After action reviews (AARs) improve team judgement when they are designed as learning conversations rather than performance evaluations. Instead of asking who made the mistake, they compare what the team expected with what actually happened, examine why the two differed, and identify specific changes for the next similar situation. This structure helps teams avoid hindsight bias, capture useful knowledge before it is forgotten, and steadily improve future decisions rather than merely explaining past outcomes . Originally developed in the military and now widely used in healthcare, emergency management and business, AARs work best when they are short, evidence based and focused on improving future judgement rather than assigning blame. [Wharton Executive Education+2cebma.org] executiveeducation.wharton.upenn.edu Wharton Executive Education After Action Review Wharton Executive EducationAfter Action Review AAR Process Nano Tools for LeadersThe After Action Review (AAR) was developed by the U... The four questions that keep reviews focused The most effective after action reviews are built around four simple questions. Their value lies not in their simplicity but in the discipline they impose on the discussion. 1. What did we expect to happen? Starting with expectations forces the team to recover its original reasoning before hindsight reshapes memories. Good reviews compare today's knowledge with yesterday's assumptions rather than judging past decisions using information that only became available later. [2. What actually happened? ] chiefexecutive.net · What actually happened? · Why did it happen? · Most importantly, how do we improve our next operation?Read more... This stage establishes a shared factual record. Teams separate observable events from interpretations, reducing arguments over recollection and preventing premature conclusions. 3. Why were there differences? This is the analytical core of the review. The aim is not merely to explain failure but to identify which assumptions proved wrong, which signals were missed, which uncertainties were underestimated, and which decisions were sound despite producing poor outcomes because of chance. 4. What should we sustain or change next time? Reviews should finish by identifying concrete behavioural or procedural adjustments. Successful practices deserve as much attention as failures because understanding why something worked makes it more likely to be repeated. [cebma.org+2Wharton Executive Education] cebma.org Guide to the After Action ReviewOne team member reported, “without an AAR you eep learning your lessons again the hard way!” An AAR is ce... Notice that none of these questions asks, \"Who caused the problem?\" Responsibility still matters, but the review's purpose is improving collective judgement. Questions centred on reasoning and evidence produce far richer learning than questions centred on individual fault. Episode 14 — Turn Lessons Learned into Capability with After Action Reviews and Follow Through Channel: Bare Metal Cyber Open on YouTube Why blame ruins feedback Many organisations hold meetings after important projects yet continue making similar mistakes . The difference is often not whether they review events but how they review them. When participants expect criticism or embarrassment, they naturally defend themselves. They explain away mistakes, minimise uncertainty, or avoid mentioning weak signals they ignored. The discussion becomes a negotiation over reputation rather than an investigation into judgement. Research on psychological safety—the shared belief that people can speak candidly without fear of humiliation—shows why this matters. Teams with greater psychological safety are more willing to report mistakes, ask questions and challenge assumptions, behaviours that support organisational learning rather than conceal problems. Psychological safety does not eliminate accountability; instead, it enables honest reporting so accountability can be based on accurate information rather than incomplete stories. [Sage Journals+2Harvard Business School] journals.sagepub.com It introduces the construct of team psychological safety—a shared... Blame also creates another subtle problem: it encourages outcome based thinking. A successful project can contain poor decisions that happened to work because circumstances were favourable. Equally, a well reasoned decision can produce an unfavourable outcome because of uncertainty or bad luck. Teams that evaluate only results often reinforce bad judgement while criticising good reasoning that encountered unpredictable events. Effective AARs therefore examine the quality of the decision process alongside the outcome itself. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org by Amy C. Edmondson... In some cases they and their teams had devoted many hours to after action reviews, postmortems, and...Read more... Video 1: Introduction to the AAR facilitation process Channel: HSE National QPS Open on YouTube How teams turn lessons into routines Many reviews produce pages of observations that are rarely used again. Teams improve judgement only when lessons become part of future decisions. Useful AARs therefore produce a small number of practical changes rather than a long catalogue of recommendations. These often include: updating decision checklists ; modifying planning assumptions; changing escalation thresholds; improving communication protocols; recording new warning indicators to monitor; clarifying roles during future incidents. The changes should be specific enough that another team could apply them without attending the original meeting. Vague conclusions such as \"communicate better\" or \"plan more carefully\" rarely change behaviour. By contrast, \"include operations staff in supplier risk meetings\" or \"require written probability estimates before approval\" creates observable habits that strengthen judgement over time. [alnap.hacdn.io+2ResearchGate] alnap.hacdn.io After Action Review Technical GuidanceThe meeting should follow an agenda, using the four guiding questions to set up the “meat” of the d... Another useful practice is assigning ownership for each lesson. Without a named individual or team responsible for implementing a change, even accurate insights are easily forgotten once normal work resumes. Reviews should examine successes as well as failures One common misconception is that after action reviews exist only for things that went wrong. In fact, successful operations often contain valuable information about sound judgement. Reviewing success helps distinguish repeatable practices from fortunate accidents. It can reveal effective coordination, accurate forecasting, timely information sharing or good contingency planning that might otherwise remain invisible because the outcome appeared routine. The military tradition that popularised AARs deliberately applies them after successful exercises as well as unsuccessful ones, recognising that sustained excellence depends on understanding why success occurred, not merely celebrating it. [Wharton Executive Education+2Wikipedia] executiveeducation.wharton.upenn.edu Wharton Executive Education After Action Review Wharton Executive EducationAfter Action Review AAR Process Nano Tools for LeadersThe After Action Review (AAR) was developed by the U... Common failure modes Even organisations committed to learning can undermine after action reviews through poor design. Frequent problems include: Waiting too long. Memories become reconstructed and important details disappear. Allowing senior voices to dominate. Junior participants often observed different evidence but may not volunteer it. Collecting opinions before facts. Speculation spreads quickly if the timeline is unclear. Trying to solve every organisational problem. Reviews become unfocused and produce little action. Recording lessons without follow through. Knowledge that never changes future practice has little value. Experienced facilitators therefore keep discussions tightly focused on the specific event, encourage contributions from everyone involved, and distinguish observed facts from interpretations before discussing improvements. [pinnacle leaders.com+2alnap.hacdn.io] pinnacle leaders.com Leaders Guide to AAR The Leader's Guide to After Action Reviews (AAR)by CA Center Training · 2013 · Cited by 5 — An AAR is a professional discussion of a trai... A practical review format"},{"title":"Decision Journals","url":"/decision-journals/","description":"A short decision journal freezes what you knew, expected and believed before the outcome rewrites the story.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Feedback","level":3,"text":"Decision Journals Can a Decision Journal Make You Less Foolable? Decision Journals Decision Journals Can a Decision Journal Make You Less Foolable? A short decision journal freezes what you knew, expected and believed before the outcome rewrites the story. A short decision journal freezes what you knew, expected and believed before the outcome rewrites the story. policy intervention What to record before the outcome How journals expose hindsight bias Turning entries into better next decisions improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback decision journals Introduction A decision journal is one of the simplest tools for improving judgement because it captures what you genuinely believed before reality revealed the answer. Instead of relying on memory—which is surprisingly easy to rewrite after the fact—you create a dated record of your reasoning, expectations and confidence. When the outcome arrives, you compare reality with your original thinking rather than with a reconstructed version of it. This matters because hindsight bias, often described as the \"I knew it all along\" effect, makes past events appear far more predictable than they really were. Once people know the outcome, they tend to remember having expected it, even when their original beliefs were quite different. A decision journal interrupts that process by freezing your thinking before hindsight can edit it. Research on judgement and forecasting consistently shows that learning improves when predictions are recorded in advance and later evaluated against what was actually written rather than what is remembered. [PubMed+2Springer] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Fifty years of hindsight bias research Reflection on...by B Fischhoff · 2025 · Cited by 13 — Fischhoff (1975) introduced tasks for... What to Record Before the Outcome The value of a decision journal depends less on length than on specificity. A one page entry written before an important decision is often enough. Record the following: The decision itself. State exactly what choice you are making. The date and context. Include the information available at the time. Future reviews should not benefit from information you did not yet possess. Your reasoning. Explain why you think this is the best option. Focus on cause and effect logic rather than conclusions alone. Alternative options. Briefly note the strongest competing choice and why you rejected it. Key assumptions . List the facts you believe to be true and the uncertainties that matter most. Expected outcomes. Describe what success and failure would look like in measurable terms. Confidence level. Assign a probability where practical—for example, \"70% chance the project finishes before October.\" Signals that would change your mind. Identify evidence that would justify revising the decision before results arrive. These elements make later review far more informative than simply asking whether the decision \"worked\". A good process can produce a poor outcome because of bad luck, and a weak process can occasionally succeed through favourable circumstances. Recording assumptions separately helps distinguish these cases. [Springer+2Stanford University] link.springer.com Knowing what has happened can degrade these evaluations, reducing forecasters' ability to learn...Read more... How Decision Journals Expose Hindsight Bias Hindsight bias is not simply forgetting details. Research suggests that outcome knowledge actively changes how people reconstruct their earlier beliefs, making events seem more foreseeable and inevitable than they originally appeared. Baruch Fischhoff's pioneering experiments demonstrated that once participants learned an outcome, they systematically overestimated how predictable it had been beforehand. Decades of subsequent research have confirmed the robustness of this effect across many settings. [PubMed+2Carlson School of Management] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Fifty years of hindsight bias research Reflection on...by B Fischhoff · 2025 · Cited by 13 — Fischhoff (1975) introduced tasks for... A decision journal blocks three common hindsight excuses. \"I knew this would happen.\" [researchgate.net] researchgate.net I Knew It Would Happen: Remembered Probabilities of...6 Jun 2026 — In short, events are viewed as more predictable in hindsight than the... The journal contains your actual forecast. If you assigned only a 40% probability to an event, the written record prevents memory from quietly turning it into an 80% prediction. \"The evidence was obvious.\" It rarely was. The journal preserves what information was genuinely available at the time, reminding you which facts were uncertain or missing. \"The decision was obviously wrong.\" After failure, people often judge the process solely by the outcome. A journal allows evaluation of whether the reasoning was sound given the evidence then available, rather than judging yesterday's decision using today's knowledge. This distinction is especially valuable in uncertain environments such as investing, business strategy, hiring, product development and public policy, where outcomes are influenced by chance as well as judgement. [ResearchGate+2Academy of Management Journals] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The Consequences of the Hindsight Bias in Medical...The hindsight bias manifests in the tendency to exaggerate the ext... The Ultimate Guide to Using a Decision Journal Channel: Farnam Street Open on YouTube Turning Entries into Better Next Decisions A decision journal becomes useful only when it is reviewed. Rather than asking, \"Was I right?\", ask questions such as: Which assumptions proved correct? Which assumptions failed? What information did I underestimate? Did I express appropriate confidence? Was I surprised for the right reasons or because I ignored warning signs? Would I make the same decision again knowing only what I knew then? This shifts attention from defending past decisions to improving future judgement. Over time, recurring patterns usually emerge. Some people consistently underestimate project delays. Others are routinely overconfident when evaluating familiar topics but overly cautious in unfamiliar ones. These patterns are difficult to detect from memory alone because memory selectively highlights dramatic successes and failures while smoothing away the everyday forecasting errors that reveal genuine habits. [University of California Press+2PMC] online.ucpress.edu Overprecision in the Survey of Professional University of California PressOverprecision in the Survey of Professional Forecasters28 Feb 2024 — We find forecasts are overly precise... Process Quality Versus Outcome Quality One of the biggest advantages of decision journals is separating decision quality from outcome quality. Imagine two managers approving similar projects. The first performs careful analysis, identifies key risks, estimates only a moderate chance of success and proceeds. The project later fails because of an unexpected regulatory change. The second ignores obvious warning signs, performs little analysis and approves a project that succeeds because a competitor unexpectedly exits the market. Looking only at outcomes suggests the second manager made the better decision. Looking at their journals suggests the opposite. This distinction prevents organisations from rewarding lucky processes or punishing thoughtful ones. It also encourages better calibration—matching confidence to evidence instead of treating every successful decision as proof of expertise. Research on forecasting repeatedly finds that accurate forecasters distinguish between uncertainty and certainty more carefully than average decision makers. [Stanford University+2Springer] stanford.edu Across a wide range of tasks, research has shown that people make poor probabilistic predictions of future events. Recently, the U.S. Int... Before You Make A Hard Decision, Use A Decision Journal (with example) Channel: Vicky Zhao Open on YouTube Combining Decision Journals with Pre Mortems Decision journals become even stronger when paired with a pre mortem. A pre mortem asks participants to imagine that a decision has already failed and then explain why. Developed by Gary Klein, this exercise encourages consideration of overlooked risks before commitment becomes psychologically difficult. The resulting concerns can be written directly into the journal as explicit assumptions or warning signals to monitor later. [theuncertaintyproject.org] theuncertaintyproject.org Pre MortemDeveloped by Gary Klein, the pre mortem analysis is a tool intended to increase the probability of a project's success. It is b... Together, the two techniques create complementary safeguards: The pre mortem broadens thinking before commitment. The decision journal preserves that thinking for later evaluation. The review compares both predictions and imagined failure modes with what actually happened. This makes it harder to claim afterwards that the risks were always obvious. Common"},{"title":"Probability Calls","url":"/probability-calls/","description":"Putting a number on a prediction turns fuzzy confidence into something that can be checked, scored and improved.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Feedback","level":3,"text":"Probability Calls Why Vague Predictions Do Not Teach Much Probability Calls Probability Calls Why Vague Predictions Do Not Teach Much Putting a number on a prediction turns fuzzy confidence into something that can be checked, scored and improved. Putting a number on a prediction turns fuzzy confidence into something that can be checked, scored and improved. mechanism Why probabilities beat vague language How to define a forecast question Checking calibration without overreacting improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback probability calls Introduction Many judgement errors persist because people never state clearly what they expected. Saying that an outcome is \"likely\", \"possible\" or \"unlikely\" feels informative, but those words are flexible enough to be reinterpreted after the event. Assigning a probability instead—such as a 30% chance, a 70% chance or a 95% chance—turns a vague opinion into a forecast that can later be checked. That simple shift creates the feedback loop needed to improve judgement: expectations become measurable, confidence becomes testable, and repeated forecasts reveal whether you are systematically overconfident, underconfident or well calibrated. Research from forecasting tournaments and decision science shows that keeping score in this way is one of the most effective ways to improve predictive judgement over time. [AI Impacts] aiimpacts.org AI ImpactsEvidence on good forecasting practices from the...Tetlock says that reading the news and generating probabilities isn't enough... Why probabilities beat vague language Words such as \"probably\", \"good chance\" or \"almost certain\" sound precise but mean different things to different people. One person's \"likely\" may imply a 60% chance, while another hears 90%. This ambiguity makes learning difficult because nobody can later agree on what was actually predicted. A numerical probability removes that ambiguity. If you predict that there is a 70% chance a project will finish on time, the forecast can eventually be judged against reality. One forecast proves little, but a collection of forecasts allows patterns to emerge. If events assigned 70% probability occur roughly seven times out of ten over many similar predictions, your confidence is well calibrated. If they occur only half the time, you have been overconfident. If they occur nine times out of ten, you have been too cautious. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Society for Judgment and Decision MakingA model based approach for the analysis of the calibration...The calibration of probability or c... This is why forecasting researchers distinguish between expressing an opinion and making a forecast. Opinions may persuade other people, but forecasts create evidence about the quality of your judgement. How to define a forecast question Not every statement can be assigned a useful probability. Good forecast questions share three features. A clear event. Everyone should agree whether it happened. \"The product launch will be successful\" is too vague. \"The product launches before 30 September\" is clear. A defined time horizon. Every forecast needs a deadline for resolution. A binary outcome. Although reality is often complex, scoring becomes much easier when the question resolves as either yes or no. For example: \"There is a 40% chance our supplier misses the August delivery deadline.\" \"There is a 75% chance inflation will remain above 3% at year end.\" \"There is a 20% chance this job applicant accepts the offer within two weeks.\" Each statement specifies what counts as success or failure before the outcome is known. That prevents hindsight from quietly changing the meaning afterwards. Professional forecasting tournaments, including those behind the Good Judgment Project, rely on carefully written binary questions for exactly this reason. Precise questions make meaningful scoring possible and reduce disputes over whether a prediction was actually correct. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Good Judgment Project The Good Judgment Project Superforecasting Philip Tetlock Channel: The Long Now Foundation Open on YouTube Checking calibration without overreacting The goal is not to make every prediction correct. A forecast with a 30% probability should be wrong most of the time. Good forecasting means matching confidence to reality. Look for long run patterns Calibration only becomes meaningful across many forecasts. If you predict twenty events at 80% probability, you would expect around sixteen to occur. A run of three unexpected failures does not automatically mean your judgement is poor. Looking at groups of forecasts avoids overreacting to chance. Random variation affects individual predictions, but repeated forecasts reveal systematic tendencies. Separate calibration from discrimination Good judgement has two related but different qualities. Calibration asks whether your stated probabilities match observed frequencies. Discrimination asks whether you successfully distinguish likely events from unlikely ones. Someone who predicts 50% for every question may end up reasonably calibrated overall if about half the events occur, but the forecasts are not useful because they fail to separate strong possibilities from weak ones. Good forecasters are both calibrated and willing to move away from 50% when the evidence supports doing so. [Coefficient Giving] coefficientgiving.org efforts to improve the accuracy of our judgments and forecasts The improvement in mean Brier scores from probability...Read more... Avoid chasing every surprise People naturally remember spectacular misses and forget routine successes. This creates a temptation to radically change forecasting habits after a single unexpected outcome. A better approach is to review forecasts periodically rather than emotionally. If repeated reviews show that predictions around 90% succeed only 70% of the time, confidence should become more cautious. If predictions around 30% almost never happen, confidence may have become too pessimistic. The objective is gradual improvement, not constant adjustment after every surprise. Keeping score with proper scoring rules Simply counting correct and incorrect predictions ignores confidence. A forecast of 51% and one of 99% both receive the same outcome if the event happens, even though the second represented far greater confidence. Forecasting therefore uses proper scoring rules , which reward honest probability estimates rather than exaggerated certainty. The most widely used is the Brier score , which measures the squared difference between the predicted probability and the eventual outcome. Lower scores indicate better forecasting performance. Importantly, the Brier score encourages reporting your genuine belief rather than inflating confidence to appear decisive. [Wharton Faculty Platform] faculty.wharton.upenn.edu 2015 superforecasters Wharton Faculty Platform2015 superforecasters.pdf Wharton Faculty Platformby B Mellers · 2015 · Cited by 319 — Brier scores are the a... For example: ForecastOutcomeInterpretation90% and the event happensStrong, accurate prediction90% and the event does not happenSevere overconfidence55% and the event happensMild confidence, modest reward50% every timeSafe from extreme errors, but rarely informative Because scoring reflects both correctness and confidence, it reveals whether mistakes come from poor information or from misplaced certainty. 'Superforecasting': The people that predict the future – BBC REEL Channel: BBC Global Open on YouTube What probability forecasting changes in everyday thinking Assigning probabilities changes reasoning before the outcome arrives, not merely afterwards. Instead of asking, \"Do I think this will happen?\", you begin asking: What evidence would justify moving from 60% to 75%? What information would make me lower this estimate? What base rate should anchor my initial judgement? What would surprise me enough to revise my view? These questions encourage continuous updating instead of defending an initial opinion. Studies of high performing forecasters consistently find that they revise probabilities incrementally as new evidence appears rather than waiting until they feel completely certain. Training, regular feedback and repeated scoring all contribute to better calibration over time. [Good Judgment+2Stanford University] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingProven methods. Powerful results. Good Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate for... Common mistakes Several habits reduce the value of probability forecasts: Using only extreme probabilities. Reserving 0% and 100% for events that are logically impossible or certain avoids pretending to know more than you do. Failing to define the event. Vague forecasts cannot be scored fairly. Judging yourself after only a few forecasts. Calibration requires repeated observations. Changing the prediction after the fact. Forecasts should be recorded before outcomes are known."},{"title":"Confident Errors","url":"/confident-errors/","description":"Being confidently wrong can be useful when corrective feedback clearly shows the gap between belief and reality.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Feedback","level":3,"text":"Confident Errors Why Confident Mistakes Can Teach More Confident Errors Confident Errors Why Confident Mistakes Can Teach More Being confidently wrong can be useful when corrective feedback clearly shows the gap between belief and reality. Being confidently wrong can be useful when corrective feedback clearly shows the gap between belief and reality. dataset evidence What makes an error memorable Why correction matters more than embarrassment How to capture high confidence misses improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback confident errors Introduction Being confidently wrong can be one of the most powerful learning experiences—but only if the mistake is followed by clear, accurate correction. Simply making errors does not reliably improve thinking. What matters is the comparison between what you strongly believed and what reality demonstrates instead. This comparison creates a distinctive learning signal that helps people update knowledge, recalibrate confidence, and remember the correct answer more effectively in the future. Research in cognitive psychology has repeatedly found that errors made with high confidence are often corrected more successfully than hesitant mistakes once learners receive immediate, specific feedback . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Learning from Errorsby J Metcalfe · 2017 · Cited by 848 — Experimental investigations indicate that errorful learning followed by c... Within a broader feedback loop for better judgement, high confidence errors deserve special attention because they reveal where our mental models diverge most sharply from reality. Rather than treating these moments as embarrassing failures, they can be viewed as high value opportunities to refine beliefs and improve future decisions. What makes a high confidence error so memorable? The surprising finding from decades of experimental research is that confident mistakes often leave a stronger learning trace than uncertain ones. Researchers refer to this as the hypercorrection effect : after receiving corrective feedback, people are more likely to remember the right answer when their original incorrect response was given with high confidence than when they were only guessing. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Errors committed with high confidence are hypercorrectedby B Butterfield · 2001 · Cited by 334 — This state of affairs implies that... At first glance, this seems counterintuitive. Strongly held beliefs might appear harder to replace than weak ones. Instead, the opposite frequently occurs because the correction violates a confident expectation. The unexpected feedback captures attention and encourages deeper processing. Several mechanisms appear to contribute: Expectation violation. Discovering that a strongly held belief is false creates a large gap between prediction and reality, making the correction more psychologically salient. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Surprising feedback improves later memory Surprising feedback improves later memory PubMed NIHby LK Fazio · 2009 · Cited by 203 — The hypercorrection effect is the findi... Greater attention to corrective information. Experiments suggest that surprising feedback receives more cognitive attention than feedback confirming existing beliefs, improving later recall . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Surprising feedback improves later memory Surprising feedback improves later memory PubMed NIHby LK Fazio · 2009 · Cited by 203 — The hypercorrection effect is the findi... Improved memory encoding. Because the correction is distinctive, learners often remember both the mistake and its replacement more effectively than after low confidence errors. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCSurprising feedback improves later memory NIHby LK Fazio · 2009 · Cited by 203 — The hypercorrection effect is the finding that high confidence errors are more likely to be... The important implication is that confidence itself is not the problem. Unchecked confidence is. Once paired with accurate feedback, confidence can become a marker pointing towards misconceptions worth correcting. Why Mistakes Make You Smarter: The Neuroscience of Learning from Errors Channel: Humberto Bortolossi Open on YouTube Why correction matters more than embarrassment The educational value of confident mistakes comes from corrective information, not from the emotional discomfort of being wrong. Janet Metcalfe's review of research concludes that errorful learning benefits memory when learners receive the correct answer after making the mistake. Without correction, repeated errors simply reinforce inaccurate knowledge. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Learning from Errorsby J Metcalfe · 2017 · Cited by 848 — Experimental investigations indicate that errorful learning followed by c... This distinction matters because many people incorrectly assume that failure automatically produces growth. In reality: An uncorrected mistake teaches very little. Delayed or ambiguous feedback weakens learning. Incorrect feedback can strengthen false beliefs. Immediate, accurate correction tied directly to the mistaken belief produces the strongest learning signal. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Learning from Errorsby J Metcalfe · 2017 · Cited by 848 — Experimental investigations indicate that errorful learning followed by c... Research also challenges the idea that embarrassment is the active ingredient. While discovering an error may produce surprise or discomfort, these emotions do not themselves improve judgement. Learning improves because attention shifts towards information that resolves the conflict between expectation and reality. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net The correction of errors committed with high confidenceThe explanation that is tested here, in two experiments, is that after... This is why productive learning environments encourage admitting uncertainty and examining mistakes rather than hiding them. The goal is to maximise correction, not humiliation. High confidence misses reveal hidden assumptions A confident error usually signals more than an incorrect fact. It often exposes an underlying assumption that has become invisible through familiarity . For example: A manager confidently predicts a product launch will finish on schedule because previous projects did. The delay reveals an unexamined assumption that supplier behaviour has not changed. A medical trainee confidently identifies the wrong diagnosis because a familiar symptom dominates attention. Feedback reveals an overlooked distinguishing feature. A forecaster confidently assigns a 90% probability to an event that fails to occur. The outcome exposes overconfidence in the forecasting model rather than simply one incorrect prediction. In each case, the valuable lesson is not merely \"I was wrong.\" It is \"What reasoning produced this confident belief?\" Corrective feedback therefore works best when it explains the gap between the original reasoning and the correct interpretation, rather than merely providing the right answer. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Learning from Errorsby J Metcalfe · 2017 · Cited by 848 — Experimental investigations indicate that errorful learning followed by c... The Hypercorrection Effect: Why We Learn More From Confident Mistakes Channel: QHat Open on YouTube How to capture high confidence misses People often forget both their original confidence and their reasoning after learning the outcome. Hindsight bias makes incorrect predictions seem less surprising than they actually were. Capturing confident errors before feedback arrives preserves the evidence needed for genuine learning. A practical approach is to record three elements before checking the answer or observing the outcome: 1. The prediction or answer. State it clearly enough that it can later be judged correct or incorrect. 2. Your confidence level. A numerical estimate, such as 80% or 95%, is more informative than saying \"fairly sure.\" 3. The reasoning. Write down the assumptions or evidence supporting the judgement. After receiving feedback, record: [researchgate.net] researchgate.net The correction of errors committed with high confidenceThe explanation that is tested here, in two experiments, is that after... What actually happened. Which assumption failed. Whether confidence should change next time in similar situations. Over time, this creates a personal database of calibration errors rather than a collection of isolated mistakes. Patterns begin to emerge: recurring blind spots, overused heuristics, or situations where confidence consistently exceeds accuracy. Limits and misunderstandings The evidence for learning from confident mistakes should not be interpreted too broadly. [researchgate.net] researchgate.net 308394659 Learning from Errors Learning from Errors Request PDFDrawing on the results of prior experimental studies, Metcalfe (2017) suggested that learners' error ge... First, the hypercorrection effect has been demonstrated most consistently in"},{"title":"Outcome Bias","url":"/outcome-bias/","description":"Good outcomes can reward bad reasoning, while bad outcomes can punish sound decisions that simply ran into bad luck.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Feedback","level":3,"text":"Outcome Bias Was It Skill, Luck or Bad Feedback? Outcome Bias Outcome Bias Was It Skill, Luck or Bad Feedback? Good outcomes can reward bad reasoning, while bad outcomes can punish sound decisions that simply ran into bad luck. Good outcomes can reward bad reasoning, while bad outcomes can punish sound decisions that simply ran into bad luck. mechanism Why results can mislead reviews The four cell process and outcome check How to reward reasoning without ignoring results improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback outcome bias Introduction A good decision can produce a bad result, and a poor decision can produce a good one. This simple fact is one of the biggest obstacles to improving judgement. When people evaluate decisions by their outcomes instead of by the quality of the reasoning available at the time, they fall into outcome bias . The consequence is a distorted feedback loop : luck gets mistaken for skill, while bad luck is mistaken for poor judgement. Over time, this encourages risky habits that happened to succeed and discourages careful thinking that happened to fail. Classic psychological research first demonstrated this effect experimentally, and more recent replication studies show that it remains a robust feature of human judgement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Outcome bias in decision evaluation Outcome bias in decision evaluation PubMed NIHby J Baron · 1988 · Cited by 1449 — In 5 studies, undergraduate subjects were giv... Within a feedback system, the central question is therefore not merely, \"Did this work?\" but \"Given what was known beforehand, was this a sound decision?\" Separating process quality from outcome quality creates feedback that teaches rather than merely rewards or punishes. Why results can mislead reviews Outcomes contain two ingredients: the quality of the decision and the role of chance. Reviews become misleading when they assume the outcome reflects only the first. Suppose two investors analyse identical information and make equally well supported decisions. One benefits from an unexpected market rally, while the other is hit by an unforeseen geopolitical shock. If their reasoning was equally sound, the difference in returns says little about who made the better decision. Yet observers—and often the decision makers themselves—naturally treat the winner as more competent. Jonathan Baron and John Hershey demonstrated this tendency in a series of influential experiments. Participants evaluated medical and financial decisions under uncertainty. Although the information available to the decision maker remained identical, merely changing the eventual outcome caused participants to judge the quality of the original decision differently. Positive outcomes led to higher ratings of competence and reasoning, while negative outcomes led to harsher evaluations—even when participants explicitly recognised that outcomes should not matter. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Outcome bias in decision evaluation Outcome bias in decision evaluation PubMed NIHby J Baron · 1988 · Cited by 1449 — In 5 studies, undergraduate subjects were giv... A large pre registered replication published in 2023 found the same basic effect using modern methods and a substantially larger sample. Participants continued to judge identical decisions differently depending on whether the outcome was favourable or unfavourable, confirming that outcome bias remains a reliable feature of human evaluation. [rips irsp.com] rips irsp.com Outcomes Affect Evaluations of Decision Qualityby S Aiyer · 2023 · Cited by 4 — Research by Baron and Hershey (1988) demonstrated that ou... The practical danger is that noisy environments produce misleading lessons: A reckless strategy that succeeds once appears brilliant. A cautious strategy that fails because of rare bad luck appears foolish. Organisations begin rewarding visible success rather than disciplined reasoning. Individuals become overconfident after lucky wins and excessively cautious after unlucky losses. In environments such as investing, medicine, business strategy or public policy, where uncertainty is unavoidable, this distortion can accumulate into systematic errors. When good decisions have bad outcomes Channel: Cassie Kozyrkov Open on YouTube The four cell process and outcome check A useful review separates decision quality from outcome quality . Rather than treating success and failure as a single scale, it helps to classify decisions into four possibilities. Decision processOutcomeInterpretationGood processGood outcomeDesired result, but success should not automatically validate every detail of the reasoning.Good processBad outcomeOften bad luck. The process may still deserve to be repeated in similar circumstances.Poor processGood outcomeLucky success. This is the most dangerous cell because it encourages repeating flawed reasoning.Poor processBad outcomeBoth the reasoning and the result require correction. The most valuable learning often comes from the two mixed cases. A good process with a bad outcome asks whether the uncertainty was recognised beforehand. If the risk was known and acceptable, the decision may still have been correct. For example, a surgeon choosing the treatment with the highest probability of success cannot guarantee survival in every individual case. A poor process with a good outcome is more deceptive. Someone who ignored evidence, skipped analysis or relied on intuition may still succeed because chance happened to favour them. Without careful review, that success reinforces precisely the habits that should be changed. The four cell approach encourages reviewers to ask two separate questions: 1. Was the reasoning appropriate given the information available at the time? 2. How much of the outcome was influenced by uncertainty beyond the decision maker's control? Only after answering both questions should performance be evaluated. How outcome bias weakens learning Outcome bias damages learning because it corrupts the feedback signal that experience provides. Instead of identifying which thinking patterns consistently produce better decisions, people begin associating emotional reactions with isolated outcomes. A painful failure creates the illusion that the underlying reasoning was flawed. An exciting success creates the illusion that every judgement leading to it was insightful. This produces several recurring problems: False confidence. Lucky successes increase confidence without increasing skill. False self doubt. Sound reasoning abandoned after an unlucky outcome may never demonstrate its long term value. Inconsistent standards. Identical decisions receive praise or criticism depending solely on events outside the decision maker's control. Poor experimentation. Teams become reluctant to test sensible innovations if any unsuccessful trial is treated as evidence of incompetence. The distortion is particularly severe in low feedback environments where important decisions are rare and outcomes unfold slowly. A single visible success or failure may dominate memory despite containing little information about the underlying quality of judgement. 5 Tools for Better Decisions The Decision Dividend 34 Channel: Greenspring Advisors Open on YouTube How to reward reasoning without ignoring results Avoiding outcome bias does not mean ignoring results. Outcomes remain essential because they reveal whether assumptions matched reality. The challenge is to use outcomes as evidence rather than as the sole measure of decision quality. Several practices help preserve this distinction. Record reasoning before acting. Decision journals capture assumptions, forecasts, confidence levels and expected risks before hindsight can reshape memory. Later reviews compare the original reasoning with what actually happened rather than relying on reconstructed recollections. Judge decisions using information available at the time. Reviewers should deliberately ignore facts that emerged only after the decision. This recreates the original uncertainty and produces a fairer assessment. Look for repeated patterns rather than isolated wins or losses. A single outcome is often dominated by randomness. Consistent performance across many decisions provides much stronger evidence of skill. Reward disciplined processes as well as successful outcomes. Organisations that recognise careful analysis, explicit assumptions and thoughtful risk management reduce the temptation to chase lucky victories. Separate accountability from luck. People remain responsible for the quality of their reasoning, preparation and risk management, but not for every random event that occurs afterwards. These practices align incentives with long term judgement rather than short term fortune. The balance between process and results Focusing on process alone would also be a mistake. A beautifully reasoned decision that repeatedly produces poor outcomes eventually signals that important assumptions are"},{"title":"Weak Feedback","url":"/weak-feedback/","description":"In noisy or delayed settings, repeated experience can build confidence without producing reliable learning.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Feedback","level":3,"text":"Weak Feedback When Experience Makes Judgement Worse Weak Feedback Weak Feedback When Experience Makes Judgement Worse In noisy or delayed settings, repeated experience can build confidence without producing reliable learning. In noisy or delayed settings, repeated experience can build confidence without producing reliable learning. population context Why some domains do not teach cleanly Valid cues, fast feedback and intuition Designing safeguards for noisy decisions improving your thinking and analytical skills feedback weak feedback Introduction Experience does not automatically produce better judgement. In some domains, repeating the same task for years can increase confidence while leaving accuracy unchanged—or even making it worse. The reason is not a lack of intelligence or effort but the quality of the feedback the environment provides. When outcomes are delayed, noisy, selective or misleading, people struggle to tell whether a decision was genuinely good or merely fortunate. Over time, lucky successes can reinforce poor habits, while sound decisions that happen to end badly may be abandoned. This distinction is central to improving thinking and analytical skills. Research on judgement, expertise and forecasting shows that intuition becomes reliable only when people repeatedly encounter valid cues and receive prompt, accurate feedback about whether those cues predicted reality. Where those conditions are absent, experience must be supplemented with deliberate safeguards rather than trusted on its own. [AdaptLX] preview prod production.gurooproducer.com AdaptLXThe Two Settings of Kind and Wicked Learning EnvironmentsAugust 13, 2021 — by RM Hogarth · 2015 · Cited by 255 — Hogarth (2001) us... Published: August 13, 2021 Why Some Domains Do Not Teach Cleanly Psychologist Robin Hogarth described the contrast between kind and wicked learning environments. A kind environment has stable rules, recurring patterns and clear feedback. Chess is a classic example: every move produces visible consequences, mistakes can be analysed immediately and similar situations recur frequently. A wicked environment has the opposite characteristics. Rules may change, many factors interact simultaneously, and feedback is delayed, incomplete or distorted. Financial markets, long term business strategy, public policy, hiring decisions and geopolitical forecasting often fall into this category. Because cause and effect are difficult to separate, people receive little trustworthy information about which aspects of their judgement actually deserve credit or blame. [AdaptLX] preview prod production.gurooproducer.com AdaptLXThe Two Settings of Kind and Wicked Learning EnvironmentsAugust 13, 2021 — by RM Hogarth · 2015 · Cited by 255 — Hogarth (2001) us... Published: August 13, 2021 The problem is not simply uncertainty. Many uncertain environments still teach effectively if outcomes are observed quickly and consistently. The deeper issue is that the feedback itself is unreliable. A poor decision may succeed because of luck, while a well reasoned decision may fail because of external events beyond anyone's control. How Experience Can Become Misleading Weak feedback environments create several systematic traps. Luck masquerades as skill. When outcomes depend heavily on chance, repeated success can convince people they possess expertise they have not actually demonstrated. Conversely, repeated bad luck can discourage effective methods before they have had enough opportunities to prove themselves. Delayed consequences weaken learning. If months or years separate judgement from outcome, it becomes difficult to remember the original assumptions accurately. People naturally reconstruct their reasoning in ways that fit what eventually happened, making genuine learning much harder. Selective feedback hides mistakes. Many professionals never discover the decisions that turned out poorly. A physician may never learn about a patient who sought treatment elsewhere. A recruiter rarely sees how rejected applicants would have performed. Investors only observe the investments they actually made, not the alternatives they ignored. Changing environments invalidate experience. Experience acquired under one set of conditions may become misleading when technology, incentives or competitors change. Past success then reflects historical circumstances rather than enduring judgement. Hogarth and colleagues argue that many judgement failures arise because the environment used for learning differs systematically from the environment in which decisions are later applied. Experience is not merely incomplete; it can become actively biased. [AdaptLX] preview prod production.gurooproducer.com AdaptLXThe Two Settings of Kind and Wicked Learning EnvironmentsAugust 13, 2021 — by RM Hogarth · 2015 · Cited by 255 — Hogarth (2001) us... Published: August 13, 2021 Kind vs. Wicked The Myth of Experience 1 Channel: Emre Soyer Open on YouTube Valid Cues, Fast Feedback and Intuition Not all intuition deserves equal trust. Daniel Kahneman and Gary Klein's influential collaboration helped reconcile two traditions in psychology by identifying the conditions under which intuitive expertise develops. Reliable intuition requires two ingredients: Valid environmental cues , where observable patterns genuinely predict future outcomes. Frequent, accurate feedback , allowing repeated correction of errors. Without both conditions, intuition has little opportunity to calibrate itself. A firefighter repeatedly exposed to recognisable physical patterns can often develop highly accurate instinctive judgement. A long term macroeconomic forecaster receives far fewer opportunities to connect specific judgements with unambiguous outcomes. This explains why experts may display extraordinary performance in one domain while showing little advantage over informed novices in another. Years of experience alone do not guarantee expertise if the environment never provides the information needed to improve. [Range Widely] davidepstein.substack.com Range Widely\"Kind\" and \"Wicked\" Learning EnvironmentsIn what Hogarth called “kind” learning environments, experience led to predictable i... Historical Examples of Misleading Experience One striking illustration comes from medicine. Robin Hogarth recounts a physician renowned for diagnosing typhoid fever at an unusually early stage by examining patients' tongues manually. His apparent success reinforced confidence in his diagnostic technique. In reality, he had unknowingly become an efficient carrier of the disease himself, infecting patients during examinations. Positive feedback consistently reinforced the wrong causal explanation. [AdaptLX] preview prod production.gurooproducer.com AdaptLXThe Two Settings of Kind and Wicked Learning EnvironmentsAugust 13, 2021 — by RM Hogarth · 2015 · Cited by 255 — Hogarth (2001) us... Published: August 13, 2021 Avalanche safety provides another example. Skiers can repeatedly make hazardous route choices without triggering an avalanche. Every safe outing appears to confirm the quality of their judgement, even though survival may largely reflect favourable conditions rather than sound decision making. The first unmistakable feedback may arrive only when an avalanche finally occurs—far too late to support gradual learning. Avalanche educators increasingly describe this as a textbook wicked learning environment. [avalanchejournal.ca] avalanchejournal.ca Wicked Learning Avalanche Journalby B Roach — This concept is that in avalanche terrain, we often lack the correct feedback to make eff... Financial markets exhibit similar dynamics. Investors often experience extended winning streaks during favourable market conditions, encouraging excessive confidence. Later losses may reflect changing conditions rather than a sudden decline in skill, making it difficult to distinguish robust strategies from temporary good fortune. [ajinkyabhat.com] ajinkyabhat.com Kind and Wicked Learning Environments Typically, these are also easier for...Read more... Why Confidence Often Increases Faster Than Accuracy Weak feedback environments produce an uncomfortable asymmetry: confidence accumulates more easily than knowledge. Every successful outcome feels like evidence supporting existing beliefs. Failed predictions, however, can often be explained away as exceptional circumstances, unusual bad luck or unforeseeable events. Because many decisions lack clear counterfactuals—what would have happened had another option been chosen—people rarely observe decisive evidence against their preferred explanations. This asymmetry helps explain why experienced professionals can sincerely disagree for decades while each believes experience has validated their judgement. Both individuals have accumulated stories consistent with their beliefs, but neither has received sufficiently diagnostic feedback to identify which interpretation is more accurate. The Myth of Experience: Kind and"},{"title":"First Answers","url":"/first-answers/","description":"The Cognitive Reflection Test shows how tempting first answers can feel right before they have been checked.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"First Answers Why First Answers Feel So Convincing First Answers First Answers Why First Answers Feel So Convincing The Cognitive Reflection Test shows how tempting first answers can feel right before they have been checked. The Cognitive Reflection Test shows how tempting first answers can feel right before they have been checked. dataset evidence What reflection tests reveal Why simple puzzles can mislead How to check appealing answers improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers Introduction The Cognitive Reflection Test is famous because its questions make a wrong answer feel almost insultingly obvious. In the classic “ bat and ball ” puzzle, many people first say the ball costs 10 cents; checking the arithmetic shows it must cost 5 cents. That small mistake captures a larger thinking problem: some answers feel convincing not because they have been tested, but because they arrived quickly, matched the surface pattern, and reduced mental effort. For improving analytical skills, the value of reflection tasks is not that they prove who is “smart”. It is that they create a clean, memorable demonstration of a first answer trap. They show how a fluent answer can outrun verification, why simple looking puzzles can mislead, and how a brief checking routine can catch errors before they harden into confidence. What reflection tests reveal Shane Frederick introduced the original three item Cognitive Reflection Test in 2005 as a short way to measure a person’s tendency to resist reporting the first answer that comes to mind. The questions were deliberately simple in wording but designed to cue an intuitive wrong response before the correct one was reached through a small amount of reflection. [bear.warrington.ufl.edu] bear.warrington.ufl.edu The bat costs $1.00 more than the ball. How much does the ball cost?Read more Cognitive Reflection and Decision Makingby S Frederick · 2005 · Cited by 8140 — By contrast, consider this problem: A bat and a ball cost... The three classic items use everyday arithmetic rather than specialist knowledge. A bat and ball problem tempts “10 cents”; a machine and widget problem tempts “100 minutes”; a lily pad growth problem tempts “24 days”. The correct answers are 5 cents, 5 minutes and 47 days. The trap is not complex mathematics. It is the mismatch between how easy the question feels and how carefully it needs to be parsed. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org 2005). The CRT was designed to assess a specific cognitive ability. A bat and a ball cost $1.10 in total. The bat costs $1.00 more than... That is why the test became influential in judgement and decision making research. It offers a compact example of “miserly processing”: accepting an answer that is available and plausible enough, rather than spending extra effort to check it. Later work found that CRT performance can predict performance on a range of heuristics and biases tasks, even when researchers account for other individual difference measures such as cognitive ability and thinking dispositions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Cognitive Reflection Test as a predictor of...by ME Toplak · 2011 · Cited by 1590 — In this study, we showed that the CRT is a... The practical lesson is narrower and more useful than “always distrust your intuition ”. First answers are often useful in familiar, low stakes situations. The CRT matters because it isolates a particular failure mode: when a problem has been built, or happens naturally, so that the surface reading triggers a seductive but wrong answer. Why simple puzzles can mislead The bat and ball puzzle works because it lets the mind replace the real question with an easier one. The phrase “£1.00 more” or “$1.00 more” invites a rough split of the total into a large part and a small part. “10 cents” feels compatible with the total of 1.10, so it slips through unless the person checks whether the bat is exactly one unit more than the ball. That checking step is tiny but decisive. If the ball cost 10 cents and the bat cost one unit more, the bat would cost 1.10, making the total 1.20. The intuitive answer violates the condition in the question. The mistake survives because the first answer satisfies a looser impression of the problem, not the actual constraint. This is the central feature of first answer traps: they do not always feel like guesses. They often feel like recognition. A person sees a familiar structure, retrieves a fitting response, and experiences fluency as evidence of correctness. Reflection tests expose the gap between “this came easily” and “this has been checked”. The same pattern appears beyond puzzle books. A headline may suggest one cause before the data support it. A project failure may seem to have an obvious culprit before alternative explanations are compared. A price, risk or probability may feel reasonable until the base numbers are written down. The CRT is useful because it gives a small, controlled model of that wider analytical hazard. Cognitive Reflection Test Channel: myeconguy Open on YouTube The evidence is useful, but not simple The CRT is often described as a measure of overriding intuition with reflection, but the research record is more complicated. Some studies suggest that CRT scores are not a pure measure of “reflection” alone, because the items also depend on numeracy and calculation skill. Sinayev and Peters argued that CRT performance can be strongly shaped by numerical ability, not only by the willingness to suppress an intuitive answer. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Frontiers Cognitive reflection vs calculation in decision makingMay 7, 2015 — by A Sinayev · 2015 · Cited by 274 — We focused on testing whether the Cognitive Reflection H... Published: May 7, 2015 Other researchers have examined how people actually solve CRT items rather than treating each answer as a simple window into either intuition or reflection. Szászi and colleagues used protocol analysis to explore solution strategies and found that CRT performance involves multiple abilities and routes, not a single mental switch from fast to slow thinking. [PhilPapers] philpapers.org Phil Papers B. Aczel Phil Papers B. Aczel There is also evidence that the “slow reasoning corrects fast intuition” story can be too neat. Bago and De Neys argued, using work on the bat and ball problem, that correct responses may sometimes have an intuitive component too. In other words, better thinkers are not always people who first think wrongly and then repair the error; in some cases, they may sense the conflict or even the correct direction earlier than the standard story suggests. [Wim De Neys] wdeneys.org Open source on wdeneys.org. The fairest interpretation is that the CRT is a strong demonstration tool and a useful research measure, but not a complete diagnostic of thinking quality. It captures a family of abilities: noticing conflict, resisting a fluent answer, parsing the wording, doing the calculation, and caring enough to verify. Those are exactly the ingredients that matter in first answer traps. Why the first answer feels so convincing First answers gain force from several overlapping cues. None of them guarantees truth, but all of them can feel like truth. Fluency feels like accuracy. An answer that comes quickly is easier to trust. The mind often treats ease of retrieval as a sign that a response is familiar, coherent or likely. Surface fit hides constraint failure. In the bat and ball problem, “10 cents” fits the rough shape of the total, but not the exact “one unit more” relationship. The answer is locally plausible and globally wrong. Stopping early saves effort. Reflection costs attention. If the problem appears easy, the mind has little motivation to spend more energy on it. Confidence can arrive before verification. People do not always wait to check an answer before feeling sure. The feeling of having solved the problem can precede the work needed to prove it. The trap is often invisible from inside. A person who gives the intuitive answer may not experience themselves as careless. They may experience the question as straightforward. That is what makes first answer traps dangerous in real decisions: the error does not necessarily announce itself as an error. How to Solve the \"If a Bat and Ball Cost $1.10 in Total...\" Cognitive Reflection Test or Question Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube Familiarity creates a second trap The original CRT has become widely known. That creates a measurement problem: people who have seen the items before may remember the answers rather than reflect in the moment. Stieger and Reips identified familiarity as a limitation for the CRT, because prior exposure can raise scores and complicate interpretation. [peerj.com] peerj.com Open source on peerj.com. The story is not one sided. Białek and Pennycook found that the CRT can remain robust to multiple exposures in some respects, with correlations to other variables persisting"},{"title":"Backwards Test","url":"/backwards-test/","description":"Putting a first answer back into the original problem is a fast way to catch answers that feel right but violate a condition.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / First Answers","level":3,"text":"Backwards Test A Quick Test for Answers That Feel Obvious Backwards Test Backwards Test A Quick Test for Answers That Feel Obvious Putting a first answer back into the original problem is a fast way to catch answers that feel right but violate a condition. Putting a first answer back into the original problem is a fast way to catch answers that feel right but violate a condition. policy intervention Restating the exact condition Substituting the answer into the problem Using the check in decisions beyond puzzles improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers backwards test Introduction A first answer can feel convincing for a simple reason: it reduces uncertainty quickly. In many cognitive reflection problems, the mistake is not that people fail to think at all, but that they stop thinking once an answer feels plausible. A fast safeguard is to work backwards from the answer you currently believe. Instead of asking, “How did I get here?”, ask, “If this answer were true, would it satisfy every condition in the original problem?” This backwards test is one of the simplest ways to catch first answer traps . It turns confidence into a hypothesis that must survive inspection. In the classic Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT), many wrong answers collapse immediately when substituted back into the problem statement. The value of the technique is not limited to puzzles. The same habit can expose flaws in estimates, explanations, forecasts and everyday decisions. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org The bat costs $1.00 more than the ball. How much does the ball cost? · If it takes 5 machines 5 minutes to make 5 widgets...Read more... Restating the Exact Condition Many first answer errors arise because people unconsciously solve a simplified version of the problem rather than the problem that was actually asked. Consider the well known bat and ball question. A common response is that the ball costs 10 cents. That answer feels natural because the total cost is $1.10 and the difference is $1.00. However, the original condition is not simply that the two prices add to $1.10. The bat must cost exactly $1.00 more than the ball. When that condition is examined carefully, the intuitive answer no longer fits. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org The bat costs $1.00 more than the ball. How much does the ball cost? · If it takes 5 machines 5 minutes to make 5 widgets...Read more... The first step in backwards testing is therefore to restate the requirement in precise terms. Before checking an answer, identify: What must be true if the answer is correct? Which constraints are non negotiable? What exact relationship does the problem describe? This matters because intuitive answers often satisfy part of the problem while violating another part. Research on cognitive reflection suggests that successful performance often depends on recognising that an initial response conflicts with the stated conditions and then reconsidering it. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Society for Judgment and Decision MakingInvestigating an alternate form of the cognitive reflection testby KS Thomson · 2016 · Cited by 9... Substituting the Answer into the Problem Once the conditions are clear, the next step is straightforward: insert the proposed answer back into the original problem. Using the bat and ball example, suppose the ball costs 10 cents. If the bat costs $1.00 more, then the bat costs $1.10. Together they cost $1.20, not $1.10. The answer fails the test immediately. The check takes only a few seconds, yet it exposes the flaw that confidence concealed. [Embrace Autism] embrace autism.com cognitive reflection test Embrace AutismCognitive Reflection Test21 Jul 2020 — Cognitive Reflection Test · A bat and a ball cost $1.10 in total. The bat costs $1.0... This form of verification is standard practice in mathematics. A proposed solution is often confirmed by substituting it back into the original equation or statement to see whether all conditions remain true. Verification is distinct from solving: solving generates an answer, while verification tests whether the answer genuinely works. [Brilliant] brilliant.org Verifying Solutions Brilliant Math & Science WikiVerifying solutions is a good way to double check the work on any problem. So... A useful routine is: Why Can't People Solve These Simple Problems? Channel: Benjamin Keep, PhD, JD Open on YouTube 1. Write down the first answer. 2. Assume it is correct. 3. Reconstruct the situation using that answer. 4. Check every stated condition. 5. Reject or revise the answer if any condition fails. The routine is deliberately mechanical. Its strength comes from reducing the influence of confidence and replacing it with a direct test. Why the Method Works The backwards test changes the mental task. Finding an answer requires generating possibilities. Checking an answer requires evaluating consequences. These are different cognitive activities. An answer that seemed persuasive during generation may appear obviously wrong when examined through its implications. Studies of cognitive reflection repeatedly show that many reasoning failures stem from accepting the first response that comes to mind without sufficient evaluation. The backwards test introduces a brief evaluation stage before commitment. [ResearchGate+2All About Psychology] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The cognitive reflection test revisited: exploring the...March 1, 2017 — 1 Mar 2017 — Individuals' propensity not to o... Published: March 1, 2017 Importantly, the check does not require discovering a better answer immediately. It only requires detecting that the current answer does not satisfy the problem. That makes it a low cost intervention with a potentially large payoff. Using the Check in Decisions Beyond Puzzles The same logic applies outside formal reasoning tasks. Suppose a manager concludes that a project delay was caused by one employee's mistake. A backwards test asks: if that explanation were completely correct, what evidence should exist? Would the timeline, resource allocation and sequence of events fit that account? If not, confidence should decrease. In forecasting, a person might predict that sales will grow by 20%. Working backwards means asking what conditions would have to hold for that outcome to occur. Are those conditions realistic? Do they conflict with current constraints? The answer becomes a testable proposition rather than an intuition . In everyday judgement, the habit can be expressed as a simple question: “If my conclusion is right, what else must be true?” That question often reveals missing assumptions , hidden contradictions or overlooked requirements. Working backward to solve problems Maurice Ashley Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Common Mistakes When Performing a Backwards Test The technique is simple, but it can fail if applied carelessly. Checking only the most obvious condition. People sometimes verify the part of the problem that first attracted attention while ignoring less prominent constraints. Effective checking requires examining all stated requirements. Testing a modified version of the answer. A common mistake is unconsciously adjusting the answer during verification. The purpose is to test the original answer exactly as it was first proposed. Treating plausibility as verification. An answer may sound reasonable without satisfying the problem. Verification requires evidence that the conditions hold, not merely a feeling that the answer makes sense. Stopping after one successful check. Complex decisions often involve multiple constraints. Passing one test does not guarantee correctness if other requirements remain unexamined. Building the Habit Backwards testing is valuable because it is small enough to use routinely. It does not require advanced mathematics, formal logic or extensive analysis. It requires only a pause between answering and believing. When a problem seems unusually easy, when an answer appears instantly, or when confidence arrives before verification, those are signals to run the check. Restate the exact condition, substitute the answer back into the problem, and see whether reality matches the claim. In the context of cognitive reflection and first answer traps, that brief routine acts as a barrier against misplaced certainty. A fluent answer may still be correct, but it must earn that status by surviving the test. PMC+2Society for Judgment and Decision Making [pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCCognitive reflection vs calculation in decision making PMCby A Sinayev · 2015 · Cited by 269 — For example, the bat and ball problem brings to mind an intuitiv... Most People Get This Wrong – What About You? Channel: Math Queen Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to A Quick Test for Answers That Feel Obvious. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Thinking,"},{"title":"Bat and Ball","url":"/bat-and-ball/","description":"The famous bat-and-ball puzzle shows why an answer can fit the total while failing the exact condition that makes it correct.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / First Answers","level":3,"text":"Bat and Ball Why the 10 Cent Answer Feels Right Bat and Ball Bat and Ball Why the 10 Cent Answer Feels Right The famous bat and ball puzzle shows why an answer can fit the total while failing the exact condition that makes it correct. The famous bat and ball puzzle shows why an answer can fit the total while failing the exact condition that makes it correct. mechanism The intuitive answer and the hidden constraint Testing the first answer backwards What the puzzle teaches beyond arithmetic improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers bat and ball Introduction The bat and ball puzzle is famous because the wrong answer appears to satisfy the question. A bat and a ball cost £1.10 in total, and the bat costs £1 more than the ball. Many people immediately answer “10 pence”. The puzzle’s power lies in showing that an answer can fit part of a problem while violating the exact condition that makes it correct. The lesson is not mainly about arithmetic. It is about verification. Good analytical thinking often depends less on producing an answer quickly and more on checking whether that answer satisfies every constraint in the problem. [Yale Law School] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative issues of whether a preference can be \"wrong\" and whether... Why the 10 Cent Answer Feels Right The intuitive answer and the hidden constraint When people hear the problem, they often split the £1.10 total into a large amount and a small amount. The pair “£1 and 10 pence” feels natural and arrives almost instantly. That rapid response is exactly what Shane Frederick designed the question to provoke when creating the Cognitive Reflection Test. Yale Law School+2Warrington College of Business [law.yale.edu] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative issues of whether a preference can be \"wrong\" and whether... The mistake is subtle. The question does not ask for two prices that add to £1.10. It asks for two prices that add to £1.10 and differ by exactly £1. The intuitive answer focuses on the total while neglecting the difference requirement. If the ball cost 10 pence, then the bat would have to cost £1.10 to be £1 more expensive. Together they would cost £1.20, not £1.10. The answer appears plausible because it partially matches the information given, yet it fails the decisive constraint. [Warrington College of Business] bear.warrington.ufl.edu frederick jep2005 The bat costs $1.00 more than the ball. How much does the ball cost? cents. Here, an intuitive...Read more... This is why the puzzle remains influential in judgement and decision making research. The error is not random. People systematically produce a response that feels right before they have checked whether it actually works. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Bat and Ball Problem: Stronger evidence in support of a...by JD Hoover · 2019 · Cited by 25 — The bat and ball problem, one of th... Daniel Kahneman: The Bat and Ball Puzzle Channel: DenkProducties Open on YouTube Testing the First Answer Backwards The fastest way to solve the puzzle is not to search for a clever method but to test the first answer against the conditions. Start with the intuitive response: Assume the ball costs 10 pence. [en.wiktionary.org] en.wiktionary.org noun · A male given name from the Germanic languages. · A surname. · A town in Weld County, Colorado. · A tiny city in Rice County, Kansa... The bat must then cost £1.10 because it is £1 more expensive. Total cost becomes £1.20. The assumption fails. Once the first answer is rejected, the correct solution becomes straightforward. Let the ball cost 5 pence: [en.wiktionary.org] en.wiktionary.org noun · A male given name from the Germanic languages. · A surname. · A town in Weld County, Colorado. · A tiny city in Rice County, Kansa... The bat costs £1.05. [allaboutpsychology.substack.com] allaboutpsychology.substack.com The Cognitive Reflection Test This simple but ingenious puzzle was devised by Shane Frederick as part of a 3 item Cognitive... The difference is exactly £1. The total is exactly £1.10. Every condition is satisfied. [allaboutpsychology.substack.com] allaboutpsychology.substack.com The Cognitive Reflection Test This simple but ingenious puzzle was devised by Shane Frederick as part of a 3 item Cognitive... What makes this checking process important is that it reverses the usual order of thinking. Instead of asking, “What answer comes to mind?”, it asks, “Does this answer survive contact with the constraints?” The puzzle demonstrates that verification can take only a few seconds yet completely change the result. Why the check is often skipped Research on the Cognitive Reflection Test suggests that many people experience the intuitive answer as sufficiently convincing and therefore never perform the verification step. The answer arrives with a feeling of fluency and completeness. Reflection begins only if something triggers doubt. [Yale Law School+2Queen's University Belfast] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative issues of whether a preference can be \"wrong\" and whether... The bat and ball problem is effective because the missing check is tiny. No advanced mathematics is required. The difference between failure and success is simply whether the proposed answer is tested against the exact wording of the question. What the Puzzle Teaches Beyond Arithmetic The broader lesson is that analytical errors often arise from constraint neglect rather than lack of knowledge. In many situations, people unconsciously replace a difficult question with an easier one: “Does this explanation fit the evidence?” becomes “Does this explanation sound plausible?” “Does this forecast fit all the data?” becomes “Does this forecast fit the trend I noticed?” “Does this plan satisfy every requirement?” becomes “Does this plan look reasonable?” The bat and ball puzzle provides a miniature version of that process. The intuitive answer satisfies a simplified version of the problem but not the actual problem. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Bat and Ball Problem: Stronger evidence in support of a...by JD Hoover · 2019 · Cited by 25 — The bat and ball problem, one of th... This is one reason the puzzle became the signature example in the Cognitive Reflection Test. Researchers have found that performance on such questions relates to a person's tendency to resist reporting the first answer that comes to mind and instead engage in additional checking and reflection. [Yale Law School+2OSF] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative issues of whether a preference can be \"wrong\" and whether... How to Solve the \"If a Bat and Ball Cost $1.10 in Total...\" Cognitive Reflection Test or Question Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube A Simple Habit for Better Thinking The most useful takeaway is practical: whenever a solution arrives unusually quickly, identify the exact constraints and test the answer against each one. For the bat and ball puzzle, the crucial question is not “Does 10 pence seem right?” but “If the ball costs 10 pence, does every condition still hold?” That small shift—from generating answers to checking constraints—is the real skill the puzzle teaches. The arithmetic is trivial. The discipline of verification is not. When analytical mistakes occur, they are often less about calculation errors than about accepting an answer before confirming that it truly satisfies the problem being solved. [Warrington College of Business+2PMC] bear.warrington.ufl.edu frederick jep2005 The bat costs $1.00 more than the ball. How much does the ball cost? cents. Here, an intuitive...Read more... Why Can't People Solve These Simple Problems? Channel: Benjamin Keep, PhD, JD Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why the 10 Cent Answer Feels Right. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman The bat and ball puzzle is one of the most famous examples of the fast thinking trap discussed in this field. See on Amazon Book The Art of Thinking Clearly By Rolf Dobelli Demonstrates how plausible answers can survive because people fail to test hidden assumptions. See on Amazon Book Predictably Irrational By Dan Ariely Explains recurring judgment errors that arise from intuitive rather than analytical processing. See on Amazon Book Superforecasting By Philip Tetlock, Dan Gardner Emphasizes checking assumptions and testing answers against evidence and constraints. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Thinking, Fast and Slow The Art of Thinking Clearly Predictably Irrational As an"},{"title":"Numeracy Limits","url":"/numeracy-limits/","description":"CRT scores can reflect calculation skill as well as reflective thinking, so a low score is not a clean diagnosis of poor reasoning.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / First Answers","level":3,"text":"Numeracy Limits Does the CRT Measure Reflection or Math? Numeracy Limits Numeracy Limits Does the CRT Measure Reflection or Math? CRT scores can reflect calculation skill as well as reflective thinking, so a low score is not a clean diagnosis of poor reasoning. CRT scores can reflect calculation skill as well as reflective thinking, so a low score is not a clean diagnosis of poor reasoning. dataset evidence Why arithmetic skill affects CRT performance What numeracy changes about score interpretation Fairer ways to read a wrong answer improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers numeracy limits Introduction The Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT) is often presented as a measure of whether people can resist a tempting first answer and stop to think. That interpretation captures part of what the test does, but it is incomplete. The classic CRT questions also require basic numerical reasoning, arithmetic fluency and an understanding of quantitative relationships. As a result, a low score does not automatically mean that someone failed to reflect. In some cases, the person may have recognised that the intuitive answer was suspicious yet still lacked the numerical skills needed to reach the correct solution. Research over the past decade has increasingly treated CRT performance as a mixture of reflection and numeracy rather than a pure measure of reflective thinking alone. [Taylor & Francis Online+2ResearchGate] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe cognitive reflection test revisited: exploring the ways...by B Szaszi · 2017 · Cited by 119 — Additionally, w... Does the CRT Measure Reflection or Math? Shane Frederick's original CRT was designed to measure the tendency to override an intuitive but incorrect response and engage in further reflection. The famous bat and ball problem succeeds because it generates an answer that feels right before arithmetic verification occurs. [American Economic Association] aeaweb.org American Economic AssociationCognitive Reflection and Decision Makingby S Frederick · 2005 · Cited by 8284 — This paper introduces a thre... The difficulty is that solving the problem correctly requires more than noticing the trap. A participant must also perform the relevant calculation. This creates an important measurement problem: the test cannot easily distinguish between someone who failed to reflect and someone who reflected but lacked the necessary numerical competence. Researchers have repeatedly found substantial relationships between CRT scores and measures of numeracy, mathematical ability and quantitative reasoning. Some studies have argued that a significant portion of the CRT's predictive power may come from its numerical demands rather than from reflection alone. [ResearchGate+2eScholarship] researchgate.net 260061139 The Cognitive Reflection Test how much more than numerical ability ResearchGate(PDF) The Cognitive Reflection Test: how much more than...Frederick's (2005) Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT) is a 3 item tas... This does not mean the CRT is \"really just a maths test\". Rather, the evidence suggests that it blends at least two ingredients: The tendency to question an intuitive response. The ability to execute the reasoning needed to replace it. A person needs both to achieve a high score. [Taylor & Francis Online+2MOspace] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe cognitive reflection test revisited: exploring the ways...by B Szaszi · 2017 · Cited by 119 — Additionally, w... Why Arithmetic Skill Affects CRT Performance The original CRT items appear simple, but each depends on quantitative concepts that not everyone handles equally well. Consider the bat and ball problem. Someone may recognise that 10 cents cannot be correct because the numbers do not quite fit. Yet moving from that insight to the correct 5 cent answer requires forming and solving a simple numerical relationship. Similarly, the machine and widget problem depends on understanding rates, while the lily pad problem requires reasoning about exponential growth rather than linear growth. Researchers examining response patterns have found that errors do not always arise because participants blindly accept the intuitive answer. Some respondents reject the intuitive answer but still fail to reach the correct one. This suggests that inadequate numerical knowledge can be a separate source of failure. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe cognitive reflection test revisited: exploring the ways...by B Szaszi · 2017 · Cited by 119 — Additionally, w... A useful way to think about CRT performance is as a sequence: How to Solve \"A man buys a pig for $60, sells it for $70, buys it back..\" Cognitive Reflection Test Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube 1. Notice that the immediate answer may be wrong. 2. Suppress the tempting response. 3. Apply the required reasoning or calculation. 4. Produce the correct answer. Failure can occur at any stage. The final score alone does not reveal which stage caused the mistake. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com According to the model of Individual Differences in Heuristics and Biases (Stanovich, K. E. (2018). Miserliness in human cognition: The... What Numeracy Changes About Score Interpretation The strongest critique of simplistic CRT interpretation is not that reflection is irrelevant. It is that reflection cannot be cleanly separated from numerical competence when using the classic items. Several studies have concluded that CRT performance is best viewed as a multifaceted construct. Reflection matters, but so do numeracy and problem solving ability. Research revisiting CRT performance has found evidence that both reflective tendencies and numerical skill independently contribute to scores. [Taylor & Francis Online+2PMC] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe cognitive reflection test revisited: exploring the ways...by B Szaszi · 2017 · Cited by 119 — Additionally, w... This changes how a low score should be interpreted. A low score may indicate: A tendency to accept the first plausible answer. Weak arithmetic fluency. Difficulty with quantitative concepts such as ratios or growth rates. Some combination of all three. Likewise, a high score does not necessarily prove exceptional reflective habits. Strong numerical skills can make the correct answer easier to reach once reflection begins. [ResearchGate+2Edith Cowan University] researchgate.net 260061139 The Cognitive Reflection Test how much more than numerical ability ResearchGate(PDF) The Cognitive Reflection Test: how much more than...Frederick's (2005) Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT) is a 3 item tas... The practical lesson for improving analytical thinking is important. If someone performs poorly on CRT items, the next question should not be \"Are they reflective?\" but \"What kind of difficulty produced the error?\" The answer may point to reasoning habits, mathematical knowledge, or both. Fairer Ways to Read a Wrong Answer Looking only at whether an answer is correct throws away useful information. Researchers studying CRT response patterns have noted that some participants generate neither the standard intuitive answer nor the correct answer. Others appear to reject the intuitive response but become stuck before reaching the solution. These patterns suggest that reflection and numeracy contribute in different ways. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe cognitive reflection test revisited: exploring the ways...by B Szaszi · 2017 · Cited by 119 — Additionally, w... A fairer interpretation of a wrong answer asks three questions: Did the person immediately choose the intuitive answer? If so, the error may reflect insufficient checking or reflection. Did the person recognise the trap but fail to solve it? That pattern points more towards a numeracy limitation than a reflection limitation. Could the person explain why the intuitive answer was wrong? Someone who can identify the flaw in the intuitive response may be demonstrating reflective thinking even if they cannot complete the calculation. These distinctions matter because the educational response differs. Reflective weaknesses improve through habits of verification and deliberate checking. Numeracy weaknesses improve through practice with quantitative reasoning and mathematical concepts. How to Solve \"If It Takes 5 Machines 5 Minutes to Make 5 Widgets..\" Cognitive Reflection Test Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube What the Evidence Means for Analytical Skill Development The broader research record still supports the CRT as a valuable indicator of thinking style. Higher CRT scores predict performance on many judgement and decision making tasks, and the test remains one of the most influential tools in the study of reasoning. [PubMed+2Springer] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Cognitive Reflection Test as a predictor of...by ME Toplak · 2011 · Cited by 1580 — In this study, we showed that the CRT is a..."},{"title":"Familiarity","url":"/familiarity/","description":"Knowing the classic CRT answers may raise scores without proving that someone has learned to catch new first-answer traps.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / First Answers","level":3,"text":"Familiarity When Knowing the Answer Becomes the Trap Familiarity Familiarity When Knowing the Answer Becomes the Trap Knowing the classic CRT answers may raise scores without proving that someone has learned to catch new first answer traps. Knowing the classic CRT answers may raise scores without proving that someone has learned to catch new first answer traps. population context How repeated exposure changes CRT scores Why memorisation is not transferable reflection Designing checks for unfamiliar traps improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers familiarity Introduction The Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT) is designed to reveal whether people can resist a tempting first answer and pause to check their reasoning. However, that goal becomes harder to interpret once the questions are widely known. If someone has already seen the classic bat and ball , machines and widgets, or lily pad problems, a high score may partly reflect memory rather than fresh reflection. The central issue is not cheating. It is measurement. A puzzle that once tested the ability to detect a first answer trap may stop doing so after repeated exposure because the trap is no longer new. Research over the last decade has examined how familiarity affects CRT performance, whether memorised answers undermine the test, and how researchers can distinguish genuine reflective thinking from simple recall. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA limitation of the Cognitive Reflection Test: familiarity by S Stieger · 2016 · Cited by 134 — To solve the CRT items, one has to ignore the first intention of the System 1 processes and switc... How Repeated Exposure Changes CRT Scores The original CRT became one of the most widely used reasoning tasks in psychology and behavioural science. Its popularity created an unusual problem: many participants began encountering the same questions repeatedly in studies, classrooms, books, websites, and social media discussions. Researchers increasingly worried that participants might remember answers rather than solve the problems afresh. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCUpon Repeated Reflection: Consequences of Frequent by JK Woike · 2019 · Cited by 24 — Repeated Exposure to the Cognitive Reflection Test. Practice effects, increases in test performance... Evidence suggests that familiarity can raise scores. Studies examining self reported prior exposure found that people who had previously seen CRT items tended to perform better than those encountering them for the first time. One analysis reported a meaningful performance advantage among familiar participants, raising concerns about whether the test was still measuring the same underlying tendency in experienced samples. [PeerJ] peerj.com exposure] had... higher CRT scores (Cohen's d = 0.41)\" (https... A limitation of the Cognitive Reflection Test: familiarity. Research... The effect is easiest to understand through the classic bat and ball question. The first time a person sees it, the intuitive answer often feels correct. After learning that the answer is actually five cents, the puzzle may never function in quite the same way again. The next encounter can become a memory retrieval task rather than a reflection task. Instead of recognising and correcting a tempting intuition , the participant may simply remember a previously learned solution. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Although there are reasons to suspect that prior exposure to the CRT undermines the validity of the test, to our knowledge this...Read more... Yet the evidence is more nuanced than a simple claim that familiarity destroys the test. Large scale analyses of repeated test takers have found surprisingly small average gains from repeated exposure alone. In one study involving more than 14,000 participants, each additional exposure produced only a tiny increase in score on average. The improvement appeared to come mainly from people who continued to engage thoughtfully with the questions rather than merely seeing them again. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentThe non effects of repeated exposure to the Cognitive...by A Meyer · 2018 · Cited by 96 — We esti... This creates an important distinction: familiarity can inflate scores, but repeated exposure does not automatically transform everyone into a high performer. How to Solve \"If It Takes 48 Days for Lily Pads to Cover a Lake...\" Cognitive Reflection Test Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube Why Memorisation Is Not Transferable Reflection A common misunderstanding is that learning the correct CRT answers demonstrates an improvement in reflective thinking. In reality, knowing the answer to a specific puzzle and developing a general habit of checking first impressions are different achievements. Imagine two people who both answer the bat and ball problem correctly. One has never seen it before and notices that the intuitive answer creates an arithmetic contradiction. The other remembers reading the solution years earlier. Their scores are identical, but the mental processes behind those scores are very different. This distinction matters because the broader goal of cognitive reflection is transfer. Reflective thinking is valuable when it helps someone recognise new situations in which an appealing answer might be wrong. Memorising three famous puzzles does not necessarily improve performance on unfamiliar judgement problems, probability questions, statistical claims , or real world decisions. The person may have learned a fact rather than a thinking habit. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Although there are reasons to suspect that prior exposure to the CRT undermines the validity of the test, to our knowledge this...Read more... Researchers therefore distinguish between: Item knowledge , where a person remembers a specific answer. Reflective disposition , where a person habitually questions intuitive responses. Reasoning skill , where a person can work through unfamiliar problems successfully. The CRT was intended to capture the latter two qualities, but familiarity introduces more influence from the first. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA limitation of the Cognitive Reflection Test: familiarity by S Stieger · 2016 · Cited by 134 — To solve the CRT items, one has to ignore the first intention of the System 1 processes and switc... This concern became significant enough that researchers began developing alternative versions of the CRT. New item sets were designed partly because the original questions had become so familiar that they risked losing their diagnostic value in frequently tested populations. [ResearchGate+2Queen's University Belfast] researchgate.net CRT 2 questions appear to rely less on numeracy than the original CRT but appear to measure closely related constructs in...Read more... The Debate: Does Familiarity Ruin the CRT? The research literature contains two positions that are often presented as contradictory but are actually addressing different questions. One line of research argues that familiarity threatens validity because prior exposure increases scores. If familiar participants systematically outperform unfamiliar participants, then raw scores may no longer provide a clean measure of spontaneous reflection. [PeerJ+2PMC] peerj.com exposure] had... higher CRT scores (Cohen's d = 0.41)\" (https... A limitation of the Cognitive Reflection Test: familiarity. Research... Another line of research argues that the CRT remains surprisingly useful even when participants have seen it before. Studies examining correlations between CRT scores and other psychological traits found that many of the relationships associated with the test remained stable despite prior exposure. In other words, familiar participants often still differed from less reflective participants in predictable ways. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cognitive reflection test is robust to multiple exposuresby M Bialek · 2018 · Cited by 234 — The cognitive reflection test (CRT... These findings can coexist. Familiarity may reduce the purity of the score while leaving much of the test's predictive value intact. A remembered answer can inflate performance on a specific item, yet individuals who score well may still differ systematically from those who score poorly on broader measures of analytical thinking. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The cognitive reflection test is robust to multiple exposuresby M Bialek · 2018 · Cited by 234 — The cognitive reflection test (CRT... For readers interested in improving their thinking, the practical lesson is straightforward: getting the right answer to a familiar CRT puzzle should not be treated as evidence that first answer traps have been mastered in general. How to Solve \"If It Takes 5 Machines 5 Minutes to Make 5 Widgets..\" Cognitive Reflection Test Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube Dr. Andrew Shtulman UBC Psychology Colloquia"},{"title":"False Fluency","url":"/false-fluency/","description":"Answers that come easily can feel more trustworthy than they deserve, especially when surface fit hides a failed constraint.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / First Answers","level":3,"text":"False Fluency Why Easy Answers Can Feel More True False Fluency False Fluency Why Easy Answers Can Feel More True Answers that come easily can feel more trustworthy than they deserve, especially when surface fit hides a failed constraint. Answers that come easily can feel more trustworthy than they deserve, especially when surface fit hides a failed constraint. mechanism Fluency as a misleading cue Surface fit versus exact constraint Spotting false confidence in real decisions improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers false fluency Introduction A common mistake in thinking is to treat mental ease as evidence. When an answer arrives quickly, fits the situation, and feels coherent, it often seems more trustworthy than it actually is. In many cases that feeling is useful: familiar problems often do have familiar solutions. The danger appears when ease of processing is mistaken for verification. An answer can feel right because it is fluent, not because it satisfies the exact requirements of the problem. Research on cognitive fluency, truth judgements, and cognitive reflection repeatedly shows that people use the subjective experience of ease as a cue when judging accuracy, confidence, and trustworthiness. [Nature] nature.com Cognitive Fluency and Judgments of Truth Decision MakingCognitive fluency refers to the subjective ease with which information is... Within the broader problem of first answer traps , false fluency is the mechanism that explains why an unchecked response can feel validated before any real checking has occurred. The answer is not merely available; it feels confirmed. Fluency as a Misleading Cue Psychologists use the term processing fluency to describe the subjective experience of information being easy to perceive, understand, recall , or manipulate mentally. People naturally treat this ease as information in its own right. When something is easy to process, it often feels more familiar, more credible, and more likely to be correct. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Epistemic Status of Processing Fluency as Source for...by R Reber · 2010 · Cited by 234 — This article combines findings from cog... Historically, this tendency makes a certain amount of sense. In everyday life, fluency and accuracy are often correlated. Familiar facts are usually easier to retrieve than unfamiliar ones. Well practised skills are usually executed more smoothly than poorly learned skills. Because ease and correctness frequently occur together, the mind learns to use fluency as a shortcut signal. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov This finding is known as the illusory truth effect, and it is... The problem is that the shortcut is imperfect. Many factors can create fluency without increasing accuracy: Repetition can make a statement feel truer. Familiar wording can make an explanation feel stronger. A simple narrative can seem more convincing than a complicated but accurate one. An intuitive answer can feel verified simply because it appeared immediately. In each case, the feeling of ease is real, but the implied conclusion may be false. [Nature+2PMC] nature.com Cognitive Fluency and Judgments of Truth Decision MakingCognitive fluency refers to the subjective ease with which information is... Research on the “illusory truth effect” illustrates this clearly. Repeated statements are more likely to be judged as true even when repetition provides no new evidence. One influential explanation is that repetition increases processing fluency, and people unconsciously interpret that fluency as a signal of truth. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The face of illusory truth: Repetition of information elicits...by A Stump · 2025 · Cited by 3 — A key explanation for this phenomeno... This is an important historical shift in understanding judgement errors. Earlier explanations often focused on ignorance or lack of information. Fluency research highlights a different failure mode: people may possess enough information to evaluate a claim but still be influenced by the feeling that it is easy to process. Why Can't People Solve These Simple Problems? Channel: Benjamin Keep, PhD, JD Open on YouTube Surface Fit Versus Exact Constraint The connection between fluency and cognitive reflection becomes clearer when examining classic first answer traps. Questions in the Cognitive Reflection Test are designed so that an immediate answer fits the surface structure of the problem while violating one of its exact constraints. The intuitive response is not random. It feels right because it matches an easily recognised pattern. Society for Judgment and Decision Making+2PMC [sjdm.org] sjdm.org Society for Judgment and Decision MakingCognitive Reflection Test (CRT)It assesses individuals' ability to suppress an intuitive and spon... Consider what happens mentally in a problem such as the bat and ball question. The first response emerges rapidly because it satisfies a rough interpretation of the numbers involved. The answer appears coherent, and that coherence creates fluency. Unless a person explicitly checks the arithmetic, the ease of retrieval can be mistaken for evidence that the solution has already been validated. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Society for Judgment and Decision MakingCognitive Reflection Test (CRT)It assesses individuals' ability to suppress an intuitive and spon... This distinction between surface fit and constraint satisfaction is central. A surface fit answer: Matches the overall appearance of the problem. Arrives quickly. Produces a feeling of completion. Requires little mental effort. A constraint satisfying answer: Works under all stated conditions. Survives explicit checking. Often requires additional reasoning. May initially feel less obvious. The key insight is that fluency primarily tracks the first category. It tells us that a response fits our current mental representation of the problem. It does not guarantee that the representation itself is complete or accurate. This explains why highly intelligent people can still fall into first answer traps. The trap does not rely on ignorance. It relies on prematurely treating a fluent interpretation as a fully analysed one. Why Confidence Often Rises Faster Than Accuracy One of the more revealing findings in this area is that confidence and correctness do not always increase together. Research examining confidence in Cognitive Reflection Test performance shows that people can distinguish between stronger and weaker answers to some extent, but their metacognitive accuracy is far from perfect. Confidence often exceeds actual performance, especially when intuitive answers feel compelling. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Overconfidence in the Cognitive Reflection Test: Comparing...by A Mata · 2023 · Cited by 17 — This research examines the metacognitiv... Fluency helps explain why. When a judgement is made smoothly, several subjective experiences occur simultaneously: The answer appears quickly. Competing alternatives are less visible. The reasoning process feels effortless. Retrieval seems immediate and complete. These experiences create a sense of certainty. Yet none directly measures whether important constraints have been overlooked. In practical terms, people frequently ask themselves the wrong question. Instead of asking, “Have I tested this answer?” they unconsciously ask, “How easily did this answer come to mind?” The second question is easier to answer, so it often substitutes for the first. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Epistemic Status of Processing Fluency as Source for...by R Reber · 2010 · Cited by 234 — This article combines findings from cog... This substitution explains why false confidence can emerge even when evidence is weak. The confidence is attached to the processing experience rather than to the quality of verification. The Illusion of Truth Channel: Veritasium Open on YouTube Spotting False Confidence in Real Decisions Outside laboratory tasks, false fluency appears in many ordinary decisions. Explanations That Feel Complete A simple explanation often feels more convincing than a complex one. Simplicity can be valuable, but it can also hide missing variables. A project failure attributed to a single obvious cause may feel satisfying while overlooking interacting factors that actually mattered. The danger is not simplicity itself. The danger is allowing ease of understanding to stand in for evidence. Forecasts and Predictions When a future outcome is described through a clear narrative, people often become more confident than the available data justify. A prediction may sound persuasive because the story is coherent and easy to imagine, not because the underlying probabilities are strong. The smoothness of the explanation becomes confused with the reliability of the forecast. Information Evaluation Repeated claims, familiar phrases, and widely circulated talking points gain fluency through exposure. As they become easier to process, they may feel"},{"title":"Lily Pads","url":"/lily-pads/","description":"The lily-pad question makes a linear guess feel natural even though doubling growth requires reasoning backwards from the final day.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / First Answers","level":3,"text":"Lily Pads The Growth Puzzle That Punishes Straight Lines Lily Pads Lily Pads The Growth Puzzle That Punishes Straight Lines The lily pad question makes a linear guess feel natural even though doubling growth requires reasoning backwards from the final day. The lily pad question makes a linear guess feel natural even though doubling growth requires reasoning backwards from the final day. mechanism Why 24 days feels tempting Reasoning backwards from a full pond Everyday risks of linear growth guesses improving your thinking and analytical skills first answers lily pads Introduction The lily pad problem is one of the most effective demonstrations of a first answer trap. A patch of lily pads doubles in size every day. If it takes 48 days to cover an entire pond, how long does it take to cover half the pond? Many people immediately answer 24 days. The correct answer is 47 days. The puzzle matters because the wrong answer does not come from poor arithmetic. It comes from applying a straight line intuition to a process that grows by doubling. The question reveals a common weakness in analytical thinking: when growth is exponential, our minds often treat it as if it were linear. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Every day, the patch doubles in size. If it takes 48 days for the patch to cover the entire lake, how long would it... Within the Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT), the lily pad question is designed to trigger a fast, plausible answer before reflection catches the hidden structure. The lesson extends far beyond puzzles. Many forecasting mistakes arise because people instinctively think in equal increments when the underlying process multiplies. Society for Judgment and Decision Making+2Wikipedia [sjdm.org] sjdm.org Every day, the patch doubles in size. If it takes 48 days for the patch to cover the entire lake, how long would it... Why 24 Days Feels Tempting The intuitive answer of 24 days emerges from a simple mental shortcut. People hear that the pond is fully covered after 48 days and unconsciously divide the period in half. If half the pond is covered, it seems natural to assume that half the time has elapsed. This is exactly how a linear process behaves: equal amounts of growth occur during equal intervals of time. The lily pad scenario is not linear. The patch does not add the same area each day. Instead, it doubles. The amount added on any given day depends on how large the patch already is. Early growth is tiny, while later growth becomes dramatic because the existing area is itself expanding. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Exponential growth Exponential growth The trap works because the linear interpretation feels effortless. The brain substitutes an easier question—“What is half of 48?”—for the actual question about a doubling process. That substitution produces an answer that feels reasonable enough to escape immediate scrutiny. [Yale Law School] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making Every day, the patch doubles in size. If it takes 48 days for the patch to cover the entire...Read more... Research on exponential growth bias has repeatedly found that people struggle to reason accurately about multiplicative change. Individuals often underestimate how quickly exponential processes accelerate, especially when they rely on intuition rather than calculation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by M Schonger · 2021 · Cited by 26 — Exponential growth bias is much smaller when doubling times are employed. Considering that in man... How to Solve \"If It Takes 48 Days for Lily Pads to Cover a Lake...\" Cognitive Reflection Test Channel: How to... Solve Puzzles Open on YouTube Reasoning Backwards From a Full Pond The quickest route to the correct answer is not to calculate forwards but to reason backwards. The key fact is that the patch doubles every day. Therefore: On day 48, the pond is completely covered. One day earlier, on day 47, the patch must have covered exactly half the pond. Doubling the half covered pond on the next day produces full coverage. That is the entire solution. No formulas are needed. The puzzle becomes easy once attention shifts from adding area to understanding the implications of doubling. Society for Judgment and Decision Making+2Embrace Autism [sjdm.org] sjdm.org Every day, the patch doubles in size. If it takes 48 days for the patch to cover the entire lake, how long would it... What makes this reasoning powerful is that it reverses the direction of thought. Instead of imagining growth accumulating from the start, it asks what must have been true immediately before the endpoint. This backward step exposes the hidden structure that the intuitive answer overlooks. The contrast between 24 days and 47 days is striking. A linear guess places the halfway point in the middle of the timeline. Exponential growth places the halfway point only one day before completion. That difference is precisely why exponential processes can appear harmless for long periods and then seem to explode suddenly. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Exponential growth Exponential growth What the Puzzle Reveals About Thinking The lily pad question is not primarily a mathematics test. It is a test of whether a person notices that their first interpretation may be wrong. Several mental habits contribute to the error: Surface matching. People recognise a familiar pattern involving halves and automatically divide the time period in half. Fluency. The answer “24” arrives immediately and feels coherent, creating a false sense of confidence. Failure to model the process. Instead of examining how the patch grows, attention focuses on the final number of days. Linear default thinking. Humans often assume change occurs in equal increments unless prompted to consider alternatives. [Yale Law School+2PMC] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making Every day, the patch doubles in size. If it takes 48 days for the patch to cover the entire...Read more... The puzzle therefore captures a broader analytical skill: recognising when a situation may require a different mental model from the one that first comes to mind. Why Can't People Solve These Simple Problems? Channel: Benjamin Keep, PhD, JD Open on YouTube Everyday Risks of Linear Growth Guesses The value of the lily pad problem lies in how often similar mistakes occur outside puzzles. Compounding and finance Investment returns, compound interest, and debt accumulation often grow multiplicatively rather than linearly. People frequently underestimate how large balances can become when growth compounds over time, especially during later stages when gains build upon previous gains. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Exponential growth Exponential growth Population and disease spread Early stages of population growth or infectious disease transmission can appear manageable because the numbers remain small. Exponential growth means that much of the total increase occurs near the end of the process, making delays in response more costly than intuition suggests. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Exponential growth Exponential growth Technology and adoption curves New technologies often appear insignificant for years before reaching a point where growth becomes visibly rapid. Observers who expect steady, linear progress may be surprised by the speed of later expansion. [Medium] medium.com Linear vs. Exponential Minds Linear vs. Exponential Minds by Futurist Jim CarrollNovember 19, 2025 — Linear vs. Exponential Minds “The rate of change outside... Published: November 19, 2025 In each case, the mistake resembles the lily pad error: assuming that being halfway through the timeline means being halfway to the outcome. A Useful Thinking Habit The most important lesson from the lily pad puzzle is not the answer itself but the method it encourages. When a situation involves percentages, compounding, doubling times, or rapid scaling, it is worth asking: Is this process additive or multiplicative? Does each step add a fixed amount, or does it grow from its current size? Would reasoning backwards reveal the structure more clearly? Those questions create a brief pause between intuition and judgement. The lily pad problem demonstrates how a single moment of reflection can replace a persuasive but incorrect answer with a more accurate understanding of how growth actually works. Yale Law School+2Society for Judgment and Decision Making [law.yale.edu] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making Every day, the patch doubles in size. If it takes 48 days for the patch to cover the entire...Read more... Our inability to understand the exponential function is our biggest weakness Prof Albert Bartlett Channel: DrAlanBarnard Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to The Growth Puzzle That Punishes Straight Lines. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman Directly addresses cognitive"},{"title":"Health Claims","url":"/health-claims/","description":"Health advice needs careful evidence checks because confident claims can mix real findings with overreach.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Health Claims How Should You Judge Health Advice? Health Claims Health Claims How Should You Judge Health Advice? Health advice needs careful evidence checks because confident claims can mix real findings with overreach. Health advice needs careful evidence checks because confident claims can mix real findings with overreach. population context Check the type of evidence Watch for causal overreach Separate advice from marketing improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims Introduction Health advice is one of the hardest places to think clearly because the claims often sound both personal and scientific: a supplement “supports immunity”, a test “detects problems early”, a diet “cuts risk”, or a routine “balances hormones”. The right response is not blanket distrust. It is analytical scepticism: asking what kind of evidence supports the claim, whether the evidence shows causation rather than association, and whether the advice is being shaped by marketing as much as by health benefit. Health claims deserve this extra care because weak evidence , selective reporting, small effects, hidden harms, and commercial incentives can make a confident message look much stronger than it really is. Regulators and evidence organisations repeatedly stress the same point: claims about health benefits should be truthful, not misleading, and supported by appropriate scientific evidence, not just plausible stories, testimonials , or isolated studies. [Federal Trade Commission] ftc.gov Federal Trade Commission Health Products Compliance Guidance Federal Trade CommissionHealth Products Compliance GuidanceDecember 20, 2022 — 20 Dec 2022 — This document provides guidance from FTC sta... Published: December 20, 2022 This matters for everyday thinking because health decisions carry asymmetric risk. Being wrong about a film recommendation costs an evening; being wrong about a treatment, supplement, screening test, diet, or self diagnosis can cost money, delay proper care, trigger anxiety, or cause direct harm. A good thinker therefore treats health advice as a claim to be checked in context: Who is the population? What outcome was measured? How large is the benefit in absolute terms? What harms were tracked? Who benefits if I believe this? What kind of evidence is actually being offered? A health claim becomes more credible when the evidence matches the strength of the promise. A story about one person feeling better can be a useful clue, but it cannot show that the product or habit caused the improvement. A laboratory study can suggest a biological mechanism, but it may not predict what happens in real people. An observational study can reveal patterns in populations, but people who choose one behaviour often differ from others in many ways. Randomised controlled trials are usually stronger for testing interventions because randomisation helps balance known and unknown differences between groups. Systematic reviews can be stronger still when they collect and appraise all relevant studies rather than relying on one convenient result. Cochrane notes that many of its reviews seek highly trustworthy evidence, typically from randomised trials , and its handbook emphasises assessing certainty, applicability, and numerical interpretation rather than treating all published findings as equal. [Cochrane] cochrane.org Open source on cochrane.org. A practical evidence check starts with the verb in the claim. “Linked to”, “associated with”, “may support”, “helps maintain”, and “clinically proven to treat” are not equivalent. A claim that a food is associated with better heart health may come from population data. A claim that a pill prevents heart attacks should normally require direct trial evidence showing fewer heart attacks, not just a change in a blood marker. The stronger, more specific, and more medical the promise, the stronger the evidence should be. A useful hierarchy is not a rigid ladder, but it prevents a common mistake: giving a weak study the authority of a strong one. Case reports and testimonials can generate questions. Mechanistic studies can explain how something might work. Observational studies can identify patterns and possible risks. Randomised trials can test interventions more directly. Systematic reviews can show whether results hold across multiple studies. Even then, a review is only as good as the studies it includes, the outcomes it prioritises, and the honesty of its interpretation. The first reader question should be: “Compared with what?” Many health claims quietly omit the comparison. A sleep supplement may improve sleep compared with doing nothing, placebo, a bedtime routine, reduced caffeine, cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia, or prescription treatment; those are very different claims. Likewise, “natural” does not mean “safer than alternatives”. The US National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health advises considering safety for each complementary product or practice on its own, because risks depend on the specific therapy, dose, user, and context. [NCCIH] nccih.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. How weak evidence gets upgraded in everyday language Health communication often turns cautious findings into stronger sounding advice. A study may find a small association in one population; a headline becomes “X prevents disease”. A trial may measure a short term biological marker; marketing becomes “supports long term health”. A subgroup result may appear only after researchers divide the data many ways; advertising becomes “shown to work in adults like you”. This is where analytical skill matters: not rejecting the evidence, but refusing to let the language outrun it. One common upgrade is moving from association to causation . If people who drink coffee have lower rates of a condition, coffee may be protective, but other explanations are possible: coffee drinkers may differ in income, occupation, smoking history, diet, sleep, access to healthcare, or baseline health. Modern causal inference frameworks ask explicit questions before treating observational evidence as causal: what is the causal question, what target effect is being estimated, what assumptions are being made, and whether a causal interpretation is tenable. [JAMA Network] jamanetwork.com Open source on jamanetwork.com. Another upgrade is moving from surrogate outcomes to real world benefit. A product might reduce inflammation markers, change cholesterol numbers, increase antibody levels, alter gut bacteria, or improve a score on a questionnaire. Those outcomes may matter, but they are not automatically the same as living longer, avoiding disease, feeling better, or functioning better. The issue is not that markers are useless; it is that a marker is a clue unless it has been validated as a reliable stand in for the outcome people actually care about. Recent UK linked work on cancer screening guidance, for example, stresses that surrogate outcomes cannot replace mortality as the definitive measure of whether a screening programme works. [University of Warwick] warwick.ac.uk warwick researchers help shape national guidance for cancer screening warwick researchers help shape national guidance for cancer screening A third upgrade is moving from relative risk to dramatic sounding benefit. “Cuts risk by 50%” may sound huge, but it means different things if the baseline risk falls from 40 in 100 to 20 in 100, or from 2 in 10,000 to 1 in 10,000. Absolute risk reduction is often more useful for decisions because it tells you how many people are actually helped. Evidence based medicine guides define absolute risk reduction as the difference in outcome rates between treatment and control groups, while relative risk describes a proportional comparison. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIRelative risk, relative and absolute risk reduction NCBIRelative risk, relative and absolute risk reduction A good rule is to translate every impressive health statistic into a plain comparison: “Out of 1,000 people like me, how many benefit, how many are harmed, and over what period?” If the answer is not available, the claim may still be interesting, but it should not be treated as settled advice. Watch for causal overreach Causal overreach happens when advice goes beyond what the evidence can support. It is especially common in nutrition, wellness, supplements, screening, and lifestyle optimisation because these areas mix real biological complexity with strong public demand for simple answers. A claim is more likely to overreach when it has one or more warning signs: It relies mainly on before and after experience. Symptoms fluctuate, placebo effects are real, and people often try several changes at once. It uses observational evidence as if it were a trial. Population patterns can be valuable, but they are vulnerable to confounding. It treats a mechanism as proof of benefit. “Reduces oxidative stress in cells” does not automatically mean it prevents disease in people. It"},{"title":"Risk Numbers","url":"/risk-numbers/","description":"A dramatic percentage can hide a tiny real-world difference unless the baseline risk and time period are made clear.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Health Claims","level":3,"text":"Risk Numbers What Does a 50 Percent Risk Cut Mean? Risk Numbers Risk Numbers What Does a 50 Percent Risk Cut Mean? A dramatic percentage can hide a tiny real world difference unless the baseline risk and time period are made clear. A dramatic percentage can hide a tiny real world difference unless the baseline risk and time period are made clear. dataset evidence Relative risk versus absolute risk Baseline risk and time horizon Translating claims into people helped or harmed improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims risk numbers Introduction A headline claiming that a treatment \"cuts your risk by 50%\" may sound dramatic, but it does not tell you how much difference the treatment is likely to make in real life. A 50% reduction can mean preventing many illnesses if the starting risk is high, or only a handful if the starting risk is very low. To judge a health claim properly, you need three pieces of information: the relative risk , the baseline (absolute) risk , and the time period over which the risk is measured. Without all three, impressive percentages can give a misleading impression of benefit. Evidence based medicine organisations increasingly recommend presenting results as natural frequencies (for example, \"5 fewer people out of 1,000\") because these are easier for patients and clinicians to interpret accurately. Cochrane+2U.S. Food and Drug Administration [cochrane.org] cochrane.org Chapter 14: Completing 'Summary of findings' tables and...For continuous outcomes, this would be stated as a mean or median valu... What does a 50 percent risk cut actually mean? Relative risk compares one group's risk with another's. If one group has half the risk of another, the relative risk is 0.5, or a 50% relative risk reduction. The missing question is: half of what? Consider two different situations: ScenarioRisk without treatmentRisk with treatmentRelative risk reductionAbsolute risk reductionA20 in 10010 in 10050%10 percentage pointsB2 in 1,0001 in 1,00050%1 person per 1,000 The relative claim is identical in both examples. The practical benefit is not. In Scenario A, ten people out of every hundred avoid the outcome. In Scenario B, only one extra person out of every thousand benefits. Neither presentation is wrong, but reporting only the relative figure makes the two situations appear equally impressive when their real world impact is very different. This distinction is a core principle of evidence based medicine and health communication. [Cochrane+2CEBM] cochrane.org Chapter 6: Choosing effect measures and computing...the risk difference (RD; also called the absolute risk reduction); and; the... Relative risk versus absolute risk Relative risk is useful because it often remains fairly consistent across different populations, allowing researchers to compare treatments efficiently. Absolute risk, however, is usually more useful for personal decision making because it reflects the chance that someone like you will actually experience the outcome. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCImplementing GRADE: calculating the risk difference by RG Newcombe · 2014 · Cited by 55 — In most contexts, estimates of relative effect of a therapy are more consistent across different... Think of the measures as answering different questions: Relative risk: How much did the treatment change the risk compared with the control group? Absolute risk: How many people actually benefit or are harmed? Absolute risk reduction: How much lower is the real world probability? [ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIRelative risk, relative and absolute risk reduction by L Irwig · 2008 · Cited by 37 — Relative risk reduction (RRR) tells you by how much the treatment reduced the risk of bad outcomes... Number needed to treat (NNT): How many people must receive the treatment for one additional person to benefit? These measures complement rather than replace one another. Good reporting presents both relative and absolute effects instead of forcing readers to infer one from the other. [Cochrane+2CEBM] cochrane.org Chapter 6: Choosing effect measures and computing...the risk difference (RD; also called the absolute risk reduction); and; the... Communicating Benefit and Risk Information Channel: U.S. Food and Drug Administration Open on YouTube Why baseline risk changes everything The same treatment can produce very different absolute benefits depending on who receives it. Suppose a medicine consistently reduces risk by 25% relative to no treatment. A person whose baseline risk is 40% would see their risk fall to about 30%, an absolute reduction of 10 percentage points. Someone whose baseline risk is 4% would see it fall to about 3%, an absolute reduction of only 1 percentage point. The treatment's biological effect has not changed. The person's starting risk has. This is why doctors consider age, existing illnesses, family history and other risk factors before recommending preventive treatments. Clinical guideline developers likewise estimate absolute benefits by applying relative effects to the baseline risk of the patient group under consideration rather than assuming one number fits everyone. [BMJ+2Cochrane] bmj.com Core GRADE 6: presenting the evidence in summary of...27 May 2025 — One can interpret hazard ratios... Because they depend on baseli... Published: May 2025 The time horizon matters as much as the percentage Risk figures always apply over a specific period, although advertisements and news reports sometimes leave this unstated. For example: A 2% chance over one month is very different from 2% over twenty years. A treatment that reduces five year risk may produce little measurable benefit over six months. Screening tests may show small annual differences that accumulate over decades. Whenever you encounter a percentage reduction, ask: Over what length of time was the outcome measured? Was the study measuring lifetime risk, yearly risk or short term risk? Would my own expected period of treatment be similar? Without the time horizon, even accurate statistics can be difficult to interpret correctly. Evidence reporting frameworks therefore encourage presenting baseline risk together with a clearly defined follow up period. [BMJ] bmj.com Core GRADE 6: presenting the evidence in summary of...27 May 2025 — One can interpret hazard ratios... Because they depend on baseli... Published: May 2025 Translating claims into people helped Many experts recommend converting percentages into natural frequencies because most people understand them more easily. Instead of saying: \"This medicine reduces stroke risk by 30%.\" Try expressing the same result as: \"Out of 1,000 people like you, about 20 would be expected to have a stroke over five years without treatment, compared with about 14 if everyone received the treatment.\" This presentation immediately reveals: how common the outcome is; how many people benefit; that most treated people would not have had the event anyway; and that some events still occur despite treatment. Cochrane recommends presenting risks per 100 or per 1,000 people in summary tables because these natural frequencies make evidence easier to understand. The FDA also advises that absolute probabilities are generally easier for consumers to interpret than relative measures alone. [Cochrane+2Sheppard] cochrane.org Chapter 14: Completing 'Summary of findings' tables and...For continuous outcomes, this would be stated as a mean or median valu... NCCMT URE What’s the Risk? Understanding Absolute and Relative Risk Reduction Channel: NCCMT CCNMO Open on YouTube Number needed to treat makes benefits concrete Absolute risk reduction also allows calculation of the Number Needed to Treat (NNT) . [cochrane.org] cochrane.org Chapter 6: Choosing effect measures and computing...the risk difference (RD; also called the absolute risk reduction); and; the... If a treatment reduces the absolute risk by: 10% , the NNT is 10 . 2% , the NNT is 50 . 1% , the NNT is 100 . An NNT of 100 means that, on average, one additional person benefits for every 100 people treated over the study period. This does not mean the treatment is ineffective. For severe outcomes such as death or stroke, even small absolute reductions may justify treatment. Conversely, for mild conditions, a small benefit may not outweigh inconvenience, cost or side effects. NNT simply makes the scale of benefit easier to visualise. [CEBM+2healthevidence.org] cebm.ox.ac.uk dditional bad outcome (death, stroke, etc.).Read more... Questions to ask when you see a dramatic percentage When a health headline highlights a large percentage improvement, pause before accepting its importance. Ask: What was the baseline risk before treatment? What is the absolute risk reduction? [cochrane.org] cochrane.org Chapter 6: Choosing effect measures and computing...the risk difference (RD; also called the absolute risk reduction); and; the... Over what period was the outcome measured? How many people actually benefit? What are"},{"title":"Diet Claims","url":"/diet-claims/","description":"Nutrition headlines often turn population patterns into personal rules before confounding and comparison groups are handled properly.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Health Claims","level":3,"text":"Diet Claims Did the Diet Cause the Benefit? Diet Claims Diet Claims Did the Diet Cause the Benefit? Nutrition headlines often turn population patterns into personal rules before confounding and comparison groups are handled properly. Nutrition headlines often turn population patterns into personal rules before confounding and comparison groups are handled properly. mechanism Why healthy user bias matters The comparison group behind the claim When observational nutrition evidence is useful improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims diet claims Introduction Nutrition headlines often sound more certain than the underlying evidence. A study reports that people who eat more nuts live longer, or that those who avoid a particular food have lower rates of disease, and the headline quickly becomes \"eat this\" or \"avoid that\". The crucial analytical question is whether the diet caused the outcome, or whether people who ate differently also differed in many other ways. Diet is closely linked to income, education, exercise, smoking, sleep, healthcare use, cooking habits, and cultural practices. Unless these differences are handled carefully, an observed association may not reflect a causal effect. Observational nutrition research remains valuable, but understanding what it can and cannot show is an essential thinking skill for evaluating health claims . [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by KC Maki · 2014 · Cited by 185 — This editorial review is intended to 1) highlight some of these limitations of observational eviden... Why healthy user bias matters One of the biggest challenges in diet research is healthy user bias. This occurs when people who adopt a supposedly healthy eating pattern also engage in many other health promoting behaviours. They may exercise more, smoke less, attend preventive health appointments, sleep better, drink less alcohol, or have greater access to healthcare. If these factors are not fully measured and adjusted for, the apparent benefit of the diet may partly reflect the healthier lifestyle rather than the food itself. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by KC Maki · 2014 · Cited by 185 — This editorial review is intended to 1) highlight some of these limitations of observational eviden... Researchers try to account for these differences using statistical adjustment, but adjustment has limits. Some behaviours are measured imperfectly, while others may not be measured at all. This leaves residual confounding: differences between groups that continue to distort the estimated effect even after sophisticated analysis. Nutrition researchers have repeatedly noted that dietary habits are unusually difficult to separate from socioeconomic and lifestyle factors because they cluster together rather than occurring independently. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineToward more rigorous and informative nutritional...by AW Brown · 2023 · Cited by 63 — The relationship of dietary... A practical implication is that very small reported risk reductions deserve particular caution. If one dietary pattern is associated with a 10–20% lower disease risk, that difference may be within the range that residual confounding could plausibly explain . Larger, consistent effects observed across different study designs are generally more persuasive than isolated modest associations. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by KC Maki · 2014 · Cited by 185 — This editorial review is intended to 1) highlight some of these limitations of observational eviden... Correlation and Causation understanding the Bradford Hill criteria in a nutshell Channel: Global Health with Greg Martin Open on YouTube The comparison group behind the claim Whenever a nutrition headline claims that a food \"reduces risk\", ask: compared with what? Dietary choices almost always replace something else. Eating more whole grains usually means eating less refined starch. Drinking water instead of sugary drinks differs from drinking water instead of milk. Replacing butter with olive oil is a different intervention from simply adding olive oil without changing anything else. Ignoring the comparison group produces misleading conclusions because foods are rarely consumed in isolation. A statement such as \"people who eat more fish have lower heart disease risk\" may reflect the benefits of replacing processed meat, rather than a unique property of fish itself. Likewise, replacing sugar sweetened beverages with artificially sweetened drinks addresses a different question from comparing artificially sweetened drinks with water. Substitution analyses, which explicitly model what food is being replaced, often provide more informative evidence than simply comparing high and low consumers of one food. [International Sweeteners Association] sweeteners.org International Sweeteners AssociationAddressing reverse causality in observational research is...Reverse causality is a major source of b... This comparison principle also explains why apparently contradictory nutrition studies sometimes coexist. Two studies may both be correct within their own comparison groups while answering different questions. Correlation vs Causation Explained: Why Patterns Can Mislead Us Channel: Sprouts · Views: 10.0K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 4 minutes 51 seconds Open on YouTube Reverse causation can make diets look harmful Sometimes illness changes diet rather than diet changing illness. This is known as reverse causation . For example, people who develop obesity, diabetes, or cardiovascular risk factors may deliberately switch to diet beverages, reduce fat intake, or change eating patterns after medical advice. An observational study may therefore find that consumers of these products have worse health outcomes —not because the products caused disease, but because higher risk individuals were more likely to choose them. Researchers increasingly attempt to reduce this problem by following people over time, measuring dietary changes repeatedly, excluding participants with existing disease, or analysing intended substitutions rather than one time dietary snapshots. These approaches cannot eliminate all bias, but they generally provide stronger evidence than simple cross sectional comparisons. [PMC+2International Sweeteners Association] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Fresh Look at Problem Areas in Research Methodology in...by NJ Temple · 2025 · Cited by 1 — Reverse causation is another source of... When observational nutrition evidence is useful Observational studies should not be dismissed simply because they cannot prove causation. Many important nutrition questions cannot realistically be answered using long term randomised trials . It would be difficult, expensive, or unethical to randomly assign thousands of people to follow specific diets for decades while ensuring perfect adherence. Instead, observational evidence is particularly valuable for: Identifying possible long term health associations. Detecting uncommon outcomes that trials may be too small to observe. Generating hypotheses for future experimental testing . Studying dietary patterns over many years under real world conditions. Confidence increases when multiple independent approaches point in the same direction. Stronger evidence emerges when observational studies, randomised trials of intermediate outcomes, biological mechanisms, and systematic reviews broadly agree rather than relying on a single study or headline. [Frontiers+2ResearchGate] frontiersin.org FrontiersDetermining Causation from Observational Studiesby GA Jelinek · 2017 · Cited by 25 — New techniques in observational epidemiolog... Plant vs. Animal Protein: How Healthy User Bias Misleads Nutrition Science Channel: Metabolic Mind Open on YouTube Questions that improve analytical thinking about diet claims Rather than asking whether a headline is true or false, ask questions that reveal how the evidence was produced. Was this an observational study or a randomised trial? Observational studies identify associations; trials are generally better at testing interventions. Who was compared with whom? The comparison group often determines what the result actually means. Could healthier lifestyles explain part of the result? Consider exercise, smoking, income, healthcare use, education, and other linked behaviours. Could reverse causation explain the finding? Did existing illness encourage people to change their diets? Is the reported effect large enough to be convincing? Small relative differences are more vulnerable to residual confounding than large, consistent effects. Has the finding been reproduced? One observational study is much less persuasive than converging evidence across multiple study designs. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by KC Maki · 2014 · Cited by 185 — This editorial review is intended to 1) highlight some of these limitations of observational eviden... A better way to read nutrition headlines Most nutrition news should be interpreted as evidence about"},{"title":"Screening","url":"/screening/","description":"Early detection can help, but screening claims need absolute benefit, false positives, overdiagnosis, and mortality outcomes in view.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Health Claims","level":3,"text":"Screening Does Early Detection Always Help? Screening Screening Does Early Detection Always Help? Early detection can help, but screening claims need absolute benefit, false positives, overdiagnosis, and mortality outcomes in view. Early detection can help, but screening claims need absolute benefit, false positives, overdiagnosis, and mortality outcomes in view. policy intervention What screening can and cannot prove False positives, overdiagnosis, and anxiety Why mortality matters more than surrogate wins improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims screening Introduction Early detection can save lives, but the slogan “catch it early” is not the same as proof that every screening programme improves health. Good analytical thinking requires separating the intuitive appeal of finding disease sooner from the evidence that earlier detection actually reduces illness or death. A screening test is offered to people without symptoms in the hope of identifying disease at a stage when treatment is more effective. That strategy can work extremely well in some circumstances, but it can also expose healthy people to false alarms, unnecessary procedures, anxiety, and treatment for conditions that would never have caused harm. [Iris] iris.who.int Iris Screening programmes: a short guide Screening programmes: a short guide IRISby World Health Organization · 2020 · Cited by 206 — The purpose of screening is to identif... The key question is therefore not simply whether a test finds more abnormalities. It is whether the entire screening programme—including testing, follow up, and treatment—leads to better outcomes than waiting until symptoms develop. That distinction is central to evidence based thinking about health claims . What screening can and cannot prove Screening is designed to shift diagnosis earlier in the course of disease. However, finding disease earlier does not automatically mean people live longer or healthier lives. A useful way to evaluate screening claims is to distinguish between what a test measures and what ultimately matters: A screening test can detect abnormalities before symptoms appear. [theguardian.com] theguardian.com While effective screening can detect serious health conditions like diabetes and high blood pressure before symptoms arise, the medical c... It can increase the number of diagnosed cases simply because more people are being examined. It can move diagnosis to an earlier date on the calendar. None of these facts alone proves that fewer people die or avoid serious illness. For example, a new blood test might double the number of cancers detected at an early stage. That sounds impressive, but if the additional cancers were slow growing and never destined to cause symptoms, the apparent success reflects more diagnoses rather than better health. This is why organisations such as the World Health Organization emphasise that screening programmes should be introduced only when there is evidence that benefits outweigh harms across the whole pathway, not merely because a disease can be detected earlier. [Iris] iris.who.int Iris Screening programmes: a short guide Screening programmes: a short guide IRISby World Health Organization · 2020 · Cited by 206 — The purpose of screening is to identif... Analytical scepticism therefore asks: Compared with people who were not screened, did screened people experience fewer deaths or serious complications? Were the benefits large enough to justify the harms? Did the evidence come from high quality trials or only from observational comparisons? These questions are more informative than asking whether the test is technically accurate. False positives, overdiagnosis, and anxiety Many people think the main risk of screening is a missed diagnosis. In reality, positive results that later prove unimportant are also a major consideration. False positives A false positive occurs when a screening test suggests disease that further testing shows is absent. Even if the mistake is eventually corrected, false positives can trigger: repeat scans or blood tests invasive biopsies financial costs weeks or months of uncertainty reduced trust in future healthcare Research has found that false positive cancer screening results can produce meaningful psychological distress and even reduce willingness to participate in later screening programmes. [Cancer.gov] cancer.gov mammogram false positives affect future screening Oct 4, 2024 — False positive screening test results can lead to fear, anxiety, and frustration with the health care system, noted Jennife... Lead time Bias Channel: H. Gilbert Welch Open on YouTube Overdiagnosis Overdiagnosis is different from a false positive. The diagnosis is technically correct: the abnormality exists. The problem is that it would never have caused symptoms or shortened the person's life. Detecting it nevertheless labels someone as a patient and often leads to treatment that offers no net benefit. This distinction is easy to miss because both the doctor and the laboratory are \"correct\" about the finding. The harm comes from treating disease that never needed treatment. Examples include certain slow growing prostate cancers and some breast cancers detected through screening programmes, where estimates suggest that a proportion of screen detected cases represent overdiagnosis, although the exact magnitude remains uncertain and varies across studies. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Review of Breast, Colorectal, Lung, and Prostate Cancer...by AA Kotwal · 2017 · Cited by 70 — Overdiagnosis also represents a signi... The cascade effect Screening can also trigger what researchers call a diagnostic cascade. One unexpected finding leads to another investigation, then another consultation, then perhaps surgery or long term monitoring. Each step may appear individually reasonable, yet the overall chain may produce more harm than benefit if the original finding was never clinically important. Modern imaging illustrates this well. Increasingly sensitive scans frequently discover small incidental abnormalities (\"incidentalomas\") that are harmless but difficult to ignore, leading to additional investigations with little evidence of improved outcomes. [The Guardian] theguardian.com While effective screening can detect serious health conditions like diabetes and high blood pressure before symptoms arise, the medical c... Why mortality matters more than surrogate wins One of the most common reasoning errors in discussions of screening is confusing surrogate measures with meaningful outcomes. A screening programme may achieve all of the following: detect more early stage disease increase five year survival after diagnosis identify smaller tumours improve laboratory markers Yet these improvements do not necessarily mean fewer people die. Several statistical effects explain why. Early Cancer Detection: Are These Tests Worth the Risk of Overdiagnosis? Channel: Barbell Medicine Open on YouTube Lead time bias Suppose two people develop the same cancer. One is diagnosed through screening at age 60 and dies at 70. The other develops symptoms at 65 and also dies at 70. The screened patient appears to have survived ten years after diagnosis, while the unscreened patient survived only five years. However, neither lived longer. Diagnosis simply occurred earlier. This is called lead time bias , and it can make survival statistics look better even when overall lifespan is unchanged. [catalogofbias.org] catalogofbias.org Lead time biasLead time bias where health outcome is the same in someone whose disease is detected by screening compared with someone who... Length time bias Screening is more likely to detect slow growing diseases because they remain detectable for longer. Rapidly growing, aggressive diseases often develop between screening intervals and may present with symptoms before the next scheduled test. As a result, screened cases naturally appear to have better outcomes even if screening itself deserves little credit. [BMJ] bmj.com Association of computed tomography screening with lung...Mar 30, 2022 — Overdiagnosis bias is the phenomenon whereby screening picks... Why disease specific mortality is not always enough Many screening trials report reductions in deaths from the target disease. That is valuable evidence, but researchers also examine broader outcomes because treatment complications and overdiagnosis can offset some of the gains. For this reason, evaluations increasingly encourage reporting both benefits and harms, including false positives, invasive follow up procedures, quality of life, and where feasible all cause mortality rather than focusing only on detection rates. [BMJ] bmj.com Quantification of harms in cancer screening trialsby B Heleno · 2013 · Cited by 181 — We included seven types of harms related to canc... Why recommendations differ between populations A screening programme that benefits one group may provide little benefit—or even net harm—to"},{"title":"Supplements","url":"/supplements/","description":"Supplement labels often sound scientific, but the real test is whether human studies show meaningful benefits and tracked harms.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Health Claims","level":3,"text":"Supplements When Supplement Claims Outrun the Evidence Supplements Supplements When Supplement Claims Outrun the Evidence Supplement labels often sound scientific, but the real test is whether human studies show meaningful benefits and tracked harms. Supplement labels often sound scientific, but the real test is whether human studies show meaningful benefits and tracked harms. policy intervention What the label is actually promising Human evidence versus plausible mechanisms Safety, interactions, and hidden tradeoffs improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims supplements Introduction Dietary supplements are often marketed with language that sounds precise and scientific: they may “support immunity”, “promote healthy sleep”, “boost energy”, or “maintain cognitive function”. These statements can create the impression that meaningful health benefits have already been demonstrated. In reality, the evidence behind many supplement claims ranges from strong to highly uncertain, depending on the ingredient, the health outcome, and the quality of the research. Developing better analytical thinking means separating what is biologically plausible from what has actually been shown in people. A supplement may influence a laboratory measurement, affect a biological pathway, or work in animals without producing a clinically important benefit in everyday human use. The key question is not whether a mechanism sounds convincing, but whether well conducted human studies show that people experience worthwhile benefits that outweigh the risks. Official guidance from the US National Institutes of Health (NIH), the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA), and consumer protection agencies consistently emphasises that many supplements require more high quality research before strong benefit claims can be justified. Office of Dietary Supplements+2U.S. Food and Drug Administration [ods.od.nih.gov] ods.od.nih.gov Learn about their label information, effectiveness, safety, risk, quality, Office of Dietary SupplementsDietary Supplements: What You Need to Know Consumer4 Jan 2023 — Important things to know about dietary sup... What the label is actually promising Many supplement labels avoid direct disease treatment claims while still implying meaningful health improvements. Understanding this distinction is one of the simplest ways to become a more critical reader. In the United States, many supplements use what are known as structure/function claims . These describe supporting normal bodily functions rather than treating disease. Examples include statements such as: \"Supports immune health\" \"Helps maintain healthy joints\" \"Promotes relaxation\" \"Supports cognitive function\" These statements differ legally from claims that a product treats arthritis, prevents Alzheimer's disease, or cures insomnia. Structure/function claims do not require pre market approval from the FDA, provided they meet legal requirements, and they must carry a disclaimer stating that the FDA has not evaluated the claim and that the product is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent disease. [U.S. Food and Drug Administration] fda.gov questions and answers dietary supplements Food and Drug AdministrationQuestions and Answers on Dietary Supplements21 Feb 2024 — These three types of claims are not approved by FDA... For consumers, however, the practical distinction is often blurred. Someone reading \"supports memory\" may reasonably infer that the supplement has been shown to improve memory in meaningful ways, even if the supporting evidence consists only of preliminary studies or indirect biological reasoning. This illustrates an important analytical lesson: the wording of a claim often communicates more certainty than the underlying evidence actually provides. Human evidence versus plausible mechanisms A common mistake is assuming that a plausible mechanism automatically predicts clinical benefit. Researchers often discover that a vitamin, mineral, or plant compound affects a biological pathway. Laboratory experiments may show antioxidant activity, changes in inflammatory markers , or hormone related effects. These findings are valuable because they generate hypotheses. They do not establish that people become healthier. Several steps separate a promising mechanism from a reliable health intervention: What You Need to Know About Dietary Supplements (Module 3) Channel: U.S. Food and Drug Administration Open on YouTube 1. The ingredient reaches the relevant tissues in humans. 2. The dose used commercially matches the dose studied. 3. The biological effect translates into a meaningful health outcome. 4. Benefits consistently outweigh harms. Many supplements fail somewhere along this chain. For example, reducing a blood marker associated with inflammation does not necessarily reduce pain or disability. Increasing antioxidant activity does not automatically lower the risk of cancer or heart disease. Raising testosterone related laboratory values does not necessarily improve strength, fertility, or wellbeing. Randomised controlled trials (RCTs) are therefore generally considered the strongest way to test whether a supplement actually improves health outcomes in people. Systematic reviews, which evaluate all relevant studies rather than a single positive trial, provide an even stronger overview when the underlying studies are of good quality. [Federal Trade Commission+2Federal Trade Commission] ftc.gov Federal Trade CommissionHealth Products Compliance Guidance20 Dec 2022 — The FTC Act doesn't require pre market approval of health claims... Why evidence gaps persist The absence of convincing evidence does not necessarily mean a supplement is ineffective. Often, it means the necessary research has not been completed or is not sufficiently reliable. Several recurring problems limit confidence in supplement research: Small studies. Trials involving only a few dozen participants can easily produce unstable or exaggerated estimates. Short follow up. A study lasting four weeks may not reveal whether benefits persist or whether delayed harms appear. Surrogate outcomes. Researchers may measure changes in blood tests instead of outcomes that matter to patients, such as fewer fractures, improved sleep quality, or reduced illness. Publication bias. Positive findings are more likely to be published than negative ones. Industry sponsorship. Commercial funding does not invalidate research, but it makes careful examination of study design and reporting especially important. Poor replication. A single encouraging study is much less persuasive if independent researchers cannot reproduce the findings. These limitations explain why initial enthusiasm for some supplements often weakens as more comprehensive evidence accumulates. When evidence is genuinely strong Analytical scepticism is not the same as assuming every supplement is ineffective. Some supplements have substantial evidence for specific populations or clearly defined medical situations. Examples include: folic acid before and during early pregnancy to reduce neural tube defects; calcium and vitamin D for certain people at risk of deficiency or bone loss; the AREDS eye vitamin formulation for slowing progression of particular forms of age related macular degeneration. These examples share important characteristics. The target population is clearly defined, the outcomes are clinically meaningful, and the recommendations are supported by multiple human studies rather than isolated laboratory findings. They are not evidence that supplements generally work, but examples showing that good evidence can exist when appropriate research has been conducted. [Office of Dietary Supplements] ods.od.nih.gov Learn about their label information, effectiveness, safety, risk, quality, Office of Dietary SupplementsDietary Supplements: What You Need to Know Consumer4 Jan 2023 — Important things to know about dietary sup... Safety, interactions, and hidden trade offs The evidence gap concerns harms as well as benefits. Many consumers assume that products sold without prescription are inherently safe, especially when labelled \"natural\". Yet natural substances can produce powerful biological effects, interact with medicines, or cause problems at high doses. Potential concerns include: interactions with blood thinning medicines, antidepressants, diabetes medication, or immunosuppressants; increased bleeding risk before surgery; liver injury associated with some herbal products; contamination, inaccurate ingredient amounts, or undeclared pharmaceutical substances in some products; adverse effects from combining multiple supplements with similar ingredients. Safety research often lags behind marketing. Rare adverse effects may only become apparent after widespread public use, particularly because pre market evidence requirements for supplements differ from those for prescription medicines in many jurisdictions. NIH guidance also notes that pregnant women,"},{"title":"Markers","url":"/markers/","description":"A number moving in the right direction is not the same as fewer illnesses, longer life, better function, or less suffering.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Health Claims","level":3,"text":"Markers When Better Numbers Do Not Mean Better Health Markers Markers When Better Numbers Do Not Mean Better Health A number moving in the right direction is not the same as fewer illnesses, longer life, better function, or less suffering. A number moving in the right direction is not the same as fewer illnesses, longer life, better function, or less suffering. dataset evidence What surrogate markers are meant to do When markers mislead health decisions How to ask for patient important outcomes improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims markers Introduction Many health claims rely on improvements in numbers rather than improvements in lives. A supplement may lower a blood test result, a drug may shrink a tumour on a scan, or a wearable device may report a better score. These changes can be meaningful, but they do not automatically mean people live longer, avoid illness, function better, or feel better. This distinction lies at the heart of analytical scepticism in health. A surrogate health marker is an indirect measure that is expected to predict a real health outcome. Sometimes surrogate markers are excellent predictors and allow treatments to be evaluated much faster. In other cases they have proved misleading, leading to treatments that improved laboratory values while producing little real world benefit or even causing harm. Learning to distinguish between a change in a marker and a change in patient important outcomes is one of the most valuable skills for evaluating health claims. [U.S. Food and Drug Administration] fda.gov facts biomarkers and surrogate endpoints Food and Drug AdministrationFDA Facts: Biomarkers and Surrogate Endpoints21 Dec 2017 — Surrogate endpoints are used when the clinical out... What surrogate markers are meant to do A surrogate marker (or surrogate endpoint in clinical trials) is a measurement that stands in for an outcome that really matters to patients. Instead of waiting years to see whether a treatment prevents strokes or extends life, researchers may measure something that changes sooner and is believed to lie on the pathway to that outcome. Examples include: Blood pressure instead of future strokes or heart attacks. LDL cholesterol instead of cardiovascular events. [ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIValidated Surrogate Endpoint NIH25 Sept 2017 — Low density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol reduction is a validated surrogate endpoint for reduction of cardiovas... HbA1c (glycated haemoglobin) instead of long term diabetic complications. HIV viral load instead of progression to AIDS. Tumour shrinkage instead of longer survival or improved quality of life. NCBI+2U.S. Food and Drug Administration [ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIValidated Surrogate Endpoint NIH25 Sept 2017 — Low density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol reduction is a validated surrogate endpoint for reduction of cardiovas... Surrogate markers exist for practical reasons. Waiting decades to measure heart attacks or dementia may make clinical trials prohibitively slow or expensive. For rapidly fatal diseases, waiting for final outcomes may also delay access to promising treatments. Regulators therefore sometimes accept validated surrogate endpoints when there is strong evidence that changes in the marker reliably predict meaningful clinical benefit. U.S. Food and Drug Administration+2U.S. Food and Drug Administration [fda.gov] fda.gov table surrogate endpoints were basis drug approval or licensure Food and Drug AdministrationTable of Surrogate Endpoints That Were the Basis of Drug...29 Apr 2026 — This surrogate endpoint table inclu... The crucial word is validated . Not every biological marker deserves equal confidence. Skills Lab: Understanding Surrogate Endpoints and Their Limitations Channel: Medscape · Views: 207 · Uploaded: March 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube When better numbers do not translate into better health The central mistake is assuming that every biomarker is a faithful substitute for what patients actually care about. Several things can go wrong. First, a marker may capture only one part of a disease. Heart disease, for example, involves inflammation, clotting, blood pressure, metabolism and many other processes. Improving one laboratory value does not guarantee that the overall disease process improves. Second, a treatment may affect both the surrogate marker and other biological pathways. Those unintended effects can offset or even outweigh the apparent benefit suggested by the marker. Third, researchers may correctly identify a risk factor without proving that altering it in a particular way improves outcomes. A factor can be associated with disease while still failing as a treatment target. These possibilities explain why regulators, evidence reviewers and clinical guideline developers distinguish between a reasonable biological theory and evidence that patients actually experience better outcomes . [The Lancet+2National Academies] thelancet.com The LancetA framework for the definition and interpretation of the use...by O Ciani · 2023 · Cited by 67 — Surrogate endpoints are used... Biomarkers and Surrogate Endpoints in Drug Development Channel: PhRMA Open on YouTube Examples that sharpen analytical thinking Some surrogate markers have proved highly reliable. [fas.org] fas.org validating surrogate endpoints Surrogate markers...Read more... Blood pressure reduction is one of the strongest examples. Across many different drug classes, lowering elevated blood pressure consistently reduces stroke risk, heart attacks and cardiovascular mortality. Similarly, lowering HIV viral load predicts improved disease control, and reducing LDL cholesterol through established treatments such as statins has repeatedly translated into fewer cardiovascular events. These markers became trusted because numerous trials confirmed that changing the marker reliably changed the clinical outcomes. [NCBI+2National Academies] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIValidated Surrogate Endpoint NIH25 Sept 2017 — Low density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol reduction is a validated surrogate endpoint for reduction of cardiovas... Other examples have been far less successful. For many years, raising HDL (\"good\") cholesterol appeared promising because people with naturally higher HDL levels tended to have lower rates of heart disease. However, several drugs that substantially increased HDL cholesterol failed to reduce heart attacks or deaths in large randomised trials. The biological association was real, but manipulating the marker did not produce the expected clinical benefit. [White Rose Research Online] eprints.whiterose.ac.uk White Rose Research OnlineTime to review the role of surrogate endpoints in health policyby O Ciani · 2017 · Cited by 178 — For example... Cancer research provides another cautionary example. Some treatments shrink tumours or delay tumour growth, yet produce little improvement in overall survival or quality of life. Tumour response can be valuable, particularly in aggressive disease, but it is not automatically equivalent to helping patients live longer or live better. This is why oncology increasingly debates how much confidence should be placed in surrogate endpoints when approving new medicines. Cancer Therapy Advisor+2rama.mahidol.ac.th [cancertherapyadvisor.com] cancertherapyadvisor.com Cancer Therapy AdvisorSurrogate Endpoints in Oncology & Use for Cancer Drug...by B Furlow — Surrogate endpoints increasingly are used fo... These contrasting cases illustrate an important reasoning principle: the usefulness of one surrogate marker says little about another. Each must earn trust through evidence rather than analogy. Why marketing often emphasises markers Commercial health claims frequently focus on measurements that improve quickly because they are easier to demonstrate. Advertisements may highlight that a product: \"Reduces inflammation markers.\" \"Balances hormone levels.\" \"Optimises blood sugar.\" \"Boosts antioxidant status.\" \"Improves gut microbiome diversity.\" These statements may describe genuine changes in laboratory measurements. The analytical question is whether those changes have been shown to produce outcomes that matter to patients. Many supplements and wellness products stop at the surrogate marker. They rarely demonstrate reductions in disease, disability, pain, hospital admissions or mortality because those studies are much larger, longer and more expensive. A sophisticated reader therefore distinguishes between: The marker changed. People became healthier because the marker changed. Those are different claims requiring different levels of evidence. How to ask for patient important outcomes When reading a study or health advertisement, a few questions can quickly reveal whether the evidence rests mainly on surrogate markers. Ask: What outcome was actually measured? Was it a laboratory value, scan or symptom score, or was it heart attacks, fractures, survival or quality of life? Has this"},{"title":"Testimonials","url":"/testimonials/","description":"Personal improvement stories can be sincere and still fail to show that a product, routine, or treatment caused the change.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Health Claims","level":3,"text":"Testimonials Why Feeling Better Is Not Proof Testimonials Testimonials Why Feeling Better Is Not Proof Personal improvement stories can be sincere and still fail to show that a product, routine, or treatment caused the change. Personal improvement stories can be sincere and still fail to show that a product, routine, or treatment caused the change. population context Why before and after stories persuade Placebo effects and regression to the mean What would separate cause from coincidence improving your thinking and analytical skills health claims testimonials Introduction Feeling better after taking a supplement, starting a diet, or trying a wellness routine is a genuine experience. The key analytical question, however, is whether the improvement was caused by the intervention. Testimonials often feel convincing because they are vivid, personal, and emotionally engaging, but they cannot by themselves distinguish between a real treatment effect, natural recovery, symptom fluctuation, placebo responses, or simple coincidence. Learning to separate these possibilities is one of the most valuable thinking skills when evaluating health claims . This matters because many illnesses and symptoms naturally improve over time, especially those that fluctuate or are sought out when they are at their worst. Without a fair comparison, almost any intervention can appear effective. Researchers therefore rely on controlled studies rather than before and after stories to determine whether a treatment genuinely works. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effect of regression to the mean on decision making in health...by V Morton · 2003 · Cited by 404 — He noted that when the average he... Why before and after stories persuade Testimonials are persuasive because they provide what appears to be direct evidence. A person says, \"I started this treatment, and now I feel better.\" The sequence seems obvious: treatment first, improvement second. The problem is that timing alone does not establish causation . Many other explanations can produce exactly the same pattern. Consider someone who develops severe back pain. After several difficult weeks they purchase a new supplement, and a week later the pain eases. The improvement feels like proof that the supplement worked. Yet several other explanations remain possible: The condition may have been recovering naturally. Symptoms may have been unusually severe when treatment began and then returned towards their typical level. Expectations of improvement may have influenced how pain was experienced or reported. Other changes—better sleep, reduced stress , exercise, or medication—may have contributed. The improvement could simply have occurred by chance at that point in time. The individual testimonial cannot distinguish between these explanations because there is no comparable group showing what would have happened without the intervention. This is why regulators caution that testimonials are not substitutes for scientific evidence. Personal stories may generate hypotheses worth studying, but they cannot demonstrate that a product or treatment caused the reported benefit. [Cochrane] cochrane.org CD003974 placebo interventions all clinical conditions Placebo interventions for all clinical conditions1 May 2022 — We studied the effect of placebo treatments by reviewing 202 trials... Published: May 2022 Placebo effects and regression to the mean Two of the most commonly misunderstood reasons why people sincerely report improvement are placebo effects and regression to the mean. They are related only in that both can make ineffective interventions appear helpful if interpreted carelessly. Placebo effects are real, but often misunderstood A placebo effect refers to improvements that arise from the context of treatment rather than from the specific active ingredient itself. Expectations, reassurance, the clinician–patient relationship, and the ritual of receiving care can all influence subjective experiences such as pain, fatigue, nausea, or anxiety. Importantly, placebo effects do not mean that an illness is imaginary. They reflect genuine psychological and biological processes that can alter how symptoms are experienced. However, systematic reviews have found that placebo treatments generally produce little or no important effect on objective disease outcomes . Their strongest and most consistent influence is on patient reported symptoms, particularly pain, although even these effects vary substantially across conditions and studies. [Cochrane] cochrane.org CD003974 placebo interventions all clinical conditions Placebo interventions for all clinical conditions1 May 2022 — We studied the effect of placebo treatments by reviewing 202 trials... Published: May 2022 Anecdotes (The Body of Evidence) Channel: The Body of Evidence Podcast · Views: 56.5K · Uploaded: July 2016 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Regression to the mean explains many apparent recoveries Regression to the mean is a statistical phenomenon that occurs whenever people seek treatment because their symptoms are unusually bad. Imagine someone whose migraine severity varies naturally from month to month. They are most likely to buy a new remedy during an exceptionally painful episode. Even if nothing is done, the next measurement is likely to be closer to their usual average rather than remaining at the extreme. Because treatment is often started at the worst point, ordinary improvement can easily be mistaken for treatment success. This phenomenon affects many areas of healthcare, from blood pressure measurements to chronic pain and mental health symptoms. It can make almost any intervention appear effective unless an untreated or appropriately controlled comparison group is included. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effect of regression to the mean on decision making in health...by V Morton · 2003 · Cited by 404 — He noted that when the average he... Symptom fluctuation creates convincing illusions Many common conditions are not stable. They naturally improve and worsen over time. Examples include: migraine chronic back pain eczema irritable bowel syndrome allergic conditions depression and anxiety symptoms many viral illnesses Someone experiencing one of these conditions often seeks help during a flare up rather than during a good period. If symptoms later return to their usual level, the timing alone can create the impression that whatever was tried caused the improvement. Wellness marketing frequently exploits this pattern. Products are promoted using dramatic \"before\" photographs or stories collected immediately before treatment and several weeks afterwards. Without knowing how similar people would have changed without the product, these stories cannot establish effectiveness. Researchers therefore compare treated participants with similar untreated or placebo groups measured over the same period. This design helps distinguish genuine treatment effects from normal symptom variation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effect of regression to the mean on decision making in health...by V Morton · 2003 · Cited by 404 — He noted that when the average he... Why sincere people can reach mistaken conclusions People who give testimonials are rarely being dishonest. Most genuinely believe their experience. Several cognitive and statistical factors make incorrect causal conclusions surprisingly easy: Selective memory. Improvements are remembered more vividly than periods when treatments produced no benefit. Confirmation bias. Once people expect success, they notice evidence supporting that expectation more readily than contradictory evidence. Multiple simultaneous changes. Diet, sleep, exercise, medication, stress, and social support often change together. Natural recovery. Many illnesses improve without specific treatment. Symptom variability. Chronic conditions often fluctuate regardless of intervention. These influences do not invalidate personal experience. They simply mean that personal experience alone cannot reliably identify which factor produced the improvement. Know when to use Sham and placebo terms Prof. Arunachalam Ramachandran Channel: SaiRaM Physio Academy Open on YouTube What would separate cause from coincidence? The strongest evidence comes from asking a comparison question rather than relying on a single story. Useful questions include: Compared with people who did not receive the treatment, did more people improve? Were participants randomly assigned to treatments? Were outcome assessors blinded where possible? Was the improvement large enough to matter clinically rather than merely reaching statistical significance? Have independent studies found similar results? Randomised controlled trials are especially valuable because they help balance differences between groups, making placebo effects, natural recovery, and regression to the mean much less likely to be mistaken for treatment efficacy. Systematic reviews strengthen the evidence further by examining the"},{"title":"Humility","url":"/humility/","description":"Good thinking is not doubting everything; it is being willing to revise beliefs when the evidence changes.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Humility How Do You Stay Open Without Drifting? Humility Humility How Do You Stay Open Without Drifting? Good thinking is not doubting everything; it is being willing to revise beliefs when the evidence changes. Good thinking is not doubting everything; it is being willing to revise beliefs when the evidence changes. mechanism Humility versus indecision Updating beliefs Reasonable confidence improving your thinking and analytical skills humility Introduction Intellectual humility is the habit of treating your own beliefs as strong, weak or revisable in proportion to the evidence behind them. It is not cynicism , which distrusts everything, nor indecision, which avoids commitment. A humble thinker can say, “I am confident about this,” while also knowing what kind of evidence would make that confidence shrink. That balance matters because good analysis depends on two abilities that often pull against each other: holding a view firmly enough to act, and holding it lightly enough to update when reality pushes back. Research commonly defines intellectual humility as recognising the limits of one’s knowledge and accepting that one’s beliefs may be wrong; reviews link it with better evidence evaluation, lower dogmatism and more constructive disagreement. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... The practical mechanism is simple but demanding: separate confidence from ego. When a belief becomes part of personal identity , changing it can feel like losing status, loyalty or self respect. Intellectual humility keeps the belief available for inspection. It asks: How do I know? How strong is the evidence? What would I expect to see if I were mistaken? That is very different from drifting into “nothing is knowable”. The point is not to doubt everything, but to make confidence answerable to evidence. Humility Is Not Indecision A common misunderstanding is that intellectual humility means always hedging, refusing to take a side, or treating every claim as equally plausible. That would make thinking worse, not better. Many decisions require action before certainty is possible: choosing a medical treatment, hiring someone, voting, investing time in a project, or deciding whether a source is trustworthy. Humility does not remove the need to decide; it improves the way confidence is formed. The useful distinction is between uncertainty about your knowledge and paralysis about your judgement . A person can recognise incomplete evidence and still make a provisional conclusion. In fact, the strongest forms of humility usually make reasoning more decisive, because they reduce the emotional need to defend a first impression. Reviews of intellectual humility describe it as an awareness of intellectual limitations, not as a blanket refusal to believe anything; it is associated with being more attentive to evidence quality and more willing to reconsider when evidence changes. [John Templeton Foundation] templeton.org John Templeton Foundation The Psychology of Intellectual Humility John Templeton Foundation The Psychology of Intellectual Humility A humble judgement often sounds like this: “I am fairly confident, because the evidence is direct and consistent.” “I am uncertain, because the evidence is indirect or comes from interested parties.” “I used to think X, but Y evidence makes me revise that.” “I still reject that claim, but I may have been too quick to dismiss the person making it.” The last example is especially important. Intellectual humility does not require giving every idea equal weight. Philosophers discussing extremist or demonstrably false views argue that a person can own their own limitations without being open to revising well supported beliefs that those views are false. In that account, humility may mean avoiding lazy assumptions about the people who hold a view, while still refusing to treat the view itself as a live possibility. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Open source on cambridge.org. That boundary protects humility from sliding into performative neutrality. In serious thinking, “both sides” is not a principle; it is sometimes a useful starting question. When the evidence is uneven, intellectual humility may require saying so. How Beliefs Actually Update Belief updating is not just adding new information to an empty container. People interpret evidence through prior assumptions, identity, trust, emotion and social context. That is why two people can see the same fact and move in different directions. Intellectual humility works by making that updating process more visible. One mechanism is metacognition , meaning the ability to think about your own thinking. Rather than asking only “Is this claim true?”, the humble thinker also asks, “What is my basis for believing it?” and “Am I more confident than my evidence warrants?” The Greater Good Science Center describes intellectual humility as recognising that one’s beliefs may be fallible because evidence can be limited, flawed or hard to evaluate. [Greater Good] greatergood.berkeley.edu what does intellectual humility look like what does intellectual humility look like A second mechanism is active open mindedness . Psychologist Keith Stanovich’s work on actively open minded thinking measures habits such as considering alternative views, noticing evidence that contradicts current beliefs and postponing closure when the evidence is still weak. Such measures are associated with better performance on reasoning and bias tasks, and with lower endorsement of superstitious and conspiracy beliefs. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement A third mechanism is evidence weighting . New information should not move every belief by the same amount. Strong evidence from a reliable method should change confidence more than a rumour, a single anecdote or an argument from someone with a clear persuasive agenda. Research on belief updating under ambiguity makes a related point: when new information arrives, people should retain conditional beliefs in which they have stronger confidence and relinquish weaker ones first. [IDEAS/RePEc] ideas.repec.org IDEAS/Re PEc Updating Confidence in Beliefs IDEAS/Re PEc Updating Confidence in Beliefs In practice, belief updating can be treated as a sequence: 1. Name the belief. Vague beliefs are hard to revise. “This policy is bad” is less updateable than “This policy is likely to increase waiting times in the first year.” 2. State current confidence. Use rough levels: low, moderate, high. The exact number matters less than noticing that confidence has a size. 3. Identify the evidence type. Is it direct measurement, expert consensus, personal experience, analogy, testimony, or inference? 4. Look for disconfirming evidence . Not to prove yourself wrong, but to test whether the belief survives contact with the best available objections. 5. Update by degree. Most evidence should shift confidence, not flip belief from 0 to 100. This is where humility differs from cynicism. Cynicism often treats new evidence as just another attempt to manipulate. Humility asks whether the evidence is strong, relevant and independent enough to deserve a change in confidence. Intellectual humility: the rewards of being willing to change your mind Channel: The Conversation Open on YouTube Why Cynicism Feels Sophisticated but Weakens Analysis Cynicism can masquerade as intelligence because it sounds guarded. It avoids naivety by assuming hidden motives, institutional failure or bad faith. Sometimes those suspicions are warranted. But as a thinking style, cynicism has a major flaw: it can become unfalsifiable. If every source is dismissed in advance, evidence can no longer correct the belief. Intellectual humility is more demanding because it does not let either trust or distrust run automatically. It asks for discrimination. Which source has relevant expertise? Which claim is supported by independent evidence? Which part of the story is well established, and which part is speculation? That selective attention is why humility can improve misinformation discernment rather than simply making people cautious about everything. A 2024 study by Toby Prike found that intellectual humility was associated with better ability to distinguish true from false news headlines, and that the effect was not merely a general tendency to call everything false or withhold judgement. Participants higher in intellectual humility showed greater misinformation discernment and metacognitive insight, not just blanket scepticism. [Advances.in] advances.in Open source on advances.in. A related 2024 meta analysis reported that intellectual humility was consistently associated with lower receptivity to misinformation across 54"},{"title":"Status Costs","url":"/status-costs/","description":"Beliefs are harder to revise when they feel tied to identity, loyalty or competence rather than evidence alone.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Humility","level":3,"text":"Status Costs Why Changing Your Mind Feels Personal Status Costs Status Costs Why Changing Your Mind Feels Personal Beliefs are harder to revise when they feel tied to identity, loyalty or competence rather than evidence alone. Beliefs are harder to revise when they feel tied to identity, loyalty or competence rather than evidence alone. mechanism How beliefs become identity signals Status threats that block updating Ways to revise a view without self erasure improving your thinking and analytical skills humility status costs Introduction Changing your mind is often presented as a purely intellectual exercise: new evidence appears, old beliefs are replaced, and better conclusions follow. In practice , the process is rarely so straightforward. Many beliefs become tied to identity , competence, loyalty or social belonging. Revising them can therefore feel less like correcting an error and more like admitting personal failure or betraying a group. The result is that people sometimes defend weak ideas not because the evidence remains persuasive, but because changing position seems socially or psychologically costly. Intellectual humility is valuable precisely because it separates the strength of a person from the permanence of their opinions. It allows beliefs to be revised without treating revision as humiliation. Research in psychology suggests that reducing threats to self worth can make people more receptive to conflicting evidence, while studies of intellectual humility consistently link it with greater willingness to recognise mistakes and update beliefs. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Using self affirmation to increase intellectual humility in debateby PHP Hanel · 2023 · Cited by 45 — We tested whether intellectual h... Why beliefs become identity signals Not every opinion becomes part of the self. Many are revised effortlessly: changing a restaurant preference or learning a new shortcut rarely threatens anyone's sense of identity. Difficulty arises when a belief signals something broader. A belief often comes to represent: membership of a political, professional or cultural community; evidence of intelligence or expertise; loyalty to friends, colleagues or family; moral character or personal values; years of previous effort or public commitment. Once this happens, criticism of the belief is easily experienced as criticism of the person. Instead of asking, \"Is this evidence convincing?\", the mind begins asking, \"What would it mean about me if this were true?\" This explains why debates frequently become less responsive to evidence as they become more public. A mistaken claim made privately can be quietly abandoned. The same claim repeated before colleagues, followers or family members becomes much harder to retract because status is now involved. Why status threats block updating The obstacle is not simply pride. Several psychological mechanisms work together. Protecting the self rather than the belief Research on self affirmation theory argues that people have a strong motivation to preserve a coherent sense of themselves as competent and morally adequate. Information that threatens this self image often produces defensive reasoning rather than careful evaluation. Experiments have shown that when participants first affirm important personal values unrelated to the disputed issue, they become more willing to engage openly with threatening information and display greater intellectual humility during discussion. The affirmation does not make people indifferent to truth. Instead, it reduces the feeling that accepting evidence would diminish their worth. [PMC+2labs.psych.ucsb.edu] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Using self affirmation to increase intellectual humility in debateby PHP Hanel · 2023 · Cited by 45 — We tested whether intellectual h... Public consistency People also care about appearing consistent. Once a position has been publicly defended, reversing course risks looking unreliable or incompetent. This creates an unfortunate incentive: the more visible a mistaken opinion becomes, the more psychological pressure exists to defend it. Escalating commitment can therefore reflect reputation management as much as genuine conviction. Growth Starts With Questioning Yourself: The Art Of Changing Your Mind w/ Charles McCaughan Channel: Phil Svitek Open on YouTube Group belonging Many beliefs function as social signals. They communicate \"who I am\" as much as \"what I think.\" Changing such beliefs may therefore appear to threaten relationships rather than merely ideas. Someone may worry about disappointing friends, losing professional credibility or appearing disloyal, even when privately persuaded by new evidence. In these situations, evidence competes with social incentives rather than replacing them. Why changing your mind can increase rather than reduce credibility People often assume that confidence requires never admitting error. Yet in many expert communities the opposite norm operates. Scientists routinely revise explanations after new experiments. Engineers modify designs after failures. Doctors update treatments as clinical evidence improves. Financial analysts alter forecasts when markets change. In each case, refusing to update would usually damage credibility more than acknowledging revision. The distinction lies between inconsistency and responsiveness. A person who changes views randomly appears unreliable. A person who explains exactly what evidence altered their judgement often appears more trustworthy because their reasoning follows observable standards rather than personal attachment. The strongest signal is not \"I was always right.\" It is \"I know why I believed that then, and I know why I believe something different now.\" Ways to revise a view without self erasure Changing your mind does not require pretending your previous reasoning was foolish. Several habits reduce the status cost of updating. Identify with the process, not the conclusion Instead of defining yourself by particular opinions, define yourself by the quality of your reasoning. Compare these identities: Identity tied to conclusions: \"I am the person who believes X.\" Identity tied to method: \"I am the kind of person who follows good evidence.\" The second identity survives belief revision intact. Explain what changed People often respect revisions more when they understand the reason. Rather than saying: \"I completely reversed my position.\" A clearer explanation is: \"The new evidence answered the question that originally made me sceptical.\" This presents updating as disciplined reasoning instead of weakness. Intellectual humility: the rewards of being willing to change your mind Channel: The Conversation Open on YouTube Revise gradually when appropriate Evidence rarely requires dramatic reversals overnight. Useful language includes: \"I'm less certain than I was.\" \"I've updated my confidence.\" \"I think the balance of evidence has shifted.\" \"Part of my earlier view still seems right, but not all of it.\" Such statements better reflect how evidence usually accumulates. Separate identity from any single mistake Research on self affirmation suggests that people become less defensive when reminded that their worth depends on many valued aspects of themselves rather than one threatened belief. A disagreement about one issue does not define the entire person. labs.psych.ucsb.edu+2LSA Technology Services [labs.psych.ucsb.edu] labs.psych.ucsb.edu sherman.lokande.muller.cohen.2021.sa Self‑Affirmation Interventionsby DK Sherman · 2021 · Cited by 72 — The earliest research in self affirmation theory showed that people c... Common traps that make updating harder Several habits unintentionally increase the personal cost of changing position. Making absolute claims . Statements such as \"Only an idiot believes otherwise\" leave little room for graceful revision. Treating disagreement as moral failure. If every disagreement becomes a judgement about character, changing sides feels like confessing guilt. Attaching self esteem to always being correct. No one can maintain this standard indefinitely. Confusing confidence with certainty. High confidence should still allow the possibility of future correction. Interpreting every concession as defeat. Productive reasoning often consists of many partial adjustments rather than dramatic reversals. Each trap makes beliefs harder to update because it increases the personal penalty attached to being wrong. A healthier model of intellectual confidence Intellectual humility does not require becoming tentative about everything or abandoning firm convictions whenever disagreement appears. It requires changing the relationship between personal status and factual accuracy. A useful mental model is that beliefs are working hypotheses rather than permanent possessions. Some will survive decades of scrutiny. Others will need refinement or replacement. Success lies not in avoiding revision but in making revision proportionate to the evidence. When"},{"title":"Confidence","url":"/confidence-03a0bf/","description":"Good judgement improves when confidence is scaled to the quality, independence and directness of the evidence behind a claim.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Humility","level":3,"text":"Confidence How Confident Should You Really Be? Confidence Confidence How Confident Should You Really Be? Good judgement improves when confidence is scaled to the quality, independence and directness of the evidence behind a claim. Good judgement improves when confidence is scaled to the quality, independence and directness of the evidence behind a claim. mechanism Why confidence should have a size Evidence types that deserve different weight Common signs of overconfidence and underconfidence improving your thinking and analytical skills humility confidence Introduction Good judgement does not require perfect certainty. It requires matching your level of confidence to the strength of the evidence available. That means being highly confident when multiple, independent, direct sources point to the same conclusion, moderately confident when the evidence is suggestive but incomplete, and cautious when the evidence is weak, indirect or conflicting. This ability—often called confidence calibration —is one of the practical foundations of intellectual humility. It allows you to act before certainty while avoiding both unwarranted certainty and unnecessary hesitation. Research across psychology, decision science and medicine consistently finds that people often miscalibrate their confidence, becoming either more certain or less certain than the evidence justifies. [PMC+2The American Journal of Medicine] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAn Experimental Study of Overconfidence Biases in Young Decision This strong alignment supports the notion that overconfidence is associated with poorer... Rather than asking, \"Am I confident?\", a better question is, \"How much confidence does this evidence deserve?\" That shift changes confidence from a personality trait into a judgement that can be adjusted as evidence improves or weakens. Why confidence should have a size Confidence is not an all or nothing state. It is better understood as a scale. The ideal is for your certainty to increase or decrease in proportion to the reliability, independence and relevance of the evidence supporting a claim. A useful way to think about this is that confidence should earn its way upwards. Every increase in confidence should be backed by something concrete, such as an additional independent source, a stronger method, direct observation instead of inference, or repeated successful predictions. Calibration researchers define good judgement as a close match between expressed confidence and actual accuracy. If someone says they are 80% confident across many comparable judgements, roughly 80% of those judgements should eventually prove correct. Perfect calibration is difficult, but it is a practical target because it reduces predictable errors in decision making. [Wiley Online Library+2American Psychological Association] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryCalibration Feedback With the Practical Scoring Rule Does...by M Martin · 2025 · Cited by 1 — We examined the effect... This perspective also explains why intellectual humility is not the same as constant doubt. A calibrated thinker may express very high confidence when the evidence genuinely warrants it. Humility lies in allowing confidence to track evidence rather than identity , emotion or social pressure . Evidence types that deserve different weight Not all evidence deserves equal confidence. Good judgement depends on recognising which forms of evidence should move your confidence substantially and which should only nudge it. Evidence characteristicAppropriate effect on confidenceMultiple independent sources reaching the same conclusionLarge increaseDirect measurements or observationsLarger increase than indirect inferenceEvidence replicated across different settingsLarger increaseSingle anecdote or personal experienceSmall increaseClaims from interested parties with incentives to persuadeLimited increase until independently verifiedEvidence that conflicts across high quality sourcesReduced confidence until resolved Several practical questions help determine how much weight evidence deserves: How direct is it? Seeing something measured directly is generally stronger than relying on several layers of interpretation. How independent is it? Ten reports that all originate from the same source do not provide ten independent pieces of evidence. How consistent is it? Agreement across unrelated studies or observers usually deserves more confidence than isolated findings. How well has it survived attempts to disprove it? Claims repeatedly tested without being overturned deserve greater confidence than claims that have never faced serious scrutiny. In evidence based medicine, frameworks such as GRADE distinguish between stronger and weaker evidence by considering study design, consistency, directness and risk of bias. Although developed for healthcare, the underlying principle applies broadly: confidence should reflect evidence quality, not simply evidence quantity. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Calibrate your confidence in research findings: A tutorial on...April 24, 2020 — 24 Apr 2020 — This article provides an acce... Published: April 24, 2020 Overconfidence Bias Psychology of Decision Making Channel: Sociologylearners Open on YouTube Common signs of overconfidence and underconfidence Poor calibration can fail in two directions. Most discussions focus on overconfidence , but excessive caution also reduces judgement quality. Overconfidence Overconfidence appears when certainty grows faster than the evidence supports. Research distinguishes several forms, including overestimating one's performance, believing one performs better than others, or expressing unwarranted precision about uncertain conclusions. [P.J. Healy] healy.econ.ohio state.edu Moore Healy Trouble With Overconfidence The Trouble With Overconfidence P.J. HealyJune 30, 2008 — by DA Moore · 2008 · Cited by 3885 — Experimental evidence shows that re... Published: June 30, 2008 Common warning signs include: Speaking in absolutes despite limited evidence. Treating confidence itself as proof. Ignoring missing information because existing evidence feels persuasive. Becoming resistant to updating after new contradictory evidence appears. Confusing expertise in one area with expertise in another. Studies also suggest that overconfidence is often fuelled by neglecting what is unknown . People naturally pay attention to the evidence they possess while overlooking information they have not yet sought or may never have encountered. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Known Unknowns: A Critical Determinant of Confidence...We propose that an important determinant of judged confidence is the... Underconfidence Underconfidence receives less attention but can also impair thinking. Someone may hesitate to act despite strong, consistent evidence or repeatedly discount their own well supported conclusions. Signs include: Demanding impossible levels of certainty before deciding. Giving weak evidence and strong evidence nearly identical confidence. Constantly reopening settled questions without new information. Assuming every disagreement means the evidence is evenly balanced. Research on calibration shows that different tasks can produce different patterns. Difficult tasks often encourage overconfidence about performance, while easier tasks sometimes produce underestimation. The goal is therefore not maximum caution but accurate calibration. [P.J. Healy] healy.econ.ohio state.edu Moore Healy Trouble With Overconfidence The Trouble With Overconfidence P.J. HealyJune 30, 2008 — by DA Moore · 2008 · Cited by 3885 — Experimental evidence shows that re... Published: June 30, 2008 A practical way to scale confidence Instead of thinking in vague terms such as \"certain\" or \"unsure\", deliberately tie confidence to identifiable properties of the evidence. One practical framework is: Low confidence: evidence is sparse, indirect, unverified or largely speculative. Moderate confidence: several credible sources support the claim, but important uncertainties remain. High confidence: multiple independent, direct and consistent sources converge, and plausible alternatives have been carefully examined. Very high confidence: conclusions have repeatedly survived testing, prediction and replication over time. The precise percentages matter less than consistency. The important habit is explaining why your confidence sits where it does. For example: \"I'm only slightly confident because this relies on a single witness.\" \"I'm reasonably confident because three independent studies reached similar conclusions.\" \"I'm highly confident because direct measurements have been replicated by different groups.\" These explanations make confidence transparent and easier to revise when new evidence appears. Why you don't need certainty to be influential Channel: Julia Galef Open on YouTube Updating confidence as evidence changes Calibration is dynamic rather than fixed. New evidence should change confidence by an amount"},{"title":"Core Beliefs","url":"/core-beliefs/","description":"People can be intellectually humble in general while still becoming defensive about beliefs tied to identity or moral commitment.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Humility","level":3,"text":"Core Beliefs Why Some Beliefs Resist Revision Core Beliefs Core Beliefs Why Some Beliefs Resist Revision People can be intellectually humble in general while still becoming defensive about beliefs tied to identity or moral commitment. People can be intellectually humble in general while still becoming defensive about beliefs tied to identity or moral commitment. population context Peripheral beliefs versus identity linked beliefs Why central commitments update more slowly How to notice defensiveness before it steers reasoning improving your thinking and analytical skills humility core beliefs Introduction Most people can be open minded about many everyday opinions while remaining surprisingly resistant to changing a small number of deeply held beliefs. The difference is not simply how much evidence supports a belief. It is often about what the belief represents. When a claim becomes connected to personal identity, moral values, trusted communities or a sense of purpose, revising it can feel less like correcting an error and more like losing part of oneself. Intellectual humility is especially valuable in these situations because it separates the question \"Is this belief true?\" from \"What does changing my mind say about me?\" Research on intellectual humility, motivated reasoning and identity protective cognition consistently suggests that people are more willing to update beliefs when doing so does not threaten their social identity or self concept. [PMC+2John Templeton Foundation] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... Peripheral Beliefs Versus Identity Linked Beliefs Not all beliefs occupy the same place in a person's mental world. Peripheral beliefs tend to concern matters with little emotional or social importance. You might easily revise an opinion about the quickest route to work, the best way to prepare a meal or the release date of a film after seeing reliable evidence. Little else changes when those beliefs change. Identity linked beliefs are different. They are woven into answers to questions such as: Who am I? Which people are \"my\" people? [dukespace.lib.duke.edu] dukespace.lib.duke.edu Four studies examined intellectual humility the degree to which people recognize that their beliefs might be wrong. Using a new Intellect... Which authorities do I trust? What kind of moral person do I believe myself to be? Changing these beliefs may imply admitting that a trusted group was mistaken, acknowledging that a long held cause was flawed or accepting that previous decisions rested on false assumptions . Because the personal costs appear much larger, evidence alone often has less influence than people expect. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com Misconceptions, Misinformation, and the Logic of Identity ...by DM Kahan · 2017 · Cited by 593 — The incidence and impact of misconc... Importantly, this does not mean identity linked beliefs are necessarily false. Many central commitments are well supported by evidence. The point is that beliefs connected to identity tend to be processed differently from beliefs that carry few personal consequences. Why Central Commitments Update More Slowly Resistance to updating is usually not caused by a single psychological bias. Several mechanisms often reinforce one another. Identity protection changes how evidence feels Research on identity protective cognition argues that people often evaluate information in ways that preserve membership within valued groups. Rather than consciously deciding to ignore evidence, they may find supportive evidence more convincing, scrutinise contradictory evidence more aggressively or trust sources associated with their own community more readily. [SSRN+2informalscience.org] papers.ssrn.com Misconceptions, Misinformation, and the Logic of Identity ...by DM Kahan · 2017 · Cited by 593 — The incidence and impact of misconc... This helps explain why intelligent, well informed people sometimes become more polarised rather than converging on shared conclusions. Identity Protective Cognition: Why We Ignore Facts That Threaten Our Identity Channel: Rational Ruminations Open on YouTube Moral commitments create additional resistance Beliefs tied to moral convictions often become difficult to revise because changing them appears to involve compromising one's character rather than merely updating factual knowledge. For example, if someone interprets a disagreement as a challenge to whether they are compassionate, responsible or loyal, the discussion quickly becomes about identity instead of evidence. Defensive reasoning becomes more likely because protecting self respect feels more urgent than evaluating competing claims . Social relationships raise the stakes Beliefs rarely exist in isolation. They are often reinforced by families, professional communities, religious organisations, political groups or friendship networks. Changing one's mind can therefore involve practical social costs: disappointing respected mentors creating conflict within close relationships losing status within a community becoming uncertain about where one belongs These costs can make even careful thinkers hesitate before reconsidering central assumptions. Confidence and identity are not the same A common misunderstanding is that strong confidence necessarily prevents intellectual humility. In reality, people may hold very confident beliefs while remaining able to specify what evidence would change their minds. The greater obstacle is often not confidence itself but identity fusion—the point at which disagreement feels like a personal attack rather than an opportunity to learn. Research on intellectual humility consistently distinguishes confidence from the willingness to acknowledge one's own fallibility. [PMC+2John Templeton Foundation] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... Why Better Evidence Alone Often Fails Many discussions assume that disagreement exists because one side lacks information. Although this is sometimes true, it is often incomplete. Studies of motivated reasoning suggest that people actively interpret information through existing beliefs and goals rather than simply absorbing facts. Evidence that supports existing commitments may receive relatively little scrutiny, while contradictory evidence is examined for flaws, alternative explanations or reasons to dismiss the source. [Taylor & Francis Online+2Springer] tandfonline.com Reasoning differently about information supporting Taylor & Francis OnlineThe skeptical import of motivated reasoning: a closer look...by M van Doorn · 2024 · Cited by 16 — Central to man... This helps explain why simply providing additional facts frequently produces disappointing results when beliefs are closely tied to identity. It also explains why debates sometimes become increasingly emotional despite increasing amounts of evidence: participants are not merely evaluating information but protecting different understandings of themselves and the groups they trust. When beliefs become identities, truth seeking becomes hard Channel: Rational Animations Open on YouTube How to Notice Defensiveness Before It Steers Reasoning The most useful skill is not eliminating defensiveness entirely. It is recognising it early enough that it does not quietly take control of judgement. Several signals commonly indicate that identity may be driving reasoning: You feel personally criticised before identifying exactly what factual claim is being challenged. You become much more interested in finding weaknesses in opposing evidence than in evaluating your own assumptions. You dismiss a source primarily because it belongs to an opposing group without first considering its actual evidence. You notice an unusually strong emotional reaction— anger , embarrassment or anxiety—before you have finished examining the argument. You find yourself thinking, \"People like us don't believe that,\" rather than asking whether the claim itself is accurate. None of these signs proves that your conclusion is wrong. They simply indicate that identity concerns may be influencing how evidence is being processed. Practising Intellectual Humility Without Losing Conviction Recognising identity linked beliefs does not require treating every opinion as equally credible or abandoning firmly supported conclusions. Instead, intellectual humility encourages habits that reduce unnecessary defensiveness: [drcharlesmrusso.substack.com] drcharlesmrusso.substack.com intellectual humility Charles M. Russo The Critical ThinkerIntellectual humility (IH) doesn't mean weak thinking; it means robust thinking: recognizing that... Separate the evidence from the person. Critiquing a claim"},{"title":"Cynicism","url":"/cynicism/","description":"Cynicism can look smart, but it weakens analysis when distrust becomes automatic and evidence can no longer correct belief.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Humility","level":3,"text":"Cynicism When Skepticism Turns Into a Trap Cynicism Cynicism When Skepticism Turns Into a Trap Cynicism can look smart, but it weakens analysis when distrust becomes automatic and evidence can no longer correct belief. Cynicism can look smart, but it weakens analysis when distrust becomes automatic and evidence can no longer correct belief. mechanism Why cynicism feels intellectually safe How blanket distrust becomes unfalsifiable What humble skepticism asks instead improving your thinking and analytical skills humility cynicism Introduction Intellectual humility depends on asking hard questions without assuming that every claim, institution or person is deceptive. That distinction separates humble skepticism from cynicism . Both can appear intellectually rigorous because both reject naïve acceptance, but they work very differently. Humble skepticism treats beliefs as provisional and lets stronger evidence replace weaker evidence. Cynicism begins from distrust and often interprets new information through that distrust, making it difficult for evidence to change its conclusions. Research on intellectual humility consistently associates humility with better evidence evaluation and a greater willingness to revise beliefs, whereas studies of cynicism suggest that chronic distrust can become a self reinforcing pattern rather than a reliable path to truth. [PMC+2Advances.in] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... Within the broader goal of improving analytical thinking, the key question is not whether to question claims , but how to question them. The most reliable thinking remains open to correction without becoming permanently suspicious. Why Cynicism Feels Intellectually Safe Cynicism offers a powerful psychological attraction because it appears to reduce the risk of being deceived. If you assume that politicians always lie, companies always manipulate, experts always protect their interests, or strangers always act selfishly, you will rarely be surprised by bad behaviour. That defensive posture creates an illusion of intellectual sophistication. Many cultures reinforce the stereotype that the most intelligent observer is the one who sees hidden motives everywhere. Yet psychological research suggests that people commonly overestimate the connection between cynicism and intelligence. Large cross national studies have found that although many people believe cynics are more competent and realistic, cynical individuals generally do not perform better on measures of cognitive ability, and often perform worse. This pattern has been called the \"cynical genius illusion\". [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Cynical Genius Illusion: Exploring and Debunking Lay...by O Stavrova · 2018 · Cited by 74 — Cynicism refers to a negative apprais... Part of cynicism's appeal comes from its emotional payoff: It protects against disappointment. It provides simple explanations for complex events. It avoids the vulnerability involved in trusting others. It creates a sense of being harder to manipulate than everyone else. These benefits are psychologically real even when the underlying beliefs are inaccurate. The emotional reward therefore helps explain why cynical worldviews can persist despite contradictory evidence. The Cynical Genius Illusion Dr. Jamil Zaki Channel: After Skool Open on YouTube How Blanket Distrust Becomes Unfalsifiable Healthy reasoning requires beliefs that can be disproved. Cynicism often escapes this requirement. Consider the claim: \"Every expert is serving hidden interests.\" No amount of honest behaviour by experts disproves this belief. Honest actions are reinterpreted as sophisticated deception, incomplete information, or strategic public relations. Every observation becomes further confirmation. This creates an unfalsifiable position. Instead of evaluating evidence, cynicism changes the interpretation of evidence so that the conclusion never changes. Several common reasoning patterns illustrate this trap: Selective confirmation. Evidence supporting distrust is remembered, while counterexamples are dismissed as exceptions. Motive substitution. Arguments are ignored in favour of presumed intentions. Moving standards of proof. Evidence sufficient to reject one claim suddenly becomes insufficient when it contradicts cynical expectations. Asymmetric updating. Negative evidence rapidly changes beliefs, whereas positive evidence is discounted. These mechanisms make cynicism resistant to correction. The issue is not simply that cynical people distrust more often; it is that distrust itself becomes insulated from evidence. Research distinguishing skepticism from cynicism repeatedly finds this difference. Skepticism focuses on evaluating the quality of claims, whereas cynicism assumes bad motives before evaluating the claims themselves. [ResearchGate+2International Journal of Communication] researchgate.net Asymmetric negative influence of cynicism and skepticismSkepticism is related to cognitive responses to the communication con... Cynicism vs. Skepticism: Why One Makes You Easier to Manipulate Channel: Allen Stafford — Think Sharper Open on YouTube What Humble Skepticism Asks Instead Humble skepticism begins from uncertainty rather than certainty. Instead of asking: \"Why should I assume they're lying?\" it asks: \"What evidence would increase or decrease my confidence?\" This difference sounds subtle but changes the entire reasoning process. A humble skeptic separates several questions that cynicism tends to merge: Is the evidence reliable? Are there independent sources? What incentives might bias the source? How strong is the evidence despite those incentives? What evidence would make me change my view? The crucial feature is that every answer remains revisable. Intellectual humility therefore does not require trusting everyone equally. It requires calibrating trust according to evidence rather than treating distrust as the default. Reviews of intellectual humility describe it as recognising the limits of one's own knowledge while remaining responsive to stronger evidence, an orientation associated with better resistance to misinformation and more accurate evidence evaluation. [PMC+2Advances.in] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... Historical Contrast: Suspicion Versus Inquiry The history of science illustrates why skepticism and cynicism should not be confused. [medium.com] medium.com Science and Skepticism vs Cynicism by Tony TonevWhat does this have to do with cynicism? While a gullible person always believes, a cyn... Scientific skepticism developed methods designed to expose error through replication, transparent evidence, peer criticism and continual testing . Scientists often assume that hypotheses may be wrong, but they do not assume that every researcher is acting dishonestly. Institutions include safeguards because bias exists, not because fraud is presumed everywhere. The same distinction appears in journalism and historical scholarship. Responsible investigators scrutinise documents, compare independent witnesses and examine incentives without concluding beforehand that every source is fabricated. Evidence determines confidence rather than confirming a predetermined attitude. Modern communication research similarly distinguishes constructive skepticism—which critically evaluates competence and evidence—from cynicism, which attributes malevolent motives as the default explanation. [International Journal of Communication] ijoc.org by O Quiring · 2021 · Cited by 100 — These considerations resemble the distinction between skepticism and cynicism in previous research... Why Cynicism Can Weaken Analysis At first glance, cynicism appears demanding because it rejects easy answers. In practice , it often reduces analytical effort. If every institution is corrupt, every statistic manipulated and every disagreement explained by hidden motives, there is little reason to compare competing evidence. Investigation stops once suspicion is established. This can produce several analytical failures: Genuine expertise is ignored alongside genuine misinformation. Reliable evidence is treated as no more credible than weak evidence . Nuanced explanations are replaced with single cause stories about corruption or self interest. Unexpected facts become psychologically difficult to accept. Ironically, research has also found that higher cynicism does not necessarily protect against false beliefs. Studies examining conspiracy theories, paranormal beliefs and other unsupported claims have reported that cynicism can positively predict endorsement of several kinds of unsubstantiated beliefs. Blanket distrust"},{"title":"Objections","url":"/objections-0bad06/","description":"Looking for contrary evidence is useful because it tests confidence, not because every claim deserves equal weight.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Humility","level":3,"text":"Objections How to Test a Belief Fairly Objections Objections How to Test a Belief Fairly Looking for contrary evidence is useful because it tests confidence, not because every claim deserves equal weight. Looking for contrary evidence is useful because it tests confidence, not because every claim deserves equal weight. policy intervention The difference between testing and surrendering When both sides framing misleads How to use strong objections without false balance improving your thinking and analytical skills humility objections Introduction Testing your own beliefs against contrary evidence is one of the most reliable ways to improve judgement. The goal, however, is not to perform neutrality by acting as though every position deserves equal confidence. Instead, the aim is to ask whether your current belief survives the strongest available objections. Good thinking distinguishes between actively searching for disconfirming evidence and pretending that all claims are equally plausible . This distinction sits at the heart of intellectual humility. Humility means recognising that you can be mistaken and deliberately looking for information that would reveal such mistakes . It does not require suspending confidence in conclusions that are already supported by strong, converging evidence. Research on intellectual humility consistently defines it as awareness of the limits of one's knowledge rather than indiscriminate doubt, while work in philosophy argues that humility and evidence based confidence can coexist. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... Why Testing a Belief Is Different from Surrendering It People often confuse openness to criticism with unwillingness to reach conclusions. These are different intellectual activities. Testing a belief asks: What evidence would genuinely weaken my conclusion? Have I examined the strongest objections? Would I recognise if reality contradicted my expectations? Surrendering a belief asks something else entirely: Should I reduce confidence simply because someone disagrees? The answer to the second question is often \"no\". Disagreement alone is not evidence that confidence should fall. What matters is the quality, independence and explanatory power of the opposing evidence. This distinction becomes especially important once evidence has accumulated unevenly. A well tested scientific explanation and a speculative alternative are not entitled to equal confidence simply because they occupy opposite sides of a debate. Intellectual humility requires openness to better evidence, not permanent indecision. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPredictors and consequences of intellectual humility by T Porter · 2022 · Cited by 359 — Intellectual humility involves acknowledging the limitations of one's knowledge and that one's bel... The Difference Between Looking for Objections and Giving Equal Weight The practical habit worth developing is not \"hear every side equally\" but \"hear the strongest criticism available.\" These approaches produce very different thinking. Testing beliefs fairlyFalse balanceSeeks the strongest counter evidenceGives equal attention to weak and strong claimsWeighs evidence by qualityWeighs positions by symmetryChanges confidence when warrantedTreats disagreement itself as evidenceDistinguishes uncertainty from settled knowledgeSuggests every issue remains equally open False balance often arises from a social instinct rather than an epistemic one. Presenting \"both sides\" can appear fair even when the available evidence is overwhelmingly one sided. In journalism this has long been recognised as a source of public misunderstanding in areas where expert consensus is strong, and the same mistake appears in everyday reasoning. Fairness therefore concerns how objections are evaluated , not whether every objection receives identical status. Consider the Opposite A Debiasing Strategy Channel: Kevin deLaplante Open on YouTube Why \"Consider the Opposite\" Works One of the most influential findings in judgement research is that simply telling people to \"be objective\" has relatively little effect. A more effective intervention is to ask them to actively construct the best case against their current conclusion. Classic experiments by Charles Lord, Mark Lepper and Elizabeth Preston showed that asking participants to \"consider the opposite\" reduced several forms of confirmation bias more effectively than general instructions to be fair or unbiased. Rather than merely encouraging neutrality, the intervention forced people to generate plausible alternative explanations and test whether their preferred interpretation was uniquely supported. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1163 — It is proposed that several biase... Subsequent research has extended this idea into other settings, including legal decision making, where structured \"consider the opposite\" procedures have been shown to improve the generation of alternative hypotheses during investigations. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Have you considered the opposite? A debiasing strategy...14 Aug 2021 — Drawing on Lord and colleagues work (1984), this study examines the potential debiasing effect of c... The important lesson is that good reasoning does not emerge from suppressing conviction. It emerges from deliberately exposing conviction to meaningful tests. When Both Sides Framing Misleads Not every disagreement deserves identical investigation. Imagine three situations: A colleague challenges your interpretation of a dataset with an alternative statistical explanation. A medical guideline is criticised using a handful of anecdotes . An established physical law is rejected without reproducible evidence. These situations all involve disagreement, but they do not create equal reasons to revise confidence. Evidence should be judged according to factors such as: methodological quality reproducibility explanatory power independence of sources consistency with broader evidence When these factors overwhelmingly favour one position, presenting both positions as equally credible creates a distorted picture rather than a balanced one. Intellectual humility therefore requires proportionality. Confidence should increase or decrease in proportion to evidence, not in proportion to the number of competing opinions. Strong Objections Are More Valuable Than Many Weak Ones Many people unintentionally protect their beliefs by engaging only with poor criticisms. Rejecting obviously weak arguments can create an illusion that the underlying belief has survived serious testing. It has not. A more demanding practice is to search for what philosophers sometimes call the strongest available objection. This means seeking critics who understand the position well enough to identify its genuine vulnerabilities rather than its caricatures. The process looks something like this: 1. State your own position as clearly as possible. 2. Identify the strongest published criticism. 3. Ask whether that criticism would change your judgement if true. 4. Examine whether existing evidence answers it. 5. Adjust confidence only if the objection genuinely weakens the evidence. This approach increases confidence when a belief survives demanding scrutiny, while also making genuine errors easier to detect. The Empty Cup Channel: WWShorter Open on YouTube Intellectual Humility Does Not Require Endless Open Mindedness One recent philosophical argument draws an important distinction between intellectual humility and open mindedness. The authors argue that there are situations—such as evaluating demonstrably false or extremist claims—where a person can remain intellectually humble without treating those claims as live possibilities. Humility may require acknowledging one's own limitations, avoiding unwarranted assumptions about other people, and remaining attentive to evidence. It does not require becoming willing to revise beliefs that are already justified by overwhelming evidence merely because someone asserts the opposite. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentIntellectual Humility Without Open Mindedness: How to...by K Peters · Cited by 8 — There are seve... This helps explain why humility is compatible with confidence. A person can honestly say: \"I recognise that I can be mistaken, but I have also examined the strongest objections and do not currently see evidence sufficient to overturn this conclusion.\" That statement expresses revisability without pretending uncertainty where the evidence does not support it. Practical Habits for Testing Beliefs Fairly The following habits strengthen reasoning without drifting into performative neutrality: Ask what would"},{"title":"Misinformation","url":"/misinformation-813a68/","description":"Recent research links intellectual humility with better separation of true and false claims, not with blanket disbelief.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Humility","level":3,"text":"Misinformation Does Humility Make You Harder to Fool? Misinformation Misinformation Does Humility Make You Harder to Fool? Recent research links intellectual humility with better separation of true and false claims, not with blanket disbelief. Recent research links intellectual humility with better separation of true and false claims, not with blanket disbelief. dataset evidence What misinformation discernment studies measure Why humility is not the same as gullibility How metacognitive insight improves source checks improving your thinking and analytical skills humility misinformation Introduction Does intellectual humility make you harder to fool? The balance of recent evidence suggests that it does—but not because intellectually humble people automatically distrust information. Instead, they appear to be better at distinguishing accurate from inaccurate claims , more aware of when they might be mistaken, and more willing to investigate uncertain information before accepting or sharing it. This is an important distinction for anyone trying to improve their thinking. The goal is not blanket scepticism, where every source is treated as equally unreliable, but better calibration: matching confidence to the quality of the evidence. This research fits naturally within the broader idea of intellectual humility without cynicism . False information spreads most easily when people are either excessively confident in their first impressions or so distrustful that they reject reliable evidence alongside unreliable claims. Studies increasingly suggest that intellectual humility helps avoid both errors by improving judgement rather than encouraging disbelief. [Advances.in+2Advances.in] advances.in Intellectual humility and misinformation receptivity A new meta analytic review demonstrates who is most resilient... What misinformation discernment studies actually measure Much discussion of misinformation assumes that the important question is whether someone \"believes fake news\". Modern research uses a more precise approach. Participants are typically shown a mixture of genuine and fabricated news headlines without being told which are which. Researchers then measure several separate abilities rather than treating every incorrect judgement as the same mistake. Common measures include: Truth discernment: how accurately someone separates true headlines from false ones. Response bias: whether someone has a general tendency to believe everything or dismiss everything regardless of evidence. Metacognitive accuracy: whether confidence rises when a judgement is correct and falls when it is mistaken. This distinction matters because two people may achieve similar overall accuracy for very different reasons. One might carefully evaluate evidence, while another simply rejects nearly everything. The second strategy may occasionally avoid believing false stories, but it also causes unnecessary rejection of true information. Recent experimental work involving news headline judgement tasks found that intellectual humility was associated with stronger truth discernment and better metacognitive insight, but not with a general tendency to label more headlines as false. In other words, humble participants were not merely more sceptical—they were better at telling the difference. [Advances.in] advances.in discernment of true and false... intellectual humility and pseudoscience beliefs, conspiratorial ideation, and susceptibility to fake ne... The Most Dangerous Cognitive Bias in Today’s Idiotic World Channel: PhiloNautica Open on YouTube Why humility is not the same as gullibility One common misconception is that admitting you could be wrong makes you easier to manipulate. The evidence points in almost the opposite direction. Intellectual humility does not ask people to lower their standards of evidence. Instead, it encourages recognising the possibility of error while continuing to evaluate claims critically. Someone high in intellectual humility may say: \"This looks convincing, but I want another source.\" \"I might be wrong, so I'll check.\" \"The evidence changed, so I'll revise my view.\" These responses differ sharply from gullibility, which accepts claims too readily, and from cynicism, which dismisses claims without evaluation. A 2024 meta analysis combining findings across multiple studies found that intellectual humility was associated with lower belief in misinformation overall, including fake news and conspiracy beliefs. The relationships were generally modest rather than dramatic, which is typical in psychological research, but they were consistent enough to suggest that humility provides a measurable protective advantage. The strongest behavioural association was with a greater willingness to investigate uncertain claims before deciding whether they were true. [Advances.in] advances.in Intellectual humility and misinformation receptivity A new meta analytic review demonstrates who is most resilient... Importantly, the same review found little evidence that humility simply makes people reject more information. Instead, it appears to encourage evidence seeking behaviours that improve judgement quality. [Advances.in] advances.in Intellectual humility and misinformation receptivity A new meta analytic review demonstrates who is most resilient... Fake News: Fact & Fiction Episode 6: How to be a critical thinker Channel: BBC Learning English Open on YouTube How metacognitive insight improves source checks Perhaps the most interesting finding is that intellectual humility appears closely connected with metacognition —thinking about the quality of one's own thinking. Researchers distinguish between making a correct judgement and knowing how likely that judgement is to be correct. Imagine reading two unfamiliar news stories. In one case, the evidence seems clear, several independent outlets agree, and the source has a strong reputation. High confidence may be justified. In the second case, the claim relies on anonymous social media posts, emotional language and no supporting documentation. Even if the conclusion eventually turns out to be right, low confidence is the better intellectual position until stronger evidence appears. Studies suggest intellectually humble individuals are better at matching confidence to actual accuracy. They are more likely to recognise when they are guessing and less likely to express unwarranted certainty. This metacognitive calibration reduces the risk that incorrect beliefs become entrenched simply because they were held confidently. [Advances.in] advances.in discernment of true and false... intellectual humility and pseudoscience beliefs, conspiratorial ideation, and susceptibility to fake ne... In practical terms, this often translates into behaviours such as: checking whether multiple independent sources agree; distinguishing original reporting from repeated commentary; noticing when confidence exceeds available evidence; delaying judgement until additional information becomes available. These habits improve information quality without requiring universal distrust. What the evidence says about investigating claims One consistent finding across studies is that intellectual humility predicts curiosity about evidence rather than passive acceptance. People scoring higher on intellectual humility report stronger intentions to: [ggsc.berkeley.edu] ggsc.berkeley.edu The authors of this study define intellectual humility as a “nonthreatening awareness of one's intellectual...Read more... investigate questionable claims; verify evidence before sharing information; engage with evidence based guidance; [par.nsf.gov] par.nsf.gov 10650198 intellectual humility misinformation receptivity meta analytic review views. Research has found that IH is correlated with less belief in misinformation, greater intentions to engage in evidence based behavi... avoid passing on information whose accuracy remains uncertain. Interestingly, these relationships are generally stronger than the relationship between humility and simply believing fewer false claims. This suggests that humility changes the process of reasoning as much as the final outcome. Instead of functioning like a built in lie detector, it encourages behaviours that make accurate conclusions more likely over time. [Advances.in] advances.in Intellectual humility and misinformation receptivity A new meta analytic review demonstrates who is most resilient... Important limits and unresolved questions The current evidence is encouraging but should not be overstated. Most observed relationships are statistically reliable but moderate in size. Intellectual humility is one influence among many. General reasoning ability, scientific literacy, media literacy, political identity , emotional investment, prior beliefs and social trust also affect susceptibility to misinformation. Researchers have also found that different questionnaires capture somewhat different aspects of intellectual humility. Some"},{"title":"Intuition","url":"/intuition/","description":"Intuition is useful for routine choices, but costly, emotional, or uncertain decisions often deserve slower checking.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Intuition When Should You Slow Down? Intuition Intuition When Should You Slow Down? Intuition is useful for routine choices, but costly, emotional, or uncertain decisions often deserve slower checking. Intuition is useful for routine choices, but costly, emotional, or uncertain decisions often deserve slower checking. mechanism Useful fast judgment Warning signs for slowing down Second pass thinking routines improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition Introduction Fast intuition is useful when the situation is familiar, feedback has been reliable, and the cost of being wrong is low. It helps you cross a road, read a friend’s mood, spot a routine work problem, or choose from options that are not very consequential. But fast judgement needs a second pass when the decision is costly, emotionally charged, unfamiliar, irreversible, or based on weak evidence . In those moments, the danger is not that intuition exists; it is that a first impression can feel like knowledge before it has earned that status. The practical skill is knowing when to interrupt yourself. Research on dual process thinking distinguishes quicker, automatic, pattern based processes from slower, more reflective ones, while also warning that the two are not always cleanly separable in real life. That nuance matters: the goal is not to worship “slow thinking” or distrust every gut feeling, but to build a trigger system for the cases where a quick answer may be overconfident, biased, or incomplete. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCDual Process Theory: Embodied and Predictive NIHby SC Bellini Leite · 2022 · Cited by 97 — Dual Process Theory is currently a popular theory for explaining why we show bounded... Fast judgement works best in “kind” environments Intuition is not magic. At its best, it is compressed experience: the mind recognises a pattern before it can fully explain the steps. Experienced firefighters, clinicians, pilots, teachers, athletes and craftspeople often notice cues that novices miss because repeated exposure has trained their attention. Gary Klein’s recognition primed decision model describes how experts in time pressured settings often do not compare many options from scratch; they recognise a plausible course of action, mentally simulate whether it will work, and adjust if it seems flawed. [Decision Skills] decisionskills.com Decision SkillsThe RPD ModelThe Recognition Primed Decision Model. The RPD model explains how people can make good decisions under diffic... That is why “trust your gut” is sometimes good advice. A nurse who has repeatedly seen patients deteriorate may sense that “something is wrong” before all the numbers make the case. A firefighter may feel that a building is unsafe before being able to list every structural cue. A musician may hear that a performance is dragging before calculating tempo. These are not irrational guesses; they are fast responses built from many encounters with similar situations. The catch is that expert intuition has boundary conditions. Daniel Kahneman and Gary Klein, often seen as representing different traditions in judgement research, reached a useful shared position: intuitive expertise is more trustworthy when the environment has enough regularity to be learned and when the person has had adequate practice with prompt, clear feedback. In low validity settings, where cues are noisy and feedback is delayed or ambiguous, confidence can grow faster than accuracy. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That distinction changes how you should treat a gut feeling. A restaurant chef’s instinct about whether onions are caramelising properly comes from a tight feedback loop: look, smell, taste, adjust. A manager’s instinct that a new market will “definitely take off” may come from far weaker evidence: selective memories, persuasive narratives, optimism, and a handful of visible successes. Both feel like intuition, but only one has been trained by repeated, corrective feedback. When familiarity is a trap Fast judgement is most dangerous when a situation looks familiar but is not actually the same as the pattern your mind has matched. This is the mechanism behind many everyday reasoning errors: the first interpretation becomes a frame, and later evidence is squeezed into it. In medicine, this is often discussed as premature closure: settling on a diagnosis too early and failing to keep plausible alternatives alive. Reviews of cognitive bias in clinical decision making identify premature closure and confirmation bias as recurring contributors to error: once a clinician forms an early impression, later information may be interpreted through that lens rather than used to reopen the question. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The same mechanism appears outside hospitals. A project leader sees a delay and immediately thinks, “The team is not working hard enough,” because that explanation was true on a previous project. A parent hears a teenager’s sharp tone and assumes disrespect, when the real issue is anxiety or exhaustion. An investor sees a familiar chart pattern and treats it as predictive, even though the wider conditions have changed. The mind saves effort by matching patterns; the risk is that it may match the wrong one. A useful warning sign is the phrase “this is just like…”. Sometimes that comparison is genuinely informative. But it deserves a check when the stakes are high, the evidence is thin, or the current case differs in important ways from the remembered one. Second pass thinking asks: What exactly is similar? What is different? What would I expect to see if my first interpretation were wrong? Warning signs for slowing down The point of a second pass is not to make every decision laborious. It is to reserve slower thinking for situations where fast judgement is more likely to misfire. The following signals are especially useful. The decision is costly, irreversible, or hard to repair. A quick instinct about what to eat for lunch rarely needs scrutiny. A quick instinct about quitting a job, signing a contract, firing someone, making a large purchase, or changing medical treatment deserves more care. The higher the cost of error, the more valuable it becomes to separate the feeling of certainty from the evidence for certainty. You are emotionally activated. Anger , fear, embarrassment, attraction, resentment and urgency can all make one interpretation feel obvious. Emotion is information, but it is not a verdict. Strong feeling should prompt a pause: Am I seeing the situation clearly, or am I trying to relieve discomfort? The first answer is fluent and satisfying. The Cognitive Reflection Test became influential because it captures a common feature of reasoning errors: an appealing answer can arrive quickly and feel right, while the better answer requires inhibition and checking. Later research cautions that the test is not a pure measure of reflection alone, because it also involves numeracy and other abilities, but the underlying lesson remains useful: ease is not the same as accuracy. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org Open source on sjdm.org. The environment does not give clear feedback. Intuition improves when mistakes are visible and timely. It becomes less reliable when feedback is delayed, ambiguous, politicised, or never arrives. Hiring, long term strategy, financial forecasting and relationship decisions often fall into this harder category: people can feel experienced while learning weakly from the past. Other informed people disagree. Disagreement does not prove you are wrong, but it is a valuable cue that the case may contain ambiguity you are underweighting. A second pass should not become a popularity contest. It should ask whether the disagreement reveals missing evidence, different assumptions, or a risk you have not priced in. You are under time pressure created by someone else. Genuine emergencies require rapid action. Manufactured urgency often benefits the person applying pressure. Sales deadlines, social pressure, public arguments and online outrage can all push the mind towards premature commitment. The RPD Model: An Intro to Recognition Primed Decision Making Channel: DecisionSkills Open on YouTube What a second pass actually does Slowing down is often misunderstood as “thinking harder”. That is too vague. A useful second pass performs specific jobs: it widens the frame, tests the first answer, searches for disconfirming evidence, and reduces avoidable variation in judgement. One job is to check whether the decision is being driven by bias. Bias means a judgement is tilted in a particular direction: towards optimism, fear, self protection, loyalty, familiarity, recent events, or a preferred conclusion. Another job is to reduce noise: unwanted variability in judgement. Two people, or the same person on two different days, may make different assessments of the same case because of mood, fatigue, framing or"},{"title":"Decision Hygiene","url":"/decision-hygiene/","description":"Structured criteria, independent scoring, and delayed final intuition can reduce bias and noisy variation in group decisions.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Intuition","level":3,"text":"Decision Hygiene How Groups Can Catch Bad Intuition Decision Hygiene Decision Hygiene How Groups Can Catch Bad Intuition Structured criteria, independent scoring, and delayed final intuition can reduce bias and noisy variation in group decisions. Structured criteria, independent scoring, and delayed final intuition can reduce bias and noisy variation in group decisions. policy intervention Bias versus noise in judgment Independent assessments before discussion Structured criteria for hiring, projects, and family decisions improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition decision hygiene Introduction When fast intuition needs a second pass, groups face a special problem: a room full of intelligent people can still reach poor conclusions. The issue is not only bias—systematic errors that push judgments in one direction—but also noise, the unwanted variation that causes different people, or even the same person at different times, to make inconsistent judgments. Research on decision hygiene argues that many group failures arise from process design rather than a lack of intelligence or integrity. Groups often improve their decisions not by trying harder to be objective, but by structuring how judgments are formed, compared, and combined. conversableeconomist.com+2Issues in Science and Technology [conversableeconomist.com] conversableeconomist.com interview with daniel kahneman noise and decision making Interview with Daniel Kahneman: Noise and Decision Making15 Apr 2022 — The structured interview breaks up the problems into dimensions, g... Decision hygiene refers to procedural safeguards that reduce both bias and noise. Common examples include independent assessments before discussion, explicit scoring criteria, delayed overall judgments, and the aggregation of multiple viewpoints. These methods are especially valuable in hiring, project selection, funding decisions, performance reviews, and major family choices where intuition is useful but should not be allowed to dominate unchecked. [Behavioral Scientist+2William Meller] behavioralscientist.org a conversation with daniel kahneman about noise It's not only noise reducing, but if you have an...Read more... Bias Versus Noise in Judgment Many people understand bias. If a hiring committee consistently favours candidates from a particular background, the error has a direction. Noise is different. It appears when equally qualified candidates receive very different evaluations from different interviewers, or when a project proposal receives dramatically different scores depending on who reviews it and when. Noise is often less visible than bias because inconsistency can look like normal disagreement. [The Uncertainty Project+2Jury Analyst] theuncertaintyproject.org They use an archery analogy to illustrate the difference.Read more The Uncertainty ProjectApplying Decision Hygiene to yield better judgment12 Apr 2024 — The authors establish that good judgment can be ha... This distinction matters because eliminating bias does not automatically eliminate noise. A group can be unbiased on average yet still make unreliable decisions because individual judgments vary unpredictably. Kahneman, Olivier Sibony, and Cass Sunstein argue that organisations frequently focus on fairness and prejudice while overlooking the equally important problem of inconsistent judgment. Issues in Science and Technology+2The Uncertainty Project [issues.org] issues.org Issues in Science and TechnologyInterview with Daniel KahnemanThe Nobel Prize winning psychologist discusses the “noise” that besets huma... The practical consequence is that two committees using identical information may reach different conclusions simply because members interpret evidence differently, emphasise different criteria, or are influenced by the order in which information appears. Decision hygiene seeks to reduce this variation by making judgment processes more consistent. [scholarworks.bgsu.edu+2Wiley Online Library] scholarworks.bgsu.edu Some assert that noise (i.e., unwanted variance) is the most neglected yet most important source of error in judgment.Read more... Book Talk: \"Noise A Flaw in Human Judgment\" (Kahneman, Sibony, Sunstein, Brockman) DLD All Stars Channel: DLD Conference Open on YouTube Why Discussion Can Corrupt Good Judgments Most groups assume discussion should begin immediately. Yet one of the strongest findings in decision hygiene research is that early discussion often contaminates information. When people exchange views before recording independent judgments, several effects can occur: Early speakers anchor the conversation. Senior or confident members gain disproportionate influence. Members suppress minority opinions. New information becomes filtered through emerging group narratives. Independent evidence is lost because participants converge too quickly. [Barron's+2The Decision Lab] barrons.com Barron's Daniel Kahneman Says Noise Is Wrecking Your Judgment Here's Why, and What to Do About It.March 28, 2024 — Daniel Kahneman, a renowned psychologist and behavioral economist, explored the conc... Kahneman has argued that meetings work best when participants arrive with pre formed individual judgments. Discussion should compare those judgments rather than create them from scratch. Otherwise, the group risks replacing multiple independent signals with a single socially constructed opinion. [Barron's] barrons.com Barron's Daniel Kahneman Says Noise Is Wrecking Your Judgment Here's Why, and What to Do About It.March 28, 2024 — Daniel Kahneman, a renowned psychologist and behavioral economist, explored the conc... Research on collective decision making supports this concern. Diverse groups can outperform individuals when they contribute genuinely independent perspectives. However, the benefits of diversity shrink when social influence causes rapid convergence before all information has been surfaced. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMaking better decisions in groups NIHby D Bang · 2017 · Cited by 256 — We highlight the advantages of group decision making in overcoming biases and searching the hy... Anonymizing Reviewers to Each Other in Peer Review Discussions: A Randomized Controlled Trial Channel: Peer Review Congress Open on YouTube Independent Assessments Before Discussion A core decision hygiene principle is simple: judge first, discuss second. Instead of opening with debate, each participant evaluates options independently. Scores, rankings, forecasts, or written assessments are collected before anyone sees other people's views. Only then does discussion begin. This approach produces several benefits: 1. Preserves information diversity. Independent judgments reveal genuine differences rather than socially influenced agreement. 2. Reduces authority effects. Senior members cannot shape every assessment before it is recorded. 3. Creates an audit trail. Groups can examine how discussion changed opinions. Noise by Daniel Kahneman, Olivier Sibony, and Cass R. Sunstein Channel: Little, Brown and Company Open on YouTube 4. Improves aggregation. Multiple independent judgments often outperform a single consensus judgment. Barron's+2William Meller The principle appears in many high stakes settings. Investment committees often collect forecasts before meetings. Forecasting tournaments require participants to submit initial probabilities independently. Academic peer review relies on separate reviews before reviewers see one another's comments. Even where discussion remains important, independence is preserved long enough to capture separate signals. WCER+2PLOS Anonymity can sometimes strengthen this effect. Studies of peer review and evaluation processes suggest that reducing status cues can lessen the influence of hierarchy and reputation on collective judgments, although anonymity alone does not solve every decision quality problem. PLOS+2PMC Structured Criteria for Hiring Decisions Hiring provides one of the clearest demonstrations of decision hygiene. Unstructured interviews often feel insightful because interviewers can follow intuition and explore whatever topics seem interesting. Yet research repeatedly finds that structured interviews tend to be more reliable predictors of performance. The reason is not that intuition disappears; rather, intuition is delayed until after evidence has been gathered systematically. conversableeconomist.com A decision hygiene hiring process typically involves: ... Defining key competencies in advance. Evaluating each competency separately. Using common questions across candidates. Recording scores before discussion. Delaying overall impressions until the end. conversableeconomist.com+2William Meller Kahneman has described structured interviewing as breaking a complex judgment into components, scoring each component independently, and postponing the global evaluation until all evidence has been reviewed. This sequencing reduces halo effects, where one strong or weak characteristic colours"},{"title":"Emotion Pause","url":"/emotion-pause/","description":"Strong feelings can carry useful information, but they also make one interpretation feel more obvious than it deserves.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Intuition","level":3,"text":"Emotion Pause Why Strong Feelings Need a Pause Emotion Pause Emotion Pause Why Strong Feelings Need a Pause Strong feelings can carry useful information, but they also make one interpretation feel more obvious than it deserves. Strong feelings can carry useful information, but they also make one interpretation feel more obvious than it deserves. population context Anger, fear, urgency, and narrowed interpretation Separating emotional data from a verdict A short pause routine for charged decisions improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition emotion pause Introduction Strong feelings are not a sign that thinking has failed. In many situations they carry useful information about goals, threats, values, relationships, or opportunities. The problem is that emotional activation can make one interpretation feel far more certain than the evidence warrants . When anger , fear, excitement, humiliation, or urgency rises sharply, the feeling itself becomes a cue that a second pass of thinking may be needed. Within the broader skill of knowing when fast intuition needs checking, emotional intensity is one of the most practical pause signals available. A decision made during a surge of emotion is not automatically wrong, but it is more likely to be based on a narrowed view of the situation, incomplete evidence, or a premature sense of certainty. Research across psychology and neuroscience shows that emotion influences attention, memory, perception, and judgement, often directing mental resources toward what feels most important at the moment. [Swiss Medical Weekly] smw.ch Swiss Medical WeeklyThe impact of emotion on perception, attention, memory...by T Brosch · 2013 · Cited by 638 — Emotion and cognition... Anger, Fear, Urgency, and Narrowed Interpretation One reason strong emotions deserve a pause is that they change what we notice. A long standing finding in psychology is that high emotional arousal tends to narrow attention. Under stress or intense activation, people focus more strongly on central cues and may overlook peripheral information. This can be useful in genuine emergencies, but it can also reduce awareness of context, alternatives, and disconfirming evidence . [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Threat But Not Arousal Narrows Attention: Evidence from Pupil...by H van Steenbergen · 2011 · Cited by 181 — According to Easterbrook... In everyday life, this narrowing often appears as a shift from possibility to certainty: An angry manager stops asking what happened and becomes convinced that someone is at fault. A worried investor stops evaluating probabilities and starts treating a feared outcome as inevitable. A partner in an argument interprets a single remark as proof of a larger pattern. A shopper facing a limited time offer feels that immediate action is necessary, even when delay would be harmless. The emotional state does not merely colour the judgement. It can shape which facts are noticed, remembered, or considered relevant in the first place. Emotion and cognition are deeply intertwined; emotions help organise attention and guide decision making rather than operating separately from thought. [Swiss Medical Weekly] smw.ch Swiss Medical WeeklyThe impact of emotion on perception, attention, memory...by T Brosch · 2013 · Cited by 638 — Emotion and cognition... Anger Creates a Sense of Obviousness Anger deserves special attention because it often feels clarifying. When people are angry, ambiguity becomes uncomfortable. Motives appear easier to infer, blame feels more justified, and action feels preferable to waiting. In some contexts anger can increase determination and goal directed behaviour, which is why it is not simply a destructive emotion. Yet the same confidence can create overcommitment to a single explanation. [Kellogg Insight] insight.kellogg.northwestern.edu Kellogg Insight How Anger Can Help Us Make Better Decisions Kellogg InsightHow Anger Can Help Us Make Better Decisions Kellogg InsightAug 1, 2019 — In a series of studies, Maimaran and colleagues... A useful question during anger is not, “Am I allowed to be angry?” It is, “What information am I currently ignoring because anger has made one story feel complete?” Fear Compresses Possibilities Into Threats Fear produces a different distortion. Research on risk perception shows that people often rely on emotional impressions when judging danger. This can be adaptive when rapid response is necessary, but it can also cause unlikely risks to feel overwhelmingly probable. The emotional experience of threat becomes part of the judgement itself. [eclass.hua.gr+2Frontiers] eclass.hua.gr Affect, Risk, and Decision Making March 28, 2012 — Risk is perceived and acted on in 2 fundamental ways. Risk as feelings refers to individuals' fast, instinctive, and int... Published: March 28, 2012 A fearful employee may interpret neutral feedback as a sign that dismissal is coming. A worried parent may treat a low probability danger as an immediate threat. A nervous investor may abandon a sound long term plan because recent losses feel intolerable. The feeling is real. The conclusion may not be. Elizabeth A. Phelps: The Impact of Everyday Emotion Channel: PsychologicalScience Open on YouTube Urgency Is Often Emotion in Disguise Many poor decisions are not made under anger or fear but under urgency. The internal message is usually some variation of: “I must answer now.” “I cannot leave this unresolved.” “If I wait, I will lose my chance.” Sometimes urgency reflects reality. More often, it reflects discomfort. The desire to end uncertainty can become stronger than the desire to make an accurate judgement. When a decision suddenly feels as though it cannot wait, that feeling alone is often a reason to ask whether the deadline is external or emotional. Separating Emotional Data From a Verdict The goal is not to suppress emotion. Strong feelings frequently contain information worth examining. Anger may signal a perceived injustice. Fear may highlight a genuine risk. Anxiety may reveal uncertainty that deserves investigation. Excitement may point toward opportunity. Emotional reactions evolved partly because they help people respond rapidly to important situations. [Temple University Beasley School of Law] law.temple.edu Emotions quickly condense an experience, and evaluate Temple University Beasley School of LawEmotional Decision Making: Hardwired and HelpfulSeptember 7, 2024 — 7 Sept 2024 — Decisions are ve... Published: September 7, 2024 The mistake is treating the emotion as a completed judgement rather than as data. Consider the difference: Emotional messagePremature verdict“Something feels unfair.”“They deliberately wronged me.”“This situation feels risky.”“This will definitely fail.”“I feel pressure .”“I must decide immediately.”“I am excited about this.”“This is obviously the best option.” The first column contains information. The second contains conclusions. Analytical thinking becomes easier when these two layers are separated. Instead of asking whether a feeling is justified, ask what the feeling might be signalling and what evidence would support or contradict the interpretation attached to it. This distinction aligns with research showing that emotions influence decisions in predictable ways without necessarily determining the correct choice. Emotions provide inputs into judgement, but they are not equivalent to judgement itself. [jenniferlerner.com] jenniferlerner.com Emotion and Decision Making Jennifer Lernerby JS Lerner · 2015 · Cited by 2 — A revolution in the science of emotion has emerged in the last few decades, with the po... A Short Pause Routine for Charged Decisions A pause does not need to be dramatic. The purpose is simply to create enough distance for alternative interpretations to become visible. Step 1: Label the Emotional State Use simple language. Angry Defensive Afraid Embarrassed Excited Pressured Resentful Research on emotional regulation consistently finds that recognising and identifying emotional states can improve self control and reduce impulsive reactions. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A systematic review of neural, cognitive, and clinical studies of...by Y Richard · 2022 · Cited by 95 — One study found that mindfuln... The label does not solve the problem. It prevents the emotion from remaining invisible. Are All Decisions Based on Emotions? Emotion vs. Cognition in Decision making Channel: Dr. Todd Grande Open on YouTube Step 2: Identify the Story Attached to the Feeling Finish the sentence: “I feel this way because I think…” Examples: “…because I think they disrespected me.” “…because I think I am about to fail.” “…because I think this opportunity will disappear.” This reveals the interpretation hidden inside the emotion. Step 3: Generate One Credible Alternative Not ten alternatives. One. If the current story is “They ignored my message because they do not care,” a credible alternative might be “They have not had time to reply.” If the current"},{"title":"Feedback Loops","url":"/feedback-loops/","description":"Gut feelings become more trustworthy when repeated practice is paired with fast, clear correction from the real world.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Intuition","level":3,"text":"Feedback Loops When Should You Trust Expert Intuition? Feedback Loops Feedback Loops When Should You Trust Expert Intuition? Gut feelings become more trustworthy when repeated practice is paired with fast, clear correction from the real world. Gut feelings become more trustworthy when repeated practice is paired with fast, clear correction from the real world. mechanism Kind versus wicked learning environments Practice, feedback, and calibrated confidence Everyday tests before trusting a gut feeling improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition feedback loops Introduction A useful rule for improving your thinking is that intuition should be trusted only after it has passed a feedback loop test. The feeling of certainty is not enough. What matters is whether repeated experience has given you accurate, corrective feedback that allows your brain to learn reliable patterns. Research on expertise shows that highly skilled intuition is real. Experienced firefighters, nurses, pilots, chess players, and craftspeople often recognise important signals before they can explain them. However, the same research shows that intuition becomes unreliable when the environment is noisy, unpredictable, or slow to provide feedback . The key question is not “Does this feel familiar?” but “Has this intuition been trained by reality?” [PubMed] nih.gov pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMedConditions for intuitive expertise: a failure to disagreeby D Kahneman · 2009 · Cited by 4040 — This article reports on an effort t... This distinction explains why some gut feelings deserve respect while others require a deliberate second pass. Dr Gary Klein: Decision Making under Uncertainty and the Role of Intuition Channel: AlphaMind Open on YouTube Kind vs. Wicked The Myth of Experience 1 Channel: Emre Soyer Open on YouTube When Should You Trust Expert Intuition? Daniel Kahneman and Gary Klein approached intuition from different traditions. Kahneman became famous for documenting judgement errors and cognitive biases, while Klein studied experts making rapid decisions under pressure . Despite their disagreements, they reached a notable point of consensus: expert intuition is trustworthy only under specific conditions. [PubMed] nih.gov pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMedConditions for intuitive expertise: a failure to disagreeby D Kahneman · 2009 · Cited by 4040 — This article reports on an effort t... Their shared answer centred on two requirements: The Myth of Experience: Kind and Wicked Learning Environments Channel: iluli by Mike Lamb Open on YouTube 1. The environment must contain stable patterns that can actually be learned. 2. The person must receive sufficient practice and clear feedback about whether their judgments were correct. PubMed+2LinkedIn Without these conditions, experience alone can create confidence without improving accuracy. Someone may spend twenty years making forecasts, hiring staff, or picking investments and still fail to develop reliable intuition if the feedback is weak or misleading. The feedback loop test therefore asks a simple question: Has reality repeatedly corrected this judgement in a way that allows learning? If the answer is unclear, caution is warranted. Kind Versus Wicked Learning Environments Psychologist Robin Hogarth described the difference between “kind” and “wicked” learning environments. This distinction helps explain why expertise develops rapidly in some domains and poorly in others. preview prod production.gurooproducer.com+2ResearchGate Kind environments teach accurate intuition A kind environment has several characteristics: Patterns are relatively stable. ... Cause and effect are connected. Feedback arrives quickly. Success and failure are visible. Similar situations occur repeatedly. preview prod production.gurooproducer.com+2LinkedIn Chess is a classic example. A player makes a move and quickly discovers whether it improved or weakened the position. Tennis provides similar feedback. The player sees the result immediately and can adjust on the next point. Over thousands of repetitions, the brain learns dependable patterns. Psychology Today Many forms of emergency response also contain enough regularity to build intuition. Experienced firefighters often recognise danger because previous situations repeatedly taught them which cues mattered. ResearchGate Wicked environments create misleading confidence A wicked environment looks very different: Feedback is delayed. Conditions for Intuitive ExpertiseKahneman and Klein (2009) show, for example, that intuitive expertise breaks down in\")... Outcomes are influenced by luck. Important information is hidden. ... Causes and effects are difficult to separate. Success can reinforce the wrong lesson. Driverless Crocodile+2YouTube Financial markets illustrate the problem. An investor may make a poor decision and still earn money because the market rises. Another may make a sound decision and lose money because of events beyond their control. The outcome alone does not clearly teach the correct lesson. ResearchGate Long term business forecasting often suffers from the same issue. Predictions may take years to resolve, and by the time results appear it can be difficult to identify which assumptions were right or wrong. The feedback loop is too weak to train intuition effectively. ResearchGate+2McKinsey & Company The danger is that people still accumulate experience in wicked environments. What they do not necessarily accumulate is expertise. Practice, Feedback, and Calibrated Confidence Many people assume that confidence signals competence. Research on expertise suggests the opposite relationship is often more useful: confidence becomes informative only when it has been calibrated against repeated feedback. PubMed Consider two professionals: A radiologist who reviews thousands of scans and later learns whether the diagnosis was correct. A commentator who makes broad predictions about future economic trends and rarely receives precise scorekeeping. Both may sound equally confident. Only one operates within a strong feedback system. This is why deliberate practice matters. Repetition alone is insufficient. Improvement comes from repetition combined with correction. A musician improves by hearing mistakes. A surgeon improves through outcome tracking and review. An athlete improves because performance is measured continuously. Feedback closes the loop between action and reality. merlin.rebrovic.net+2preview prod production.gurooproducer.com Kahneman and Klein argued that evaluating intuition requires evaluating the environment in which it was formed. The central question is not whether someone is experienced but whether their experience occurred in a setting that supports genuine learning. PubMed+2Semantic Scholar Why experience sometimes fails Several factors can prevent intuition from improving: Outcomes are rare, making learning slow. Feedback arrives months or years later. Success depends heavily on chance. People remember wins more vividly than mistakes. Incorrect beliefs are never systematically checked. ResearchGate+2Driverless Crocodile Under these conditions, confidence can increase while accuracy remains unchanged. The person feels wiser because they have accumulated stories, but the stories have not necessarily taught valid lessons. Everyday Tests Before Trusting a Gut Feeling Most decisions do not require extensive analysis. However, before relying on instinct in an important situation, it is worth running a few quick checks. Has this judgement been tested repeatedly? Ask whether your intuition comes from many comparable situations with known outcomes. A project manager who has overseen dozens of similar projects may have useful intuition about schedules. A first time founder predicting the future of a new market probably has much weaker grounds for instinctive certainty. Did feedback arrive clearly and quickly? The shorter the delay between judgement and outcome, the easier it is to learn. Cooking, sports, and many technical skills provide rapid correction. Strategic forecasting, politics, and long term investing often do not. preview prod production.gurooproducer.com Could luck explain the outcome? A correct prediction is not always evidence of skill. If a judgement succeeded once, ask whether the same reasoning would work repeatedly under similar conditions. One successful outcome may reflect chance rather than expertise. Is the environment stable? Patterns learned in one environment may fail when conditions change. A manager's instincts developed during rapid growth may become unreliable during recession. A clinician's experience with common cases may not apply to unusual presentations. Expertise is often more specific than people realise. ResearchGate Have you kept score? One of the simplest ways to improve intuition is to track predictions and decisions. Recording expectations before outcomes are known creates a personal feedback system. Over time, it becomes easier"},{"title":"False Familiarity","url":"/false-familiarity/","description":"A situation can feel familiar while hiding crucial differences that make the first pattern match misleading.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Intuition","level":3,"text":"False Familiarity When Familiar Situations Fool Your Judgment False Familiarity False Familiarity When Familiar Situations Fool Your Judgment A situation can feel familiar while hiding crucial differences that make the first pattern match misleading. A situation can feel familiar while hiding crucial differences that make the first pattern match misleading. mechanism Why the first comparison feels convincing Key differences that fast thinking misses Questions that break a misleading pattern match improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition false familiarity Introduction A dangerous mistake in high stakes decisions is not complete ignorance but false familiarity : the feeling that a new situation is essentially the same as one you have seen before when, in fact, crucial differences make the old pattern unreliable. Fast intuition depends on pattern recognition. Most of the time that is useful. The problem arises when familiarity creates an illusion of understanding and causes people to stop looking for disconfirming details. In medicine, business, investing, emergency response, and other consequential settings, this can lead to premature conclusions, overlooked risks, and costly errors. Research on intuition and expertise repeatedly finds that successful pattern recognition depends on whether the environment is predictable and whether decision makers receive reliable feedback. When those conditions are weak, familiarity can create confidence without accuracy. [PubMed+2Semantic Scholar] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Conditions for intuitive expertise: a failure to disagreeby D Kahneman · 2009 · Cited by 3941 — This article reports on an effort t... Why the First Comparison Feels Convincing False familiarity begins with a strength of human cognition. The brain is designed to simplify complex situations by matching them to known patterns. Instead of analysing every detail from scratch, it asks a rapid question: “What does this remind me of?” This shortcut is often effective because many everyday situations genuinely do resemble previous experiences. The difficulty is that similarity is not the same as equivalence. A situation can share a few highly visible features with an earlier case while differing in the details that actually determine the outcome. The sense of familiarity becomes especially persuasive under pressure . Time constraints, uncertainty, stress , and information overload all increase reliance on pattern recognition. In these conditions, the first plausible interpretation often feels not merely likely but obviously correct. Once that feeling appears, alternative explanations receive less attention. Studies of diagnostic reasoning identify this tendency as a major contributor to error because the initial framing of a problem shapes what information people notice and how they interpret later evidence. [BMJ Open+2PMC] bmjopen.bmj.com BMJ OpenIdentifying early warning signs for diagnostic errors in...by J Balla · 2012 · Cited by 60 — The findings of this study identify... Another reason false familiarity is convincing is that successful past experiences are memorable. A manager who previously solved a project delay by increasing accountability may instinctively apply the same explanation to a new delay. An investor who profited from a market pattern may assume the pattern remains predictive. A clinician who recently diagnosed a common illness may be more inclined to see the same illness again. The earlier success provides a compelling story, even when the underlying conditions have changed. [PMC+2Fitzgerald Health Education Associates] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive biases in diagnosis and decision making during...by CS Webster · 2021 · Cited by 80 — Cognitive bias is typically defined a... Evolve Webinar Series 2021 – Cognitive biases in clinical decision making Channel: RACP Open on YouTube Key Differences That Fast Thinking Misses The defining feature of false familiarity is not that the recognised pattern is completely wrong. It is that the pattern is incomplete. Fast thinking tends to focus on similarities while overlooking differences that may carry greater diagnostic value. Several types of differences are especially likely to be missed: Changed context. A strategy that worked in one market, organisation, or economic environment may fail when incentives, competitors, or regulations differ. Different base rates. A common explanation may become less likely when the surrounding circumstances change, even if the symptoms look similar. Atypical presentations. Important cases often resemble ordinary cases until a less obvious clue is recognised. Hidden interactions. Multiple factors can combine to produce outcomes that look familiar on the surface but arise from entirely different causes. Medical diagnosis provides a clear example. Experienced clinicians frequently use pattern recognition to identify routine conditions. However, reviews of diagnostic error consistently note that atypical cases can be mistaken for familiar ones. When practitioners settle too quickly on an initial diagnosis, they may fail to investigate alternatives that better explain the evidence. This phenomenon, often called premature closure, is repeatedly identified as a significant source of diagnostic error. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCclinical decision making in a case of aortic dissection by B Kumar · 2011 · Cited by 41 — Premature closure is a type of cognitive error in which the physician fails to consider reasonable a... During the COVID 19 pandemic, some clinicians encountered a particularly vivid version of this problem. Because COVID became highly salient, respiratory symptoms were often interpreted through that lens, occasionally delaying consideration of other conditions that shared similar features. The familiar pattern was not irrational; it was simply over applied. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by VR Ramnath · 2020 · Cited by 10 — Representativeness bias may label all patients with acute respiratory failure as having COVID 19... The same mechanism appears in organisational settings. Leaders often rely on analogies to previous successes. Yet strategic decisions frequently occur in environments that evolve rapidly and provide delayed feedback. Under those conditions, the resemblance between two situations can be much weaker than it first appears, making intuition less dependable than it feels. [McKinsey & Company+2PubMed] mckinsey.com strategic decisions when can you trust your gut McKinsey & CompanyStrategic decisions: When can you trust your gut?1 Mar 2010 — Nobel laureate Daniel Kahneman and psychologist Gary Klei... Why High Stakes Decisions Are Especially Vulnerable False familiarity becomes more dangerous as consequences increase. In low stakes situations, an incorrect pattern match may cause a minor inconvenience. In high stakes situations, the same mental shortcut can affect health, safety, finances, careers, or public policy. The decision maker often faces an uncomfortable combination of urgency and uncertainty. Ironically, these are precisely the conditions that encourage greater dependence on rapid pattern recognition. A second problem is that high stakes environments often provide poor feedback. When outcomes emerge months or years later, people may never learn which features of the situation truly mattered. Kahneman and Klein's work on intuitive expertise emphasises that reliable intuition develops most effectively when people operate in environments with regular patterns and timely, accurate feedback. Without those conditions, confidence can exceed actual predictive skill. [PubMed+2Semantic Scholar] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Conditions for intuitive expertise: a failure to disagreeby D Kahneman · 2009 · Cited by 3941 — This article reports on an effort t... This creates a subtle trap. The more experienced someone becomes, the larger their library of familiar patterns grows. Expertise is valuable, but it can also increase the temptation to classify new situations too quickly. The danger is not expertise itself. The danger is failing to notice when a situation falls outside the boundaries of past experience. Philosophy Roulette 534 Conditions for Intuitive Expertise. Daniel Kahneman & Gary Klein Channel: nogre0 Open on YouTube Questions That Break a Misleading Pattern Match The most effective response to false familiarity is not abandoning intuition. It is deliberately testing the first pattern that comes to mind. Several questions can force a useful second look: What would have to be different for this analogy to fail? This question shifts attention from similarities to differences. Instead of asking why the situation resembles a previous case, it asks what conditions would invalidate the comparison. What evidence does not fit my first explanation? When people become attached to an early interpretation, contradictory information is often dismissed as noise. Actively searching for mismatched evidence helps expose"},{"title":"Fluent Answers","url":"/fluent-answers/","description":"Some wrong answers feel right because they arrive easily, making reflection useful before accepting a satisfying first response.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Intuition","level":3,"text":"Fluent Answers Why Easy Answers Can Be Wrong Fluent Answers Fluent Answers Why Easy Answers Can Be Wrong Some wrong answers feel right because they arrive easily, making reflection useful before accepting a satisfying first response. Some wrong answers feel right because they arrive easily, making reflection useful before accepting a satisfying first response. dataset evidence What fluent wrong answers reveal Why reflection is not just intelligence Simple checks for satisfying first responses improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition fluent answers Introduction Some mistakes are difficult to detect because they feel correct from the moment they appear in consciousness. The Cognitive Reflection Test (CRT), introduced by psychologist Shane Frederick in 2005, became influential because it isolates this phenomenon in a remarkably simple way. Its questions are designed to trigger answers that arrive quickly, confidently, and fluently—yet are wrong. The test does not primarily measure whether someone knows a mathematical trick. Instead, it examines whether they pause long enough to question a response that feels immediately satisfying. [Yale Law School] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making wrong answer\" much as 10 cents is the wrong answer in the \"bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative... This matters for improving analytical thinking because many everyday errors do not arise from ignorance. They arise from accepting an answer before checking it. The CRT provides one of the clearest demonstrations that ease of retrieval and accuracy are not the same thing. [Yale Law School] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making wrong answer\" much as 10 cents is the wrong answer in the \"bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative... What Fluent Wrong Answers Reveal The classic CRT contains three short problems. Each has an intuitively appealing answer that comes to mind almost instantly. For the famous bat and ball problem, many people respond “10 cents”. The answer feels right because it fits the numbers mentioned in the question and requires almost no mental effort. Yet a brief calculation shows the correct answer is 5 cents. [Yale Law School+2mindyourdecisions.com] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making wrong answer\" much as 10 cents is the wrong answer in the \"bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative... The importance of this result is not the arithmetic itself. Researchers designed the questions so that the incorrect answer possesses high cognitive fluency. In psychology, fluency refers to the subjective ease with which information is processed. Ideas that arrive smoothly often feel more believable, familiar, and convincing than ideas that require effort. The CRT exploits that tendency by making the wrong answer easier to generate than the right one. Society for Judgment and Decision Making+2PMC [sjdm.org] sjdm.org It assesses individuals' ability to suppress an intuitive and spontaneous (\"system 1\") wrong answer...Read more... Historically, the test became a useful demonstration of a broader insight from reasoning research: people often confuse the feeling of correctness with correctness itself. The intuitive response is attractive not because it has been verified, but because it arrives first and with little resistance. [Yale Law School] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making wrong answer\" much as 10 cents is the wrong answer in the \"bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative... The three original CRT questions also reveal a consistent pattern. The most common errors are not random mistakes. Large numbers of participants independently produce the same wrong answers—10 cents, 100 minutes, and 24 days—because those answers emerge naturally from superficial interpretation of the problem structure. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cognitive reflection test Cognitive reflection test Every Cognitive Skill Explained (In 3 Minutes) Channel: Helpful Professor Explains! Open on YouTube Why the Wrong Answer Feels So Good Fluent wrong answers typically share several features: They are available immediately. They require little working memory. They appear to match the wording of the problem. They create a sense of closure. They reduce uncertainty quickly. The result is a subtle illusion: because the answer was easy to produce, it feels as though it has already been checked. The CRT demonstrates that these are separate processes. Generating an answer and evaluating an answer are not the same thing. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCCognitive reflection vs calculation in decision making PMCby A Sinayev · 2015 · Cited by 269 — For example, the bat and ball problem brings to mind an intuitiv... Why Can't People Solve These Simple Problems? Channel: Benjamin Keep, PhD, JD Open on YouTube Why Reflection Is Not Just Intelligence One reason the CRT attracted so much attention is that it challenged a common assumption about reasoning. People often imagine that analytical mistakes happen because someone lacks ability. The CRT suggested a more interesting possibility: many errors occur because a person fails to interrogate an appealing first response. [Yale Law School] law.yale.edu Frederick Cognitive Reflectionand Decision Making wrong answer\" much as 10 cents is the wrong answer in the \"bat and ball\" problem. Whatever stance one adopts on the contentious normative... Subsequent research complicated the picture. CRT performance correlates with measures of cognitive ability and numeracy, but reflection and ability are not identical. Solving a CRT item requires at least two steps. First, a person must notice that the intuitive answer may be wrong. Second, they must have the skills needed to work out the correct answer. Someone can fail at either stage. [PMC+2Wikipedia] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCCognitive reflection vs calculation in decision making PMCby A Sinayev · 2015 · Cited by 269 — For example, the bat and ball problem brings to mind an intuitiv... This distinction helps explain why reflection is not merely a synonym for intelligence. A highly capable person can accept an attractive first answer without scrutiny. Conversely, a person of more modest ability may still demonstrate strong reflective habits by checking assumptions, questioning confidence, and resisting premature closure . The CRT became influential because it highlighted the importance of that reflective checkpoint. [Society for Judgment and Decision Making] sjdm.org It assesses individuals' ability to suppress an intuitive and spontaneous (\"system 1\") wrong answer...Read more... Researchers have also found that CRT performance predicts success on a range of reasoning tasks involving common biases and heuristics. People who more readily question initial intuitions tend to perform better when confronted with misleading but tempting responses in other contexts. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cognitive reflection test Cognitive reflection test Debates About What the CRT Measures Over time, researchers have debated whether the CRT measures reflection, intelligence, numeracy, or some combination of all three. Modern assessments generally treat it as a multifaceted measure rather than a pure test of one psychological trait. Some people answer correctly because they engage in reflection; others may possess the mathematical competence needed to solve the problems once conflict is detected. [Wikipedia+2Scholars Commons] Wikipedia Cognitive reflection test Cognitive reflection test The debate itself reinforces the central lesson. Even if the CRT is not a perfect measure of reflection, its questions reliably expose situations where an answer's fluency exceeds its accuracy. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cognitive reflection test Cognitive reflection test Simple Checks for Satisfying First Responses The practical value of the CRT is not memorising its questions. Many people have seen them before, reducing their usefulness as a test. Its enduring contribution is a habit of mind: treating unusually satisfying answers as signals for inspection rather than immediate acceptance. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cognitive reflection test Cognitive reflection test When a response arrives with striking ease, several checks can help: Ask what assumption generated the answer. Fluent errors often depend on an unexamined assumption hidden inside the first interpretation of a problem. Try a quick verification. The correct CRT answers usually emerge after a simple check rather than extensive analysis. In many real world situations, a brief verification step catches a surprising number of mistakes. Notice feelings of closure. A sudden sense that the problem is already solved can be informative. It may indicate correctness, but it can also indicate that the mind has stopped searching. Look for alternative"},{"title":"Premature Closure","url":"/premature-closure/","description":"Early diagnostic certainty can narrow attention too soon, causing later evidence to be fitted around the wrong story.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Intuition","level":3,"text":"Premature Closure The Diagnosis Trap of Deciding Too Soon Premature Closure Premature Closure The Diagnosis Trap of Deciding Too Soon Early diagnostic certainty can narrow attention too soon, causing later evidence to be fitted around the wrong story. Early diagnostic certainty can narrow attention too soon, causing later evidence to be fitted around the wrong story. population context How early impressions become diagnostic anchors Confirmation bias after the first label Second pass questions that keep alternatives alive improving your thinking and analytical skills intuition premature closure Introduction Premature closure is one of the clearest examples of why fast intuition sometimes needs a second pass. In medicine, it occurs when a clinician settles on a diagnosis before the available evidence justifies that level of certainty. Once an early explanation feels convincing, attention can narrow, alternative possibilities receive less scrutiny, and new information may be interpreted as support for the original idea rather than as a reason to reconsider it. Researchers and patient safety organisations consistently identify premature closure as a common contributor to diagnostic error, delayed diagnosis, and missed diagnosis. [PubMed+2MSD Manuals] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Premature diagnostic closure: an avoidable type of errorby M Vázquez Costa · 2013 · Cited by 18 — The main cause of failures in cli... The importance of the phenomenon extends beyond medicine. Clinical diagnosis provides a vivid real world example of a broader thinking problem: the tendency to stop searching once a plausible answer appears. Because patients often present with incomplete, ambiguous, or evolving evidence, diagnosis is a setting where the costs of deciding too soon can be unusually high. [Springer] link.springer.com The challenge of cognitive science for medical diagnosisby P Croskerry · 2023 · Cited by 54 — A common cause of diagnostic error... How Early Impressions Become Diagnostic Anchors Many diagnoses begin with pattern recognition. An experienced clinician may rapidly recognise a familiar cluster of symptoms and generate a likely explanation within seconds. Most of the time, this ability is useful and efficient. Problems arise when the initial impression becomes an anchor rather than a hypothesis. [MSD Manuals] msdmanuals.com This is one of the most common errors; clinicians make an initial diagnosis (often...Read more... Premature closure is often described as accepting a diagnosis before alternative explanations have been adequately explored. Rather than continuing to gather evidence and test competing possibilities, the diagnostic process effectively stops. Subsequent questions, examinations, and tests may then focus on confirming the preferred explanation rather than challenging it. [PSNet+2MSD Manuals] psnet.ahrq.gov The fallacy of a single diagnosis. PSNetPremature closure occurs when clinicians accept a diagnosis before it has been confirmed a... A common mechanism is deceptively simple: 1. A patient presents with symptoms. 2. An early pattern seems familiar. 3. A provisional diagnosis is formed. 4. The clinician's search for alternatives weakens. 5. Contradictory evidence receives less attention. This progression can happen even among highly experienced professionals. Research on clinical reasoning shows that expertise does not eliminate vulnerability to cognitive bias. In some situations, extensive experience may actually strengthen confidence in an early pattern match, increasing the risk of overlooking exceptions or atypical presentations. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Avoiding premature closure and reaching diagnostic...by E Krupat · 2017 · Cited by 50 — However, more recent studies indicate that... A frequently cited illustration involves serious conditions that initially resemble common ones. Aortic dissection, for example, can present as chest pain that resembles more familiar cardiac problems. In a published case analysis, avoiding premature closure required persistent attention to the differential diagnosis—the list of plausible alternatives—rather than accepting the first explanation that appeared to fit. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCclinical decision making in a case of aortic dissection by B Kumar · 2011 · Cited by 41 — Premature closure is a type of cognitive error in which the physician fails to consider reasonable a... 🚨 CODE RED: 10 Fatal ER Mistakes That Kill Patients (And How to Fix Them FAST!) Emergency Medicine Channel: Dr. Amr Elmoheen Open on YouTube Why the First Label Changes What We Notice The danger of premature closure is not merely that the first diagnosis might be wrong. The deeper problem is that the first diagnosis changes how later evidence is interpreted. Psychologists and patient safety researchers often describe this interaction through anchoring and confirmation bias. Anchoring occurs when the initial impression exerts disproportionate influence. Confirmation bias occurs when people preferentially seek, notice, or remember information that supports their current belief. Together, they can create a self reinforcing cycle. [PSNet+2PMC] psnet.ahrq.gov PSNet Diagnostic Errors PSNet Diagnostic Errors PSNet AHRQAnchoring heuristic (premature closure), Relying on initial diagnostic impression, despite subsequen... Once a patient has been labelled as having a particular condition, several things can happen: Supporting findings are treated as highly significant. Ambiguous findings are interpreted in favour of the diagnosis. Contradictory findings are discounted as exceptions. Alternative explanations receive less active consideration. The result is not usually reckless decision making. More often, it is a gradual narrowing of attention. Clinicians may sincerely believe they are following the evidence while unconsciously giving greater weight to information that preserves the original diagnosis. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net It has been suggested that confirmation bias can arise from an attempt to avoid...Read more... Evidence from analyses of diagnostic failures repeatedly identifies premature closure, anchoring, and confirmation bias among the most common cognitive contributors to error. During reviews of clinical cases, these biases frequently appear together rather than in isolation. A study examining cognitive biases in hospital care found premature closure among the most commonly implicated reasoning failures, often alongside anchoring and confirmation bias. [PMC+2Lippincott Journals] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCClinical reasoning in dire times Analysis of cognitive biases in...by M Coen · 2022 · Cited by 24 — They concluded that premature closure (72.2%), anchoring (61.1%), and... When Confidence Becomes the Warning Sign One of the most counterintuitive findings from the literature is that diagnostic mistakes are not always concentrated in the most confusing cases. Some occur precisely because the clinician feels unusually certain. When uncertainty is obvious, people tend to slow down, seek additional information, and consult alternatives. Premature closure is more likely when a diagnosis feels straightforward. Confidence can signal that the mind has successfully recognised a pattern—but it can also signal that the search process has ended too early. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net 19179531 Premature conclusions in diagnostic reasoning ResearchGate(PDF) Premature conclusions in diagnostic reasoning8 Feb 2026 — Among them, overconfidence is manifested as cognitive biases... This distinction matters because many diagnostic errors are not caused by ignorance. Reviews of clinical reasoning suggest that errors frequently arise from the way information is processed rather than from a complete lack of medical knowledge. In other words, the clinician may know the correct diagnosis but fail to consider it because the reasoning process has already converged on another explanation. [Fitzgerald Health Education Associates+2Delta Psychology] fhea.com cognitive errors in clinical diagnosis availability bias and premature closure Fitzgerald Health Education AssociatesCognitive Errors in Clinical Diagnosis: Availability Bias and...24 Sept 2021 — Errors in diagnosti... For thinking more broadly, this highlights an important lesson: certainty is not always evidence of correctness. Sometimes it is evidence that the search for alternatives has stopped. Second Pass Questions That Keep Alternatives Alive Research on diagnostic excellence increasingly focuses not only on recognising bias but also on creating habits that interrupt it. The goal is not to eliminate intuition. Fast pattern recognition remains essential in medicine. The goal is to ensure that an initial judgement remains provisional until it survives further scrutiny. [codex.ucsf.edu] codex.ucsf.edu primer 3 role clinical reasoning diagnostic excellence Primer 3: The Role of Clinical Reasoning in Diagnostic...25 Jun 2025 — This primer focuses on the role of"},{"title":"Job Offers","url":"/job-offers/","description":"Comparing job offers is a practical test of analytical thinking because it mixes facts, preferences, risk, and uncertainty.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Job Offers How Do You Compare Job Offers Clearly? Job Offers Job Offers How Do You Compare Job Offers Clearly? Comparing job offers is a practical test of analytical thinking because it mixes facts, preferences, risk, and uncertainty. Comparing job offers is a practical test of analytical thinking because it mixes facts, preferences, risk, and uncertainty. population context Define decision criteria Weigh trade offs Test assumptions about fit improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers Introduction Comparing job offers clearly is a practical test of analytical thinking because there is rarely one “correct” answer. A higher salary may come with a longer commute, weaker benefits, less autonomy, more risk, or fewer learning opportunities. A lower paid role may be better if it builds valuable skills, protects health, fits your life, and has a credible path to progression. The aim is not to turn a personal decision into a fake equation, but to make the trade offs visible before emotion, urgency, or one impressive number dominates the choice. A good comparison separates three questions: what each offer is objectively worth, how each job would probably feel week by week, and which option best serves your next career chapter. Research and labour market frameworks support this broader view. The OECD treats job quality as multi dimensional, including earnings quality, labour market security and the working environment, while the CIPD’s Good Work Index examines pay and benefits alongside autonomy, voice, wellbeing, skills development and job satisfaction. [OECD] oecd.org Job qualityBy examining earnings quality, labour market security and the working environment, the framework offers a comprehensive as... Why the “best offer” is not just the highest salary Salary is visible, comparable and emotionally powerful, so it often becomes the anchor for the whole decision. But an offer is really a bundle: base pay, bonus structure, pension or retirement contributions, health cover, paid leave, flexibility, commute, working hours, manager quality, role scope, learning curve, employment security and future optionality. The US Bureau of Labor Statistics’ Employer Costs for Employee Compensation data illustrates why this matters: in March 2026, private industry wages and salaries averaged $32.60 per hour while benefits averaged $14.01 per hour, meaning benefits accounted for about 30% of private sector compensation costs. [Bureau of Labor Statistics] bls.gov Bureau of Labor Statistics ECEC Home: U.S Bureau of Labor StatisticsDecember 28, 2022 — Employer costs for private industry workers averaged $32.60 per hour worked for wages and s... Published: December 28, 2022 The analytical mistake is to compare only the easiest line item. A £60,000 offer with expensive travel, weak pension contributions and routine late evenings may be less attractive than a £55,000 offer with strong benefits, a shorter commute and protected learning time. Conversely, a lower paid “mission driven” job can be a poor trade if it relies on vague purpose while offering weak management, limited progression and chronic overload. A useful first pass is to convert the offer into practical value: Offer elementWhat to checkWhy it changes the decisionBase salaryGuaranteed annual pay before taxSets the floor, but not the full packageVariable payBonus, commission, equity, profit shareMay be valuable, but depends on probability and rulesBenefitsPension, healthcare, insurance, leave, childcare, training budgetOften materially changes total compensationTime costCommute, expected hours, travel, time zone overlapChanges effective hourly pay and quality of lifeRiskProbation terms, company stability, funding, contract typeAffects security and downside exposureGrowthSkills, manager, promotion path, marketabilityDetermines what the job makes possible later This is where analytical thinking improves the choice: it stops you from asking, “Which offer looks best?” and makes you ask, “Which offer creates the best total outcome under realistic assumptions ?” Define decision criteria before the offers blur together The clearest comparisons start before scoring. Write down the criteria that matter, then define what good, acceptable and poor look like for each. Without definitions, people tend to retrofit their criteria around the offer they already prefer. A strong set of criteria usually includes: Financial floor: the minimum compensation needed for stability, savings, debt repayment and dependants. Role quality: whether the work uses and stretches the skills you actually want to build. Manager and team: whether you would trust the person setting priorities, giving feedback and judging performance. Autonomy: how much control you have over methods, schedule, judgement and problem solving. Learning and progression: whether the role creates evidence of growth, not just promises of growth. Lifestyle fit: commute, flexibility, travel, intensity, on call expectations and recovery time. Security and risk: contract terms, company health, industry outlook and redundancy exposure. Values and motivation: whether the work feels worthwhile enough to sustain effort without becoming self exploitation. These criteria map closely onto established job quality thinking. The OECD’s job quality framework emphasises earnings, security and working environment, while CIPD’s UK Good Work Index uses a broader set of dimensions including pay and benefits, job design, work life balance, health and wellbeing, voice, relationships and skills development. [OECD] oecd.org measuring and assessing job quality 5jrp02kjw1mr en Measuring and Assessing Job Qualityby S Cazes · 2015 · Cited by 365 — The approach to job quality taken is explicitly multi dimension... The key analytical move is to make criteria personal but not impulsive. “I want prestige” is too vague. “I want a role that makes me more credible for senior product roles within two years” is testable. “I want flexibility” is vague. “I need to pick up my child by 17:30 three days a week without career penalty” is decision ready. Weigh trade offs instead of pretending every factor is equal A decision matrix can help when two or more offers are close. The basic idea is simple: choose criteria, assign each criterion a weight, score each offer against each criterion, then compare the weighted totals. Career resources commonly recommend this type of structured matrix for comparing multiple job offers, while warning that the total should inform judgement rather than replace it. [NUS] nus.edu.sg Job Decision MatrixThis Job decision matrix can be used to weigh up a single offer according to different factors, or to compare how m... A simple version looks like this: CriterionWeightOffer A scoreOffer A weightedOffer B scoreOffer B weightedTotal compensation25%82.0061.50Learning and progression25%61.5092.25Manager and team20%71.4081.60Work life fit20%51.0081.60Security10%80.8060.60 Total 100% 6.70 7.55 The numbers are not magic. Their value is that they expose disagreement inside your own thinking. If Offer A pays more but Offer B wins after weighting, the decision becomes more honest: either you genuinely value growth and work life fit more than extra pay, or your weights need revisiting. The matrix is especially useful for spotting three common errors: One factor dominance: letting salary, brand name or remote work decide everything. Hidden double counting: scoring “great culture”, “nice people” and “supportive manager” separately when they reflect the same evidence. Unexamined weights: saying career growth matters most, then giving it only a small influence in the final decision. The best use of a matrix is not to obey the highest total automatically. It is to force a better conversation with yourself: “What would have to be true for the lower scoring offer to still be right?” How to Negotiate Your Job Offer Prof. Deepak Malhotra (Harvard Business School) Channel: Deepak Malhotra Open on YouTube Test assumptions about fit before accepting “Fit” is one of the most important and least reliable parts of an offer decision. Candidates often judge it from interviews, employer branding and a few friendly conversations, but interviews are staged encounters. The real job may depend more on manager habits, team norms, workload spikes, political constraints and how success is measured. Before accepting, convert impressions into assumptions and test them. For example: ImpressionTestable assumptionEvidence to seek“The team seems collaborative”People share information early and resolve conflict constructivelyAsk how decisions are made when teams disagree“There is room to grow”Promotion criteria are clear and previous hires have progressedAsk for examples of recent internal moves“The role is strategic”The job includes real decision influence, not just reportingAsk what decisions this role owns in the first six months“The hours are reasonable”Peak periods are manageable and exceptions are"},{"title":"Growth Proof","url":"/growth-proof/","description":"A role that promises development is stronger when it produces visible skills, credible examples and a path to the next job.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Job Offers","level":3,"text":"Growth Proof Is the Growth Promise Real? Growth Proof Growth Proof Is the Growth Promise Real? A role that promises development is stronger when it produces visible skills, credible examples and a path to the next job. A role that promises development is stronger when it produces visible skills, credible examples and a path to the next job. dataset evidence Separating learning opportunities from vague optimism Checking promotion paths and recent internal moves Judging marketable skills after the first year improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers growth proof Introduction A job offer that promises rapid growth is valuable only if that growth becomes visible evidence that you can use in your next career move. Many employers advertise learning, mentorship and promotion opportunities, but the strongest opportunities leave a trail of proof: new responsibilities, measurable achievements, recognised skills, and a credible record of people progressing into more senior roles. Evaluating this evidence is one of the most effective ways to avoid being persuaded by optimistic language alone. When comparing offers, the key question is not, \"Will I learn?\" Almost every job involves some learning. Instead ask, \"What concrete evidence will exist after my first year that makes me more valuable to future employers?\" Framing the decision this way turns vague promises into testable claims . Separating genuine learning opportunities from vague optimism Growth claims vary enormously in quality. Statements such as \"fast paced environment\", \"lots of exposure\", or \"excellent career opportunities\" sound attractive but often describe aspirations rather than outcomes . A stronger signal is when an employer can point to structured development, measurable skill building, and examples of employees who have advanced. Look for evidence such as: A documented training programme with specific technical or professional skills. Regular feedback and development reviews rather than only annual appraisals. Opportunities to own increasingly complex projects. Formal mentoring or coaching. Access to recognised certifications or qualifications. Cross functional work that broadens experience beyond a narrow job description. Research consistently shows that learning is most effective when it is connected directly to career development rather than offered as isolated training. LinkedIn's 2025 Workplace Learning Report found that organisations with mature career development programmes combine learning with leadership development, coaching and internal mobility, producing stronger promotion outcomes and higher engagement than organisations where learning exists without clear career pathways. [LinkedIn] business.linkedin.com workplace learning report 2025 Workplace Learning ReportUncover data, trends, and actionable advice to drive career growth, talent retention, and organizat... By contrast, an employer whose development story depends almost entirely on enthusiasm, culture slogans or future intentions is asking candidates to trust promises that may never materialise. How to test whether promotion paths are real A promotion path becomes credible when it is visible in recent employee careers rather than described only during recruitment. Useful questions include: How many people on this team were promoted during the past two years? Where did the previous person in this role move next? What distinguishes employees who are promoted quickly? Are vacancies usually filled internally or externally? How long do people typically stay at each level? Recruiters may not have every answer, but organisations with genuine internal progression usually provide concrete examples instead of general assurances. Public information can also help. Professional networking profiles often reveal whether employees regularly move into broader responsibilities inside the organisation or whether advancement mainly requires leaving the company. A pattern of internal moves across multiple employees is stronger evidence than a single success story. LinkedIn's Career Development Index illustrates why these indicators matter. The index incorporates measurable factors including internal career moves, leadership skill development and career oriented job postings. Organisations scoring highly show higher promotion rates and stronger leadership pipelines than organisations with weaker career development practices. [LinkedIn] business.linkedin.com workplace learning report 2025 Workplace Learning ReportUncover data, trends, and actionable advice to drive career growth, talent retention, and organizat... One important caution is survivorship bias. Employers naturally introduce candidates to employees whose careers progressed exceptionally well. Ask whether these examples represent the typical experience or the exceptional one. 2 Ways to Know If You've Hit Your Career Ceiling Big Think Channel: Big Think Open on YouTube Judge the manager as carefully as the company Even excellent organisations vary dramatically between teams. Much of a person's development depends on the immediate manager rather than the employer's official policies. Managers who genuinely accelerate careers often: Delegate meaningful responsibility early. Give detailed feedback instead of only praise. Explain business decisions rather than assigning isolated tasks. Encourage presentations, customer interaction or leadership opportunities. Help employees build visibility across the organisation. The opposite pattern is equally informative. If interviewers cannot explain how junior staff develop new responsibilities, or describe growth only as \"working hard\", the advertised learning may consist largely of accumulating workload rather than acquiring valuable capability. LinkedIn's research also highlights that managers are frequently the limiting factor. Many organisations recognise the importance of career development, but respondents identify lack of managerial time and support—not lack of executive interest—as one of the biggest barriers to employee growth. [LinkedIn] business.linkedin.com workplace learning report 2025 Workplace Learning ReportUncover data, trends, and actionable advice to drive career growth, talent retention, and organizat... What should exist after your first year? The strongest test of a growth promise is imagining your curriculum vitae after twelve months. A productive first year should allow you to add evidence such as: Leading a significant project. Managing budgets, customers or stakeholders. Supervising colleagues or mentoring newer staff. Learning industry standard tools or technologies. Delivering measurable business improvements. Completing recognised professional qualifications. Demonstrating increased decision making authority. Notice that each item describes observable evidence rather than effort. Future employers rarely reward someone simply because they worked hard. They reward demonstrated capability. Recent labour market research also suggests that richer skill portfolios—not merely longer experience—are associated with better labour market outcomes. Workers who accumulate broader and more specific skills tend to be more likely to occupy higher paid roles, reinforcing the importance of choosing work that visibly expands capabilities rather than only extending tenure. [arXiv] arxiv.org Multidimensional Skills as a Measure of Human Capital: Evidence from LinkedIn ProfilesSeptember 27, 2024... Published: September 27, 2024 Job Progression vs Career Growth Channel: Huzaifa Asif Open on YouTube Skills that remain valuable outside the employer Some organisations develop highly specialised knowledge that is difficult to transfer elsewhere. Others provide experience recognised across an entire industry. When assessing growth, distinguish between: Strong evidence of transferable growthWeaker evidence of growthManaging people or projectsLearning only company specific proceduresWidely recognised software or technical toolsProprietary internal systems onlyCommercial decision makingRoutine administrative repetitionCustomer facing responsibilityLimited visibility outside one teamCross functional collaborationHighly isolated workIndustry recognised certificationsInternal training with no external recognition Transferable skills increase future options because they remain valuable even if circumstances change or the employer disappoints. Warning signs that growth is mostly marketing Certain patterns appear repeatedly when organisations overstate development opportunities. Be cautious if: Every employee receives the same generic promise of \"rapid progression\". Promotion criteria are unclear or constantly changing. Most senior positions are hired externally. Employees remain in identical roles for many years without expanded responsibilities. Development depends entirely on unpaid overtime or \"proving commitment\". Interviewers struggle to describe recent examples of internal advancement. The role offers exposure"},{"title":"Downside Risk","url":"/downside-risk/","description":"Thinking through base, upside and downside cases helps you price uncertainty before a bonus, equity grant or exciting title sways you.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Job Offers","level":3,"text":"Downside Risk What If the Offer Goes Wrong? Downside Risk Downside Risk What If the Offer Goes Wrong? Thinking through base, upside and downside cases helps you price uncertainty before a bonus, equity grant or exciting title sways you. Thinking through base, upside and downside cases helps you price uncertainty before a bonus, equity grant or exciting title sways you. event time window Base, upside and downside cases for the first year Bonuses, equity and start up risk under uncertainty Exit options if the role is mis sold improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers downside risk Introduction Every job offer contains uncertainty. The visible parts of the package—salary, title and benefits—can distract from risks that only become apparent after you join: a manager leaves, promised bonuses fail to materialise, funding tightens, priorities change, or the role turns out to be very different from the interview. Thinking explicitly about downside scenarios is not pessimism. It is a practical way to improve decision quality by testing whether an offer still makes sense if important assumptions prove wrong. A useful approach is to compare a realistic base case, an optimistic upside case and a credible downside case for the first year. This helps separate attractive opportunities from offers that only look good if everything goes perfectly. Base, upside and downside cases for the first year Instead of asking whether an offer is \"good\", ask what is most likely to happen under three different but plausible scenarios. ScenarioTypical assumptionsQuestions to ask Base case Most promises are delivered, normal performance, business remains stableWould I still be happy if everything is merely average? Upside case Strong performance, rapid learning, promotion opportunities, higher bonusHow much additional value is genuinely achievable? Downside case Reorganisation, weak manager, lower bonus, changing priorities, slower growthCould I tolerate this outcome financially and professionally? The base case should reflect normal business conditions rather than the most optimistic recruiting pitch. The downside case should not assume disaster, but it should include events that occur regularly across organisations, such as restructurings, delayed projects, leadership changes or tighter budgets. A useful test is to identify the assumptions that matter most. If the offer only looks attractive because you expect an exceptional bonus, rapid promotion or an unusually supportive manager, your decision depends heavily on events you cannot control. Common first year downside scenarios include: Your hiring manager leaves within six months. Performance targets become more demanding after joining. Expected hybrid or remote working arrangements change. A promised project is cancelled. Team restructuring changes your responsibilities. The probation period is extended or expectations become unclear. Recruitment promises were informal rather than written into the contract. Thinking through these possibilities helps distinguish between manageable uncertainty and unacceptable risk. Bonuses, equity and start up risk under uncertainty Variable compensation deserves separate analysis because it often carries more uncertainty than base salary. Should You Accept Or Decline Your Job Offer? How To Evaluate A Job Offer. Channel: A Life After Layoff Open on YouTube Bonuses are not guaranteed income Many candidates unconsciously treat a target bonus as part of their annual salary. In reality, bonus payments usually depend on combinations of company performance, business unit results and individual objectives. Questions worth asking include: How often has the company actually paid the target bonus? Are bonus targets objective or discretionary? Must you still be employed on the payment date? What happens if objectives change during the year? Are new employees eligible immediately? Research reviewed by the Chartered Institute of Personnel and Development (CIPD) shows that financial incentives can improve performance, but their effectiveness depends heavily on perceived fairness and transparent processes. Employees respond less positively when reward systems appear arbitrary or poorly explained. [CIPD] cipd.org Financial incentives: an evidence review Financial incentives: an evidence review scentific summaryJanuary 21, 2022 — In the realm of financial incentives, a recent longitu... Published: January 21, 2022 Rather than valuing a bonus at its maximum advertised amount, estimate an expected value based on realistic probabilities . Equity can be valuable—or worth very little Equity compensation introduces another layer of uncertainty. Questions to investigate include: What percentage ownership do the shares actually represent? What is the current valuation based on? How long is the vesting schedule? What happens if you leave before vesting? Are there restrictions on selling shares? Has the company previously raised funding successfully? Many employees mentally anchor on impressive headline valuations without considering dilution, future fundraising, tax consequences or the possibility that there may never be a liquidity event. A useful analytical habit is to separate: Guaranteed value: salary and contractual benefits. Probable value: bonuses based on realistic performance. Speculative value: equity whose eventual worth may range from zero to substantial gains. Treating speculative compensation as guaranteed income increases decision risk. Start up uncertainty is broader than failure People often think only about bankruptcy when assessing start up risk. In practice , more common issues include changing priorities, delayed fundraising, product pivots, management turnover and repeated organisational restructuring. Young firms often offer faster learning, broader responsibilities and potentially significant upside. However, they may also experience greater operational uncertainty than established employers, even if they compensate employees through higher salaries or more secure contract terms in some cases. OECD research suggests that once comparable workers are considered, young firms can offer competitive pay and permanent contracts, illustrating that start up risk cannot be judged from company age alone. The important question is how individual firms manage uncertainty rather than assuming all start ups are either highly risky or exceptionally rewarding. [OECD] oecd.org Do start ups provide better jobs? What new...18 Nov 2025 — New OECD analysis using data from France and Portugal challenges the assu... Useful indicators include: cash runway and recent funding; customer concentration; leadership stability; hiring pace relative to revenue; employee turnover; whether growth depends on future investment rounds. 7 Red Flags With Your New Job Offer Channel: A Life After Layoff Open on YouTube What if the role is mis sold? Not every disappointing role results from deliberate deception. Interviews naturally emphasise attractive aspects of the job, while changing business conditions can alter priorities before a new employee even starts. However, some warning signs deserve closer attention: Interviewers describe the role inconsistently. Responsibilities remain unusually vague. Multiple interviewers give conflicting answers about reporting lines. High turnover is dismissed without explanation. Success measures cannot be clearly described. Important promises are made verbally but omitted from written documentation. The best defence is evidence rather than optimism. Ask for examples of recent projects, team structure, typical performance objectives and how success is evaluated after six or twelve months. Planning your exit before you need it Considering exit options is not a sign that you expect failure. It reduces the emotional pressure to accept unnecessary risk. Before accepting an offer, consider: Financial resilience. How many months could you manage if the role ended unexpectedly? Transferable skills. Would the experience strengthen your CV even if the company struggled? Professional network. Will you continue building relationships outside the organisation? Contract terms. Understand probation arrangements, notice periods, restrictive covenants and any repayment clauses for bonuses or training. Marketability. Would six to twelve months in the role make you more employable than remaining in your current position? A strong exit option changes negotiations as well. People who know they can recover from a poor outcome are less likely to overvalue uncertain promises during recruitment. 3 Red Flags in a Job Offer You Should NEVER Ignore Channel: The Recruitment Show by Lewis Maleh Open on YouTube A practical downside risk checklist Before accepting an offer, ask yourself: 1. What assumptions must be true for this offer to outperform my alternatives? 2. Which parts of the compensation are guaranteed and which are uncertain? 3. If the manager changed after three months, would I"},{"title":"Scorecards","url":"/scorecards/","description":"A weighted matrix can reveal whether your stated priorities match the offer you feel drawn to accepting.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Job Offers","level":3,"text":"Scorecards Can a Scorecard Clarify the Choice? Scorecards Scorecards Can a Scorecard Clarify the Choice? A weighted matrix can reveal whether your stated priorities match the offer you feel drawn to accepting. A weighted matrix can reveal whether your stated priorities match the offer you feel drawn to accepting. policy intervention Choosing criteria before scoring the offers Setting weights without pretending every factor is equal Using the total as a prompt, not a verdict improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers scorecards Introduction When two job offers are both attractive, intuition alone often struggles to separate them. A weighted decision matrix, sometimes called a weighted scorecard, provides a structured way to compare offers against the factors that genuinely matter to you rather than allowing the most obvious feature—usually salary—to dominate the decision. Instead of assuming every criterion is equally important, it assigns greater influence to the factors you value most, such as career development, flexibility, manager quality or financial security. This approach reflects the principles of multi criteria decision analysis (MCDA), a family of methods designed for decisions involving competing objectives. [1000minds] 1000minds.com Multi Criteria Decision Analysis (MCDA/MCDM)MCDA, also known as Multi Criteria Decision Making (MCDM), is about making decisions... The value of a scorecard is not that it produces a mathematically perfect answer. Its real strength is revealing whether your stated priorities are consistent with the offer you feel drawn towards. If the numbers and your instincts disagree, that is often the beginning of a better conversation with yourself rather than the end of the decision. Can a Scorecard Clarify the Choice? A simple pros and cons list treats every item as though it has identical importance. A weighted matrix recognises that this is rarely true. For one person, a flexible working arrangement may be worth more than a modest salary increase. For another, a stronger pension or a stable employer may outweigh almost everything else. By making priorities explicit before comparing offers, the scorecard reduces the influence of common decision traps such as anchoring on salary, recency bias after an impressive interview, or being swayed by a prestigious company name. Decision matrix frameworks are widely used because they make trade offs visible rather than leaving them implicit. [ASQ+2Lucid Software] asq.org What is a Decision Matrix? Pugh, Problem, or Selection GridA decision matrix evaluates and prioritizes a list of options and is a deci... The matrix is particularly valuable when: both offers satisfy your minimum requirements; each role has meaningful advantages and disadvantages; family, financial and career objectives compete with one another; emotions fluctuate from day to day depending on recent conversations or negotiations. How to use a Decision Matrix Channel: Learn Management Online (Michael James) Open on YouTube Choosing Criteria Before Scoring the Offers The quality of the matrix depends far more on the criteria than on the arithmetic. Choose criteria before you begin scoring individual offers. Otherwise it is easy to invent new criteria that conveniently favour the option you already prefer. A practical scorecard often includes around six to ten criteria, such as: CriterionWhat you are really assessingTotal compensationSalary, bonus, pension and valuable benefits togetherCareer developmentSkills, mentoring, promotion opportunities and future employabilityManager and teamTrust, communication and quality of leadershipFlexibilityRemote working, autonomy, predictable hoursJob securityStability of employer, funding, contract termsNature of the workInterest, challenge and alignment with long term goalsLifestyle impactCommute, travel, workload and recovery timeOrganisational cultureValues, collaboration and psychological safety The exact list should reflect your own circumstances. Someone changing careers may legitimately give much greater importance to learning than immediate earnings, while someone supporting a family may reasonably prioritise income stability. Frameworks for assessing job quality also emphasise looking beyond pay alone. The OECD, for example, evaluates jobs using earnings quality, labour market security and the working environment rather than salary in isolation. [OECD] oecd.org Job qualityThe OECD has developed a framework to measure and assess the quality of jobs that considers three objective and measurable... How To Use A Weighted Decision Matrix (Google Sheets) Channel: MR. LONG CTE Open on YouTube Setting Weights Without Pretending Every Factor Is Equal The defining feature of a weighted matrix is that not every criterion counts equally. [safetyculture.com] safetyculture.com decision matrix How to Use a Decision Matrix: A Guide28 Apr 2026 — By visually organizing options against weighted criteria, it helps reduce bias and dec... One straightforward approach is: Decision Matrix Analysis Channel: EPM Open on YouTube 1. List every criterion. 2. Assign each a weight representing its importance—for example on a scale from 1–10 or as percentages adding to 100. 3. Score each offer consistently against every criterion. 4. Multiply each score by its weight. 5. Add the weighted scores to produce an overall total. Lucid Software The difficult part is not the multiplication; it is assigning honest weights. Useful questions include: If I could improve only one aspect of either job, which would matter most? Which factors would still matter after two years? Which criteria are merely desirable, and which would genuinely affect my wellbeing or career trajectory? Which compromises would I regret six months after starting? Avoid distributing weights almost evenly across every category. If every factor receives similar importance, the weighting exercise contributes little beyond a simple checklist. A practical rule is to reserve the largest weights for only two or three genuinely decisive criteria. This forces prioritisation rather than creating an illusion that everything matters equally. Scoring Consistently Rather Than Precisely The numerical scores should reflect consistent judgement, not false precision. For example, if using a five point scale: 5 = strongly exceeds expectations 4 = better than expected ... 3 = acceptable 2 = noticeable weaknesses 1 = clearly unsatisfactory Define these meanings before scoring either offer. Where evidence is uncertain, score conservatively. For instance, an employer's promise of rapid promotion should not receive the same score as a documented promotion pathway with employees who have actually progressed. Similarly, distinguish between observable facts and optimistic assumptions : Confirmed hybrid working deserves more confidence than \"we're considering changing the policy.\" Written equity terms deserve more weight than informal promises. Conversations with future colleagues often provide stronger evidence than recruitment marketing materials. Using the Total as a Prompt, Not a Verdict The highest numerical score should begin reflection, not end it. Three outcomes are particularly informative. The winner is clear. If one offer scores substantially higher and you remain comfortable with the result after reviewing your assumptions, the matrix has probably helped organise your thinking. The totals are almost identical. A difference of one or two points usually indicates that the offers are genuinely close. Rather than chasing mathematical certainty, examine which individual criteria created the difference. Your emotions reject the highest score. This is often the most valuable outcome. If your preferred offer loses convincingly yet you still want to choose it, ask why. Perhaps: an important criterion was omitted; the weights do not reflect your real priorities; you have information that was never incorporated into the scoring; or your emotional response highlights a value that is difficult to quantify. The matrix has still succeeded because it exposed the disagreement between your explicit reasoning and your intuition. Test How Sensitive the Decision Really Is A useful final check is a simple sensitivity test. Adjust one or two important weights slightly and see whether the ranking changes. For example: increase career development from 20% to 25%; reduce salary importance from 30% to 25%; increase flexibility after considering commuting time. If tiny changes reverse the outcome, your decision is finely balanced and deserves further investigation rather than immediate acceptance. If one offer remains ahead despite several reasonable adjustments, you can be more confident that the recommendation reflects your priorities rather than arbitrary numbers. Sensitivity testing is a recognised practice in multi criteria decision analysis because rankings can sometimes depend heavily on how criteria are weighted. 1000minds Common Mistakes"},{"title":"Manager Fit","url":"/manager-fit/","description":"The person setting priorities, feedback and expectations can change the same role from energising to exhausting.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Job Offers","level":3,"text":"Manager Fit Why the Manager May Matter Most Manager Fit Manager Fit Why the Manager May Matter Most The person setting priorities, feedback and expectations can change the same role from energising to exhausting. The person setting priorities, feedback and expectations can change the same role from energising to exhausting. population context How managers shape autonomy and priorities Questions that test feedback and support Warning signs in success measures and communication improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers manager fit Introduction When comparing job offers , it is easy to focus on salary, title or benefits because they are visible and easy to compare. Yet one of the strongest influences on whether a job becomes rewarding or draining is often the person you report to. Your manager decides which work is most important, how success is measured, how mistakes are handled, how much autonomy you receive, and whether development conversations are genuine or merely procedural. For that reason, accepting a job offer is partly accepting a working relationship. Even an attractive role can become frustrating under a manager who provides unclear priorities, inconsistent feedback or little support. Conversely, a demanding role can become an excellent career move when led by a manager who creates trust, removes obstacles and develops people. Research consistently shows that managers have a substantial influence on engagement, wellbeing and performance. [Gallup.com] gallup.com How to Improve Employee Engagement in the Workplace February 21, 2020 — Learn how to improve employee engagement. Discover the true drivers of engagement, the best survey questions and team... Published: February 21, 2020 Why the manager may matter more than the job description Job descriptions describe responsibilities. Managers define the day to day experience of carrying them out. Two people hired into identical roles can have dramatically different experiences because their managers differ in how they: prioritise competing demands; delegate authority; provide coaching and feedback; respond to mistakes; recognise good work; protect employees from unnecessary interruptions; advocate for resources and career opportunities. This helps explain why organisations with similar pay and benefits can produce very different employee experiences. Gallup's long running workplace research argues that managers account for around 70% of the variation in employee engagement between teams, highlighting how much immediate leadership shapes working life. [Gallup.com] gallup.com How to Improve Employee Engagement in the Workplace February 21, 2020 — Learn how to improve employee engagement. Discover the true drivers of engagement, the best survey questions and team... Published: February 21, 2020 Research on workplace wellbeing points in the same direction. Employees consistently identify supportive leadership, meaningful feedback, appropriate autonomy and manageable demands as important resources that improve satisfaction and reduce the risk of burnout. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Frontiers Exploring Well being at Work—An Interview Study on How FrontiersExploring Well being at Work—An Interview Study on How...August 2, 2021 — by S Zutavern · 2021 · Cited by 21 — Motivational des... Published: August 2, 2021 How managers shape autonomy and priorities One of the first questions to ask is not simply, \"Will I have autonomy?\" but, \"How does this manager decide when to give it?\" Good managers usually combine clear expectations with freedom over execution. They define outcomes while allowing employees to exercise judgement appropriate to their experience. Poor managers often create the opposite combination: vague objectives coupled with constant intervention. Useful signs of healthy management include: Clear explanations of what success looks like. Priorities that are reviewed openly when circumstances change. Authority matching responsibility. Coaching rather than constant monitoring. Willingness to explain the reasoning behind decisions. Warning signs include: Constantly shifting priorities without explanation. Approval required for routine decisions. Ambiguous ownership of work. Frequent urgency without clear business reasons. Success measured differently from week to week. Studies of motivation repeatedly find that autonomy supports engagement when combined with competence, meaningful work and supportive relationships rather than existing in isolation. Simply being \"left alone\" is not enough if expectations are unclear or support disappears when problems arise. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org Frontiers Exploring Well being at Work—An Interview Study on How FrontiersExploring Well being at Work—An Interview Study on How...August 2, 2021 — by S Zutavern · 2021 · Cited by 21 — Motivational des... Published: August 2, 2021 Questions to Ask Your Future Boss’s Manager in an Interview Great Job Interview Questions to Ask Channel: Ben Talks Talent Interview Tips Open on YouTube Questions that test feedback and support Interviews often focus on whether you can do the job. They are also an opportunity to understand how the manager manages. Questions that frequently reveal useful information include: \"How do you define success for someone in this role during the first six months?\" \"How often do you have one to one meetings with your team?\" \"Can you describe someone who has done particularly well in this position?\" \"What usually distinguishes high performers from average performers?\" \"Can you tell me about a recent piece of difficult feedback you gave someone?\" \"How do you help people recover when projects do not go as planned?\" \"How are priorities handled when several urgent requests arrive at once?\" The answers matter as much as the content. Managers who naturally discuss coaching, learning, collaboration and clear expectations often describe concrete examples. Managers who repeatedly return to vague statements such as \"I just expect people to get on with it\" or \"Everyone should know what to do\" may reveal a less structured approach to support. Pay attention to whether the interviewer speaks about developing people or merely evaluating them. A manager who only discusses performance ratings may be signalling a culture focused on judgement rather than improvement. Warning signs in success measures and communication Many management problems appear long before an employee joins. The challenge is noticing them. Potential warning signs include: Unclear success measures. Different interviewers give conflicting descriptions of priorities. Constant emphasis on \"firefighting\". Every project is described as urgent or chaotic. Blame focused language. Previous employees are repeatedly criticised without discussion of organisational learning. Defensive answers. Reasonable questions about workload or expectations are treated as signs of weak commitment. Avoidance of specifics. The manager cannot describe recent examples of successful onboarding, promotion or development. Poor communication during recruitment. Frequent unexplained delays, inconsistent information or repeated last minute changes may reflect broader management habits. None of these signs proves that a manager is ineffective. However, several appearing together deserve careful attention because they suggest patterns rather than isolated incidents. How to Evaluate Your Future Boss in a Job Interview Channel: Andrew LaCivita Open on YouTube Read behaviour, not just promises Interviewers often describe themselves as supportive, collaborative or empowering. Those words have little value unless accompanied by evidence. Look instead for observable behaviours. For example: ClaimBetter evidence\"We value feedback.\"The manager explains the structure of one to ones, reviews and coaching conversations.\"We trust our people.\"Employees make meaningful decisions without unnecessary approval layers.\"We invest in development.\"Examples of recent promotions, mentoring or funded learning are readily available.\"We encourage ownership.\"The manager explains how responsibility increases as employees gain experience. Specific examples are usually more informative than polished leadership language. Looking beyond the hiring manager The hiring manager matters most, but they also operate within a wider system. Useful additional perspectives include: Future teammates. Cross functional colleagues. Employees who recently joined. Employees who have remained with the team for several years. If several people independently describe similar patterns—such as regular coaching, stable priorities or frequent last minute changes—the consistency itself becomes useful evidence. It is also worth asking how long the manager has been in the role, how frequently the team changes, and whether recent departures reflect promotions or unwanted turnover. These questions help distinguish temporary disruption from recurring management problems. A practical way to weigh manager quality"},{"title":"Job Fit","url":"/job-fit/","description":"Interview impressions become more reliable when you turn them into specific questions about daily work, decisions and pressure.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Job Offers","level":3,"text":"Job Fit How Do You Test Job Fit? Job Fit Job Fit How Do You Test Job Fit? Interview impressions become more reliable when you turn them into specific questions about daily work, decisions and pressure. Interview impressions become more reliable when you turn them into specific questions about daily work, decisions and pressure. mechanism Turning vague impressions into checkable assumptions Questions that reveal manager and team habits Signals that culture claims may be oversold improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers job fit Introduction Accepting a job offer always involves uncertainty, but you can reduce that uncertainty by treating your impressions as hypotheses rather than facts. Interviews are carefully managed events, whereas your experience of the job will be shaped by ordinary days: how priorities change, how managers respond under pressure , how meetings are run, and what happens when deadlines slip. The aim is not to predict every detail, but to replace vague feelings such as \"the team seemed nice\" or \"the culture felt collaborative\" with specific, testable assumptions . Organisational psychologists have long argued that candidates make better decisions when they receive a realistic picture of both the advantages and the challenges of a role. This idea, known as a realistic job preview , helps applicants judge whether the work genuinely matches their preferences rather than relying on polished recruitment messages. U.S. Office of Personnel Management+2ResearchGate [opm.gov] opm.gov realistic job previews Office of Personnel ManagementRealistic Job PreviewsA Realistic Job Preview (RJP) is a recruiting approach used by an organization to com... Turn vague impressions into checkable assumptions People naturally fill gaps in information with optimistic assumptions, especially after receiving an offer. A strong interview, an engaging manager or an attractive salary can create a \"halo effect\", where one positive feature colours every other judgement. Instead of asking whether the job \"feels right\", translate each impression into a question that can be verified. For example: Initial impressionBetter assumption to test\"The team seems supportive.\"How are new employees trained? Who helps when someone gets stuck?\"The work sounds interesting.\"What tasks fill a normal Tuesday rather than an exceptional project?\"The manager seems flexible.\"How are priorities changed when deadlines collide?\"There are good growth opportunities.\"Which employees were promoted during the past two years, and what did they do to earn those promotions?\"The workload sounds manageable.\"What does a genuinely busy week look like in hours, meetings and expected availability ? This approach changes the interview from a sales conversation into an information gathering exercise. Behavioural research on recruitment recommends treating interviews primarily as opportunities to collect relevant evidence rather than making rapid overall judgements. [CIPD] cipd.org Make sure tests are relevant to the job and fit for purpose... Ask questions that expose daily work rather than company slogans Culture is difficult to judge from statements such as \"we value collaboration\" or \"we move fast\". Everyday routines reveal much more. Questions that often produce concrete answers include: \"Can you describe a typical week in this role?\" \"What usually causes people in this position to have difficult weeks?\" \"How often do priorities change unexpectedly?\" \"What decisions can someone make independently?\" \"What meetings are fixed every week?\" \"How is success measured during the first six months?\" \"What separates someone who performs adequately from someone who performs exceptionally?\" These questions encourage interviewers to describe behaviour instead of aspirations. If speaking with future colleagues, ask about ordinary working practices rather than satisfaction alone: \"When someone makes a mistake, what usually happens?\" \"How often do people ask each other for help?\" \"What surprises new hires most?\" \"Which parts of the job are harder than candidates expect?\" Specific examples are generally more informative than broad opinions. A response describing a recent project, deadline or disagreement provides stronger evidence than statements such as \"everyone gets on well\". Should You Accept Or Decline Your Job Offer? How To Evaluate A Job Offer. Channel: A Life After Layoff Open on YouTube Test the manager, not just the organisation For many employees, the immediate manager influences day to day satisfaction more than the company's public reputation. Rather than asking whether the manager is \"good\", explore how they actually work. Useful areas include: Feedback : How frequently do one to one meetings occur? Is feedback mainly corrective or continuous? Decision making: Which decisions require approval, and which are delegated? Availability: When urgent problems appear, how quickly is support normally provided? Expectations: How are priorities communicated when several tasks compete? Development: What has the manager done to help previous team members progress? Where possible, compare answers from multiple interviewers. Consistency increases confidence. Significant differences may indicate that expectations vary between individuals or that important practices are not well established. Look for evidence that claims match behaviour Every employer presents itself positively during recruitment. The useful question is whether the evidence matches the message. Examples include: \"We support learning.\" Look for funded training, protected learning time, recent promotions and examples of employees acquiring new responsibilities. \"We have excellent work life balance.\" Ask how often people work evenings, how annual leave is protected and whether urgent messages outside working hours are genuinely unusual. \"We're highly collaborative.\" Ask how cross team projects are organised, how disagreements are resolved and whether performance is mainly measured individually or collectively. \"We trust people.\" Ask which decisions junior employees can make without approval and whether flexible working arrangements are widely used or require special permission. Claims supported by concrete examples deserve more confidence than repeated values statements. Questions to Ask at the End of an Interview: CULTURE QUESTIONS Channel: Life Work Balance Open on YouTube Request a realistic picture of the job Many organisations now deliberately provide realistic job previews because they improve long term matching between candidates and roles. A realistic preview includes both attractive and difficult aspects of the position rather than acting purely as recruitment marketing. Research suggests that better informed candidates are more likely to make decisions they remain satisfied with after joining. U.S. Office of Personnel Management+2Oxford Education [opm.gov] opm.gov realistic job previews Office of Personnel ManagementRealistic Job PreviewsA Realistic Job Preview (RJP) is a recruiting approach used by an organization to com... If one is not offered, you can often create your own by asking: \"What part of this job do people usually underestimate?\" \"Why have previous people left this role?\" \"What usually causes the most stress?\" \"What would make someone decide this role is not for them?\" \"If I joined tomorrow, what would probably be the hardest adjustment during the first month?\" An employer willing to discuss genuine difficulties often provides more trustworthy information than one presenting only positives. Signals that culture claims may be oversold No single warning sign proves that a job is unsuitable. However, several patterns deserve closer examination. Watch for situations where: Every answer remains abstract despite requests for examples. Different interviewers describe conflicting expectations. Nobody can explain how performance is actually evaluated. Questions about workload receive defensive or evasive responses. Staff describe frequent \"firefighting\" while leaders insist workloads are consistently manageable. High turnover is dismissed without explanation. Interviewers repeatedly describe overtime as \"just part of being passionate.\" Equally, be cautious if every challenge is portrayed as exciting. Healthy organisations usually acknowledge that certain aspects of the work are demanding while explaining how they are managed. Why you should consider Realistic Job Previews Channel: Corvirtus Open on YouTube Small tests that reveal a great deal Some of the most informative evidence comes from interactions outside formal interviews. Where appropriate, consider: Meeting future colleagues informally. Asking to observe part of a normal team meeting. Reviewing documents produced by the team if confidentiality permits. Completing a realistic work sample or practical exercise. Speaking with someone who recently joined the department. These experiences reduce reliance on imagination because they expose actual"},{"title":"Total Pay","url":"/total-pay/","description":"A job offer's real value depends on benefits, variable pay, time costs and risk, not just the headline salary.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Job Offers","level":3,"text":"Total Pay What Is the Offer Really Worth? Total Pay Total Pay What Is the Offer Really Worth? A job offer's real value depends on benefits, variable pay, time costs and risk, not just the headline salary. A job offer's real value depends on benefits, variable pay, time costs and risk, not just the headline salary. mechanism Base pay, bonuses and benefits as a full package Commute, hours and expenses as hidden costs Comparing guaranteed value with uncertain upside improving your thinking and analytical skills job offers total pay Introduction A job offer is worth more—or less—than its advertised salary. The real value comes from total compensation : guaranteed pay, bonuses, pension contributions, insurance, paid leave, flexibility, equity, and the time and money you must spend to do the job. Analytical thinking helps you avoid anchoring on the headline salary by comparing what is certain, what is conditional, and what each offer actually delivers in daily life. This matters because benefits represent a substantial share of employment costs. In the United States, for example, employer paid benefits account for roughly 30% of private sector compensation costs on average, demonstrating that two jobs with identical salaries can differ significantly in overall value. [Bureau of Labor Statistics] bls.gov • Supplemental pay Bureau of Labor StatisticsEmployer Costs for Employee Compensation March 2026June 10, 2026 — Total benefit costs consist of five major... Published: June 10, 2026 Base pay, bonuses and benefits form one package The simplest mistake is treating salary as the entire reward. Instead, think of an offer as three layers: Guaranteed compensation: base salary and any fixed allowances. Variable compensation: annual bonuses, commission, profit sharing or long term incentives. Benefits: pension contributions, health insurance, life insurance, paid leave, parental leave, training budgets, wellness benefits and other employer funded support. Many employers describe this as a total compensation or total rewards package because employees receive value through multiple channels rather than wages alone. While exact packages differ by employer and country, compensation frameworks consistently distinguish between fixed pay, variable pay and indirect benefits. [chrc ccdp.gc.ca] chrc ccdp.gc.ca Open source on gc.ca. For example, compare two offers: ElementOffer AOffer BBase salary£62,000£58,000Employer pension contribution3%10%Annual bonusUp to 20%NonePrivate health coverNoYesExtra annual leaveStandard days The higher salary is not automatically the better financial choice. If the bonus is uncertain while the pension and additional leave are guaranteed, Offer B may provide more dependable long term value. Hidden costs reduce real compensation Compensation should also be measured against what the job requires from you. A salary increase can disappear surprisingly quickly when the role demands longer commuting, unpaid overtime or expensive travel. These costs rarely appear in offer letters, yet they affect both finances and quality of life. Key hidden costs include: Commuting expenses: fuel, rail fares, parking or vehicle depreciation. Commuting time: hours that reduce leisure, family time or opportunities for further learning. Longer working hours: reducing effective hourly earnings. Meals, clothing or equipment: if these are not reimbursed. Relocation or regular travel: particularly when allowances only partly cover expenses. One useful calculation is your effective hourly pay : Annual guaranteed compensation ÷ total annual hours actually devoted to work (including routine commuting where relevant). A role paying £65,000 but requiring frequent unpaid evening work may deliver a lower effective hourly return than a £58,000 role with predictable hours and a short commute. 9 Benefits That Can Beat a Higher Salary Channel: JobLeads Open on YouTube Guaranteed value versus uncertain upside Variable compensation deserves special scrutiny because people naturally overweight optimistic outcomes . Ask what is guaranteed Base salary is usually contractual. Bonuses, commissions and equity often are not. Questions worth answering include: Is the bonus discretionary or formula based? How often has it actually been paid? What percentage of employees achieve the target? Can the company change the scheme? Does payment require remaining employed on a specific date? A \"20% bonus opportunity\" may have little practical value if most employees historically receive only a fraction of it. Equity can be valuable—or worthless Start up and high growth companies frequently offer share options or restricted stock instead of higher cash salaries. Equity may become highly valuable if the company succeeds, but several uncertainties affect its value: the company's future performance; dilution from future fundraising; vesting schedules; tax treatment; whether there will ever be a liquid market to sell the shares. Analytically, equity should usually be viewed as potential upside , not as a guaranteed replacement for cash compensation. Benefits often have larger financial value than they appear Many benefits are overlooked because they are not paid directly into a bank account. Examples include: employer pension contributions; private medical insurance; disability or income protection insurance; life insurance; enhanced parental leave; paid professional qualifications; childcare support; employee share purchase plans; additional paid holiday. Some of these would be expensive to purchase independently. Others reduce financial risk rather than increasing income. A generous income protection policy, for example, may provide more practical security than a modest salary increase. Research on total compensation also suggests that employees' perceptions of the complete package—not salary alone—are associated with higher job satisfaction and organisational commitment, although workplace culture and management quality remain important influences alongside compensation. [Redalyc.org] redalyc.org This cross sectional and confirmatory research has... What Is Included In My Total Compensation Package? Channel: Labor and Employment Law Expert Open on YouTube Compare offers using expected value rather than best case thinking Good analytical decisions separate certainty from possibility. A practical comparison can be organised into three columns: CategoryQuestionsGuaranteed valueWhat will I definitely receive?Probable valueWhat is likely, based on realistic assumptions ? Upside potentialWhat could happen if everything goes well? Suppose two offers are presented: Offer X: £70,000 salary with a bonus \"up to 25%\". Offer Y: £66,000 salary with a 12% employer pension contribution, generous health cover and an extra week's annual leave. If historical evidence suggests that employees usually receive only a 5% bonus, the expected financial difference narrows substantially. The guaranteed benefits in Offer Y may outweigh the uncertain additional cash in Offer X. This approach helps avoid optimism bias—the tendency to assume favourable outcomes without sufficient evidence. Questions that reveal the real value of an offer Before accepting a role, seek evidence rather than relying on marketing language. Useful questions include: How is the bonus calculated? What percentage of employees earned the target bonus last year? What pension contribution does the employer make? How many days are employees typically expected to work beyond contracted hours? Are commuting or travel costs reimbursed? How does equity vest, and what happens if I leave? Which benefits begin immediately, and which require a qualifying period? Specific answers are generally more informative than broad statements such as \"excellent benefits\" or \"competitive compensation\". Base Salary vs Total Compensation What Actually Matters Channel: CorporateHR Open on YouTube A better mental model The most reliable way to compare offers is to think of compensation as a portfolio rather than a single number. A strong offer combines: substantial guaranteed income; valuable employer funded benefits; [peoplemanagingpeople.com] peoplemanagingpeople.com total compensation HR's Guide to Total Compensation in 20268 Jan 2026 — What Is Total Compensation? Total compensation refers to the complete package of pay... realistic—not merely theoretical—variable pay; manageable time and commuting costs; an acceptable balance between security and upside. Looking beyond base salary does not mean ignoring pay. Instead, it means recognising that salary is only one component of what an employer is actually offering. Careful comparison of guaranteed value, hidden costs and uncertain rewards produces decisions that are less vulnerable to anchoring, wishful thinking and persuasive marketing language. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Is the Offer Really Worth?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article."},{"title":"Metacognition","url":"/metacognition/","description":"Metacognition helps people monitor understanding, choose strategies, and notice when confidence outruns evidence.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Metacognition How Do You Check Your Own Thinking? Metacognition Metacognition How Do You Check Your Own Thinking? Metacognition helps people monitor understanding, choose strategies, and notice when confidence outruns evidence. Metacognition helps people monitor understanding, choose strategies, and notice when confidence outruns evidence. mechanism What metacognition means Before during after questions Strategy switching when stuck improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition Introduction Metacognition means checking the quality of your own thinking while you are thinking. It is the skill behind questions such as: “Do I actually understand this?”, “What evidence supports my confidence?”, “Is this strategy working?”, and “Should I switch approach?” In the wider task of improving thinking and analytical skills, metacognition matters because it turns reasoning from a private blur into something you can monitor, test and adjust. The core mechanism is simple but powerful: first you monitor your own understanding, confidence and progress; then you use that information to control what you do next. Research on metacognition often describes this monitoring control loop: people judge how well their thinking or memory is going, then use that judgement to change behaviour, allocate effort, seek help, slow down, self test, or abandon an unproductive strategy. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentThe relation between metacognitive monitoring and control22 Sept 2009 — Monitoring means the abili... What metacognition means Metacognition is often described as “thinking about thinking”, but that phrase can sound more mystical than it is. In practice , it is a set of ordinary but disciplined mental checks. A learner, analyst or decision maker notices the state of their own mind and asks whether that state is a reliable guide. A useful distinction is between metacognitive knowledge and metacognitive regulation . Metacognitive knowledge is what you know about yourself, the task and available strategies: for example, “I tend to skim too quickly when I think I already know the topic”, or “this problem needs a diagram, not just verbal reasoning”. Metacognitive regulation is the active process of planning, monitoring and evaluating your thinking while doing the work. MIT’s Teaching and Learning Lab describes metacognition as using knowledge of the task, strategies and oneself to plan learning, monitor progress and evaluate outcomes . [tll.mit.edu] tll.mit.edu Teaching + Learning Lab MITMetacognition is the process by which learners use knowledge of the task at hand, knowledge of learning stra... That makes metacognition different from merely being reflective after the fact. Reflection can be useful, but metacognition is more immediate. It can happen before a task, while the task is unfolding, and after the result is known. The Education Endowment Foundation summarises metacognitive and self regulated approaches as helping learners think about their own learning explicitly, often through strategies for planning, monitoring and evaluating. [EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and self regulationMetacognition is the learner's ability to be aware of, reflect on, and direct their thinking. Self re... For analytical thinking, the same structure applies beyond classrooms. Before reading a report, you can ask what would count as strong evidence. During the reading, you can notice when a claim feels persuasive but thinly supported. After forming a view, you can ask what you would have predicted in advance and whether you are updating honestly. Metacognition is therefore not a separate “study skill”; it is the control system for better judgement. The monitoring control loop The most useful way to understand metacognition is as a feedback loop . At the object level, you are doing the task: reading, solving, comparing evidence, writing, estimating, diagnosing or deciding. At the metacognitive level, you are watching that task unfold: “Am I making progress?”, “Which part is unclear?”, “How confident am I?”, “What does my confidence rest on?” If monitoring detects a problem, control should change the next move. This is why metacognition improves thinking rather than merely describing it. Monitoring without control becomes anxious self commentary. Control without monitoring becomes random strategy switching. The benefit comes when the two are linked: a person notices a mismatch between confidence and evidence, then changes behaviour. A practical example is reading a difficult article. A non metacognitive reader may keep moving because the sentences feel familiar. A metacognitive reader checks for usable understanding: “Could I explain the central claim without looking?”, “Can I identify the evidence?”, “Which paragraph would I challenge?” If the answer is no, the reader can slow down, reread selectively, summarise, draw a concept map, look up a term, or test recall. Research on metacognitive measurement reflects this same structure. A review by Rivers notes that metacognition can be measured differently depending on whether the target is knowledge, monitoring or control: surveys can assess metacognitive knowledge, while predictions compared with actual performance can assess monitoring accuracy, and learning decisions can reveal control. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Measuring Metacognitive Knowledge, Monitoring, and Control...by ML Rivers · 2020 · Cited by 78 — Metacognition can be measured in a v... Before, during and after questions Metacognition becomes more useful when it is turned into timely questions. The point is not to interrogate every thought endlessly. The point is to put a few checks at the moments when poor thinking usually slips through. Before: set the target and choose a strategy Before starting, metacognitive questions prevent vague effort. They make the task concrete enough to monitor. Good before questions include: What exactly am I trying to work out? A clear question stops the mind from collecting interesting but irrelevant information. What kind of task is this? A factual recall task, a causal explanation, a judgement under uncertainty and a creative design problem need different strategies. What would count as good evidence? This protects against accepting whatever evidence is easiest to find. What is my first instinct, and why might it be wrong? This helps separate a useful hunch from premature certainty. Which strategy should I start with? For example: self testing, drawing the problem, listing assumptions, comparing explanations, seeking a counterexample or checking base rates. The Education Endowment Foundation argues that metacognitive strategies are most effective when taught and applied within normal subject content, rather than as detached “thinking skills” lessons. That principle also applies to adults: metacognition works best when attached to a real task, not practised as abstract self improvement language. [EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and self regulationMetacognition is the learner's ability to be aware of, reflect on, and direct their thinking. Self re... How to Think About Thinking — The Metacognition Explained Channel: AI Labs: Brain Science & Nexus Open on YouTube During: check whether the current approach is working During the task, metacognition is about detecting drift. Many thinking errors happen because a person continues with a strategy that no longer fits the problem. Useful during questions include: Can I explain what I just learned in my own words? Which part is still unclear? Am I confusing familiarity with understanding? Is my confidence rising because the evidence is stronger, or because the idea feels fluent? What would I expect to see if my current explanation were wrong? Am I making progress, or just spending time? This matters because people often misread ease as mastery. Research on “illusions of competence” shows that learners can feel they know material because it looks familiar during study, even when they may struggle to retrieve it later. Koriat and Bjork’s work on study judgement found that such illusions can be reduced when learners are made more sensitive to the conditions under which they will later need to retrieve knowledge. [Springer] link.springer.com Open source on springer.com. After: evaluate the result and update the method After completing a task, the main question is not simply “Was I right?” It is “What did this reveal about how I think?” Good after questions include: What did I predict, and what happened? Where was I overconfident or underconfident? Which strategy helped most? Where did I get stuck, and what got me moving again? What should I do differently next time? What evidence would make me revise this conclusion? The after stage is where calibration improves. Calibration means the match between confidence and actual accuracy. If someone is highly confident when"},{"title":"After Review","url":"/after-review/","description":"The best after-task review asks what your result revealed about your judgement, not only whether you were right.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Metacognition","level":3,"text":"After Review What Did This Reveal About Your Thinking? After Review After Review What Did This Reveal About Your Thinking? The best after task review asks what your result revealed about your judgement, not only whether you were right. The best after task review asks what your result revealed about your judgement, not only whether you were right. policy intervention Comparing predictions with what happened Finding overconfidence, underconfidence, and stuck points Turning one result into a better next strategy improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition after review Introduction An after action review is most valuable when it improves your future judgement rather than merely explaining a past result. The central question is not simply, “Was I right?”, but “What did this outcome reveal about how I was thinking?” That shift turns reflection into a metacognitive tool: instead of rewarding lucky successes or dwelling on obvious mistakes , it examines the quality of the reasoning that produced them. This approach is especially important because outcomes alone are unreliable teachers. A good decision can lead to a poor result through bad luck, while a weak decision can appear successful because circumstances happened to favour it. Effective after action reviews therefore compare predictions with reality, examine confidence as well as correctness, and identify thinking habits that should be repeated, adjusted or abandoned. Research on after action reviews, judgement biases and metacognition consistently shows that structured reflection is most useful when it focuses on decision processes rather than outcomes alone. [Better Evaluation+2American Psychological Association] betterevaluation.org Better EvaluationAfter action reviewThe after action review (AAR) is a simple method for facilitating an assessment of organisational per... Comparing Predictions with What Actually Happened The foundation of a useful review is a comparison between what you expected beforehand and what actually occurred afterwards. Without an explicit prediction, hindsight quickly fills the gap by convincing you that events were obvious all along. Many after action review frameworks therefore begin with questions such as: [youtube.com] youtube.com An Effective After Action Review (AAR Find Out Why You're REALLY Losing MTG Limited The Reflect Method... What did I expect to happen? What actually happened? Why was there a difference? What should I change next time? This sequence originated in military training but has since spread to healthcare, education and organisational learning because it keeps attention on learning rather than blame. The comparison between expectation and reality creates a measurable feedback loop instead of relying on vague memories. [Better Evaluation+2My College] betterevaluation.org Better EvaluationAfter action reviewThe after action review (AAR) is a simple method for facilitating an assessment of organisational per... For individual thinking, the same structure works well after completing an analysis, making a forecast or solving a difficult problem. Before reviewing the result, reconstruct your original reasoning as accurately as possible: What evidence did I rely on? Which assumptions mattered most? How confident was I? What alternative outcomes did I consider? Only after documenting those answers should you compare them with reality. This preserves information that hindsight would otherwise distort. Avoid Reviewing Only the Outcome A common mistake is treating success as proof of good thinking and failure as proof of poor thinking. Decision researchers call this outcome bias : judging the quality of a decision primarily by what happened rather than by the information available when the decision was made. A well reasoned decision can fail because of chance, while a poorly reasoned decision can succeed through luck. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Outcome bias Outcome bias For that reason, an after action review should separate two questions: [youtube.com] youtube.com An Effective After Action Review (AAR Find Out Why You're REALLY Losing MTG Limited The Reflect Method... 1. Was the reasoning sound given what was known at the time? 2. What did the outcome teach that was genuinely unavailable beforehand? Keeping these questions separate prevents every bad result from becoming evidence that the original thinking was necessarily flawed. How This Poker Strategy Solves Your Toughest Money Decisions (Thinking in Bets) Channel: Trip2Wealth Open on YouTube Finding Overconfidence, Underconfidence and Stuck Points The most valuable discoveries usually concern calibration: whether confidence matched reality. Detecting overconfidence Overconfidence does not simply mean feeling confident. It means expressing more certainty than the evidence justified. Reviews often reveal patterns such as: dismissing alternative explanations too early; stopping information gathering once a preferred answer appeared; expressing near certainty despite substantial uncertainty; treating familiarity as evidence of accuracy. Research consistently identifies overconfidence as one of the most common judgement biases affecting professional decision making across domains. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Impact of Cognitive Biases on Professionals' Decision...by V Berthet · 2022 · Cited by 305 — First, the literature reviewed shows... Rather than asking, \"Why was I wrong?\", ask: What made me so certain? Which evidence did I ignore? At what point should I have become less confident? These questions target the thinking habit rather than the specific mistake. Detecting underconfidence Reviews also uncover the opposite problem. Perhaps your reasoning was largely correct, but you repeatedly delayed acting because you underestimated your understanding. Alternatively, you may have generated useful ideas but abandoned them too quickly after encountering uncertainty. Repeated patterns of unnecessary hesitation deserve as much attention as repeated overconfidence because both reduce judgement quality. Identifying stuck points Sometimes the review shows neither excessive confidence nor excessive doubt. Instead, it reveals recurring bottlenecks: becoming trapped in the first framing of a problem; collecting information without integrating it; spending too long refining low value details; failing to notice when a strategy had stopped working. These \"stuck points\" often indicate that future effort should focus on changing process rather than acquiring more knowledge. An Effective After Action Review (AAR) Channel: TEC Canada Open on YouTube Protecting the Review from Hindsight Bias One reason after action reviews often fail is hindsight bias: once the outcome is known, people remember their earlier beliefs as being much closer to reality than they actually were. This creates the illusion that they \"knew it all along\", reducing opportunities for genuine learning. The Decision Lab+2Carlson School of Management [thedecisionlab.com] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabHindsight BiasThe hindsight bias describes our tendency to look back at an unpredictable event and think it was easily pr... Several practical habits reduce this problem: Record predictions before the outcome is known. Estimate probabilities rather than making absolute claims. Keep notes explaining why you believed what you believed. Compare today's review against those original records instead of memory. Research also suggests that outcome feedback has two competing effects. It can distort memory through hindsight bias, but it can also improve future judgement when people extract diagnostic information from the feedback rather than simply rewriting their memories of earlier beliefs. [Carnegie Mellon University] cmu.edu Outcome Feedback Carnegie Mellon UniversityOutcome Feedback: Hindsight and Informationby SJ Hoch · 1989 · Cited by 247 — Although \"hindsight bias\" researc... Find Out Why You're REALLY Losing MTG Limited The Reflect Method Channel: The Limited Lab Open on YouTube Turning One Result into a Better Next Strategy An after action review becomes valuable only when it changes future behaviour. [youtube.com] youtube.com An Effective After Action Review (AAR Find Out Why You're REALLY Losing MTG Limited The Reflect Method... Instead of producing a long list of observations, identify one or two specific adjustments that directly address the thinking pattern you discovered. For example: Review findingBetter next strategyI became certain after only two supporting facts.Delay committing until deliberately seeking contradictory evidence.I repeatedly underestimated task complexity.Break future work into explicit intermediate estimates before starting.I ignored uncertainty in my forecasts.Record probability ranges rather than single predictions.I abandoned promising approaches too early.Define in advance how much evidence is needed before changing strategy. The improvement target is the decision process, not merely the outcome."},{"title":"Before Checks","url":"/before-checks/","description":"Starting with the right question, evidence standard, and strategy prevents vague effort from becoming wasted effort.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Metacognition","level":3,"text":"Before Checks The Questions to Ask Before You Begin Before Checks Before Checks The Questions to Ask Before You Begin Starting with the right question, evidence standard, and strategy prevents vague effort from becoming wasted effort. Starting with the right question, evidence standard, and strategy prevents vague effort from becoming wasted effort. policy intervention Clarifying the exact task before gathering information Choosing strategies for different kinds of problems Setting evidence standards before first impressions harden improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition before checks Introduction Many reasoning failures begin before any evidence is gathered or analysis starts. The task is vague, the success criteria are unclear, and the chosen method does not match the problem. Metacognition is often described as monitoring and regulating your thinking while you work, but one of its highest leverage uses happens before the work begins: asking questions that help you choose the right strategy. Research on metacognition consistently identifies planning as a core component of effective thinking, alongside monitoring and evaluation. People who deliberately analyse the task, consider available strategies, and clarify goals before acting tend to learn and solve problems more effectively. [tll.mit.edu+2EEF] mit.edu Metacognition Teaching + Learning Lab MITMetacognition is the process by which learners use knowledge of the task at hand, knowledge of learning stra... The practical value is straightforward. A fact finding task, a forecasting task, a creative task, and a decision task may all look similar at first glance, yet each requires different evidence, tools, and standards of proof. The questions asked before starting often determine whether effort becomes insight or wasted motion. Clarifying the Exact Task Before Gathering Information One of the most common thinking errors is solving the wrong problem well. People frequently begin collecting information before defining what they are actually trying to accomplish. The result is often impressive looking activity that produces little value. Metacognitive planning starts with understanding the nature of the task itself: what kind of problem is this, what outcome is required, and what would count as success? Research frameworks on metacognition emphasise knowledge of the task as a prerequisite for selecting effective strategies. [tll.mit.edu] mit.edu Metacognition Teaching + Learning Lab MITMetacognition is the process by which learners use knowledge of the task at hand, knowledge of learning stra... Before beginning, ask: What specific question am I trying to answer? Is this a problem of explanation, prediction, decision making, or creation? What output is actually required? What would make me conclude that the task is complete? What information is necessary, and what is merely interesting? Consider the difference between these two tasks: “Learn about electric vehicles.” [simplypsychology.org] simplypsychology.org Learn about psychological theories, classic studies, mental health disorders, Simply Psychology: Study Guides for Psychology StudentsComplete guide to psychology for students, educators & enthusiasts... “Decide whether an electric vehicle would reduce my total transport costs over five years.” The first invites endless information gathering. The second immediately suggests relevant evidence, comparison criteria, and calculations. The strategic path becomes clearer because the task has been defined more precisely. A useful warning sign is when the objective cannot be expressed in one clear sentence. Ambiguous objectives often generate unfocused analysis. Mastering Metacognition: Learn How to Learn and Become Your Own Teacher Channel: Powerful Learning Open on YouTube Choosing Strategies for Different Kinds of Problems Once the task is defined, the next question is whether the planned strategy matches the problem type. Research on metacognition highlights that effective learners and problem solvers do not rely on a single universal method. Instead, they develop knowledge about which strategies work for which tasks and adjust accordingly. [tll.mit.edu+2PMC] mit.edu Metacognition Teaching + Learning Lab MITMetacognition is the process by which learners use knowledge of the task at hand, knowledge of learning stra... Different problems benefit from different approaches: Problem typeUseful strategy questionsFact findingWhich sources are most reliable?DiagnosisWhat alternative explanations could fit the evidence?Decision makingWhat criteria matter most and what trade offs exist?ForecastingWhat assumptions am I making about the future?Creative workHow many alternatives should I generate before choosing one?LearningHow will I test whether I truly understand this? For example, when diagnosing why a project failed, gathering more facts may not be enough. The critical strategy may be generating competing explanations and actively trying to disprove them. By contrast, when estimating future demand, the key challenge may be identifying assumptions and uncertainties rather than collecting additional historical details. A simple metacognitive question can prevent strategic mismatch: “If someone highly skilled at this task were starting today, would they approach it the same way I am about to?” That question often reveals when a familiar but inappropriate method is being applied simply because it is comfortable. Setting Evidence Standards Before First Impressions Harden Many analytical mistakes arise because standards of proof are chosen after evidence has already begun to influence judgement. Psychological research on reasoning repeatedly shows that people are vulnerable to confirmation bias: once an initial belief forms, later evidence is often interpreted through that belief rather than evaluated independently. One practical defence is to establish evidence standards before exposure to persuasive information. This turns evaluation into a pre commitment exercise rather than a post hoc justification process. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Metacognition: Monitoring and Controlling One's...29 Jul 2020 — Metacognitive monitoring refers to activities that inv... Useful questions include: What evidence would genuinely change my mind ? What evidence would count against my preferred conclusion? How strong must the evidence be before I act? Am I looking for certainty, probability, or merely enough confidence to proceed? What level of uncertainty is acceptable in this context? The required evidence standard should depend on the consequences of being wrong. A low stakes decision may justify acting on limited information. A high stakes decision involving safety, large financial commitments, or long term consequences often requires stronger evidence and more deliberate verification. Establishing these standards beforehand reduces the temptation to lower the bar for evidence that supports a preferred conclusion and raise it for evidence that challenges it. The Three Part Before Task Check Many planning frameworks in metacognition organise thinking around planning, monitoring, and evaluation. Before task questions fit into the planning phase and can be condensed into three checks. [EEF+2CloudFront] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and Self Regulated LearningTeachers should support pupils to plan, monitor, and evaluate their learning through explicit... Task Check Ask: What exactly am I trying to accomplish? What does success look like? What constraints matter? This prevents effort from drifting away from the original goal. Metacognition A Brief Explainer Channel: Education Endowment Foundation Open on YouTube Strategy Check Ask: What kind of problem is this? Which approach best fits this problem type? What alternatives should I consider? This improves the match between task and method. Evidence Check Ask: What would count as convincing evidence? What would disconfirm my current view? How confident do I need to be before acting? This establishes quality standards before judgement becomes emotionally invested in a particular answer. A Practical Example: Investigating a Claim Imagine encountering a surprising claim online. A weak approach begins immediately with searching for supporting examples. A stronger metacognitive approach pauses first. Task clarification: Am I trying to determine whether the claim is true, estimate how likely it is, or decide whether it matters? Strategy selection: Should I look for original sources, expert assessments, statistical evidence, or competing explanations? Evidence standards: What quality of evidence would be sufficient? Would anecdotes count? Would I require independent verification? Only after these questions are answered does information gathering begin. The difference may appear minor, but it fundamentally changes the investigation. Instead of collecting material that feels persuasive, the thinker collects"},{"title":"Confidence","url":"/confidence/","description":"Confidence becomes more useful when it is checked against the quality of the evidence behind it.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Metacognition","level":3,"text":"Confidence When Confidence Gets Ahead of Evidence Confidence Confidence When Confidence Gets Ahead of Evidence Confidence becomes more useful when it is checked against the quality of the evidence behind it. Confidence becomes more useful when it is checked against the quality of the evidence behind it. mechanism Why fluent explanations can feel true Questions that separate evidence from ease Using predictions and outcomes to improve calibration improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition confidence Introduction Confidence is useful only when it tracks reality. In metacognition , one of the most important skills is learning to match how certain you feel to the strength of the evidence you actually possess. When confidence rises faster than evidence, judgement becomes vulnerable to error. When confidence is lower than the evidence warrants , good opportunities can be missed. The goal is not to become less confident, but to become better calibrated. Calibration refers to the relationship between confidence and accuracy. A well calibrated thinker who is 70% confident in a set of judgements should be correct roughly 70% of the time. A poorly calibrated thinker may feel 95% certain while being right only half the time. Research on overconfidence consistently finds that people often express greater certainty than their accuracy justifies, especially in difficult tasks and uncertain environments. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Overconfidence effect Overconfidence effect Within metacognition, calibration matters because it acts as a quality control mechanism. It forces a distinction between feeling convinced and being well supported. Why Fluent Explanations Can Feel True One reason confidence often outruns evidence is that the human mind uses mental shortcuts when judging certainty. A particularly important shortcut is processing fluency: the subjective feeling that something is easy to understand. Information that is clear, familiar, smoothly written, or repeatedly encountered often feels more credible than information that is difficult to process. The problem is that ease of processing and quality of evidence are not the same thing. Research on metacognitive illusions shows that fluency can create an “illusion of knowing”, leading people to believe they understand or remember material better than they actually do. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMetacognitive Illusion in Category Learning NIHby J Wang · 2019 · Cited by 18 — This study revealed that processing fluency and held beliefs were two factors that cause this m... This creates a common pattern: A claim is presented clearly. Understanding feels effortless. The feeling of ease is mistaken for evidence. Confidence increases without a corresponding increase in justification. Studies of learning repeatedly find that people often overestimate future recall when information feels easy to process during study. Learners can leave a session feeling highly confident yet perform poorly when tested later. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Illusions of Competence and Overestimation of Associative...by AD Castel · 2007 · Cited by 211 — We hypothesized that encoding flu... The same mechanism appears outside education. A persuasive presentation, a confident speaker, or a coherent narrative can generate strong feelings of certainty even when the underlying evidence is weak. The mind unconsciously substitutes one question—“How easy does this feel?”—for a harder question—“How well supported is this?” [MDPI] mdpi.com For example, high fluency with shallow Metacognitive Monitoring in Reading Comprehensionby V Markovich · 2026 — This may result in an “illusion of knowing”, particularly wh... This is why metacognitive monitoring requires attention not only to conclusions but also to the source of confidence. A feeling of certainty is itself evidence about a mental state, not necessarily evidence about reality. Cognition Lecture 6 5 Metamemory Channel: Paul Merritt Open on YouTube Questions That Separate Evidence from Ease A practical way to improve calibration is to challenge confidence before accepting it. The key is to examine whether confidence is being generated by evidence or by psychological comfort. Several questions are especially useful. What observations would change my mind ? Confidence based on evidence should be responsive to new evidence. If no imaginable observation could alter a conclusion, certainty may be resting on commitment rather than justification. What is the strongest evidence against my view? People naturally search for supporting information. Calibration improves when confidence is tested against contradictory evidence rather than built exclusively from confirming examples. Am I recalling evidence or explanations? A detailed explanation can create a powerful sense of understanding. Yet being able to repeat an explanation is not the same as possessing independent evidence for it. The distinction becomes important when evaluating forecasts, diagnoses, interpretations, and strategic decisions. How much of my confidence comes from familiarity? Repeated exposure increases perceived truthfulness. Familiar claims often feel more credible simply because they have been encountered before, not because they have stronger support. Could I assign a probability instead of using a certainty label? Statements such as “definitely”, “obviously”, or “there is no doubt” hide uncertainty. Numerical probabilities force greater precision. A thinker who says “I am 65% confident” is making a claim that can later be evaluated against outcomes. These questions work because they redirect attention away from the feeling of knowing and back towards the reasons for knowing. Using Predictions and Outcomes to Improve Calibration The most reliable way to improve calibration is to compare confidence with what actually happens. Prediction creates accountability. Before an outcome is known, a person records a judgement and attaches a probability to it. Later, the outcome provides feedback . Over time, patterns emerge. If events assigned an 80% probability occur only half the time, confidence is too high. If events assigned a 40% probability occur far more often than expected, confidence is too low. Calibration improves when these mismatches become visible. [PMC+2ResearchGate] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAccuracy of forecasts in strategic intelligence NIHby DR Mandel · 2014 · Cited by 161 — Overconfidence, in contrast, indicates that forecasts are communicated with unwarranted cer... Forecasting research provides a clear example. Philip Tetlock's work on expert judgement found that many experts expressed confidence levels that exceeded their actual predictive accuracy. Later forecasting tournaments showed that performance improved when forecasters regularly updated beliefs, expressed uncertainty probabilistically, and received continuous feedback on outcomes. [ResearchGate+2Cambridge University Press & Assessment] researchgate.net Research Gate Expert Political Judgment: How Good is It? How can We...Here, Philip E. Tetlock explores what constitutes good judgment in predicting future events, and looks at why experts are o... The strongest forecasters do not simply gather more information. They continually compare belief strength with evidence strength. Research from forecasting competitions found that training, feedback, and explicit probability judgements improved forecasting accuracy and calibration. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment+2Good Judgment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentThe impact of training and practice on judgmental accuracy...by W Chang · 2016 · Cited by 150 — T... A simple personal version of this process can be applied to everyday thinking: Superforecasting Philip Tetlock Channel: The Long Now Foundation Open on YouTube 1. Make a prediction. 2. Assign a probability. 3. Record it. 4. Check the outcome later. 5. Compare confidence with reality. 6. Adjust future confidence accordingly. This transforms confidence from a feeling into a measurable skill. Why Watching Tutorials Keeps You Stuck (The Fluency Trap) Channel: Tiny Aha Lens Open on YouTube When Confidence Gets Ahead of Evidence The most dangerous mistakes often occur not when evidence is absent but when confidence becomes detached from evidence quality. Several warning signs suggest this has happened: Certainty increases without new information. Confidence is justified by familiarity rather than verification. Contradictory evidence is ignored or dismissed. Explanations become more elaborate while evidence remains unchanged. Predictions are not tracked against outcomes. Overconfidence is not merely excessive optimism. It is a calibration failure: the level of certainty exceeds what the evidence supports. Studies of judgement repeatedly find that people can be highly confident and still systematically wrong. [Wikipedia+2PMC] Wikipedia Overconfidence effect Overconfidence effect The metacognitive goal is"},{"title":"Drift Checks","url":"/drift-checks/","description":"Mid-task monitoring helps you notice when you are spending time without making real progress.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Metacognition","level":3,"text":"Drift Checks Are You Progressing, or Just Continuing? Drift Checks Drift Checks Are You Progressing, or Just Continuing? Mid task monitoring helps you notice when you are spending time without making real progress. Mid task monitoring helps you notice when you are spending time without making real progress. mechanism Signs that the current approach has stopped working Questions that detect confusion, fluency, and drift How to switch strategies without random flailing improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition drift checks Introduction A common reason people fail on complex tasks is not that they lack ability, but that they continue using a strategy that has quietly stopped fitting the problem. During task checks for strategy drift are short, deliberate moments of metacognitive monitoring that ask whether the current approach is still producing meaningful progress. Rather than waiting until the end of a task to discover that hours were spent unproductively, these checks create a feedback loop : monitor what is happening, compare it with the goal, and adjust if necessary. Research on metacognition consistently shows that effective learners and problem solvers monitor their progress and use those observations to regulate their behaviour rather than simply working harder with the same method. [Sanlab+2NSW Education] sanlab.psych.ucla.edu Sanlab Self‐regulated Learning: Beliefs, Techniques, and Illusions Self‐regulated Learning: Beliefs, Techniques, and IllusionsJanuary 3, 2013 — by N Kornell · Cited by 1 — As Nelson and Narens (1990... Published: January 3, 2013 Unlike broad reflection before or after a task, drift checks happen while the work is unfolding. Their purpose is not to interrupt concentration constantly, but to prevent persistence from turning into stubbornness. What strategy drift looks like Strategy drift occurs when your actions no longer match the demands of the task, yet you continue because the current routine feels familiar. The danger is that familiarity often creates a false sense of progress. Drift commonly develops in subtle ways: continuing to gather information when enough evidence already exists to decide; repeatedly rereading difficult material instead of testing whether it can be recalled or explained; refining small details before the main argument or solution is established; applying a technique that worked earlier even though the problem has changed; spending increasing time without reducing uncertainty or producing useful output. The defining feature is not effort but diminishing returns. Time continues to pass, yet each additional minute contributes less towards the objective. Metacognitive research describes this as a breakdown between monitoring and control. Monitoring should detect that progress has slowed or stopped. Control should then alter the strategy. When monitoring is weak, people often mistake continued activity for genuine advancement. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment+2Sanlab] cambridge.org University Press & Assessment Metacognition (Chapter 4 more... Signs that the current approach has stopped working One useful principle is to judge a strategy by its outputs rather than by how busy it makes you feel. Warning signs include: The same obstacle keeps reappearing. You repeatedly solve the wrong sub problem or return to the same confusion. Confidence rises without stronger evidence. Material feels increasingly familiar, yet you cannot explain it independently or solve a fresh example. Progress becomes mechanical. You continue because stopping would require deciding what to do next. New information changes nothing. Additional reading, calculations or notes fail to influence your understanding or decisions. The goal becomes blurred. You remember what you are doing but lose sight of why you are doing it. These signals matter because people are often poor judges of learning when relying on feelings alone. Fluency—the sense that information is easy to process—can be mistaken for genuine understanding, even when later recall or transfer is weak. Research on self regulated learning repeatedly identifies this gap between subjective confidence and actual performance as a major reason why learners persist with ineffective strategies. [Sanlab+2PMC] sanlab.psych.ucla.edu Sanlab Self‐regulated Learning: Beliefs, Techniques, and Illusions Self‐regulated Learning: Beliefs, Techniques, and IllusionsJanuary 3, 2013 — by N Kornell · Cited by 1 — As Nelson and Narens (1990... Published: January 3, 2013 Questions that expose confusion, fluency and drift Good drift checks rely on concrete questions rather than vague impressions. They should produce evidence that the current strategy is either working or failing. How to Use Metacognition & Thinking Strategy to Solve Problems (Think Better) 105 Channel: Henry's Millions Open on YouTube Detecting confusion Ask questions that reveal whether you know where the difficulty lies. Examples include: What exactly is preventing progress? Can I identify the specific missing piece? Am I solving the intended problem or an easier substitute? Being able to name the obstacle is itself useful information. A vague feeling of being stuck often becomes manageable once the obstacle is defined. Detecting false fluency Instead of asking, \"Does this make sense?\", ask questions that require demonstration. Examples include: Could I explain this without looking? Could I predict the next step before seeing it? Could I solve a similar problem from scratch? These questions replace familiarity with retrieval or application, both of which provide more reliable evidence of understanding. [Sanlab+2jasonfinley.com] sanlab.psych.ucla.edu Sanlab Self‐regulated Learning: Beliefs, Techniques, and Illusions Self‐regulated Learning: Beliefs, Techniques, and IllusionsJanuary 3, 2013 — by N Kornell · Cited by 1 — As Nelson and Narens (1990... Published: January 3, 2013 Detecting strategy drift Finally, ask whether the current method is still justified. Useful prompts include: If I were starting this task now, would I choose this approach? What evidence says this strategy is still productive? What has changed since I began? If another person watched my last twenty minutes, would they describe genuine progress or repetition? What result should I have produced by now if this method were working? Notice that these questions focus on observable outcomes rather than motivation. They ask whether the strategy deserves to continue. Self Regulated Learning Explained: How to Become Your Own Teacher Channel: Powerful Learning · Views: 1.5K · Uploaded: July 2025 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube How to switch strategies without random flailing Recognising drift does not mean abandoning a task at the first sign of difficulty. Effective metacognition balances persistence with adaptation. A practical sequence is: 1. Pause briefly. Stop long enough to evaluate rather than reacting impulsively. 2. Identify the failure mode. Is the problem missing knowledge, an unsuitable method, excessive detail, or a misunderstanding of the goal? 3. Change one important variable. For example: move from passive reading to self testing; sketch a diagram instead of reasoning verbally; generate an example before reading another explanation; seek disconfirming evidence instead of supporting evidence; break the task into smaller checkpoints. 4. Set a short review point. Decide in advance when to assess whether the new approach is producing measurable improvement. Changing only one major feature makes it easier to determine whether the adjustment helped. Switching everything simultaneously makes success or failure difficult to interpret. This reflects the metacognitive cycle described in educational research: plan, monitor, evaluate, then regulate behaviour based on the information obtained rather than habit alone. [EEF+2EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and Self Regulated LearningTeachers should support pupils to plan, monitor, and evaluate their learning through explicit... A simple example of drift detection Imagine analysing a research paper. For forty minutes you have highlighted sentences and written detailed notes. You feel productive because the pages are becoming colourful. A drift check asks different questions: Can I state the paper's central claim from memory? What evidence supports that claim? Could I explain why the authors preferred this method? Have my notes improved my understanding, or merely recorded the text? If you cannot answer those questions, highlighting has become disconnected from the goal. A better strategy might be to stop annotating temporarily and write a short summary from memory, then compare it with the paper to locate genuine gaps. The task has not changed, but the strategy has. How to Think About Thinking — The Metacognition Explained Channel: AI Labs: Brain Science & Nexus Open on YouTube Building drift checks into demanding work Strategy checks are most"},{"title":"Real Tasks","url":"/real-tasks/","description":"Metacognitive strategies work best when they are attached to real content rather than taught as detached thinking slogans.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Metacognition","level":3,"text":"Real Tasks Why Thinking Skills Need Real Problems Real Tasks Real Tasks Why Thinking Skills Need Real Problems Metacognitive strategies work best when they are attached to real content rather than taught as detached thinking slogans. Metacognitive strategies work best when they are attached to real content rather than taught as detached thinking slogans. population context Why abstract strategy lessons often stay vague Embedding planning, monitoring, and evaluation in content Examples from learning, analysis, and workplace judgement improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition real tasks Introduction Metacognitive strategies are most effective when they are taught as part of real work rather than as isolated lessons about \"thinking skills\". Learners become better at planning, monitoring and evaluating their thinking when those habits are practised while solving mathematics problems, analysing historical sources, writing essays, conducting science investigations or making workplace decisions . Evidence from education research consistently shows that metacognitive instruction is strongest when it is integrated into normal subject teaching instead of being treated as a separate generic programme. [EEF+2CloudFront] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and self regulation The research evidence indicates that explicitly teaching strategies to help pupils to plan, monitor EEFMetacognition and self regulationThe research evidence indicates that explicitly teaching strategies to help pupils to plan, monitor a... This matters because thinking is always about something. The knowledge, evidence and methods used in a discipline shape which questions should be asked, what counts as good reasoning and how progress should be monitored. Teaching metacognition inside authentic tasks helps learners develop habits that transfer to similar situations because they learn not only what to think but also how to regulate their thinking while doing meaningful work . [National Academies+2CloudFront] nationalacademies.org National AcademiesChapter: 3 Learning and TransferMetacognitive approaches to instruction have been shown to increase the degree to which... Why abstract strategy lessons often stay vague Many programmes have tried to teach broad skills such as \"critical thinking\", \"problem solving\" or \"learning to learn\" as subjects in their own right. Although these ideas sound appealing, they often remain too detached from the situations in which people actually need them. A learner may understand the advice \"check your assumptions\" yet have little idea what assumptions matter when interpreting a graph in geography, evaluating evidence in history or debugging computer code. The strategy remains abstract until it is connected to the knowledge structures and standards of a particular discipline. Research reviews reflect this concern. The Education Endowment Foundation (EEF) advises that planning, monitoring and evaluation strategies should be explicitly taught and then applied through normal curriculum content rather than delivered as separate \"thinking skills\" lessons. Likewise, research on learning transfer shows that metacognitive instruction is more likely to generalise when learners understand both the subject matter and the strategies they are using. [EEF+2CloudFront] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and self regulation The research evidence indicates that explicitly teaching strategies to help pupils to plan, monitor EEFMetacognition and self regulationThe research evidence indicates that explicitly teaching strategies to help pupils to plan, monitor a... Another reason isolated strategy lessons disappoint is that learners rarely experience the decisions that make metacognition necessary. Real tasks involve uncertainty, competing interpretations and incomplete understanding. Those are precisely the moments when monitoring one's own thinking becomes valuable. Embedding planning, monitoring and evaluation in subject content The common framework of planning, monitoring and evaluation does not change across subjects, but the questions asked at each stage do. Planning begins with the demands of the task Planning is more than deciding to \"work hard\". Effective planning identifies the specific challenges of the content. Before beginning a history essay, planning may involve identifying the type of evidence that would support a causal claim. Before solving an algebra problem, it may involve deciding whether a diagram or symbolic manipulation is likely to be more useful. Before reading a scientific paper, it may involve predicting what methods should be reported if the conclusions are well supported. Teachers can make this process visible by modelling their own decisions aloud instead of presenting only polished solutions. Thinking aloud helps students observe expert judgement that would otherwise remain hidden. [CloudFront+2EEF] d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net Cloud Front Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Guidance reportThis new evidence review has provided a clear er picture of the app... Let's think about metacognition: how deliberate teacher modelling develops expert learners webinar Channel: Ark Curriculum Plus Open on YouTube Monitoring happens during the work, not afterwards Monitoring means checking whether current understanding matches the demands of the task. Useful questions vary by discipline: In mathematics: \"Does each step logically follow from the previous one?\" In reading: \"Can I explain this paragraph without looking back?\" In science: \"Does this result fit my prediction, or might there be another explanation?\" In business analysis: \"Am I relying on evidence or simply confirming my first impression?\" These questions are tied directly to the work being completed rather than functioning as generic reminders to \"think carefully\". Evaluation improves future performance Evaluation asks what should change next time. Rather than simply asking whether an answer was correct, learners examine why a particular strategy succeeded or failed. They distinguish between mistakes caused by misunderstanding the content, choosing an unsuitable strategy or monitoring their progress poorly. Repeated cycles of planning, monitoring and evaluation gradually build independent judgement instead of dependence on constant external feedback. [CloudFront+2lifescied.org] d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net Cloud Front Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Guidance reportThis new evidence review has provided a clear er picture of the app... What effective classroom practice looks like Research based guidance consistently recommends explicit instruction rather than assuming metacognitive skills emerge naturally. Common features include: Teachers model their reasoning while solving authentic subject problems. Students practise using the same thinking processes with carefully designed scaffolding. Prompts become less structured as learners gain independence. Reflection focuses on strategy choices within the completed task rather than generic opinions about learning. The emphasis is not on adding another lesson to the timetable but on making normally invisible expert thinking visible during ordinary teaching. Recent EEF guidance highlights modelling, structured questioning, activating prior knowledge and metacognitive classroom dialogue as practical ways to achieve this integration. [CloudFront+2EEF] d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net Cloud Front Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Guidance reportThis new evidence review has provided a clear er picture of the app... Examples across different kinds of work Learning mathematics Instead of teaching \"check your work\" as a slogan, a teacher might ask students to predict which solving method will be most efficient before beginning. While working, students compare intermediate results against their expectations. Afterwards, they analyse whether a different representation—such as a diagram, equation or table—would have reduced errors. Metacognition becomes inseparable from mathematical reasoning rather than an additional exercise. Reading and writing Strong readers do not merely decode words. They notice confusion, identify missing background knowledge and adjust their reading speed. During writing, learners can pause at key points to ask whether each paragraph actually supports the intended argument or merely repeats information. Reflection is anchored in the quality of the text being produced rather than in vague impressions of effort. Teach Metacognition to Your Students 2 Techniques That Actually Work Channel: Challenge Innovate Grow: Teacher & Learner Centre Open on YouTube Scientific investigation Laboratory work naturally supports metacognition because experiments involve prediction, observation and revision. Students can record: what outcome they"},{"title":"Reading Checks","url":"/reading-checks/","description":"A few well-timed checks can reveal whether you understand a text or are only moving through familiar words.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Metacognition","level":3,"text":"Reading Checks Do You Understand It, or Just Recognize It? Reading Checks Reading Checks Do You Understand It, or Just Recognize It? A few well timed checks can reveal whether you understand a text or are only moving through familiar words. A few well timed checks can reveal whether you understand a text or are only moving through familiar words. mechanism Why familiar sentences can feel falsely clear Self explanation checks that expose gaps When to reread, summarise, map, or test recall improving your thinking and analytical skills metacognition reading checks Introduction When people read difficult material, the biggest danger is often not misunderstanding but failing to notice that they have misunderstood. A passage can feel clear because the words are familiar, the writing flows smoothly, or the argument sounds plausible. Yet when asked to explain the central claim, connect the ideas, or apply the concept to a new situation, many readers discover that their understanding is far weaker than they assumed. Research on metacomprehension—the ability to judge how well you understand a text—shows that people are often inaccurate in assessing their own comprehension and can become overconfident when reading feels easy. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk The most widely studied interventions include keyword generation, UCL DiscoveryMeta Analysis of Metacomprehension Accuracy and...June 28, 2022 — by C Yang · 2022 · Cited by 43 — Monitoring the quality o... Published: June 28, 2022 Within metacognition , monitoring understanding while reading is the mechanism that checks whether comprehension is genuinely occurring. It is the difference between recognising sentences and constructing a usable mental model of what those sentences mean. Effective readers periodically test their understanding, detect confusion early, and adjust their approach before misconceptions become entrenched. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersMetacognitive Monitoring of Text Comprehensionby C Mirandola · 2018 · Cited by 30 — The ability to assess and monitor one's own... Do Familiar Sentences Feel Clear Because You Understand Them? A common reading illusion arises when familiarity is mistaken for comprehension. Seeing terms repeatedly can create a strong sense of knowing even when understanding remains shallow. Researchers studying the “illusion of knowing” have found that learners often become overconfident about material they have merely encountered rather than truly mastered. This overconfidence is a form of metacognitive error: confidence rises faster than actual understanding. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe Illusion of Knowing in Metacognitive Monitoring by MM Avhustiuk · 2018 · Cited by 74 — The research has shown that the illusion of knowing can occur in all types of metacognitive jud... Difficult texts are especially vulnerable to this problem. Academic articles, technical reports, philosophical arguments and complex analyses often contain terminology that readers recognise from previous exposure. Recognition creates a feeling of fluency, and fluency can be mistaken for comprehension. The reader keeps moving forward because nothing seems obviously wrong. Reading fluency itself can sometimes contribute to miscalibrated confidence. Research on reading comprehension monitoring has found that stronger reading fluency does not automatically guarantee more accurate self assessment; in some circumstances fluent readers can become more overconfident about what they know. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMetacognitive Monitoring in Reading Comprehension by V Markovich · 2026 — This study examined associations between vocabulary knowledge, reading fluency, cognitive flexibility, and met... This is why the subjective feeling of “this makes sense” is a weak test of understanding. The key question is not whether the text feels familiar, but whether you can use the information without looking at it. Self Explanation Exposes Hidden Gaps One of the most effective comprehension checks is self explanation. Instead of asking whether a passage seems clear, ask yourself to explain its meaning in your own words. The power of self explanation comes from forcing understanding into an active form. A paragraph may seem obvious while it is in front of you, but gaps become visible when you attempt to reconstruct the argument independently. Research on metacomprehension interventions repeatedly identifies self explanation as one of the approaches that improves readers' ability to judge their own understanding accurately. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk The most widely studied interventions include keyword generation, UCL DiscoveryMeta Analysis of Metacomprehension Accuracy and...June 28, 2022 — by C Yang · 2022 · Cited by 43 — Monitoring the quality o... Published: June 28, 2022 Useful self explanation prompts include: What is the main claim of this section? What evidence supports that claim? How does this idea connect to the previous section? What assumptions does the author rely on? How would I explain this to someone unfamiliar with the topic? The moment your explanation becomes vague, circular or dependent on quoting the original text, you have identified a comprehension gap. That discovery is valuable. Effective monitoring is not proving that you understand; it is detecting where you do not. 9 Comprehension Strategies for Science of Reading Lessons Channel: Farrah Henley Education, LLC Open on YouTube The Fastest Comprehension Checks During Reading Monitoring works best when it occurs periodically rather than only at the end of a chapter. A few brief checks can reveal whether understanding is keeping pace with reading: Pause and recall. After a page or section, look away and state the central point from memory. If you cannot do so, comprehension may be weaker than it felt while reading. Research on retrieval practice shows that recalling information provides a stronger indicator of learning than repeated reading alone. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineDo students practise retrieval when they study on their own?by JD Karpicke · 2009 · Cited by 1329 — Repeated testi... Prediction checks. Before reading the next section, predict what should come next. If the author's argument consistently surprises you in ways you cannot explain, you may not have grasped the underlying structure. Question generation. Generate one or two questions that the passage should answer. Formulating meaningful questions requires identifying the core ideas and relationships. Confidence ratings. Briefly estimate how confident you are that you could explain the section tomorrow. Comparing later performance against these predictions helps calibrate future judgments and reduces overconfidence. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk The most widely studied interventions include keyword generation, UCL DiscoveryMeta Analysis of Metacomprehension Accuracy and...June 28, 2022 — by C Yang · 2022 · Cited by 43 — Monitoring the quality o... Published: June 28, 2022 These checks interrupt passive recognition and replace it with evidence about actual understanding. When to Reread, Summarise, Map, or Test Recall Monitoring is only half of the process. Once a comprehension problem is detected, the reader must decide what action to take. Reread When the Problem Is Local Rereading is most useful when confusion stems from a specific paragraph, definition or logical step. If a single sentence contains unfamiliar terminology or a critical inference, targeted rereading can repair the problem efficiently. However, repeated rereading can also create an illusion of mastery because familiarity increases with every exposure. Studies comparing rereading with more active approaches show that rereading often increases confidence more rapidly than actual long term retention. [Springer] link.springer.com Comparing the effectiveness of multiple text reading and...by P Seban · 2025 · Cited by 10 — Regarding metacognitive monitoring... The practical rule is simple: reread to resolve a specific obstacle, not merely because more exposure feels productive. Summarise When the Structure Is Unclear If you understand individual paragraphs but cannot describe the overall argument, write a brief summary. A useful summary captures: 1. The main claim. 2. The supporting evidence. 3. The logical connection between them. 4. The conclusion. Summary generation has been repeatedly studied as a metacomprehension intervention because it forces readers to organise ideas rather than merely recognise them. [UCL Discovery] discovery.ucl.ac.uk The most widely studied interventions include keyword generation, UCL DiscoveryMeta Analysis of Metacomprehension Accuracy and...June 28, 2022 — by C Yang · 2022 · Cited by 43 — Monitoring the quality o... Published: June 28, 2022 Create a Concept Map When Relationships Matter Some texts are difficult because they contain many interconnected ideas rather than isolated facts. In these"},{"title":"Online Claims","url":"/online-claims/","description":"Better thinkers ask what the source actually shows before accepting a confident headline or viral claim.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Online Claims Can You Trust This Claim? Online Claims Online Claims Can You Trust This Claim? Better thinkers ask what the source actually shows before accepting a confident headline or viral claim. Better thinkers ask what the source actually shows before accepting a confident headline or viral claim. population context Source checks Evidence versus headline What would verify it improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims Introduction A strong thinker does not ask first, “Do I agree with this?” but “What does the evidence actually show?” That question is especially important for news headlines, social posts, viral screenshots, short videos and confident online claims , because the most shareable version of a story is often not the best evidenced version. False or misleading information can spread quickly online; a major study of Twitter found that false news travelled farther, faster and more broadly than true news, partly because it tended to feel more novel. [MIT News] news.mit.edu study twitter false news travels faster true stories 0308 MIT NewsStudy: On Twitter, false news travels faster than true stories8 Mar 2018 — A new study by three MIT scholars has found that false... Evidence quality is not the same as source prestige, tone, popularity or visual polish. A claim is stronger when it can be traced to an identifiable original source, checked against independent evidence, placed in its proper context and corrected if new information changes the picture. This page focuses on a practical skill: learning to separate what a headline or post says from what the underlying evidence can actually support. Why Confident Claims Deserve a Slower Read Online claims often arrive already packaged with certainty: a dramatic headline, a cropped chart, a short clip, a screenshot of a “document”, or a post that says “the media will not show you this”. The problem is not simply that some claims are false. It is that the surface cues people use under time pressure are often weak signals of truth. A professional looking website can be misleading; a rough phone video can be authentic; a real image can be attached to the wrong date, place or event. Research on digital evaluation helps explain why “just read the page carefully” is not enough. In a study comparing professional fact checkers, historians and Stanford undergraduates, fact checkers were better at evaluating unfamiliar online sources because they left the original site quickly and looked across the web for outside evidence. The researchers called this “ lateral reading ”: checking what other reliable sources say about the source or claim, rather than staying trapped inside one polished page. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. That matters because many ordinary readers do the opposite. They read vertically: they inspect the page’s design, scroll through its “About” section, notice official sounding language and decide whether it feels credible. Those signals can help, but they are easy to imitate. Better thinkers treat a webpage as one piece of evidence, not as the whole courtroom. Source Checks: Who Is Making the Claim, and How Would They Know? The first evidence quality question is not “Is this source good or bad?” It is more precise: “Is this source in a position to know this particular thing?” A newspaper may be reliable on reported events but weak on a technical medical claim. A government department may be authoritative for official statistics but self interested when defending its own performance. A social media account may have genuine footage but no reliable knowledge of what happened before or after the clip. Useful source checks include: Identity : Is the author, organisation or account clearly identifiable? Access: Did they witness the event, obtain documents, analyse data, or simply repeat another post? Method: Do they explain how they know? Independence: Can the claim be confirmed outside the source’s own network? Track record: Have reputable outlets, researchers or fact checkers previously found the source reliable or unreliable? Correction behaviour: Does the source correct mistakes visibly? The SIFT method, developed by Mike Caulfield and widely used in digital literacy teaching, captures this habit in four moves: stop, investigate the source, find better coverage, and trace claims back to their original context. Its value is that it begins with restraint. Before arguing with a claim, sharing it, or dismissing it, the reader pauses long enough to ask what kind of evidence would settle the question. [University of Chicago Library Guides] guides.lib.uchicago.edu University of Chicago Library Guides The SIFT Method University of Chicago Library Guides The SIFT Method A source check should not become a lazy shortcut. “This outlet is usually good” is not proof that this article is right. “This account is biased” is not proof that this clip is fake. Evidence quality depends on the relationship between the claim and the support offered for it. Evidence Versus Headline: What Is Actually Being Proved? Headlines compress. Social posts exaggerate. Video captions interpret. A claim can be misleading even when every visible element contains a piece of truth. The practical skill is to translate the headline into a testable claim and then compare it with the body of evidence. For example, a headline might say that a study “proves” a food causes disease. The actual paper may show only a correlation, a small effect, a result in animals, or a finding limited to a particular group. A post might say “Video shows riots today”, while the video is real but from a different city and year. Reuters Fact Check regularly documents this kind of mismatch: authentic footage or real images are often recirculated with a false location, date or explanation. [Reuters] reuters.com Open source on reuters.com. Misleading headlines matter because they shape memory and interpretation. A study on subtle misinformation in news headlines found that misleading headlines affected readers’ memory, reasoning, behavioural intentions and impressions, even when the article itself contained more nuanced information. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This is why the headline should be treated as a claim about the evidence, not as evidence itself. The reader’s job is to ask: Does the article support the headline, or merely gesture towards it? Are the strongest words in the headline repeated in the evidence? Are uncertainty, caveats or limits hidden lower down? Does the claim depend on one anecdote, one expert, one study, or one unnamed source? Would the conclusion still hold if the headline were rewritten in plainer, narrower terms? A careful reader often ends up with a smaller but truer version of the claim. “This proves corruption” may become “this raises a question about a contract award”. “Scientists say X is dangerous” may become “one early study found a possible association that needs replication”. That narrowing is not pedantry. It is the difference between reacting to a story and understanding it. Sort Fact from Fiction Online with Lateral Reading Channel: Digital Inquiry Group · Views: 295.0K · Uploaded: January 2020 · Length: 3 minutes 48 seconds Open on YouTube What Good Evidence Looks Like in News and Online Claims High quality evidence has several features. It is traceable, relevant, proportionate, independently checkable and honestly limited. The strongest evidence does not merely sound authoritative; it lets a reader see how the conclusion was reached. A claim about public spending is stronger when it links to budget documents, official statistics or audited accounts. A claim about a court case is stronger when it refers to filings, judgments or named legal records rather than anonymous summaries. A claim about a scientific issue is stronger when it points to peer reviewed research, systematic reviews, or expert bodies that explain the state of the evidence. A claim about a fast moving event is stronger when it distinguishes confirmed facts from early reports. Good evidence also matches the scale of the claim. A single eyewitness may support “someone saw smoke near the station”; it cannot by itself support “the attack was carried out by X group”. A before and after photo may show visible damage; it does not automatically prove who caused it. A survey can describe what its sample said; it cannot safely stand for a whole population unless the sampling method supports that inference. Professional fact checking organisations make this logic visible by publishing claim specific verdicts, methods and corrections policies. The International Fact Checking Network’s Code of Principles stresses nonpartisanship, source transparency, transparent methodology and open corrections for fact checking organisations. [IFCN Code of Principles] ifcncodeofprinciples.poynter.org Open source on poynter.org. Full Fact likewise describes fact checking as a"},{"title":"Claim Size","url":"/claim-size/","description":"Small pieces of evidence can support modest observations, but broad accusations need stronger, more independent support.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Online Claims","level":3,"text":"Claim Size Big Claims Need Bigger Evidence Claim Size Claim Size Big Claims Need Bigger Evidence Small pieces of evidence can support modest observations, but broad accusations need stronger, more independent support. Small pieces of evidence can support modest observations, but broad accusations need stronger, more independent support. mechanism What proportionate evidence means Why anecdotes, photos, and single witnesses have limits How to scale confidence to the support available improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims claim size Introduction Good reasoning depends not only on whether there is evidence, but on whether the evidence is strong enough for the size of the claim being made. A small observation can justify a cautious conclusion, while a sweeping accusation or extraordinary assertion requires much broader, more reliable support. This is one of the most important habits for evaluating news, social media posts and online debates: match your confidence to the evidence, rather than to how confidently the claim is presented. Many reasoning errors arise because this proportionality is ignored. A dramatic headline may rest on a single anecdote, while a careful scientific conclusion may be supported by years of independent studies. Learning to distinguish between these situations helps prevent both gullibility and excessive scepticism. What Proportionate Evidence Means The amount and quality of evidence needed should increase with the scope and certainty of the claim. For example: A single photograph may support the modest claim that someone captured an unusual image . Several independent witnesses, video footage and official records might support the claim that an event occurred at a particular place and time . Demonstrating that a government deliberately organised a nationwide conspiracy would require a much larger body of mutually reinforcing evidence, because the claim is broader, more complex and has many more opportunities for alternative explanations. The key question is not simply, \"Is there evidence?\" but, \"Is this evidence sufficient for this specific conclusion?\" A useful mental habit is to ask whether the conclusion says more than the evidence actually establishes. If it does, confidence should be reduced until stronger support appears. Why Bigger Claims Need Bigger Evidence Larger claims place a heavier burden on the available evidence because they make stronger predictions and rule out more competing explanations. Suppose someone says: \"I saw smoke over the hill.\" \"The entire forest is on fire.\" \"The fire was deliberately started by a criminal organisation.\" Each statement requires progressively stronger evidence. Observing smoke may justify the first statement. The second needs broader observation, perhaps satellite images or multiple reports. The third introduces intent and organised action, demanding investigative evidence rather than visual observation alone. This principle has long appeared in philosophy and science. It is often summarised by the phrase \"extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence\", popularised by Carl Sagan, although similar ideas can be traced back much earlier, including to the philosopher David Hume and the mathematician Pierre Simon Laplace. The central idea is not that unusual claims are automatically false, but that claims which conflict with well established evidence require correspondingly stronger support before confidence is warranted. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCExtraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence by PE Tressoldi · 2011 · Cited by 121 — “Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence” was a phrase made popular by Carl Sagan... Importantly, researchers have also cautioned against treating this principle as a licence to dismiss genuinely novel discoveries. Strong evidence should increase confidence regardless of whether the conclusion is surprising. The goal is proportionality, not automatic rejection of new ideas. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net 309305745 Do Extraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence Do Extraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence?3 May 2026 — In 1979 astronomer Carl Sagan popularized the aphorism “e... Published: May 2026 Deductive vs Inductive Reasoning Valid & Sound vs Strong & Cogent Channel: Thinking About Stuff Open on YouTube Why Anecdotes, Photos and Single Witnesses Have Limits Many [online claims rely on evidence that is genuine but incomplete.]( endnote 3 \"Snippet: orgExtraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence'Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence' (sometimes shortened to ECREE...\") Anecdotes Personal experiences can show that something happened to one individual. They are much weaker evidence for claims about how common something is or whether one event caused another. For example, one person's experience after taking a medicine cannot establish that the medicine always causes that effect. It may be an important clue worth investigating, but not a final answer. Photographs A photograph demonstrates that a scene existed in front of a camera at one moment. It usually cannot establish: what happened immediately before or after; where the image was taken unless independently verified; why the people involved acted as they did; or whether the image has been cropped or presented without context. A real image can therefore support a modest factual observation while being insufficient for broader interpretations. Single Witnesses Eyewitness testimony is valuable, especially when given promptly and independently. However, memory is imperfect, perception is limited, and people only observe part of an event. A lone witness may provide credible evidence that deserves investigation. A large accusation based solely on one account, however, normally requires corroboration from documents, recordings, physical evidence or additional independent witnesses before strong conclusions are justified. Scale Confidence Instead of Choosing Between Belief and Disbelief People often treat claims as either accepted or rejected. Better reasoning uses a spectrum of confidence. Instead of asking, \"Do I believe this?\", ask: What does the current evidence justify? How certain should I be? What additional evidence would change my mind ? For example: Evidence availableReasonable confidenceOne anonymous social media postVery low confidence in broad conclusionsMultiple independent reports with consistent detailsModerate confidence that the reported event occurredIndependent investigations, original documents and corroborating evidenceHigh confidence in well defined claimsExtensive independent replication over timeVery high confidence, while remaining open to future revision Confidence should grow gradually as stronger evidence accumulates rather than jumping immediately from uncertainty to certainty. William Lane Craig Gives an Extraordinary Answer to an Ordinary Objection w/ @ReasonableFaithOrg Channel: Cross Examined Open on YouTube Common Ways Claim Size Becomes Inflated Online discussions frequently expand modest evidence into much larger conclusions. Examples include: From one example to a general rule. One incident becomes \"this always happens.\" From correlation to causation . Two events occurring together become proof that one caused the other. From uncertainty to certainty. Preliminary findings are reported as settled facts. From isolated wrongdoing to universal guilt. One person's actions become evidence against an entire organisation or group. From unanswered questions to affirmative claims. Lack of an immediate explanation becomes \"proof\" of a preferred explanation. Each step increases the size of the claim without necessarily increasing the quality of the supporting evidence. A Practical Habit for Reading News When encountering a striking headline, match the evidence to the conclusion before deciding how confident to be. Ask yourself: 1. Exactly what is being claimed? 2. How broad is that claim? 3. What evidence is actually presented? 4. Could the same evidence support a smaller, more cautious conclusion? 5. What additional independent evidence would be needed before accepting the larger claim? Often the evidence supports a narrower statement than the headline suggests. Recognising this difference allows you to remain open minded without becoming either credulous or dismissive. Do extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence? Channel: Majesty of Reason Open on YouTube The Main Takeaway Strong thinking is not about demanding impossible proof for every statement, nor about accepting dramatic claims on thin evidence. It is about keeping confidence proportional to the available support. Small pieces of evidence can justify modest observations. Broad accusations, sweeping explanations and claims that overturn well established knowledge require broader, more independent and more reliable evidence. Matching evidence strength to claim size helps prevent both overconfidence and"},{"title":"Corrections","url":"/corrections/","description":"Visible corrections do not make a source perfect, but they show whether mistakes are treated as public responsibilities.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Online Claims","level":3,"text":"Corrections Does the Source Correct Its Mistakes? Corrections Corrections Does the Source Correct Its Mistakes? Visible corrections do not make a source perfect, but they show whether mistakes are treated as public responsibilities. Visible corrections do not make a source perfect, but they show whether mistakes are treated as public responsibilities. policy intervention Why correction behaviour matters What transparent fact checking policies reveal How to read corrections without blind trust improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims corrections Introduction A source that corrects its mistakes is not necessarily more accurate than one that never appears to make them. However, visible correction behaviour is one of the strongest practical signals that a publisher treats accuracy as an ongoing responsibility rather than a one off claim. In journalism and fact checking, new evidence emerges, witnesses change their accounts, official records are amended, and reporters discover errors. The important question is not whether mistakes happen—they inevitably do—but whether the organisation acknowledges them openly, explains what changed, and preserves a trustworthy record of the update. Transparent corrections therefore serve as evidence quality signals: they reveal how a source responds when reality conflicts with what it previously published. Why correction behaviour matters Reliable information systems are designed to improve over time. A correction policy recognises that published information is provisional in the sense that it remains open to revision if stronger evidence appears. Rather than weakening credibility, a clear correction can strengthen it by demonstrating accountability. By contrast, organisations that quietly edit articles without explanation, delete incorrect posts without acknowledgement, or leave known errors uncorrected make it much harder for readers to judge whether the published record can be trusted. When changes are invisible, readers cannot distinguish between genuine improvement and attempts to erase mistakes. Professional news organisations typically describe corrections as part of their editorial standards. Reuters, for example, states that it corrects errors \"promptly, clearly and comprehensively\" and explicitly says corrections should not be hidden inside later updates. Accuracy is treated as more important than speed, even in fast moving news coverage. [Reuters Agency] reutersagency.com Open source on reutersagency.com. The principle extends beyond individual stories. A correction policy communicates that: factual claims remain open to review; readers have a way to report possible errors; editors accept responsibility for mistakes; the publication values an accurate historical record rather than protecting appearances. Disinformation & The Public: How journalists can champion news literacy & empower their communities Channel: NPC Journalism Institute Open on YouTube What transparent fact checking policies reveal A published corrections policy provides insight into how an organisation governs evidence. It shows not simply that corrections are possible, but how decisions are made when evidence changes. Strong policies commonly include several features. Clear distinction between different kinds of changes. Many organisations separate substantial factual corrections from minor typographical fixes or contextual updates. Reuters' fact checking operation, for example, distinguishes between corrections, refiles and updates depending on whether the underlying factual assessment has changed. Major corrections receive explanatory notices placed prominently within the article rather than silent edits. [Reuters] reuters.com About Reuters Fact Check Reuters ha establecido una unidad de fact checking que revisa material visual y declaraciones en redes sociales. Los hallazgos se publica... Visible notices. Reliable publishers generally explain what changed instead of replacing text without comment. Readers should not have to compare archived versions to discover that an important claim was altered. Public reporting channels. Many editorial policies invite readers to submit evidence of possible errors. This does not mean every complaint results in a correction, but it demonstrates that challenges can enter the editorial process rather than being ignored. [Reuters] reuters.com About Reuters Fact Check Reuters ha establecido una unidad de fact checking que revisa material visual y declaraciones en redes sociales. Los hallazgos se publica... Proportionate responses. Good policies distinguish between insignificant mistakes, such as spelling errors, and errors that materially affect the meaning, evidence or conclusions of a story. More significant errors deserve more prominent corrections. These governance practices are valuable because they make editorial reasoning observable rather than hidden. How to read corrections without blind trust Finding a correction should not lead either to automatic distrust or automatic confidence. Instead, treat it as another piece of evidence about the publication's editorial standards. Useful questions include: Was the correction easy to find? A correction buried deep in an article carries less accountability than one clearly identified at the top. Does it explain what changed? Vague statements such as \"updated for clarity\" are less informative than descriptions of the specific factual error. Was the headline corrected if necessary? If the headline still communicates the original mistake, the correction may not adequately repair the public record. Is the original publication date preserved alongside the correction date? This helps readers understand the sequence of events. Does the correction reflect new evidence or simply improve wording? The distinction matters when evaluating whether the underlying claim has changed. A transparent correction should allow readers to reconstruct what happened without requiring them to guess. What are Journalism Ethics? Channel: National Endowment for Democracy Open on YouTube The warning signs of poor correction practices Some editorial behaviours should reduce confidence, particularly when they appear repeatedly. These include: deleting incorrect material without acknowledgement; making substantial edits with no explanation; refusing to correct demonstrable factual errors; removing criticism while leaving inaccurate claims in place; publishing corrections only after sustained public pressure despite clear evidence; repeatedly making similar factual mistakes without changing editorial practice . One particularly concerning practice is the \"stealth correction\": altering published content without leaving any visible indication that a change occurred. Similar concerns have been raised in scientific publishing, where undisclosed post publication changes can undermine confidence in the integrity of the published record because readers cannot tell that earlier versions were inaccurate. [arXiv] arxiv.org The existence of stealth corrections in scientific literature a threat to scientific integritySeptember 10, 2024... Published: September 10, 2024 Silent edits are not always inappropriate—for example, correcting an obvious typing error may not require a formal notice—but substantive factual changes should normally leave an audit trail. Corrections are evidence of accountability, not proof of accuracy A common misunderstanding is that a source with many corrections must be less reliable than one with few or none. The opposite may sometimes be true. Large news organisations often publish thousands of stories every day. A newsroom with rigorous monitoring and a visible corrections process may accumulate more public correction notices simply because it actively identifies and records mistakes. Meanwhile, a publisher that rarely admits errors may appear cleaner while actually correcting less. Research on audience attitudes suggests that people expect news organisations to correct mistakes promptly and transparently, although corrections alone do not automatically restore trust for every reader. Existing trust influences how corrections are interpreted, making transparency necessary but not sufficient for maintaining credibility. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Do Not Stand Corrected: Transparency and Users...June 23, 2016 — In this study, we investigate Swedish citizens' attit... Published: June 23, 2016 For this reason, correction policies should be interpreted alongside other evidence quality signals such as sourcing, attribution, independence, and the ability to trace claims back to original evidence. The practical takeaway When assessing news or online claims , do not ask only whether a source has ever made mistakes. Ask what happens after mistakes are discovered. A trustworthy source makes corrections public, explains what changed, distinguishes between minor edits and substantive factual revisions, and preserves a visible"},{"title":"Headline Claims","url":"/headline-claims/","description":"Misleading headlines can turn limited findings into stronger conclusions than the article or study can honestly support.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Online Claims","level":3,"text":"Headline Claims When Headlines Outrun the Evidence Headline Claims Headline Claims When Headlines Outrun the Evidence Misleading headlines can turn limited findings into stronger conclusions than the article or study can honestly support. Misleading headlines can turn limited findings into stronger conclusions than the article or study can honestly support. dataset evidence How headlines compress or exaggerate claims Signs that caveats are buried below the headline How to rewrite a claim in narrower terms improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims headline claims Introduction Headlines are designed to compress complex information into a few words. That compression is useful, but it also creates a recurring problem: a headline can sound far more certain than the evidence underneath it actually is. Readers often remember the headline long after they have forgotten the details, meaning an exaggerated framing can shape beliefs even if the article itself later adds qualifications. This matters because evidence comes in different strengths. A small observational study, an experiment in mice, or a single statistical association cannot support the same level of certainty as a large, well conducted randomised trial or a body of replicated research. Improving your analytical skills therefore means learning to distinguish what the headline claims from what the evidence genuinely demonstrates. How headlines compress or exaggerate claims Most overstated headlines do not invent facts outright. Instead, they strengthen the relationship between the evidence and the conclusion. Common forms of exaggeration include: Turning association into causation . A study finds that two variables are linked, but the headline declares that one causes the other. Treating preliminary findings as settled knowledge. Early research becomes \"scientists prove\". Generalising beyond the study population. Results from one age group, country, or medical condition become claims about everyone. Extending animal or laboratory findings to humans. Research in mice or cell cultures becomes advice for people. Ignoring uncertainty. Confidence intervals, limitations, and competing explanations disappear from the summary. Each step may seem small, yet together they can substantially change what readers believe the evidence shows. Why the article is often more cautious than the headline Journalists and editors face pressure to make stories concise and engaging. Headlines also have severe space limits. Unfortunately, the shortest wording is not always the most accurate. A typical pattern looks like this: Evidence in the studyHeadline version\"People who reported eating more nuts had lower rates of disease.\"\"Eating nuts prevents disease.\"\"Researchers observed an association.\"\"Researchers discover the cause.\"\"This finding requires further study.\"\"Scientists have the answer.\" The article itself may explain that the study was observational, that alternative explanations exist, or that the effect was modest. Readers who stop at the headline never encounter those important qualifications. Research into health and science reporting has repeatedly found that exaggeration often appears before journalists even write their stories. Studies comparing peer reviewed papers, university press releases and subsequent news reports found that stronger claims in press releases were strongly associated with stronger claims in news coverage. Importantly, there was little evidence that exaggeration increased news uptake. [BMJ+2PMC] bmj.com exaggerated causal claims were associated with 2.7 news... Why Nutrition Headlines Mislead You (Even When They're True) Channel: Learn Data Easy Open on YouTube Signs that caveats are buried below the headline A useful habit is to compare the certainty of the headline with the certainty of the evidence described later. Warning signs include: The headline uses words such as \"proves\", \"shows\", \"reveals\", \"causes\" while the article later says \"may\", \"is associated with\", \"suggests\", or \"researchers caution\" . The headline gives behavioural advice that the researchers themselves never offered. The first paragraph is definitive, but the study limitations appear only near the end. Important methodological details—small sample size, observational design, or lack of replication—are mentioned only after several paragraphs. The article quotes independent experts expressing caution that the headline does not reflect. The placement of caveats matters because many readers never reach the later paragraphs where the uncertainties are explained. How To Spot Bad Science Reporting Channel: Above The Noise Open on YouTube A concrete example: correlation becomes causation One of the most common headline errors occurs when observational studies are reported as if they established cause and effect. Imagine researchers find that people who sleep less than six hours are more likely to develop a particular illness. The evidence could support statements such as: People with shorter sleep tended to have higher rates of the illness. The study found an association between sleep duration and illness. More research is needed to determine whether poor sleep contributes directly. An overstated headline would instead read: \"Lack of sleep causes the illness.\" That stronger wording ignores other possibilities. Poor health may reduce sleep rather than the reverse. A third factor, such as stress or socioeconomic circumstances, could influence both. Without an experimental design or other strong causal evidence, the study cannot distinguish between these explanations. Why overstatement can begin before publication Many readers assume exaggeration is introduced only by journalists. The evidence suggests the picture is more complicated. Research comparing scientific papers with university and journal press releases found that stronger causal claims, more direct health advice and broader inferences often appeared in the press releases themselves. Those stronger claims then tended to appear in subsequent news coverage. [BMJ+2PMC] bmj.com exaggerated causal claims were associated with 2.7 news... Follow up research also challenged a common assumption within science communication: making headlines and summaries more cautious did not appear to reduce media interest. Trials in which press releases used more accurate causal language and included explicit caveats found that news coverage was not significantly reduced, while the resulting reporting better reflected the underlying evidence. [Springer Nature Link] link.springer.com We aimed to improve the alignment between causal claims and evidence, Springer Nature LinkClaims of causality in health news: a randomised trialby RC Adams · 2019 · Cited by 70 — Misleading news claims can b... This suggests that accuracy and readability are not necessarily in conflict. How to rewrite a claim in narrower, evidence based terms One of the best ways to test a headline is to rewrite it so that it matches the evidence as closely as possible. Instead of: \"Coffee prevents dementia.\" Write: \"Study finds an association between coffee consumption and lower dementia rates.\" Instead of: \"Scientists prove screen time damages children's brains.\" Write: \"Researchers report evidence linking higher screen time with certain developmental measures, but the study cannot establish causation.\" Instead of: \"New drug cures cancer.\" Write: \"Early clinical trial reports promising results for a new treatment in a limited group of patients.\" Notice that the rewritten versions are less dramatic, but they communicate more accurately what the evidence actually supports. Practical questions to ask before accepting a headline When you encounter a strong claim, ask yourself: 1. What exactly did the study measure? 2. Does the evidence demonstrate causation or only association? 3. Who was studied, and can the findings reasonably be generalised? 4. Are important limitations missing from the headline? 5. Would replacing \"causes\" with \"is associated with\" better match the evidence? These questions take only a few moments but often reveal whether a headline has outrun the underlying research. Why this matters for analytical thinking Headlines are powerful because they provide the first—and often only—mental frame through which people interpret a story. When that frame overstates what the evidence proves, readers can come away with more confidence than the underlying research justifies. Developing the habit of separating the headline from the evidence is therefore not merely a media literacy skill. It is a broader thinking skill: recognising the difference between what has been observed, what has been inferred, and what has actually been demonstrated. That distinction helps readers evaluate claims more accurately, avoid being persuaded by unwarranted certainty, and make decisions that better reflect the quality of the available evidence. Correlation vs. Causation: Don't Confuse Relationship with"},{"title":"Lateral Reading","url":"/lateral-reading/","description":"A polished page is only a starting point; stronger source checks look outside the site for independent context and reputation.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Online Claims","level":3,"text":"Lateral Reading Why Smart Readers Leave the Page Lateral Reading Lateral Reading Why Smart Readers Leave the Page A polished page is only a starting point; stronger source checks look outside the site for independent context and reputation. A polished page is only a starting point; stronger source checks look outside the site for independent context and reputation. mechanism What lateral reading means in practice How outside checks beat surface credibility cues Common mistakes when judging unfamiliar websites improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims lateral reading Introduction A polished website, a confident author biography and an impressive logo can all create an illusion of credibility. Professional fact checkers know this, which is why they often do something that surprises most readers: they leave the page almost immediately. Instead of judging a source by its appearance or self description, they search for independent information about who is behind it, what others say about it, and whether its claims stand up elsewhere. This approach is known as lateral reading , and research consistently shows that it is one of the most effective ways to evaluate unfamiliar online sources. [Stanford Digital Repository] stacks.stanford.edu Wineburg Mc Grew Lateral Reading and the Nature of Expertise political fact checking organizations. Eight...Read more... Lateral reading is especially valuable when you encounter a website, organisation or social media account you have never seen before. Rather than asking, \"Does this page look trustworthy?\", you ask, \"What does the wider web reveal about this source?\" That shift changes the task from evaluating presentation to evaluating reputation and evidence. What lateral reading means in practice Traditional reading moves vertically through a page. You inspect the design, read the \"About Us\" section, look for contact information and examine the writing style. Those details can sometimes help, but they are also among the easiest features to imitate. Lateral reading follows a different sequence: [scienceofboosting.org] scienceofboosting.org Lateral Reading BoostingMay 4, 2023 — Lateral reading is a simple heuristic for online fact checking: Open multiple tabs in your browser and search the W... Published: May 4, 2023 1. Pause before accepting or sharing the claim. 2. Open new browser tabs. [scienceofboosting.org] scienceofboosting.org Lateral Reading BoostingMay 4, 2023 — Lateral reading is a simple heuristic for online fact checking: Open multiple tabs in your browser and search the W... Published: May 4, 2023 3. Search for the organisation, author or website by name. 4. Look for reporting, academic references or independent discussion. 5. Compare the claim with higher quality or primary sources. Researchers studying professional fact checkers found that experts routinely left unfamiliar websites to investigate them elsewhere, while many students and even experienced historians remained on the original page and were more easily misled by professional looking design and authoritative language. [Stanford Digital Repository] stacks.stanford.edu Wineburg Mc Grew Lateral Reading and the Nature of Expertise political fact checking organizations. Eight...Read more... The goal is not to distrust every new source. It is to gather enough outside context to understand what the source is before deciding how much confidence it deserves. How outside checks beat surface credibility cues Many online credibility cues are weak because they can be manufactured cheaply. A sophisticated layout, an official sounding domain name or a lengthy mission statement says very little about whether claims are accurate. Independent checks are stronger because they rely on evidence outside the source's own control. Check what others say about the source Search for the organisation's name alongside terms such as: review criticism fact check funding ownership controversy Independent reporting may reveal whether the organisation has recognised expertise, a history of publishing corrections , undisclosed advocacy, or repeated factual errors. Equally important, you may discover that the source is widely respected within its field. Sort Fact from Fiction Online with Lateral Reading Channel: Digital Inquiry Group · Views: 295.0K · Uploaded: January 2020 · Length: 3 minutes 48 seconds Open on YouTube Look for expertise, not impressive titles A source may describe itself as an institute, academy or research centre. Lateral reading asks different questions: Is it recognised by other experts? Has its work been cited independently? Does it have relevant expertise for this specific topic? Someone may be highly qualified in one discipline but commenting outside their area of competence. Likewise, an advocacy group may accurately present some evidence while selectively omitting contrary findings. Find better coverage Sometimes the original page is not worth evaluating in depth because stronger reporting already exists. If an unfamiliar website claims that a major scientific breakthrough has occurred, search directly for the underlying event rather than continuing to analyse the original article. If no reputable scientific institutions or established news organisations are reporting the same development, that absence itself becomes useful evidence. This principle is central to Mike Caulfield's SIFT framework, which encourages readers to \"find better coverage\" instead of becoming trapped evaluating a questionable source in isolation. [Hapgood] hapgood.us SIFT (The Four Moves SIFT (The Four Moves) Hapgood19 Jun 2019 — key approach is the Stop, Investigate, Find and Trace (SIFT) technique, a fact checki... Trace claims back to their origin Many online articles simply repeat earlier material. When possible, locate: the original research paper the official report the court judgment the government dataset the complete speech or interview Doing so often reveals missing qualifications, uncertainty or context that disappeared as the claim spread across multiple websites. A practical example Imagine you encounter an unfamiliar website claiming that a city has banned a common household product because of new scientific evidence. A vertical reader might inspect the page carefully, notice references, see photographs and conclude that it appears professional. A lateral reader would instead: search for the organisation behind the article look for reporting from established local news organisations check whether the city council or regulator has published an announcement search for the scientific study being cited compare how multiple independent sources describe the decision Within a few minutes, it often becomes clear whether the claim is well supported, exaggerated or entirely fabricated. Lateral Reading: Detecting Online Sources You Can Trust Channel: OSLIS Elementary Videos Open on YouTube Why reputation alone is not enough Lateral reading is not simply replacing one authority with another. [scienceofboosting.org] scienceofboosting.org Lateral Reading BoostingMay 4, 2023 — Lateral reading is a simple heuristic for online fact checking: Open multiple tabs in your browser and search the W... Published: May 4, 2023 Finding that a source has a good reputation does not automatically validate every claim it makes. Likewise, discovering that an organisation has political views or commercial interests does not automatically invalidate everything it publishes. Instead, reputation provides context for interpreting evidence. The key questions remain: Does this source have relevant expertise? Can its claims be independently verified? Are primary sources available? Do multiple independent sources reach similar conclusions? The method encourages proportional confidence rather than absolute trust. Common mistakes when judging unfamiliar websites Several habits repeatedly lead readers astray. Reading too deeply before knowing the source. Spending ten minutes analysing an article from an unknown website before spending thirty seconds checking who produced it often wastes time. Trusting appearance over reputation. Modern website templates allow almost anyone to create pages that resemble established institutions. Reading only the \"About\" page. Organisations naturally present themselves positively. Independent descriptions provide a more balanced perspective. Assuming search ranking equals credibility. Search engines optimise for many factors besides reliability. High placement is not evidence that claims are accurate. Stopping after finding one confirming source. Reliable verification usually requires genuinely independent confirmation rather than multiple sites repeating the same original report. Confusing criticism with disproof. A source may have critics yet still produce accurate work. The question is whether criticism concerns evidence, methodology or simply disagreement. Intro to Lateral Reading Teaching Online Fact Checking Channel: icivics Open on"},{"title":"Trace Claims","url":"/trace-claims/","description":"A claim becomes easier to judge when readers can follow it back to the first document, dataset, video, or eyewitness account.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Online Claims","level":3,"text":"Trace Claims Where Did This Claim Really Start? Trace Claims Trace Claims Where Did This Claim Really Start? A claim becomes easier to judge when readers can follow it back to the first document, dataset, video, or eyewitness account. A claim becomes easier to judge when readers can follow it back to the first document, dataset, video, or eyewitness account. mechanism Why reposts and screenshots weaken evidence How to find the original source or context When the source trail goes cold improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims trace claims Introduction One of the quickest ways to judge an online claim is to ask a simple question: where did this information first come from? Viral posts, screenshots, clipped videos and copied headlines often travel much farther than the original material they describe. As they are reposted, important details can disappear, captions can change, and speculation can gradually become presented as fact. Tracing a claim back to its earliest identifiable source does not guarantee that the claim is true. However, it gives you the best chance of seeing the evidence in its original form: the full document instead of a cropped screenshot, the complete video instead of a short clip, the original dataset instead of a chart, or the eyewitness account before other people added interpretation. This habit is a core part of evidence based thinking and is reflected in the SIFT digital literacy method, which encourages readers to trace claims , quotations and media back to their original context before deciding what to believe. [University of Chicago Library Guides] guides.lib.uchicago.edu University of Chicago Library GuidesThe SIFT Method Evaluating Resources and Misinformation30 Jun 2025 — Trace Claims, Quotes, and Medi... Why reposts and screenshots weaken evidence Every step away from the original source creates another opportunity for information to change. A screenshot of a headline may omit the article itself. A quote card may remove qualifying sentences. A short video may begin after the crucial event has already happened. Even when every repost is made in good faith, the audience gradually loses access to the information needed to evaluate the claim. Several common problems arise: Loss of context. A photograph from years ago may be presented as if it depicts a current event. Selective editing. Short clips can omit what happened immediately before or after. Broken attribution. A claim originally described as \"unconfirmed\" may later circulate without that qualification. Repeated copying. Hundreds of accounts may repeat exactly the same statement, creating the appearance of independent confirmation when they all rely on one original source. This is why counting reposts is a poor substitute for evaluating evidence. A thousand copies of the same unsupported claim do not create a thousand independent pieces of evidence. How to find the original source or context Tracing a claim is often less about advanced investigation than about following a careful chain backwards. Start with the claim itself First isolate exactly what is being claimed. Instead of searching for the wording used in a viral post, reduce it to its essential factual question. For example: Is this supposedly based on a scientific paper? Is it describing an official statistic? Is it quoting a named person? Is it referring to a video or photograph? This helps distinguish evidence from commentary. The claim usually points towards the type of original source you should expect to find. Sort Fact from Fiction Online with Lateral Reading Channel: Digital Inquiry Group · Views: 295.0K · Uploaded: January 2020 · Length: 3 minutes 48 seconds Open on YouTube Look for the first publication Search for distinctive phrases from the claim while avoiding assumptions that later articles are original. Useful indicators include: the earliest publication date; direct links to reports, datasets or court documents; named organisations that produced the information; original press releases or research papers rather than summaries. If every article cites another report, continue following the citations until you reach the first identifiable source rather than stopping at the first article you encounter. Guidance based on the SIFT method recommends tracing claims back to the original publication instead of relying on repeated summaries. [researchguides.austincc.edu] researchguides.austincc.edu Oftentimes, the more a story is shared, the more it can acquire misleading context in the form...Read more... Find the primary evidence Different claims have different primary sources. Claim typeBest original evidenceScientific findingResearch paper, preprint or journal articleGovernment statisticOfficial dataset or statistical releaseLegal claimCourt judgment or filed documentsSpeech or quotationFull transcript or recordingVideo incidentEarliest upload or original recordingPhotographOriginal image and accompanying metadata where available Reading the original material often reveals important limitations that disappear in later retellings. A research paper may report a modest statistical association while news coverage describes a dramatic breakthrough. An official report may include uncertainty ranges or methodological caveats omitted from social media summaries. Tracing images and videos Visual material often feels persuasive because it appears to show events directly. Yet images and videos are especially vulnerable to losing context. Reverse image search tools can help identify where an image appeared previously, whether it has been reused from an unrelated event, or whether higher resolution versions exist with additional information. Similarly, locating an earlier upload of a video may reveal a longer recording that changes how the incident is understood. Educational guides on evaluating online media consistently recommend tracing visual material back to its earliest available publication before accepting captions or interpretations attached to later reposts. [Rowan University LibGuides+2LAVC Library] libguides.rowan.edu Rowan University LibGuidesEvaluating Images & Media Evaluating Online Sources10 Nov 2025 — TRACE claims, quotes, and media back to the... When examining visual evidence, ask: Does a longer version exist? When was this first published? Who recorded it? Has the location or date been independently confirmed? Does the accompanying caption appear only in later reposts? The answers often separate genuine documentation from misleading presentation. Exploring the SIFT Method: Emily Evaluates, Part 5 Channel: shapirolibrary Open on YouTube When the source trail goes cold Sometimes the trail simply ends. Perhaps the viral post refers vaguely to \"experts\", \"a recent study\", or \"internal documents\" without naming them. A screenshot may show no publication date, no author and no identifiable organisation. A quotation may circulate widely without anyone linking to where it was supposedly said. When this happens, the absence of evidence becomes informative. A claim is not automatically false because the original source cannot be found. However, confidence should decrease when: no primary document can be located; every article merely repeats another report; citations point in circles; the alleged source never actually published the quoted claim; only screenshots exist where original documents should be available. Rather than filling gaps with assumptions, treat unsupported claims as unresolved until better evidence appears. Common mistakes when tracing evidence Even careful readers can fall into predictable traps. Mistaking the earliest search result for the earliest source. Search engines rank pages by many factors besides publication date. Confusing popularity with confirmation. Hundreds of websites may all rely on one mistaken report. Stopping at a press release. Press releases summarise evidence from the perspective of the issuing organisation. Whenever practical, read the underlying report or study. Ignoring corrections . Original publishers sometimes issue updates or corrections that later screenshots never reflect. Assuming a primary source is automatically correct. A first hand witness can still misremember events, a government report can contain errors, and a scientific paper can later be revised. Finding the original source is the beginning of evaluation, not the end. Evaluating Evidence: Crash Course Navigating Digital Information 6 Channel: CrashCourse Open on YouTube A practical habit that improves judgement Professional fact checkers often work backwards rather than forwards. Instead of asking whether a viral post looks convincing, they ask what original evidence would have to exist if the claim were true, then look for that evidence directly. This approach—sometimes called tracing to the original context—is closely related to lateral reading , where readers leave the page in front of them to investigate claims independently rather than judging"},{"title":"Visual Checks","url":"/visual-checks/","description":"Photos and videos can be real but still mislead when they are cropped, recaptioned, edited, staged, or reused from another event.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Online Claims","level":3,"text":"Visual Checks Can You Trust That Viral Video? Visual Checks Visual Checks Can You Trust That Viral Video? Photos and videos can be real but still mislead when they are cropped, recaptioned, edited, staged, or reused from another event. Photos and videos can be real but still mislead when they are cropped, recaptioned, edited, staged, or reused from another event. policy intervention Ways real visuals become misleading Reverse image search and geolocation basics Context clues that change what a video proves improving your thinking and analytical skills online claims visual checks Introduction Photos and videos often feel like the strongest kind of evidence because they appear to show events directly. Yet a genuine image can still support a false claim if it is shared with the wrong date, location or explanation. A cropped photograph can hide crucial details, an old video can be presented as today's news, and authentic footage from one country can be falsely labelled as another. Before treating a visual post as evidence—or passing it on—it is worth checking not only whether it is real, but also whether it proves what the accompanying caption claims . Professional fact checkers routinely verify visuals through independent methods rather than accepting them at face value, and many of the same techniques are available to the public. [InVID project+2Citizen Evidence Lab] invid project.eu InVID projectInVID Verification PluginThe provided tools allow you to quickly get contextual information on Facebook and YouTube videos... Ways Real Visuals Become Misleading The most common problem is not fabricated imagery but genuine media presented out of context. A photograph or video can mislead in several ways: Reused from another event. During wars, natural disasters and protests, years old footage is frequently recirculated as if it were recorded that day. Reverse image searches often reveal earlier appearances that expose the false timeline. [Citizen Evidence Lab] citizenevidence.org how to use reverse image search for human rights investigations Citizen Evidence LabHow to: Use Reverse Image Search for Human Rights...29 Jan 2020 — A reverse image search allows an investigator to s... Recaptioned. A real image may depict one place while the caption claims it was taken somewhere else. Cropped. Removing parts of a frame can conceal people, signs or actions that change the interpretation. Edited. Images may be altered by removing, adding or blending elements. Some edits are subtle enough that they require closer inspection. Staged. A scene may genuinely have been photographed, yet participants deliberately created it for the camera rather than documenting a spontaneous event. Selective. A short clip may omit what happened immediately before or after, making actions appear justified or unprovoked when the full sequence tells a different story. These possibilities mean that authenticity alone is not enough. The key question is whether the visual accurately represents the claim being made about it. Reverse Image Search: The Skill Every Student Needs Now Channel: Digital Learning Consultant Open on YouTube Reverse Image Search and Geolocation Basics One of the fastest ways to investigate a suspicious image is a reverse image search. Instead of searching with words, you upload the image or a screenshot so search engines look for visually similar matches. If an image appeared online months or years before the event it supposedly documents, that is strong evidence that the current claim is misleading. Reverse searches can also uncover higher resolution originals, news reports, or the account that first published the media. Fact checkers often search across multiple services because each indexes different parts of the web. [Citizen Evidence Lab+2First Draft] citizenevidence.org how to use reverse image search for human rights investigations Citizen Evidence LabHow to: Use Reverse Image Search for Human Rights...29 Jan 2020 — A reverse image search allows an investigator to s... For videos, searching the whole file is usually less effective. Instead: 1. Pause the video at distinctive frames. 2. Capture screenshots showing landmarks, vehicles, clothing or signs. 3. Perform reverse image searches on those individual frames. 4. Compare the results with the claim being made. Verification tools such as the InVID WeVerify browser extension automate much of this process by extracting key frames, displaying metadata where available and linking directly to multiple reverse image search engines. [WeVerify] weverify.eu Verification pluginThe InVID WeVerify verification plugin is one of the most powerful tools for spotting misinformation online.Re... Geolocation adds another layer of checking. Ask whether visible features match the claimed location: Do road markings, licence plates or traffic signs fit the country? Are mountains, skylines or coastlines consistent with maps or satellite imagery? Do shop names, language and architecture match the claimed place? Are weather conditions and vegetation plausible for that season? You do not always need to identify the exact location. Showing that a claimed location is implausible can already weaken the evidence considerably. Context Clues That Change What a Video Proves Videos often appear persuasive because movement creates a stronger sense of reality than a still photograph. However, context determines what a video actually demonstrates. Pay attention to: Beginning and ending. Viral clips often start after the crucial events have already occurred. Original upload date. An upload timestamp may differ from the date being claimed. Audio. Music, narration or dubbed speech can dramatically alter interpretation. In some cases, audio has been replaced entirely. Camera perspective. A single viewpoint may exclude important activity occurring outside the frame. Compression and reposting. Multiple generations of uploads can remove metadata and visual detail, making forensic analysis harder. It is also worth asking whether independent reporting supports the same interpretation. If a dramatic incident genuinely occurred in a public place, multiple photographs, videos or eyewitness accounts often emerge from unrelated sources. How to do a reverse image search Channel: AFP News Agency Open on YouTube A Practical Five Minute Verification Routine For everyday social media use, you do not need to perform a professional investigation. A short pause before sharing can eliminate many false claims. 1. Read the caption separately from the visual. Ask what the image itself actually shows. 2. Search for earlier appearances. Use reverse image search or screenshots from videos. 3. Look for the original uploader. Earlier uploads generally provide more reliable context than widely reshared copies. 4. Compare with trusted reporting. Check whether reputable news organisations or established fact checkers describe the same event in the same way. 5. Delay sharing if key questions remain unanswered. Uncertainty is a reason to wait, not a reason to speculate. This approach reflects the broader habit of lateral reading : looking beyond the post itself instead of relying on its presentation or popularity. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Why AI Makes Context Checks Even More Important Generative AI has made convincing synthetic images and videos easier to produce, but experts caution that context remains as important as pixel level analysis. Many misleading viral posts still rely on older techniques such as recycled footage, deceptive captions or selective editing rather than fully AI generated media. Verification therefore depends less on spotting visual artefacts than on tracing provenance, comparing independent sources and checking whether the claimed time and place withstand scrutiny. [The Verge] theverge.com Trusted digital investigators like The New York Times, Bellingcat, and Indicator rely on rigorous verification protocols to discern real... Rather than asking, \"Does this look real?\", a stronger analytical habit is to ask, \"What exactly does this visual prove, and what independent evidence supports that conclusion?\" That shift turns an emotionally compelling post into a claim that can be tested instead of merely believed. How to Use Google Reverse Image Search to Fact Check Images Channel: Common Sense Education Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can You Trust That Viral Video?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Calling Bullshit By Carl T. Bergstrom, Jevin Darwin West Builds skepticism and evidence evaluation skills that apply directly to misleading viral visuals and online misinformation. See on Amazon Book Factfulness By Hans Rosling, Ola Rosling et al. Helps readers recognize common reasoning errors and avoid being misled by compelling but inaccurate narratives. See on Amazon Book The Skeptics' Guide to the Universe By Dr. Steven"},{"title":"Teach It","url":"/teach-it/","description":"Explaining an idea simply to someone outside the field quickly reveals whether you understand it or only recognize it.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Teach It Can You Explain It Without Jargon? Teach It Teach It Can You Explain It Without Jargon? Explaining an idea simply to someone outside the field quickly reveals whether you understand it or only recognize it. Explaining an idea simply to someone outside the field quickly reveals whether you understand it or only recognize it. policy intervention Teach back as a test Plain language explanation Where confusion appears improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it Introduction Plain English teaching is a simple thinking test: take an idea you believe you understand, explain it clearly to someone outside the field, and watch where your explanation breaks. If you can only repeat labels, quote definitions, or lean on specialist language, you may recognise the idea without yet understanding it. The test matters because good thinking is not just having the right answer; it is being able to show how the answer works, what it depends on, and where it stops applying. This is often associated with the “Feynman technique”, but the practical value is broader than one famous scientist. It draws on several well supported findings: people often overestimate how well they understand mechanisms until asked to explain them; retrieval and self explanation can strengthen learning; and teach back methods in healthcare show how asking someone to explain information in their own words reveals misunderstanding more reliably than asking “do you understand?” [PMC+2andymatuschak.org] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... Teach back as a test of understanding The core move is not performance. It is diagnosis. Instead of asking, “Could I recognise this idea on a page?”, ask, “Could I explain it to a bright beginner without hiding behind vocabulary?” That shift exposes the difference between stored wording and usable understanding. A good teach back test has four parts. First, choose one narrow idea: not “economics”, but “why price caps can create shortages”; not “machine learning”, but “why overfitting makes a model perform well in training and badly later”. Second, explain it aloud or in writing for a specific non specialist audience. Third, remove unnecessary jargon, or define the unavoidable terms in everyday language. Fourth, mark every place where you hesitate, use a vague word, skip a causal step, or say “it just means…” without being able to show how. Healthcare uses a related method in a high stakes setting. The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality describes teach back as asking patients to state, in their own words, what they need to know or do; if a misunderstanding appears, the clinician clarifies and checks again. The point is not to test the patient’s intelligence, but to test whether the explanation worked. That principle transfers well to analytical thinking: if your audience cannot teach your idea back, your explanation may be unclear; if you cannot teach it forward, your own grasp may be incomplete. [AHRQ] ahrq.gov Open source on ahrq.gov. The strongest version of the exercise treats confusion as useful evidence. When you cannot explain why one step follows another, you have found a learning target. When your listener asks a basic question that you cannot answer, they have not derailed the exercise; they have located the edge of your understanding. Why plain language reveals false confidence Plain English is not a cosmetic style choice. It removes a common hiding place. Technical words can be useful shorthand among experts, but they can also let a person feel fluent while leaving the mechanism unclear. Richard Feynman’s famous warning in his 1974 Caltech address was that “you must not fool yourself”, and his broader teaching style repeatedly distinguished knowing the name of something from knowing what is going on. [calteches.library.caltech.edu] calteches.library.caltech.edu Open source on caltech.edu. Cognitive science gives this habit a sharper name: the illusion of explanatory depth. In the classic work by Leonid Rozenblit and Frank Keil, people tended to believe they understood everyday mechanisms more deeply than they did, until they were asked to explain them in detail. The illusion was especially strong for explanatory knowledge: people could feel that they understood how something worked because they had a familiar mental label for it, not because they could actually describe the causal chain. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... This is why the plain English test is stricter than a quiz on terms. A person can define “inflation”, “cognitive bias”, “compound interest”, “natural selection”, or “correlation” and still fail to explain how the idea behaves in a real case. Plain language forces the hidden work into view: Causal gaps: “X affects Y” becomes “What exactly changes first, and what happens next?” Empty abstractions: “It optimises outcomes ” becomes “What is being improved, for whom, and at what cost?” Borrowed authority: “Studies show…” becomes “What did the study compare, and what would count as a weaker finding?” False precision: “It is statistically significant” becomes “How big was the effect, and does it matter in practice ?” Category confusion: “It is biased” becomes “Biased in which direction, compared with what standard?” The test is demanding because plainness does not mean dumbing down. It means preserving the real structure of the idea while removing avoidable obstacles. That is why plain language guidance from public sector and usability organisations emphasises audience, structure, active wording, familiar terms, and testing whether readers can actually use the information. [Digital.gov+2Nielsen Norman Group] digital.gov Plain language guide series Plain language guide series The thinking mechanisms behind the method Plain English teaching works because it combines several mental actions that are easy to avoid during passive study. First, it requires retrieval . You have to bring the idea out of memory rather than simply recognise it when someone else presents it. Retrieval practice research shows that recalling information can support longer term learning more effectively than simply rereading, especially when the task requires effort rather than easy recognition. [Center for Teaching and Learning] ctl.wustl.edu Open source on wustl.edu. Second, it requires self explanation . When you explain an idea, you must connect steps, supply missing links, and notice contradictions. A classic study by Michelene Chi and colleagues found that prompting students to self explain while reading a passage improved understanding compared with rereading, and later reviews have treated self explanation as a useful, though design sensitive, learning strategy. [andymatuschak.org] andymatuschak.org Chi et al 1994 Eliciting self explanations improves understanding Eliciting Self Explanations Improves Understandingby MTH CHI · 1994 · Cited by 4299 — This study extends that finding, showing that self... Third, it creates a mild version of learning by teaching . Research on learning by teaching and the protégé effect suggests that preparing to teach can change how learners process material: they organise it more carefully, anticipate questions, and become more aware of what a learner would need. The evidence is not a magic guarantee; studies also find that benefits depend on interaction, feedback , and task design. But the basic lesson is practical: teaching forces organisation, and organisation reveals gaps. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Finally, the method improves metacognition , the ability to judge your own understanding. Many people mistake ease for mastery: a familiar term feels known, a fluent article feels absorbed, a highlighted page feels learned. Teaching in plain English replaces that feeling with a more concrete signal: Can you reconstruct the idea, make it intelligible, answer basic questions, and repair the explanation when it fails? How to run a plain English teaching test A useful version can take ten minutes. The goal is not to produce a perfect lecture, but to create a fast, honest check of understanding. 1. Pick a small claim, not a whole subject. “Why does sleep affect memory?” is too broad for one pass. “Why might spaced revision beat cramming?” is better. 2. Name the audience. Explain it to a 14 year old, a new colleague, a sceptical manager, a patient, a voter, or a friend with no background in the field. 3. Give the idea in one plain sentence. For example: “Spaced revision works because each act of remembering strengthens the route back to the information, especially after some forgetting has begun.” 4. Explain the mechanism step by step. Avoid saying “basically” as a substitute for the missing step. Use an example, a"},{"title":"Causal Chains","url":"/causal-chains/","description":"A plain explanation improves when it replaces vague claims like 'X affects Y' with the steps in between.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teach It","level":3,"text":"Causal Chains Can You Show the Missing Middle? Causal Chains Causal Chains Can You Show the Missing Middle? A plain explanation improves when it replaces vague claims like 'X affects Y' with the steps in between. A plain explanation improves when it replaces vague claims like 'X affects Y' with the steps in between. mechanism How causal gaps appear in explanations Turning abstractions into step by step examples Where to stop without oversimplifying improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it causal chains Introduction A plain English explanation becomes genuinely useful when it does more than state that one thing influences another. It shows the missing middle: the sequence of changes that connects a cause to its effect. This is the essence of a causal chain explanation. Instead of saying, “ Stress reduces performance,” a stronger explanation identifies what changes first, what changes next, and why those intermediate steps matter. Building these chains is one of the most reliable ways to test your own understanding because it forces you to replace vague associations with a concrete mechanism. Research on the illusion of explanatory depth shows that people often believe they understand a process until they are asked to explain its causal steps in detail. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue that the illusion of depth seen with explanatory knowledge is a separate phenomenon f... Within the broader practice of explaining ideas in plain English, causal chains answer a specific question: Can you show how the outcome happens, rather than merely asserting that it does? How causal gaps appear in explanations Many explanations sound convincing because they connect two ideas with words such as causes , leads to , affects , or results in . Those words often hide a missing mechanism. Compare these explanations: Weak: \"Exercise improves mood.\" Stronger: \"Exercise raises heart rate and stimulates the release of signalling chemicals associated with reward and stress regulation. It also improves sleep and creates repeated experiences of competence, all of which can contribute to improved mood over time.\" The second explanation is not simply longer. It identifies intermediate changes instead of treating cause and effect as adjacent events. The same pattern appears in everyday reasoning: \"Higher prices reduce demand.\" \" Feedback improves learning.\" \"Competition increases innovation.\" Each statement may often be true, but none explains why . Until the intermediate steps are supplied, the explanation remains incomplete. Research on the illusion of explanatory depth demonstrates that this is exactly where overconfidence appears. People frequently believe they understand mechanisms because they recognise familiar labels or outcomes. Only when asked to explain each step do they discover that several links in the chain are missing. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue that the illusion of depth seen with explanatory knowledge is a separate phenomenon f... A useful warning sign is any explanation that could replace the words \"causes\" or \"affects\" with \"somehow\". If the sentence still sounds grammatically acceptable, the mechanism may not yet be understood. Turning abstractions into step by step examples The simplest way to improve an explanation is to replace a direct arrow with a sequence of smaller changes. Instead of writing: Lack of sleep reduces concentration. Expand it into a causal chain: 1. Sleep is shortened. 2. Fatigue increases. 3. Sustained attention becomes harder. 4. More distractions interrupt working memory. 5. Mistakes become more frequent. 6. Performance on concentration heavy tasks declines. Every step should answer the question, \"What changed between the previous stage and the next?\" The same approach works for abstract concepts. How to Master Causal Thinking Channel: Phil McKinney Open on YouTube Example: Why does compound interest become powerful? A weak explanation says: Compound interest makes savings grow faster. A causal chain explanation shows the mechanism: [plato.stanford.edu] plato.stanford.edu causal explanation science For a discussion of earlier accounts of explanationRead more... Interest is added to the original savings. Future interest is calculated on both the original savings and previously earned interest. The amount generating interest therefore becomes larger after each period. As the base grows, each future increase becomes larger than the last. The explanation has shifted from a slogan to a process. Example: Why does misinformation spread quickly? Rather than saying: [jonwilliamson.uk] jonwilliamson.uk s a tool that helps us to predict, explain and control our world, rather than as a relation.Read more... Social media spreads misinformation. A mechanism based explanation might be: A surprising claim attracts attention. More users react and share it. Recommendation systems detect high engagement. The content is shown to more people. Greater exposure creates additional sharing. The cycle repeats before careful verification catches up. Notice that each stage describes an observable change. The explanation becomes testable because each link could, in principle, be examined separately. Building explanations one link at a time A useful discipline is to imagine that every arrow in a diagram must be replaced with a sentence. Instead of: A → B Ask: What changes first? What does that change directly affect? Why does that produce the next change? Which step depends on assumptions rather than evidence? Often one sentence becomes four or five. For example: Better education increases economic growth. might become: People acquire more knowledge and skills. Workers perform tasks more efficiently. Firms can produce more output with similar resources. Higher productivity supports greater incomes. Greater income contributes to overall economic growth. Whether every link is equally strong is a separate question. The important point is that the reasoning has become visible instead of remaining hidden inside a single verb. This mirrors how mechanistic explanations are treated in many scientific disciplines: understanding improves by identifying the intermediate processes that connect an intervention to an outcome, rather than treating cause and effect as isolated facts. [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy] plato.stanford.edu causal explanation science For a discussion of earlier accounts of explanationRead more... Cause and Effect in Risk: A Smarter Way to Analyze Failures Channel: Asset Insights Open on YouTube Where to stop without oversimplifying A common concern is that every explanation could be expanded indefinitely. That is true. Every mechanism eventually contains smaller mechanisms. The goal is therefore not to explain everything. It is to explain enough that each step feels understandable without requiring another unexplained leap. A practical stopping point is reached when: each transition follows naturally from the previous one; specialised terms have either been avoided or briefly explained; no step depends on \"and then it happens\"; the remaining details belong to a lower level of explanation than your audience currently needs. For example, explaining why a bridge stays upright usually requires discussing forces and load distribution. It does not require deriving the equations of material science unless the audience is studying engineering. Good explanations therefore match the depth of the reader while preserving the integrity of the causal chain. Common mistakes when explaining mechanisms Several habits create the illusion of explanation without providing one. Replacing mechanisms with labels. Saying \"because of motivation\", \"because of inflation\", or \"because of neural activity\" often substitutes a category name for an explanation. The next question should be, \"How does that produce the outcome?\" Skipping invisible transitions. Many explanations jump from an initial event to a distant consequence, leaving several causal links implicit. Confusing correlation with mechanism. Two variables may regularly occur together without one producing the other. Showing a causal chain requires explaining the process, not merely reporting the association. Packing multiple changes into one sentence. Long explanations sometimes hide several independent causal steps inside a single clause. Separating them often reveals assumptions that deserve closer examination. Using causal chains as a thinking test When teaching yourself or someone else, a simple exercise can reveal hidden gaps. Choose one statement you believe you understand, such as: \"Remote working increases productivity.\" Then repeatedly ask: What changes immediately? What changes because of that? What happens next? Which link is weakest? Could another mechanism produce the same outcome? If you reach a point where you can only repeat the original claim in different words, you have"},{"title":"False Depth","url":"/false-depth/","description":"People often feel they understand simple things until they have to explain the mechanism step by step.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teach It","level":3,"text":"False Depth Why Simple Ideas Stop Feeling Simple False Depth False Depth Why Simple Ideas Stop Feeling Simple People often feel they understand simple things until they have to explain the mechanism step by step. People often feel they understand simple things until they have to explain the mechanism step by step. dataset evidence What the illusion of explanatory depth means Why everyday mechanisms expose false confidence How plain English explanation punctures the illusion improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it false depth Introduction Many of the ideas that feel most familiar are the ones we understand least well. The illusion of explanatory depth is the tendency to believe we understand how something works until we are asked to explain it step by step. A bicycle, a zipper, a toilet, a refrigerator, or even a simple economic idea can seem completely obvious—right up to the moment someone asks, \"Exactly what happens next?\" That question often exposes missing links in our mental model rather than a lack of intelligence. Research shows that this gap between perceived and actual understanding is especially strong for explanations of causal mechanisms, making it highly relevant to anyone trying to improve their thinking through plain English explanation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... What the illusion of explanatory depth means The illusion of explanatory depth (often abbreviated to IOED) was identified by cognitive scientists Leonid Rozenblit and Frank Keil. Their experiments showed that people consistently rated their understanding of everyday devices and mechanisms highly before attempting an explanation, then reduced those ratings after trying to explain how the same objects actually worked. The explanation itself—not simply being tested—revealed the limits of their knowledge. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... A crucial feature of the illusion is that it concerns explanatory knowledge , not simply remembering facts. Someone may correctly know that bicycles use chains, toilets flush with water, or inflation raises prices. Those facts can all be true while the person remains unable to describe the causal sequence connecting one event to another. This distinguishes explanatory understanding from other kinds of knowledge. Research has found much weaker overconfidence for remembering facts, recounting stories, or describing procedures than for explaining how systems actually work. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... Why everyday mechanisms expose false confidence Everyday objects create a perfect environment for false confidence because they are familiar without being fully visible. You operate a zipper hundreds of times without seeing how its teeth interlock. You flush a toilet without observing the pressure changes, valves and water trap. You steer a bicycle without consciously understanding why leaning causes the wheels to stabilise. Daily success creates the feeling that understanding exists even when only the surface behaviour is known. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... Several features of ordinary life strengthen the illusion: Repeated use feels like understanding. Successful interaction is mistaken for knowledge of the underlying mechanism. Visible parts hide invisible processes. People see handles, pedals and buttons but not the causal chain beneath them. Simple outcomes conceal complex systems. A familiar action may depend on dozens of interacting components. Language substitutes for explanation. Knowing labels such as \"pressure\", \"electricity\" or \"memory\" can create the impression that the mechanism itself has been understood. [PMC+2Jorge Arango] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... This explains why people are often surprised by how difficult apparently simple questions become: How does a bicycle stay balanced? Why does a refrigerator remove heat instead of simply producing cold? What prevents toilet gases from entering the house after flushing? How does a cylinder lock distinguish the correct key? Many people can describe what these objects do but struggle to explain why each step follows from the previous one. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... The Illusion of Explanatory Depth: You Don’t Understand It Like You Think Channel: Bull and Thread Open on YouTube Why explanation changes your confidence The important discovery is not merely that people explain poorly. It is that attempting an explanation changes their self assessment . In the original experiments, participants first estimated how well they understood an object. They then produced a detailed explanation before rating themselves again. Confidence consistently dropped after the explanation attempt, but rose somewhat again after participants read an expert explanation. This pattern suggests the exercise improves calibration rather than simply making people less confident. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... The act of explaining forces several hidden questions into the open: Which component causes the next event? What assumption have I skipped? Where does energy, force or information actually come from? What would happen if one part were removed? Each unanswered question reveals a genuine gap rather than creating one. How plain English explanation punctures the illusion Within plain English teaching , the illusion of explanatory depth becomes a practical diagnostic tool. Specialist vocabulary often compresses several causal steps into a single word. When those terms are removed, the explanation must stand on the underlying mechanism instead of technical shorthand. Consider the difference between these two explanations: \"Inflation is caused by excess demand.\" \"When many buyers compete for limited goods, sellers discover they can raise prices without losing enough customers. Those higher prices spread because firms also face higher costs and changing expectations.\" The second explanation is imperfect but exposes whether the speaker understands the sequence rather than merely recognising terminology. A useful plain English explanation usually answers questions such as: What starts the process? What changes first? What changes next? Why does each step follow? Under what conditions would this explanation fail? If any answer depends on phrases like \"it just happens\", \"that's how it works\", or repeated technical labels, the illusion has probably been uncovered rather than solved. V. Humility and human cognition. Part 4: The illusion of explanatory depth (Prof. Frank Keil) Channel: Philosophy at the University of Edinburgh Open on YouTube Why the illusion persists The illusion is surprisingly persistent because much of human knowledge is distributed across other people. In everyday life, we rarely need complete explanations ourselves. We rely on mechanics, engineers, doctors, electricians and countless other specialists. This division of cognitive labour is efficient, but it can blur the distinction between knowledge available to society and knowledge stored in our own minds . Because we know an explanation exists somewhere, we can mistakenly feel as though we possess it ourselves. [PMC+2JSaw] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1504 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... Modern technology can amplify this effect. Search engines and online information make explanations instantly accessible, but easy access is not the same as internal understanding. Being able to retrieve an answer later can inflate present confidence about what one actually knows. [JSaw] jsaw.lib.lehigh.edu Strength and Persistence of the Illusion of Explanatory Depthby J Wilson — These findings led Rozenblit and Keil (2002) to conclude t... 55 The Illusion of Explanatory Depth feat. Steven Sloman Channel: unSILOed Podcast with Greg LaBlanc Open on YouTube Why this matters for better thinking The illusion of explanatory depth is not simply an interesting laboratory finding. It affects learning, judgement and reasoning. Someone who mistakes familiarity for understanding is more likely to stop asking questions too early,"},{"title":"Jargon Gaps","url":"/jargon-gaps/","description":"Technical words can create a false feeling of fluency when the real causal steps are still missing.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teach It","level":3,"text":"Jargon Gaps When Jargon Hides What You Do Not Know Jargon Gaps Jargon Gaps When Jargon Hides What You Do Not Know Technical words can create a false feeling of fluency when the real causal steps are still missing. Technical words can create a false feeling of fluency when the real causal steps are still missing. mechanism Why familiar labels feel like understanding How to translate technical terms without losing meaning Examples of vague words that need testing improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it jargon gaps Introduction Technical vocabulary is essential for experts, but it can also become a convenient substitute for understanding. A person who says that a market is experiencing \"liquidity constraints\", that a disease involves \"immune dysregulation\", or that an AI model \"generalises from latent representations\" may sound knowledgeable while still being unable to explain what actually happens, in what order, and why. The problem is not jargon itself. The problem is using jargon as a stopping point instead of as compressed shorthand for a causal explanation. This matters because improving your thinking depends on distinguishing genuine understanding from verbal fluency. Plain English explanation works as a test precisely because it forces technical labels to unfold into the underlying mechanism. If that expansion fails, the label may be hiding a gap rather than representing mastery. Research on the illusion of explanatory depth consistently shows that people often overestimate how well they understand causal systems until they are asked to explain them step by step. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1499 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... Why Familiar Labels Feel Like Understanding Jargon creates an impression of understanding for several psychological reasons. First, specialist words are efficient within a community. Experts use them because everyone involved already shares the detailed model behind the term. The label compresses hundreds of ideas into a few syllables. Someone learning the field may acquire the label long before acquiring the underlying model. Second, recognising a familiar word feels similar to understanding it. Cognitive research suggests that people frequently confuse familiarity with explanatory knowledge. Knowing that a concept has a recognised name can produce confidence that exceeds what they could actually explain. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1499 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... Third, technical language often conceals missing causal links. Consider these statements: \"The treatment works by modulating inflammation.\" \"The economy adjusted because of market forces.\" \"The algorithm optimises the objective function.\" Each statement may be true, but none answers the questions that matter: What changes first? What causes the next step? Under what conditions does the explanation fail? Why does this process produce the claimed result rather than a different one? The technical phrase has replaced the explanation instead of summarising one. Why Teaching is a Top Tier Learning Strategy The Feynman Technique Channel: Nursing Without Notes Open on YouTube How Jargon Becomes a Hiding Place The danger is rarely deliberate deception. More often, people unconsciously use specialist vocabulary to bridge gaps they have not yet recognised. Several patterns appear repeatedly. Replacing mechanisms with categories. Saying \"confirmation bias\" identifies a phenomenon, but it does not explain why a particular person ignored evidence in a specific situation. Replacing processes with names. Saying \"inflation\" does not explain how rising demand, constrained supply, wage growth or monetary conditions combine to increase prices. Replacing explanation with abstraction. Words such as \"optimisation\", \"emergence\", \"adaptation\" or \"complexity\" often describe that something happens without explaining how it happens. The illusion becomes stronger because these labels are genuinely meaningful within expert conversations. When experts speak to one another, the omitted details are already understood. Outside that context, the same words can create an appearance of precision while leaving the causal story almost empty. Research on the illusion of explanatory depth suggests that explanatory knowledge is especially vulnerable to this mistake. People tend to discover the gap only when they must produce a detailed causal account rather than merely recognise or define a concept. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1499 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... How to Translate Technical Terms Without Losing Meaning A useful thinking exercise is to treat every important technical term as a placeholder that must eventually expand into ordinary language. Instead of asking, \"Can I define this term?\", ask, \"Can I unpack this term?\" For each piece of jargon, answer four questions: 1. What is physically or logically happening? 2. What causes the first change? 3. What sequence connects the beginning to the outcome? 4. What evidence would show this explanation is wrong? For example: Jargon: \"The vaccine stimulates an immune response.\" Plain English explanation: \"The vaccine introduces material that resembles part of a virus. Certain immune cells recognise it as foreign. Those cells activate other immune cells, which produce antibodies and long lasting memory cells. If the real virus appears later, those memory cells respond much faster.\" The technical sentence is not incorrect. The plain English version simply reveals the mechanism hidden inside the label. The goal is not to eliminate technical language. Once the mechanism is understood, the specialised term becomes a useful abbreviation rather than a substitute for thought. The Feynman Technique: How to Learn Anything in 4 Steps (Richard Feynman) Channel: 𝐅𝐞𝐲𝐧𝐦𝐚𝐧 𝐃𝐢𝐚𝐫𝐲 Open on YouTube Examples of Vague Words That Need Testing Some words deserve immediate suspicion because they often conceal incomplete understanding. Vague expressionBetter questionEfficientEfficient compared with what alternative?RobustRobust against which kinds of failure?OptimisedWhat objective is being optimised, and what trade offs are accepted?IntelligentWhich specific capabilities justify that description?ComplexWhich interacting parts create the complexity?SystemicWhich connected parts produce the system wide effect?NaturalWhat biological or physical process do you actually mean?SustainableSustainable under what resources, timescale or constraints? These words are not inherently misleading. They become misleading when they end the explanation instead of beginning it. How Feynman Explained Anything to Anyone Channel: Feynman Archives Open on YouTube A Quick Test for Hidden Knowledge Gaps One reliable diagnostic is to remove every technical term temporarily. Suppose someone explains machine learning by saying: \"The model generalises because of regularisation.\" Now ban both \"generalises\" and \"regularisation\". Can the explanation continue? A stronger explanation might be: \"During training, the model could memorise every example, including accidental patterns that only appear in this dataset. The training process includes penalties that discourage overly complicated solutions, making it more likely that the model learns patterns that also appear in new data.\" If removing the jargon causes the explanation to collapse, the speaker may have learned terminology before learning the mechanism. Researchers have repeatedly shown that asking people to generate detailed explanations reduces inflated confidence because it exposes these missing links. Simply attempting to explain often recalibrates people's judgement of what they actually know. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by L Rozenblit · 2002 · Cited by 1499 — We argue here that people's limited knowledge and their misleading intuitive epistemology comb... The Aim Is Precision, Not Simplicity Plain English should not be confused with oversimplification. Some ideas are genuinely difficult, and technical vocabulary often exists because ordinary language alone becomes cumbersome. The real test is whether the specialist term could, if necessary, be expanded into a clear causal explanation appropriate for the audience. Experts usually can move comfortably between both levels. They know when shorthand is sufficient and when the underlying mechanism must be unpacked. Someone with weaker understanding often moves only in one direction: from explanation to terminology. Someone with deeper understanding can move in both directions, compressing detailed knowledge into precise jargon when appropriate and expanding that jargon back into plain language whenever explanation demands it. That flexibility is one of the clearest"},{"title":"Explain Recall","url":"/explain-recall/","description":"Explaining from memory forces recall, connection-making, and gap-finding more strongly than rereading alone.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teach It","level":3,"text":"Explain Recall Why Explaining Beats Rereading Alone Explain Recall Explain Recall Why Explaining Beats Rereading Alone Explaining from memory forces recall, connection making, and gap finding more strongly than rereading alone. Explaining from memory forces recall, connection making, and gap finding more strongly than rereading alone. mechanism Retrieval as a stronger test than recognition Self explanation as gap finding work Why preparing to teach changes how you study improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it explain recall Introduction One of the strongest ways to test whether you genuinely understand an idea is to close the book and explain it from memory. Reading can create a powerful feeling of familiarity , but familiarity is not the same as understanding. Self explanation—trying to teach a concept in your own words without looking at your notes—forces you to retrieve information, connect ideas into a coherent explanation, and expose the places where your understanding is incomplete. Research in cognitive psychology consistently shows that this combination of retrieval and explanation produces more durable learning than repeated rereading or passive review alone. [psychnet.wustl.edu] psychnet.wustl.edu Roediger Karpicke 2006 PPS The Power of Testing Memoryby HL Roediger III · Cited by 3306 — In this article, we review research from both experimental and educationa... Within the broader idea of plain English teaching as a thinking test, self explanation is valuable because it measures what you can reconstruct yourself rather than what you can recognise on a page. The difficulty of the exercise is not a flaw; it is the mechanism through which better understanding develops. Why recognition feels like learning but recall reveals understanding Reading the same material several times usually becomes easier. Sentences feel familiar, examples seem obvious, and technical terms appear recognisable. Unfortunately, this ease can be misleading. Recognition depends on external cues. When the information is in front of you, your brain can often identify the correct idea without having to rebuild it. Retrieval asks a different question: can you produce the idea without those cues? That difference is crucial because real thinking rarely comes with the answer already visible. This distinction explains the well known \" testing effect\", more accurately called retrieval practice . Experiments by Henry Roediger and Jeffrey Karpicke found that students who spent more time actively recalling material retained substantially more after a delay than students who spent the same time rereading it, even though the rereading group often felt more confident immediately afterwards. The act of retrieval strengthens later access to the memory instead of merely refreshing familiarity. [psychnet.wustl.edu] psychnet.wustl.edu Roediger Karpicke 2006 PPS The Power of Testing Memoryby HL Roediger III · Cited by 3306 — In this article, we review research from both experimental and educationa... Repeated rereading therefore has a paradoxical weakness: it often improves confidence faster than it improves long term understanding. Retrieval practice feels harder precisely because it demands reconstruction rather than recognition. [Department of Education] education ni.gov.uk Department of Education Retrieval Practice Department of EducationRetrieval Practice:April 29, 2025 — Karpicke and Roediger (2008) noted that students who repeatedly re read texts... Published: April 29, 2025 The Testing Effect & Retrieval practice: the number 1 study hack Channel: Presenting Psychology Open on YouTube Self explanation turns recall into a search for gaps Simply recalling isolated facts is valuable, but explaining those facts in plain language demands something richer. A successful explanation requires you to: retrieve the relevant facts; organise them into a logical sequence; identify causal relationships rather than isolated definitions; translate specialist language into everyday language; and notice where the explanation becomes vague or incomplete. Each hesitation is informative. If you cannot explain why one step follows another, you have discovered a genuine gap instead of merely discovering that you forgot a word. This process also counters the illusion of explanatory depth—the tendency to believe we understand how something works until we attempt a detailed explanation. People commonly overestimate their understanding of mechanisms because they possess familiar labels or broad intuitions. Requiring a full explanation exposes the missing causal links. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Illusion of explanatory depth Illusion of explanatory depth For example, someone may confidently define \"inflation\" or \"natural selection\" while reading a textbook. Asking them to explain, from memory, exactly how one event leads to the next often reveals missing steps they did not realise were missing. The Testing Effect: The Science of How To Actually Remember Channel: Dr. Erik Albinson Learning & Performance Coach · Views: 740 · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Why preparing to teach changes how you study Knowing that you will have to explain something later changes how you approach learning in the first place. Instead of trying to memorise sentences, learners begin asking questions such as: Why does this happen? What causes the next step? Could I explain this without using technical vocabulary? What example would make this clear? These questions encourage organisation rather than accumulation. Instead of storing disconnected facts, learners build networks of relationships that can be reconstructed later. Research on self explanation has repeatedly shown that generating explanations while learning improves understanding because learners actively integrate new information with what they already know rather than copying information passively. Combined with retrieval practice, explanation becomes both a learning activity and a diagnostic tool. [My College] my.chartered.college using evidence based practices that support metacognition My CollegeUsing evidence based practices that support metacognition26 Feb 2024 — The aim of this article is to investigate the efficacy o... Importantly, the goal is not to deliver a polished lecture. The value comes from discovering where the explanation fails. Why explaining is harder than rereading—and why that matters Many learners judge study methods by how fluent they feel. By that standard, rereading appears highly effective because every pass becomes easier. The evidence points in the opposite direction. Learning strategies that feel effortful during study often produce stronger long term retention than strategies that feel smooth and comfortable. Retrieval requires mental effort because memories must be reconstructed, and that reconstruction itself strengthens later recall . Researchers sometimes describe this as a \"desirable difficulty\": appropriately challenging retrieval improves learning even though it feels less successful at the time. [psychnet.wustl.edu] psychnet.wustl.edu Roediger Karpicke 2006 PPS The Power of Testing Memoryby HL Roediger III · Cited by 3306 — In this article, we review research from both experimental and educationa... This helps explain a common experience: During rereading: \"I know this already.\" During explanation: \"I thought I knew this, but I cannot explain why this step happens.\" The second experience is more valuable. It transforms hidden ignorance into visible learning targets. This One Study Technique Changes Everything (Most Students Miss It) Channel: Dr. Yu Ling Lee Open on YouTube A practical explain from memory routine A simple routine combines retrieval with self explanation without requiring elaborate tools. 1. Study one small concept. 2. Close the book or notes. 3. Explain the idea aloud or on paper using ordinary language. 4. Mark every hesitation, missing step, or vague phrase. 5. Return briefly to the source only to repair those specific gaps. 6. Repeat the explanation from memory until the complete chain can be reconstructed without prompts. Notice that the checking stage comes after the explanation, not during it. Looking at the notes too early turns retrieval back into recognition. Keeping explanations narrow also improves accuracy. Explaining \"why vaccines stimulate immune memory\" or \"why overfitting reduces performance on new data\" is usually more productive than attempting to explain an entire discipline at once. What the evidence suggests Across decades of educational psychology, retrieval practice has become one of the most consistently supported learning strategies for improving long term retention. Studies have shown benefits across different ages, subjects and learning environments, while reviews conclude that actively retrieving information generally outperforms additional study time devoted solely to rereading. [psychnet.wustl.edu+2Wikipedia] psychnet.wustl.edu"},{"title":"Teach Back","url":"/teach-back/","description":"Asking someone to explain an idea back in their own words often finds confusion that yes-or-no checks miss.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teach It","level":3,"text":"Teach Back The Question That Finds Hidden Confusion Teach Back Teach Back The Question That Finds Hidden Confusion Asking someone to explain an idea back in their own words often finds confusion that yes or no checks miss. Asking someone to explain an idea back in their own words often finds confusion that yes or no checks miss. policy intervention Why 'Do you understand?' is a weak check How teach back works in healthcare and learning How to respond when the explanation fails improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it teach back Introduction A teach back question is designed to uncover misunderstanding, not to confirm agreement. Instead of asking, “Do you understand?”, it asks someone to explain an idea, decision, or process in their own words. That small change often exposes gaps that a simple “yes” cannot. Within the broader practice of using plain English explanation as a thinking test, teach back is especially valuable because it reveals whether someone has built a working mental model rather than merely recognising familiar terminology. Healthcare has developed teach back into a practical safety tool because misunderstandings about medicines, treatment plans, or follow up care can have serious consequences. The same principle applies to learning, management, and analytical thinking: when people explain an idea back in their own words, hidden confusion becomes visible early enough to correct it. [AHRQ] ahrq.gov Use the Teach Back Method: Tool 5The teach back method is a way of checking understanding by asking patients to state in their own wo... Why “Do you understand?” is a weak check The question “Do you understand?” feels polite and efficient, but it provides remarkably little information. Many people answer “yes” because they believe they understand, because they do not want to appear confused, or because they assume the missing pieces will become clear later. In other cases, they may understand individual facts but not how those facts fit together. Research and practical guidance from the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) emphasise that yes or no questions are not teach back questions precisely because they fail to distinguish confidence from comprehension. The person giving the explanation receives reassurance without evidence that the explanation actually worked. [AHRQ] ahrq.gov Use the Teach Back Method: Tool 5The teach back method is a way of checking understanding by asking patients to state in their own wo... This weakness becomes especially important when people are learning causal ideas rather than memorising facts. Someone may confidently recognise terms such as \" feedback loop \", \"opportunity cost\", or \"correlation\" while being unable to explain how those ideas operate in a concrete situation. Teach back makes that distinction visible because it requires reconstruction rather than recognition. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Use and Effectiveness of the Teach Back Method in Patient...by PH Yen · 2019 · Cited by 612 — By using the teach back method, HCPs ca... How teach back reveals hidden confusion A good teach back question does not ask for repetition. It asks for reconstruction. [ahrq.gov] ahrq.gov Tool: Teach Back Agency for Healthcare Research and...In a teach back, you ask the patient or family caregiver to explain the info... Instead of repeating the original wording, the learner must: organise the information into a coherent explanation; identify the important steps; connect causes with consequences; translate technical language into everyday language; and explain what actions should follow. Each of these steps tests understanding in a different way. If someone can only quote definitions, they may have memorised vocabulary rather than built an explanatory model. If they skip important links in the chain, those missing links identify exactly where further teaching is needed. This explains why teach back is a stronger thinking test than recall alone. Remembering isolated facts is compatible with misunderstanding how those facts relate to one another. Explaining an idea forces those relationships into the open. [AHRQ] ahrq.gov Use the Teach Back Method: Tool 5The teach back method is a way of checking understanding by asking patients to state in their own wo... Health Literacy in Community Pharmacy The 'Teach Back' Method Channel: CDHN Open on YouTube How teach back works in healthcare and learning Healthcare provides one of the clearest demonstrations of teach back because misunderstandings can directly affect safety. Rather than ending an explanation with: \"Do you understand how to take this medicine?\" a clinician might ask: \"When you get home, how will you take this medicine tomorrow?\" Or instead of asking whether discharge instructions make sense: \"Can you tell me, in your own words, what you'll do if your symptoms get worse?\" These questions reveal practical misunderstandings that otherwise remain hidden. A patient may remember the medicine's name but confuse the dosage, misunderstand when to seek emergency help, or incorrectly combine treatments. The explanation can then be corrected immediately rather than after a preventable mistake. AHRQ also recommends \"show me\" demonstrations for physical tasks such as using inhalers or changing dressings, since being able to describe a procedure does not always mean being able to perform it correctly. [AHRQ] ahrq.gov Use the Teach Back Method: Tool 5The teach back method is a way of checking understanding by asking patients to state in their own wo... The same structure transfers naturally into education and analytical work. Rather than asking: \"Does that chapter make sense?\" ask: \"How would you explain the main idea to a colleague?\" \"Why does the second step follow from the first?\" \"What problem is this method trying to solve?\" \"What would happen if one assumption changed?\" These questions expose whether the learner has formed a usable explanation rather than merely recognising familiar material. What effective teach back questions look like Strong teach back questions share several characteristics. [ahrq.gov] ahrq.gov Tool: Teach Back Agency for Healthcare Research and...In a teach back, you ask the patient or family caregiver to explain the info... They ask for explanation, not confirmation. Instead of: \"Do you understand inflation?\" ask: \"How would you explain inflation to someone leaving school?\" They focus on action when appropriate. Instead of: \"Is the process clear?\" ask: \"What would you do first after reading these instructions?\" They require causal reasoning. Instead of: \"Do you know why this works?\" ask: \"Why does this step make the next one possible?\" They encourage ordinary language. Instead of encouraging technical vocabulary, invite explanations that avoid jargon wherever possible. Translating specialised language into everyday speech often exposes where understanding depends on memorised labels. These questions shift attention from recalling words towards demonstrating understanding. What Is Teach Back? (The Communication Technique That Improves Understanding) Channel: Quizlet Open on YouTube Signs that the explanation has failed A failed teach back is not simply a wrong answer. More often, it reveals specific patterns of misunderstanding. Common warning signs include: explaining procedures without explaining why they matter; repeating phrases almost word for word from the original explanation; skipping critical intermediate steps; using technical vocabulary correctly but inconsistently; giving examples that contradict the intended principle; confusing conditions with outcomes (\"this happens because...\" when the causal direction is reversed); or changing the topic when asked for a causal explanation. These failures are valuable because each one identifies a different instructional problem. Someone who repeats memorised wording may need opportunities to translate concepts. Someone who consistently skips intermediate steps may need help building a clearer mental model rather than receiving more information. How to respond when the explanation fails Teach back is not a test that produces pass or fail results. It is a feedback loop. [ahrq.gov] ahrq.gov Tool: Teach Back Agency for Healthcare Research and...In a teach back, you ask the patient or family caregiver to explain the info... Healthcare guidance consistently recommends treating misunderstanding as evidence that the explanation should change, not evidence that the learner has failed. A common framing is: \"I may not have explained that clearly. Let me try a different way.\" This matters because it reduces embarrassment and encourages honest responses. People are more willing to expose confusion when the emphasis is on improving the explanation rather than judging their ability. [AHRQ] ahrq.gov Use the Teach Back Method: Tool 5The teach back method is a way of checking understanding by asking patients to state in their own wo... An effective response usually follows four"},{"title":"10 Minute Test","url":"/10-minute-test/","description":"A short teaching exercise can quickly show which parts of an idea are clear, vague, missing, or assumed.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teach It","level":3,"text":"10 Minute Test A Fast Way To Test One Idea 10 Minute Test 10 Minute Test A Fast Way To Test One Idea A short teaching exercise can quickly show which parts of an idea are clear, vague, missing, or assumed. A short teaching exercise can quickly show which parts of an idea are clear, vague, missing, or assumed. policy intervention Choosing a claim small enough to test Explaining to a specific beginner audience Marking weak spots and revising once improving your thinking and analytical skills teach it 10 minute test Introduction A ten minute plain English test is a quick way to discover whether you genuinely understand one idea or merely recognise its terminology. Instead of trying to teach an entire subject, you choose a single, narrow claim, explain it in everyday language to an imagined beginner, and note every point where your explanation becomes vague, incomplete, or dependent on jargon. The goal is not to produce a polished lesson. It is to expose the hidden gaps that ordinary reading, highlighting, or recognising definitions often fail to reveal. Research on self explanation, retrieval practice , teach back , and the illusion of explanatory depth all point to the same practical lesson: attempting to explain an idea from memory is a far more reliable test of understanding than simply feeling familiar with it. [Department of Education+2PMC] education ni.gov.uk Department of Education Retrieval Practice Department of EducationRetrieval Practice:April 29, 2025 — Retrieval Practice has consistently been found to be one of the most effective... Published: April 29, 2025 Choosing a claim small enough to test The most common reason this exercise fails is that the chosen topic is too broad. A ten minute session cannot meaningfully test your understanding of \"economics\", \"artificial intelligence\", or \"evolution\". It can test a single explanatory claim. Good examples include: Why higher interest rates usually reduce inflation. Why overfitting causes a machine learning model to perform poorly on new data. Why compound interest grows faster over long periods. Why a vaccine can protect someone without giving them the disease. Poor choices include: Economics. Biology. Climate change. Programming. A useful rule is that the claim should answer one \"why\" or \"how\" question. If you find yourself needing several pages of notes before you can begin, the scope is too large. Reducing the size of the claim makes it much easier to identify the precise point where your understanding becomes uncertain rather than simply overwhelming yourself with complexity. Explaining to one specific beginner The exercise works best when you imagine a real audience instead of \"the general public\". Choose someone with no specialist background but enough everyday knowledge to follow ordinary conversation—for example, a curious teenager, a colleague from another profession, or a relative who enjoys learning new things. Explain without consulting notes. This retrieval step matters because recalling knowledge from memory exposes weaknesses that remain hidden when the source material is open in front of you. Research on retrieval practice consistently shows that actively recalling information improves both learning and the ability to detect missing knowledge better than passive review alone. [Department of Education] education ni.gov.uk Department of Education Retrieval Practice Department of EducationRetrieval Practice:April 29, 2025 — Retrieval Practice has consistently been found to be one of the most effective... Published: April 29, 2025 Aim for a simple structure: You Don't Know Anything The Illusion of Explanatory Depth FutureIQ Channel: Future IQ Open on YouTube 1. State what the idea is. 2. Explain why it matters. 3. Describe what happens step by step. 4. Give one concrete example. 5. Mention one important limitation or exception if it is central to understanding. This structure naturally forces causal thinking instead of encouraging lists of definitions. Marking weak spots instead of hiding them The value of the exercise comes from recording failures rather than avoiding them. While explaining, mark every place where you: pause for several seconds; use a technical term without defining it; replace explanation with phrases such as \"basically\", \"it's just\", or \"obviously\"; skip directly from cause to conclusion without describing what happens in between; realise you cannot answer a likely beginner's question. These are not mistakes to erase. They are evidence showing exactly where further learning should be focused. Healthcare's teach back approach follows a similar principle. Instead of asking whether someone understands instructions, clinicians ask them to explain the information in their own words. Any misunderstanding is treated as feedback about the explanation rather than as personal failure, allowing clarification before moving on. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Evaluation of teach back and plain language acceptability...by AA Sleiman · 2025 · Cited by 1 — This study advances clinician patient... Revise only once A common temptation is to keep rewriting until the explanation sounds elegant. That is not the purpose of a ten minute test. Instead: identify the missing knowledge; consult one reliable source to fill only those gaps; explain the same idea again immediately; stop after one revision. Keeping the cycle short prevents the exercise from becoming ordinary note taking. It also creates a clear comparison between the first explanation and the second, making improvements visible. Educational research on retrieval and feedback suggests that recalling information first and then correcting errors produces stronger learning than repeatedly rereading material without testing yourself. [Department of Education] education ni.gov.uk Department of Education Retrieval Practice Department of EducationRetrieval Practice:April 29, 2025 — Retrieval Practice has consistently been found to be one of the most effective... Published: April 29, 2025 The Feynman Technique — Stop Memorizing, Start Understanding Channel: Feynman Archives Open on YouTube A worked example Suppose the claim is: \"Price ceilings can create shortages.\" A weak explanation might be: \"Prices are kept low, which affects supply and demand.\" Although technically correct, it leaves almost every important mechanism unexplained. After revision, the explanation becomes: \"If the government sets the maximum price below what many buyers are willing to pay, more people want to buy the product because it is cheaper. At the same time, some producers make less because selling becomes less profitable. More buyers and fewer sellers mean demand becomes greater than supply, so some people cannot buy the product even though they want it.\" The improvement comes from adding the missing causal chain rather than introducing more specialist vocabulary. Signs the test is working The exercise is succeeding when you notice yourself changing how you think, not merely polishing how you speak. Typical indicators include: replacing abstract words with concrete actions; identifying assumptions you had not realised you were making; discovering that two ideas you treated as identical are actually different; finding questions you cannot yet answer; reducing the explanation while increasing its precision. Paradoxically, many people feel less confident after the first attempt because they discover how much they do not yet understand. Research on the illusion of explanatory depth suggests this reduction in overconfidence is often a sign that self assessment has become more accurate. [Department of Education] education ni.gov.uk Department of Education Retrieval Practice Department of EducationRetrieval Practice:April 29, 2025 — Retrieval Practice has consistently been found to be one of the most effective... Published: April 29, 2025 How to Use the Feynman Technique Study Tips How to Study Channel: Socratica Open on YouTube Common mistakes Several habits reduce the value of the exercise: Choosing a topic that is too large. Narrow claims reveal specific weaknesses. Checking notes while explaining. This measures reading ability rather than understanding. Replacing explanation with terminology. Naming a concept is not the same as explaining its mechanism. Trying to sound expert. The purpose is clarity, not sophistication. Rewriting indefinitely. One focused revision is usually enough to reveal whether the explanation has genuinely improved. Why this short exercise improves analytical thinking The ten minute plain English test works because it turns understanding into something observable. Instead of asking whether an idea feels familiar, it asks whether you can reconstruct the reasoning in your own words, connect each step, and survive simple questions from a beginner. By limiting the exercise to one narrowly defined claim and one revision, it remains practical enough to repeat regularly while providing immediate evidence about what you know, what"},{"title":"Probabilities","url":"/probabilities/","description":"Many hard choices involve uncertainty, likelihoods, and competing values rather than one clean right answer.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Probabilities How Do You Think Through Uncertainty? Probabilities Probabilities How Do You Think Through Uncertainty? Many hard choices involve uncertainty, likelihoods, and competing values rather than one clean right answer. Many hard choices involve uncertainty, likelihoods, and competing values rather than one clean right answer. mechanism Probability questions Trade off decisions Confidence and uncertainty improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities Introduction Analytical thinking through uncertainty means replacing “Will this work?” with better questions: “How likely is each outcome?”, “What would it cost if I am wrong?”, “Which values are in tension?”, and “How confident should I be?” Many difficult choices are not puzzles with one clean answer. They are probability and trade off problems: taking a job, launching a project, judging a medical test, buying insurance, accepting a risk, or deciding whether more information is worth the delay. Good thinking here does not remove uncertainty. It makes uncertainty visible enough to reason with. The core mechanism is simple: separate the decision into probabilities, outcomes, values, and confidence. Probabilities describe how likely things are. Outcomes describe what may happen. Values describe what you care about. Confidence describes how much weight your estimate deserves. Research on forecasting, risk communication, decision analysis and behavioural economics shows that people can improve this kind of judgement, especially when they use explicit probabilities, base rates, natural frequencies , feedback , sensitivity checks and structured comparison of competing objectives. [GOV.UK+3Coefficient Giving+3PMC] openphilanthropy.org how accurate are our predictions overconfident vs. underconfident. If a forecaster is well calibrated for...Read more... Why Uncertain Choices Need a Different Kind of Thinking A certain decision can be judged mainly by fit: does this action match the goal? An uncertain decision has to be judged before the result is known. That distinction matters because a good decision can still have a bad outcome, and a lucky outcome can hide a poor decision. Someone who makes a sensible investment may lose money because of a rare shock; someone who takes a reckless shortcut may get away with it once. Analytical thinking tries to judge the quality of the reasoning at the time of choice, not merely the outcome afterwards. Decision analysis treats uncertainty as something to be represented, not wished away. The UK government’s Green Book, for example, frames appraisal as assessing the costs, benefits and risks of different options in a structured way so decision makers receive objective, evidence based advice rather than a single unsupported recommendation. It also warns that a benefit cost ratio alone is not a complete measure of value for money, because risk appetite, uncertainty and distributional effects can change what counts as a good choice. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK the green book 2026 the green book 2026 The practical shift is from “Which option is best?” to “Which option is best under which assumptions ?” That is why uncertainty thinking often uses scenarios, decision trees , ranges, sensitivity analysis and explicit risk appetite. These tools are not only for governments or analysts. The same logic applies to everyday choices: a cheaper flat may be better if commuting time stays manageable, worse if transport reliability collapses, and unacceptable if the loss of social time matters more than the rent saving. Probability Questions: From Vague Possibility to Usable Estimates A probability question asks for a degree of belief, not a guarantee. “There is a risk the project overruns” is vague. “I think there is a 35% chance it overruns by more than two months” is much more useful, because it can be challenged, compared, updated and scored later. The number does not need to be perfect to be valuable; it forces the thinker to say whether they mean a remote possibility, a serious risk, or the most likely outcome. Forecasting research makes this visible. The Good Judgment Project, led by Philip Tetlock and Barbara Mellers, studied large scale geopolitical forecasting and found that accuracy improved through methods such as training, teaming, aggregation and repeated scoring. Open Philanthropy’s review of its own forecasting practice describes calibration curves: if a forecaster assigns many events 65–75% confidence, roughly that share should later come true if the forecaster is well calibrated in that range. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Open source on goodjudgment.com. A useful probability habit is to begin with a base rate. Before asking whether this particular start up will succeed, ask how often similar start ups succeed. Before deciding whether one applicant will thrive in a role, ask what proportion of similar hires have done well. Before believing a dramatic diagnosis from a test result, ask how common the condition is in the tested population. Base rates do not settle the question, but they stop the most vivid detail from becoming the whole story. Probability also becomes easier when expressed as counts rather than abstract percentages. Research on natural frequencies shows that people often reason better when a problem is framed as “10 out of 1,000” rather than “1%”, especially in Bayesian tasks such as interpreting medical test results. A 2015 paper found that natural frequency formats improved Bayesian inferences by medical students across more complex tasks by an average of 37 percentage points compared with probability formats. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The reason is mechanical, not magical. Natural frequencies preserve the nested structure of the problem. If 1,000 people are tested, 10 have the disease, 9 of those test positive, and 99 people without the disease also test positive, the question becomes: out of all positive tests, how many are true positives? That is much easier to see than the same problem expressed as conditional probabilities. [cogsci.ucsd.edu] cogsci.ucsd.edu How to Improve Bayesian Reasoning Without Instruction How to Improve Bayesian Reasoning Without Instruction For practical thinking, probability questions should be written in a way that could later be checked: “What is the chance this project exceeds £50,000 by 31 December?” “What is the chance this candidate is still performing well after one year?” “What is the chance this supplier misses the agreed delivery date?” “What is the chance this symptom resolves without treatment within two weeks?” Each version defines the outcome, threshold and time frame. That prevents the common escape hatch of saying “I was basically right” after the fact. Trade Off Decisions: When the Hard Part Is Not the Probability Some decisions are hard because probabilities are unknown. Others are hard because the values conflict. A job offer may be more secure but less meaningful. A policy may be cost effective on average but unfairly distribute burdens. A medical treatment may improve survival odds but reduce quality of life. Analytical thinking for trade offs does not pretend these conflicts vanish; it makes the exchange explicit. Multi criteria decision analysis, or MCDA, is one formal way to do this. The UK Civil Service describes MCDA as useful when decision makers face multiple options with several conflicting objectives, mixed criteria that cannot be easily compared, or different stakeholder perspectives. The point is not to turn every value into a fake precision score. It is to clarify which criteria matter, how options perform against them, and where judgement rather than calculation is doing the work. [analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk] analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk an introductory guide to mcda an introductory guide to mcda A practical trade off analysis usually has four parts. First, name the options. Second, name the criteria that genuinely matter. Third, decide which criteria are essential, important or merely nice to have. Fourth, test whether the preferred option changes when the weights change. If a decision flips whenever one assumption moves slightly, it is fragile. If it remains attractive across several reasonable assumptions, it is more robust. The trap is to compare options only on the easiest to measure dimension. Money, time and headline risk often dominate because they are visible. Less visible factors, such as reversibility, reputation, learning value, stress, fairness, resilience and option value, may matter just as much. A lower cost option that locks you into a brittle path may be worse than a more expensive option that preserves flexibility. Trade offs also require noticing opportunity cost: what you give up by choosing one path over another. The real cost of saying yes to a demanding project is not only the hours it consumes, but the other projects, recovery time, relationships or learning opportunities those hours can no longer"},{"title":"Base Rates","url":"/base-rates-271245/","description":"Base rates keep vivid details from crowding out what usually happens in similar decisions.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Probabilities","level":3,"text":"Base Rates Start With What Usually Happens Base Rates Base Rates Start With What Usually Happens Base rates keep vivid details from crowding out what usually happens in similar decisions. Base rates keep vivid details from crowding out what usually happens in similar decisions. mechanism Why base rates come before case details How to find a useful comparison class When the base rate should be adjusted improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities base rates Introduction Many judgement errors begin with a simple mistake: focusing on what makes a case feel special before asking what usually happens in similar cases. A compelling story, an impressive interview, a worrying symptom or an unusual detail can dominate attention even when the strongest initial evidence is the underlying frequency of the event. Starting with the base rate—the proportion of similar cases that typically produce a particular outcome—provides an anchor that prevents vivid but potentially misleading information from taking over. Research in psychology, decision science and medicine consistently shows that people often neglect base rates, yet also shows that this tendency can be reduced when information is presented clearly and when decision makers deliberately begin with an appropriate comparison class. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Interestingly, in addition to... Why base rates come before case details A base rate is the general prevalence of an outcome within a relevant group before considering the unique features of the current case. It answers questions such as: How many similar businesses become profitable? How often does this medical condition occur in people like this patient? What proportion of applicants with similar backgrounds succeed in this role? How frequently do projects of this size exceed their budget? This starting point matters because uncertainty has two sources: 1. The general pattern shared by many similar cases. 2. The specific evidence that makes the current case different. Good probabilistic reasoning combines both. The mistake known as base rate neglect occurs when the second source receives almost all the attention while the first is largely ignored. Classic experiments by psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky demonstrated that people often rely on descriptions that seem representative of a category while underweighting statistical information about how common the category actually is. Later research has confirmed that this tendency appears in many settings, although its strength depends on how information is presented and how familiar people are with the task. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment+2Wikipedia] cambridge.org Interestingly, in addition to... The practical consequence is straightforward: before asking, \"Does this look like an exception?\", first ask, \"What normally happens?\" How to find a useful comparison class The value of a base rate depends entirely on choosing the right comparison class. A poor comparison can be as misleading as having no comparison at all. A useful comparison class should resemble the current decision on the factors that genuinely influence the outcome rather than on superficial similarities. For example: A software start up should not be compared with all new businesses, but with firms of similar age, industry, funding and business model. A job applicant should be compared with previous hires into the same role, not with employees across the whole organisation. A house purchase should be compared with similar properties in the same market rather than national averages. Finding an appropriate comparison class usually involves asking three questions: 1. What outcome am I trying to predict ? 2. Which past cases were genuinely similar before their outcomes were known? 3. How many such cases are available? This approach reduces hindsight bias because similarity is defined using information available at the time of decision rather than by selecting examples that happened to produce the desired outcome. The Base Rate Fallacy: Why 99% Accuracy Fails Channel: Learn Fast · Views: 11 · Uploaded: April 2026 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Beware of comparison classes that are too broad or too narrow An overly broad comparison class hides important differences. For example, saying \"most businesses fail\" tells an entrepreneur little if their company belongs to a well funded niche with very different survival rates. An overly narrow comparison class creates another problem: the sample becomes too small to estimate reliable probabilities . Looking only at \"companies founded by left handed engineers in coastal cities during leap years\" produces numbers that appear precise but are based on too little evidence. The goal is not the most detailed comparison possible. It is the most informative comparison supported by enough evidence. When the base rate should be adjusted A base rate is a starting estimate, not a final verdict. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Base rate fallacy Base rate fallacyKahneman considers base rate neglect to be a specific form of extension neglect. Richard Nisbett has argued that some... After establishing what usually happens, ask whether the current case contains evidence strong enough to justify moving away from that starting point. Examples include: A patient belongs to a low risk population but has highly reliable diagnostic evidence. A project resembles previous failures but now uses a proven technology that removes a major historical risk. An applicant has characteristics that previous successful employees rarely possessed but that objective evidence shows are strongly predictive of performance. The adjustment should depend on the strength and reliability of the new evidence, not on how vivid or emotionally compelling it feels. One useful mental sequence is: 1. Estimate the base rate. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Base rate fallacy Base rate fallacyKahneman considers base rate neglect to be a specific form of extension neglect. Richard Nisbett has argued that some... 2. Examine the case specific evidence. 3. Ask how much that evidence should change the initial estimate. 4. Reach an updated judgement. This mirrors the logic behind Bayesian reasoning, even if no formal calculations are performed. Rather than replacing the base rate with new information, the new information modifies it. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Interestingly, in addition to... Why memorable stories often defeat statistics Human attention naturally favours concrete stories over abstract percentages. A detailed biography can feel more informative than knowing that only a small fraction of similar people achieve a particular outcome. Yet many individually persuasive details have surprisingly little predictive value. This happens because descriptive information often creates a strong sense of representativeness. If someone resembles our mental image of a successful entrepreneur, talented student or skilled detective, we instinctively raise our estimate of success even when success is objectively rare. Stories remain valuable—they may contain genuine evidence—but they should be interpreted against the background of what normally happens rather than replacing it altogether. Research suggests that making base rate information more explicit reduces this bias, especially when people are encouraged to integrate rather than ignore prior probabilities. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Interestingly, in addition to... The Representativeness Heuristic (Intro Psych Tutorial 93) Channel: PsychExamReview Open on YouTube A medical example: why prevalence matters Medical testing provides one of the clearest demonstrations of why base rates matter. Imagine a disease that is rare in the general population. Even a highly accurate screening test can produce many false positives simply because healthy people greatly outnumber people with the disease. Without considering the disease's prevalence, many people—including trained professionals in some studies—overestimate the probability that a positive test means the patient actually has the condition. The prevalence of the disease is part of the calculation, not an optional extra. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org It also showed physicians interpreting Cambridge University Press & AssessmentPhysicians neglect base rates, and it mattersby RM Hamm · 1996 · Cited by 23 — A recent study show... Researchers have repeatedly found that understanding improves when probabilities are expressed as natural frequencies rather than abstract percentages. Instead of saying: \"The disease prevalence is 1%.\" It is often easier to reason with: \"Out of every 1,000 people, about 10 have the disease.\" Presenting information this way makes the relationship between true positives, false positives and total tested people more transparent, improving Bayesian reasoning across medicine, education and other applied"},{"title":"Decision Trees","url":"/decision-trees/","description":"Decision trees turn uncertain choices into visible branches of probabilities, outcomes, and possible costs.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Probabilities","level":3,"text":"Decision Trees Map the Branches Before Choosing Decision Trees Decision Trees Map the Branches Before Choosing Decision trees turn uncertain choices into visible branches of probabilities, outcomes, and possible costs. Decision trees turn uncertain choices into visible branches of probabilities, outcomes, and possible costs. policy intervention When a decision tree is useful How to assign probabilities and outcomes Where hidden assumptions enter the tree improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities decision trees Introduction Decision trees are a practical way to compare choices when the future is uncertain. Instead of asking which option looks best today, they map each decision into a series of possible events, attach probabilities and consequences to each branch, and make the assumptions behind a choice visible. This helps distinguish a decision that is well reasoned from one that merely turns out well by luck. Decision trees are especially valuable when choices unfold over time, involve several uncertain events, or require weighing costs against potential gains. They are widely used in business strategy, engineering, healthcare, public policy and finance because they make complex decisions easier to analyse, communicate and revise as new information becomes available. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCDecision Analysis and Cost effectiveness Analysis NIHby HF Ryder · 2009 · Cited by 100 — The goal of decision analysis is to facilitate sound decisions in complex and uncertain situ... When a decision tree is useful A decision tree is most useful when a decision involves distinct alternatives followed by uncertain events rather than a single predictable outcome. Typical situations include: Choosing between competing projects with uncertain returns. Deciding whether to buy insurance or self insure against losses. Comparing career opportunities with different levels of salary, promotion prospects and job security. Planning phased investments where later decisions depend on early results. Deciding whether to gather more evidence before committing to an expensive action. Unlike a simple pros and cons list, a decision tree captures the order in which decisions occur. It distinguishes between decisions under your control and chance events that are outside your control. This sequencing is important because many real world decisions allow you to learn something before making the next commitment. Decision analysis refers to this as sequential decision making. [Independent Management Consultants] umbrex.com Independent Management ConsultantsDecision Tree Analysis ExplainedDecision Tree Analysis evaluates choices, probabilities, and payoffs to... A typical decision tree contains three main elements: [asana.com] asana.com decision tree analysis Decision Tree Analysis: 5 Steps with Expected Value [2025]30 Nov 2025 — Decision tree analysis helps you map decisions, probabilitie... Decision nodes , representing choices you control. Chance nodes , representing uncertain events with assigned probabilities. Outcome (terminal) nodes , representing the final costs, benefits or utilities associated with each path. [Asana] asana.com decision tree analysis Decision Tree Analysis: 5 Steps with Expected Value [2025]30 Nov 2025 — Decision tree analysis helps you map decisions, probabilitie... How to assign probabilities and outcomes The quality of a decision tree depends less on drawing the branches than on estimating the numbers attached to them. A practical workflow is: Decision Tree and Expected Monetary Value for the PMP Exam Channel: CorneliusFichtner Open on YouTube 1. Define the available decisions. 2. List the major uncertain events following each decision. 3. Estimate the probability of each event. 4. Assign a value to every final outcome. 5. Calculate the expected value for each branch by multiplying outcomes by their probabilities and summing the results. For example, imagine deciding whether to launch a new product. Launch immediately. 40% chance of earning £500,000. 60% chance of losing £100,000. Run a pilot first. Pilot costs £40,000. Successful pilot increases confidence before committing to full launch. Failed pilot allows the company to abandon the project early. Although the pilot adds an immediate cost, it may improve the overall expected outcome by preventing expensive failures. This illustrates why decision trees are particularly valuable for staged decisions rather than one off choices. [Independent Management Consultants] umbrex.com Independent Management ConsultantsDecision Tree Analysis ExplainedDecision Tree Analysis evaluates choices, probabilities, and payoffs to... Estimating probabilities realistically Many people instinctively rely on intuition when assigning probabilities. Better estimates usually combine several sources: Historical data from similar situations. Base rates from comparable projects or industries. Expert judgement when data are limited. Updated evidence as new information becomes available. Probabilities should represent uncertainty honestly rather than confidence or optimism. If several possible outcomes are mutually exclusive, their probabilities should add up to 100%. [interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl] interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl The expected value of the return of the actions is as follows: Buying shares: 0.2( 2 %) + 0.3...Read more... Measuring outcomes Money is often used because it is easy to compare, but many important decisions involve additional considerations, including: Time. Personal satisfaction. Reputation. Safety. Environmental or social effects. Decision analysis often uses the broader idea of utility , which represents the overall value of an outcome rather than financial return alone. This allows decision makers to reflect differing attitudes towards risk instead of assuming that everyone values gains and losses equally. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCDecision Analysis and Cost effectiveness Analysis NIHby HF Ryder · 2009 · Cited by 100 — The goal of decision analysis is to facilitate sound decisions in complex and uncertain situ... Where hidden assumptions enter the tree Decision trees appear objective, but every tree reflects assumptions that deserve scrutiny. Making those assumptions explicit is one of the tool's greatest strengths. Common hidden assumptions include: Probability estimates. Small changes can alter which branch appears preferable. Outcome values. Different stakeholders may value the same outcome differently. Missing branches. Rare but high impact events are sometimes omitted because they are inconvenient or difficult to estimate. Independence. Trees often assume uncertain events occur independently when they may actually influence one another. Future behaviour. A tree may assume that decision makers will follow the planned strategy even after new information emerges. For example, a company might build a decision tree assuming it will abandon a product if market testing fails. If managers ignore that rule because they have become emotionally committed to the project, the expected value calculated from the tree no longer reflects what will actually happen. [people.stern.nyu.edu] people.stern.nyu.edu SCENARIO ANALYSIS, DECISION TREES AND...The expected value of a decision tree is heavily dependent upon the assumption that we will stay... Sensitivity analysis helps expose these assumptions by asking questions such as: What if success is only half as likely? What if costs rise by 20%? What probability would make the preferred decision change? Rather than producing one \"correct\" answer, this reveals which assumptions genuinely matter and which have little effect on the final recommendation. Decision Tree Analysis with Example and Expected Value Easy & Fun Calculation Channel: Rocky River Woodworks Open on YouTube A career decision example Suppose someone must choose between remaining in a secure role or joining a start up. The secure role offers stable income with limited upside. The start up offers several possible futures: rapid growth, moderate success or failure. A decision tree allows each of these possibilities to be represented explicitly rather than discussed vaguely. The analysis may reveal that the start up has a higher expected financial return but also much greater variability. Once non financial factors—such as work life balance, learning opportunities or family commitments—are included, the preferred option may change. The tree therefore supports discussion rather than replacing judgement. This illustrates an important point: decision trees do not decide for you. They organise evidence so that trade offs become easier to evaluate. Strengths and limitations Decision trees offer several practical advantages. They make reasoning transparent, encourage explicit probability estimates, support communication among groups and can incorporate sequential decisions where later choices depend on earlier results. They are also relatively easy to revise when new"},{"title":"Downside Risk","url":"/downside-risk-62ef42/","description":"Expected value helps compare gambles, but it can hide ruinous, irreversible, or unfair downside risks.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Probabilities","level":3,"text":"Downside Risk When Expected Value Is Not Enough Downside Risk Downside Risk When Expected Value Is Not Enough Expected value helps compare gambles, but it can hide ruinous, irreversible, or unfair downside risks. Expected value helps compare gambles, but it can hide ruinous, irreversible, or unfair downside risks. mechanism How expected value simplifies uncertainty Why ruin changes the decision How risk appetite limits good looking bets improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities downside risk Introduction Expected value is one of the most useful ideas in decision making because it combines the probability of different outcomes with their consequences into a single average. It helps compare uncertain options that cannot be judged by looking only at the most likely result. However, expected value is not a complete decision rule. A choice can have an attractive average outcome while still exposing you to a small chance of catastrophic, irreversible, or personally unacceptable harm. Better analytical thinking therefore requires asking two questions rather than one. First, what is the expected value of this decision? Second, is there any outcome that would be unacceptable even if it is unlikely? Keeping these questions separate helps explain why sensible people sometimes reject positive expected value opportunities, buy insurance, diversify investments, or refuse bets that appear favourable on paper. How expected value simplifies uncertainty Expected value works by multiplying each possible outcome by its probability and adding the results together. If one option has a higher expected value than another, it produces the better average result over many similar decisions. This averaging process is powerful because it avoids focusing only on the best case or worst case scenario. For repeated, independent decisions—such as quality control, insurance pricing, or large numbers of comparable investments—expected value is often the right starting point. The simplification is also its limitation. Expected value compresses an entire distribution of outcomes into a single number. Two choices can have identical expected values while exposing the decision maker to very different patterns of risk. For example: Option A guarantees a gain of £100. Option B offers a 99% chance of gaining £102 but a 1% chance of losing £10,000. Depending on the precise figures, Option B could have a higher expected value than Option A. Yet many people would reasonably reject it because the rare loss is severe enough to outweigh the modest improvement in average return. The expected value calculation is mathematically correct. The mistake is assuming that the average alone captures everything that matters. Gambler's Ruin: The Math That Destroys Your Bankroll Channel: AxiomMotion Open on YouTube Why ruin changes the decision Expected value works best when losses are recoverable and decisions can be repeated many times. It becomes much less informative when one bad outcome permanently prevents future opportunities. This idea is often described as risk of ruin . Ruin does not necessarily mean bankruptcy. It refers to any outcome from which recovery is impossible or so costly that future gains become irrelevant. Examples include: losing all investment capital; permanent disability from an avoidable risk; destroying a company's reputation; suffering an irreversible environmental disaster; ending a career through one reckless decision. Once ruin occurs, future positive expected values cannot compensate because the decision maker no longer has the opportunity to benefit from them. This is why survival itself becomes an objective alongside maximising average returns. Research on risk of ruin and long term betting strategies shows that maximising average gains without considering survival can lead to eventual failure even when individual bets have positive expected value. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Risk of ruin Risk of ruin A simple illustration makes the distinction clear. Imagine repeatedly accepting a gamble with a slight positive expected value but a small probability of losing everything each time. On paper, each individual gamble is favourable. Over many repetitions, however, the cumulative probability of eventual ruin becomes substantial. Long term success therefore depends not only on expected gain but also on avoiding irreversible losses. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Kelly criterion Kelly criterion Behavioral Finance Basics Expected Value vs. Real World Decisions Using the St. Petersburg Paradox Channel: Dr. Bryan Foltice Behavioral Finance Open on YouTube Why averages can hide unacceptable outcomes Several features of real world decisions make expected value an incomplete guide. Catastrophic losses are not ordinary losses A loss that permanently changes your situation is different from a routine setback. Losing 5% of an investment portfolio and losing 100% are not merely different in size—they change what decisions remain available afterwards. This asymmetry explains why businesses maintain emergency reserves, governments prepare for rare disasters, and individuals buy insurance despite its negative expected monetary value. The aim is not to maximise average wealth but to prevent unacceptable states. One off decisions differ from repeated decisions Expected value assumes that similar decisions can often be repeated. Many important choices cannot. Examples include: choosing whether to undergo a high risk medical procedure; launching a company using all personal savings; entering a dangerous expedition; accepting a job that requires relocating a family. There may never be enough repetitions for the long run average to dominate the single realised outcome. In these situations, downside protection deserves greater weight than expected value alone. Small probabilities still matter People sometimes dismiss events with very low probabilities . Yet a tiny probability multiplied by an enormous consequence can become highly relevant. This is particularly important when consequences are irreversible. Even if the probability remains uncertain, the possibility of extreme damage justifies examining the downside separately rather than allowing it to disappear inside an average calculation. How risk appetite limits good looking bets Different people can rationally reject the same positive expected value opportunity because they have different limits on acceptable downside risk. Risk appetite reflects the amount and type of loss someone is prepared to tolerate in pursuit of potential gains. It depends on factors such as: available financial reserves; ability to recover from setbacks; responsibilities to others; legal or ethical obligations; psychological tolerance for uncertainty. An entrepreneur with diversified assets may accept risks that would be reckless for someone investing their entire life savings. Likewise, an airline or nuclear operator is expected to reject risks that might be acceptable in low consequence consumer markets because the downside includes loss of life and widespread harm. Rather than asking only, \"Is this gamble favourable on average?\", disciplined decision makers ask: What is the worst plausible outcome? Can I survive it? Can I recover from it? Would I still accept this outcome if it occurred tomorrow? These questions place boundaries around expected value reasoning instead of replacing it. Why Risk Management Beats Entry Selection — ArcAlpha Academy 3.01 Channel: ArcAlpha Open on YouTube Balancing expected value with downside protection Good analytical thinking combines expected value with explicit constraints on unacceptable loss. A practical sequence is: 1. Estimate the major outcomes and their probabilities. 2. Calculate the approximate expected value. 3. Identify outcomes that would be irreversible or intolerable. 4. Decide whether those outcomes are acceptable regardless of their contribution to the average. 5. Modify the decision if necessary by reducing exposure, diversifying, buying insurance, limiting position size, or avoiding the risk altogether. Investment practice illustrates this balance. The Kelly criterion seeks to maximise long run growth by recommending an optimal fraction of wealth to risk when probabilities are known. In practice, however, many investors deliberately use only a fraction of the recommended amount because probability estimates are imperfect and reducing exposure substantially lowers the chance of severe drawdowns or ruin. [Wikipedia+2Frontiers] Wikipedia Kelly criterion Kelly criterion The broader lesson extends well beyond finance. Expected value is an excellent tool for comparing uncertain opportunities, but it should always be checked against the possibility of outcomes that would permanently damage your ability to pursue future opportunities. Key takeaway Expected value answers an important question: what happens on average? It does not answer a different, equally important question: what happens if things go"},{"title":"Calibration","url":"/calibration-576c1f/","description":"Calibration shows whether confidence levels match what actually happens over many predictions.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Probabilities","level":3,"text":"Calibration Are Your Confidence Levels Honest? Calibration Calibration Are Your Confidence Levels Honest? Calibration shows whether confidence levels match what actually happens over many predictions. Calibration shows whether confidence levels match what actually happens over many predictions. dataset evidence What calibration means in forecasting How repeated scoring reveals overconfidence How to improve estimates with feedback improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities calibration Introduction Forecast calibration asks a simple but powerful question: when you say something has a 70% chance of happening, does it actually happen about 70% of the time across many similar predictions? This is different from merely being right or wrong on individual forecasts. A forecaster can occasionally make accurate predictions while consistently overstating or understating their confidence. Calibration therefore provides an evidence based way to judge whether confidence claims deserve trust. Within analytical thinking, calibration is valuable because it separates persuasive certainty from demonstrated reliability. Instead of judging confidence by tone, credentials or intuition , it evaluates whether stated probabilities match reality over repeated observations. This makes calibration a cornerstone of probabilistic reasoning in fields ranging from weather forecasting and medicine to intelligence analysis and everyday decision making. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... What calibration means in forecasting Calibration measures the relationship between predicted probabilities and observed outcomes . Imagine a forecaster makes one hundred predictions, each assigned a probability of 80%. If around eighty of those events actually occur, the forecaster is well calibrated at the 80% confidence level. If only sixty occur, they have been overconfident. If ninety five occur, they have been underconfident. This differs from asking whether a forecaster is simply \"accurate\". Accuracy concerns whether predictions are correct overall. Calibration concerns whether confidence levels honestly represent uncertainty. A useful way to visualise this is through a calibration curve (sometimes called a reliability diagram). Predictions are grouped into probability ranges—for example 10%, 30%, 50%, 70% and 90%—and compared with the proportion of events that actually happened. Perfect calibration produces points close to a diagonal line where predicted and observed frequencies match. [scikit learn] scikit learn.org 1.16. Probability calibrationThe calibration module allows you to better calibrate the probabilities of a given model, or to... Good calibration has two important characteristics: Reliable confidence. A stated probability has the meaning it claims. Consistency over time. Calibration is assessed across many forecasts, not isolated successes or failures. A single correct 95% prediction proves nothing about calibration. Only repeated measurement can reveal whether confidence estimates are honest. Why repeated scoring reveals overconfidence Human judgement is often more confident than justified by the evidence. Without systematic feedback , this tendency is difficult to notice because memorable successes overshadow forgotten mistakes . Forecasting research addresses this by requiring participants to assign explicit probabilities before outcomes are known, then scoring those predictions after events resolve. Repeated scoring exposes patterns that intuition alone misses. For example: Someone who frequently assigns 95% confidence but is correct only 75% of the time is substantially overconfident. Someone who rarely exceeds 60% confidence despite being correct almost every time is underconfident. A well calibrated forecaster can still be wrong on individual predictions because unlikely events sometimes occur. The emphasis on repeated measurement also guards against hindsight bias. Once an outcome becomes known, people often feel they \"knew it all along\", making their original uncertainty seem smaller than it really was. Recording probabilities beforehand preserves an objective record for later evaluation. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... Handling Uncertainty in Clinical Conversations: Communicating Risk While Building Trust EBHC Explain Channel: Everblink Healthcare Pvt Ltd Open on YouTube Calibration is not the same as forecasting skill Calibration is necessary but not sufficient for excellent forecasting. Consider two forecasters: Forecaster A predicts every event with 50% confidence. Forecaster B confidently distinguishes likely from unlikely events while remaining well calibrated. Forecaster A may appear reasonably calibrated because about half of all events occur, but their forecasts provide little useful information. They never express meaningful differences between situations. Forecaster B is both calibrated and informative, assigning high probabilities when evidence strongly supports an outcome and low probabilities when it does not. Forecast researchers therefore distinguish between: Calibration (reliability): Do stated probabilities match observed frequencies? Resolution or sharpness: Do forecasts meaningfully separate likely from unlikely events? An ideal forecaster achieves both. Honest probabilities alone are valuable, but the greatest practical value comes from combining reliable confidence with informative discrimination. [Wikipedia+2Emergent Mind] Wikipedia Brier score Brier score What scoring rules actually measure Forecast tournaments commonly use proper scoring rules , mathematical measures that reward honest probability estimates rather than exaggerated confidence. The best known example is the Brier score , which measures the squared difference between predicted probabilities and actual outcomes. Lower scores indicate better probabilistic forecasts. However, an important subtlety is that a low Brier score alone does not prove good calibration. Recent methodological work shows that the score combines several properties—including calibration and discrimination—and can sometimes appear favourable even when systematic probability bias exists. Analysts therefore often examine calibration curves alongside numerical scores instead of relying on a single metric. [PMC+2Wikipedia] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov We can have perfect predictions, but low or high Brier score due to the underlying...Read more... This distinction matters because different forecasting systems may have similar overall scores while displaying very different confidence behaviour. Evidence that calibration can improve Research suggests calibration is a learnable skill rather than a fixed personal trait. The Good Judgment Project, originally conducted as part of a large forecasting programme, found that forecasting performance improved through structured training, repeated practice, teamwork, aggregation of independent judgements and continual feedback. Participants repeatedly compared their stated probabilities with actual outcomes, allowing them to adjust their confidence over time. [Good Judgment] goodjudgment.com Good JudgmentThe Science Of SuperforecastingGood Judgment research discovered four keys to accurate forecasting: talent spotting, trainin... More recent experimental work has also found that interactive calibration training can reduce overconfidence. In one study involving repeated sports forecasting over an eleven week period, participants receiving structured calibration training improved the quality of their probabilistic judgements compared with their starting performance. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryCalibration training for improving probabilistic judgments...by R Gruetzemacher · 2024 · Cited by 4 — We describe an... The common feature across these studies is not simply making more forecasts, but receiving systematic feedback about whether confidence levels matched reality. Sensitivity and Specificity Explained Clearly (Biostatistics) Channel: MedCram Medical Lectures Explained CLEARLY Open on YouTube How to improve your own estimates with feedback Improving calibration requires creating a feedback loop between predictions and outcomes. A practical approach is: 1. Assign explicit probabilities rather than vague phrases such as \"probably\" or \"unlikely\". 2. Record predictions before outcomes are known. 3. Review groups of forecasts , not individual successes or failures. 4. Compare predicted probabilities with observed frequencies. 5. Adjust future confidence levels when persistent overconfidence or underconfidence appears. Many people also benefit from using a standard probability scale—for example 10%, 30%, 50%, 70% and 90%—instead of making"},{"title":"Test Results","url":"/test-results/","description":"Natural frequencies can make false positives and true positives easier to compare than percentages alone.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Probabilities","level":3,"text":"Test Results Why Positive Tests Can Mislead Test Results Test Results Why Positive Tests Can Mislead Natural frequencies can make false positives and true positives easier to compare than percentages alone. Natural frequencies can make false positives and true positives easier to compare than percentages alone. dataset evidence The test result problem in plain counts How false positives change the answer Why natural frequencies reduce confusion improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities test results Introduction Natural frequencies are one of the simplest tools for thinking clearly about medical test results. Instead of presenting information as percentages or conditional probabilities , they describe what happens in a fixed group of real people—for example, \"out of every 1,000 people tested\". This small change makes it much easier to judge what a positive result actually means, especially when the disease is uncommon. Rather than asking readers to mentally combine prevalence, sensitivity and false positive rates, natural frequencies let them compare true positives and false positives directly. This reduces common reasoning errors and improves decision making for patients, doctors and students alike. [MPG.PuRe] pure.mpg.de Pu Re Gigerenzer, G 2011). What are natural frequencies? DoctorsJanuary 16, 2024 — by G Gigerenzer · 2011 · Cited by 137 — As shown in the figure, natural f... Published: January 16, 2024 The Test Result Problem in Plain Counts A positive test result is often interpreted as meaning that a person almost certainly has the condition. In reality, that depends on how common the condition is and how often the test gives false alarms. Consider a screening test with the following characteristics: The disease affects 1% of the population. The test detects 90% of people who truly have the disease. The test incorrectly produces a positive result in 9% of healthy people. These figures can sound reassuring until they are translated into natural frequencies. [researchgate.net] researchgate.net What are natural frequencies? Request PDFNatural Frequencies Improve Public Understanding of Medical Test Results: An Experimental Study on Various Bayesian Inferenc... Imagine testing 1,000 people : 10 actually have the disease. Of those 10, 9 receive a positive result. 990 do not have the disease. Of those 990 healthy people, about 89 still receive a positive result. That means there are 98 positive tests altogether , but only 9 belong to people who genuinely have the disease. A person with a positive result therefore has roughly a 9 in 98 chance—about 9% —of actually having the condition. Around nine out of ten positive results are false positives in this example. [MPG.PuRe] pure.mpg.de Pu Re Gigerenzer, G 2011). What are natural frequencies? DoctorsJanuary 16, 2024 — by G Gigerenzer · 2011 · Cited by 137 — As shown in the figure, natural f... Published: January 16, 2024 The percentages and the natural frequency version describe exactly the same evidence. The difference is that the second version makes the comparison immediately visible. Sensitivity and Specificity Explained Clearly (Biostatistics) Channel: MedCram Medical Lectures Explained CLEARLY Open on YouTube How False Positives Change the Answer The surprising feature of many screening tests is not that they make mistakes , but that even a relatively low false positive rate can dominate the results when the disease itself is rare. Suppose a disease affects only 1 in 1,000 people. Even an excellent test may examine hundreds or thousands of healthy people for every person who truly has the condition. A small percentage of false alarms among this much larger healthy group can therefore outnumber the genuine positive results. This is why it is important to distinguish between two different questions: How good is the test at detecting disease? (Sensitivity) If my result is positive, what is the chance I actually have the disease? (Positive predictive value) These are not the same quantity. People—including experienced clinicians—often confuse them when information is presented only as conditional probabilities. Studies have repeatedly shown that physicians frequently overestimate the probability that a positive screening result indicates real disease when prevalence is low. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu health stats Department of StatisticsHelping Doctors and Patients Make Sense of Health Statisticsby G Gigerenzer · Cited by 1853 — The calculation usi... Natural frequencies expose this difference because they preserve the actual counts of people in each group instead of hiding them inside percentages. Why Natural Frequencies Reduce Confusion Natural frequencies help because they match the structure of the question people are trying to answer. Instead of asking someone to combine several percentages mentally, they simply ask: \"Among everyone who tested positive, how many actually have the disease?\" Once the information is arranged as counts, the answer becomes a straightforward comparison. Researchers have shown several reasons this works: The base rate (how common the disease is) remains visible rather than disappearing inside percentages. The relevant groups can be counted directly. The calculation no longer requires mentally reconstructing Bayes' theorem from separate conditional probabilities. Visual aids such as frequency trees or icon arrays become much easier to interpret. [MPG.PuRe+2PMC] pure.mpg.de Pu Re Gigerenzer, G 2011). What are natural frequencies? DoctorsJanuary 16, 2024 — by G Gigerenzer · 2011 · Cited by 137 — As shown in the figure, natural f... Published: January 16, 2024 The improvement is not merely theoretical. Experiments have found that both members of the public and health professionals answer diagnostic questions more accurately—and often more quickly—when identical information is expressed as natural frequencies instead of conditional probabilities. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Simple tools for understanding risks: from innumeracy to insightby G Gigerenzer · 2003 · Cited by 1095 — Presenting information in nat... Handling Uncertainty in Clinical Conversations: Communicating Risk While Building Trust EBHC Explain Channel: Everblink Healthcare Pvt Ltd Open on YouTube Evidence That the Format Matters One influential line of research asked doctors to interpret the result of a mammography screening test. When presented with probabilities, many substantially overestimated the chance that a woman with a positive result actually had breast cancer. In one study described by Gerd Gigerenzer, most of 160 practising gynaecologists estimated the probability at around 80–90%, when the correct answer for the example was roughly 9%. After brief instruction in converting the information into natural frequencies, the vast majority were able to calculate the correct positive predictive value. [MPG.PuRe] pure.mpg.de Pu Re Gigerenzer, G 2011). What are natural frequencies? DoctorsJanuary 16, 2024 — by G Gigerenzer · 2011 · Cited by 137 — As shown in the figure, natural f... Published: January 16, 2024 Similar findings have been reported across studies involving medical students, clinicians and members of the public. The benefit is robust enough that natural frequency representations have become a widely recommended tool in risk communication and medical education. [PMC+2BMJ Decision Trees] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Simple tools for understanding risks: from innumeracy to insightby G Gigerenzer · 2003 · Cited by 1095 — Presenting information in nat... More recent research also suggests that natural frequencies remain the best format for understanding single diagnostic test results, although additional approaches—such as likelihood ratios—may be useful in more complex situations involving multiple sequential tests. [Springer Nature Link] link.springer.com However, they may be less suitable in...Read more... gerd gigerenzer risk communication Channel: gocognitive Open on YouTube Practical Lessons When Reading a Positive Test Result Natural frequencies encourage a more careful interpretation of medical evidence. [researchgate.net] researchgate.net What are natural frequencies? Request PDFNatural Frequencies Improve Public Understanding of Medical Test Results: An Experimental Study on Various Bayesian Inferenc... When looking at any diagnostic test, it helps to ask questions such as: Out of 1,000 people like me, how many actually have the condition? How many true positives does the test produce? How many false positives occur? Among everyone who tests positive, how many genuinely have the disease? Many medical organisations now encourage presenting screening information in absolute numbers alongside percentages because patients generally understand these formats better and make more informed decisions. [BMJ Decision Trees] dtb.bmj.com more... Natural frequencies do not make a test more or less accurate. They make the evidence easier to interpret,"},{"title":"Trade Offs","url":"/trade-offs/","description":"Weighted criteria make conflicting values visible when no option wins on every important dimension.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Probabilities","level":3,"text":"Trade Offs What Matters Most When Values Clash? Trade Offs Trade Offs What Matters Most When Values Clash? Weighted criteria make conflicting values visible when no option wins on every important dimension. Weighted criteria make conflicting values visible when no option wins on every important dimension. policy intervention How to choose criteria that matter How weights change the preferred option How sensitivity checks reveal fragile choices improving your thinking and analytical skills probabilities trade offs Introduction When two or more options each satisfy different values, there is often no objectively “correct” answer. The challenge is not simply gathering more facts but deciding how much different considerations—such as cost, safety, fairness, speed, flexibility or environmental impact—should count. Weighted criteria provide a structured way to make those value judgements explicit rather than leaving them hidden or inconsistent. This approach is widely used in multi criteria decision analysis (MCDA), a family of methods designed for decisions involving competing objectives. Instead of pretending that every criterion matters equally, it asks decision makers to identify the factors that genuinely matter, assign each an appropriate importance, and examine how different options perform against those priorities. Modern government guidance, including the UK Green Book's supplementary guidance on MCDA, recommends this approach where options must be judged across several objectives that cannot easily be reduced to a single monetary measure. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK use of multi criteria decision analysis in options appraisal of economic cases Use of Multi Criteria Decision Analysis in options appraisal...16 May 2024 — MCDA is a technique that helps decision makers make rationa... Published: May 2024 Why weighting matters when values conflict Many difficult choices involve competing values rather than competing facts. For example: A transport project may maximise economic growth but reduce local green space. A software platform may offer greater flexibility but lower security. A job offer may increase income while reducing family time. A healthcare intervention may improve outcomes but cost substantially more. Simply listing pros and cons rarely resolves these conflicts because it leaves unanswered the crucial question: which considerations deserve greater influence? Weighted criteria force that conversation into the open. Instead of saying \"both cost and quality matter\", the decision process asks whether quality is slightly more important than cost, twice as important, or perhaps less important in this particular decision. That distinction often explains why reasonable people reach different conclusions even when they agree on the facts. MCDA is specifically intended for these situations involving multiple conflicting objectives and diverse stakeholder preferences. [GOV.UK+2analysisfunction.civilservice.gov.uk] GOV.UK use of multi criteria decision analysis in options appraisal of economic cases Use of Multi Criteria Decision Analysis in options appraisal...16 May 2024 — MCDA is a technique that helps decision makers make rationa... Published: May 2024 How to choose criteria that matter Good criteria describe the objectives of the decision, not simply convenient measurements. [youtube.com] youtube.com recursor to a lot of algorithms and scoring... Useful criteria generally share several characteristics: Relevant: Each criterion reflects something decision makers genuinely care about. Distinct: Criteria should avoid substantial overlap that would count the same benefit twice. Understandable: Everyone involved should interpret each criterion in the same way. Assessable: Options should be capable of being evaluated consistently against the criterion. For example, when selecting between office locations, criteria might include: CriterionWhy it mattersStaff travel timeProductivity and employee wellbeingAnnual operating costFinancial sustainabilityExpansion potentialFuture flexibilityEnvironmental performanceOrganisational sustainability goalsAccessibilityEqual access for employees and visitors Notice that these criteria express objectives rather than preferred options. \"Close to headquarters\" is not itself a criterion unless proximity serves a broader objective such as collaboration or reduced travel. A common mistake is creating too many criteria. Long lists often contain duplicated ideas or minor considerations that obscure the genuinely important trade offs. Government MCDA guidance recommends carefully structuring objectives before assigning weights because unclear criteria produce misleading results regardless of how sophisticated the scoring becomes. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK use of multi criteria decision analysis in options appraisal of economic cases Use of Multi Criteria Decision Analysis in options appraisal...16 May 2024 — MCDA is a technique that helps decision makers make rationa... Published: May 2024 Weighing Your Options: A Step by Step Guide to the Weighted Decision Matrix Channel: Decision Lab Open on YouTube How weights change the preferred option Weights express relative importance, not absolute importance. [people.cs.nott.ac.uk] people.cs.nott.ac.uk Criteria Decision Analysis: A Primerby PO Siebers — Assign weights for each of the criterion to reflect their relative importance to the... Imagine three criteria: CriterionWeightSafety50%Cost30%Flexibility20% Suppose Option A scores exceptionally well on safety but poorly on flexibility, while Option B performs moderately well across all three. If safety receives half of the total weight, Option A may clearly emerge as the preferred choice. However, if flexibility becomes much more important—for example because future uncertainty has increased—the weights might change to: Safety: 35% Cost: 25% Flexibility: 40% Without changing any factual information about the options, Option B could now become the better overall choice. Nothing \"went wrong\". The preferred option changed because the underlying values changed. This illustrates an important principle: weighted analysis does not discover hidden objective truth. Instead, it makes explicit how stated priorities produce particular recommendations. Assigning weights thoughtfully The exact numerical values matter less than the reasoning behind them. Several approaches are commonly used: Direct allocation: Distribute 100 points across the criteria according to importance. Pairwise comparison: Compare two criteria at a time, asking which matters more and by how much. Ranking followed by refinement: Order criteria first, then estimate the relative gaps between them. Stakeholder elicitation: Combine or compare weights from different participants when multiple perspectives must be represented. Pairwise comparison is often easier than assigning numbers immediately because people generally find relative judgements more natural than absolute percentages. Whatever approach is used, the discussion that produces the weights is often more valuable than the final numbers themselves. Disagreements about weights frequently reveal hidden assumptions , conflicting organisational priorities or different understandings of the decision's purpose. Research reviewing MCDA methods consistently identifies preference elicitation—the process of identifying and expressing stakeholder priorities—as a central stage rather than a technical afterthought. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov How to support the application of multiple criteria decision...by M Cinelli · 2020 · Cited by 518 — We introduce a review of the rese... Multiple Criteria Decision Analysis Data Analysis Quick Wins 1 Channel: Absent Data Open on YouTube How sensitivity checks reveal fragile choices A weighted decision should never end with a single calculated score. Instead, ask: What happens if one important weight increases by 10%? Does the preferred option remain the same? Which weight would need to change before another option becomes preferable? Are there criteria where small disagreements completely alter the ranking? These questions form the basis of sensitivity analysis . Sensitivity analysis tests whether a recommendation is robust or fragile. A robust decision continues to favour the same option even when reasonable changes are made to the weights. A fragile decision changes winner after only small adjustments. This does not invalidate the analysis, but it signals that the recommendation depends heavily on assumptions about priorities rather than overwhelming evidence. For example: If increasing the weight on cost from 25% to 28% changes the preferred option, the decision is highly sensitive. If the preferred option remains unchanged even when several weights move substantially, confidence in the recommendation increases. Modern MCDA guidance consistently recommends sensitivity analysis because weight estimates are never perfectly precise. Exploring plausible alternative weightings is therefore part"},{"title":"Problem Framing","url":"/problem-framing/","description":"Many weak conclusions begin with a poorly defined problem, so the first skill is knowing exactly what question you are answering.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Problem Framing Are You Solving the Right Problem? Problem Framing Problem Framing Are You Solving the Right Problem? Many weak conclusions begin with a poorly defined problem, so the first skill is knowing exactly what question you are answering. Many weak conclusions begin with a poorly defined problem, so the first skill is knowing exactly what question you are answering. mechanism Define the real question Separate symptoms from causes Choose the right problem type improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing Introduction Problem framing is the thinking step that happens before you start collecting evidence, building an argument or choosing a solution. It asks a simple but often neglected question: “What problem am I actually trying to solve?” That matters because a clever answer to the wrong question can still produce a bad decision. Research and practice in decision analysis, problem structuring, root cause work and organisational judgement all point to the same mechanism: the frame determines what evidence you look for, which causes you treat as relevant, which options you consider, and what counts as success. [PubsOnline+2Cambridge University Press & Assessment] pubsonline.informs.org What's the Problem? An Introduction to Problem Structuring MethodsNew problem structuring methods (PSMs) provide decision maker... The practical skill is not to find one perfect wording of the problem immediately. It is to pause long enough to test whether the first wording is too narrow, too solution shaped, too symptom focused or too vague. Good framing turns “we need to fix this” into a clearer judgement task: what is happening, who is affected, what outcome would count as improvement, what kind of problem is this, and what must be understood before reasoning can safely begin. Why the First Frame Quietly Controls the Whole Answer A problem frame is a boundary. It decides what belongs inside the analysis and what is treated as background noise. If the frame is “sales are down because marketing is weak”, the mind starts looking for marketing fixes. If the frame is “repeat customers are not returning”, the same situation may point to product quality, pricing, onboarding, support or mismatched expectations. The facts have not changed, but the search space has. This is why experienced problem solvers often treat framing as a separate stage rather than as a preamble. Problem structuring methods in operational research were developed partly because traditional analytical techniques are often strong once a problem is well defined, but much weaker when the real difficulty is deciding what the problem is in the first place. These methods are especially useful when there are multiple stakeholders, conflicting priorities, uncertainty and no obvious single metric of success. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org What's the Problem? An Introduction to Problem Structuring MethodsNew problem structuring methods (PSMs) provide decision maker... A poor frame usually fails in one of four ways: It smuggles in a solution. “How do we automate this?” assumes automation is the answer before the problem has been understood. It names a symptom as the problem. “The team is missing deadlines” may be visible, but the underlying issue could be unclear priorities, unrealistic estimates, dependency delays or decision bottlenecks. It is too broad to reason about. “How do we improve performance?” gives no clear boundary, evidence need or success criterion. It uses the wrong problem type. A complicated technical fault, a human coordination issue and a contested policy dilemma require different reasoning methods. The point is not to delay action indefinitely. It is to prevent premature certainty. In decision quality thinking, a “proper frame” is one of the basic requirements of a good decision because it shapes the alternatives, information and trade offs that follow. [Decision Professionals] decisionprofessionals.com Decision ProfessionalsDecision QualityThis framework proposed captures the quality of decisions, with the evidence that all decisions sha... Define the Real Question The fastest way to improve a problem frame is to turn a loose concern into a specific question. A concern says, “This is bad.” A useful question says, “What must be understood or decided next?” That shift matters because reasoning needs a target. A strong problem question usually contains five elements: 1. The current condition: what is happening now? 2. The desired condition: what would be better? 3. The gap: what is the difference between the two? 4. The affected people or system: who or what is experiencing the gap? 5. The decision need: what choice, explanation or judgement depends on the answer? For example, “Our meetings are unproductive” is a complaint. A better frame might be: “Why are project meetings failing to produce clear decisions, and what meeting format would reduce rework without slowing coordination?” This wording does not solve the problem, but it improves the reasoning path. It identifies the visible failure, the desired improvement, the likely decision area and the trade off: reducing rework without adding drag. This also protects against false clarity. In organisational decision making, Kahneman, Lovallo and Sibony argued that leaders can often detect bias in other people’s recommendations more easily than in their own thinking, which is why structured questions and checklists are useful before major decisions. Their checklist approach asks whether alternatives have been properly explored, whether the right information has been gathered and whether the numbers are grounded rather than merely persuasive. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org Harvard Business Review The Big Idea: Before You Make That Big Decision… Harvard Business Review The Big Idea: Before You Make That Big Decision… A practical framing test is to ask: “If I answered this question well, would it actually help me decide what to do?” If the answer is no, the question is probably not yet the real question. It may be too abstract, too emotional, too broad or too detached from the decision that has to be made. Separate Symptoms from Causes Many weak conclusions begin by treating the most visible problem as the deepest problem. Symptoms are what attract attention: delays, complaints, errors, churn, conflict, cost overruns, low marks, poor health indicators, falling engagement. Causes are the mechanisms that produce those symptoms. Framing improves when you keep the two separate long enough to avoid fixing the surface while leaving the generator untouched. Root cause techniques such as the Five Whys are popular because they force this pause. The method repeatedly asks why a problem occurred until the analysis moves from the visible event towards a more basic process, design or system condition. Public sector and healthcare improvement guides describe it as a simple way to uncover not only delivery glitches but also organisational or team process issues. [NHS England] england.nhs.uk England Five whys England Five whys Used well, this is not a ritual of literally asking “why” five times. It is a discipline of not stopping at the first plausible explanation. Consider a student who says, “I am bad at essays.” That frame is vague and identity based. A symptom cause separation might reveal several different problems: the student does not understand the question; the student understands the topic but cannot structure an argument; the student has evidence but weak paragraph control; the student starts too late and cannot revise; the student is trying to sound academic rather than answer directly. Each cause suggests a different remedy. More reading helps only one of them. A template helps another. Earlier drafting helps another. Without framing, “improve essays” becomes an unfocused effort; with framing, the student can reason about the actual bottleneck. There is a caution, though. Root cause language can create false confidence if people force a complex situation into a single cause. Some problems have interacting causes, feedback loops or contested interpretations. In those cases, “the root cause” may be less useful than “the most actionable cause we can test next”. Recent work on causal mechanism information suggests that even when people are shown causal pathways, they may still prefer interventions that fix visible symptoms rather than indirect interventions aimed at deeper causes. That makes symptom cause separation a behavioural challenge, not just an information problem. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. What is Problem Framing? The Key to Strategic Decisions Channel: Design Sprint Academy Open on YouTube Choose the Right Problem Type Not every problem should be framed in the same way. A major source of bad reasoning is using the method that suits one problem type on a different kind of problem. Before reasoning, ask what sort of difficulty you are facing. A useful distinction is between clear"},{"title":"Decision Checks","url":"/decision-checks/","description":"Structured checks can expose missing alternatives, weak evidence, and overconfident numbers before a major decision.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Problem Framing","level":3,"text":"Decision Checks A Checklist Before the Big Decision Decision Checks Decision Checks A Checklist Before the Big Decision Structured checks can expose missing alternatives, weak evidence, and overconfident numbers before a major decision. Structured checks can expose missing alternatives, weak evidence, and overconfident numbers before a major decision. policy intervention Why leaders need pre decision checks Testing alternatives and evidence quality Using checklists without turning them into bureaucracy improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing decision checks Introduction Before a major decision gains momentum, the most valuable question is often not “What should we do?” but “Are we solving the right problem in the right way?” Decision quality checklists exist to test the problem frame before persuasive arguments, political commitments, or detailed analysis make the original framing difficult to challenge. Rather than predicting the correct answer, these checklists help decision makers detect missing alternatives, weak evidence , hidden assumptions , and unjustified confidence. Research on decision quality consistently identifies framing, alternatives, information quality, and reasoning quality as core determinants of good decisions, regardless of the eventual outcome. [Decision Professionals+2Rockflow] decisionprofessionals.com Decision ProfessionalsDecision QualityThis framework proposed captures the quality of decisions, with the evidence that all decisions sha... A useful checklist is therefore not a bureaucratic form. It is a structured attempt to answer a simple question: “If this frame is wrong, how would we know before committing resources?” Why Leaders Need Pre Decision Checks Many poor decisions begin with a frame that feels obvious. Once a team agrees that a problem is caused by one factor, evidence gathering often becomes a search for confirmation rather than a search for understanding. Studies of cognitive bias show that professionals remain vulnerable to overconfidence , confirmation bias, anchoring, and related judgement errors even when they possess expertise and experience. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Impact of Cognitive Biases on Professionals' Decision...by V Berthet · 2022 · Cited by 305 — First, the literature reviewed shows... This is why decision quality practitioners distinguish between decision quality and outcome quality. A good outcome can result from a flawed process, just as a sound process can sometimes produce a disappointing outcome because of uncertainty. The quality check occurs before the outcome is known. [PMC Training] pmctraining.com PMC Training The Decision Making Mistake That Costs Teams the Most Decision quality is an assessment of the reasoning process at the moment a decision was made.Read more... A pre decision checklist is especially valuable when: The decision is expensive or difficult to reverse. Stakeholders strongly favour one explanation. Forecasts contain substantial uncertainty. Time pressure encourages premature agreement. The proposed solution emerged before the problem was carefully defined. In these situations, the checklist acts as a forcing mechanism that slows commitment long enough to examine whether the frame itself deserves confidence. A Checklist Before the Big Decision The following checklist focuses specifically on testing the problem frame, not on evaluating detailed solutions. 1. Have We Defined the Purpose Clearly? A frame should state what success looks like rather than merely describe what is currently frustrating. Questions to ask: What decision are we actually trying to make? What outcome are we trying to improve? How will we know the problem has been solved? Are we describing a symptom or an underlying issue? A useful warning sign is solution language embedded in the problem statement. For example, “How do we automate this process?” already assumes automation is the answer. A stronger frame asks what performance gap exists and what outcomes matter. An Introduction to Decision Quality in Under Five Minutes Strategic Decisions Group Channel: Strategic Decisions Group Open on YouTube 2. Have We Considered Genuine Alternatives? Decision quality frameworks consistently emphasise the importance of creative, workable alternatives because a decision with only one apparent option is often not a real decision. [Decision Professionals+2Sokkeldirektoratet] decisionprofessionals.com Decision ProfessionalsDecision QualityThis framework proposed captures the quality of decisions, with the evidence that all decisions sha... Questions to ask: What are at least three fundamentally different ways to view this problem? If our preferred explanation were false, what else could explain the situation? What option would a sceptical outsider propose? What happens if we do nothing? The objective is not to generate endless possibilities. It is to prevent the first framing from monopolising attention. 3. What Evidence Would Change Our Minds? Weak frames often survive because teams collect supporting evidence but never identify disconfirming evidence. Questions to ask: What facts would seriously challenge our current framing? Have we actively searched for contradictory evidence? Are we relying on anecdotes when broader data exist? Which assumptions are supported by evidence and which are merely plausible? This check helps counter confirmation bias and congruence bias, both of which encourage people to test whether their preferred explanation is correct rather than whether competing explanations might fit better. [PSA] professionalstandards.org.uk As is.Read more PSAAdvice on biases in fitness to practise decision making...June 10, 2021 — Congruence bias – tied to confirmation bias this is where t... Published: June 10, 2021 4. Are We Confusing Precision with Certainty? Decision makers often become more confident than their evidence justifies. Research identifies overconfidence as one of the most common and persistent biases in professional judgement. [PMC+2Economic and Business Review] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Impact of Cognitive Biases on Professionals' Decision...by V Berthet · 2022 · Cited by 305 — First, the literature reviewed shows... Questions to ask: Which numbers are estimates rather than measurements? What confidence range accompanies our forecasts? Are we using precise figures that imply more certainty than exists? What assumptions would have to remain true for our prediction to hold? A forecast stated as “12.7%” may appear rigorous while resting on highly uncertain assumptions. The checklist should force discussion of uncertainty, not merely calculations. 5. Have We Examined the Frame from Other Perspectives? Problem frames determine what gets included and excluded from consideration. Decision education research describes a helpful frame as one that clarifies purpose, scope, and perspective. [decisioneducation.org] decisioneducation.org Helpful FrameA decision frame has three components: (1) Purpose—what we hope to accomplish by this decision; (2) Scope—what to include an... Questions to ask: Who experiences this problem differently? Which stakeholders would describe the issue in another way? What information sits outside our current scope but may matter? What would the problem look like from the customer's, citizen's, employee's, or competitor's perspective? A frame that survives multiple viewpoints is generally stronger than one that depends on a single perspective. Made a good decision with a bad outcome? No worries! Decision quality vs. Outcome quality Channel: Insight Connect Open on YouTube Testing Alternatives and Evidence Quality The strongest framing checks combine alternative generation with evidence review rather than treating them as separate activities. One practical technique is to create competing problem statements and compare the evidence supporting each. For example: Customer dissatisfaction is caused by slow service. Customer dissatisfaction is caused by unclear expectations. Customer dissatisfaction is caused by inconsistent quality. The team then asks which evidence supports each frame and what evidence contradicts it. This prevents the discussion from becoming a contest between advocates of one preferred explanation. Another useful approach is the pre mortem. Developed by cognitive psychologist Gary Klein, a pre mortem asks participants to imagine that a proposed plan has already failed and then identify the reasons why. By assuming failure rather than merely discussing possible risks, teams often surface concerns that would otherwise remain unspoken. Research and practitioner accounts suggest that this “prospective hindsight” approach improves the identification of weaknesses and hidden assumptions. [Psychology Today+3Harvard Business Review+3garyklein] hbr.org performing a project premortem Harvard Business ReviewPerforming a Project PremortemReprint: F0709A In a premortem, team members assume that the project they are"},{"title":"Framing Test","url":"/framing-test/","description":"A better question makes evidence gathering sharper, faster, and less biased.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Problem Framing","level":3,"text":"Framing Test Five Questions Before You Chase Evidence Framing Test Framing Test Five Questions Before You Chase Evidence A better question makes evidence gathering sharper, faster, and less biased. A better question makes evidence gathering sharper, faster, and less biased. policy intervention Current condition and desired condition The gap, affected people, and decision need How to tell if the question is still too vague improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing framing test Introduction Before collecting evidence, it is worth spending a few minutes testing whether the question itself is worth answering. Many reasoning failures begin not with bad evidence, but with a vague, incomplete or misleading problem statement. Research on decision framing and problem structuring consistently shows that the way a problem is defined influences what information people seek, which options they consider and what they ultimately decide. A better question makes evidence gathering faster, more relevant and less vulnerable to confirmation bias. [The Uncertainty Project+2PubsOnline] theuncertaintyproject.org The Uncertainty ProjectDecision FramingEffectively frame a decision in a clear, concise, and objective way to set up the process for prod... A practical way to improve framing is to run a simple five question test before collecting any evidence. The goal is not to find the perfect wording. It is to transform a loose concern into a decision ready question that can guide useful investigation. Current Condition and Desired Condition The first two questions establish whether there is a meaningful gap to investigate. Question 1: What is happening now? Describe the current condition in observable terms. Avoid explanations, blame or proposed solutions. Focus on what can actually be seen. Weak framing: Why is our software platform failing? Stronger framing: Customer support requests have increased by 35% in three months and renewal rates have fallen. The stronger version separates observation from interpretation. Evidence collection can now begin from a clear starting point rather than an assumption. Question 2: What would a better condition look like? Reasoning improves when the destination is explicit. A desired condition creates a reference point for judging evidence and evaluating options. Weak framing: How do we improve customer experience? Stronger framing: What changes would reduce support requests while maintaining renewal rates above the previous year's level? Decision framing research emphasises the importance of clarifying desired outcomes and decision criteria before evaluating alternatives. Without a defined target, evidence gathering often becomes unfocused and endless. [The Uncertainty Project+2decisioneducation.org] theuncertaintyproject.org The Uncertainty ProjectDecision FramingEffectively frame a decision in a clear, concise, and objective way to set up the process for prod... Day 3: Business Questions & Problem Framing Data Analytics for Beginners Channel: EDUSHARK TRAINING Open on YouTube The Gap, Affected People, and Decision Need Once the current and desired conditions are clear, three further questions determine whether the issue is actionable. Question 3: What gap actually needs explanation or action? The gap is the difference between the current condition and the desired condition. Examples: Sales are lower than target. Waiting times exceed service standards. Project delivery is slower than required. This question prevents a common mistake: collecting information about everything instead of investigating the specific discrepancy that matters. Question 4: Who or what is affected by the gap? Many poorly framed questions ignore stakeholders or affected systems. Compare: Why are meetings inefficient? with: Why are project teams failing to reach decisions in meetings, causing rework and delays for engineering and operations staff? The second version immediately sharpens evidence gathering. Different stakeholders may experience different aspects of the same problem. Problem structuring approaches were developed partly because complex situations often involve multiple affected groups with different perspectives and priorities. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org What's the Problem? An Introduction to Problem Structuring...New problem structuring methods (PSMs) provide decision makers wi... Question 5: What decision depends on the answer? [decisionframeworks.com] decisionframeworks.com what is decision framing ?27 Jul 2024 — Framing is about the choices we make in what we say, how we say it, what we emphasize, and what we leave unsaid, and how t... This is the most important test. If no decision depends on the answer, the investigation may not be worth conducting. Examples: Which staffing model should we adopt? Should we redesign the onboarding process? Should we continue, expand or stop a programme? A decision focused question prevents evidence gathering from turning into information accumulation. Decision quality frameworks treat a useful frame as one of the foundations of good judgement because it determines what information is relevant in the first place. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Decision quality Decision quality The Five Questions Working Together The test can be applied quickly to almost any issue. Consider the complaint: \"Employee morale is low.\" Running the framing test produces: 1. What is happening now? Employee engagement survey scores have fallen for two consecutive quarters. 2. What would better look like? Engagement scores return to the previous year's level. 3. What gap matters? The decline in engagement over the last six months. 4. Who is affected? Employees, team leaders and retention outcomes. 5. What decision depends on the answer? Whether management should change workload allocation, communication practices or career development policies. Notice what has happened. The problem has not been solved, but it has become specific enough to guide evidence collection. The search for information now has boundaries and purpose. How to Tell If the Question Is Still Too Vague Even after using the five question test, some problem statements remain weak. Several warning signs indicate that further framing work is needed. The question already contains the solution Questions such as: How do we automate this process? How do we hire more staff? How do we introduce artificial intelligence? assume the answer before evidence has been collected. A better frame focuses on the underlying gap: What is causing processing delays, and which intervention would reduce them most effectively? This distinction matters because framing can subtly bias the options people consider. Studies of framing effects show that presentation and wording can influence judgement even when underlying facts remain unchanged. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision LabFraming effectThe Framing effect is the principle that our choices are influenced by the way they are framed through diff... The Art of Decision Making: Unpacking the Uncertainty Project with Kyle Byrd Channel: Barry O'Reilly Open on YouTube The scope is too broad Questions such as: How do we improve education? How do we improve company performance? have no practical boundary. Evidence gathering becomes inefficient because almost any fact might seem relevant. A stronger version narrows the decision: Which interventions would improve reading attainment among Year 7 pupils in schools with declining literacy scores? Success cannot be recognised If nobody can explain what improvement would look like, evidence cannot be evaluated properly. Questions that lack measurable or observable outcomes often produce endless debate because participants are judging different goals without realising it. Different people are answering different questions One of the strongest indicators of poor framing is disagreement about what problem is being discussed. For example: Finance sees a cost problem. Operations sees a process problem. Customers experience a service problem. Problem structuring research emerged precisely because groups frequently fail to agree on the nature of the problem before attempting analysis. Clarifying the frame often resolves confusion that additional evidence alone cannot fix. [PubsOnline] pubsonline.informs.org What's the Problem? An Introduction to Problem Structuring...New problem structuring methods (PSMs) provide decision makers wi... How to Make Better Decisions (Problem Framing) Channel: Design Sprint Academy Open on YouTube Why This Test Improves Evidence Collection The five question framing test works because it changes the role of evidence. Without framing, people often collect information that supports existing beliefs or preferred solutions. With framing, evidence serves a specific decision need. Instead of asking: What information can I find? the thinker asks: What information would help determine the size of the gap, identify affected parties and support the"},{"title":"Problem Types","url":"/problem-types/","description":"Clear, complicated, complex, and wicked problems fail when treated as the same kind of task.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Problem Framing","level":3,"text":"Problem Types What Kind of Problem Are You Solving? Problem Types Problem Types What Kind of Problem Are You Solving? Clear, complicated, complex, and wicked problems fail when treated as the same kind of task. Clear, complicated, complex, and wicked problems fail when treated as the same kind of task. population context Clear and complicated problems Complex problems and small experiments Wicked problems and contested success improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing problem types Introduction One of the fastest ways to improve your thinking is to stop assuming that every problem can be solved in the same way. A broken machine, a software architecture decision, a cultural change programme, and a national housing shortage may all be called “problems”, but they differ fundamentally in how causes work, how evidence accumulates, and what success looks like. When people apply the wrong reasoning style to the wrong problem type, they often become trapped in cycles of overconfidence , endless analysis, or ineffective action. Research on decision making, complexity, and policy design repeatedly shows that effective reasoning begins with recognising what kind of problem you are facing. [The Cynefin Co] thecynefin.co The Cynefin Co About The Cynefin CoAbout Cynefin FrameworkThe Cynefin Framework was developed to help leaders understand their challenges and to make decisi... A useful distinction separates problems into clear, complicated, complex, and wicked forms. Each requires a different relationship between analysis, expertise, experimentation, and judgement. The key skill is not choosing the smartest reasoning method in general, but choosing the reasoning method that fits the problem. The Cynefin Framework Channel: CognitiveEdge Open on YouTube Clear and Complicated Problems Clear and complicated problems both have underlying cause and effect relationships, but they differ in how difficult those relationships are to understand. When best practice is enough Clear problems are situations where the correct response is already known. The cause and effect relationship is stable, repeatable, and widely understood. A missing file, a standard payroll calculation, or a routine safety checklist usually falls into this category. In these cases, the most effective reasoning approach is often not deep analysis but reliable execution. Checklists , standard operating procedures, and established best practices outperform creativity because the challenge is consistency rather than discovery. Frameworks such as Cynefin describe this as a domain where people should identify the situation, categorise it correctly, and apply proven practice . [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. The common mistake is overcomplicating a clear problem. Organisations sometimes hold lengthy meetings to debate issues that already have known solutions. Wicked Problems in Urban Planning Rittel & Webber’s Groundbreaking Theory Explained Channel: Sustainable Solutions Open on YouTube When expertise and analysis matter Complicated problems are different. The answer exists, but finding it requires investigation, specialist knowledge, or careful analysis. Examples include: Designing a bridge. Diagnosing a rare engineering fault. Planning a spacecraft mission. Determining the cause of a manufacturing defect. In these situations, evidence gathering and expert judgement become valuable. Multiple valid solutions may exist, but rigorous analysis can identify better and worse options. The relationship between cause and effect is discoverable even if it is not immediately obvious. Cynefin characterises this domain as one where leaders should analyse, consult experts, and compare alternatives before acting. [TXM Lean Solutions+2Open Practice Library] txm.com Lean Solutions Cynefin Framework Explained TXM Lean SolutionsCynefin Framework Explained The 5 Domains with...Approach for Complicated Problems: · Assess the situation and Sense... A useful historical comparison illustrates the distinction. A routine aircraft maintenance procedure is largely a clear problem. Designing a new aircraft engine is a complicated problem. Both are technical, but one depends primarily on following established knowledge while the other depends on generating and applying expert knowledge. The failure mode here is assuming that more expertise automatically solves every problem. That assumption works reasonably well in complicated domains but often breaks down elsewhere. Complex Problems and Small Experiments Complex problems look similar to complicated ones at first, yet they behave differently in an important way: the future cannot be fully predicted from existing knowledge. In complex systems, many interacting elements influence one another. Human behaviour, organisational culture, markets, ecosystems, and social networks frequently display these characteristics. Cause and effect may only become visible after events unfold. [Wikipedia+2castintelligence.com] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Consider launching a new product. A technical team may analyse customer preferences, pricing, and competitors, but no amount of modelling can perfectly predict how consumers will react. Unexpected feedback loops, trends, and social influences can reshape outcomes . Because prediction is limited, the reasoning method changes. Rather than asking, “What is the optimal solution?”, the more useful question becomes, “What can we learn through safe to fail experiments?” Researchers and practitioners working with complexity frameworks often recommend a pattern of: Cynefin explained: What is the Cynefin Framework? Channel: Online PM Courses Mike Clayton Open on YouTube 1. Run small probes or experiments. 2. Observe what emerges. 3. Adapt based on feedback. 4. Expand successful approaches. 5. Stop unsuccessful ones quickly. Wageningen University & Research eDepot+3SI Labs+3Open Practice Library This differs sharply from the reasoning used for complicated problems. If an engineer is calculating the load bearing capacity of a bridge, experimentation is usually secondary to analysis. If a company is trying to change workplace culture, experimentation is often primary because the system itself changes in response to intervention. One sign that a problem is complex is that experts disagree despite having access to the same information. Another is that successful solutions in one context fail when copied elsewhere. In these environments, learning matters more than certainty. Wicked Problems and Contested Success Wicked problems occupy an even more challenging category. The concept emerged from the work of Horst Rittel and Melvin Webber, who argued that many social and policy challenges differ fundamentally from technical problems. sympoetic.net+2Springer The defining feature of a wicked problem is not merely complexity. It is disagreement about what the problem actually is and what success should mean. Examples often include: Housing affordability. Climate policy. Healthcare reform. Educational inequality. Urban development. In these cases, stakeholders frequently disagree on goals, causes, trade offs , and acceptable outcomes. A proposed solution may improve one measure while worsening another. There is rarely a definitive stopping point at which everyone agrees the problem has been solved. Rittel and Webber argued that solutions to wicked problems are often judged as better or worse rather than true or false. sympoetic.net+2Wikipedia The British housing shortage provides a useful illustration. Different groups attribute the problem to planning rules, construction costs, infrastructure constraints, skills shortages, financing conditions, environmental regulation, population growth, or political choices. Each explanation implies a different solution. Because stakeholders value outcomes differently, even defining success becomes contested. The Times Reasoning about wicked problems therefore requires methods that go beyond technical analysis: Stakeholder mapping. Deliberation and negotiation. Explicit discussion of values. Scenario exploration. Iterative policy adjustment. Recognition of trade offs rather than optimisation. The failure mode is treating a wicked problem as if it were merely a difficult engineering challenge. More data and better models can help, but they cannot eliminate disagreements about values, priorities, or acceptable risks. ResearchGate+2Sage Journals Why Misclassification Creates Bad Decisions Many reasoning failures occur because people correctly analyse the wrong type of problem. Several recurring mistakes appear across organisations and public life: Using procedures for complex situations. Leaders impose rigid rules on evolving human systems and become surprised when behaviour changes. Using expert analysis for wicked problems. Technical reports answer factual questions while political and value conflicts remain unresolved. Using experimentation where reliability is required."},{"title":"Hidden Assumptions","url":"/hidden-assumptions/","description":"A problem statement can quietly choose the answer before the reasoning has begun.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Problem Framing","level":3,"text":"Hidden Assumptions When the Question Already Contains the Answer Hidden Assumptions Hidden Assumptions When the Question Already Contains the Answer A problem statement can quietly choose the answer before the reasoning has begun. A problem statement can quietly choose the answer before the reasoning has begun. mechanism How solution shaped wording narrows the search Common signs of a premature frame How to rewrite the question before deciding improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing hidden assumptions Introduction A solution shaped problem statement is a question that quietly decides the answer before the reasoning has begun. Instead of asking what is causing a problem or what outcome is desired, it asks how to implement a particular remedy. The result is that alternatives disappear from consideration, evidence is filtered through a narrow lens, and hidden assumptions become difficult to notice. In practical terms, “How do we automate this process?” and “What is causing delays in this process?” may lead to completely different investigations, even when they refer to the same situation. Research on decision framing, problem structuring and decision quality consistently shows that the way a problem is framed influences which options are considered, what information is collected, and what counts as a successful outcome. [Decision Professionals+2Structured Decision Making] decisionprofessionals.com Decision ProfessionalsDecision QualityThis framework proposed captures the quality of decisions, with the evidence that all decisions sha... This matters because people often spend most of their effort improving answers while leaving the question itself unexamined. A well argued solution can still fail if it solves the wrong problem. How Solution Shaped Wording Narrows the Search The mechanism is simple: the wording of the problem defines the search space. Consider these two questions: “How do we get employees back into the office?” “What working arrangement best supports productivity, coordination and retention?” The first assumes that returning to the office is the objective. The second treats office attendance as one possible option among several. The difference is not semantic. It changes what evidence becomes relevant, which stakeholders are consulted, and which alternatives are allowed into the discussion. Decision researchers describe framing as the process that determines how a decision is scoped and approached. Once a frame is adopted, people tend to search within it rather than question it. [Structured Decision Making] structureddecisionmaking.org framing the decision Structured Decision MakingFraming the decision15 Aug 2023 — A frame is a perspective on a decision that defines how that decision is scop... A useful way to think about solution shaped questions is that they contain an unspoken sentence: “How do we automate this?” really means “Automation is the right answer; help us implement it.” “How do we train staff to comply?” implies “Staff behaviour is the main problem.” “How do we cut costs?” implies “Cost reduction should take priority over other goals.” The hidden claim often receives less scrutiny than the proposed solution itself. This is one reason why operational research developed problem structuring methods. These approaches were created for situations where disagreement exists not only about the answer but about what the problem actually is. Rather than assuming a single correct formulation, they encourage exploration of competing representations of the situation. [theorsociety.com+2ResearchGate] theorsociety.com wicked problems faced by the managers of organisations.Read more... Hidden Assumptions That Travel Inside the Question A solution shaped problem statement usually carries several assumptions at once. Assumptions about causes Many questions assume a cause before evidence supports it. For example: “How do we motivate employees to work harder?” “How do we stop customers abandoning their baskets?” The first assumes motivation is the main constraint. The second assumes abandonment is a behavioural problem rather than a pricing issue, a usability issue, or a deliberate comparison shopping behaviour. When the assumed cause is wrong, reasoning becomes an exercise in elaborating an error. Problem Framing // How to prioritise the target audience and define the problem statement Channel: Design Sprint Academy Open on YouTube Assumptions about goals A question can also smuggle in priorities. “How do we maximise efficiency?” assumes efficiency is the dominant objective. Yet a situation may require balancing efficiency against resilience, safety, quality or employee wellbeing. Decision quality frameworks emphasise that framing should make objectives explicit rather than treating them as unquestioned givens. [Decision Professionals+2Decision Frameworks] decisionprofessionals.com Decision ProfessionalsDecision QualityThis framework proposed captures the quality of decisions, with the evidence that all decisions sha... Assumptions about available options Some questions exclude possibilities before they are evaluated. “Which replacement car should I buy?” assumes buying another car is necessary. A broader frame might include public transport, cycling, car sharing or changing travel patterns. Structured decision making guidance frequently uses examples like this to show how an initial frame can prematurely restrict alternatives. [Structured Decision Making] structureddecisionmaking.org framing the decision Structured Decision MakingFraming the decision15 Aug 2023 — A frame is a perspective on a decision that defines how that decision is scop... Assumptions about who matters Questions often privilege one stakeholder perspective. “How do we reduce support costs?” frames the issue from the organisation's viewpoint. “How do we reduce support costs while maintaining customer satisfaction?” recognises that more than one perspective is relevant. The narrower the stakeholder set, the greater the risk that important consequences remain invisible. Common Signs of a Premature Frame Several warning signs suggest that a problem statement may already contain an answer. A preferred solution appears in the question. If a proposed technology, policy, process or intervention is named before the problem has been analysed, the frame may be solution shaped. Only one class of answers seems possible. A useful problem statement should allow multiple plausible explanations or interventions. If every reasonable answer points in the same direction, the frame may be too restrictive. The question begins with implementation rather than understanding. “How do we roll this out?” and “How do we convince people?” often appear before the underlying need has been tested. Alternative explanations sound irrelevant. When people react to competing explanations with “that's not what we're discussing”, the frame may already have excluded possibilities that deserve attention. Disagreement focuses on tactics rather than diagnosis. Teams frequently debate which version of a solution to adopt while never examining whether the solution category itself is appropriate. Problem framing practitioners often describe this pattern as jumping from symptoms to remedies without spending enough time clarifying the actual decision or problem definition. [Lean Enterprise Institute+2Atlassian] lean.org Enterprise Institute Problem Framing at the Fuzzy Front End of Lean Product Lean Enterprise InstituteProblem Framing at the Fuzzy Front End of Lean Product...August 20, 2014 — 20 Aug 2014 — Lean emphasizes proble... Published: August 20, 2014 Reframing: How to Solve the Right Problems? Thomas Wedell Wedellsborg Channel: Brightline Open on YouTube Why Smart People Miss the Assumptions The danger is not confined to inexperienced thinkers. Hidden assumptions are difficult to detect precisely because they are embedded in language that feels reasonable. Several factors contribute: Familiar solutions become mentally available first. Organisational incentives reward action more than reframing. Previous successes encourage reuse of old remedies. Teams often inherit problem definitions from earlier discussions. Once a frame becomes accepted, questioning it can appear obstructive. Yet studies of framing effects show that different presentations of the same decision can produce different preferences and choices, even when the underlying facts remain unchanged. Presenting alternatives side by side tends to reduce this effect because it exposes options that a narrow frame might hide. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Framing Options as Choice or Opportunity14 Apr 2014 — A \"choice\" frame, where all treatment options are explicit, is less likely to... The challenge is not that people are irrational. It is that the frame determines what seems worth considering. How to Rewrite the Question Before Deciding The goal is not to eliminate assumptions entirely. Every problem statement"},{"title":"Symptoms","url":"/symptoms/","description":"Visible trouble is often only the signal, not the thing that needs fixing.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Problem Framing","level":3,"text":"Symptoms Are You Fixing the Signal or the Source? Symptoms Symptoms Are You Fixing the Signal or the Source? Visible trouble is often only the signal, not the thing that needs fixing. Visible trouble is often only the signal, not the thing that needs fixing. mechanism Why symptoms attract premature fixes How cause search changes the remedy When several causes matter at once improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing symptoms Introduction When something goes wrong, the visible problem is usually the first thing we notice. A late project, a recurring argument, a missed bill payment, or a constantly cluttered kitchen all present themselves as obvious failures. Yet in many cases the visible failure is a symptom rather than the cause. The symptom is what appears on the surface; the cause is the mechanism that keeps producing it. This distinction matters because remedies aimed at symptoms often provide temporary relief while leaving the underlying process unchanged. Effective problem framing therefore begins with a simple question: are you fixing the signal that something is wrong, or the source that keeps generating the signal? Research and practice in root cause analysis consistently emphasise that lasting improvement comes from understanding underlying causes rather than repeatedly treating recurring effects. [Tableau+2SafetyCulture] tableau.com Root cause analysis: Definition, examples and a how ...Root cause analysis (RCA) is the process of discovering the root causes of... Are You Looking at a Symptom or a Cause? A symptom is an observable sign that a problem exists. A cause is a factor that contributes to producing that symptom. The two are connected, but they are not the same thing. Consider these everyday examples: Symptom: You are frequently late for work. Possible causes: poor sleep, unrealistic morning routines, unreliable transport, or leaving too little margin for delays. Symptom: Your inbox is constantly overflowing. Possible causes: unclear priorities, excessive subscriptions, poor filtering systems, or avoidance of difficult messages. Symptom: Household bills are paid late. Possible causes: fragmented record keeping, lack of reminders, cash flow problems, or administrative overload. In each case, the symptom is real. It needs attention. But treating only the symptom often means the same problem returns. Root cause practitioners frequently describe symptoms as indicators rather than explanations. The symptom tells you where to investigate, not necessarily what to fix. [ATD] td.org ATDFind the Root Cause, Not Just the SymptomDefinitions of Symptoms and Causes · Content. A symptom is a sign or indication of a root cau... Why Symptoms Attract Premature Fixes Symptoms are attractive because they are visible, immediate and emotionally satisfying to address. When a room is messy, cleaning it feels productive. When deadlines are missed, demanding faster work feels decisive. When customers complain, issuing refunds feels responsive. The trouble is that these actions may remove evidence of the problem without changing the process that generated it. Several psychological tendencies push people towards symptom focused solutions: Visibility bias: We naturally focus on what we can see directly. Urgency pressure : Immediate problems demand attention now, while causes often require investigation. Desire for closure: People prefer a quick answer to an uncertain search. Action bias: Doing something feels better than pausing to diagnose. This tendency appears in organisations as well as personal life. Research discussed by Harvard Business School notes that many leaders believe organisations struggle more with diagnosing problems correctly than with implementing solutions once a problem is understood. [Harvard Business School] online.hbs.edu root cause analysis Harvard Business SchoolRoot Cause Analysis: What It Is & How to Perform One3 Dec 2024 — According to research described in the Harvard Bu... The result is a familiar cycle: 1. A symptom appears. 2. A quick fix removes the symptom temporarily. 3. The underlying mechanism remains. 4. The symptom returns. 5. More effort is spent fighting the recurrence. Repeated firefighting often signals that diagnosis has not yet reached the cause. What is 5 Whys (Root Cause Analysis)? From A Business Professor Channel: Business School 101 Open on YouTube How Cause Search Changes the Remedy Finding a cause changes not only what you do but also where you intervene. Imagine a student who repeatedly submits assignments late. A symptom focused remedy might be: Send reminders. Increase monitoring. Add penalties. A cause focused investigation might reveal: The student misunderstands assignment requirements. Instructions arrive through multiple channels. Planning skills are weak. Work begins too late because tasks feel overwhelming. Once the mechanism becomes clearer, the remedy changes. Instead of increasing pressure, the solution may involve simplifying instructions, improving planning routines, or breaking work into smaller stages. The key shift is from asking: \"How do I stop this problem?\" to: [ \"What keeps producing this problem?\"] tulip.co What are the Five Whys? A Tool For Root Cause Analysis TulipLearn about the Five Whys and how manufacturers use this problem solving me... Root cause methods such as the \"Five Whys\" were developed specifically to encourage movement from visible effects towards underlying causal chains . The exact number of questions is less important than the habit of tracing cause and effect relationships beyond the first explanation. [ResearchGate+2Tulip] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The Five Whys TechniqueThe Five Whys technique is a simple but powerful way to troubleshoot problems by exploring cause... Following the Chain Without Stopping Too Early A common mistake is stopping the investigation at the first plausible explanation. Suppose someone frequently forgets appointments. The reasoning might proceed as follows: Why was the appointment missed? Because it was forgotten. That answer merely restates the symptom. Continuing: Why was it forgotten? Because it was not entered into a calendar. Continuing further: Why was it not entered? Because appointments are recorded in several different places. Continuing: Why are they recorded in several places? Because there is no single trusted system. Now the investigation points towards a more actionable mechanism. A reliable calendar process may solve many future misses, not just the latest one. This illustrates an important principle: useful causes are not merely earlier events. They are factors that can realistically be changed to reduce recurrence. [Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services] cms.gov Centers for Medicare & Medicaid ServicesFive Whys Tool for Root Cause AnalysisOverview: Root cause analysis is a structured team process... BEFORE You Do A 5 WHYs Root Cause Analysis Watch This… Channel: Stefano The Agile Business Analyst Open on YouTube When Several Causes Matter at Once Real life problems rarely have one perfect root cause. A missed deadline may result from: Unrealistic planning. Frequent interruptions. Unclear requirements. Delayed decisions from others. Looking for a single cause can therefore become misleading. Experts in problem framing and diagnosis increasingly emphasise that complex problems often emerge from interacting factors rather than one hidden culprit. Reframing exercises and systems oriented approaches are valuable precisely because they reveal multiple contributing mechanisms operating together. [Harvard Business Review+2Harvard Business Review] hbr.org do you understand the problem youre trying to solve Harvard Business ReviewDo You Understand the Problem You're Trying to Solve?3 Apr 2024 — In this episode, you'll learn how to reframe tou... In everyday diagnosis, it is often more useful to ask: \"Which causes contribute most to this outcome?\" than: \"What is the one true cause?\" A recurring household conflict, for example, may arise from stress , poor communication, conflicting expectations and lack of time. Removing any one factor may help, even if it does not eliminate the problem entirely. Distinguishing Causes From Blame People often avoid searching for causes because they fear it will become a search for someone to blame. Cause analysis and blame assignment are different activities. Blame asks: \"Whose fault was this?\" Cause analysis asks: [tableau.com] tableau.com Root cause analysis: Definition, examples and a how ...Root cause analysis (RCA) is the process of discovering the root causes of... \"What conditions allowed this to happen?\" A forgotten task may involve a person's mistake, but the broader cause may involve unclear responsibilities, poor reminders or an overloaded schedule. Many root cause frameworks explicitly warn against reducing diagnosis to individual fault because doing so can hide the larger mechanisms that make errors more likely. [Adobe for"},{"title":"Wicked Frames","url":"/wicked-frames/","description":"Some problems stay hard because people disagree about what the problem even is.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Problem Framing","level":3,"text":"Wicked Frames When People Cannot Agree on the Problem Wicked Frames Wicked Frames When People Cannot Agree on the Problem Some problems stay hard because people disagree about what the problem even is. Some problems stay hard because people disagree about what the problem even is. population context Why wicked problems resist final wording Values, trade offs, and disputed success How framing can make disagreement visible improving your thinking and analytical skills problem framing wicked frames Introduction Some problems remain difficult not because the facts are unknown, but because people cannot agree on what the problem actually is. This is a defining feature of many “wicked problems” — complex social, organisational and public policy challenges where different groups hold different values, priorities and interpretations of reality. In these situations, disagreement begins before solutions are discussed. Stakeholders may frame the same situation as a safety issue, a fairness issue, an economic issue, a rights issue or a governance issue, with each framing implying a different path forward. Rittel and Webber’s foundational work on wicked problems highlighted that there is no definitive formulation of such problems and that different explanations lead to different responses. [Urban Policy] urbanpolicy.net Rittel Webber 1973 DilemmasInAGeneralTheoryOfPlanning 160. III. Planning Problems are Wicked Problems. A great many barriers keep us from...Read more... For anyone seeking to improve their thinking and analytical skills, this matters because reasoning quality depends heavily on how the problem is framed. If stakeholders are arguing about different versions of the problem, evidence alone rarely resolves the conflict. Why Wicked Problems Resist Final Wording Many technical problems can be defined with reasonable precision. A machine either fails to meet a specification or it does not. Wicked problems are different because their boundaries, causes and goals are themselves disputed. Rittel and Webber argued that there is no definitive formulation of a wicked problem. The way a problem is described depends on which stakeholders are consulted, which consequences are emphasised and which values are treated as important. In practice , defining the problem is inseparable from proposing a solution. [Urban Policy] urbanpolicy.net Rittel Webber 1973 DilemmasInAGeneralTheoryOfPlanning 160. III. Planning Problems are Wicked Problems. A great many barriers keep us from...Read more... Consider a city facing rising housing costs. Different stakeholders might frame the issue as: A shortage of housing supply. Excessive speculation and investment. A failure of wage growth. An environmental sustainability challenge. A planning and zoning problem. A social justice issue. Each framing highlights some facts and downplays others. None is necessarily false, but each creates a different decision agenda. Research on wicked problems consistently emphasises that stakeholder perceptions, interests and values shape how issues are scoped and prioritised. The conflict is not merely about evidence; it is about which interpretation deserves to guide action. [OUP Academic+2Taylor & Francis Online] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicForty years of wicked problems literature: forging closer links...by BW Head · 2019 · Cited by 740 — The wicked problems per... This explains why debates over climate policy, urban development, healthcare reform or organisational change often appear to stall. Participants may seem to be discussing solutions while actually disagreeing about the nature of the underlying problem. Wicked Problems No Easy Solutions Explained (18 Minutes) Channel: BioTech Whisperer Open on YouTube Values, Trade Offs, and Disputed Success A central reason for stakeholder conflict is that wicked problems contain value judgements that cannot be settled scientifically. For many organisational and social issues, stakeholders care about different outcomes : Efficiency versus equity. Security versus privacy. Economic growth versus environmental protection. Short term stability versus long term resilience. Individual freedom versus collective welfare. These are not simply factual disagreements. They reflect competing views about what ought to matter. The wicked problems literature repeatedly notes that solutions are rarely judged as true or false. Instead, they are judged as better or worse according to different stakeholder values. Because people use different standards of evaluation, they can look at the same outcome and reach opposite conclusions. [Wikipedia+2Taylor & Francis Online] Wikipedia Wicked problem Wicked problem For example, a new public transport project may be celebrated by environmental advocates because it reduces emissions, criticised by local residents because it changes neighbourhood character, and questioned by taxpayers because of cost. The disagreement is not merely about transport engineering. It concerns what success should mean. This is why attempts to produce a single “objective” definition of many social problems often fail. Stakeholders are not only disagreeing about facts; they are disagreeing about which goals deserve priority. Research on governance and systems thinking highlights that wicked problems commonly involve actors with conflicting interests and values, making consensus on problem definition inherently difficult. [OECD] oecd.org Systemic Thinking for Policy Making (EN Systemic Thinking for Policy Making (EN)May 15, 2025 — complex and ill defined problems with multiple interacting actors, often with... Published: May 15, 2025 How Frames Create Winners and Losers Problem framing is not neutral. Every frame highlights certain interests and makes others less visible. A useful way to understand stakeholder conflict is to ask three questions: 1. Who benefits if this framing becomes dominant? 2. Whose concerns become central? 3. Which alternatives become easier or harder to consider? Suppose an organisation experiences declining employee morale. If leaders frame the issue as a productivity problem, the response may focus on performance management and incentives. If employees frame it as a workload problem, attention may shift towards staffing levels and expectations. If human resources frames it as a culture problem, solutions may focus on leadership behaviour and communication. The same observable situation can produce different interventions because each frame identifies a different root cause. This dynamic is particularly important in governance contexts. Policy scholars note that pluralistic societies naturally contain multiple legitimate viewpoints. Wicked problems emerge partly because stakeholders possess different experiences, interests and moral commitments, making competing frames unavoidable rather than exceptional. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com OUP AcademicForty years of wicked problems literature: forging closer links...by BW Head · 2019 · Cited by 740 — The wicked problems per... Recognising this helps analysts avoid a common mistake: assuming that disagreement reflects ignorance. Often the disagreement reflects genuine differences in priorities and worldviews. Wicked Problems in Urban Planning Rittel & Webber’s Groundbreaking Theory Explained Channel: Sustainable Solutions Open on YouTube How Framing Can Make Disagreement Visible The goal is not always to force agreement on a single definition. In many cases, better thinking begins by making competing frames explicit. Problem structuring approaches were developed partly to help groups work with uncertainty and conflict before rushing into solution design. Rather than asking participants to agree immediately, these methods encourage them to surface assumptions , concerns and interpretations. [ResearchGate+2cci.drexel.edu] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Problem Structuring MethodsProblem structuring methods (PSMs) are a broad group of model based problem handling approac... Useful questions include: What problem do different stakeholders believe they are facing? What outcomes does each group consider successful? Which values are driving those preferences? What assumptions support each framing? What evidence would change minds , and what disagreements are fundamentally value based? Answering these questions often reveals that apparent factual disputes are actually conflicts over goals or priorities. For example, a debate about whether a company should require office attendance may initially appear to concern productivity data. Closer examination may reveal competing values: flexibility, collaboration, autonomy, fairness, career development and organisational identity . Once those values are visible, the disagreement becomes easier to understand even if it is not fully resolved. Thinking Better When Frames Conflict When faced with a wicked problem, analytical skill involves more than collecting evidence. It requires recognising that multiple stakeholders may be"},{"title":"Project Failure","url":"/project-failure/","description":"When a project stalls, better analysis looks beyond blame to goals, authority, priorities, and feedback loops.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Project Failure Is the System Creating the Delay? Project Failure Project Failure Is the System Creating the Delay? When a project stalls, better analysis looks beyond blame to goals, authority, priorities, and feedback loops. When a project stalls, better analysis looks beyond blame to goals, authority, priorities, and feedback loops. population context Blame versus system diagnosis Feedback loops and authority Better project explanations improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure Introduction When a project stalls, the weakest explanation is often the most emotionally satisfying one: someone was lazy, careless, difficult or incompetent. Sometimes that is true. More often, delay is produced by the system around the people: unclear goals, split authority, shifting priorities, optimistic plans, weak feedback loops and decision rules that reward reassurance over reality. System level reasoning does not excuse poor performance. It asks a better diagnostic question: what conditions made this failure likely, repeated or hard to correct? For analytical thinking, this matters because project failure is rarely a single event. It is usually a chain: early ambiguity creates hidden work; hidden work breaks schedules; schedule pressure suppresses bad news; suppressed bad news delays decisions; delayed decisions make the original plan less realistic. The useful thinker looks beyond blame to the mechanism. Blame versus system diagnosis Blame asks, “Who caused the delay?” System diagnosis asks, “Why did this delay keep making sense to the people inside the project?” That shift is not soft or evasive. It is more demanding, because it requires evidence about incentives, governance, information flow and the gap between the official plan and the real work. A stalled software rollout, for example, may appear to be a delivery team failure. A system level explanation may show something different: users were not involved early enough; requirements changed because senior sponsors had not agreed the real purpose; technical teams were measured on milestone completion rather than operational readiness; and no one had enough authority to stop the launch date from dominating every other judgement. The “project manager failed” story is simpler, but it may not explain why competent people repeatedly made poor decisions. Project management research and professional guidance increasingly frame success as more than hitting a fixed deadline and budget. The Project Management Institute’s 2025 report argues that project professionals need business acumen: understanding the organisation, its strategy, finance, stakeholders and the value a project is meant to deliver, not merely managing tasks against a plan. The report links higher business acumen proficiency with stronger project outcomes across business goals, schedule, budget and failure experience. [Project Management Institute] pmi.org Project Management Institute This distinction changes the quality of analysis. If the explanation is “the team missed the deadline”, the remedy is pressure. If the explanation is “the delivery system rewarded optimistic reporting and deferred unresolved dependencies”, the remedy is different: change escalation routes, make uncertainty visible, clarify decision rights , and measure progress by tested integration rather than slide deck completion. When the plan is not the work Many projects fail because the plan describes a simplified version of the work. This is especially common when a project crosses organisational boundaries, uses new technology, depends on contractors, or must satisfy multiple stakeholders with different definitions of success. The Crossrail programme is a useful example because the later problem was not simply that construction was “late”. The National Audit Office found in 2021 that, despite efforts to control cost and schedule after 2019, the programme was further from completion and more complicated than Crossrail Ltd or its sponsors understood. The result was a further forecast cost increase of £1.9 billion and 10 to 20 months of delay. The same report highlighted the complexity of bringing together trains, signalling, stations and other assets, and the need for several organisations to work closely to complete the programme. National Audit Office (NAO)+2National Audit Office (NAO) [nao.org.uk] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail – a progress update National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail – a progress update That is a system level failure mode: the visible schedule gave more confidence than the underlying integration work justified. In such cases, asking whether a team is “on track” can be misleading unless “track” includes interface testing, unresolved defects, dependency risk, staffing capability and operational acceptance. A better analytical question is not “Are the milestones green?” but “What would have to be true for this milestone to mean real progress?” A project may report progress because documents have been signed, components have been built or contracts have been let. Yet the harder question is whether the parts work together under real conditions. Feedback loops and authority A feedback loop is the route by which evidence changes decisions. In a healthy project, bad news travels quickly, reaches someone with authority, and changes priorities, funding, design or timing. In a weak project, bad news is collected but softened, delayed, disputed or buried until it becomes too expensive to act on. Systems thinking is valuable here because it treats a project as interacting parts rather than a list of tasks. PMI’s systems thinking guidance describes the approach as a way to understand complex interactions and feedback loops, especially in high risk environments and under time pressure. It stresses that understanding the relationships between parts of a problem is the first step to designing a better solution. [Project Management Institute] pmi.org Project Management Institute Systems Thinking Boosts Project Outcomes PMI Project Management Institute Systems Thinking Boosts Project Outcomes PMI Authority matters because feedback without power becomes theatre. A risk register that no one acts on is not a feedback loop; it is a record of ignored knowledge. A steering group that receives bad news but cannot change scope, budget, staffing or deadline pressure is not governing the system; it is observing it. The National Audit Office’s 2025 work on mega project governance makes this point in institutional terms. It defines governance as the system that provides the framework for managing organisations or projects and making decisions, including formal structures, processes, people and behaviours. It says good governance is clear about who has authority and accountability, and about the investing organisation’s role in defining and controlling the project. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk Open source on nao.org.uk. That definition is useful far beyond public infrastructure. A delayed project often needs answers to four authority questions: Who can change the deadline when evidence shows it is unrealistic? Who can reduce scope without treating it as failure? Who can resolve conflicts between departments, suppliers or users? Who is rewarded for surfacing risk early rather than preserving confidence? If the answer to these questions is unclear, the project may continue to fail even when everyone works hard. Breaking down barriers to implementing good practice in Project Management Project Management SG Channel: BCS Member Groups Open on YouTube How “good news” becomes a failure mechanism One of the most dangerous project cultures is not open dishonesty but disciplined optimism. People learn which messages are welcome. Risks become “watch items”. Delays become “recoverable”. Dependencies become “being managed”. Teams stop lying to others only after they have first learned to lie to themselves. The Challenger disaster remains a powerful case because it shows how repeated survival can be mistaken for safety. NASA’s own later safety material describes Diane Vaughan’s concept of normalisation of deviance: people become accustomed to deviations from rules and stop treating them as deviant. In the shuttle case, O ring erosion and blow by were accepted as risks despite design expectations of no joint failures. The same NASA material warns that past success can reinforce trust in risky practice instead of objective assessment. [SMA NASA] sma.nasa.gov SMA NASAThe Cost of Silence: Normalization of Deviance and Groupthink SMA NASAThe Cost of Silence: Normalization of Deviance and Groupthink Columbia Magazine’s 2025 account of Vaughan’s work makes the analytical lesson even sharper. Vaughan initially expected to find calculating misconduct, but her archival research led her to a more systemic account: NASA had routines, language and decision structures that made the O ring problem appear manageable. Repeated fixes seemed to work"},{"title":"Crossrail","url":"/crossrail/","description":"Crossrail shows how large projects can fall behind when sponsors underestimate the difficulty of joining many working parts into one service.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Project Failure","level":3,"text":"Crossrail What Crossrail Reveals About Hidden Complexity Crossrail Crossrail What Crossrail Reveals About Hidden Complexity Crossrail shows how large projects can fall behind when sponsors underestimate the difficulty of joining many working parts into one service. Crossrail shows how large projects can fall behind when sponsors underestimate the difficulty of joining many working parts into one service. entity What the programme thought was complete Why trains, stations and signalling had to work together Lessons for judging complex project progress improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure crossrail Introduction Crossrail, now operating as the central section of the Elizabeth line, is often described as a project that was delayed and over budget. That description is accurate but incomplete. The more important lesson is that the programme demonstrated how easily decision makers can mistake visible construction progress for genuine readiness. By the time tunnels, stations and track looked largely complete, the most difficult work still lay ahead: proving that hundreds of interconnected systems could operate safely and reliably as a single railway. For anyone interested in improving analytical thinking, Crossrail is a powerful reminder that complex systems should be judged by successful integration rather than by the completion of individual components. The programme's difficulties were not primarily about digging tunnels or building stations. They arose from underestimating the effort required to combine trains, signalling, software, communications, safety systems, operations and multiple organisations into one functioning service. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Completing Crossrail NAO report3 May 2019 — This report examines the causes of the cost increases and delays to the Crossrail programme... Published: May 2019 What the programme thought was complete By 2018, much of Crossrail's physical infrastructure appeared finished. New stations stood in central London, tunnels had been excavated, track had been installed and trains had been delivered. From a traditional construction perspective, the programme looked close to completion. However, infrastructure projects reach a critical point where progress changes from building individual assets to making every asset work together. The National Audit Office (NAO) concluded that Crossrail's leadership, together with its sponsors, underestimated how much work remained once construction was largely complete. Rather than approaching the finish line, the programme still faced an enormous volume of testing , commissioning, assurance and defect resolution before passengers could be carried safely. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail a progress update NAO reportJul 9, 2021 — Despite efforts to control costs and schedule in 2019, the programme was further from complet... This distinction matters because different kinds of work produce different signals. Construction creates visible progress that politicians, journalists and the public can easily observe. Integration work is largely invisible. Software interfaces, signalling validation, operational rehearsals and safety assurance generate little visible evidence even though they often determine whether a railway can actually open. The consequence was an optimism gap. Decision makers could point to completed stations and installed equipment while the underlying operational readiness remained far less advanced than appearances suggested. The NAO later concluded that when the programme was reset in 2019 it was \"further from completion and more complicated\" than Crossrail Ltd or its sponsors understood. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail a progress update NAO reportJul 9, 2021 — Despite efforts to control costs and schedule in 2019, the programme was further from complet... Crossrail Risk Management Channel: Association for Project Management Open on YouTube Why trains, stations and signalling had to work together Crossrail was never simply a civil engineering project. It was a system of systems in which each part depended on many others behaving correctly at the same time. Several different categories of integration had to succeed simultaneously: Rolling stock and signalling: Trains needed to operate under multiple signalling environments, including conventional Network Rail signalling, the European Train Control System and Communications Based Train Control within the central tunnels. Stations and railway systems: Fire alarms, platform equipment, ventilation, lifts, power supplies, communications and control rooms all had to exchange information correctly during both normal operation and emergencies. Operational procedures: Drivers, controllers, station staff, maintenance teams and emergency services needed validated procedures supported by working technology. Safety assurance: Regulators required evidence that the railway behaved safely not only when every component worked, but also when equipment failed or unexpected situations occurred. [Crossrail Learning Legacy+2Crossrail Learning Legacy] learninglegacy.crossrail.co.uk Crossrail Learning LegacyCrossrail System Integration – The Practicalities of Integrating...Successful system integration (SI) on Crossr... None of these challenges could be solved independently. A software change affecting signalling could require further train testing. Station modifications might alter safety certification. Operational procedures could change because of engineering findings. Every completed task potentially created new dependencies elsewhere. This is characteristic of complex systems: progress is not additive. Completing 90% of individual tasks does not imply that 90% of the integrated system is ready. Railway Systems Integration – Dealing with Complexity Channel: IET Institution of Engineering and Technology Open on YouTube Hidden complexity grows at interfaces One reason Crossrail became difficult to predict is that complexity accumulated at interfaces rather than inside individual contracts. Many contractors successfully delivered their own assets. The greater challenge was ensuring that separately designed and separately delivered components interacted correctly after installation. Every interface between organisations represented a potential point where assumptions , technical standards or schedules could diverge. The NAO identified several structural contributors to this problem, including the very large number of contracts, bespoke designs and limited standardisation across the programme. These factors increased the amount of coordination required during final delivery, making completion substantially harder than straightforward construction metrics suggested. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk Crossrail a progress update 2 National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail – a progress updateJul 9, 2021 — The work to bring the railway into service was made more complex by... Crossrail's own later technical publications similarly describe successful system integration as encompassing technical compatibility, operational readiness and organisational coordination. The lesson is significant because engineering success depended not only on whether equipment functioned individually but also on whether organisations could jointly manage testing, assurance and operational acceptance. [Crossrail Learning Legacy] learninglegacy.crossrail.co.uk Crossrail Learning LegacyCrossrail System Integration – The Practicalities of Integrating...Successful system integration (SI) on Crossr... Why schedules became unreliable Large projects usually estimate how long construction activities will take. Integration work is much harder to estimate because new problems often emerge only when previously separate systems are connected. Testing frequently uncovers unexpected interactions: Software behaves differently under operational loads. Equipment supplied by different contractors may interpret standards differently. Safety evidence reveals additional verification work. Operational exercises expose issues that design reviews never identified. Each discovery creates further work, which then requires additional testing before confidence can be restored. The NAO's investigation into Crossrail concluded that the programme suffered from compressed schedules, unrealistic assumptions about completion and an absence of a realistic delivery plan as pressures increased. Rather than recognising integration uncertainty early, the programme continued to work towards dates that became progressively less achievable. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Completing Crossrail NAO report3 May 2019 — This report examines the causes of the cost increases and delays to the Crossrail programme... Published: May 2019 Importantly, this was not simply poor scheduling. It reflected a"},{"title":"Green Milestones","url":"/green-milestones/","description":"A project can look on schedule while the hardest work of making systems, teams and users fit together is still unresolved.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Project Failure","level":3,"text":"Green Milestones When Green Milestones Hide Real Project Risk Green Milestones Green Milestones When Green Milestones Hide Real Project Risk A project can look on schedule while the hardest work of making systems, teams and users fit together is still unresolved. A project can look on schedule while the hardest work of making systems, teams and users fit together is still unresolved. mechanism Why milestone completion can mislead Integration tests that reveal real progress Questions that expose hidden work improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure green milestones Introduction A project can appear healthy because every milestone on the dashboard is green, yet still be heading towards delay or failure. The reason is simple: many milestones measure the completion of individual tasks rather than proving that the finished pieces work together. The greatest uncertainty in complex projects often lies not in building separate components but in integrating technologies, organisations, processes and users into a functioning whole. For anyone seeking to improve their analytical thinking, this is an important lesson. A green milestone is evidence that something was completed. It is not necessarily evidence that the overall system is ready. The critical question is therefore not \"Have we finished each part?\" but \"Have we demonstrated that the parts work together under realistic conditions?\" Why milestone completion can mislead Traditional project plans divide work into manageable packages: a software module is coded, a station is constructed, equipment is installed or documentation is approved. These events are easy to measure and report. Integration is different. It depends on relationships between completed items rather than the items themselves. This creates an important asymmetry. Individual teams can legitimately report success even while the overall project remains unable to function. Every supplier may deliver its contractual obligations, yet the combined system may still fail because interfaces, assumptions or operational procedures do not match. Several mechanisms contribute to this illusion: Milestones measure outputs rather than outcomes . Finishing construction, writing software or installing equipment does not prove the complete service works. Dependencies remain hidden. One team may depend on another's unfinished work, but both report progress independently. Interface problems emerge late. Separate components often appear correct until they exchange data, power, signals or operational responsibilities. Success is easier to verify locally than globally. Local testing usually happens before full system testing, leaving the most difficult problems until the end. This explains why project reporting frequently shows steady progress followed by a sudden crisis during commissioning or deployment. The apparent deterioration is often not caused by new failures but by previously invisible integration risks becoming visible. Master the Process Domain for PMP Success! Channel: Praizion (Leadership, Agile, PMP) Open on YouTube Why integration is usually the hardest work Integration is frequently underestimated because it involves uncertainty rather than production. Teams know roughly how long it takes to manufacture a component or write a feature. They cannot predict with the same confidence how many unexpected interactions will emerge once independent systems begin working together. Complex projects often require integration across several dimensions simultaneously: Technical compatibility between systems. Operational procedures used by different organisations. Data standards and communications. Safety assurance and regulatory approval. User behaviour and training. Maintenance and support arrangements. Each interface increases the number of possible failure paths. As the number of interfaces grows, integration effort often expands faster than the number of individual components. Experience from the Crossrail programme illustrates this point. Later reviews emphasised that successful system integration required technical integration, operational integration and organisational integration throughout the project lifecycle, rather than treating integration as a final activity before opening. The programme also highlighted the importance of testing at the right stage—too early and components are immature, too late and defects cannot be resolved without major schedule impacts. [Crossrail Learning Legacy] learninglegacy.crossrail.co.uk Crossrail Learning LegacyCrossrail System Integration – The Practicalities of Integrating...Successful system integration (SI) on Crossr... The \"Watermelon\" Project: Why Your \"Green\" Status Reports Are Actually Failing Channel: Resilient Foundations Open on YouTube Why dashboards stay green until late Many reporting systems unintentionally reward optimism. If milestone status is based on whether planned deliverables have been completed, managers have little incentive to delay declaring success while awaiting evidence that interfaces function correctly. A completed work package becomes \"green\" even if downstream teams cannot yet use it. This creates a reporting pattern sometimes described as \"watermelon status\": green on the outside, red inside. Although the metaphor is informal, the underlying mechanism is well recognised in project governance. Several organisational pressures reinforce it: Teams are evaluated on delivering their own scope. Schedule commitments become psychologically difficult to challenge. Escalating uncertainty may be interpreted as poor performance. Integration responsibility is often shared, making ownership unclear. Executives receive summary indicators that hide unresolved dependencies. As a result, dashboards may communicate confidence while the most difficult engineering and organisational work remains unfinished. Crossrail: when construction progress hid integration risk Crossrail provides a well documented example of how visible progress can conceal unfinished integration work. Much of the railway infrastructure, stations and physical construction advanced substantially before the programme entered its most difficult phase. However, opening the railway required far more than completed assets. Hundreds of thousands of physical assets had to be supported by extensive assurance documentation, multiple signalling environments, operational procedures, software, testing and safety certification before passenger service could begin. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail – a progress update July 8, 2021 — 9 Jul 2021 — When complete, the railway will be around 73 miles (118 kilometres) long, stopping at 41 stations, including... Published: July 8, 2021 Subsequent reviews concluded that the challenge was not simply construction delay but insufficient appreciation of system integration complexity. The National Audit Office found that previous management continued believing ambitious opening dates remained achievable despite repeated missed milestones and an unrealistic completion plan. [National Audit Office (NAO)] nao.org.uk National Audit Office (NAO)Crossrail – a progress update July 8, 2021 — 9 Jul 2021 — When complete, the railway will be around 73 miles (118 kilometres) long, stopping at 41 stations, including... Published: July 8, 2021 Parliamentary evidence after the delay highlighted another important lesson. Officials argued that the project had not reoriented quickly enough from construction management towards systems integration. They also described a broader culture of optimism in which years of successful construction encouraged stakeholders to believe remaining work would progress similarly smoothly, even though the character of the project had fundamentally changed. [UK Parliament Committees] committees.parliament.uk UK Parliament Committees Oral evidence UK Parliament CommitteesOral evidence Crossrail: progress review 6 Mar 20196 Mar 2019 — What we saw in the final stages of this proje... Later lessons learned from the recovery emphasised that project baselines should include not only physical construction but also testing, integration testing, rework and assurance activities, because these become the dominant work during the final stages of complex programmes. [APM] apm.org.uk Crossrail Project 2019 2023 Completing the Elizabeth LineApril 6, 2023 — 23 Mar 2023 — The baseline must reflect all work: physical... Published: April 6, 2023 Integration tests that reveal real progress The most informative milestones are not those showing that components exist but those demonstrating that they operate successfully together. Examples include: End to end operational testing. A complete workflow succeeds under realistic operating conditions rather than within isolated subsystems. Interface verification. Every connection between organisations, software, equipment or processes performs correctly. Failure scenario testing. Recovery from"},{"title":"Risk Registers","url":"/risk-registers/","description":"A risk register only helps when the evidence reaches people who can change scope, funding, staffing or timing.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Project Failure","level":3,"text":"Risk Registers When Risk Registers Become Ignored Knowledge Risk Registers Risk Registers When Risk Registers Become Ignored Knowledge A risk register only helps when the evidence reaches people who can change scope, funding, staffing or timing. A risk register only helps when the evidence reaches people who can change scope, funding, staffing or timing. policy intervention The difference between recording and governing risk Why bad news gets softened before decisions How authority turns feedback into action improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure risk registers Introduction A risk register is meant to help decision makers act before uncertainty becomes failure. It does not reduce risk simply by documenting it. When organisations faithfully record growing problems yet leave scope, budget, staffing, priorities and deadlines unchanged, the register becomes a historical record rather than a management tool. This distinction matters because many troubled projects show warning signs long before they experience major delays or cost overruns. The failure is often not one of detection but of governance: information reaches the organisation, but not the people—or not in the form—that can authorise meaningful change. Effective analytical thinking therefore asks not only whether risks were identified, but whether the governance system converted evidence into decisions. National Audit Office (NAO)+2Government Project Delivery [nao.org.uk] nao.org.uk governance and decision making on mega projects National Audit Office (NAO)Governance and decision‑making on mega‑projects14 Mar 2025 — The purpose of this report is to improve how gove... The difference between recording and governing risk A risk register is designed to collect information about uncertain events, assess their likelihood and impact, assign owners and track responses. Professional project management guidance treats it as a living management tool rather than a compliance document. Recording risks is only the first stage; management requires continuous review, reassessment and escalation when circumstances change. [Project Management Institute+2Prince2 Wiki] pmi.org Project Management InstituteRisk analysis and managementThe medium for recording all identified risks throughout the project is the risk... The crucial distinction is that a register has no authority of its own. It cannot: approve additional funding; reduce project scope; [esa.int] esa.int gement. – programmatics and politics. – requirements. – technology and... delay a launch; add specialist staff; resolve conflicts between stakeholders; or accept strategic trade offs . Only governance structures can do those things. If review meetings treat the register as evidence that risks are \"being managed\" simply because they have been logged, documentation replaces intervention. Several project management commentators describe this as confusing visibility with control: organisations become highly proficient at describing risks while remaining unable or unwilling to change the conditions creating them. [Canstead Learning+2normanmarks.wordpress.com] cansteadlearning.com Risks are categorized. Heat maps are presented. And yet, issues still emerge late, escalate poorly, and impact outcomes.Read more... For someone trying to improve analytical skills, this distinction is important. The presence of sophisticated reporting should never be taken as evidence that a feedback system is functioning. A good diagnostic question is: What decision changed because this risk appeared? If the answer is \"none\", the register may be providing information without influence. Why Your Risk Registers Fail and How RAID in Jira Can Help Channel: SoftComply Open on YouTube Why bad news gets softened before decisions Projects rarely ignore risks because nobody noticed them. More commonly, warnings become progressively weaker as they move through management layers. Several organisational pressures contribute to this process. Optimism about recovery. Teams frequently believe the next milestone will recover lost time or reduce technical uncertainty. Risks therefore remain labelled as manageable long after contingency plans should have been activated. Fear of being seen as obstructive. Reporting serious risks can be interpreted as poor performance rather than accurate diagnosis. Staff may therefore frame problems as temporary or already under control. Escalation fatigue. If previous warnings produced no practical response, teams learn that escalation creates work without changing outcomes . Eventually the register continues to be updated while genuine concern declines. Schedule commitment. Once senior leaders publicly commit to delivery dates, evidence threatening those commitments can become politically difficult to present. Instead of asking whether the schedule remains realistic, reviews concentrate on how the existing schedule might still be achieved. These mechanisms do not necessarily involve dishonesty. Rather, each reporting layer tends to reduce uncertainty into language that appears manageable. By the time senior decision makers review the register, severe uncertainties may appear as routine monitoring items rather than signals requiring strategic intervention. This dynamic has been observed repeatedly in studies of project governance and organisational risk communication. PECB+3National Audit Office (NAO)+3National Audit Office (NAO) [nao.org.uk] nao.org.uk governance and decision making on mega projects National Audit Office (NAO)Governance and decision‑making on mega‑projects14 Mar 2025 — The purpose of this report is to improve how gove... Why Your Cyber Risk Register Is Bigger Than It Should Be GRC Channel: Karimah Ari Open on YouTube Why ownership alone does not solve the problem Most risk registers include an owner for every significant risk. This is good practice , but ownership can become misleading when the owner lacks authority over the underlying cause. For example: a technical lead may own an integration risk but cannot obtain additional funding; a project manager may own a supplier dependency but cannot renegotiate the contract; a delivery team may own staffing risks while recruitment decisions sit elsewhere. In these situations, assigning ownership can unintentionally create the appearance of accountability without granting the ability to change outcomes. A more useful distinction separates operational ownership from decision authority . Operational owners monitor and explain risks. Decision authorities determine whether budgets, scope, governance or schedules should change. Unless the escalation route between those two roles is explicit, risks accumulate while responsibility becomes blurred. Government guidance on programme governance therefore stresses clearly defined reporting lines, escalation arrangements and decision rights linked to agreed risk tolerances. [Government Project Delivery+2GOV.UK] projectdelivery.gov.uk ernment Project Delivery Chapter 20 Risk managementThe framework should include arrangements for managing and where necessary escalating risks in line with agreed risk toler... How authority turns feedback into action Risk registers become valuable when they are directly connected to governance decisions rather than periodic reporting. Several practices distinguish effective governance from passive documentation. Clear escalation thresholds. Rather than simply recording likelihood and impact, organisations define conditions that automatically require senior review—for example, when contingency falls below a specified level or when schedule confidence drops beneath an agreed threshold. [Government Project Delivery] projectdelivery.gov.uk ernment Project Delivery Chapter 20 Risk managementThe framework should include arrangements for managing and where necessary escalating risks in line with agreed risk toler... Decision oriented reviews. Instead of asking whether risks have been updated, governance meetings ask what decisions are required because of current evidence. This shifts attention from administration to intervention. Alignment with organisational risk appetite. Projects need explicit agreement about which risks can be accepted locally and which exceed delegated authority. Without that distinction, serious risks remain trapped within project teams. [PRINCE2] prince2.com They turn uncertainty into visibility, and visibility into control.Read more The risk register: What to include (and what to avoid)November 11, 2025 — 11 Nov 2025 — Ultimately, the most valuable risk registe... Published: November 11, 2025 Monitoring response effectiveness. Closing a risk because a mitigation action was completed is different from demonstrating that exposure genuinely decreased. Effective governance measures outcomes rather than completed paperwork. [PECB] pecb.com 12 Reasons for Risk Management FailureFailing to monitor risks and reassess them regularly can lead to unforeseen issues as the risks... These"},{"title":"Deviance Drift","url":"/deviance-drift/","description":"Small accepted exceptions can become the new normal until teams stop recognising that the project is operating outside safe limits.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Project Failure","level":3,"text":"Deviance Drift How Risk Starts Feeling Normal Deviance Drift Deviance Drift How Risk Starts Feeling Normal Small accepted exceptions can become the new normal until teams stop recognising that the project is operating outside safe limits. Small accepted exceptions can become the new normal until teams stop recognising that the project is operating outside safe limits. mechanism How repeated survival teaches false confidence From shuttle safety to everyday project habits Warning signs that exceptions have become routine improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure deviance drift Introduction Normalisation of deviance describes a gradual shift in judgement in which practices that were originally recognised as unsafe, exceptional or outside agreed standards come to be treated as normal because they repeatedly appear to work. The danger is not that people suddenly become reckless. Rather, each successful outcome without an immediate failure encourages the belief that the original safety margin was overly cautious. Over time, the organisation stops seeing itself as taking unusual risks at all. This mechanism is especially important for improving analytical thinking because it explains why intelligent, conscientious teams can collectively drift into poor decisions without anyone deliberately choosing an obviously dangerous course. The concept, developed by sociologist Diane Vaughan through her study of the Challenger disaster, has since become a widely used explanation for failures in engineering, healthcare and project management. [University of Chicago Press] press.uchicago.edu University of Chicago PressThe Challenger Launch DecisionShe reveals how and why NASA insiders, when repeatedly faced with evidence that... How repeated survival teaches false confidence The most important feature of normalisation of deviance is that success becomes misleading evidence. Projects rarely move directly from safe practice to serious failure. Instead, a small exception is made: a test is shortened, a review is skipped, a deadline is protected by accepting an unresolved issue, or a known defect is judged \"acceptable for now\". If nothing visibly goes wrong, the exception becomes easier to justify next time. Eventually the exception no longer feels like an exception. This creates a dangerous feedback loop : A deviation from the intended process occurs. The project succeeds despite the deviation. The absence of immediate harm is interpreted as proof that the deviation is safe. The deviation becomes routine. Future decisions begin from the new baseline rather than the original standard. The key analytical mistake is confusing survival with evidence of safety . A project may avoid disaster because of luck, hidden safety margins or favourable circumstances rather than because the shortcut was genuinely harmless. Every successful outcome increases confidence while providing very little information about how close the project came to failure. [PubMed+2Cranfield University DSpace] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov When Doing Wrong Feels So Right: Normalization of...by MR Price · 2018 · Cited by 116 — Normalization of deviance is a term first... Columbia Accident Investigation Board Report Press Briefing, August 26, 2003 Channel: Space Policy and Politics Open on YouTube From shuttle safety to everyday project habits The Challenger disaster remains the defining historical example because it illustrates this gradual drift rather than a single catastrophic mistake. Engineers at NASA and contractor Morton Thiokol had observed erosion and damage to the solid rocket booster O rings on previous shuttle flights. These observations did not initially fit the intended design assumptions . However, because successive launches completed successfully, the damage gradually came to be regarded as an acceptable operating condition rather than evidence that the design required correction. Vaughan argued that decision makers did not suddenly abandon safety standards. Instead, they slowly redefined what counted as acceptable evidence of risk. [University of Chicago Press+2Columbia Magazine] press.uchicago.edu University of Chicago PressThe Challenger Launch DecisionShe reveals how and why NASA insiders, when repeatedly faced with evidence that... This is precisely why the Challenger case remains valuable for ordinary projects. Most organisations never face life or death engineering decisions, but many experience comparable forms of drift: Temporary workarounds become permanent operating procedures. Schedule pressure justifies incomplete documentation. Risk registers accumulate unresolved items that no longer receive attention. Defects are repeatedly accepted because previous releases caused no visible problems. Governance meetings approve exceptions so frequently that they cease to function as exceptions. None of these decisions appears dramatic in isolation. Their significance lies in the accumulated change to what the organisation considers normal. Research on project governance has argued that normalised deviance develops through a sequence of individually understandable choices that become institutionalised until the organisation loses sight of its original standards. [Cranfield University DSpace] dspace.lib.cranfield.ac.uk Open source on cranfield.ac.uk. The lesson is not that every exception is wrong. Projects often require informed trade offs . The problem begins when repeated exceptions quietly redefine the baseline without deliberate review. Why ordinary decisions are especially vulnerable Large disasters often attract attention because of spectacular technical failures, but normalisation of deviance usually grows through routine administrative decisions. Several organisational pressures make this mechanism particularly attractive: Deadline pressure. Teams under delivery pressure naturally ask whether a known issue is \"good enough for now\". Repeating this judgement without reassessing cumulative risk gradually lowers standards. Familiarity . Hazards that are encountered repeatedly become psychologically less alarming. What once prompted escalation becomes another routine item on a meeting agenda. Performance incentives. If managers are rewarded for maintaining delivery dates rather than exposing uncertainty, evidence supporting continuation receives more attention than evidence supporting delay. Incremental change. Small adjustments rarely trigger the scrutiny that would accompany a major policy change. Because each individual step appears modest, no single decision feels like crossing an important boundary. These pressures do not require dishonesty or incompetence. They arise from normal organisational behaviour, making them particularly difficult to recognise while a project is underway. [Cranfield University DSpace+2IDEAS/RePEc] dspace.lib.cranfield.ac.uk Open source on cranfield.ac.uk. Why Great Teams Stop Speaking Up Channel: BUILT Leaders Open on YouTube Warning signs that exceptions have become routine Analytically, the important question is not whether a project contains exceptions—it almost certainly does—but whether the organisation still recognises them as exceptions. Warning signs include: Teams describing known deviations as \"the way we always do it\". Risk acceptance being justified primarily by previous successful outcomes . Temporary fixes remaining in place for months without formal review. Safety or quality thresholds gradually changing without explicit approval. Repeated waivers becoming easier to obtain than resolving the underlying problem. Engineers or specialists expressing concern while management increasingly views those concerns as routine rather than unusual. Another warning sign is a subtle change in language. Teams stop asking, \"Does this meet the intended standard?\" and instead ask, \"Have we got away with this before?\" The first question measures performance against design intent; the second measures it against recent experience. Preventing deviance drift in project decisions The practical defence against normalisation of deviance is not to ban every exception but to prevent today's exception from silently becoming tomorrow's standard. Useful practices include: Review repeated waivers together. A single approved exception may be reasonable; ten similar exceptions indicate that the underlying process or design needs attention. Separate schedule decisions from technical judgement. Technical experts should be able to describe risk without simultaneously defending delivery commitments. Record the original rationale. Comparing current practice with the original design assumptions helps reveal how far operational behaviour has drifted. Treat successful near misses as learning opportunities. A problem that caused no visible harm should still be analysed if it required unusual recovery or depended on fortunate circumstances. Invite periodic external review. Fresh reviewers are less likely to accept local assumptions that long serving teams"},{"title":"Cost Optimism","url":"/cost-optimism/","description":"Cost overruns often persist because project approval rewards confidence before it rewards accuracy.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Project Failure","level":3,"text":"Cost Optimism Why Weak Project Estimates Keep Winning Approval Cost Optimism Cost Optimism Why Weak Project Estimates Keep Winning Approval Cost overruns often persist because project approval rewards confidence before it rewards accuracy. Cost overruns often persist because project approval rewards confidence before it rewards accuracy. dataset evidence Technical error versus strategic misrepresentation What transport cost overrun data suggests How funding contests distort early forecasts improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure cost optimism Introduction Large projects rarely begin with forecasts that everyone knows are impossible. More often, they begin with estimates that are genuinely optimistic, selectively optimistic, or deliberately understated because optimistic proposals are more likely to secure approval than cautious ones. This creates a system level problem: even if individual participants believe they are acting rationally, the overall funding process can reward inaccurate forecasts over realistic ones. Understanding this mechanism is an important analytical skill because it shifts attention away from blaming forecasting mistakes alone and towards examining the incentives that shape forecasts before a project is approved. Research on transport infrastructure provides one of the strongest evidence bases for this phenomenon. Decades of data show that cost overruns are not merely random forecasting errors. Instead, the persistence and direction of those errors suggest that the institutional environment often favours projects whose initial estimates appear unusually attractive. [Taylor & Francis Online+2arXiv] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineCurbing Optimism Bias and Strategic Misrepresentation in...by B Flyvbjerg · 2008 · Cited by 696 — Second, the pap... Why do weak estimates repeatedly win approval? The striking feature of many large infrastructure projects is not simply that estimates are wrong. It is that they are wrong in a consistent direction. Initial costs tend to be too low while projected benefits are often too high. If forecasting errors arose mainly from technical uncertainty, the mistakes would be expected to scatter around the true value. Some projects would prove cheaper than expected, others more expensive, with no systematic pattern. Instead, large datasets of transport projects show persistent underestimation of costs across countries and over long periods, suggesting that more than technical forecasting limitations are at work. [ResearchGate+2arXiv] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) How Optimism Bias and Strategic Misrepresentation...12 May 2016 — People tend to judge future events inaccurately in t... Published: May 2016 This matters because approval decisions are typically competitive. Governments and investors rarely fund every proposed railway, road or airport. Projects therefore compete on apparent affordability and expected returns. In such contests, promoters who present conservative estimates may lose funding to promoters whose forecasts appear more attractive, even if those forecasts are less realistic. The incentive structure therefore rewards confidence before it rewards accuracy. Technical error versus strategic misrepresentation Researchers commonly distinguish between two broad explanations for persistent cost underestimation. Scenario Planning: 5 Step Process to See the Future Channel: Online PM Courses Mike Clayton Open on YouTube Technical error The technical explanation assumes that planners are trying to estimate honestly but face imperfect information. Sources of error include: incomplete geological information; immature engineering designs; uncertain material prices; changing regulations; limited historical data. Under this explanation, improving forecasting models, collecting better information and hiring more experienced estimators should gradually eliminate systematic errors. Yet this prediction is difficult to reconcile with decades of evidence showing remarkably persistent cost overruns despite advances in engineering, software and project management. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) How Optimism Bias and Strategic Misrepresentation...12 May 2016 — People tend to judge future events inaccurately in t... Published: May 2016 How Big Things Get Done: a conversation with Professor Bent Flyvbjerg Channel: Sydney Executive Plus Open on YouTube Strategic misrepresentation The second explanation focuses on incentives rather than capability. Here, promoters recognise that optimistic forecasts increase the probability of receiving approval, funding or political support. Costs may therefore be understated, consciously or semi consciously, because realistic estimates would make the project appear less competitive. Bent Flyvbjerg and colleagues describe this as strategic misrepresentation : forecasts become advocacy documents rather than neutral predictions. Rather than asking \"What is most likely?\", decision makers begin asking \"What estimate is most likely to secure approval?\" [Springer+2Taylor & Francis Online] link.springer.com Optimism and Misrepresentation in Early Project Developmentby B Flyvbjerg · Cited by 71 — This chapter identifies optimism bias a... The distinction is analytically important. If inaccurate forecasts result primarily from technical limitations, the solution is better forecasting methods. If they result primarily from incentive problems, technical improvements alone cannot solve them because the underlying reward structure remains unchanged. What transport cost overrun data suggests Transport infrastructure has become the best studied field for examining forecasting bias because many comparable projects exist across different countries. Large datasets assembled by Flyvbjerg and collaborators found substantial average cost overruns across rail, bridge, tunnel and road projects, with rail projects exhibiting particularly large overruns on average. More importantly, the pattern remained remarkably stable over many decades, despite improvements in forecasting techniques. [World Bank+2Taylor & Francis Online] documents.worldbank.org •. The participation of risk capital should not mean that...Read more... The persistence of these findings weakens the claim that inaccurate forecasts are mainly the product of immature forecasting methods. If technical capability were the dominant cause, one would expect forecasting accuracy to improve steadily over time. Instead, the evidence suggests that institutional behaviour changes much more slowly than forecasting technology. More recent reviews have also argued against reducing every cost overrun to optimism bias alone. Reviews published in 2023 note that although optimism bias clearly contributes to inaccurate estimates, its interaction with organisational incentives, governance structures and political pressures remains insufficiently understood. Rather than treating every inaccurate forecast as a psychological mistake, current research increasingly examines how behavioural and institutional mechanisms reinforce one another. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com The article identifies...Read more... How funding contests distort early forecasts Competitive funding creates several predictable pressures. Selection effects. Among many proposed projects, the ones with the most attractive benefit cost ratios naturally receive greater attention. If forecasts contain uncertainty, the projects with the largest underestimates of cost are also more likely to appear attractive enough to be approved. Political timing. Decision makers often seek approval before uncertainty has been fully resolved. Early estimates therefore receive greater political attention than later, more accurate estimates. Escalation after approval. Once substantial political commitment has been made, revising estimates upwards becomes easier than cancelling the project entirely. Costs therefore tend to rise after approval rather than preventing approval in the first place. These mechanisms do not require widespread dishonesty. Even without explicit deception, organisations learn which styles of forecasting are rewarded. Over time, optimistic assumptions become embedded in routine practice because they improve the probability of obtaining resources. [World Bank+2Project Management Institute] documents.worldbank.org •. The participation of risk capital should not mean that...Read more... Webinar – The reality check: benchmarking for increased cost confidence June 2025 Channel: BCIS Open on YouTube Why optimism can be individually rational but socially harmful A useful systems insight is that behaviour benefiting one participant may damage the wider decision making system. For an individual project sponsor, presenting an optimistic estimate may increase the chance of winning funding. For the funding system as a whole, however, repeated optimism produces distorted priorities. The consequences include: projects entering"},{"title":"Decision Rights","url":"/decision-rights/","description":"Projects recover faster when someone clearly has the authority to change deadlines, reduce scope and resolve conflicts.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Project Failure","level":3,"text":"Decision Rights Who Has Power When Plans Stop Working? Decision Rights Decision Rights Who Has Power When Plans Stop Working? Projects recover faster when someone clearly has the authority to change deadlines, reduce scope and resolve conflicts. Projects recover faster when someone clearly has the authority to change deadlines, reduce scope and resolve conflicts. policy intervention Deadline, scope and conflict authority Why steering groups can become observers Decision rules that reward reality over reassurance improving your thinking and analytical skills project failure decision rights Introduction Projects rarely recover because people simply work harder. They recover because someone has the recognised authority to change the plan when the original assumptions stop matching reality. That authority includes deciding whether deadlines move, scope is reduced, funding changes, unresolved conflicts are settled, or the project should even continue at all. Without clear decision rights, teams often recognise problems early but cannot act on them. They continue executing an increasingly unrealistic plan while waiting for approvals that never arrive or are repeatedly deferred. From a systems perspective, decision rights are a governance mechanism rather than a management technique. They determine who is allowed to replace wishful thinking with operational reality. Organisations that define these rights clearly are generally better able to recover struggling projects because decisions happen before delays compound into crises. [Government Project Delivery+2Project Management Institute] projectdelivery.gov.uk ernment Project Delivery Chapter 13 The governance and management of...A governance and management framework sets out the authority limits, decision making roles and rules... Deadline, scope and conflict authority Most failing projects eventually confront three questions: Can the deadline change? Can the promised scope be reduced? Who decides when stakeholders disagree? These are governance questions rather than scheduling questions. A project manager may identify that the delivery date is impossible, but recovery cannot begin if nobody with sufficient authority is prepared to approve a revised schedule. Likewise, engineers may identify a smaller, achievable release, but if no sponsor can formally remove lower priority features, the team continues chasing an unattainable objective. Successful governance frameworks therefore distinguish between operational decisions, which remain with the project team, and strategic decisions, which belong to sponsors or governance boards. A well designed framework specifies authority limits, escalation routes, accountabilities and the conditions under which plans must be reconsidered rather than defended. [Government Project Delivery] projectdelivery.gov.uk ernment Project Delivery Chapter 13 The governance and management of...A governance and management framework sets out the authority limits, decision making roles and rules... The practical consequence is that recovery becomes faster because escalation leads directly to decisions instead of additional discussion. Why steering groups can become observers Steering committees are intended to provide strategic direction, approve major changes and resolve issues beyond the authority of the delivery team. In practice , however, many drift into becoming reporting forums instead of decision forums. Several patterns explain this failure: Meetings are dominated by status updates rather than decisions. Committee members lack the authority to commit their organisations. Difficult issues are repeatedly deferred until the next meeting. Members reward reassuring progress reports more than uncomfortable evidence. Escalations return to the project team without a clear ruling. When this happens, governance adds delay instead of removing it. Teams continue working despite knowing that important assumptions have become false because nobody exercises the authority that governance was designed to provide. Research on steering committees consistently identifies strategic decision making, resource allocation, risk ownership and conflict resolution as their primary purpose. Studies of project governance likewise find that effective steering committees contribute to trust and project performance because participants know where difficult decisions will actually be made. [pmi nl.nl+2emerald.com] pmi nl.nl the importance of steering committees to project managers 21957 They determine the project's scope, objectives, and key performance indicators...Read more... The distinction is subtle but important. A steering committee that merely receives information is an observer. One that actively changes priorities, approves trade offs and resolves conflicts is exercising governance. Project Governance Channel: Standarity Open on YouTube Decision rules that reward reality over reassurance Projects rarely fail because warning signs are invisible. More often, warning signs fail to trigger meaningful decisions. Recovery oriented governance replaces vague judgement with explicit decision rules. Examples include: Schedule thresholds: if completion confidence falls below an agreed level, executive review becomes mandatory. Scope rules: if critical milestones cannot be met, predefined lower priority features are removed before adding more resources. Risk thresholds: unresolved high impact risks automatically require sponsor intervention. Dependency rules: projects cannot pass governance gates while critical external dependencies remain unverified. Evidence requirements: progress is demonstrated through tested deliverables rather than presentation slides or percentage complete estimates. These rules reduce the influence of optimism because they define when authority must be exercised instead of allowing uncomfortable decisions to be postponed indefinitely. Phase gate governance follows this principle by requiring explicit \"go\", \"pause\", \"redirect\" or \"stop\" decisions before projects move into subsequent stages. The gate is valuable only if decision makers are genuinely willing to reject or reshape unrealistic plans rather than treating every review as automatic approval. [Project Management Institute] pmi.org Open source on pmi.org. Why unclear authority keeps failing projects alive Many organisations unintentionally distribute responsibility without distributing authority. For example: The project manager is accountable for delivery but cannot change deadlines. Technical specialists identify unacceptable risks but cannot halt deployment. Sponsors own funding but rarely participate in operational trade offs. Business departments demand additional features without accepting schedule consequences. Everyone is responsible for success, yet no single person can authorise the changes needed to restore feasibility. This creates a predictable behavioural pattern. Teams continue producing reports, updating plans and working harder because those actions remain within their authority. The decisions that would genuinely improve the situation—reducing scope, extending time, reallocating resources or cancelling work—remain outside anyone's practical control. From a systems viewpoint, this is not primarily a motivation problem. It is a governance bottleneck. Ricardo Vargas Teaches How to Navigate the Political Landscape During a Project Recovery Channel: Ricardo Vargas Open on YouTube Recovery often depends on changing the governance, not just the plan Major recovery efforts frequently begin with changes to decision structures rather than changes to technical work. Recovery programmes commonly introduce: a single accountable executive sponsor; shorter escalation paths; [pmexams.com] pmexams.com Establishing the Project Governance Structure13 Apr 2026 — Learn how to define decision rights, roles, and escalation paths (including sp... authority delegated to a recovery leader; more frequent governance reviews focused on decisions rather than reporting; explicit ownership of cross organisational conflicts. The objective is not to centralise every decision but to ensure that decisions affecting feasibility are made quickly enough to influence outcomes . Guidance on project recovery similarly recommends streamlining governance, clarifying escalation paths and empowering leaders to make time sensitive trade offs rather than allowing approval processes to become another source of delay. [PL Projects] plprojects.co.uk PL ProjectsEffective Strategies for Project Recovery: How to Get Your...June 9, 2025 — 9 Jun 2025 — Decision Making Authority: Streamlin... Published: June 9, 2025 Decision rights as protection against optimism bias One reason governance matters is that organisations naturally resist acknowledging that plans have become unrealistic. The National Audit Office has repeatedly identified optimism bias, weak challenge and delayed intervention as recurring causes of poor project performance. Governance is"},{"title":"Critical Thinking","url":"/critical-thinking/","description":"Critical thinking is purposeful, self-regulated judgment, not just scepticism or having sharper opinions.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Critical Thinking What Does Critical Thinking Really Mean? Critical Thinking Critical Thinking What Does Critical Thinking Really Mean? Critical thinking is purposeful, self regulated judgment, not just scepticism or having sharper opinions. Critical thinking is purposeful, self regulated judgment, not just scepticism or having sharper opinions. mechanism Judgment not cynicism Skills and dispositions Explaining the route to conclusions improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking Introduction Critical thinking is best understood as purposeful, self regulated judgement: the deliberate process of deciding what to believe or what to do by interpreting information, analysing reasons, evaluating evidence, drawing inferences, and explaining how the conclusion was reached. That matters because it separates critical thinking from two common impostors: reflexive cynicism and clever opinion making. A critical thinker is not simply someone who doubts everything, wins arguments, or spots flaws quickly. The stronger habit is more disciplined: asking what question is being answered, what evidence is available, what assumptions are being made, what alternatives remain plausible, and how confident the conclusion should be. The influential Delphi Report defined critical thinking in exactly this decision focused way, as “purposeful, self regulatory judgment” directed towards deciding “what to believe or what to do”. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight Assessment... Within improving your thinking and analytical skills , this definition is useful because it makes better thinking visible. It turns “be more critical” into a route: clarify the issue, test the support, compare interpretations, and show why the answer follows. It also makes critical thinking a practice rather than a personality trait. People improve not by becoming permanently suspicious, but by learning when to slow down, how to handle evidence, and how to revise a judgement without treating revision as defeat. Why judgement, not cynicism, is the centre The word “critical” often sounds negative in ordinary speech, as though a critical person is mainly fault finding. In the critical thinking tradition, however, the root idea is closer to discernment: making reliable judgements from reliable information. Monash University’s student guidance puts the point plainly: applying critical thinking does not mean being negative or focusing on faults; it means clarifying your thinking so that you can break down information, interpret it, and arrive at an informed decision or judgement. [Monash University] monash.edu University What is critical thinking? Monash UniversityWhat is critical thinking? Student Academic Success... This distinction is not cosmetic. Cynicism begins with distrust and often ends there. Purposeful judgement begins with a question and moves towards a justified answer. A cynical reader of a health claim, for example, might say, “They are probably lying.” A credulous reader might say, “It sounds scientific, so it is probably true.” A critical thinker asks something more useful: What exactly is being claimed? What kind of evidence would support it? Is the evidence from a controlled study, a personal anecdote, a marketing page, or an expert body? Are there conflicts of interest? How strong should my conclusion be? That is why critical thinking is not the opposite of trust. It is the discipline of proportioning trust to reasons. Sometimes the critical judgement is “this claim is weak”. Sometimes it is “this claim is well supported”. Sometimes it is “the evidence is incomplete, so the responsible answer is provisional”. The outcome is not always rejection; it may be acceptance, suspension of judgement, a narrower conclusion, or a decision to seek better evidence. Historically, this emphasis on judgement links modern critical thinking theory to John Dewey’s older idea of reflective thinking. Dewey described reflective thinking as active, persistent and careful consideration of a belief in light of the grounds supporting it and the further conclusions to which it tends. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy traces critical thinking as an educational goal through Dewey, later assessment work, Bloom’s taxonomy, and Robert Ennis’s influential definition of critical thinking as reasonable reflective thinking focused on deciding what to believe or do. [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy] plato.stanford.edu Encyclopedia of Philosophy Critical Thinking (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)... The historical pattern is revealing. Critical thinking did not develop mainly as a technique for sounding sceptical. It developed as an educational ideal for judgement under uncertainty: how to think carefully enough that belief and action are answerable to reasons. The mechanism: deciding what to believe or do The phrase “purposeful, self regulatory judgement” contains three important claims. First, critical thinking is purposeful . It is directed at a task: understanding a text, judging a policy proposal, diagnosing a problem, deciding whether to trust a source, choosing between options, or explaining a conclusion. Without a purpose, “thinking critically” can become endless commentary. With a purpose, it has a standard of success: did the reasoning help answer the question? Second, it is judgement . Critical thinking does not stop at collecting information. It weighs, compares, and decides. That does not mean rushing to certainty. A good judgement may be tentative, conditional, or limited: “The evidence points this way, but only for this population”; “This explanation is more plausible than the alternatives”; “This is not yet knowable from the available data.” The Delphi Report’s definition connects judgement to interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, and explanation, which means the conclusion should be traceable to the work done along the way. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight Assessment... Third, it is self regulatory . The thinker monitors the process rather than merely applying intelligence outward. Self regulation asks: Am I interpreting the claim fairly? Have I ignored evidence because it is inconvenient? Am I using a double standard? Is my confidence stronger than the evidence? The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy notes that critical thinkers have both abilities and dispositions that lead them to think critically when appropriate, and that the educational goal is not just a one sentence definition but adoption of criteria and standards for careful thinking. [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy] plato.stanford.edu Encyclopedia of Philosophy Critical Thinking (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)... A practical example makes the mechanism clearer. Suppose a manager hears that remote workers are “less productive”. A non critical response might accept or reject the claim based on preference. Purposeful judgement would separate the issue into parts: What does “productive” mean here: output, availability, quality, collaboration, speed, profit, customer satisfaction? What comparison is being made: the same workers before and after remote work, different teams, or industry averages? What evidence is available: measured outcomes, impressions, surveillance metrics, missed deadlines, or isolated complaints? What alternative explanations exist: unclear goals, poor management, weak tools, meeting overload, selection effects? What decision follows: change policy, improve measurement, run a trial, or withhold judgement? The point is not to make the issue complicated for its own sake. It is to prevent the conclusion from arriving before the reasoning. Critical Thinking with Argument Maps Channel: Argumentful · Views: 8.5K · Uploaded: July 2020 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Skills and dispositions must work together Critical thinking has a skill side and a character side. The skill side includes clarifying meanings, identifying arguments, analysing assumptions, evaluating evidence, making inferences, and explaining conclusions. The disposition side is the willingness to use those skills when they are inconvenient, effortful, socially awkward, or personally threatening. The Delphi Report’s expert consensus described the ideal critical thinker as inquisitive, well informed, trustful of reason, open minded, flexible, fair minded in evaluation, honest about personal biases, prudent in judgement, willing to reconsider, clear about issues, orderly in complex matters, diligent in seeking relevant information, and persistent in seeking results as precise as the situation permits. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight Assessment... This is why someone can be verbally sharp yet not especially critical in the deeper"},{"title":"Clear Question","url":"/clear-question/","description":"A clear question keeps critical thinking from becoming endless commentary and makes evidence easier to test.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Critical Thinking","level":3,"text":"Clear Question What Question Are You Really Answering? Clear Question Clear Question What Question Are You Really Answering? A clear question keeps critical thinking from becoming endless commentary and makes evidence easier to test. A clear question keeps critical thinking from becoming endless commentary and makes evidence easier to test. mechanism Why purpose comes before critique How vague questions distort evidence Turning broad doubts into testable questions improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking clear question Introduction Critical thinking begins before evidence is collected or judged. The first task is to identify the precise question that needs answering. Without a clear question, even excellent evidence can be misused because it may answer a different problem from the one actually under consideration. This is why influential definitions of critical thinking describe it as purposeful judgement: thinking is directed towards deciding what to believe or what to do about a specific issue, not simply accumulating facts or criticising ideas in general. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight AssessmentIt defines critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment – the cog... A well defined question gives inquiry a destination. It determines what counts as relevant evidence, what standards should be used to evaluate that evidence, and what kind of conclusion would genuinely answer the problem. Conversely, a vague or shifting question encourages endless debate, selective use of evidence, and arguments in which participants appear to disagree even though they are answering different questions. Why purpose comes before critique Purposeful judgement is impossible without knowing the object of the judgement. Before asking whether evidence is convincing, a critical thinker asks: What exactly am I trying to decide? This may seem obvious, yet many reasoning failures arise because people move directly to supporting or attacking claims without agreeing on the question. The Delphi Report's description of critical thinking includes interpretation as one of its core skills precisely because understanding the issue precedes evaluating it. Interpretation involves clarifying meaning, identifying the problem, and recognising what is actually being asked before analysing reasons or drawing conclusions. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight AssessmentIt defines critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment – the cog... Consider the statement: \"Artificial intelligence is changing education.\" Before looking for evidence, several different questions could be hidden inside it: Is AI improving examination results? Is it making cheating easier? Is it changing how teachers work? Should schools encourage or restrict its use? Is the change beneficial overall? Each question demands different evidence. Statistics about plagiarism cannot answer whether AI improves learning. Teacher surveys cannot settle whether students' understanding has improved. Without clarification, people often present perfectly accurate evidence that nevertheless fails to answer the question under discussion. Purpose therefore acts as a filter. It prevents inquiry from expanding into every related issue and keeps judgement proportional to the decision being made. How To Identify The Problem In Case Study Case Study Analysis Solutions Case Study Solution Channel: TheCaseSolutions com Open on YouTube How vague questions distort evidence Evidence does not possess meaning independently of a question. The same information may support one conclusion, weaken another, or be entirely irrelevant depending on what is being asked. Several common reasoning errors arise from poorly defined questions. Answering an easier question. Psychologists have observed that people often substitute a difficult question with one that feels easier to answer. Instead of asking, \"Is this policy effective?\", they unconsciously answer, \"Do I like the people proposing it?\" The evidence collected then reflects the substitute question rather than the original decision. Mixing descriptive and evaluative questions. A discussion may begin by asking whether something happened but gradually become an argument about whether it was morally acceptable. Historical evidence can establish events, but it cannot alone determine ethical judgement. Confusing these different questions produces unnecessary disagreement. Changing the question during debate. Someone challenged to support a broad claim may quietly retreat to a narrower version once evidence becomes difficult. For example: Original question: \"Does this treatment cure depression?\" Revised question: \"Can this treatment help some people under certain conditions?\" The second question may have a positive answer while the first does not. Unless the shift is recognised, participants may believe a claim has been supported when, in reality, the claim itself has changed. Collecting evidence without stopping criteria. If the question remains vague, there is no obvious point at which enough evidence has been gathered. Inquiry becomes an endless search because success has never been defined. These distortions explain why careful thinkers spend time refining questions before evaluating sources. Turning broad doubts into testable questions Many everyday problems begin as broad feelings of uncertainty rather than clear questions. Instead of asking: \"Is social media bad?\" \"Can you trust the news?\" \"Is remote working better?\" critical thinkers transform them into questions that evidence can realistically answer. A useful sequence is: 1. Identify the decision. What belief or action depends on the answer? 2. Define the key terms. What exactly counts as \"bad\", \"trust\", or \"better\"? 3. Specify the scope. Which people, places, or situations are being discussed? 4. Choose the comparison. Better than what alternative? 5. Identify the standard of success. What evidence would genuinely change the conclusion? For example, \"Is remote working better?\" might become: \"For software developers working full time, does remote working improve productivity over a six month period compared with office based work?\" Although narrower, this question is far easier to investigate. Relevant evidence becomes clearer, competing explanations can be examined, and conclusions can be appropriately limited. The goal is not to make questions artificially complicated but to make them answerable. How to Master Problem Solving Skills: 5 Steps & Root Cause Analysis Channel: iCertGlobal Open on YouTube Questions that improve evidence quality Experienced critical thinkers often use clarification questions before reading studies, reports, or arguments. These questions reduce the risk of evaluating evidence against the wrong standard. Useful examples include: What decision depends on this answer? What is the precise claim being made? Which part of the claim is uncertain? How would I recognise a satisfactory answer? What evidence would count against my current view? Am I trying to explain, predict , compare, or decide? These questions shape the investigation before evidence begins to accumulate. They also reveal hidden assumptions that might otherwise remain unnoticed. For instance, asking whether a medicine \"works\" assumes agreement about what counts as success. Is the aim complete recovery, symptom reduction, longer survival, improved quality of life, or fewer side effects? Each standard points towards different evidence. A practical example Imagine reading the headline: \"Coffee reduces the risk of heart disease.\" A rapid reaction might be either acceptance or scepticism. A more disciplined approach begins by clarifying the question. Possible interpretations include: Does coffee directly reduce heart disease? Is coffee associated with lower rates in observational studies? Does the effect apply to everyone or only certain groups? Compared with drinking no coffee or with sugary drinks? How large is the reduction in risk? Only after these questions are clarified does it become possible to judge whether the evidence is appropriate. A randomised clinical trial, a long term observational study, or laboratory research each answers different kinds of questions and carries different strengths and limitations. The clarification stage therefore protects against both overconfidence and unnecessary scepticism. Problem Solving and Decision Making Channel: Prof. Suresh Bada Math Open on YouTube Clear questions lead to better judgement Purposeful judgement is not simply about evaluating evidence carefully; it is about evaluating evidence for the right question. A clearly defined question narrows the inquiry, identifies relevant standards, exposes hidden assumptions, and prevents discussions from drifting into unrelated issues."},{"title":"Thinking Habits","url":"/thinking-habits/","description":"Critical-thinking skills matter most when dispositions like truth-seeking, open-mindedness, and systematicity make people use them.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Critical Thinking","level":3,"text":"Thinking Habits Why Smart People Still Need Thinking Habits Thinking Habits Thinking Habits Why Smart People Still Need Thinking Habits Critical thinking skills matter most when dispositions like truth seeking, open mindedness, and systematicity make people use them. Critical thinking skills matter most when dispositions like truth seeking, open mindedness, and systematicity make people use them. policy intervention The difference between ability and willingness Seven dispositions that support better judgement Negative habits that make sharp reasoning unreliable improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking thinking habits Introduction Critical thinking is often described as a set of skills: analysing arguments, evaluating evidence, recognising assumptions , and drawing justified conclusions. Yet those skills only improve judgement when people are willing to use them, especially when doing so is uncomfortable or inconvenient. A person may know how to detect weak evidence but still ignore it if it challenges a cherished belief, threatens their status, or requires admitting a mistake. Research on critical thinking has therefore distinguished between ability and disposition : the habits of mind that determine whether critical thinking skills are actually put into practice. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov Facione, Peter A. TITLE. Critical Thinking: A Statement of Expert Consensus for Purposes of Educational Assessment and. Instruction. Rese... This distinction matters because many real world failures of judgement are not caused by an absence of reasoning ability. They arise when capable people stop asking difficult questions, become overconfident, search only for confirming evidence, or rush to conclusions. Purposeful judgement therefore depends on cultivating thinking habits that consistently direct attention towards evidence, fairness, and self correction rather than towards comfort or certainty. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight AssessmentCritical Thinking: What It Is and Why It CountsHow do you define \"critical thinking\"? How do you use it in your day to... The difference between ability and willingness Knowing how to think critically is not the same as choosing to think critically. The expert consensus behind the Delphi Report describes critical thinking as both a collection of cognitive skills and a corresponding \"critical spirit\" or disposition. In practice , these two components reinforce one another. Skills provide the tools for analysing information, while dispositions determine whether those tools are used carefully, consistently, and honestly. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov Facione, Peter A. TITLE. Critical Thinking: A Statement of Expert Consensus for Purposes of Educational Assessment and. Instruction. Rese... Consider two people reading a controversial health claim. Both understand how to assess evidence. One immediately accepts information that supports existing beliefs without checking sources. The other deliberately looks for contrary evidence, considers alternative explanations, and is prepared to revise an opinion if stronger evidence appears. Their reasoning ability may be similar, but their dispositions lead to very different outcomes . This explains why educational researchers increasingly treat critical thinking as partly motivational. The question is not only Can this person evaluate evidence? but also Will they do so when it is inconvenient, emotionally uncomfortable, or socially costly? Studies of critical thinking disposition repeatedly find that willingness to engage with evidence predicts whether reasoning skills are consistently applied in academic, professional, and clinical settings. [ResearchGate+2PubMed] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The Disposition Toward Critical Thinking24 Dec 2025 — This essay presents a researchbased expert consensus definition o... Ep. 124 Cognitive Energy and Critical Thinking with Dr. Christopher Dwyer Channel: Social Engineer, LLC Open on YouTube Seven dispositions that support better judgement The Delphi project and subsequent work on the California Critical Thinking Disposition Inventory identified seven broad habits that characterise people who regularly use critical thinking skills well. These are not fixed personality traits but patterns of behaviour that can be encouraged through education and deliberate practice . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Critical thinking disposition as a measure of competent...by NC Facione · 1994 · Cited by 1288 — Delphi Report's consensus definit... Truth seeking. A commitment to following evidence wherever it leads rather than defending existing opinions. Truth seeking requires asking difficult questions, examining contrary evidence, and preferring accuracy over being right. Open mindedness. A willingness to consider alternative viewpoints fairly, including arguments that conflict with one's own beliefs. Open mindedness does not require accepting every idea equally; it requires evaluating competing ideas by their evidence. Analyticity. Looking for reasons, recognising potential weaknesses in arguments, and anticipating possible problems before reaching conclusions instead of relying on intuition alone. Systematicity. Approaching problems in an organised and methodical way. Rather than jumping between ideas, systematic thinkers gather information, compare alternatives, and work through evidence step by step. Critical thinking self confidence. Confidence in one's ability to reason carefully without confusing confidence with certainty. This encourages independent judgement while remaining open to correction. Inquisitiveness. Intellectual curiosity that motivates learning beyond immediate practical necessity. Curious people actively seek understanding instead of stopping once a minimally acceptable answer appears. Cognitive maturity. Recognition that complex problems rarely have perfectly simple answers. Mature thinkers tolerate uncertainty, understand that reasonable disagreement is possible, and proportion conclusions to the available evidence. [journals.healio.com+2PubMed] journals.healio.com Critical Thinking Disposition as a Measureby NC Facione · 1994 · Cited by 1288 — Seven CT dispositional subscales were created: inquisiti... These dispositions complement one another. Open mindedness without analyticity may lead to entertaining every claim equally. Analyticity without truth seeking may become sophisticated rationalisation. Systematicity without inquisitiveness can become rigid procedure. Reliable judgement emerges from their interaction rather than from any single habit. Why uncomfortable evidence is the real test Many reasoning tasks are straightforward when evidence supports existing beliefs. Dispositions become most important when evidence points elsewhere. Truth seeking often requires admitting previous mistakes . Open mindedness may require giving serious attention to ideas associated with rival groups or unpopular viewpoints. Cognitive maturity demands accepting that some questions remain uncertain even after careful investigation. These moments expose the difference between genuine critical thinking and motivated reasoning. Motivated reasoning occurs when people unconsciously use their intelligence to defend preferred conclusions instead of evaluating evidence impartially. Research in psychology consistently shows that higher reasoning ability alone does not eliminate this tendency. In some situations, more knowledgeable people become better at constructing arguments for conclusions they already favour rather than becoming more objective. Strong critical thinking dispositions help interrupt that process by redirecting attention towards evidence instead of identity or emotion. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight AssessmentCritical Thinking: What It Is and Why It CountsHow do you define \"critical thinking\"? How do you use it in your day to... In professional environments, this matters because many costly errors develop gradually rather than through obvious mistakes. Teams that encourage questioning assumptions, inviting criticism, and revisiting decisions are often more reliable than equally skilled teams that discourage dissent. Negative habits that make sharp reasoning unreliable High intelligence can coexist with poor judgement when unhelpful thinking habits dominate. Several dispositions consistently undermine the practical value of reasoning skills. Confirmation seeking. Looking mainly for evidence that supports existing beliefs while ignoring conflicting information. Overconfidence. Assuming initial conclusions are probably correct without checking competing explanations. Defensive reasoning. Treating disagreement as a personal attack rather than an opportunity to improve understanding. Premature closure. Ending investigation once an acceptable explanation appears instead of asking whether better evidence exists. Intellectual laziness. Relying on shortcuts, slogans, or authority"},{"title":"Critical Trust","url":"/critical-trust/","description":"Critical thinking is not automatic distrust; it means matching trust to the quality of the reasons available.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Critical Thinking","level":3,"text":"Critical Trust Is Doubt Helping or Just Hardening? Critical Trust Critical Trust Is Doubt Helping or Just Hardening? Critical thinking is not automatic distrust; it means matching trust to the quality of the reasons available. Critical thinking is not automatic distrust; it means matching trust to the quality of the reasons available. mechanism How cynicism differs from judgement When acceptance is the critical conclusion Using confidence levels instead of yes or no answers improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking critical trust Introduction Critical thinking is often confused with permanent doubt, but the two are not the same. The aim of purposeful judgement is not to reject claims by default but to match your level of trust to the quality of the available reasons. Sometimes the most critical conclusion is that a claim is well supported and should be accepted. At other times the evidence is too weak, conflicting, or incomplete, making a qualified judgement more appropriate than either belief or rejection. This distinction matters because reflexive cynicism can be as misleading as unquestioning acceptance. A disciplined thinker adjusts confidence to evidence instead of treating every claim as either certainly true or certainly false. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe “Delphi Report” has come to be regarded internationally as a seminal document... Is doubt helping or just hardening? Healthy doubt asks questions that can be answered. Cynicism often assumes the answer before the investigation begins. A critically minded person asks: What exactly is being claimed? What evidence supports it? Are there credible alternative explanations? How confident should I be given what I know? A cynical person often begins from a different premise: \"They are probably lying.\" \"Experts always have an agenda.\" \"Nothing can be trusted.\" The difference is subtle but important. Critical thinking treats evidence as capable of changing one's mind. Cynicism frequently treats distrust itself as evidence. If no possible evidence could increase your confidence in a claim, you are no longer evaluating it—you are defending a fixed position. The influential Delphi Report describes critical thinking as \"purposeful, self regulatory judgement\" involving interpretation, analysis, evaluation and reflective self correction. The inclusion of self correction is significant: good judgement must remain open to revision when better evidence appears. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe “Delphi Report” has come to be regarded internationally as a seminal document... How cynicism differs from disciplined judgement Although both scepticism and cynicism question claims, they operate very differently. Disciplined judgementCynicismBegins with uncertaintyBegins with distrustSeeks evidence before decidingTreats suspicion as sufficientCan accept, reject or suspend judgementTends towards rejection regardless of evidenceChanges with new informationOften resists revisionDistinguishes stronger from weaker sourcesTreats all sources as equally suspect This distinction explains why critical thinkers may sometimes appear trusting. They are not trusting because they avoid scrutiny; they are trusting because scrutiny has produced adequate reasons for confidence. Research in psychology also suggests that cynicism should not be mistaken for scientific scepticism. Studies have found that scientific scepticism—evaluating claims against evidence—is associated with lower endorsement of unsupported beliefs, whereas general cynicism does not reliably produce better judgement and may even accompany greater acceptance of some unsubstantiated beliefs. Simply distrusting people is therefore not the same as evaluating claims carefully. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Skepticism, cynicism, and cognitive style predictors of the...January 4, 2022 — Discussion centers on whether cynicism and s... Published: January 4, 2022 Why Don’t People Think? Critical Thinking in an Age of Reaction Channel: Zero Distortion with Russ Bates Open on YouTube When acceptance is the critical conclusion One of the most common misunderstandings about critical thinking is the belief that a \"critical\" person should always find flaws. In reality, careful evaluation often supports accepting a claim. For example: A medicine supported by multiple well designed clinical trials deserves more confidence than one promoted through testimonials alone. A scientific finding independently replicated by different research groups deserves greater trust than a single preliminary study. An official statistic with transparent methods generally deserves more confidence than an anonymous social media graphic with no identifiable source. Acceptance becomes the critical conclusion when the evidence is: relevant to the claim; collected using reliable methods; independently confirmed where possible; stronger than the competing explanations. Rejecting well supported conclusions merely to appear independent is not evidence of critical thinking. It is another form of bias. Purposeful judgement therefore asks not, \"Can I find a reason to doubt this?\" but, \"How much confidence is justified by the evidence available?\" Why all or nothing thinking causes problems Real world evidence rarely produces absolute certainty. Many important decisions—from choosing a medical treatment to evaluating economic forecasts—must be made under uncertainty. Critical thinking therefore works better with degrees of confidence than with simple yes or no categories. Philosophers have long recognised suspension of judgement as a legitimate intellectual position alongside belief and disbelief. When evidence is insufficient, withholding commitment is often the most rational response rather than forcing a premature conclusion. [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy] plato.stanford.edu Encyclopedia of Philosophy Skepticism Stanford Encyclopedia of PhilosophySkepticism Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophyby J Comesaña · 2001 · Cited by 77 — Philosophically i... All or nothing thinking creates predictable errors: treating incomplete evidence as proof; demanding impossible certainty before accepting anything; confusing low confidence with zero confidence; treating uncertainty as evidence against a claim. These habits encourage overconfidence in both believers and doubters. Cynicism vs. Skepticism: Why One Makes You Easier to Manipulate Channel: Allen Stafford — Think Sharper Open on YouTube Using confidence levels instead of yes or no answers A more reliable habit is to estimate confidence rather than asking whether something is simply true or false. Instead of saying: \"This must be true.\" consider: \"The current evidence makes this highly likely.\" Instead of saying: \"That cannot be trusted.\" consider: \"The available evidence is weak, so my confidence is low.\" Confidence can increase or decrease as better information becomes available. A practical scale might look like this: Very high confidence: supported by multiple independent, high quality sources. Moderate confidence: generally supported but with recognised limitations or unanswered questions. Low confidence: limited, conflicting or indirect evidence. No justified conclusion: insufficient information to favour one explanation over another. Thinking this way reduces unnecessary certainty while avoiding the paralysis of believing nothing. Practical habits for critical trust Developing disciplined trust is less about becoming more suspicious than about becoming more proportionate. Useful habits include: Separate the claim from the person making it. Look for evidence before looking for confirmation of your existing opinion. Ask what evidence would change your mind. Distinguish confidence from certainty. Revise conclusions when stronger evidence appears. Accept well supported claims without treating acceptance as intellectual weakness. These habits keep judgement flexible rather than defensive. Evaluating Arguments – How to Spot Strong and Weak Claims Channel: NUITEQ Chorus Educational K 12 Content Open on YouTube The balance worth aiming for Purposeful judgement occupies the space between credulity and cynicism. It recognises that some claims deserve confidence, some deserve rejection, and many deserve provisional acceptance while evidence continues to develop. The defining feature of critical thinking is therefore not relentless doubt but proportional trust: believing as strongly as the reasons justify, remaining willing to revise those beliefs, and resisting the temptation to mistake permanent suspicion for intellectual rigour. [Insight Assessment+2Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe Delphi Report Insight AssessmentThe “Delphi Report”"},{"title":"Delphi Standards","url":"/delphi-standards/","description":"The Delphi Report gives critical thinking a practical standard: interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, and explanation.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Critical Thinking","level":3,"text":"Delphi Standards What Counts as a Good Critical Judgement? Delphi Standards Delphi Standards What Counts as a Good Critical Judgement? The Delphi Report gives critical thinking a practical standard: interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, and explanation. The Delphi Report gives critical thinking a practical standard: interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, and explanation. dataset evidence The core judgement skills in the Delphi definition Why explanation matters after inference How the ideal critical thinker behaves improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking delphi standards Introduction The American Philosophical Association's Delphi Report remains one of the most influential attempts to define what counts as good critical judgement. Rather than treating critical thinking as intelligence, scepticism, or debate skill, the report defines it as purposeful, self regulatory judgement aimed at deciding what to believe or what to do . That judgement is not a single mental act but a coordinated process involving interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, and explanation, guided by reflective self correction. The definition emerged from a structured Delphi study in which experts from philosophy, education, psychology and related disciplines worked towards consensus on the skills and dispositions that should underpin critical thinking education and assessment. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICFebruary 20, 2011 — by PA Facione · 1990 · Cited by 1116 — We understand critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment... Published: February 20, 2011 Within the broader idea of purposeful judgement, the Delphi standards are valuable because they make good thinking observable. Instead of asking whether someone is \"a critical thinker\", they ask whether a judgement has been reached through identifiable reasoning processes and whether the reasoning can be explained and revised when necessary. What Counts as a Good Critical Judgement? The Delphi Report does not describe critical thinking as a collection of isolated techniques. It presents judgement as an integrated process in which several cognitive skills support one another. A strong judgement is one that is based on evidence, sensitive to context, open to revision, and capable of being justified to others. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICFebruary 20, 2011 — by PA Facione · 1990 · Cited by 1116 — We understand critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment... Published: February 20, 2011 The report identifies six core cognitive skills. Interpretation – understanding the meaning of information, experiences, data or events. Before evaluating evidence, a thinker must first determine what is actually being claimed or observed. Analysis – identifying arguments, assumptions , relationships and reasons. Analysis separates conclusions from supporting evidence and distinguishes relevant from irrelevant information. Evaluation – assessing the credibility of sources and the strength of arguments. This includes judging whether evidence genuinely supports a conclusion and recognising weaknesses or uncertainty. Inference – drawing reasonable conclusions from available evidence while recognising the limits of what the evidence can justify. Explanation – clearly stating both the conclusion and the reasoning that produced it, including the evidence, methods and standards relied upon. Self regulation – monitoring one's own thinking, recognising possible bias or error, and revising judgements when new evidence warrants it. Although often highlighted separately in later summaries, reflective self correction is built into the report's overall definition of critical thinking. [ERIC+2Insight Assessment] files.eric.ed.gov ERICFebruary 20, 2011 — by PA Facione · 1990 · Cited by 1116 — We understand critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment... Published: February 20, 2011 These skills are intended to work together rather than in sequence. Interpretation informs analysis; analysis supports evaluation; evaluation shapes inference; explanation communicates the judgement; self regulation continually checks every stage. 5 tips to improve your critical thinking Samantha Agoos Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Why Explanation Matters After Inference One of the Delphi Report's distinctive contributions is its insistence that arriving at a conclusion is not enough. A judgement becomes intellectually accountable only when the thinker can explain why that conclusion follows from the available evidence. The report therefore treats explanation as more than communication. Explanation requires identifying the evidential, conceptual, methodological, contextual or evaluative considerations that justify the judgement. If two people reach the same conclusion for entirely different reasons, the quality of their critical thinking may differ substantially because only one may be able to demonstrate sound reasoning. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICFebruary 20, 2011 — by PA Facione · 1990 · Cited by 1116 — We understand critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment... Published: February 20, 2011 This emphasis has practical consequences. It exposes hidden assumptions. It allows others to evaluate the reasoning instead of merely accepting the conclusion. It makes disagreement more productive because people can compare reasons rather than simply opposing opinions. It supports learning, since weak reasoning becomes visible and therefore improvable. In educational settings, this explains why assessments of critical thinking often ask students to justify answers rather than merely selecting the correct option. The explanation reveals the quality of judgement that the final answer alone cannot show. The Ideal Critical Thinker Is More Than a Skilled Reasoner The Delphi project did not stop with defining cognitive skills. The expert panel also described the personal dispositions associated with high quality judgement. Their \"ideal critical thinker\" combines reasoning ability with habits of mind that encourage those abilities to be used consistently. [Insight Assessment] insightassessment.com Insight Assessment The Delphi Report This human cognitive process of reflectively deciding what to believe or what to do...Read more... According to the consensus description, an ideal critical thinker is characterised by qualities such as: inquisitiveness and intellectual curiosity; being well informed while recognising the limits of one's knowledge; trust in reasoned inquiry rather than impulse or authority alone; open mindedness towards alternative viewpoints; flexibility when considering different possibilities; fairness in evaluating competing arguments; honesty about personal biases and assumptions; willingness to reconsider conclusions when evidence changes; precision where the situation demands it; persistence when problems are complex. These characteristics matter because technical reasoning skills alone do not guarantee sound judgement. Someone may be highly capable of analysing arguments yet consistently ignore evidence that challenges existing beliefs. The Delphi panel therefore argued that effective critical thinking depends on both competence and disposition: knowing how to think critically and being willing to do so. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICFebruary 20, 2011 — by PA Facione · 1990 · Cited by 1116 — We understand critical thinking to be purposeful, self regulatory judgment... Published: February 20, 2011 Teach Critical Thinking Channel: GrapplingIgnorance Open on YouTube Why the Delphi Standards Changed Critical Thinking Education Before the Delphi project, educators often agreed that critical thinking was important but disagreed about exactly what should be taught or assessed. The Delphi study used a structured expert consensus method to identify shared principles across disciplines, producing a practical framework that could guide curriculum design and assessment. [PhilPapers] philpapers.org Peter Facione, Critical Thinking: A Statement of Expert...by P Facione · 1990 · Cited by 6816 — This is the full version of th... Its influence can be seen in several areas: university critical thinking courses organised around identifiable reasoning skills; [faculty.uml.edu] faculty.uml.edu The APA Delphi Report. Critical Thinking: A Statement of Expert. Consensus...Read more... assessment instruments designed to measure interpretation, analysis, evaluation and inference rather than factual recall alone; professional education, particularly in fields such as healthcare, where judgement under uncertainty is central to practice ; later research that expanded the framework by examining both cognitive skills and thinking dispositions. [faculty.uml.edu+2Insight Assessment] faculty.uml.edu The APA Delphi Report. Critical Thinking: A Statement of Expert. Consensus...Read more... Although researchers continue to debate the best ways to measure or teach critical thinking,"},{"title":"Remote Work","url":"/remote-work/","description":"The remote-work productivity debate shows why critical thinkers define terms, compare evidence, and test alternative explanations.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Critical Thinking","level":3,"text":"Remote Work Are Remote Workers Really Less Productive? Remote Work Remote Work Are Remote Workers Really Less Productive? The remote work productivity debate shows why critical thinkers define terms, compare evidence, and test alternative explanations. The remote work productivity debate shows why critical thinkers define terms, compare evidence, and test alternative explanations. population context Defining productivity before measuring it Separating data from workplace impressions Alternative explanations that change the decision improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking remote work Introduction Are remote workers really less productive? The strongest evidence suggests that there is no simple yes or no answer. Productivity depends on what is being measured, which jobs are being examined, how work is organised, and whether the comparison is between fully remote, hybrid, or office based work. That makes the debate an excellent test of purposeful judgement. Rather than accepting confident claims from either side, a critical thinker begins by defining terms, comparing like with like, and asking whether alternative explanations better account for the evidence. The remote work debate demonstrates a central lesson in critical thinking: disagreements often arise not because people are looking at different facts, but because they are measuring different things. Before deciding whether remote work \"works\", it is necessary to ask what \"productive\" actually means. Defining productivity before measuring it One of the most common reasoning errors in workplace debates is treating productivity as though it were a single, obvious quantity. In reality, it can refer to several different outcomes : Individual output, such as completed sales calls, software features, reports or cases processed. Team performance, including coordination, quality of decisions and speed of delivery. Organisational performance, such as profitability, innovation, customer satisfaction or revenue. Long term capability, including employee retention, training, mentoring and knowledge sharing. These measures do not always move together. A remote worker may complete more individual tasks because there are fewer interruptions, while a team may find brainstorming or onboarding new colleagues more difficult. Both observations can be true simultaneously. Evidence from the UK and US labour statistics and research reviews reflects this complexity. Randomised experiments at individual firms have often found small positive effects of remote or hybrid work on measurable individual productivity alongside reduced staff turnover, but these findings cannot automatically be generalised to every occupation or organisation. [Bureau of Labor Statistics] bls.gov remote work productivity Bureau of Labor StatisticsThe rise in remote work since the pandemic and its impact...Oct 31, 2024 — A few randomized experiments at ind... For a critical thinker, the first question is therefore not \"Is remote work productive?\" but \"Productive according to which definition?\" Separating data from workplace impressions The debate is also a reminder that personal impressions are weaker evidence than carefully designed comparisons. Managers may sincerely believe employees are less productive because they cannot directly observe them. Employees may sincerely believe they are more productive because they experience fewer interruptions and less commuting stress . Neither perception is sufficient evidence on its own. Better evidence comes from studies that compare similar workers under controlled conditions. One influential example is the randomised trial conducted at Trip.com, where more than 1,600 employees were randomly assigned either to hybrid working or to full time office attendance. The study found that employees working from home two days each week maintained similar performance and promotion rates while experiencing substantially lower resignation rates and greater job satisfaction. Because assignment was random, the researchers could attribute differences more confidently to the work arrangement rather than to employee preferences or ability. [Nature] nature.com productivity, hybrid remote work can leave performance unchanged.Read more... Earlier work by the same research group studying call centre employees found a sizeable increase in measured performance during full time home working, largely because workers took fewer breaks and experienced fewer distractions in quieter environments. However, that improvement occurred in a highly structured job with easily measured output, illustrating why evidence from one occupation should not automatically be extended to another. [Stanford Graduate School of Business] gsb.stanford.edu Graduate School of Business Does Working from Home Work? Evidence from a Chinese...Home working led to a 13% performance increase, of which about 9% was from working more minutes per shift (few... The lesson for critical thinking is that anecdotes —even thousands of them—cannot substitute for research designs that reduce bias. Nicholas Bloom on why Remote Work is Good for Growth Channel: IMF PODCASTS Open on YouTube Why different studies reach different conclusions Conflicting headlines do not necessarily indicate that researchers disagree. Often they are answering different questions. Several factors commonly explain why studies report different findings: Different occupations. Measuring productivity is relatively straightforward for call centres or administrative processing but much harder for research, design, management or creative work. Different time horizons. A worker may produce more today while a company loses opportunities for mentoring, innovation or relationship building over several years. Different work arrangements. Fully remote work is not the same as hybrid work. Many studies find hybrid arrangements outperform either extreme because they combine focused work at home with regular face to face collaboration. [OECD+2Stanford News] oecd.org 7fe47de2 en the role of telework for productivity during and post covid ...by C CRISCUOLO · 2021 · Cited by 174 — Respondents, on average, find... Different outcome measures. One study may focus on output, another on retention, another on collaboration or well being. A critical judgement therefore depends on recognising that apparently contradictory findings may simply be measuring different aspects of organisational performance. Alternative explanations that change the decision Purposeful judgement also requires asking whether another explanation fits the evidence better than the obvious one. Suppose a company reports falling productivity after adopting remote work. Several explanations are possible: Remote working genuinely reduced effectiveness. Managers introduced remote work during a broader organisational disruption. [bls.gov] bls.gov remote work productivity Bureau of Labor StatisticsThe rise in remote work since the pandemic and its impact...Oct 31, 2024 — A few randomized experiments at ind... Employees were adapting to unfamiliar digital tools. Economic conditions reduced demand. New recruits received weaker mentoring. Performance measures changed. Similarly, higher productivity among remote workers may not result solely from working at home. Employees choosing remote roles may already be more experienced, more autonomous or better suited to independent work. Researchers therefore value experiments, natural experiments and statistical methods that account for these competing explanations rather than simply comparing one group with another. Studies examining collaboration during the pandemic illustrate this point well: simple before and after comparisons suggested collaboration increased, but more careful causal analysis showed that broader pandemic effects and changing work patterns explained much of the difference, with remote working itself shifting effort towards more focused individual work and less spontaneous collaboration. [arXiv] arxiv.org How Work From Home Affects Collaboration: A Large Scale Study of Information Workers in a Natural Experiment During COVID 19July 30... The critical thinking habit is to ask not merely \"What happened?\" but \"What else could explain what happened?\" Remote work, productivity and innovation Nicholas Bloom Innoscape Talk 6.1 Channel: CIEB Universität Basel Open on YouTube Hidden assumptions behind strong opinions The intensity of the remote work debate often comes from assumptions that remain unstated. [bls.gov] bls.gov remote work productivity Bureau of Labor StatisticsThe rise in remote work since the pandemic and its impact...Oct 31, 2024 — A few randomized experiments at ind... For example: Seeing employees means they are working. Collaboration always requires physical presence. Quiet concentration is always more valuable than spontaneous interaction. All jobs require similar patterns of communication. Every organisation has identical goals. Once these"},{"title":"Self Checks","url":"/self-checks-dc6ba6/","description":"Self-regulation turns critical thinking inward by asking whether your own standards are fair, consistent, and evidence-led.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Critical Thinking","level":3,"text":"Self Checks How to Catch Your Own Double Standards Self Checks Self Checks How to Catch Your Own Double Standards Self regulation turns critical thinking inward by asking whether your own standards are fair, consistent, and evidence led. Self regulation turns critical thinking inward by asking whether your own standards are fair, consistent, and evidence led. mechanism Bias checks inside the thinking process Questions that expose uneven standards Revising confidence without losing face improving your thinking and analytical skills critical thinking self checks Introduction Purposeful judgement in critical thinking is not only about evaluating other people's claims. It also requires monitoring your own reasoning while you are using it. This process is known as self regulation : deliberately checking whether your standards are consistent, whether you are giving evidence the weight it deserves, and whether your confidence still matches what you actually know. Rather than assuming bias only affects other people, self regulation assumes that every thinker is vulnerable to predictable distortions such as confirmation bias, motivated reasoning and overconfidence . The practical goal is not to become perfectly objective, but to catch these distortions early enough to improve the quality of a judgement before acting on it. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement by KE Stanovich · 2023 · Cited by 133 — Actively open minded thinking (AOT) is measured by items that tap the willingness to consider... Within critical thinking, self regulation is the mechanism that turns good intentions into reliable decisions. It asks not, \"Can I find flaws in someone else's argument?\" but, \"Am I applying the same standards to my own conclusion?\" Bias checks inside the thinking process Self regulation is a continuous monitoring process rather than a final inspection. Instead of waiting until a decision has been made, effective thinkers repeatedly compare their reasoning against a few simple questions: Have I looked for evidence that could prove me wrong? Would I accept this evidence if it supported the opposite conclusion? Am I interpreting ambiguous information differently because of what I already believe? Is my confidence greater than the strength of the available evidence? These questions interrupt automatic thinking before it hardens into certainty. Research on metacognition —the ability to think about one's own thinking—shows that people who monitor the quality of their reasoning are generally better at recognising uncertainty and adjusting their confidence appropriately. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMetacognitive sensitivity: The key to calibrating trust by D Lee · 2025 · Cited by 25 — Metacognitive sensitivity, measured by meta d', estimates how effectively confidence judgments disting... The aim is not constant self doubt. Instead, it is calibration: making confidence proportional to evidence. A well calibrated thinker can be highly confident when evidence is strong and openly uncertain when evidence is incomplete. Why confirmation bias survives intelligence One of the most important findings in cognitive psychology is that intelligence alone does not eliminate bias. Confirmation bias encourages people to seek, remember and interpret information that supports an existing belief while discounting conflicting evidence. Because this often happens unconsciously, simply intending to be objective is usually insufficient. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCStop Fooling Yourself! Diagnosing and Treating Confirmation...by RT Born · 2024 · Cited by 9 — Confirmation bias (CB) is a cognitive bias that allows us to fo... Self regulation works by inserting deliberate pauses into reasoning: separate gathering evidence from interpreting it; actively search for disconfirming information; delay commitment to a preferred explanation until competing explanations have been considered. Studies of judicial decision making illustrate this principle clearly. Once judges or investigators form an early theory of a case, later evidence is more likely to be interpreted in ways that reinforce that theory. Deliberately separating evidence collection from conclusion formation reduces this risk. [Erasmus University Rotterdam] pure.eur.nl Repub 124724 Erasmus University RotterdamEUR Research Information PortalToday — by S van Brussel · 2020 · Cited by 42 — The confirmation bias appears... Implicit Bias and Self Regulation Channel: Purdue University Diversity Open on YouTube Questions that expose uneven standards Double standards are often easier to detect through comparison than through introspection. Instead of asking whether your reasoning is \"objective\", compare how you treat similar situations. Useful self checks include: Would I accept this evidence if it supported the opposite side? Many reasoning errors become visible when beliefs are reversed. If identical evidence suddenly seems weak only because it favours a different conclusion, your evaluation may be driven by preference rather than evidence. Am I demanding different levels of proof? People frequently require overwhelming evidence before changing a favoured belief while accepting relatively weak evidence for beliefs they already hold. Self regulation looks for symmetry in evidential standards. Have I considered realistic alternatives? Actively generating competing explanations weakens the tendency to stop searching once the first plausible answer appears. Research on actively open minded thinking consistently links willingness to consider alternatives with better judgement and more accurate confidence calibration. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentThe role of actively open minded thinking in information...by U Haran · 2013 · Cited by 427 — We... Could someone explain my conclusion without mentioning the evidence? If the explanation depends mainly on identity, emotion, loyalty or preference, evidence may be playing a smaller role than assumed. These questions are effective because they test the reasoning process itself rather than merely inspecting its outcome. Revising confidence without losing face One reason people resist correcting themselves is psychological rather than intellectual. Changing a judgement can feel like admitting failure or inconsistency. Self regulation reframes revision as evidence that the thinking process is functioning properly. Critical thinkers distinguish between: changing an opinion because new evidence appeared; changing confidence because evidence became stronger or weaker; abandoning a conclusion because its supporting assumptions proved false. These are signs of disciplined reasoning, not weakness. Research on overconfidence shows that people commonly overestimate the accuracy of their own judgements across domains ranging from medicine to business and everyday decision making. Better performance often comes not from becoming less confident overall, but from matching confidence more closely to actual accuracy. [APA] apa.org This bias is thought to be robust...Read more... A useful habit is expressing conclusions in graduated language: \"The evidence strongly supports...\" \"The balance of evidence currently suggests...\" \"There are several plausible explanations...\" \"I do not yet know.\" This approach preserves intellectual honesty without requiring false certainty or unnecessary hesitation. Why Metacognition Is the ONLY Way to Beat a Master Manipulator (2026) Channel: Dark Psyche Zone Open on YouTube Building habits that reduce bias over time Self regulation is more effective when it becomes routine rather than occasional. Research on metacognition and actively open minded thinking suggests several habits that strengthen resistance to biased reasoning: Seek contradiction deliberately. Before finalising a judgement, spend time looking specifically for credible evidence that challenges it rather than only confirming it. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCStop Fooling Yourself! Diagnosing and Treating Confirmation...by RT Born · 2024 · Cited by 9 — Confirmation bias (CB) is a cognitive bias that allows us to fo... Separate observation from interpretation. Record what is directly known before explaining what it might mean. Estimate confidence numerically when appropriate. Assigning approximate confidence levels encourages calibration instead of treating every conclusion as simply true or false. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMetacognitive sensitivity: The key to calibrating trust by D Lee · 2025 · Cited by 25 — Metacognitive sensitivity, measured by meta d', estimates how effectively confidence judgments disting... Review significant decisions afterwards. Compare predictions with actual outcomes . Looking back for calibration errors often reveals recurring personal biases more effectively than relying on memory alone. Treat disagreement as diagnostic. When informed people reach different conclusions, ask"},{"title":"Transfer","url":"/transfer/","description":"Reasoning skills matter most when they transfer from lessons and books into messy decisions outside the classroom.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Transfer Will Thinking Skills Work in Real Life? Transfer Transfer Will Thinking Skills Work in Real Life? Reasoning skills matter most when they transfer from lessons and books into messy decisions outside the classroom. Reasoning skills matter most when they transfer from lessons and books into messy decisions outside the classroom. population context Why transfer is hard Use across domains Practice with real problems improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer Introduction Thinking skills “work in real life” only when they survive transfer: the move from a lesson, book, course or workshop into a different, messier situation where no one labels the problem for you. That transfer is the hard part. Research on learning consistently finds that people may perform well on taught examples yet fail to recognise the same underlying pattern in a new context; near transfer is easier than far transfer, and spontaneous transfer is not something educators or learners can safely assume. [National Academies] nationalacademies.org National AcademiesHow People Learn: Brain, Mind, Experience, and SchoolStudents' abilities to transfer what they have learned to new situ... For improving your thinking and analytical skills , this changes the target. The goal is not just to know a list of fallacies, biases or reasoning steps. It is to build habits that appear during real decisions: comparing explanations before committing, asking what evidence would change your mind, checking whether a familiar situation is actually different, and practising with problems that resemble the complexity of life outside the classroom. Why Transfer Is Hard The central problem is that people often learn the surface form of a task, not the deeper structure. A student may learn how to spot a weak causal claim in a psychology class, then fail to apply the same reasoning to a fitness advert, a workplace dashboard or a political claim. The reasoning pattern is similar, but the cues look different. The National Academies’ How People Learn frames transfer as a key sign of flexible learning: instruction may look successful if students remember taught facts, yet that does not prove they can use what they know in new settings. [National Academies] nationalacademies.org National AcademiesHow People Learn: Brain, Mind, Experience, and SchoolStudents' abilities to transfer what they have learned to new situ... This is why “critical thinking” can be misleading if it is treated as a detachable mental muscle. Thinking is always about something. To judge a medical claim, a budget forecast or a historical argument, you need some domain knowledge : what counts as evidence, what errors are common, which mechanisms are plausible, and which comparisons are fair. Daniel Willingham’s work on teaching critical thinking stresses this tension: general reasoning principles exist, but they are difficult to apply without relevant background knowledge and practice recognising when the principle fits. [Education NSW] education.nsw.gov.au Education NSWHow to Teach Critical Thinking Willingham earned his B.A. from Duke University in 1983 and his Ph.D. in. Cognitive Psychology from. Harvard University in 1990.Read more... Transfer is also hard because real situations do not announce which skill is needed. In a class, the chapter title may tell you that the day’s topic is “confirmation bias” or “correlation and causation ”. In life, the prompt is more ambiguous: a colleague is confident, a graph looks persuasive, a friend forwards a health claim, or a deadline makes the first workable answer feel good enough. That is a recognition problem before it is a reasoning problem. A useful distinction is between near transfer and far transfer . Near transfer happens when the new situation closely resembles the original practice , such as applying one spreadsheet checking routine to a similar report. Far transfer asks for a bigger leap, such as using the logic of experimental controls from science to evaluate a business pilot. Perkins and Salomon’s work on transfer also distinguishes “low road” transfer, where well practised routines are triggered automatically by familiar cues, from “high road” transfer, where the learner deliberately abstracts a principle and applies it elsewhere. [jaymctighe.com] jaymctighe.com Transfer of Learning Perkins and Salomon Transfer of Learning by Perkins and SalomonIn terms of the rough near far distinction, near transfer is much more likely than far transfer... The practical risk is overconfidence. A person can read about bias, practise textbook examples, and still assume the lesson will naturally appear when money, status, identity or time pressure is involved. Transfer usually needs design: varied examples, explicit comparison, reflection, feedback and repeated use in realistic settings. What Actually Transfers Across Domains Some parts of thinking are more portable than others. Broad questions such as “What is the claim?”, “What evidence supports it?”, “What else could explain this?”, and “What would I expect to see if this were false?” can travel across domains. But they do not work equally well everywhere unless they are paired with enough knowledge of the situation. In other words, general reasoning prompts are useful handles, not complete substitutes for expertise. Meta analytic evidence suggests that critical thinking instruction can improve critical thinking, especially when it is explicit, purposeful and supported by opportunities for practice. Abrami and colleagues’ widely cited meta analysis found positive effects from instructional interventions, with stronger results where critical thinking was an explicit instructional goal rather than merely an assumed by product of studying a subject. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. The transfer debate is not simply “general skills versus domain knowledge”. The more realistic view is that transferable thinking grows from a combination of both. Halpern’s model of teaching critical thinking for transfer includes dispositions, explicit skills instruction, structure training and metacognitive monitoring: learners need to know the thinking move, want to use it, recognise when it applies, and monitor whether they are applying it well. [eipd.dcs.wisc.edu] eipd.dcs.wisc.edu Halpern Critical Thinking 1998 Halpern Critical Thinking 1998 A physics student, for example, may learn to test competing explanations when diagnosing a mechanics problem. That habit can help outside physics only if the student learns the deeper move: distinguish rival explanations, identify what each predicts, and look for discriminating evidence. A study on domain specific instruction in physics found that carefully designed instruction could support near and far transfer of critical thinking skills, but the authors emphasised systematic design, teacher coaching, feedback and student reflection rather than expecting transfer to happen automatically. [Lirias] lirias.kuleuven.be Lirias Systematic design of domain specific instruction on near Lirias Systematic design of domain specific instruction on near The transferable unit is therefore rarely a slogan such as “be sceptical”. It is more often a practised pattern: Separate claim, evidence and explanation. A claim says what is true; evidence is what supports it; an explanation says why it might be true. Compare alternatives before judging. Good analysis asks whether another explanation fits the same facts better. Look for the missing base rate or denominator. A vivid number may mislead if the relevant comparison is absent. Ask what would change the conclusion. This turns disagreement into a testable question. Use a checklist at the moment of decision. Transfer improves when cues in the real environment trigger the skill. These patterns can travel, but they travel best when practised across varied, concrete cases rather than memorised as abstract advice. Daniel Willingham Cognitive Psychology at the University of Virginia Channel: The College Board Open on YouTube Real World Evidence Is Promising, but Uneven The strongest case for real world transfer is not that thinking courses magically improve every decision. It is that some forms of instruction and assessment do connect with behaviour outside the test. Heather Butler and colleagues have studied whether critical thinking predicts “real world outcomes ” such as avoiding negative life events. Their work found that people with higher critical thinking scores reported fewer negative outcomes, and that critical thinking added predictive value beyond intelligence in those studies. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Open source on wiley.com. That finding matters because it separates thinking skill from simply being bright. Intelligence helps with many tasks, but critical thinking measures try to capture whether people evaluate evidence, avoid poor inferences and reason through practical scenarios. The evidence does not"},{"title":"Transfer Gap","url":"/transfer-gap/","description":"People often learn the surface cues of a lesson, not the deeper pattern needed to use the skill in messy real situations.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Transfer","level":3,"text":"Transfer Gap Why Good Thinking Lessons Do Not Stick Transfer Gap Transfer Gap Why Good Thinking Lessons Do Not Stick People often learn the surface cues of a lesson, not the deeper pattern needed to use the skill in messy real situations. People often learn the surface cues of a lesson, not the deeper pattern needed to use the skill in messy real situations. mechanism Surface cues versus deep structure Why real problems do not label the skill How varied examples make recognition easier improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer transfer gap Introduction Many critical thinking lessons succeed inside the classroom yet fail to change how people reason in everyday life. The main reason is not that learners forget the material. Instead, they often learn the surface cues of a lesson rather than the deeper reasoning pattern that should apply across different situations. A student who can identify a weak argument in a textbook may not notice the same flaw in a news article, workplace proposal or social media post because the context looks different. Research on learning transfer consistently shows that applying knowledge in unfamiliar settings is far more difficult than applying it to similar examples, and that this transfer rarely happens automatically. [The Learning Scientists+2Jay McTighe] learningscientists.org The Learning Scientists Depth Structure and Transfer in Critical Thinking The Learning ScientistsDepth Structure and Transfer in Critical Thinking...September 23, 2021 — 23 Sept 2021 — How do this recognition o... Published: September 23, 2021 Understanding this transfer gap explains why many well designed critical thinking courses produce disappointing real world results. The problem is less about teaching reasoning itself than about helping learners recognise when the same reasoning should be used. Surface Cues Versus Deep Structure The central mechanism behind failed transfer is the difference between surface features and deep structure . Surface features are the visible details of a problem: the topic, setting, vocabulary or people involved. Deep structure is the underlying pattern of reasoning that determines how the problem should be analysed. In many experiments, learners successfully solve a taught problem but fail to recognise another problem that shares the same logical structure because its surface details have changed. [Vanderbilt CDN Dev] cdn dev.vanderbilt.edu Seeing Deep Structure From the Interactions of Surface Features Vanderbilt CDN DevSeeing Deep Structure From the Interactions of Surface ...by MTH Chi · 2012 · Cited by 374 — Transfer is typically tho... For example, someone may correctly explain in class that correlation does not prove causation when discussing health research. Later, the same person may accept a claim that a company's profits rose immediately after a management change and therefore the new manager must be responsible. The statistical reasoning required is essentially identical, but the business context disguises the shared structure. Experts differ from novices partly because they organise knowledge around these deeper principles rather than around memorable examples. Research on expertise has repeatedly found that experienced problem solvers classify situations according to underlying principles, whereas beginners tend to classify them by obvious surface similarities. [Vanderbilt CDN Dev] cdn dev.vanderbilt.edu Seeing Deep Structure From the Interactions of Surface Features Vanderbilt CDN DevSeeing Deep Structure From the Interactions of Surface ...by MTH Chi · 2012 · Cited by 374 — Transfer is typically tho... This explains why simply memorising lists of logical fallacies or cognitive biases rarely produces flexible reasoning. Learners remember labels attached to familiar examples rather than developing mental models that travel across contexts. Conceptual Understanding and Learning Transfer Channel: Julie Stern Open on YouTube Why Real Problems Do Not Label the Skill Classroom exercises usually announce which thinking skill is required. Everyday decisions almost never do. A textbook chapter titled \"Confirmation Bias\" tells students what kind of mistake to look for before they begin. Outside education there is no such prompt. A colleague confidently presents sales figures, a friend forwards a health claim, or an online discussion becomes emotionally charged. Before reasoning can begin, the learner must first recognise that a familiar analytical strategy is relevant. This recognition step is often the weakest link. Researchers describe spontaneous transfer—the unprompted application of previously learned knowledge—as uncommon unless instruction deliberately prepares learners for it. Simply knowing a principle does not guarantee that it will be retrieved when circumstances change. [The Learning Scientists] learningscientists.org The Learning Scientists Depth Structure and Transfer in Critical Thinking The Learning ScientistsDepth Structure and Transfer in Critical Thinking...September 23, 2021 — 23 Sept 2021 — How do this recognition o... Published: September 23, 2021 Several factors make recognition especially difficult: Real situations combine many competing signals rather than presenting neatly isolated problems. Decisions often involve time pressure , emotion or social consequences that compete for attention. Everyday claims rarely resemble the carefully simplified examples used during instruction. People naturally focus on the content of an argument before noticing its underlying structure. As a result, the obstacle is frequently not poor reasoning but failure to notice that reasoning should begin. Why Familiar Examples Can Become a Trap Worked examples are valuable for introducing new ideas, but they can unintentionally encourage context bound learning. Suppose every classroom discussion of confirmation bias involves political examples. Learners may gradually associate the concept with politics rather than with selective evidence in general. When they later evaluate investment decisions, hiring choices or medical advice, they may fail to recognise the same cognitive pattern because it appears in an unfamiliar setting. Educational psychologists have long observed that knowledge becomes tied to the circumstances in which it was learned. When instruction repeatedly pairs one reasoning principle with one type of example, learners build a narrow association instead of an abstract concept. [Jay McTighe] jaymctighe.com Jay McTigheTransfer of Learning by Perkins and SalomonTransfer of learning occurs when learning in one context or with one set of materia... This does not mean examples are harmful. The problem arises when learners encounter too few different examples to separate the principle from the surrounding context. How Varied Examples Make Recognition Easier One of the strongest evidence based ways to improve transfer is to expose learners to problems that look different on the surface but share the same underlying reasoning. Instead of teaching a principle through one domain, instructors can deliberately compare examples drawn from unrelated fields. A lesson on evaluating evidence might pair: a medical study, a marketing campaign, a historical explanation, and a workplace performance report. Although the subjects differ, each requires asking similar questions about evidence quality, alternative explanations and causal inference. Research refers to this as helping learners compare problems with different surface structures but common deep structures. Such comparison encourages abstraction: learners begin noticing the reasoning pattern itself rather than the surrounding story. Explicit discussion of similarities between examples further strengthens later recognition in new contexts. [The Learning Scientists+2Vanderbilt CDN Dev] learningscientists.org The Learning Scientists Depth Structure and Transfer in Critical Thinking The Learning ScientistsDepth Structure and Transfer in Critical Thinking...September 23, 2021 — 23 Sept 2021 — How do this recognition o... Published: September 23, 2021 The goal is not simply more practice , but more varied practice. Diversity of context helps separate the principle from the example. Daniel Willingham Cognitive Psychology at the University of Virginia Channel: The College Board Open on YouTube Why Knowing Is Different From Recognising Critical thinking is often described as a collection of skills, but effective transfer depends just as much on retrieval. A learner may genuinely understand probabilistic reasoning, evidence evaluation or hypothesis testing. Yet unless those ideas are activated at the right moment, behaviour will look no different from someone who never learned them. This distinction explains why people sometimes perform impressively on examinations while making poor real world decisions. Classroom assessment frequently measures whether students can apply a skill immediately after being told which skill is relevant."},{"title":"Debiasing","url":"/debiasing/","description":"Debiasing training is most promising when learners practise, get feedback and later face problems that are not neatly labelled.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Transfer","level":3,"text":"Debiasing Can Bias Training Survive Real Life? Debiasing Debiasing Can Bias Training Survive Real Life? Debiasing training is most promising when learners practise, get feedback and later face problems that are not neatly labelled. Debiasing training is most promising when learners practise, get feedback and later face problems that are not neatly labelled. policy intervention What short debiasing interventions have shown Why feedback beats passive awareness Limits of bias reduction claims improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer debiasing Introduction Most debiasing programmes succeed at one thing: teaching people to recognise a named bias immediately after a lesson. The more difficult question is whether that learning survives outside the classroom, when nobody labels the problem as \"confirmation bias\" or \"anchoring\". Research suggests that some short interventions—particularly interactive games and carefully designed training videos—can produce measurable improvements that transfer to new judgement tasks. However, the strongest evidence comes from programmes that make learners practise decisions, receive immediate feedback , and later face unannounced problems rather than textbook examples. The overall picture is cautiously optimistic: transfer is possible, but it is neither automatic nor universal, and claims that a single intervention permanently eliminates bias are not supported by the evidence. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Debiasing Training Improves Decision Making in the Fieldby AL Sellier · 2019 · Cited by 182 — The results provide promising evidenc... What short debiasing interventions have shown One of the best known programmes was developed by researchers including Carey Morewedge and colleagues. Participants completed either an interactive computer game or an instructional video that taught several cognitive biases relevant to intelligence analysis, including confirmation bias, the bias blind spot and the fundamental attribution error. Unlike many earlier studies, participants were tested not only immediately afterwards but again weeks later using different bias tasks. The results were encouraging. Both formats reduced susceptibility to several biases immediately after training, and some improvements remained after follow up testing . However, the interactive game consistently produced larger and more durable reductions than the instructional video. Rather than simply defining biases, the game placed players in situations where they repeatedly made judgements, experienced the consequences of biased reasoning and practised corrective strategies. [Sage Journals+2Medical Xpress] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsImproved Decision Making With a Single Training...by CK Morewedge · 2015 · Cited by 589 — Participants received a single tr... The important feature was not entertainment for its own sake. The game repeatedly elicited the very biases it aimed to reduce, then explained what had happened and allowed players to try again. This combination of experience, explanation and corrective practice appears more effective than passive exposure to definitions alone. [BYU ScholarsArchive] scholarsarchive.byu.edu BYU ScholarsArchiveMitigation of Cognitive Bias with a Serious Gameby NE Dunbar · 2017 · Cited by 31 — We conducted two studies to examin... Perhaps the strongest evidence for transfer came later when researchers tested whether the training influenced decisions in a setting where participants were not expecting a bias exercise. Graduate students received confirmation bias training before or after analysing an unannounced business case modelled on the decision preceding the Space Shuttle Challenger disaster. Those who had received training were significantly less likely to adopt the inferior, hypothesis confirming solution and instead generated more evidence that challenged their initial assumptions . Because participants were not explicitly reminded about confirmation bias during the exercise, the study provides unusually strong evidence that debiasing can influence real judgement rather than only laboratory test performance. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Debiasing Training Improves Decision Making in the Fieldby AL Sellier · 2019 · Cited by 182 — The results provide promising evidenc... Dr. Carey Morewedge, Boston University (Sept 25, 2019)—UBC Psychology Colloquia 2019 2020 Channel: UBC Psychology Open on YouTube Why feedback beats passive awareness Simply learning that cognitive biases exist rarely changes behaviour by itself. Many people readily acknowledge that confirmation bias or overconfidence affect human judgement while continuing to believe that they themselves are relatively immune—a phenomenon known as the bias blind spot. Interactive interventions attempt to overcome this problem by making people experience their own errors. Instead of presenting a lecture, they require decisions, expose mistakes , explain why those mistakes occurred and immediately encourage another attempt. This creates several learning advantages: learners receive personalised evidence that they are susceptible to bias; corrective strategies are practised rather than merely described; feedback arrives immediately after the mistaken judgement; repeated examples encourage abstraction of a general reasoning strategy rather than memorisation of one example. Studies comparing serious games with equivalent instructional videos suggest that this active learning process helps explain why games often outperform passive formats, even when both communicate similar information. [BYU ScholarsArchive+2Sage Journals] scholarsarchive.byu.edu BYU ScholarsArchiveMitigation of Cognitive Bias with a Serious Gameby NE Dunbar · 2017 · Cited by 31 — We conducted two studies to examin... The same principle appears in broader educational research: transfer improves when learners repeatedly apply an idea across varied situations instead of only recognising it in the original teaching context. Debiasing interventions that embed feedback inside realistic decisions therefore align well with what is already known about transfer of learning. [BYU ScholarsArchive] scholarsarchive.byu.edu BYU ScholarsArchiveMitigation of Cognitive Bias with a Serious Gameby NE Dunbar · 2017 · Cited by 31 — We conducted two studies to examin... Can games teach judgement beyond laboratory examples? A related line of research concerns misinformation rather than traditional decision biases. The online game Bad News asks players to create misleading content themselves, exposing them to weakened examples of manipulation techniques such as emotional language, conspiracy narratives and impersonation. The intervention draws on psychological inoculation theory: practising recognition of misleading techniques before encountering them in real life may increase resistance later. Experimental studies have found that people who played the game became better at identifying common misinformation techniques in novel examples presented afterwards. Replication studies with control groups generally support these improvements, although the measured effects are typically modest rather than dramatic. The evidence suggests that the intervention teaches recognition of manipulation strategies rather than broad scepticism towards all information. [Nature+2PMC] nature.com wrong: the correction of misinformation...Read more... This illustrates an important distinction about transfer. Successful interventions often teach deeper patterns—for example, recognising emotional manipulation or searching for disconfirming evidence—rather than memorising individual false claims. When later examples differ in content but use the same underlying strategy, learners have a greater chance of recognising the problem. [sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk] sdmlab.psychol.cam.ac.uk Bad News Game Social Decision Making LabThe Bad News Game is a multiple award winning fake news intervention aimed at building psycholo... New Evidence Reveals Training Can Reduce Cognitive Bias And Improve Channel: Business News Open on YouTube Why transfer remains difficult Although the evidence is stronger than many people assume, important limitations remain. First, most studies measure transfer over weeks or months rather than years. Long term maintenance probably requires repeated use, refresher training or continued application. Second, improvements are rarely complete. Participants still display biases after training; they simply do so less often or less strongly. Debiasing reduces vulnerability rather than producing perfectly rational judgement. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsImproved Decision Making With a Single Training...by CK Morewedge · 2015 · Cited by 589 — Participants received a single tr... Third, many interventions target specific families of biases. A programme that improves resistance to confirmation bias does not necessarily reduce every other cognitive bias. Researchers increasingly distinguish"},{"title":"Domain Knowledge","url":"/domain-knowledge/","description":"General reasoning prompts work best when paired with enough background knowledge to know what counts as evidence in the situation.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Transfer","level":3,"text":"Domain Knowledge Why Thinking Skills Still Need Knowledge Domain Knowledge Domain Knowledge Why Thinking Skills Still Need Knowledge General reasoning prompts work best when paired with enough background knowledge to know what counts as evidence in the situation. General reasoning prompts work best when paired with enough background knowledge to know what counts as evidence in the situation. population context What general prompts can and cannot do How expertise changes evidence judgement When a familiar rule is the wrong fit improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer domain knowledge Introduction General reasoning prompts such as “What is the evidence?”, “What alternative explanations exist?” and “What would change my mind ?” are valuable because they can be applied in many situations. They are not, however, sufficient on their own. Good judgement depends on knowing enough about the subject to recognise which evidence matters, which comparisons are fair, which mechanisms are plausible and which warning signs are genuinely important. Research in cognitive science consistently shows that thinking skills are closely tied to domain knowledge rather than operating as a completely general mental ability. [American Federation of Teachers+2danielwillingham.com] aft.org We've seen that domain knowledge is a crucial driver of thinking skill. What knowledge is...Read more... This does not mean that critical thinking cannot transfer across domains. Rather, transfer works best when broad reasoning habits are combined with enough background knowledge to interpret a new situation correctly. The same reasoning questions may travel from medicine to history to finance, but the answers depend on understanding how evidence works in each field. What General Prompts Can and Cannot Do General thinking prompts provide a useful framework for slowing down intuitive judgement. Questions such as: What exactly is the claim? What evidence supports it? Could something else explain the result? What evidence would change the conclusion? remain useful almost everywhere. The limitation is that these prompts do not tell you how to answer them. Knowing that you should \"evaluate the evidence\" is only the beginning. Evaluating evidence requires knowing what counts as strong evidence in the particular domain. For example: In medicine, well designed randomised controlled trials often deserve more weight than personal testimonials . In history, contemporary documents must be interpreted alongside knowledge of who wrote them, why they wrote them and the historical context. In engineering, physical constraints and known failure mechanisms may outweigh intuition . In financial markets, historical averages may matter less when structural conditions have changed. The reasoning pattern remains similar, but the standards used to judge evidence differ substantially. Simply applying generic scepticism without understanding those standards often leads to poor decisions rather than better ones. [Education NSW] nsw.gov.au We have seen that domain knowledge is a crucial driver of thinking skills.Read more Education NSWHow to Teach Critical ThinkingFebruary 13, 2025 — by DT Willingham · Cited by 153 — The importance of background knowledge t... How Expertise Changes Evidence Judgement Domain knowledge improves judgement in several distinct ways beyond simply providing more facts. It changes what you notice Experts often recognise meaningful patterns that novices overlook. A radiologist may immediately notice an abnormality on an image that appears ordinary to everyone else. An experienced historian may recognise that a document's wording reflects political motives of the period. A software engineer may instantly identify a security risk hidden within otherwise functional code. These differences are not simply faster reasoning. They arise because experience changes which features appear important in the first place. Daniel Willingham Cognitive Psychology at the University of Virginia Channel: The College Board Open on YouTube It changes which questions become relevant Beginners often ask whether evidence exists. Experts ask whether the evidence could exist if the proposed explanation were true. For example, experienced scientists routinely consider: possible confounding variables measurement reliability selection effects statistical power alternative causal mechanisms Without enough background knowledge, many of these possibilities never occur to the decision maker. It changes how uncertainty is interpreted Experts usually become better at recognising genuine uncertainty rather than treating every unknown as equally important. Someone unfamiliar with climate science, medicine or economics may assume that disagreement automatically means \"nobody knows.\" Someone who understands the field can distinguish between: questions with overwhelming evidence, questions with moderate uncertainty, genuinely unresolved research problems. Knowing where uncertainty actually lies is itself a form of expertise. [danielwillingham.com] danielwillingham.com Occasional Paper Series: How to Teach Critical Thinkingby DT Willingham · Cited by 153 — The importance of background knowledge to critic... Why More Knowledge Does Not Automatically Mean Better Thinking Although domain knowledge is essential, expertise creates its own risks. Experts can become overconfident in familiar methods, overlook novel evidence or assume that techniques successful in one context automatically apply elsewhere. Cognitive scientists sometimes refer to the \"curse of knowledge\": once people understand something deeply, they may underestimate how difficult it is for others or overlook assumptions they no longer notice. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Curse of knowledge Curse of knowledge Good judgement therefore requires balancing two strengths: enough expertise to understand the evidence, enough intellectual humility to question established interpretations when new evidence appears. The best decision makers continually update both their knowledge and their reasoning habits. When a Familiar Rule Is the Wrong Fit Many reasoning errors occur because people correctly remember a useful principle but apply it in the wrong setting. Consider several common examples. \"Correlation does not imply causation.\" This is an important warning, but it does not mean every observed relationship is meaningless. In epidemiology, economics and social science, multiple independent lines of evidence can collectively support causal conclusions even when controlled experiments are impossible. \"Trust the experts.\" Expert opinion deserves weight, but only within the relevant domain. A distinguished physicist is not automatically an authority on nutrition, constitutional law or educational policy. \"Always demand more evidence.\" Additional evidence is valuable only if it addresses the important uncertainties. Collecting more low quality observations may add less value than obtaining one carefully designed measurement. These examples illustrate that reasoning rules are rarely wrong. They become misleading when detached from the context that originally gave them meaning. The Role of Knowledge Dan Willingham Channel: GPB Education Open on YouTube Why Different Subjects Judge Evidence Differently One reason transfer is difficult is that different disciplines have developed distinct standards for deciding what counts as convincing evidence. DomainTypical evidence emphasisCommon judgement questionScienceControlled experiments, replication, predictionDoes the explanation fit all available observations?HistoryPrimary sources, corroboration, contextHow reliable is this account given who created it?LawWitness testimony, legal standards, admissible evidenceHas the required burden of proof been met?EngineeringTesting, safety margins, physical constraintsWill the system work reliably under realistic conditions?BusinessMarket behaviour, incentives, practical outcomesWill this decision produce sustainable results? The general habit of evaluating evidence transfers across these areas, but the evidence itself—and the standards used to judge it—changes considerably. This is why effective thinkers learn both general reasoning strategies and the norms of the fields in which they make important decisions. Irreplaceable Skills in the AI Age Channel: Insight Jam Open on YouTube Building Just Enough Domain Knowledge Becoming an expert in every subject is impossible. Fortunately, better judgement usually requires sufficient understanding rather than complete mastery. Useful habits include: Learn the basic vocabulary so that evidence can be interpreted accurately rather than by guesswork. Understand the major mechanisms that experts believe produce outcomes in the field. Know which forms of evidence practitioners regard as strongest and weakest. Learn the most common misconceptions and failure modes within that domain. Recognise where genuine expert disagreement exists instead of"},{"title":"Near vs Far","url":"/near-vs-far/","description":"Near transfer is easier than far transfer because the new situation shares more visible cues with the original practice.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Transfer","level":3,"text":"Near vs Far How Far Can a Thinking Skill Travel? Near vs Far Near vs Far How Far Can a Thinking Skill Travel? Near transfer is easier than far transfer because the new situation shares more visible cues with the original practice. Near transfer is easier than far transfer because the new situation shares more visible cues with the original practice. mechanism Near transfer in similar tasks Far transfer across unfamiliar settings How to practise the bridge between them improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer near vs far Introduction The value of a thinking skill is measured not by how well you use it in practice exercises, but by whether it appears when you face a real decision. This ability to carry a skill from one situation to another is known as transfer . The easiest form is near transfer , where the new task closely resembles the one you practised. The harder form is far transfer , where the surface details are different but the same underlying principle still applies. More than a century of research suggests that near transfer is common, while far transfer is much less reliable unless people deliberately learn to recognise deeper patterns rather than familiar examples. [Jay McTighe+2Structural Learning] jaymctighe.com Jay McTigheTransfer of Learning by Perkins and SalomonNear transfer refers to transfer between very similar contexts, as for instance whe... For improving analytical thinking, this distinction matters because everyday decisions rarely announce which reasoning tool you should use. Buying insurance, judging a health claim, evaluating a workplace proposal and deciding whether to trust a striking statistic may all require the same habits of evidence based thinking, even though they look unrelated at first glance. Near transfer in similar tasks Near transfer occurs when two situations share obvious features. The learner recognises the new problem because it resembles one already solved. In everyday life, near transfer often feels effortless because the visible cues are familiar: Using the same budgeting spreadsheet for this month's household expenses as last month's. Applying a checklist for proofreading one report to another report in the same format. Spotting misleading percentages in a second newspaper article after analysing a similar one earlier in the week. Using the same framework for comparing two job offers after previously comparing university courses. The classic explanation dates back to the work of Edward Thorndike and Robert Woodworth in the early twentieth century. They argued that transfer depends on the degree to which two situations share common elements , rather than on the existence of a general mental faculty that automatically improves reasoning everywhere. Their idea challenged the older belief that studying demanding subjects alone would naturally strengthen thinking across all areas of life. [Structural Learning] structural learning.com Structural LearningTransfer of Learning: A Complete Guide5 days ago — Near transfer happens more naturally, while far transfer requires e... David Perkins and Gavriel Salomon later described much near transfer as low road transfer . After enough practice, familiar cues trigger well learned routines with little conscious effort. A pilot's pre flight checklist, a nurse's medication verification routine or an accountant's reconciliation process are examples where repeated exposure encourages automatic application in similar situations. [Jay McTighe] jaymctighe.com Jay McTigheTransfer of Learning by Perkins and SalomonNear transfer refers to transfer between very similar contexts, as for instance whe... This automaticity is valuable because it reduces mental effort for routine decisions. However, it also creates a limitation: if the appearance of the problem changes substantially, the routine may never be activated. Far transfer across unfamiliar settings Far transfer asks a more demanding question: can you recognise that two very different situations share the same logical structure? Instead of repeating a familiar procedure, you must ignore distracting surface differences and identify the underlying principle. For example: Someone who understands experimental controls in science may apply the same reasoning when assessing whether a company's new sales strategy really caused higher revenue. A person who has learned to distinguish correlation from causation in psychology may recognise the same mistake in financial commentary or social media discussions. A habit of comparing competing explanations while reading history may later improve decisions about workplace problems, where several causes could explain declining performance. These examples require abstraction. The learner must mentally translate one domain into another before applying the reasoning strategy. Research consistently finds this kind of transfer much harder than educators once hoped. Reviews of cognitive training and \"brain training\" programmes repeatedly conclude that while people generally improve at tasks very similar to those they practise, convincing evidence for broad far transfer into unrelated skills is limited. Improvements often remain close to the original training unless the new task shares important structural features. [nifdi.org+2Structural Learning] nifdi.org Near and far transfer in cognitive trainingNear transfer occurs when skills learned in one context are applied to similar contexts, while... This does not mean far transfer is impossible. Rather, it appears to depend on more than repeated exposure. Learners usually need to identify general principles, compare examples across contexts and consciously search for situations where those principles apply. What Is ‘Transfer of Learning’ and How Does It Help Students? Channel: Education Week Open on YouTube Why far transfer so often fails Many thinking skills appear portable in theory but remain tied to the circumstances in which they were learned. Several mechanisms explain this gap. Surface features dominate attention. People naturally notice obvious similarities before deeper relationships. A medical study and a business experiment look different even if both require the same logic of comparison. Context provides hidden prompts. During training, learners are told what topic they are studying. Outside training, nobody labels a conversation as an exercise in confirmation bias or probabilistic reasoning. Knowledge and reasoning interact. Analytical habits depend on having enough background knowledge to recognise when a principle is relevant. Without understanding how scientific evidence, financial statements or legal arguments normally work, even excellent reasoning strategies may not activate appropriately. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Transfer Of LearningTransfer includes near transfer (to closely related contexts and performances) and far transfer (to... Time pressure encourages familiar routines. Everyday decisions often reward speed rather than careful abstraction. Under deadlines, people default to previously successful habits instead of searching for deeper analogies. The result is that someone may genuinely understand a reasoning principle in one setting while failing to notice that it applies elsewhere. How to practise the bridge between them Fortunately, research suggests that transfer can be strengthened when learning is designed specifically to encourage it rather than assuming it will emerge automatically. Compare different examples that share one principle Instead of practising only one type of problem, deliberately study several situations that rely on the same reasoning. For example, examine: a medical headline, an investment advertisement, a workplace performance dashboard, and a sports statistic, all through the question: Does this evidence justify the conclusion? The changing contexts encourage attention to the underlying reasoning rather than the surface story. [Jay McTighe] jaymctighe.com Jay McTigheTransfer of Learning by Perkins and SalomonNear transfer refers to transfer between very similar contexts, as for instance whe... The Future Belongs to People with Many Interests Channel: MIND ARCHITECTS Open on YouTube Name the principle, not just the example Rather than remembering, \"This article confused correlation with causation,\" state the more general lesson: \"Whenever two events occur together, I should ask whether another explanation could account for both.\" General statements travel more easily across domains than memories tied to one specific case. Look backwards from new decisions Perkins and Salomon describe high road transfer as deliberately searching memory for relevant ideas when facing a new situation. Instead of asking, \"Have I seen this exact problem before?\", ask: What does this situation remind me of? Have I encountered a similar pattern elsewhere? Which reasoning strategy worked then? This conscious search helps build bridges between apparently unrelated"},{"title":"Checklists","url":"/checklists/","description":"A short checklist can help thinking skills appear when pressure, confidence or familiarity would otherwise hide the need for them.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Transfer","level":3,"text":"Checklists Can a Checklist Make Better Thinking Show Up? Checklists Checklists Can a Checklist Make Better Thinking Show Up? A short checklist can help thinking skills appear when pressure, confidence or familiarity would otherwise hide the need for them. A short checklist can help thinking skills appear when pressure, confidence or familiarity would otherwise hide the need for them. policy intervention Why decision moments need visible cues What a useful thinking checklist includes Where checklists fail or become mechanical improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer checklists Introduction A thinking checklist is not a substitute for judgement. Its value is that it increases the chance that good judgement appears at the moment it is needed. Under time pressure, familiarity , confidence or routine, people often fail to recognise that a decision deserves deliberate analysis. A short, well designed checklist acts as a visible cue that interrupts automatic thinking long enough to trigger questions such as \"What evidence supports this?\", \"What alternatives have I ignored?\" or \"What would change my mind ?\". Research across aviation, healthcare and other high risk fields suggests that checklists are most effective when they are designed as decision prompts rather than bureaucratic paperwork, helping experts remember critical thinking steps instead of replacing expertise. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe Checklist Manifesto: How to Get Things Right NIHby L Nasir · 2010 · Cited by 13 — Going through a checklist at the beginning of a multi faceted process empowers a process for e... Within the broader challenge of transferring thinking skills into everyday life, this matters because many failures are recognition failures rather than reasoning failures. People often know how to think critically but do not notice when to apply those skills. A checklist changes the environment so that the decision itself reminds the person to think. Why decision moments need visible cues Most mistakes in complex work do not happen because people have never learned the relevant principle. They happen because attention narrows, memory becomes overloaded, or the situation feels so familiar that important questions are skipped. Psychologists studying transfer of learning have long distinguished between possessing a skill and recognising when it should be used. A decision checklist bridges that gap by embedding a reminder directly into the workflow. Rather than expecting someone to remember a lesson from training weeks or months later, the checklist presents the cue exactly when a choice must be made. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineIdentifying obstacles to transfer of critical thinking skillsby LM van Peppen · 2022 · Cited by 39 — To illustrate... This explains why checklists have become common in domains where expertise alone is insufficient: Pilots pause before critical flight stages despite extensive training. Surgical teams conduct brief safety checks immediately before procedures. Emergency clinicians increasingly use cognitive aids during rare or stressful situations. Industrial operators pause before irreversible actions affecting safety or quality. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov How to WHO: lessons from aviation in checklists and debriefsby IA Guy · 2022 · Cited by 26 — This exploration of attitudes and logisti... In each case, the checklist is designed to trigger thinking at a predictable decision point rather than to teach the underlying knowledge from scratch. A related idea in cognitive psychology is the \"cognitive forcing strategy\": a deliberately learned question or routine that interrupts common reasoning errors. Instead of relying on intuition alone, the decision maker forces themselves to consider overlooked possibilities before committing. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive forcing strategies in clinical decisionmakingby P Croskerry · 2003 · Cited by 618 — Three levels of cognitive forcing str... A Free Summary of the Book The Checklist Manifesto by Atul Gawande Channel: Readitfor.me Open on YouTube What a useful thinking checklist includes A thinking checklist differs from a procedural checklist. A procedural checklist ensures that actions are completed in the correct order. A thinking checklist prompts reflection before choosing among alternatives. The most useful versions are remarkably short because long lists are unlikely to be used under pressure . Typical prompts include: Clarify the claim. What decision am I actually making? Check the evidence. What facts support this conclusion? Consider alternatives. What is another plausible explanation? Look for missing information. What important uncertainty remains? Test confidence. What evidence would make me change my mind? Check consequences. Is this decision reversible, or should I slow down? These questions deliberately interrupt common thinking traps such as confirmation bias, premature closure and overconfidence. Rather than telling someone what to conclude, they increase the likelihood that the conclusion has been examined properly. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive forcing strategies in clinical decisionmakingby P Croskerry · 2003 · Cited by 618 — Three levels of cognitive forcing str... Another important design principle is choosing the right pause point. A checklist used after the decision has effectively been made has little value. Effective implementations define clear trigger moments—for example, before approving a large purchase, before diagnosing a difficult case, before publishing an important report, or before committing a project plan. [exida] exida.com can checklists prevent human error Can Checklists Prevent Human Error?8 Jul 2021 — DO CONFIRM checklist – team members perform tasks from memory and then stop to revie... Why short checklists often outperform long ones Research and practical experience consistently favour concise checklists that support memory instead of overwhelming it. Their purpose is not to document every possibility but to highlight the few questions that people are most likely to overlook. Successful checklists usually share several characteristics: They contain only the highest value prompts. They are written in plain language. They fit naturally into existing workflows. They identify a specific moment when they should be used. They encourage discussion where teams are involved. Atul Gawande's work on medical and aviation inspired checklists emphasises that good checklists leave room for professional judgement. They reduce routine omissions so that experts can concentrate on genuinely difficult decisions rather than basic procedural errors. [Atul Gawande] atulgawande.com Atul Gawande The Checklist Manifesto Atul GawandeThe Checklist Manifesto Atul GawandeThe book's main point is simple: no matter how expert you may be, well designed check l... The Checklist Manifesto by Atul Gawande: 7 Minute Summary Channel: SnapTale Audiobook Summaries Open on YouTube Where checklists fail or become mechanical Checklists are not universally beneficial. Used badly, they become another form to complete without improving thinking. One common failure is tick box compliance . When organisations reward completion rather than reflection, people learn to satisfy the paperwork instead of engaging with the questions. The checklist remains visible, but the thinking disappears. Studies of checklist implementation repeatedly note that organisational culture and communication matter as much as the checklist itself. [PMC+2BJA Anaesthesia] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov How to WHO: lessons from aviation in checklists and debriefsby IA Guy · 2022 · Cited by 26 — This exploration of attitudes and logisti... Another problem is excessive length. Every additional item competes for attention. In urgent situations, overly detailed lists are more likely to be skipped altogether or followed mechanically without considering whether they fit the current circumstances. [exida] exida.com can checklists prevent human error Can Checklists Prevent Human Error?8 Jul 2021 — DO CONFIRM checklist – team members perform tasks from memory and then stop to revie... Checklists also struggle with novel situations. They work best for recurring decision points where certain thinking failures are predictable. They cannot anticipate every unusual circumstance, and they should never replace expertise, situational awareness or deeper reasoning. [BJA Anaesthesia] bjanaesthesia.org.uk BJA AnaesthesiaChecklists, cognitive aids, and the future of patient safetyby CS Webster · 2017 · Cited by 42 — A systematic review of th... Finally, some evidence suggests that cognitive checklists are most effective when users can revisit observations, reconsider evidence and revise their judgement. If circumstances make reflection impossible, simply presenting a checklist may have little effect. [Royal College of Physicians of Edinburgh] rcpe.ac.uk Royal College of"},{"title":"Outcomes","url":"/outcomes/","description":"The most useful test of thinking instruction is whether people handle new decisions differently, not whether they can define concepts.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Transfer","level":3,"text":"Outcomes Does Better Thinking Change Real Choices? Outcomes Outcomes Does Better Thinking Change Real Choices? The most useful test of thinking instruction is whether people handle new decisions differently, not whether they can define concepts. The most useful test of thinking instruction is whether people handle new decisions differently, not whether they can define concepts. dataset evidence Negative life events as outcome measures Decision processes versus test scores What the evidence can and cannot prove improving your thinking and analytical skills transfer outcomes Introduction The strongest test of whether thinking skills matter is not whether someone can define concepts such as confirmation bias or correlation versus causation . It is whether they make better decisions when facing unfamiliar, messy situations where nobody tells them which thinking skill to use. Researchers increasingly distinguish between performance on thinking tests and real world outcomes : financial choices, health decisions, interpersonal problems, workplace judgement and other consequences of everyday reasoning. Evidence suggests that stronger critical thinking is associated with fewer costly mistakes across many areas of life, even after accounting for intelligence. At the same time, the evidence is more nuanced than simple claims that \"critical thinking causes success\". Most studies are correlational rather than experimental, meaning they show relationships rather than definitive proof of cause and effect. The most useful conclusion is that real world outcomes provide an important validity check on thinking instruction, but they are an imperfect one. [UMass Amherst] umass.edu UMass AmherstPredicting real world outcomes Critical thinking ability is a...August 28, 2017 — by HA Butler · Cited by 2 — Despite evid... Published: August 28, 2017 Why Real World Outcomes Matter More Than Test Scores Many educational programmes judge success by improvements on classroom assessments. While useful, these measures answer only a limited question: can participants perform well on the kinds of problems they were trained to solve? A more demanding question is whether improved reasoning transfers into everyday decisions. Someone who genuinely thinks more effectively should, over time, be more likely to: evaluate evidence before acting; avoid preventable financial and administrative mistakes; recognise misleading claims; revise beliefs when evidence changes; make decisions that reduce avoidable negative consequences. This shifts evaluation from knowledge about thinking to behaviour influenced by thinking. A person who scores highly on a reasoning assessment but repeatedly makes poor financial, medical or interpersonal decisions has not demonstrated the kind of transfer that matters most outside education. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by HA Butler · 2024 · Cited by 97 — The researchers found that those who scored higher on the critical thinking assessment experienced... YouTube Maggie Toplak: What IQ Tests Miss Maggie Toplak: What IQ Tests Miss Open on YouTube Negative Life Events as Outcome Measures One influential line of research asked a practical question: do better critical thinkers experience fewer avoidable negative life events? Researchers developed inventories covering everyday problems across multiple domains rather than relying solely on examination scores. These inventories included events such as: paying avoidable late fees; allowing food to spoil unnecessarily; excessive consumer debt; serious financial errors; interpersonal conflicts; poor business decisions. [workplaceinsight.net] workplaceinsight.net critical thinking ability better predictor life decisions intelligence Critical thinking ability is a better predictor of life decisions...28 Jul 2017 — The avoidance of poor outcomes gave an indirect measur... The assumption was not that every bad outcome reflects poor thinking. Illness, accidents and bad luck happen. Instead, across many different situations, people with stronger reasoning skills should accumulate fewer preventable errors if those skills genuinely influence behaviour. Studies using the Halpern Critical Thinking Assessment found exactly this pattern. Individuals with higher critical thinking scores reported fewer negative life events across diverse areas of daily life. Importantly, this relationship appeared in multiple samples and was later examined across different countries, suggesting the finding was not confined to one educational setting. [Gredos+2UMass Amherst] gredos.usal.es The Halpern Critical Thinking Assessment and real world...February 15, 2024 — by HA Butler · 2012 · Cited by 217 — The purpose of... Published: February 15, 2024 PSU's Dr. Keith Stanovich What Intelligence Tests Miss Channel: Portland State University Open on YouTube Decision Processes Versus Test Scores Perhaps the most interesting finding is that critical thinking and intelligence are not interchangeable. Intelligence tests measure abilities such as reasoning speed, memory and pattern recognition. These predict many important educational and occupational outcomes. However, they do not necessarily capture whether someone routinely: seeks disconfirming evidence ; questions first impressions; weighs alternative explanations; notices when confidence exceeds evidence. In a study of community adults and university students, both IQ and critical thinking related to fewer negative life events. However, critical thinking explained additional variation beyond intelligence alone and proved the stronger predictor of real world decision outcomes. In other words, being intellectually capable is not identical to consistently applying good judgement. [UMass Amherst] umass.edu UMass AmherstPredicting real world outcomes Critical thinking ability is a...August 28, 2017 — by HA Butler · Cited by 2 — Despite evid... Published: August 28, 2017 This distinction aligns with a broader literature arguing that successful reasoning depends not only on cognitive capacity but also on habits of reflective thinking—choosing to pause, evaluate evidence and override intuitive but potentially misleading responses. Reviews of everyday critical thinking similarly conclude that measures of applied reasoning predict meaningful behavioural differences outside educational settings. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by HA Butler · 2024 · Cited by 97 — The researchers found that those who scored higher on the critical thinking assessment experienced... What Real World Measures Capture Better Than Classroom Tests Real life outcomes have several advantages over traditional assessments. First, they involve unprompted application . Everyday life does not announce that a situation requires critical thinking. People must recognise this themselves. Second, they include competing pressures . Time limits, emotions, social influence and uncertainty all affect judgement in ways that simplified classroom exercises often cannot reproduce. Third, they evaluate long term behavioural patterns rather than isolated performances. One excellent answer on an examination says little about hundreds of decisions made over months or years. For these reasons, outcome based measures often provide stronger evidence that thinking skills have transferred beyond instruction into everyday behaviour. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by HA Butler · 2024 · Cited by 97 — The researchers found that those who scored higher on the critical thinking assessment experienced... What the Evidence Cannot Prove The findings are encouraging but should not be overstated. Most studies showing links between critical thinking and life outcomes are correlational. Several alternative explanations remain possible. People with stronger critical thinking may also differ in ways that contribute to better outcomes, including: conscientiousness; self control; educational opportunities; socioeconomic circumstances; accumulated domain knowledge . Although researchers often control statistically for intelligence and some demographic variables, observational studies cannot eliminate every possible confounding factor. Higher critical thinking may contribute to better outcomes, but successful life experiences may also reinforce better thinking habits over time. Another limitation concerns measurement. Many real world outcome inventories rely on self reported life events. Participants may forget events, interpret questions differently or under report mistakes. Researchers have worked to validate these instruments across cultures, but self report remains less objective than direct observation of behaviour. [Gredos+2SciELO] gredos.usal.es The Halpern Critical Thinking Assessment and real world...February 15, 2024 — by HA Butler · 2012 · Cited by 217 — The purpose of... Published: February 15, 2024 Intelligence is not Rationality: A Cognitive Scientific Diagnosis of the Alignment Problem Channel: Anagoge Podcast Open on YouTube Why Decision Quality Is Not the Same as Decision"},{"title":"Retrieval","url":"/retrieval/","description":"Trying to recall an idea before checking notes reveals gaps and makes later feedback more useful.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Retrieval Testing Yourself Can Make You A Better Thinker When The “test” Retrieval Retrieval Can Testing Yourself Make You Smarter? Trying to recall an idea before checking notes reveals gaps and makes later feedback more useful. Trying to recall an idea before checking notes reveals gaps and makes later feedback more useful. policy intervention Why recall beats recognition Feedback after retrieval Simple practice formats improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval Introduction Testing yourself can make you a better thinker when the “test” is used as a learning tool, not as a judgement of ability. Retrieval practice means trying to bring an idea back from memory before checking notes, slides, answers or a search engine. That small act turns vague familiarity into evidence: either the idea is available, connected and usable, or it is not yet secure. For improving analytical skills, this matters because good thinking depends not only on storing information, but on noticing gaps, correcting errors and applying knowledge under real conditions. The strongest finding is simple: practice tests often improve later learning more than rereading or passive review. Classic laboratory studies showed that taking memory tests can improve long term retention, and later reviews found benefits across many classroom settings, content areas and assessment formats. Retrieval practice is not magic, and it should not be confused with high stakes exam pressure . Its value is more practical: it makes understanding observable, gives feedback something to attach to, and trains the mind to reconstruct ideas rather than merely recognise them. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov taking memory tests improves long term retentionby HL Roediger · 2006 · Cited by 4826 — Taking a memory test not only assesses what... Why recall beats recognition Rereading feels productive because the material becomes familiar. The problem is that familiarity is a weak test of understanding. A paragraph can look clear on the page while the underlying idea remains unavailable when you have to explain , compare or use it. Retrieval practice exposes that difference. When you close the book and ask, “What was the argument?” or “Why does this method work?”, you replace the feeling of knowing with a direct sample of what you can actually produce. This is why retrieval practice belongs inside a broader programme of better thinking. Analytical skill often fails at the point where a person assumes they understand a concept because they recognise its words. In a policy discussion, for example, someone may recognise phrases such as “opportunity cost”, “base rate” or “confounding variable” without being able to use them to evaluate a real claim. Retrieval practice forces the learner to rebuild the idea: define it, give an example, explain why it matters and notice what is missing. A large meta analysis of practice testing found that practice tests were generally more beneficial for learning than restudying and other non testing comparison conditions. That does not mean every quiz is useful, or that testing should replace teaching. It means that the act of attempting recall is itself part of learning, not merely a way to measure learning after the fact. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Rethinking the Use of Tests: A Meta Analysis of Practice Testing Sage JournalsRethinking the Use of Tests: A Meta Analysis of Practice Testing Olusola O. Adesope, Dominic A. Trevisan, Narayankripa Sun... The distinction is especially important for deeper understanding. Recognition asks, “Have I seen this before?” Retrieval asks, “Can I reconstruct it, distinguish it from similar ideas and use it without the prompt in front of me?” The second question is closer to the demands of real thinking: explaining a decision, solving a problem, spotting a weak argument or transferring a principle to a new case. How retrieval makes understanding visible Retrieval practice works partly because it creates evidence. Before recall, a learner may only have a private impression: “I think I get it.” After recall, there is an observable answer, sketch, explanation, prediction or error. That visible output can then be inspected. For analytical skill, this is the crucial move. A person cannot improve a hidden misunderstanding. But once they write down a rough answer, several things become clearer: Missing links: they may remember the conclusion but not the reasoning that supports it. Fragile definitions: they may use the right term but blur it with a neighbouring concept. False fluency: they may explain smoothly until a concrete example is required. Unusable knowledge: they may recall facts but fail to apply them to a new problem. Overconfidence: they may discover that a topic felt mastered only because the notes were visible. Research on judgements of learning supports this point. Studies have found that predictions made after retrieval attempts can be more accurate than predictions made after restudy, because the learner has better information about what they could actually recall and how fluently they recalled it. In other words, retrieval practice improves not only memory, but also metacognitive monitoring: the ability to judge the state of one’s own knowledge. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersFrontiers Retrieval Practice Facilitates Judgments of Learning Through Multiple Mechanisms: Simultaneous and Independent Contr... This is why retrieval practice is useful beyond school revision. In professional thinking, it can be used before a meeting, after reading a report or while learning a technical concept. Trying to summarise the case from memory before opening the source material gives a sharper picture of what is understood, what is merely familiar and what needs checking. Feedback after retrieval is where the gaps become useful The most productive moment is often not the recall attempt itself, but the comparison that follows. After trying to answer, the learner checks the source, model answer, teacher explanation or worked example. The gap between the attempted answer and the better answer becomes targeted feedback. Feedback matters because retrieval can otherwise reinforce errors or leave misconceptions untouched. A failed attempt is not automatically harmful, but it needs correction. Research on unsuccessful retrieval attempts has found that failed tests followed by immediate feedback can produce better retention than simply presenting the correct information, suggesting that even an initial failure can prepare the mind to learn when feedback arrives. [Bjork Learning and Forgetting Lab] bjorklab.psych.ucla.edu Bjork Learning and Forgetting Lab For better understanding, feedback should do more than mark an answer as right or wrong. It should answer three questions: 1. What was missing? The learner may have omitted a condition, step, exception or causal link. 2. What was confused? They may have mixed up similar concepts or used a term too broadly. 3. What should be done next? The next retrieval attempt should target the weak point, not repeat the same vague review. This is where retrieval practice becomes a feedback policy rather than a memory trick. Low stakes recall creates frequent small errors; feedback converts those errors into information; another retrieval attempt checks whether the correction has stuck. The loop is simple but powerful: recall, compare, correct, retry. Classroom research has also stressed the importance of feedback. A review of applied work at Columbia Middle School reported that retrieval practice improved long term classroom learning, that delayed quizzes were particularly useful for retention, and that quizzes with feedback improved both learning and metacognitive awareness. [PDF Retrieval Practice] pdf.retrievalpractice.org PDF Retrieval Practice Henry Roediger Retrieval Practice to Enhance Learning and Retention Channel: Montana State University Open on YouTube What retrieval practice looks like in real study Retrieval practice does not require formal exams. It can be brief, informal and ungraded. The common feature is that the learner must attempt to produce an answer before looking. A few simple formats are especially useful for understanding: Closed book explanation. After reading a section, close the source and explain the idea in plain language. Then check the original and revise the explanation. This is useful for concepts, arguments and theories. Brain dump. Write everything remembered about a topic for three to five minutes. Then organise it: What are the main ideas? Which parts are evidence? Which parts are examples? What is missing? Teaching guidance from Washington University’s Center for Teaching and Learning lists “brain dumps” as one practical retrieval format, alongside low stakes quizzes and short “two things” recall activities. [Center for Teaching and Learning] ctl.wustl.edu Open source on wustl.edu. Two question check. After a lesson, article or"},{"title":"Application","url":"/application/","description":"Asking where an idea would change a decision shows whether knowledge can move beyond definitions into real analysis.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Retrieval","level":3,"text":"Application Can the Idea Survive a Real Example? Application Application Can the Idea Survive a Real Example? Asking where an idea would change a decision shows whether knowledge can move beyond definitions into real analysis. Asking where an idea would change a decision shows whether knowledge can move beyond definitions into real analysis. policy intervention Why definitions alone are too easy How to design decision based prompts How application practice supports transfer improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval application Introduction Retrieval practice is most valuable when it shows whether knowledge can be used, not merely repeated. A learner who can define a concept may still fail to recognise when it matters in a real decision. Application prompts are designed to close that gap. Instead of asking, “What is this idea?”, they ask, “When would this idea change your judgement?” or “Which option would you choose, and why?” That shift matters because analytical thinking depends on transferring knowledge into unfamiliar situations rather than reproducing memorised wording. Research on retrieval practice consistently shows that testing can improve not only retention but also the ability to apply knowledge to new contexts, especially when learners retrieve information while explaining, comparing or making decisions rather than recalling isolated facts. [PubMed+2Andyʼs working notes] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The critical role of retrieval practice in long term retentionby HL Roediger 3rd · 2011 · Cited by 2839 — The power of retrieval pr... Why definitions alone are too easy Definitions are an important starting point, but they provide only a partial picture of understanding. A learner may correctly define \"confirmation bias\", \"opportunity cost\" or \"correlation\" while failing to notice these ideas in a practical problem. Recognition of terminology is easier than selecting and applying the correct concept under realistic conditions. This difference explains why many learners appear confident during revision yet struggle when confronted with novel examples. A definition often relies on familiar wording from a textbook, whereas application requires choosing relevant information, ignoring distractions and explaining why a concept changes the outcome. These additional demands are much closer to the reasoning required in professional work, policy analysis and everyday decision making. [The Learning Scientists] learningscientists.org The Learning Scientists Optimising Learning Using Retrieval Practice The Learning ScientistsOptimising Learning Using Retrieval PracticeSeptember 27, 2018 — 27 Sept 2018 — Retrieval practice, or reconstruct... Published: September 27, 2018 A useful test therefore asks questions such as: Which principle is most relevant here? Would using this idea alter your recommendation? What evidence would make you change your mind? Which option violates the concept, even if it initially appears attractive? These prompts force learners to reconstruct knowledge rather than simply recognise familiar language. Understanding and Applying Retrieval Practice in Lesson Planning Channel: morganeua Open on YouTube How to design decision based prompts Good application prompts begin with a realistic decision rather than an abstract request for explanation. The learner should have to select, justify and defend a choice using the target concept. Effective prompts typically have four characteristics. Present a concrete situation. Instead of asking for a definition, introduce a short scenario with enough detail to require interpretation. Rather than: \"Define base rate neglect.\" Ask: \"A doctor receives a positive screening result for a rare disease. What additional information is essential before concluding the patient probably has the disease?\" The learner must retrieve both the concept and its practical implications. Require a decision. The prompt should end with an action, recommendation or judgement. Examples include: Which proposal should receive funding? Which explanation is better supported? Should the manager intervene now or collect more evidence? Which statistic would you trust? A decision creates consequences for the reasoning instead of rewarding memorised wording alone. Include plausible distractions. Real analytical problems rarely contain only relevant information. Introducing irrelevant but believable details tests whether learners know what actually matters. For example, a policy scenario might mention political popularity, media attention and cost, while the critical concept concerns confounding variables or selection bias. Learners who truly understand the concept can identify the information that should influence the decision and ignore what should not. Ask for justification, not only an answer. A brief explanation reveals whether the decision resulted from sound reasoning or guessing. The explanation often exposes misconceptions that a multiple choice answer would hide. Research suggests retrieval is especially valuable when learners elaborate during recall rather than producing isolated facts, because elaborated retrieval strengthens meaningful connections that support later transfer. [Andyʼs working notes] notes.andymatuschak.org Retrieval practice and transfer learning Andyʼs working notesRetrieval practice and transfer learning16 Apr 2024 — This meta analysis of transfer effects in retrieval practice fi... What application prompts look like Well designed application prompts can remain short while demanding substantial thinking. Instead of recalling terminology, they ask learners to use concepts in unfamiliar settings. Weak retrieval promptStrong application promptWhat is opportunity cost?Which investment should the council reject after considering opportunity cost? Explain why.Define confirmation bias.Which investigator is more vulnerable to confirmation bias in this case, and what should they do differently?What is a confounding variable?Which conclusion about this health study becomes questionable once the missing variable is considered?What is marginal analysis?At what point should the company stop expanding production? Justify your recommendation. Notice that each stronger prompt requires the learner to identify where the concept changes a practical judgement rather than merely recalling its wording. How application practice supports transfer The central goal is transfer: using knowledge beyond the exact situation in which it was learned. Researchers distinguish between near transfer , where new problems closely resemble previous ones, and far transfer , where knowledge must be adapted to substantially different situations. Retrieval practice generally produces reliable benefits for near transfer and smaller but meaningful improvements for broader transfer, especially when learners repeatedly retrieve ideas using varied examples rather than identical questions. [Andyʼs working notes+2Squarespace] notes.andymatuschak.org Retrieval practice and transfer learning Andyʼs working notesRetrieval practice and transfer learning16 Apr 2024 — This meta analysis of transfer effects in retrieval practice fi... One influential experiment showed that repeated testing produced better performance on inference and transfer questions than repeated studying, demonstrating that retrieval can strengthen flexible understanding rather than rote memory alone. [Andyʼs working notes] notes.andymatuschak.org Retrieval practice and transfer learning Andyʼs working notesRetrieval practice and transfer learning16 Apr 2024 — This meta analysis of transfer effects in retrieval practice fi... Later work has also found that varying the examples used during repeated retrieval encourages learners to develop multiple retrieval routes to the same underlying concept. Instead of associating an idea with one familiar example, they begin recognising the deeper pattern across different contexts. That flexibility is precisely what analytical thinking requires. [Squarespace] static1.squarespace.com Retrieving and Applying Knowledge to Different Examples...by AC Butler · Cited by 87 — However, there is growing interest in... At the same time, research cautions against expecting dramatic improvements in every form of complex reasoning. Transfer becomes harder as tasks grow more complicated, meaning application prompts should progress gradually from familiar to unfamiliar situations instead of jumping immediately to highly novel problems. [Oxford University Research Archive] ora.ox.ac.uk ford University Research ArchiveAn investigation into the impact of retrieval practice...February 10, 2023 — by P Robotham · 2020 — St... Published: February 10, 2023 How to Study Effectively 3: Active Retrieval & Desirable Difficulties Channel: PsychExamReview Open on YouTube Common mistakes when writing application prompts Several design choices reduce the value of application practice. Disguised definition questions. Simply changing names or numbers"},{"title":"Brain Dumps","url":"/brain-dumps/","description":"A short brain dump can turn scattered recall into a map of missing steps, weak evidence and ideas that need repair.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Retrieval","level":3,"text":"Brain Dumps What a Brain Dump Really Shows Brain Dumps Brain Dumps What a Brain Dump Really Shows A short brain dump can turn scattered recall into a map of missing steps, weak evidence and ideas that need repair. A short brain dump can turn scattered recall into a map of missing steps, weak evidence and ideas that need repair. policy intervention What to write in three to five minutes How to sort facts, claims and examples How to turn gaps into the next practice target improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval brain dumps Introduction A brain dump is one of the simplest forms of retrieval practice : you close your notes, set a short timer, and write everything you can remember about a topic from memory. Its value is not the amount you recall but the pattern of what appears, what is missing, and how ideas connect. That makes a brain dump especially useful for improving reasoning rather than merely memorising facts. Instead of asking, \"Do I recognise this?\", it asks, \"Can I reconstruct the argument without prompts?\" Research on retrieval practice consistently shows that actively recalling information strengthens learning more effectively than passive review, while also revealing misconceptions and gaps that guide later study. Center for Teaching and Learning+2Cult of Pedagogy [ctl.wustl.edu] ctl.wustl.edu Retrieval practice is the strategy of recalling facts, concepts, or events from memory in order to enhance learning... Within a broader programme of retrieval practice, the brain dump serves as a diagnostic tool. It creates a visible record of your thinking before outside information influences it, allowing you to inspect the reasoning itself rather than your ability to recognise the correct answer afterwards. Pooja K. Agarwal, Ph.D. [retrievalpractice.org] retrievalpractice.org Pooja K Agarwal, Ph.D.Brain Dump: A small strategy with a big impact30 Nov 2017 — Retrieval practice can take 5 minutes or less. A few tips: Have... What a brain dump really shows Many learners assume a brain dump measures memory alone. In practice, it exposes the structure of understanding. Someone may remember a conclusion perfectly but forget the evidence that supports it. Another person may recall several isolated facts yet fail to explain how they relate. A third may confidently define a concept but struggle to produce a concrete example. These are different kinds of learning problems, and each suggests a different next step. Because the exercise happens before reopening notes, it also reduces the illusion of competence created by familiar looking material. Instead of relying on recognition, the learner sees which parts of an explanation can genuinely be reconstructed from memory. This makes the exercise valuable for developing analytical thinking as well as long term retention. Center for Teaching and Learning+2Structural Learning [ctl.wustl.edu] ctl.wustl.edu Retrieval practice is the strategy of recalling facts, concepts, or events from memory in order to enhance learning... What to write in three to five minutes The exercise works best when it is deliberately brief. A short time limit encourages recall rather than perfection. Begin with a clear prompt rather than a broad subject. Instead of writing about \"statistics\", ask yourself: Why does correlation not imply causation ? How does this policy produce its intended effect? What are the steps in this argument? What assumptions does this explanation depend on? Then write continuously for three to five minutes without checking any source. Do not edit, reorganise or correct while writing. The goal is to capture your current mental model exactly as it exists. Useful material to include includes: definitions you can remember; causal links between ideas; examples or case studies; exceptions or limitations; questions that occur naturally while writing. Research based guidance on classroom retrieval practice recommends keeping activities low stakes, individual and closed book where practical, precisely because the objective is learning rather than assessment. Even a five minute free recall exercise can strengthen later understanding while exposing areas that need attention. Pooja K. Agarwal, Ph.D.+2Center for Teaching and Learning [retrievalpractice.org] retrievalpractice.org Pooja K Agarwal, Ph.D.Brain Dump: A small strategy with a big impact30 Nov 2017 — Retrieval practice can take 5 minutes or less. A few tips: Have... Quick Tip Retrieval Practice with Brain Dumps Channel: Side by Side Consulting Open on YouTube How to sort facts, claims and examples The first draft of a brain dump is often chaotic. That disorder is useful because it reflects how knowledge is currently organised in memory. The next step is to classify what you wrote. A simple three column structure helps reveal reasoning weaknesses. TypeQuestion to askFactsWhat information do I remember?ClaimsWhat conclusions or explanations am I making?ExamplesWhat evidence or situations support those claims? Once the information is separated, inspect the links between the columns. A reasoning chain becomes much easier to evaluate: Does every important claim have supporting evidence? Do the examples actually demonstrate the claim? Have I skipped intermediate steps? Did I assume something without stating it? Are definitions being used consistently? This process often uncovers \"hidden jumps\" in reasoning. For example, a learner may write: Inflation rose. Interest rates increased. Spending slowed. Only after sorting the ideas does it become obvious that the causal mechanism—higher borrowing costs reducing demand—is missing entirely. Similarly, someone explaining evolution might remember natural selection and adaptation but forget variation within populations, making the explanation incomplete rather than incorrect. These missing links are exactly the points where further retrieval practice should concentrate. How to turn gaps into the next practice target The most valuable part of a brain dump begins after you compare it with reliable notes or reference material. Rather than simply filling every blank, classify each gap according to its cause. Missing facts indicate knowledge that has not yet been retained. Broken reasoning suggests that individual facts exist but have not been connected into a coherent explanation. Weak examples often show that understanding remains abstract and may not transfer well to new situations. False confidence appears when an explanation sounded convincing until comparison revealed omitted assumptions or incorrect details. Each category suggests a different follow up activity. If facts are missing, another retrieval session after spaced review may be sufficient. If reasoning is incomplete, practise reconstructing the entire causal chain without looking. If examples are weak, retrieve fresh examples rather than rereading definitions alone. This approach turns the brain dump into a planning tool rather than a grading exercise. The purpose is not to count mistakes but to identify the smallest change that will improve future thinking. How To Use The Free Recall Method To Learn Anything Faster Channel: Matthew Smith Open on YouTube Common patterns that reveal missing reasoning With repeated use, certain patterns tend to recur. Strong beginnings, weak endings. People often remember definitions but forget implications or applications. Conclusions without evidence. A learner recalls what is true but not why it is true. Lists without relationships. Multiple facts appear, yet no explanation connects them. Examples replacing explanations. A memorable example is recalled while the underlying principle is forgotten. Circular explanations. A concept is defined by repeating the original claim in different words rather than providing a genuine mechanism. These patterns matter because analytical skill depends on connecting information, not merely accumulating it. A brain dump makes those connections—or their absence—visible enough to improve. Mistakes that reduce the value of brain dumps Several habits weaken the exercise. Looking at notes \"just to check one point\" changes the task from retrieval into copying. Trying to produce polished prose encourages editing rather than honest recall. Writing for too long shifts attention towards completeness instead of diagnosis. Treating every omission as failure discourages experimentation. Retrieval practice works best when learners expect imperfect recall and use the resulting errors as guidance for future study rather than evidence of poor ability. Center for Teaching and Learning+2Pooja K. Agarwal, Ph.D. [ctl.wustl.edu] ctl.wustl.edu Retrieval practice is the strategy of recalling facts, concepts, or events from memory in order to enhance learning... Making brain dumps part of regular thinking A single brain dump provides a snapshot of current understanding. Repeating the exercise over time shows how reasoning develops. For analytical subjects, useful prompts include reconstructing an"},{"title":"Closed Book","url":"/closed-book/","description":"Closing the source and explaining an idea in plain language exposes what you can actually reconstruct, not just recognise.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Retrieval","level":3,"text":"Closed Book Can You Explain It Without Looking? Closed Book Closed Book Can You Explain It Without Looking? Closing the source and explaining an idea in plain language exposes what you can actually reconstruct, not just recognise. Closing the source and explaining an idea in plain language exposes what you can actually reconstruct, not just recognise. policy intervention Why recognition can feel like understanding How to write a useful closed book explanation How to compare, correct and retry improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval closed book Introduction Closing your notes and explaining an idea from memory is one of the simplest ways to discover whether you genuinely understand it. While rereading often creates a comforting sense of familiarity , a closed book explanation requires you to reconstruct the idea without prompts. That difference matters because analytical thinking depends on being able to explain, connect and apply knowledge when the original source is no longer available. Research on retrieval practice consistently shows that actively recalling information strengthens learning and exposes misunderstandings that passive review can hide. [PMC+2PsychNet] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe Use of Retrieval Practice in the Health Professions by MJ Serra · 2025 · Cited by 16 — Retrieval practice, or the active recall of information from memory, is a highly effective learning... A good closed book explanation is therefore not a memory contest. It is a diagnostic tool. If your explanation becomes vague, skips key steps or falls apart when you try to give an example, those failures identify exactly what still needs to be learned. Instead of treating mistakes as evidence of failure, this approach uses them as information for improving understanding. Why recognition can feel like understanding One reason people overestimate their understanding is that recognition is easier than recall . Looking at notes, diagrams or highlighted text provides constant cues. The material feels familiar, and that feeling is often mistaken for mastery. Psychologists describe this as an illusion of competence . During study, learners can become highly confident because the correct information is visible. When those cues disappear, however, they may struggle to explain even the central idea. Studies of learning judgements have shown that people commonly overestimate future recall when information is present during study, and that retrieving information provides a more accurate picture of what they actually know. [Bjork Learning and Forgetting Lab+2PubMed] bjorklab.psych.ucla.edu Bjork Learning and Forgetting LabIllusions of Competence in Monitoring One's Knowledge...by A Koriat · 2005 · Cited by 610 — The authors... A closed book explanation strips away those external supports. Instead of asking: \"Does this paragraph look familiar?\" it asks: \"Can I explain why this works?\" \"Can I reconstruct the reasoning?\" \"Can I use the idea in a different situation?\" Those questions resemble the demands of real analysis far more closely than simple recognition. The warning signs of weak understanding When explaining without looking, several patterns commonly appear. Missing causal links. You remember the conclusion but cannot explain why it follows. Definition without meaning. You can repeat technical terms but cannot explain them in ordinary language. Procedural gaps. You know the first and last steps but forget the reasoning in between. Example failure. You can state the rule but cannot produce or evaluate a concrete example. Concept confusion. Similar ideas merge together because their differences were never fully understood. These weaknesses often remain invisible while notes are open because the missing pieces are supplied automatically by the page. The Feynman Technique Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube How to write a useful closed book explanation The goal is not to reproduce a textbook paragraph word for word. A more useful test is whether you can explain the idea clearly enough that another interested person could follow your reasoning. A practical sequence is: 1. Study normally. 2. Close every source. 3. Write or speak an explanation entirely from memory. 4. Use plain language wherever possible. 5. Include one original example or application . 6. State any uncertainties instead of guessing. 7. Only then reopen the source. This sequence turns retrieval into evidence. The written explanation becomes an observable record of what your memory actually contains rather than what feels familiar. Aim for explanation rather than recital A weak explanation often sounds like disconnected notes: \"Retrieval practice improves memory because testing effect... active recall... stronger learning.\" A stronger explanation connects the ideas: \"Retrieval practice improves learning because reconstructing information strengthens the memory itself. When I have to retrieve an idea without looking, I discover whether I really understand it. If I cannot explain part of the reasoning, I know exactly what needs further study.\" Notice that the second explanation contains relationships, causes and consequences instead of isolated phrases. Research on self explanation and elaborative interrogation suggests that generating explanations helps learners build more coherent mental models, particularly when they are required to explain why something is true rather than merely restate it. [The Learning Scientists+2PubMed] learningscientists.org The Learning ScientistsElaboration as Self explanation20 Feb 2020 — Self explanation involves students trying to explain concepts to them... Use ordinary language first An effective check is whether you can temporarily avoid specialist vocabulary. Suppose you are learning about confirmation bias. Instead of beginning with the formal definition, first explain it as: \"People naturally notice evidence that agrees with what they already believe and overlook evidence that disagrees.\" Only afterwards introduce the technical term. If you cannot explain the idea without relying on jargon, the underlying concept may still be fragile. How to compare, correct and retry The learning happens after the explanation, not before. Once you reopen the original material, compare it carefully with what you produced from memory. Instead of simply marking answers right or wrong, ask: Which important ideas were completely absent? Which relationships did I misunderstand? Which examples were inaccurate? Which parts sounded confident but were actually incorrect? Which sections became vague because I relied on recognition instead of understanding? These comparisons identify specific weaknesses that can be repaired immediately. Using Desirable Difficulties to Enhance Learning, Dr. Robert Bjork Channel: LastingLearning.com Open on YouTube Correct the explanation, not just the notes Many learners simply reread the original chapter after spotting mistakes. A more effective approach is to rewrite the explanation itself. For example: [pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov of face memory: Clarity breeds familiarity PMCby HM Kleider · 2003 · Cited by 83 — When people perform a recognition memory task, they... Original explanation Compare with source Add missing reasoning Remove incorrect claims Produce a fresh closed book explanation without looking again Each cycle requires another reconstruction from memory rather than another exposure to the material. Retrieval practice research consistently finds that learning improves when recall attempts are followed by corrective feedback. The retrieval attempt identifies gaps, while accurate feedback prevents those gaps from becoming permanent misconceptions. [PMC+2PsychNet] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe Use of Retrieval Practice in the Health Professions by MJ Serra · 2025 · Cited by 16 — Retrieval practice, or the active recall of information from memory, is a highly effective learning... What makes this different from memorising A common misunderstanding is that closed book explanation encourages rote learning. Used well, it does the opposite. Pure memorisation focuses on reproducing exact wording. Closed book explanation focuses on reconstructing meaning. A learner who genuinely understands should usually be able to: explain the idea differently from the textbook, answer \"why\" as well as \"what\", generate a new example, [researchgate.net] researchgate.net PDF) Effective Use of Elaborative Interrogation to Improve...Mar 28, 2025 — For example, elaborative interrogation, a strategy in which... distinguish the concept from similar ideas, and recognise the limits of their own explanation. These are signs of usable knowledge rather than rehearsed language. This also explains why closed book explanations fit naturally alongside retrieval practice for improving analytical thinking. Analytical work rarely asks people to reproduce paragraphs verbatim. It asks them to justify decisions, interpret evidence, evaluate competing explanations"},{"title":"Low Stakes","url":"/low-stakes/","description":"Retrieval practice is most useful when it feels diagnostic rather than punitive, so small ungraded quizzes can support learning without constant judgement.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Retrieval","level":3,"text":"Low Stakes Testing Yourself Without Turning It Into Pressure Low Stakes Low Stakes Testing Yourself Without Turning It Into Pressure Retrieval practice is most useful when it feels diagnostic rather than punitive, so small ungraded quizzes can support learning without constant judgement. Retrieval practice is most useful when it feels diagnostic rather than punitive, so small ungraded quizzes can support learning without constant judgement. population context Why stakes change how retrieval feels How delayed quizzes support retention How to keep practice diagnostic and repeatable improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval low stakes Introduction Retrieval practice works best when learners treat a quiz as a way to discover what they know rather than to prove their ability. That distinction is more important than it first appears. A low stakes quiz is not simply a smaller version of an exam. Its purpose is to strengthen memory, reveal gaps in understanding and guide the next round of study without attaching meaningful consequences to mistakes . When learners expect judgement, rankings or penalties, attention can shift from improving understanding to protecting self esteem or chasing marks. Used thoughtfully, frequent, ungraded quizzes help keep retrieval focused on learning instead of performance. Research consistently shows that this approach can improve retention while, in many settings, reducing rather than increasing test anxiety. [ResearchGate+2Center for Teaching and Learning] researchgate.net low stakes testing may be an effective method of reducing test anxiety.Read more Do Practice Tests (Quizzes) Reduce or Provoke...Aug 25, 2023 — A Meta Analytic Review Practice testing is a powerful tool to... Why stakes change how retrieval feels The same act of recalling information can feel completely different depending on what is attached to the result. A learner answering five questions in a private notebook experiences a different psychological task from a learner answering the same five questions that determine a grade or public ranking. High stakes assessments naturally encourage performance goals: avoiding failure, protecting reputation or maximising marks. These goals are appropriate in formal examinations, but they can interfere with everyday learning because learners become less willing to guess, expose misunderstandings or attempt difficult questions. Low stakes quizzes instead encourage an improvement mindset. Errors become information about what needs further work rather than evidence of personal ability. [My College+2EEF] my.chartered.college low stakes but desirable difficulty how do we balance the two My CollegeLow stakes but desirable difficulty – how do we balance the two?20 Sept 2022 — There is a balance in creating something that is... This distinction also explains why retrieval practice should not be confused with constant testing. The educational benefit comes from the effort of recalling information and receiving useful feedback , not from repeatedly judging performance. How delayed quizzes support retention One of the most useful features of low stakes quizzes is that they can be delayed. Instead of testing immediately after studying, learners revisit ideas hours, days or weeks later, when recall requires genuine reconstruction rather than short term familiarity. That delay introduces what cognitive psychologists describe as a \"desirable difficulty\": retrieval becomes effortful enough to strengthen long term memory without becoming impossible. The temporary struggle is part of the learning process, provided the learner eventually receives corrective feedback. [Formative Action+2PMC] formative action.com desirable difficulties no pain no gain Formative ActionNo pain, no gain – on desirable difficulties13 May 2024 — Examples of desirable difficulties are retrieval practice (retr... Published: May 2024 Because there is little penalty for forgetting, delayed quizzes encourage honest diagnosis: forgotten concepts become visible before major assessments; partial understanding is easier to identify; learners discover which topics require another review; successful recall becomes progressively easier over repeated sessions. The quiz therefore becomes part of the learning cycle rather than its endpoint. Low Stakes Quizzing and Retrieval Practice Channel: Making Teaching Easier To Understand 💥 Open on YouTube How to keep practice diagnostic and repeatable The design of the quiz matters as much as its frequency. Low stakes retrieval is most effective when it continually generates information that learners can act upon. Useful features include: No meaningful grade. Completion credit, participation marks or no marks at all reduce pressure while maintaining engagement. Immediate or prompt feedback. Learners should compare their answers with correct explanations before misconceptions become reinforced. Short quizzes. Two to ten questions are often enough to identify strengths and weaknesses without creating exam like fatigue. Regular repetition. Small quizzes repeated across weeks generally produce better long term learning than occasional large tests. Mixed content. Including questions from earlier topics helps prevent forgetting and encourages connections across ideas. Private reflection. Learners benefit when incorrect answers become personal feedback rather than public embarrassment. My College+3Center for Teaching and Learning+3Georgia Online Learning & Development [ctl.wustl.edu] ctl.wustl.edu Quizzes can be free response, multiple choice, or another format. In class...Read more... Importantly, diagnostic quizzes should answer the question, \"What should I study next?\" rather than, \"How good am I?\" Can low stakes quizzes reduce exam anxiety? A common concern is that more quizzes simply create more stress . The available evidence suggests that this depends heavily on how the quizzes are implemented. Classroom research involving more than 1,400 secondary school students found that retrieval practice programmes not only improved learning but also reduced reported test anxiety for many students. Later reviews and a meta analysis similarly concluded that practice testing does not inevitably provoke anxiety and that genuinely low stakes implementation may reduce it overall. [Evidence Based Education+2ResearchGate] evidencebased.education Evidence Based EducationRetrieval Practice and Student Wellbeingby K Jones — Kate Jones exlains the importance of retrieval practice stud... Several mechanisms may explain this pattern: repeated exposure makes recall feel more familiar; learners gain more accurate knowledge of what they know; uncertainty decreases because weaknesses are identified early; success accumulates through repeated practice rather than relying on one major examination. However, these benefits are not guaranteed. Students with high existing test anxiety sometimes avoid self testing altogether because retrieval itself feels threatening. This creates an unfortunate cycle: the students who might benefit most from diagnostic retrieval may engage with it least unless teachers or learners deliberately keep the experience supportive and non punitive. [Evidence Based Education] evidencebased.education Evidence Based EducationRetrieval Practice and Student Wellbeingby K Jones — Kate Jones exlains the importance of retrieval practice stud... Making Low Stakes Practice Tests More Effective Channel: Edutopia Open on YouTube The main risk: low stakes can become \"secretly high stakes\" Not every quiz labelled \"low stakes\" actually feels low stakes. Practice quizzes begin to resemble examinations when they are: heavily weighted towards final grades; publicly ranked or compared; used primarily to identify weaker students; too long or too infrequent; accompanied by social pressure or embarrassment after mistakes. Research on assessment motivation also shows that reducing consequences too far can lower effort. If learners believe a quiz has absolutely no purpose, they may not engage seriously enough to produce useful retrieval. The practical challenge is therefore balance: enough importance that students try to recall honestly, but not enough that fear of failure dominates attention. [PMC+2My College] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCEffort in Low Stakes Assessments NIHby Y Attali · 2016 · Cited by 59 — Past research supports the assumption that tests with no personal consequences, that is, low... Making retrieval part of thinking rather than judging For improving thinking and analytical skills, the value of low stakes quizzes lies less in measuring knowledge than in exposing reasoning. Questions that ask learners to explain a concept, justify a conclusion, distinguish similar ideas or predict an outcome encourage retrieval that goes beyond memorising isolated facts. After attempting an answer, learners should compare their reasoning with the correct explanation and ask: Which part of my reasoning was missing? Which concept did I"},{"title":"Self Checks","url":"/self-checks/","description":"Practice tests can improve judgement about what is truly secure by replacing a feeling of knowing with recall evidence.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Retrieval","level":3,"text":"Self Checks Do You Know It, or Just Know It Looks Familiar? Self Checks Self Checks Do You Know It, or Just Know It Looks Familiar? Practice tests can improve judgement about what is truly secure by replacing a feeling of knowing with recall evidence. Practice tests can improve judgement about what is truly secure by replacing a feeling of knowing with recall evidence. mechanism Why predictions after restudy are often overconfident How retrieval gives better evidence about learning How to rate confidence after recall improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval self checks Introduction Practice tests do more than strengthen memory. They also provide evidence about what you genuinely know, making them one of the best opportunities to improve metacognition —the ability to judge the state of your own knowledge accurately enough to choose the right next step. The crucial question after a practice test is not simply \"What score did I get?\" but \"What does this performance tell me about what I can reliably recall, explain and apply?\" Research consistently shows that predictions made after retrieval attempts are better calibrated than predictions made after rereading because recall reveals the actual state of learning instead of relying on a feeling of familiarity . [Taylor & Francis Online+2SUNY Research Connect] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineTesting and metacognition: retrieval practise effects on...However, research primarily focussed on retrieval prac... Within retrieval practice , these metacognitive checks are valuable because they reduce overconfidence , expose hidden weaknesses and guide more efficient study decisions. Rather than treating a practice test as the end of learning, they turn it into a diagnostic tool. Do You Know It, or Just Know It Looks Familiar? Many learners mistake recognition for understanding. Seeing a familiar definition or recognising the correct answer in notes often produces a convincing feeling of knowing, even when the information cannot be recalled independently. This \"illusion of competence\" has been documented repeatedly in research on learning and metamemory. Familiarity is informative, but it is a poor substitute for successful retrieval when predicting future performance. [Columbia University+2PMC] columbia.edu Columbia UniversityMetacognitive Judgments and Control of Studyby J Metcalfe · Cited by 634 — In a recent article, Dunlosky and Lipko (20... A completed practice test changes the situation. Instead of asking how familiar the material feels, it provides direct evidence: Which answers came immediately without guessing? Which required long pauses or partial reconstruction? Which were confidently wrong? Which could be recalled but not fully explained? Which disappeared entirely without prompts? These observations are far more diagnostic than impressions formed while reading notes because they are based on performance rather than appearance. Why Predictions After Restudy Are Often Overconfident Restudying creates fluent processing. The material becomes easier to read, key terms seem obvious and explanations feel clear while they remain visible. Unfortunately, processing fluency is often mistaken for durable learning. Researchers studying judgments of learning have shown that people frequently predict high future performance immediately after rereading, even when later recall proves much weaker than expected. Learners often rely on cues such as ease of reading, familiarity or confidence while studying instead of cues that genuinely predict future memory. [tauberlab.com+2Columbia University] tauberlab.com TCU Memory & Aging LabDunlosky, J., & Tauber, S. K. (2014). Understanding people's metacognitive judgments: An isomechanism framework and... Practice tests interrupt this illusion because they replace subjective impressions with observable evidence. If retrieval is incomplete or slow, that difficulty becomes information rather than merely an unpleasant experience. Robert and Elizabeth Bjork have described this broader principle through the idea of \"desirable difficulties\": experiences that feel harder during learning can produce stronger long term retention and more realistic self evaluation. [Bjork Learning and Forgetting Lab] bjorklab.psych.ucla.edu robert a bjork publications Bjork Learning and Forgetting LabRobert A. Bjork – PublicationsBjork, R. A., & Bjork, E. L. (2019). The myth that blocking one's study or... Importantly, difficulty alone is not the goal. The value comes from difficulty revealing what has actually been learned. What Is Metacognition? Steps to Boost Learning in 10 Minutes Channel: Challenge Innovate Grow: Teacher & Learner Centre Open on YouTube How Retrieval Gives Better Evidence About Learning A retrieval attempt produces several signals that learners can use when judging their understanding. These signals are largely unavailable during passive review. Research suggests that people naturally incorporate multiple sources of evidence after retrieval, including whether the answer was correct, how confident they were during recall and how easily the answer came to mind. Together these cues produce more accurate judgments about future remembering than restudying alone. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersRetrieval Practice Facilitates Judgments of Learning...by X Chen · 2019 · Cited by 9 — Retrieval practice allows people to base... After each practice question, consider three separate pieces of evidence: Recall success. Could the answer be produced without looking? Retrieval quality. Was the answer complete, precise and well explained, or only partially reconstructed? Retrieval effort. Did the answer appear quickly or only after extended searching? These dimensions matter because identical scores can hide very different states of learning. Two learners may both answer correctly, but one retrieves immediately while the other reconstructs the answer slowly from fragments. The first is generally better positioned for later retention. Equally important are incorrect answers given with high confidence. Although they reveal misconceptions, they also provide valuable learning opportunities because learners often pay greater attention when surprised by confident errors , increasing the chance of correcting them after feedback. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Hypercorrection (psychology Hypercorrection (psychology How to Rate Confidence After Recall Confidence ratings become useful only when they are attached to completed retrieval attempts rather than predictions made beforehand. A practical scale might look like this: Very high confidence: recalled immediately, explained accurately and could apply the idea in a new example. Moderate confidence: recalled correctly but with hesitation or missing details. Low confidence: answer required guessing, prompting or reconstruction. No confidence: unable to retrieve without consulting the material. The important habit is calibration rather than optimism. Accurate confidence ratings help identify material that genuinely requires additional retrieval practice instead of encouraging unnecessary review of already secure topics. Research on retrieval based monitoring shows that confidence judgments become more accurate after testing because learners have access to better diagnostic cues than after passive study. [Taylor & Francis Online+2Frontiers] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineTesting and metacognition: retrieval practise effects on...However, research primarily focussed on retrieval prac... Study Smart: Understanding Metacognition Channel: NC State Open on YouTube Turning Metacognitive Judgments into Better Study Decisions Monitoring only improves learning if it changes what happens next. In the classic framework of metacognition, monitoring and control are linked processes: learners evaluate their knowledge, then regulate subsequent study accordingly. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Beyond the Prompt: Investigating Retrieval Based Monitoring...by M Wu · 2025 — Metacognitive monitoring involves the evaluation of an... After reviewing a practice test, useful decisions include: Prioritising concepts that were forgotten entirely over those recalled confidently. Revisiting explanations rather than isolated facts when answers lacked reasoning. Scheduling another retrieval session instead of immediately rereading. Mixing weak and strong topics to verify that improvement transfers beyond immediate repetition. Retesting corrected misconceptions after a delay rather than assuming that reading the correct answer has fixed them. This approach prevents wasted effort. Instead of studying everything equally, learners allocate attention according to evidence gathered from retrieval. Common Mistakes When Checking Your Own Learning Several habits weaken the value of metacognitive checks. Equating correct recognition with mastery. Choosing the correct option in multiple choice questions may conceal weak independent recall. Ignoring retrieval"},{"title":"Feedback Loop","url":"/feedback-loop/","description":"Retrieval practice works best when an attempted answer is checked, corrected and tested again before the error hardens.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Retrieval","level":3,"text":"Feedback Loop Why Wrong Answers Can Help Learning Feedback Loop Feedback Loop Why Wrong Answers Can Help Learning Retrieval practice works best when an attempted answer is checked, corrected and tested again before the error hardens. Retrieval practice works best when an attempted answer is checked, corrected and tested again before the error hardens. mechanism Why recall without feedback can mislead What good feedback should identify How to run the recall, compare, correct, retry loop improving your thinking and analytical skills retrieval feedback loop Introduction Retrieval practice is most effective when it is part of a feedback loop rather than a one off memory test. Trying to recall an answer exposes what you know, but the learning benefit comes from comparing that attempt with the correct information, understanding the mistake, and trying again. Without correction, an incorrect answer can become more familiar simply because it has been repeated. With timely, accurate feedback, the same error becomes a signal that directs attention to exactly what needs to change. Research consistently shows that combining retrieval with corrective feedback improves long term retention, reduces persistent misconceptions and produces more reliable learning than retrieval alone in many situations. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov giving the correct answer), but feedback enhances the benefits of testing. In addition...Read more... Why recall without feedback can mislead Attempting to retrieve information is valuable because it reveals the current state of memory. However, that alone does not guarantee improvement. If a learner confidently recalls an incorrect explanation and never checks it, the act of retrieval may strengthen the accessibility of that wrong response. This risk is especially important when the learner cannot independently detect the error. Multiple choice questions, for example, may expose learners to plausible but incorrect alternatives. Without corrective feedback, some of those incorrect options can later be remembered as true. Experiments have shown that providing feedback after testing greatly reduces this problem while preserving the benefits of retrieval practice . [PsychNet] psychnet.wustl.edu Butler et al 2008 JEPLMC C., & Roediger, H. L., III (2008). Feedback enhances the positive effects and reduces the negative effects of multiple choice testing. Me... The danger is therefore not that retrieval causes errors, but that uncorrected errors become familiar. A feedback loop interrupts this process by replacing a mistaken memory with a more accurate one before the incorrect version becomes entrenched. What good feedback should identify The most useful feedback does more than mark an answer as right or wrong. It explains what was missing, confused or incomplete so that the learner can update their mental model. Effective feedback typically identifies: [pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov giving the correct answer), but feedback enhances the benefits of testing. In addition...Read more... The correct answer so there is no uncertainty about the target. The precise error , distinguishing between factual mistakes , missing steps and conceptual misunderstandings. Why the error occurred , such as confusing similar concepts or applying a rule in the wrong context. The key difference between the incorrect and correct responses. An opportunity to retrieve again , allowing the corrected knowledge to be reconstructed rather than merely reread. This kind of feedback treats mistakes as diagnostic information. Instead of viewing an error as failure, it uses the error to pinpoint exactly where learning should occur. Research on feedback during retrieval practice shows that explanatory feedback generally produces stronger later memory than simple right or wrong signals because it supports reconstruction rather than passive recognition. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effect of feedback type on enhancing subsequent memoryby L Wang · 2021 · Cited by 8 — Compared to the condition of no feedback, giving... Making Retrieval Practice a Classroom Routine Channel: Edutopia · Views: 283.0K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 3 minutes 51 seconds Open on YouTube Why wrong answers often become memorable after correction One of the more surprising findings in learning research is that some mistakes are corrected exceptionally well. When learners make an error with high confidence and immediately discover the correct answer, they often remember that correction better than information they never misunderstood. This phenomenon is known as the hypercorrection effect . One explanation is that confident mistakes create a strong mismatch between expectation and reality. The unexpected correction captures attention, making the revised information more memorable than routine confirmation of something already believed. [Springer Nature Link] link.springer.com Springer Nature LinkThe hypercorrection effect persists over a week, but high...by AC Butler · 2011 · Cited by 143 — When subjects faile... Not every mistake produces this effect. Errors that receive no correction, vague feedback or feedback long after the learner has forgotten the original attempt are less likely to generate the same durable learning. How to run the recall, compare, correct, retry loop A productive feedback loop is simple but deliberate. 1. Recall . Close the notes and answer from memory, even if uncertain. 2. Compare. Check the answer against a trusted source rather than relying on intuition. 3. Correct. Identify exactly what differed and rewrite or explain the correct version in your own words. 4. Retry. Attempt the same question again without looking at the answer. 5. Revisit later. Return after a delay to confirm that the correction has been retained. The final retrieval attempt is important because it confirms that the correction has become part of memory rather than remaining something that merely looked familiar while reading. Research on unsuccessful retrieval attempts demonstrates that trying to answer before seeing the solution can enhance later learning, provided that accurate feedback follows the attempt. The effort invested in retrieval appears to prepare the mind to encode the correction more effectively. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov These results demonstrate that retrieval attempts enhance future learning; they...Read more... When should feedback be given? There is no single timing that is optimal for every learning situation. Immediate feedback is particularly useful when learners are likely to remember their incorrect reasoning or when misconceptions could quickly become reinforced. It prevents repeated practice of the same mistake and is especially valuable for beginners learning unfamiliar material. [PsychNet] psychnet.wustl.edu Butler et al 2008 JEPLMC C., & Roediger, H. L., III (2008). Feedback enhances the positive effects and reduces the negative effects of multiple choice testing. Me... Delayed feedback can also be effective under some conditions. Waiting allows learners another opportunity to retrieve the answer before seeing the correction, increasing retrieval effort. Recent research suggests that both immediate and delayed feedback can support learning, with the best choice depending on the task, the learner's prior knowledge and the purpose of the practice. [Nature] nature.com Timing of feedback and retrieval practice: a laboratory...by S Aljabri · 2024 · Cited by 6 — This study aims to examine the effect... Rather than asking whether feedback should always be immediate or always delayed, a more useful question is whether the timing supports another meaningful retrieval before forgetting becomes overwhelming. Common mistakes that weaken the feedback loop Several habits reduce the value of retrieval practice even when learners believe they are checking their work. Looking at the answer before making a genuine retrieval attempt. Checking only whether an answer was correct without understanding why. Repeating the correction by rereading instead of retrieving it again. Ignoring confident mistakes because they feel embarrassing rather than informative. Practising only questions already answered correctly instead of revisiting corrected errors. Each of these shortcuts interrupts the cycle that transforms mistakes into stronger understanding. The Testing Effect: The Science of How To Actually Remember Channel: Dr. Erik Albinson Learning & Performance Coach · Views: 740 · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Why feedback loops improve analytical thinking Analytical thinking depends on more than remembering isolated facts. It requires recognising faulty assumptions , distinguishing similar concepts and revising beliefs when evidence contradicts them. Feedback rich retrieval practice trains exactly these habits. Each cycle of recall, correction and renewed recall strengthens not only memory but also the ability to monitor one's"},{"title":"Objections","url":"/objections/","description":"Seeking the strongest case against your conclusion helps test whether your reasoning can survive serious challenge.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Objections What Is the Best Case Against You? Objections Objections What Is the Best Case Against You? Seeking the strongest case against your conclusion helps test whether your reasoning can survive serious challenge. Seeking the strongest case against your conclusion helps test whether your reasoning can survive serious challenge. policy intervention Steelman the opposition Use objections constructively Decide after challenge improving your thinking and analytical skills objections Introduction Strong objections before you decide are not a sign that your thinking is weak. They are a way of finding out whether your conclusion can survive contact with its most serious alternatives. The practice is simple: before committing, deliberately ask for the strongest case against your preferred answer, not the easiest objection to dismiss. That can mean steelmanning an opponent’s view, running a pre mortem, assigning a devil’s advocate, comparing rival hypotheses , or asking what evidence would make you change your mind. The point is not to become paralysed by doubt. It is to make the decision less dependent on first impressions, group confidence, status pressure, or evidence gathered only to support what you already wanted to do. This matters because confirmation bias is not a rare flaw in unusually closed minded people. Raymond Nickerson’s widely cited review defines it as seeking or interpreting evidence in ways that favour existing beliefs, expectations, or a current hypothesis, and documents it across many practical settings. [pages.ucsd.edu] pages.ucsd.edu Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation bias, as the term is typically used in the psychological literat... Published: October 6, 2004 Good analytical skill therefore requires more than “being rational” in the abstract. It needs a repeatable moment, before the decision hardens, when the best available objections are allowed to do real work. Why the best objection is different from ordinary disagreement Ordinary disagreement often comes too late, too vaguely, or too defensively. Someone says, “I’m not sure,” and the group treats that as a mood rather than a reason. Someone raises a risk, and the proposer answers it point by point without reconsidering the plan. Someone plays “devil’s advocate” by making a weak version of the opposing case, which only makes the original conclusion feel stronger. A strong objection routine is more demanding. It asks: if an intelligent, well informed critic thought our conclusion was wrong, what would their best argument be? What evidence would they emphasise? Which assumption would they attack first? What would they say we are underestimating? That distinction is close to what people now call steelmanning: constructing the most persuasive version of the opposing case before responding to it. In analytical decision making, the value is not politeness for its own sake. It is diagnostic. A weak objection tells you little; a strong objection reveals where your conclusion is load bearing. If your decision depends on a market growing quickly, a team delivering on schedule, a source being reliable, or a policy working as intended, the strongest objection points to the assumption that needs testing first. This is why “strong objections before you decide” belongs inside improving analytical skills rather than simply communication skills. It changes the sequence of thought. Instead of deciding first and defending afterwards, you expose the decision to organised challenge while revision is still cheap. Steelman the opposition Steelmanning is most useful when the issue is emotionally attractive, socially loaded, or already surrounded by a preferred story. The stronger your attachment to a conclusion, the more tempting it is to answer only the weakest critics. A steelman reverses that habit. A practical steelman has three parts . First, state the opposing view in language its own supporters would recognise. Second, identify the evidence or values that make it plausible. Third, name the condition under which it might beat your current conclusion. For example, if you favour launching a product quickly, the steelman is not “some cautious people hate risk”. It might be: “The strongest case against launching now is that early failure would damage trust, support capacity is not ready, and a smaller private release would produce better evidence without creating public expectations.” That kind of objection is harder to brush aside because it does not caricature the critic. It also separates two questions that are often blurred: whether the goal is desirable, and whether the chosen route is robust. The same principle appears in structured analytic work. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses, associated with former CIA analyst Richards Heuer, asks analysts to compare multiple plausible explanations against the evidence rather than build a case for the hypothesis that first seems likely. A primer on the method describes it as evaluating plausible and mutually exclusive hypotheses, while Heuer’s own work stresses that alternative hypotheses should be carefully considered, especially those not easily disproved by available information. [Futuribles] futuribles.com Analysis of Competing Hypotheses Analysis of Competing HypothesesSeptember 4, 2019 — Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH) is an intelligence analysis method b... Published: September 4, 2019 The practical lesson is simple: do not merely ask whether your conclusion has support. Ask whether another conclusion explains the same evidence as well or better. Steelmanning is especially powerful when it produces a sentence like this: “The best case against us is not that we are stupid; it is that we are relying on an assumption we have not yet earned.” That sentence often marks the beginning of better thinking. Use objections constructively The main danger with objections is that they turn into theatre. A team appoints a critic, everyone waits for the ritual challenge, and then the original plan continues unchanged. Constructive objection has to be built into the decision process, not added as a decorative final step. One well known method is the pre mortem. Gary Klein describes it as an exercise after a team has been briefed on a plan: members imagine the plan has failed and then generate the threats and hurdles that could explain the failure. [garyklein] gary klein.com Open source on gary klein.com. In his Harvard Business Review account, the team is asked to assume the project has already failed and then write down every plausible reason, including concerns people might otherwise avoid mentioning because they seem impolitic. [Harvard Business Review] hbr.org performing a project premortem performing a project premortem The psychological move is important: it gives permission to surface doubts without making the doubter seem disloyal. A pre mortem works because it changes the question. “Will this fail?” invites reassurance. “It has failed; what happened?” invites diagnosis. The second question often uncovers operational problems that ordinary optimism hides: dependence on one supplier, weak user adoption, unclear ownership, unrealistic timing, political resistance, missing data, or a hidden incentive that will push people away from the intended behaviour. A second method is “consider the opposite”. In medical and investigative settings, debiasing literature describes this as deliberately seeking evidence that would support a decision opposite to the initial impression. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. A 2021 study in criminal investigation examined “consider the opposite” as a cognitive support strategy for detectives’ professional judgement, grounding the method in the need to counter known shortcomings in investigative decision making. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. The method is modest but useful: before concluding, ask what you would look for if your current answer were false. A third method is structured devil’s advocacy. In group decisions, devil’s advocacy and dialectical inquiry have been studied as ways to introduce cognitive conflict before commitment. A classic strategic decision making study found that groups using dialectical inquiry and devil’s advocacy made higher quality decisions than consensus groups, while later summaries note that these approaches can reduce process losses in face to face decision teams. [JSTOR] jstor.org Experiential Effects of Dialectical Inquiry, Devil's Advocacy, Experiential Effects of Dialectical Inquiry, Devil's Advocacy, The key is structure. A devil’s advocate who merely irritates the room is not enough; the role must be authorised to test assumptions, request evidence, and force comparison with alternatives. Constructive objections should therefore be specific enough to change the decision. A good objection does at least one of the following:"},{"title":"Rival Hypotheses","url":"/rival-hypotheses/","description":"Comparing rival explanations against the same evidence helps prevent one plausible story from becoming the only story.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Objections","level":3,"text":"Rival Hypotheses Could Another Explanation Fit Better? Rival Hypotheses Rival Hypotheses Could Another Explanation Fit Better? Comparing rival explanations against the same evidence helps prevent one plausible story from becoming the only story. Comparing rival explanations against the same evidence helps prevent one plausible story from becoming the only story. dataset evidence List plausible explanations Test evidence against each option Watch for surviving alternatives improving your thinking and analytical skills objections rival hypotheses Introduction When you are close to making a decision, the greatest risk is often not a lack of evidence but becoming attached to the first explanation that seems convincing. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH) offers a practical way to resist that tendency. Rather than asking, \"How can I prove my preferred explanation?\", it asks, \"Which explanation survives the evidence best after every plausible alternative has had a fair chance?\" Originally developed by intelligence analyst Richards Heuer to improve analytical judgement under uncertainty, the approach can be adapted to everyday decisions involving careers, finances, relationships, health, or major purchases. [ialeia.org] ialeia.org PDF] The Psychology of Intelligence Analysis December 13, 2005 — Heuer's concept of “Analysis of Competing Hypotheses” (ACH) is among his most important contributions to the developm... Published: December 13, 2005 Within the broader practice of testing strong objections before deciding, ACH shifts attention from defending one attractive story to comparing several plausible stories against the same evidence. That comparison often reveals that evidence is less decisive than it first appeared, or that an overlooked explanation fits the facts equally well. Why one convincing story is rarely enough Most real world evidence is compatible with more than one explanation. A delayed reply from a colleague could indicate disinterest, excessive workload, technical problems, or simple forgetfulness. Falling sales could reflect weaker demand, stronger competitors, poor marketing, seasonal variation, or measurement error. The challenge is not finding evidence for one explanation; it is determining whether that evidence distinguishes between competing explanations. Research on confirmation bias shows that people naturally seek, interpret, and remember information that supports their existing beliefs while giving less weight to contradictory evidence. Simply becoming aware of this tendency does not reliably eliminate it, which is why structured comparison techniques have been developed. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias ACH addresses this by making alternative explanations explicit before evaluating the evidence. List plausible explanations before collecting more evidence The first practical step is not to choose a favourite hypothesis but to generate several reasonable alternatives. For everyday decisions, these alternatives do not need to be exhaustive. They should simply represent genuinely different explanations that could account for what you currently observe. Suppose you are deciding whether to leave your current job because you feel increasingly dissatisfied. Instead of assuming that \"my job is the problem\", you might consider: The role itself no longer matches your interests. The current manager is the main source of dissatisfaction. Temporary workload or personal stress is colouring your judgement. Career progression has stalled rather than the work itself becoming unsuitable. Another employer would probably create similar frustrations. Several of these explanations may initially seem plausible. Their value lies in forcing the decision to compete against realistic alternatives rather than against no alternative at all. Intelligence Analysis Skills: Analysis of Competing Hypotheses (Part 1) Channel: Brian Urlacher Open on YouTube Test the same evidence against every explanation The defining feature of ACH is that evidence is examined across all hypotheses rather than building a separate case for each one. Heuer argued that the most useful evidence is not necessarily evidence that supports a hypothesis, but evidence that clearly contradicts competing explanations because it has high \"diagnostic\" value. [ialeia.org] ialeia.org PDF] The Psychology of Intelligence Analysis December 13, 2005 — Heuer's concept of “Analysis of Competing Hypotheses” (ACH) is among his most important contributions to the developm... Published: December 13, 2005 A simple comparison table can make this clearer. EvidenceJob mismatchManager problemTemporary stressCareer stagnationEnjoyed similar work previouslyWeakensNeutralSupportsNeutralProblems began after management changedNeutralStrongly supportsWeakensNeutralDissatisfaction continues during holidaysSupportsNeutralWeakensNeutralPromotion opportunities disappearedNeutralNeutralNeutralSupports Notice that the goal is not to count supporting observations. Instead, ask which pieces of evidence genuinely separate one explanation from another. Many facts are consistent with several hypotheses. Those facts contribute relatively little to deciding between them. Give extra weight to disconfirming evidence One of ACH's most important insights is that analysts should actively search for evidence that would weaken their preferred explanation rather than accumulate additional supporting examples. Suppose you believe that a friend's recent behaviour means they are avoiding you. Instead of looking for further signs of distance, deliberately search for observations that would make this explanation less convincing. For example: Have they become less responsive to everyone? Have they recently experienced major work or family pressures? Do they still initiate contact in other ways? Is there evidence that communication problems affect multiple relationships rather than only yours? Disconfirming evidence often provides more information than additional confirming evidence because it directly challenges the explanation you currently favour. This emphasis on inconsistency rather than confirmation distinguishes ACH from ordinary intuition . [SANS Internet Storm Center] isc.sans.edu SANS Internet Storm CenterAnalysis of Competing Hypotheses (ACH part 1)28 May 2017 — ACH is an analytic process that identifies a set of... Published: May 2017 Watch for explanations that refuse to disappear Sometimes one explanation clearly outperforms the others. Often it does not. A valuable outcome of ACH is recognising that several explanations remain viable because available evidence genuinely cannot distinguish between them. Rather than treating this as failure, it changes the decision itself. Instead of asking: Which explanation is definitely correct? the question becomes: Which explanation currently has the strongest support, and what evidence would most efficiently distinguish between the remaining possibilities? This naturally encourages better information gathering. If you are deciding whether to buy a house, for example, uncertainty about future local employment might dominate the decision. Rather than collecting more property photographs or reading more estate agent descriptions, you might investigate employer announcements, infrastructure projects, or population trends because those observations better discriminate between competing forecasts. Analysis of Competing Hypotheses Channel: S2 Underground Open on YouTube Everyday situations where rival hypotheses improve judgement ACH is most useful when evidence is incomplete and emotions make one explanation especially attractive. Common applications include: Financial decisions: distinguishing between a temporary market decline and a permanent change in business quality before investing. Workplace problems: separating personality conflict from genuine organisational dysfunction. Health choices: recognising that one symptom may have several possible causes while awaiting professional assessment. Relationships: considering misunderstanding, external stress, changing priorities, or communication failures before assuming harmful intent. Major purchases: deciding whether dissatisfaction with a current product reflects genuine limitations or unrealistic expectations. In each case, comparing explanations reduces the chance that the first plausible narrative becomes the only narrative considered. Recognise the limits of the method ACH is not a guarantee of correct decisions. Research evaluating its effectiveness has produced mixed findings. Some studies suggest that structured comparison can reduce aspects of confirmation bias, while others find that analysts do not consistently follow the method, and that its benefits depend on the complexity of the task, the quality of evidence, and how carefully the process is applied. Some experiments have even found increases in inconsistency or judgement errors when users apply the technique mechanically without"},{"title":"Opposite Test","url":"/opposite-test/","description":"Considering the opposite means deliberately looking for evidence that would support the answer you first rejected.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Objections","level":3,"text":"Opposite Test What Would You Look For If Wrong? Opposite Test Opposite Test What Would You Look For If Wrong? Considering the opposite means deliberately looking for evidence that would support the answer you first rejected. Considering the opposite means deliberately looking for evidence that would support the answer you first rejected. mechanism Reverse the first explanation Search for disconfirming evidence Use the test without endless doubt improving your thinking and analytical skills objections opposite test Introduction First impressions often feel like conclusions. A plausible explanation appears quickly, fits the facts you have noticed, and begins shaping what you look for next. The problem is that once an initial explanation takes hold, people naturally become better at finding evidence that supports it than evidence that challenges it. The \"considering the opposite\" technique is a practical way to interrupt that process. Instead of asking, \"Why is my first explanation correct?\", it asks, \"If this explanation were wrong, what would I expect to find instead?\" This simple shift does not require abandoning your judgement or treating every possibility as equally likely. Its purpose is to prevent early impressions from hardening into unquestioned beliefs before the evidence has been properly tested. Psychological research has repeatedly shown that deliberately generating reasons why an initial conclusion might be false reduces several forms of confirmation bias more effectively than merely trying to \"be objective\". [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... What Would You Look For If Wrong? The central mechanism is surprisingly straightforward. When people form an early explanation, they usually begin interpreting new information through that explanation. Evidence that fits seems persuasive; evidence that conflicts is often dismissed as an exception or explained away. This does not usually happen through deliberate dishonesty. It is a normal feature of human reasoning. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 Considering the opposite deliberately reverses that sequence. Rather than asking how new evidence supports your preferred explanation, you ask what evidence would convince a reasonable person that another explanation is better. That changes three parts of the thinking process at once: it expands the range of explanations under consideration; it encourages active searches for contradictory information instead of supportive information alone; it weakens the tendency to interpret every ambiguous fact as confirming the original belief. The aim is not to replace one certainty with another. It is to delay commitment until competing explanations have received a fair test. How to Overcome Confirmation Bias Channel: Phil McKinney Open on YouTube Reverse the First Explanation First explanations are often generated rapidly because they are coherent, familiar or emotionally satisfying—not necessarily because they are the most accurate. Imagine arriving at work and seeing a colleague who avoids eye contact and gives a brief reply. Your immediate explanation might be that they are annoyed with you. Once that interpretation forms, every later interaction may appear to confirm it. The opposite test asks a different question: \"Suppose they are not annoyed. What else would explain exactly the same behaviour?\" Possible alternatives might include: they are under unusual time pressure ; they have received worrying personal news; they are concentrating on another problem; they simply did not notice your greeting. The important point is not that one of these alternatives must be correct. The value comes from forcing your mind to search beyond the first narrative that felt convincing. Research by Charles Lord, Mark Lepper and Elizabeth Preston demonstrated that asking participants to consider explanations opposite to their current belief reduced biased evaluation of evidence more effectively than simply instructing them to be fair or unbiased. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... CONFIRMATION BIAS: The Hidden Bias Controlling Your Thoughts (And How to Break Free!) Channel: Tiyana J Open on YouTube Search for Disconfirming Evidence Considering the opposite only works if it changes what evidence you seek. Instead of collecting additional examples that support your preferred explanation, deliberately ask what observations would weaken it. Useful questions include: What evidence would surprise me? What observation would force me to revise my view? Which fact have I quietly assumed rather than verified? If another explanation were true, what would I expect to see that I have not yet checked? For example, if you conclude that declining sales are caused by poor staff performance, confirming evidence is easy to find: missed tasks, slow service or customer complaints. The opposite test asks whether the evidence also fits other explanations: Has customer traffic changed? Have competitors altered prices? Has the business relocated? Have seasonal patterns changed? Searching for these possibilities increases the chance that genuinely diagnostic evidence—not merely supportive evidence—drives the decision. This distinction is important because confirmation bias often operates through selective information gathering rather than outright misinterpretation. People naturally ask questions that are likely to confirm their current hypothesis instead of questions that best distinguish between competing hypotheses. [UC San Diego Pages] pages.ucsd.edu UC San Diego Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UC San Diego PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confi... Published: October 6, 2004 Why This Works Better Than Simply \"Being Objective\" Many people respond to bias by telling themselves to stay open minded. Unfortunately, psychological evidence suggests that this instruction is often too vague to change reasoning. The considering the opposite strategy is more effective because it replaces a general intention with a concrete cognitive task. Instead of trying not to be biased, you actively construct evidence for an alternative explanation. That matters because generating an opposing explanation forces retrieval of information that would otherwise remain mentally unavailable. It interrupts the automatic tendency to strengthen the first interpretation simply because it came first. Experiments comparing these approaches found that explicit opposite generation reduced biased judgement more reliably than generic instructions to avoid bias. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... Use the Test Without Endless Doubt Considering the opposite is not a licence for permanent scepticism. There comes a point when enough evidence supports one explanation that further searching produces little additional value. The purpose is to improve decisions before commitment, not prevent commitment altogether. A practical stopping rule is: Introduction to Cognitive Bias: Crash Course Scientific Thinking 1 Channel: CrashCourse Open on YouTube 1. Write down your initial explanation. 2. Generate at least one serious alternative. 3. Identify the evidence that would distinguish between them. 4. Gather that evidence where reasonably possible. 5. Decide using whichever explanation best fits the available facts. Once genuinely discriminating evidence has been examined, continuing to invent increasingly unlikely alternatives usually creates delay without improving judgement. The goal is disciplined testing rather than infinite uncertainty. Common Mistakes Several habits make the opposite test much less effective. Inventing a weak alternative. If the competing explanation is obviously implausible, rejecting it teaches you nothing. The alternative should be one that an informed, reasonable person might genuinely believe. Looking only for confirming examples. Simply imagining another possibility is insufficient if you continue collecting evidence only for your preferred explanation. Treating every explanation as equally likely. Considering the opposite is about testing, not pretending that all ideas deserve equal confidence regardless of evidence. Changing the standard of proof. A common form of confirmation bias is demanding much stronger evidence for ideas you dislike than for ideas you already favour."},{"title":"Devil s Advocate","url":"/devil-s-advocate/","description":"Devil's advocacy only helps when the critic has enough structure and authority to test assumptions before consensus locks in.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Objections","level":3,"text":"Devil s Advocate When Dissent Actually Improves Decisions Devil s Advocate Devil s Advocate When Dissent Actually Improves Decisions Devil's advocacy only helps when the critic has enough structure and authority to test assumptions before consensus locks in. Devil's advocacy only helps when the critic has enough structure and authority to test assumptions before consensus locks in. policy intervention Separate useful challenge from theatre Give the critic real authority Compare advocacy with consensus pressure improving your thinking and analytical skills objections devil s advocate Introduction Strong objections improve decisions only if they are heard before a group becomes committed to a preferred answer. Assigning a devil's advocate is one of the oldest structured methods for achieving this. Instead of waiting for disagreement to arise naturally, the group deliberately gives someone responsibility for challenging the leading proposal, exposing hidden assumptions , identifying overlooked risks, and forcing alternatives to compete with the favoured plan. Research suggests that this kind of organised dissent can improve the quality of strategic decisions, but only when it is genuine rather than symbolic. A critic who lacks authority, information, or protection is unlikely to change the group's thinking, while a well supported challenge can prevent premature consensus and reveal weaknesses that enthusiastic agreement conceals. [Academy of Management Journals+2ResearchGate] journals.aom.org Members of such groups reported Academy of Management JournalsExperiential Effects of Dialectical Inquiry, Devil's Advocacy...by DM Schweiger · 1989 · Cited by 928 — Co... Within the broader practice of making strong objections before deciding, devil's advocacy is distinctive because it changes the group's process rather than simply encouraging individuals to \"think critically\". The intervention is designed to interrupt consensus before it hardens. Separate useful challenge from theatre Many meetings claim to welcome a \"devil's advocate\", yet the role often becomes theatrical. Someone raises superficial objections, everyone responds quickly, and the original proposal proceeds unchanged. This appearance of scrutiny can actually reinforce overconfidence because participants believe the idea has survived criticism when it has only defeated weak arguments. Effective devil's advocacy is different. The advocate is expected to construct the strongest credible case against the preferred option, using evidence that informed critics would actually rely on. The goal is not obstruction or negativity but testing whether the proposal remains convincing after its assumptions have been examined. Research on strategic decision making distinguishes this structured conflict from casual disagreement. Laboratory and managerial studies found that groups using formal devil's advocacy produced higher quality strategic decisions than groups relying on ordinary consensus discussions because participants engaged in more critical re evaluation of assumptions rather than simply defending initial preferences. [Academy of Management Journals+2JSTOR] journals.aom.org Members of such groups reported Academy of Management JournalsExperiential Effects of Dialectical Inquiry, Devil's Advocacy...by DM Schweiger · 1989 · Cited by 928 — Co... Useful devil's advocacy therefore asks questions such as: Which assumption would cause this plan to fail if it proved false? What evidence have we ignored because it conflicts with our preferred explanation? Which realistic alternative deserves another comparison? What would an informed outsider criticise first? These questions shift discussion from defending positions to testing them. Red Teaming for Decision Support Channel: Rune Storesund Open on YouTube Give the critic real authority The role only works when the advocate has enough influence to slow the group's rush towards agreement. If everyone understands that the criticism is merely ceremonial, members quickly discount it. Several conditions consistently appear in research and practical guidance: Access to the same information as everyone proposing the plan. Permission to delay commitment until key objections receive answers. Support from the meeting leader , who treats objections as part of the process rather than personal resistance. Protection from social penalties , so challenging the majority does not damage status or relationships. Irving Janis included impartial leadership, systematic procedures, and encouragement of critical evaluation among the methods for reducing groupthink. His broader work argued that groups become vulnerable when pressure for agreement suppresses realistic appraisal of alternatives. Later work has continued to position devil's advocacy as one structured safeguard against those pressures. [Springer Nature Link+2pressbooks.montgomerycollege.edu] link.springer.com Springer Nature Linktheir effects and the devil's advocacy as a preventive measureby M Akhmad · 2021 · Cited by 43 — Janis's groupthink f... Authority also means timing. Objections raised after executives have publicly endorsed a proposal rarely produce meaningful revision because changing course now carries political and reputational costs. The advocate is most effective before public commitment creates psychological pressure to defend the emerging consensus. Why structured dissent changes group decisions The value of devil's advocacy is not simply that more criticism appears. It changes how information is processed. Without organised challenge, groups often search for confirming evidence, interpret ambiguous information in favour of the preferred option, and stop exploring alternatives once enough members express confidence. A designated critic interrupts this pattern by making contradictory evidence impossible to ignore. Studies comparing consensus discussions with structured conflict have found several recurring effects: More alternatives receive serious consideration. Assumptions are re examined rather than accepted automatically. Participants become more willing to revise recommendations. Final decisions tend to be judged as higher quality, even though discussions may take longer and feel less comfortable. [Academy of Management Journals+2JSTOR] journals.aom.org Members of such groups reported Academy of Management JournalsExperiential Effects of Dialectical Inquiry, Devil's Advocacy...by DM Schweiger · 1989 · Cited by 928 — Co... An important finding is that these benefits often increase with practice. Groups unfamiliar with structured disagreement may initially experience discomfort or reduced satisfaction because consensus arrives more slowly. As members gain experience, decision quality improves while the extra time required decreases. [Academy of Management Journals] journals.aom.org Members of such groups reported Academy of Management JournalsExperiential Effects of Dialectical Inquiry, Devil's Advocacy...by DM Schweiger · 1989 · Cited by 928 — Co... Structured Conflict Channel: GreggU Open on YouTube Compare advocacy with consensus pressure Consensus itself is not the problem. Agreement reached after alternatives have survived serious testing can be a sign of strong reasoning. The danger is premature consensus —agreement that emerges before assumptions have been challenged. The contrast can be summarised as follows: Consensus pressureEffective devil's advocacySeeks rapid agreementDelays commitment until key assumptions are testedRewards confidenceRewards well supported criticismTreats disagreement as disruptionTreats disagreement as diagnostic informationFocuses on defending one proposalCompares competing explanations or plansMay discourage minority viewsGives minority objections a recognised role Because the advocate has an assigned responsibility, criticism becomes a normal part of the group's procedure rather than an act of personal opposition. This distinction helps reduce the social cost of dissent while increasing the chance that overlooked evidence will receive attention. [Harvard Business School+2nationalforum.com] online.hbs.edu It is that individual or group's Harvard Business School3 Group Decision Making Techniques for Success HBS Online26 Jul 2018 — In this type of decision making, begin by... The Devil’s Advocate Michael Roberto TEDxBryantU Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube When devil's advocacy is not enough Assigning one critic is not a guarantee of better decisions. Several limitations appear repeatedly in the research: A single advocate can become isolated if every other member defends the preferred proposal. If the advocate is assigned weak arguments instead of the strongest available case, the exercise merely confirms existing beliefs. Powerful leaders may unintentionally signal that criticism is unwelcome despite formally assigning the role. Repeatedly assigning the same individual risks labelling that person as permanently negative rather than analytically"},{"title":"Pre Mortems","url":"/pre-mortems/","description":"A pre-mortem asks people to imagine failure has already happened so hidden risks become easier to name.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Objections","level":3,"text":"Pre Mortems Why Did the Plan Fail Already? Pre Mortems Pre Mortems Why Did the Plan Fail Already? A pre mortem asks people to imagine failure has already happened so hidden risks become easier to name. A pre mortem asks people to imagine failure has already happened so hidden risks become easier to name. policy intervention Turn confidence into a failure story Collect risks people avoid saying aloud Convert failure paths into tests improving your thinking and analytical skills objections pre mortems Introduction A pre mortem is a structured decision making exercise that asks a team to imagine that a plan has already failed and then explain , in detail, why it failed. Rather than asking, “What might go wrong?”, participants assume failure is a fact and work backwards to identify its causes. This small shift in perspective often makes it easier to notice overlooked risks, challenge optimistic assumptions , and raise concerns that might otherwise remain unspoken. The technique is especially valuable before launching products, policies, major projects, or strategic decisions, when changing course is still relatively inexpensive. Rather than replacing ordinary risk analysis, a pre mortem strengthens it by exposing hidden failure paths before they become real. [Harvard Business Review] Decide which risks deserve preventive action, additional evidence or revised assumptions before proceeding. Harvard Business Review+2strategyunitwm.nhs.uk Open source on hbr.org. Within the broader practice of seeking strong objections before making decisions, a pre mortem serves a specific purpose: it converts confidence into a realistic failure story that can be tested and improved. Pre Mortem: Avoid Project Failure with This Simple Trick! Channel: Online PM Courses Mike Clayton Open on YouTube Why imagine failure before anything has gone wrong? Many planning discussions suffer from a predictable imbalance. Teams devote most of their energy to explaining why a plan will succeed, while treating doubts as obstacles to overcome rather than evidence to investigate. Optimism, commitment to previous work, and social pressure can all make criticism feel disloyal, particularly when senior people have already expressed enthusiasm. A pre mortem changes the social rules. Instead of asking participants to attack the plan directly, it tells them that the failure has already happened. Their task is no longer to oppose colleagues but to explain an established outcome. Gary Klein, who developed the method, argued that this legitimises concerns that knowledgeable people often hesitate to voice during conventional planning meetings. Daniel Kahneman has similarly described the technique as valuable because it gives doubt an accepted place in the decision process rather than forcing it to compete with group optimism. [Harvard Business Review] Decide which risks deserve preventive action, additional evidence or revised assumptions before proceeding. Harvard Business Review+2strategyunitwm.nhs.uk Open source on hbr.org. The psychological mechanism is often described as prospective hindsight : mentally placing yourself in a future where the outcome is already known, then reasoning backwards to discover its causes. Earlier research on prospective hindsight found that this perspective improves people's ability to generate plausible explanations for future events compared with ordinary forecasting. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. How to Use a Premortem to Stress Test Your Strategy in Stage 4 Design Thinking (APPLY) Channel: Create & Innovate with Andrew & Gaia Grant Open on YouTube Turn confidence into a failure story A useful pre mortem is deliberately concrete. Participants should imagine that the launch, investment, policy or project failed spectacularly six or twelve months later. They then write down specific reasons for that failure before discussion begins. An effective sequence is: Nobel Laureate Daniel Kahneman on de biasing thinking in decision making Channel: Kepner Tregoe Open on YouTube 1. Present the proposed plan and ensure everyone understands it. 2. Announce that the project has already failed. [theuncertaintyproject.org] theuncertaintyproject.org Pre MortemIt is based on 'prospective hindsight' – imagining that the project has already failed and then generating plausible reasons fo... 3. Ask each participant to spend several minutes silently writing every plausible reason for the failure. 4. Collect reasons in a round robin format so every participant contributes. 5. Group similar risks together. 6. Decide which risks deserve preventive action, additional evidence or revised assumptions before proceeding. Harvard Business Review+2strategyunitwm.nhs.uk The initial period of independent writing is important. If discussion starts immediately, early speakers often shape everyone else's thinking. Individual reflection allows quieter participants and technical specialists to identify risks that dominant voices might overlook. The resulting list should describe mechanisms rather than vague anxieties. Compare these examples: Weak: \"Customers might not like it.\" Better: \"Early customers misunderstand the pricing, producing negative reviews that damage trust before customer support has prepared standard responses.\" The second explanation points directly to something that can be investigated or tested. Collect risks people avoid saying aloud One of the greatest strengths of a pre mortem is not simply generating more risks but uncovering the kinds of concerns people often suppress. These commonly include: Assumptions that everyone privately doubts but believes others accept. Political concerns about challenging senior leaders. Operational weaknesses that appear embarrassing. Dependencies on suppliers, regulators or partners that receive too little attention. Overly optimistic schedules created by external pressure rather than realistic estimates. Research and practitioner experience repeatedly suggest that teams frequently possess scattered fragments of warning information before failures occur. The difficulty is often not ignorance but reluctance to assemble those fragments into an explicit challenge to the preferred plan. A pre mortem temporarily changes the incentive structure by making criticism part of the assigned task rather than an act of opposition. Harvard Business Review+2CIPD This depends on psychological safety—the shared expectation that people can raise concerns without punishment or ridicule. Without that environment, participants may still censor themselves even when asked to imagine failure. The exercise therefore works best when leaders visibly welcome uncomfortable observations instead of immediately defending the existing plan. CIPD Convert failure paths into tests The value of a pre mortem lies in what happens afterwards. Producing a long list of possible disasters is useful only if those scenarios become practical changes. For each identified failure path, ask: Which assumption would have to be true for this failure to occur? Can we test that assumption before launch? Can we reduce the likelihood of this failure? Can we reduce its consequences if it happens anyway? What evidence would convince us to delay or redesign the plan? For example: Failure storyUseful follow up testCustomer demand was overestimated.Run a limited pilot with real purchasing behaviour rather than relying only on surveys.Support staff became overwhelmed after launch.Stress test staffing assumptions using projected peak demand.Critical supplier missed delivery dates.Develop alternative suppliers or contingency inventory before launch.New users abandoned the product after registration.Conduct usability testing focused on onboarding before public release. The objective is to convert imagined failures into measurable questions. Instead of arguing abstractly about optimism or pessimism, the discussion becomes evidence driven. Common mistakes that weaken pre mortems Although simple, the technique is often applied poorly. A frequent mistake is treating the exercise as a routine brainstorming session. If participants merely list generic risks such as \"budget problems\" or \"poor communication\", they gain little beyond an ordinary project review. Another mistake is allowing managers to rebut each concern immediately. Once people see objections being defended against rather than investigated, they quickly stop offering their strongest reservations. Teams also weaken the exercise by attempting to eliminate every conceivable risk. A pre mortem is not intended to produce exhaustive catalogues of unlikely scenarios. Its purpose is to identify the few realistic failure mechanisms that deserve immediate attention before commitment. Finally, organisations sometimes conduct a pre mortem because governance procedures require it, then proceed without modifying the plan. The exercise only improves decisions if identified risks alter priorities, testing, sequencing or launch"},{"title":"Red Teams","url":"/red-teams/","description":"Red teams challenge official assumptions so organisations can find blind spots before a crisis exposes them.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Objections","level":3,"text":"Red Teams Can Outside Challenge Prevent Groupthink? Red Teams Red Teams Can Outside Challenge Prevent Groupthink? Red teams challenge official assumptions so organisations can find blind spots before a crisis exposes them. Red teams challenge official assumptions so organisations can find blind spots before a crisis exposes them. policy intervention Why internal confidence can mislead How red teams test assumptions What preparedness failures reveal improving your thinking and analytical skills objections red teams Introduction Strong objections are most valuable before a crisis, when assumptions can still be challenged without immediate operational pressure. One of the most effective ways organisations try to institutionalise that challenge is through red teaming : assigning an independent group to question prevailing assumptions, test plans from an adversary's perspective, and expose vulnerabilities before they become real world failures. Rather than assuming the official plan is correct, a red team asks what would have to be true for it to fail. In preparedness, this approach is especially important because confidence can be misleading. Emergency plans are often built over many years, endorsed by respected experts and repeatedly reused. Those features create stability, but they can also encourage groupthink, where shared assumptions become so widely accepted that alternative possibilities receive little serious attention. Recent inquiries into pandemic preparedness have concluded that a lack of organised challenge contributed to important planning failures, leading to renewed interest in formal red team processes. [UK Covid 19 Inquiry] covid19.public inquiry.uk UK Covid 19 InquiryModule 1 report: The resilience and preparedness of the...The UK failed to plan to prevent the widespread illness and... Why internal confidence can mislead Preparedness planning rewards coordination, consistency and clear chains of command. Those are essential during emergencies, but they can unintentionally discourage disagreement during planning. Groups with long experience often become attached to successful doctrines, previous exercises and established risk models. Over time, assumptions become embedded in planning documents, training programmes and institutional culture. Once this happens, dissent can appear unnecessary or even disruptive, particularly if it comes from junior staff or outsiders. The UK Ministry of Defence's Red Teaming Handbook identifies several recurring psychological and organisational risks, including obedience to authority, optimism bias, commitment to existing plans, and the tendency to search only for information that supports current thinking. Rather than treating these as individual failings, the handbook argues they are predictable features of group decision making that require deliberate countermeasures. [GOV.UK] assets.publishing.service.gov.uk Teaming Handbook, 3rd EditionJune 25, 2021 — A failure to challenge (faulty) guidance can occur due to social pressures... Published: June 25, 2021 Preparedness planning is especially vulnerable because success is difficult to measure. If a major emergency never occurs, organisations rarely discover whether their assumptions were genuinely robust or merely untested. RED TEAM Thinking Revolutionises 21st Century Leadership! redteaming Channel: The Rohit Gautam Show Open on YouTube How red teams test assumptions A red team is not simply a committee of critics. Its purpose is to improve decisions by systematically examining how accepted views might be wrong. Effective red teams typically: [croakey.org] croakey.org red teams may help to improve pandemic responses and other lessons from abroad Red teams may help to improve pandemic responses, and...25 Jul 2024 — The report describes a failure to identify those who were vulnerab... Challenge the assumptions underlying official plans rather than only reviewing the final document. Develop plausible alternative scenarios that existing models may overlook. Test whether evidence genuinely supports the preferred conclusion or merely appears consistent with it. Examine plans from the perspective of adversaries, affected communities or independent experts. Force planners to explain why competing hypotheses have been rejected. Look for hidden dependencies, single points of failure and unrealistic expectations about implementation. The goal is not to produce disagreement for its own sake. Instead, it is to identify assumptions that have become invisible precisely because everyone shares them. The Ministry of Defence handbook describes numerous structured techniques, including alternative hypothesis analysis, assumption checks, alternative futures exercises and planning reviews specifically designed to expose cognitive bias before decisions become fixed. It emphasises that these methods should be integrated throughout the decision process rather than applied only at the final approval stage. [GOV.UK] assets.publishing.service.gov.uk Teaming Handbook, 3rd EditionJune 25, 2021 — A failure to challenge (faulty) guidance can occur due to social pressures... Published: June 25, 2021 Red Team Thinking and Finding Opportunities in Chaos with Bryce Hoffman Channel: Barry O'Reilly Open on YouTube What preparedness failures reveal Large scale preparedness failures often become case studies in the cost of insufficient challenge. The first report of the UK Covid 19 Inquiry concluded that pandemic planning had become overly focused on influenza based assumptions. Institutions largely prepared for managing the consequences of a widespread outbreak rather than examining whether different pathogens might require fundamentally different strategies. The inquiry found that no independent body consistently questioned whether the overall preparedness system remained fit for purpose or whether key assumptions had become outdated. [UK Covid 19 Inquiry] covid19.public inquiry.uk UK Covid 19 InquiryModule 1 report: The resilience and preparedness of the...The UK failed to plan to prevent the widespread illness and... One striking finding was not simply that some predictions proved wrong, but that too few people were asking whether the planning framework itself required revision. The inquiry highlighted fragmented responsibility, insufficient independent assessment and planning documents that had become increasingly difficult to scrutinise effectively. [UK Covid 19 Inquiry] covid19.public inquiry.uk UK Covid 19 InquiryModule 1 report: The resilience and preparedness of the...The UK failed to plan to prevent the widespread illness and... Following these findings, the inquiry recommended the routine use of external red teams to challenge prevailing assumptions and reduce the effects of groupthink in future emergency planning. Contemporary reporting noted that the recommendation aimed specifically at introducing structured outside criticism before consensus hardened into policy. [The Guardian] theguardian.com Key government figures, including former Prime Minister David Cameron and current Chief Medical Officer Prof Chris Whitty, admitted that... Designing red teams that actually improve decisions Simply appointing a \"devil's advocate\" rarely achieves the benefits associated with successful red teaming. Poorly designed challenge exercises can become performative, where objections are expected but not genuinely considered. Several characteristics distinguish effective red teams: Independence. Members should have enough organisational distance to question assumptions without fearing career consequences. Access to evidence. Challengers need the same information available to planners; otherwise criticism becomes speculative rather than analytical. Authority to influence decisions. Findings must reach senior decision makers before plans become politically or institutionally committed. Method rather than personality. Effective red teaming relies on structured analytical techniques rather than relying on naturally sceptical individuals. Psychological safety. Leaders must visibly reward rigorous criticism. If disagreement carries reputational costs, formal red teams quickly lose effectiveness. Government guidance stresses that red teams are one component of broader quality assurance rather than a substitute for expert review, operational testing or scientific advice. Their distinctive contribution is organised independent challenge aimed at exposing blind spots before implementation. [Civil Servant] civilservant.org.uk Civil Servant Policy Process Civil ServantPolicy Process Red TeamsA 'Red Team' to provide an independent challenge to assumptions, and identify weaknesses in, an or... Limits and trade offs Red teams are not guaranteed to prevent failure. An ineffective red team may suffer the same biases as the organisation it examines. Alternatively, leaders may commission a review but ignore uncomfortable findings, turning the exercise into little more than symbolic"},{"title":"Steelmanning","url":"/steelmanning/","description":"A real steelman makes the opposing case strong enough to expose which assumptions your preferred answer depends on.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Objections","level":3,"text":"Steelmanning Can the Best Opposing Case Beat Yours? Steelmanning Steelmanning Can the Best Opposing Case Beat Yours? A real steelman makes the opposing case strong enough to expose which assumptions your preferred answer depends on. A real steelman makes the opposing case strong enough to expose which assumptions your preferred answer depends on. policy intervention State the rival view fairly Find the evidence that makes it plausible Name the condition that would change the decision improving your thinking and analytical skills objections steelmanning Introduction Steelmanning a rival view means deliberately constructing the strongest credible version of the best argument against your preferred conclusion before you commit to a decision. Unlike a straw man, which weakens an opposing position to make it easier to defeat, a steelman aims to make the alternative as persuasive and accurate as its supporters would recognise it. The purpose is not to be fair for its own sake. It is to expose which assumptions your own judgement depends on, reduce confirmation bias, and identify the evidence that would genuinely justify changing course. Research on confirmation bias consistently shows that people naturally seek and interpret information that supports what they already believe, making this step a practical safeguard rather than an intellectual luxury. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias What makes a real steelman different? A genuine steelman is demanding because it requires temporarily suspending the urge to defend your preferred answer. Instead of asking, \"How can I prove I'm right?\", you ask, \"What is the strongest reason an informed, reasonable person would disagree?\" This differs from ordinary disagreement in several important ways: It represents the rival view faithfully. Someone who holds that position should be able to say, \"Yes, that's my argument.\" It strengthens rather than weakens the opposing case. You remove obvious flaws, clarify ambiguities, and include the strongest available supporting evidence. It focuses on the central disagreement. Rather than collecting many minor objections, it identifies the few assumptions on which the decision actually depends. The philosophical principle of charity has long argued that arguments should be interpreted in their strongest rational form before criticism. Steelmanning extends that idea into practical decision making by treating accurate reconstruction as part of good reasoning rather than good manners. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Principle of charity Principle of charity State the rival view fairly The first task is to describe the competing position without adding exaggerations or removing important qualifications. A useful test is simple: if someone who disagrees with you would immediately object to your description of their position, you have not yet produced a steelman. Instead of writing: \"Opponents are simply afraid of change.\" write something closer to: \"The strongest argument for delaying implementation is that current evidence is incomplete, early mistakes could create lasting costs, and gathering another three months of data may substantially reduce uncertainty.\" Notice that this version does not concede the conclusion. It merely presents the reasoning in its strongest credible form. In organisations, this often means separating disagreement over goals from disagreement over methods . Two people may agree completely on the desired outcome while disagreeing about whether the proposed route is sufficiently robust. this is how to improve your critical thinking according to neuroscience THE ONLY VIDEO THAT WORKS Channel: erin meryl study Open on YouTube Find the evidence that makes the rival view plausible A steelman without evidence is merely a more polite opinion. After stating the rival case, actively search for the strongest evidence supporting it. This often means looking beyond sources that normally reinforce your own beliefs. Useful questions include: Which published evidence would supporters cite first? Which historical examples favour their prediction? Which experts disagree with my current position? Which assumptions in my own reasoning are least well supported? This stage deliberately interrupts confirmation bias. Decades of psychological research show that people naturally search for confirming evidence and often evaluate supporting evidence more generously than contradictory evidence. A steelman reverses that instinct by treating opposing evidence as diagnostically valuable rather than inconvenient. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias The goal is not equal weighting of every opinion. Weak objections remain weak. The point is that your preferred conclusion should survive the strongest available competing explanation, not merely the weakest criticism. Name the condition that would change the decision The most valuable part of a steelman is identifying the decision changing condition . Many debates continue because participants never specify what evidence would actually alter their judgement. Ask questions such as: What observation would make the rival explanation more convincing than mine? What future result would justify changing plans? Which assumption, if disproved, would require a different decision? For example: Current preferenceStrong rival viewDecision changing conditionLaunch the product immediatelyDelay because support systems are untested and reputational damage from early failures could outweigh first mover advantageIf pilot testing reveals significantly higher failure rates than expected, postpone launch and improve operational readiness.Expand into a new marketWait because customer demand estimates rely on limited dataDelay expansion if independent market testing fails to confirm projected demand.Invest in Project AChoose Project B because its expected return is lower but uncertainty is substantially smallerSwitch if revised risk estimates show Project A's downside exceeds acceptable limits. The condition should be concrete enough that someone else could later evaluate whether it occurred. How To Steel Man An Opposing Argument (feat. Sam Harris and Jordan Peterson) Channel: eBiz Facts – Business Ideas Open on YouTube Why steelmanning improves analytical judgement Steelmanning works because it changes the sequence of thinking. Without it, reasoning often follows this pattern: 1. Form an initial conclusion. 2. Collect supporting evidence. 3. Defend the conclusion against criticism. With steelmanning, the sequence becomes: 1. Form a provisional conclusion. 2. Build the strongest competing explanation. 3. Compare both explanations against the available evidence. 4. Commit only after testing the assumptions that separate them. This process resembles structured analytical techniques developed for intelligence and decision analysis, such as comparing competing hypotheses rather than simply accumulating evidence for the first plausible explanation. The emphasis shifts from proving one answer correct to discovering which explanation best fits the evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Common mistakes that look like steelmanning Several habits imitate steelmanning without providing its analytical benefits. Choosing an obviously weak opponent. If the rival position is easy to dismiss, it cannot reveal weaknesses in your own reasoning. Arguing against motives instead of arguments. Explaining why someone supposedly believes something is rarely a substitute for addressing their evidence. Adding claims the rival side never made. A steelman strengthens existing arguments rather than inventing new ones. Treating balance as accuracy. Every position does not deserve equal confidence. Steelmanning requires presenting the best opposing case fairly, not pretending that all conclusions are equally well supported. Stopping after describing the rival view. The exercise is incomplete unless you identify what evidence would actually favour one explanation over the other. How to Win ANY Workplace Disagreement (Without Being the Boss) Channel: Firm Learning Open on YouTube A practical routine before committing to a decision For important decisions, a concise routine is often enough: 1. Write your current conclusion in one sentence. 2. Rewrite the strongest opposing conclusion as its supporters would recognise it. 3. List the best evidence supporting that rival view. 4. Identify the single assumption that most clearly separates the two positions. 5. Specify the observation or evidence that would cause you to change your decision. 6. Only then decide whether your original conclusion still appears stronger. This routine is intentionally brief. Its value comes from forcing your preferred answer to compete with its strongest credible alternative while changing direction is still relatively inexpensive. By making the rival case difficult to dismiss, you gain a clearer picture of what your own judgement truly depends"},{"title":"Teaching","url":"/teaching/","description":"Thinking improves most when skills are named, practiced, and applied directly rather than assumed to develop by exposure.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Teaching Can Thinking Be Taught Directly? Teaching Teaching Can Thinking Be Taught Directly? Thinking improves most when skills are named, practiced, and applied directly rather than assumed to develop by exposure. Thinking improves most when skills are named, practiced, and applied directly rather than assumed to develop by exposure. policy intervention Explicit instruction Practice in real subjects Feedback and transfer improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching Introduction Thinking can be taught directly, but not by asking learners to “be more critical” and hoping the skill appears. The strongest approach is to name the thinking move, model it, let learners practise it in real subject matter, and give feedback on the quality of their reasoning. This matters because analytical skill is often treated as a by product of education, work experience or debate. The evidence points the other way: people improve most when reasoning is made visible and practised deliberately, especially through explicit critical thinking goals, dialogue, authentic tasks, mentoring, metacognitive routines and feedback. A major meta analysis of critical thinking instruction found a positive average effect across experimental and quasi experimental studies, while guidance on metacognition similarly stresses explicit teaching, modelling and scaffolding of planning, monitoring and evaluation. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think CriticallyCT is an explicit objective in the infusion course but not in the immers... Within the broader goal of improving thinking and analytical skills, explicit instruction is the policy intervention that turns a vague aspiration into teachable practice . It asks schools, universities, professional training programmes and workplace learning systems to decide which reasoning skills they expect people to use, where those skills will be practised, how performance will be judged, and how learners will be helped to transfer the skill beyond the first example. What explicit thinking instruction changes The central shift is from exposure to instruction. In an exposure model, learners read demanding texts, solve difficult problems, join discussions or complete projects, and the institution assumes that better thinking will emerge. Sometimes it does. Often, however, learners simply become more familiar with the content while keeping the same hidden reasoning habits: jumping from evidence to conclusion, overlooking alternatives, confusing confidence with accuracy, or failing to ask what would change their mind. Explicit instruction makes the target skill public. Instead of saying “analyse this article”, a teacher might ask learners to identify the main claim, separate evidence from interpretation, list unstated assumptions , compare two explanations, judge source reliability and explain which piece of evidence carries the most weight. In science, the equivalent might be making a data based decision under uncertainty; in history, weighing competing accounts; in law, distinguishing precedent from policy argument; in management, testing whether a diagnosis of a problem fits the available evidence. This is not the same as reducing thinking to a rigid formula. The point is to give learners enough structure to see what good reasoning looks like before expecting independence. The Education Endowment Foundation’s guidance on metacognition describes this as helping pupils plan, monitor and evaluate their learning by explicitly teaching, modelling and scaffolding strategies, rather than assuming pupils will discover these routines on their own. [EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and Self Regulated Learning13 Nov 2025 — This guidance report reviews the... learning through explicitly teaching, mode... A simple lesson sequence captures the governance logic: 1. Name the move: “Today we are distinguishing evidence from explanation.” 2. Model the move: the teacher thinks aloud through an example. 3. Practise with support: learners try the same move on a related problem. 4. Give feedback: the teacher comments on the reasoning, not just the answer. 5. Fade support: prompts are gradually removed. 6. Transfer: learners apply the move in a new subject, case or decision. The governance challenge is that each step has to be planned. If a curriculum says critical thinking matters but assessments reward only recall , teachers and learners receive a mixed signal. If professional training praises analytical judgement but never defines what counts as sound evidence, novices are left guessing. Explicit instruction is not just “telling” One common misunderstanding is that explicit instruction means lecturing about logic, bias or fallacies. Direct explanation can help, but it is not enough. Thinking improves when learners use the named skill repeatedly and receive information about how well they used it. The critical thinking meta analysis by Abrami and colleagues is useful here because it distinguishes different course approaches. In an immersion approach, students engage deeply with subject matter but critical thinking is not made an explicit objective. In an infusion approach, critical thinking is taught within the subject and made explicit. In a mixed approach, learners receive explicit critical thinking instruction as a separate strand while also applying it inside subject learning. The review found that instructional design matters: making critical thinking an explicit objective, combining it with dialogue, and anchoring it in authentic tasks were all part of the evidence base reviewed. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think CriticallyCT is an explicit objective in the infusion course but not in the immers... That distinction matters for policy. A school or university can add more projects, debates and inquiry tasks without improving reasoning if students are not taught how to reason within those tasks. Conversely, a standalone “thinking skills” course can become sterile if learners never use the skills on real content. The practical answer is not “skills instead of knowledge” or “knowledge instead of skills”. It is explicit thinking instruction inside knowledge rich work. Rosenshine’s principles of instruction help explain why. His evidence informed account of effective teaching emphasises presenting new material in small steps, modelling, guided practice, checking understanding and obtaining a high success rate before moving to independence. Those routines are not limited to basic skills; they are especially relevant when the target is invisible cognitive work, because learners cannot copy what they cannot see. [American Federation of Teachers] aft.org American Federation of TeachersPrinciples of Instruction: Research Based Strategies That...March 7, 2012 — by B Rosenshine · 2012 · Cite... Published: March 7, 2012 Practice has to happen in real subjects Thinking skills are partly general, but they do not float free of content. A learner can understand the idea of “consider alternative explanations” in the abstract and still fail to use it well when interpreting a medical claim, a spreadsheet, a political speech or an experimental result. Transfer is hard because knowledge is often tied to the context in which it was learned. The National Academies’ work on learning and transfer notes that transfer is more likely when learners understand underlying principles and apply them across more than one context. [National Academies] nationalacademies.org National AcademiesChapter: 3 Learning and TransferOne frequently used teaching technique is to get learners to elaborate on the examples... This is why explicit instruction should not be confined to generic study skills lessons. It works best when thinking moves are attached to the intellectual demands of a real subject: In science: “What does this dataset allow us to conclude, and what uncertainty remains?” In history: “Which source is closer to the event, and what motive might shape its account?” In literature: “Which interpretation explains more of the text with fewer strained assumptions?” In mathematics: “Which representation makes the structure of the problem clearer?” In civic education: “Which claim is factual, which is evaluative, and which evidence would be needed?” A concrete example comes from physics education research. Holmes, Wieman and Bonn redesigned introductory laboratory work so students repeatedly made quantitative comparisons between datasets and between data and models, with feedback and fading prompts. After the explicit prompts were removed, students in the experimental condition were far more likely than control students to improve their experimental methods and to identify limitations of a model using data; the gains also persisted into a later course. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Teaching Critical Thinking arXiv Teaching Critical Thinking The lesson is not"},{"title":"Argument Maps","url":"/argument-maps-eb4bd0/","description":"Argument maps turn claims, objections, and rebuttals into inspectable structures that can be practised and improved.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teaching","level":3,"text":"Argument Maps Can Mapping Arguments Make Thinking Clearer? Argument Maps Argument Maps Can Mapping Arguments Make Thinking Clearer? Argument maps turn claims, objections, and rebuttals into inspectable structures that can be practised and improved. Argument maps turn claims, objections, and rebuttals into inspectable structures that can be practised and improved. policy intervention How argument maps expose reasoning structure Where mapping helps learners revise arguments Limits of one off diagramming exercises improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching argument maps Introduction Argument mapping is the practice of turning reasoning into a visual structure so that claims, supporting reasons, objections and rebuttals can be inspected rather than inferred from continuous prose. Instead of asking learners merely to write or debate, it asks them to make the logic of their thinking explicit. Within explicit thinking instruction, this matters because reasoning often improves only after learners can see where it is incomplete, unsupported or internally inconsistent. Research over the past two decades suggests that well designed argument mapping activities can improve critical thinking, particularly when they involve repeated practice, structured feedback and progressively more complex tasks rather than isolated diagramming exercises. [ResearchGate+2University of Southampton Web Archive] researchgate.net 304805904 Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills19 Feb 2026 — Like any worthwhile skill, argument mapping requires... Argument maps do not replace writing, discussion or subject knowledge. Rather, they function as a visible thinking tool that helps learners examine how evidence connects to conclusions before those ideas are expressed in essays, reports or debates. How argument maps expose reasoning structure Most arguments in ordinary writing are only partly visible. Authors often leave assumptions unstated, combine several reasons into one sentence, or move quickly from evidence to conclusion without making the intermediate steps explicit. Readers therefore have to reconstruct the reasoning mentally. Argument mapping externalises that process. A typical map separates: the main conclusion; individual supporting reasons; evidence for each reason; [linkedin.com] linkedin.com ons. Example: Reason 1: Critical thinking improves students'... objections or counterarguments; rebuttals that answer those objections; assumptions that connect premises to conclusions. By representing each proposition as a separate node connected by support or objection links, learners can inspect the logical architecture rather than concentrating only on the persuasiveness of the language. This distinction is important because strong rhetoric can disguise weak reasoning, while clearly structured arguments make logical weaknesses easier to identify. [ResearchGate+2Harvard Graduate School of Education] researchgate.net 304805904 Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills19 Feb 2026 — Like any worthwhile skill, argument mapping requires... Argument maps also encourage learners to distinguish between different kinds of relationships. Two pieces of evidence may independently support a conclusion, while two premises may only work when combined. A visual representation makes these differences immediately apparent in ways that paragraphs often do not. Another benefit is that maps naturally reveal missing elements. A conclusion with no supporting evidence, a chain resting on an unsupported assumption, or an unanswered objection becomes visually obvious. Rather than relying solely on teacher judgement, learners can often identify these weaknesses themselves during revision. Critical Thinking with Argument Maps Channel: Argumentful · Views: 8.5K · Uploaded: July 2020 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Where mapping helps learners revise arguments The greatest educational value of argument mapping lies not in producing attractive diagrams but in supporting revision. When learners first construct a map, they frequently discover that what appeared convincing in prose contains hidden gaps. A supporting reason may be irrelevant, evidence may fail to justify the stated claim, or multiple conclusions may have become unintentionally mixed together. Revising the map before revising the written argument allows structural problems to be corrected early. Research on argument mapping based instruction consistently reports that improvement comes through deliberate practice . Students repeatedly analyse existing arguments, reconstruct them as maps, compare alternative structures, receive feedback, and gradually learn to produce stronger reasoning independently. This approach aligns with broader evidence that explicit practice with feedback is more effective than expecting critical thinking to emerge indirectly from content learning alone. [ResearchGate+2ajet.org.au] researchgate.net 304805904 Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills19 Feb 2026 — Like any worthwhile skill, argument mapping requires... Several practical revision benefits emerge repeatedly. Separating evidence from interpretation. Learners become more likely to ask whether a statement is an observable fact, an inference, or a conclusion requiring further support. Testing completeness. Every important claim should connect to supporting reasons. Empty branches quickly reveal unsupported assertions. Strengthening counterargument. Because objections occupy visible positions within the map, students are encouraged to address opposing evidence rather than ignoring it. Reducing ambiguity. Long sentences often contain several distinct propositions. Breaking them into separate nodes forces greater precision in wording and reasoning. These features explain why argument mapping is increasingly used not only in philosophy but also in law, medicine, business, history and scientific reasoning, where evaluating competing explanations is central to good judgement. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net 304805904 Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills19 Feb 2026 — Like any worthwhile skill, argument mapping requires... What the evidence suggests The research literature generally reports positive educational effects, although the size of those effects depends heavily on implementation. Early work led by Tim van Gelder and colleagues found that courses centred on intensive computer supported argument mapping produced substantial improvements on independent critical thinking assessments. The proposed explanation was not simply that diagrams are visually appealing, but that they reduce unnecessary cognitive demands while making inferential relationships explicit enough for repeated deliberate practice. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net 304805904 Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills Using Argument Mapping to Improve Critical Thinking Skills19 Feb 2026 — Like any worthwhile skill, argument mapping requires... Subsequent experimental work has reported additional benefits. Studies comparing learning through argument maps with conventional text have found improvements in comprehension and recall of argumentative structure, suggesting that visual organisation can help learners process complex reasoning more effectively. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net The evaluation of argument mapping as a learning toolCompared with mind maps, Argument mapping can more clearly indicate the... Research has also explored collaborative settings. In one study of online university debates, students jointly constructed argument maps over several weeks while receiving instructor feedback. Their critical thinking became progressively deeper across the sequence of activities, although higher order creative reasoning remained comparatively difficult. The findings suggest that mapping is most effective when integrated with discussion, peer interaction and feedback rather than treated as an isolated exercise. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Exploring the Effects of Argument Map Supported Online...by X Chen · 2022 · Cited by 32 — The current study aimed to explore the effe... More recent reviews conclude that argument visualisation shows considerable promise across higher education, but they also note substantial variation in research methods, instructional designs and assessment measures. This means that broad claims should be interpreted cautiously: evidence supports the educational value of mapping, but the strongest gains occur under specific teaching conditions rather than automatically whenever diagrams are introduced. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryArgument Mapping in Higher Education: A Systematic...15 Oct 2025 —"},{"title":"Course Design","url":"/course-design/","description":"Deep subject work does not reliably build critical thinking unless the reasoning goal is named and practised inside the task.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teaching","level":3,"text":"Course Design Do Projects Teach Thinking by Themselves? Course Design Course Design Do Projects Teach Thinking by Themselves? Deep subject work does not reliably build critical thinking unless the reasoning goal is named and practised inside the task. Deep subject work does not reliably build critical thinking unless the reasoning goal is named and practised inside the task. policy intervention What immersion leaves implicit How infusion changes subject learning When mixed courses add value improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching course design Introduction Do demanding projects automatically produce better thinkers? Research suggests the answer is usually no. Students often become more knowledgeable about a subject without necessarily improving how they evaluate evidence, question assumptions or justify conclusions. The key distinction is between immersion , where learners engage in intellectually demanding subject work but critical thinking remains implicit, and infusion , where the same subject matter is taught alongside explicit reasoning goals, modelling and feedback . A third option, the mixed approach , combines explicit instruction in general thinking principles with subject specific practice. This distinction has become one of the central debates in critical thinking education because it determines whether thinking is treated as a hoped for by product of learning or as a deliberate instructional objective. Research spanning several decades, from John Ennis's framework through modern meta analyses, consistently indicates that making thinking explicit produces more reliable improvements than relying on immersion alone. [Sage Journals+2Taylor & Francis Online] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think CriticallyIn previous analyses of current approaches (e.g., Abrami et al., 2008)... What immersion leaves implicit The immersion approach assumes that students will acquire critical thinking simply by engaging in demanding disciplinary work. A history student evaluates primary documents, a chemistry student designs experiments, or an engineering student solves complex design problems. The expectation is that repeated exposure to authentic intellectual work will naturally cultivate sound reasoning. This approach has obvious strengths. Students work with genuine disciplinary problems rather than artificial exercises. They learn that reasoning is inseparable from subject knowledge, and they avoid treating critical thinking as an isolated school subject detached from real decisions. The weakness is that many of the most important reasoning processes remain invisible. An instructor may ask students to \"analyse\", \"evaluate\" or \"justify\" without ever explaining what distinguishes strong analysis from weak analysis. Students who already possess effective reasoning habits may improve further, while others simply imitate disciplinary conventions without understanding the reasoning behind them. Research on higher education repeatedly identifies this hidden curriculum. Teachers often expect students to develop analytical abilities through participation in seminars, laboratory work or projects, yet rarely define the thinking processes being assessed. As a result, learners frequently mistake task completion for improved reasoning. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineEnabling critical thinking development in higher education...by B Golden · 2023 · Cited by 170 — Ennis (Citation1... Immersion also makes feedback less precise. A tutor may comment that an essay lacks depth or that an argument is weak without identifying the underlying reasoning error—for example: confusing evidence with interpretation; ignoring alternative explanations; relying on unsupported assumptions; drawing conclusions that exceed the available evidence. Without explicit language for these reasoning moves, students may improve the assignment while leaving their thinking habits largely unchanged. researchEDHome 2020 Greg Ashman: Explicit teaching, critical thinking and creativity Channel: ResearchED Open on YouTube How infusion changes subject learning Infusion keeps the authentic subject matter but makes critical thinking an explicit instructional goal. Rather than assuming students will discover good reasoning independently, instructors identify the thinking skills required for success and teach them within normal disciplinary activities. The subject remains central, but reasoning is deliberately named, modelled, practised and assessed. For example, in a history course an infusion lesson might require students to: distinguish factual claims from historical interpretation; compare competing explanations for the same event; identify assumptions within primary sources; explain why one body of evidence deserves greater weight than another. In biology, students may be asked not simply to interpret experimental results but also to evaluate competing hypotheses, identify limitations in the experimental design and specify what additional evidence would change their conclusion. The difference is subtle but important. The project has not changed; the instructional attention has shifted from merely completing the task to understanding how high quality reasoning operates inside it. Ennis's influential classification distinguished infusion from immersion precisely on this basis: both teach through ordinary subjects, but only infusion explicitly teaches and evaluates critical thinking within those subjects. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think CriticallyIn previous analyses of current approaches (e.g., Abrami et al., 2008)... Why explicit reasoning improves learning Infusion affects learning through several complementary mechanisms. First, it creates a shared vocabulary. Students learn what teachers mean by evidence, inference, assumptions, validity and alternative explanations instead of inferring these concepts indirectly. Second, instructors can model expert thinking aloud. Rather than presenting only finished answers, teachers reveal how experts decide which evidence matters, reject weak explanations and revise conclusions. Third, assessment becomes aligned with reasoning. Instead of grading only factual accuracy or final products, instructors can reward the quality of justification, evaluation and decision making. Finally, explicit reasoning supports transfer. Students are more likely to recognise the same analytical pattern when it appears in another discipline because they have learned the underlying reasoning strategy rather than merely copying disciplinary routines. [Taylor & Francis Online+2PMC] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineEnabling critical thinking development in higher education...by B Golden · 2023 · Cited by 170 — Ennis (Citation1... What the evidence says about explicit versus implicit approaches The research literature consistently finds that explicit approaches outperform purely implicit ones, although the size of the advantage depends on implementation. John Ennis proposed four broad instructional models: General approach – critical thinking taught as a separate subject. [academypublication.com] academypublication.com An Exploration into Research on Critical Thinking and Its...Paul stressed that these fundamental standards require being infused in all... Infusion – explicit thinking instruction embedded within disciplinary teaching . Immersion – subject teaching assumes thinking develops implicitly. Mixed approach – combines general instruction with infused disciplinary practice . [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov How to teach critical thinking: an experimental study with three...by A Orhan · 2022 · Cited by 53 — In the literature, approaches to... Subsequent reviews have largely retained this framework. The large meta analysis by Abrami and colleagues examined hundreds of experimental and quasi experimental comparisons and found that critical thinking can be taught successfully, but instructional design matters. Interventions that made critical thinking objectives explicit generally produced stronger gains than approaches that expected improvement to emerge automatically from difficult coursework. The review also identified dialogue, authentic problems and mentoring as additional features associated with better outcomes . [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsStrategies for Teaching Students to Think CriticallyIn previous analyses of current approaches (e.g., Abrami et al., 2008)... More recent reviews continue to reach similar conclusions. While immersion has educational value, particularly for developing disciplinary expertise, explicit and structured instruction tends to produce more consistent improvements in critical thinking performance than implicit exposure alone. [Nature] nature.com Effectiveness of training actions aimed at improving critical...by JM Marcos Vílchez · 2025 · Cited by 3 — The classic framework"},{"title":"Model Moves","url":"/model-moves/","description":"Learners reason better when teachers make invisible expert thinking visible before asking for independent analysis.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teaching","level":3,"text":"Model Moves Why Students Need to See Thinking First Model Moves Model Moves Why Students Need to See Thinking First Learners reason better when teachers make invisible expert thinking visible before asking for independent analysis. Learners reason better when teachers make invisible expert thinking visible before asking for independent analysis. mechanism What a reasoning move looks like Teacher think alouds and worked examples Fading prompts without removing support improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching model moves Introduction Good analysis is difficult to imitate when the mental steps behind it remain invisible. Novices often see an expert produce a strong answer, but they do not see the sequence of decisions, checks and revisions that led there. Modelling reasoning moves before asking learners to analyse independently addresses this problem by making expert thinking visible. Rather than presenting only a finished solution, teachers reveal how they identify evidence, test assumptions , consider alternatives and monitor their own thinking. Research across cognitive science, metacognition and instructional design shows that this combination of explicit modelling, worked examples and gradually reduced support is especially effective for beginners because it reduces unnecessary cognitive load while building reusable reasoning strategies. [Taylor & Francis Online+2EEF] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe effect of worked examples on learning solution steps...by O Chen · 2023 · Cited by 59 — The worked example ef... Within explicit teaching of thinking skills, modelling is the bridge between explanation and independent performance. It gives learners a way to imitate the reasoning process rather than merely copying the final answer. What a reasoning move looks like A reasoning move is a deliberate mental action that advances an analysis. Unlike a subject fact, it is transferable across many contexts. For example, a historian, scientist and business analyst may all use the same reasoning moves even though they work with different evidence. Common reasoning moves include: Identifying the central claim before judging it. Separating observation from interpretation. Asking what evidence supports each conclusion. Looking for alternative explanations. Testing whether a conclusion still holds if one assumption changes. Deciding which evidence deserves the greatest weight. Recognising uncertainty instead of forcing premature certainty. When experts perform these actions silently, learners often assume good analysts simply \"know the answer\". Live modelling corrects that misconception by showing that expert judgement is built from many small, observable decisions rather than intuition alone. Think aloud techniques are specifically designed to externalise these otherwise hidden cognitive processes. Support for Early Career Teachers+2newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz [support for early career teachers.education.gov.uk] support for early career teachers.education.gov.uk Support for Early Career Teachers Theory It is when a teacher narrates their thoughts... A useful distinction is between explaining and modelling. Explaining tells students what a strategy is. Modelling demonstrates the strategy while it is actually being used. The latter exposes uncertainty, revision and decision making that are normally invisible. Why teacher think alouds work A think aloud is a live demonstration of reasoning in which the teacher verbalises the decisions normally kept internal. Instead of saying only, \"This source is unreliable,\" the teacher narrates the evaluation process: \"This statistic initially looks convincing, but I notice it comes from an interested organisation. Before accepting it, I want to know who collected the data and whether another source reaches the same conclusion.\" That narration shows not only the conclusion but also the questions that generated it. Research on metacognitive instruction consistently recommends making planning, monitoring and evaluation explicit rather than assuming learners will discover these habits independently. The Education Endowment Foundation similarly argues that think alouds help pupils acquire a toolkit of strategies by observing expert decision making in action. [EEF+2EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk eef blog the what why when and how of using a think aloud in maths EEFThe what, why, when and how of using a 'Think Aloud'…22 Feb 2022 — Providing a Think Aloud model helps pupils to develop a toolkit of... Effective think alouds usually include several features: Purposeful pauses. The teacher stops to explain why a choice is being made. Visible uncertainty. Experts demonstrate that changing one's mind in response to evidence is normal. Attention to mistakes. Incorrect paths are acknowledged and corrected rather than hidden. Reasoning vocabulary. Terms such as \"assumption\", \"evidence\", \"counter example\" and \"confidence\" become attached to concrete examples rather than abstract definitions. One common mistake is over performing expertise by presenting flawless reasoning. Learners often benefit more when teachers model how experts recover from uncertainty or recognise an initial misconception than when every step appears effortless. This aligns with work distinguishing perfect \"expert models\" from \"coping models\", where learners observe realistic problem solving rather than idealised performance. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov They also model how ERICExpert and Coping Models in Writing Instruction and LiteracyJune 29, 2021 — by ZA Traga Philippakos · 2021 · Cited by 22 — Teachers t... Published: June 29, 2021 Using a 'Think Aloud' to support pupils' problem solving Channel: Education Endowment Foundation Open on YouTube Why worked examples improve early analytical learning Worked examples extend modelling by providing complete demonstrations that learners can study before attempting similar tasks themselves. Instead of solving every problem from scratch, novices first examine how an expert approached one. Cognitive Load Theory explains why this matters. Beginners possess limited working memory and relatively undeveloped mental frameworks for organising information. If they must simultaneously understand new concepts and invent solution strategies, much of their mental effort is consumed by searching for what to do next rather than understanding why particular decisions make sense. Worked examples reduce this unnecessary search, allowing learners to focus on the underlying reasoning. [Taylor & Francis Online+2UK] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe effect of worked examples on learning solution steps...by O Chen · 2023 · Cited by 59 — The worked example ef... A worked analytical example is most valuable when it explains decisions, not merely outcomes. For instance, after each reasoning step, annotations might answer questions such as: Why was this evidence selected? Why was this alternative rejected? What assumption is being tested? What would weaken this conclusion? Research also shows that prompting learners to generate brief self explanations while studying worked examples improves understanding because they actively connect each step to the underlying principle rather than passively reading the solution. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Worked example effect Worked example effect Fading prompts without removing support The objective is not permanent dependence on teacher modelling but gradual independence. Educational research refers to this as fading guidance. A typical progression might look like this: 1. The teacher models every reasoning decision. 2. Students analyse a similar case using structured prompts. 3. Some prompts disappear while others remain. 4. Students justify their own reasoning with occasional feedback . 5. Students complete comparable analyses independently. The critical idea is that support decreases only after learners have developed enough understanding to benefit from greater independence. Removing guidance too early often overloads novices, while maintaining detailed guidance for advanced learners can become inefficient. This balance is reflected in the expertise reversal effect. Instructional methods that strongly benefit beginners—including detailed worked examples—may become unnecessary or even counterproductive once learners have built sufficiently rich mental models. At that stage, increasingly open ended analytical tasks encourage further growth. [UK+2mr barton maths] uky.edu Kalyuga2007 Article ExpertiseReversalEffectAndItsI UKExpertise Reversal Effect and Its Implications for Learner ...by S Kalyuga · 2007 · Cited by 1568 — This paper reviews recent empirica... Adaptive fading, where support is reduced according to learner progress rather than a fixed schedule, has shown advantages over both immediate independence and rigidly timed withdrawal of guidance. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net The expertise reversal effect and worked examples in...These"},{"title":"Physics Labs","url":"/physics-labs/","description":"Explicit lab prompts can teach students to compare data, test models, and improve methods under uncertainty.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teaching","level":3,"text":"Physics Labs What Physics Labs Reveal About Teaching Judgement Physics Labs Physics Labs What Physics Labs Reveal About Teaching Judgement Explicit lab prompts can teach students to compare data, test models, and improve methods under uncertainty. Explicit lab prompts can teach students to compare data, test models, and improve methods under uncertainty. dataset evidence Why routine labs may miss analytical skill Data comparison as a repeated decision What persisted after prompts were removed improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching physics labs Introduction Physics laboratories are often assumed to teach analytical thinking simply because students collect data. Research suggests that this assumption is mistaken. Traditional \"cookbook\" laboratories, where students follow predetermined procedures to reproduce known results, can leave students with little opportunity to decide what evidence is trustworthy or how to improve an investigation. By contrast, redesigned physics laboratories that explicitly require students to compare datasets, test models against evidence, and revise their methods provide repeated practice in scientific judgement rather than procedural compliance. This approach offers one of the clearest demonstrations that analytical thinking can be taught directly within normal subject teaching , rather than as a separate critical thinking course. [ResearchGate+2PNAS] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Measuring the impact of an instructional laboratory We have analyzed the impact of taking an associated lab course on the scores on final exam questions in two large introductory physics co... Why routine labs may miss analytical skill Many introductory physics laboratories have traditionally focused on verifying textbook laws. Students are given detailed instructions, expected outcomes and fixed procedures. Success is often measured by completing the experiment correctly rather than by making thoughtful decisions about uncertain evidence. Research by Natasha Holmes, Carl Wieman and colleagues challenged this model after earlier work found that conventional physics laboratories produced little measurable improvement in students' conceptual understanding of lecture material despite the considerable time invested in them. This prompted a shift in emphasis: instead of asking whether laboratories reinforce physics content, the researchers asked whether they could explicitly teach experimental judgement. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Measuring the impact of an instructional laboratory We have analyzed the impact of taking an associated lab course on the scores on final exam questions in two large introductory physics co... The redesigned laboratories retained familiar experiments, such as pendulum measurements, but changed what students were expected to think about. Rather than confirming a known answer, students had to decide: whether two measurements genuinely differed or only appeared different because of uncertainty; whether a theoretical model adequately matched the observations; what additional measurements would most reduce uncertainty; how the experiment itself could be improved. These decisions mirror the reasoning used by practising scientists, where evidence is rarely perfect and conclusions must be justified rather than simply reported. [PNAS] pnas.org Teaching critical thinkingby NG Holmes · 2015 · Cited by 479 — We present a simple learning framework that employs cycles of decision... Carl Wieman: Taking a Scientific Approach to Science Education Channel: Carnegie Mellon University Open on YouTube Data comparison as a repeated decision The distinctive feature of the transformed laboratories was not more sophisticated equipment but explicit prompts directing students to make evidence based decisions repeatedly. Instead of calculating uncertainty merely as a mathematical exercise, students were asked to use quantitative comparisons between datasets and between data and theoretical models. Whenever discrepancies appeared, they had to decide whether the difference was meaningful, identify possible explanations, and modify either their measurements or their experimental methods before collecting further data. This cycle of comparison, judgement and revision was repeated throughout the course. [PNAS] pnas.org Teaching critical thinkingby NG Holmes · 2015 · Cited by 479 — We present a simple learning framework that employs cycles of decision... The instructional framework deliberately embedded several thinking moves: Compare evidence quantitatively rather than relying on visual inspection or intuition . Judge competing explanations using statistical evidence rather than preference. Act on the judgement by redesigning measurements or collecting additional data. Evaluate the result before beginning another cycle. The important point is that students were not merely told to \"think critically\". They were repeatedly required to make concrete decisions under uncertainty and immediately experience the consequences of those decisions in the next stage of the experiment. [PNAS] pnas.org Teaching critical thinkingby NG Holmes · 2015 · Cited by 479 — We present a simple learning framework that employs cycles of decision... This design reflects an important distinction in thinking instruction. Analytical skill develops not from hearing explanations about scientific reasoning but from repeatedly practising the specific judgements that scientists routinely make. What persisted after prompts were removed One of the strongest findings from this work came after the explicit instructional support was gradually withdrawn. During the early part of the transformed course, students received direct prompts telling them to compare data, evaluate models and improve their methods. Later laboratory activities removed many of these prompts. Despite this reduction in guidance, students continued making sophisticated evidence based decisions on their own, while students in the conventional laboratory generally did not adopt these behaviours spontaneously. [PNAS] pnas.org Teaching critical thinkingby NG Holmes · 2015 · Cited by 479 — We present a simple learning framework that employs cycles of decision... The researchers also found that these students transferred their judgement skills to unfamiliar experiments that were structurally different from the earlier activities. Rather than simply repeating memorised procedures, they independently questioned assumptions , evaluated conflicting evidence and proposed improvements to experimental design. This persistence after scaffolding was removed is important because it suggests that students had learned a general reasoning habit rather than a scripted classroom routine. [PNAS] pnas.org Teaching critical thinkingby NG Holmes · 2015 · Cited by 479 — We present a simple learning framework that employs cycles of decision... Dr. Natasha G. Holmes: The Trouble with Traditional Physics Labs Channel: MU Society of Physics Students Open on YouTube Evidence that the approach measures real judgement As physics laboratory instruction increasingly focused on reasoning rather than verification, researchers also needed better ways to measure improvement. This led to the development of the Physics Lab Inventory of Critical Thinking (PLIC), an assessment specifically designed to evaluate how students judge data, evaluate methods and decide what to trust in experimental situations. Unlike traditional tests that reward factual recall, the PLIC presents students with realistic experimental scenarios requiring evidence based decisions about data quality, uncertainty, models and methodological choices. Validation involved thousands of students across dozens of institutions, providing evidence that the instrument measures critical thinking within experimental physics rather than general physics knowledge. [APS Links] link.aps.org APS LinksQuantifying critical thinking: Development and validation of the...by C Walsh · 2019 · Cited by 148 — Introductory physics lab... Subsequent research comparing different laboratory designs across more than 100 institutions found that laboratories explicitly focused on experimentation skills consistently outperformed laboratories primarily designed to reinforce lecture concepts. Activities encouraging student decision making and scientific communication explained much of this advantage, whereas simply spending more time on modelling activities contributed comparatively little on its own. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Skills focused lab instruction improves critical thinking Skills focused lab instruction improves critical thinking...April 11, 2022 — 25 Feb 2026 — We show that labs focused on deve... Published: April 11, 2022 Dr. Natasha G. Holmes (Cornell) September 23rd 2016 Channel: Faculty of Science, University of Manitoba Open on YouTube What this case reveals about teaching judgement The transformed physics laboratory provides"},{"title":"Reasoning Feedback","url":"/reasoning-feedback/","description":"Useful feedback names the strength of the reasoning route, not only whether the final answer was correct.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teaching","level":3,"text":"Reasoning Feedback Feedback That Teaches Better Judgement Reasoning Feedback Reasoning Feedback Feedback That Teaches Better Judgement Useful feedback names the strength of the reasoning route, not only whether the final answer was correct. Useful feedback names the strength of the reasoning route, not only whether the final answer was correct. policy intervention Why right or wrong marking is too thin Feedback on claims, evidence, and uncertainty Rubrics that reward reasoning quality improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching reasoning feedback Introduction Improving analytical skill depends on more than telling learners whether they reached the correct answer. The most useful feedback explains why a line of reasoning was strong, weak or incomplete, so that the learner can apply the same judgement in a different situation. This shifts feedback from simple evaluation (\"correct\" or \"incorrect\") to guidance about claims, evidence, assumptions , alternatives and uncertainty. Research on formative feedback consistently finds that feedback is most effective when it helps learners understand how to improve their thinking, not merely how to fix a single response. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org For this reason, scholars have been working on developing models FrontiersA Review of Feedback Models and Theories: Descriptions...December 31, 2021 — by AA Lipnevich · 2021 · Cited by 401 — The posit... Published: December 31, 2021 Within explicit teaching of thinking skills, reasoning focused feedback is the mechanism that makes judgement visible. It tells learners not only what happened, but what kind of thinking produced the outcome . Why right or wrong marking is too thin A mark or model answer can indicate whether a learner reached the expected conclusion, but it often conceals the quality of the reasoning that produced it. Two learners may arrive at the same answer through very different processes: one by carefully weighing evidence, another by guessing or following an unreliable shortcut. Equally, a thoughtful argument can produce the wrong conclusion because of incomplete information rather than poor reasoning. When assessment rewards only final answers, learners naturally optimise for answer production instead of analytical judgement. They become less likely to: explain why evidence supports a conclusion; consider alternative explanations; identify assumptions; express appropriate uncertainty; or reflect on what would change their mind. Educational researchers have long argued that formative feedback should modify the learner's thinking, not simply verify correctness. Reviews of feedback theory similarly conclude that effective feedback provides information that helps learners close the gap between current reasoning and desired reasoning rather than merely reporting success or failure. [myweb.fsu.edu] myweb.fsu.edu shute 2007 f Focus on Formative Feedbackby VJ Shute · 2007 · Cited by 474 — Formative feedback represents information communicated to the learner that... This distinction becomes particularly important in disciplines where multiple defensible answers exist, including history, law, medicine, engineering and public policy. In these fields, reasoning quality often matters more than arriving at one predetermined conclusion. Feedback on claims, evidence and uncertainty Reasoning focused feedback works because it comments on the components of judgement rather than the outcome alone. Claims Feedback should identify whether the central claim follows logically from the available information. Instead of: \"Your conclusion is wrong.\" A reasoning focused version might be: \"Your conclusion goes beyond what your evidence currently supports.\" The learner receives information about the relationship between conclusion and evidence rather than simply being told the answer. Evidence Strong analytical feedback discusses the quality and use of evidence. Useful comments include observations such as: the strongest evidence was ignored; one source was treated as more reliable than it deserved; contradictory evidence was not addressed; evidence was described but not interpreted; examples were anecdotal rather than representative. Such feedback teaches learners to inspect how evidence functions within an argument instead of merely collecting more facts. Using SLS Features to Nurture 21CC Critical Adaptive and Inventive Thinking CAIT in Students v2 Channel: lookang AI Open on YouTube Reasoning process Good feedback also highlights the reasoning pathway itself. For example: \"You considered two competing explanations before deciding.\" \"You ruled out alternatives too quickly.\" \"Your assumptions remained implicit.\" \"You identified an important limitation in the data.\" Notice that these comments describe thinking behaviours that can transfer into future problems. Uncertainty Analytical judgement rarely involves complete certainty. Learners therefore benefit from feedback on how they expressed confidence. Examples include: recognising when evidence justifies only a tentative conclusion; distinguishing speculation from inference; identifying missing information; stating what additional evidence would strengthen confidence. Research on feedback consistently emphasises that high quality feedback is specific, explanatory and directed towards improvement rather than simple evaluation. [myweb.fsu.edu] myweb.fsu.edu shute 2007 f Focus on Formative Feedbackby VJ Shute · 2007 · Cited by 474 — Formative feedback represents information communicated to the learner that... Feedback should recognise strong reasoning as well as errors Many feedback systems focus almost exclusively on mistakes . While correction is important, learners also need to understand which reasoning strategies they should repeat. Comments such as these reinforce productive habits: \"You tested your first explanation against competing possibilities.\" \"You distinguished observation from interpretation.\" \"You recognised uncertainty rather than overstating confidence.\" \"You changed your position when stronger evidence appeared.\" This differs from general praise like \"Good work.\" General praise identifies success without identifying its cause. Process oriented feedback instead identifies the thinking behaviour responsible for success, making it easier for learners to reproduce that behaviour elsewhere. This principle aligns with broader feedback models that distinguish information about task completion from information about the processes and self regulation underlying performance. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org For this reason, scholars have been working on developing models FrontiersA Review of Feedback Models and Theories: Descriptions...December 31, 2021 — by AA Lipnevich · 2021 · Cited by 401 — The posit... Published: December 31, 2021 What Feedback Should Do (That AI Can’t) Professor Dylan Wiliam Channel: Nurture Formative Assessment & Feedback Solution Open on YouTube Rubrics that reward reasoning quality One practical way to embed reasoning feedback is through assessment rubrics that separate reasoning quality from answer accuracy. A reasoning rubric might evaluate: DimensionHigh quality reasoning looks likeClaimClear, proportionate to available evidenceEvidenceRelevant, reliable and accurately interpretedAlternative explanationsSerious competing explanations consideredAssumptionsExplicitly identified where importantLogicConclusions follow from premises without major gapsUncertaintyAppropriate confidence and acknowledgement of limitations Such rubrics communicate that analytical skill is not a hidden expectation but an observable performance. They also improve consistency between teaching and assessment. If classrooms encourage questioning and evidence evaluation but examinations reward only memorised answers, learners receive conflicting signals. Explicit reasoning criteria reduce this mismatch. Practical examples of reasoning feedback Consider a history assignment asking why an empire declined. Answer focused feedback [arxiv.org] arxiv.org Source details in endnotes. \"Incorrect. Economic factors were more important.\" Reasoning focused feedback [pure.tue.nl] pure.tue.nl feedback: a learning theory perspective1 Jan 2013 — Shute (2008), reasoning from cognitivism, indicated that the extent of feedback's ef... \"You relied mainly on political evidence while giving little attention to economic sources. Your conclusion therefore reflects only part of the available evidence. Consider whether incorporating trade data would change your judgement.\" The second comment teaches a reasoning strategy that applies beyond the immediate topic. Similarly, in science: Answer focused \"Hypothesis rejected.\" Reasoning focused \"You interpreted correlation as evidence of causation . What alternative explanations could account for the same pattern?\" Or in business: Answer focused \"Recommendation not convincing.\" Reasoning focused \"You identified the operational problem clearly, but your recommendation assumes"},{"title":"Transfer","url":"/transfer-dc92b5/","description":"Thinking moves transfer more reliably when learners apply the same principle across varied real contexts.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Teaching","level":3,"text":"Transfer Why Thinking Skills Do Not Travel Easily Transfer Transfer Why Thinking Skills Do Not Travel Easily Thinking moves transfer more reliably when learners apply the same principle across varied real contexts. Thinking moves transfer more reliably when learners apply the same principle across varied real contexts. mechanism Why context locks in reasoning habits Using varied examples to teach underlying principles Designing transfer tasks without generic drills improving your thinking and analytical skills teaching transfer Introduction Teaching a thinking skill once does not guarantee that learners will use it elsewhere. A student who carefully evaluates evidence in a history lesson may fail to do the same when interpreting a scientific claim or making a financial decision. This failure is known as a problem of transfer : applying knowledge or reasoning learned in one context to a different one. Research consistently shows that transfer, especially between different subjects or real world situations, is much harder than educators often assume. [rapunselshair.pbworks.com+2Taylor & Francis Online] rapunselshair.pbworks.com When and Where Do We Apply What We Learn? SM Barnett · 2002 · Cited by 3474 — Despite a century's worth of research, arguments surrounding the question of whether far tran... Within explicit thinking skills instruction, transfer is therefore not an optional final step but a teaching objective in its own right. The most successful approaches deliberately help learners recognise the underlying principle that connects apparently different problems, rather than expecting them to notice those connections independently. Why context locks in reasoning habits People rarely store knowledge as isolated, abstract rules. Instead, they remember ideas together with the situations in which they were learned. When a familiar context reappears, the associated reasoning strategy is triggered. When the context changes substantially, the learner may fail to recognise that the same thinking process still applies. [rapunselshair.pbworks.com] rapunselshair.pbworks.com When and Where Do We Apply What We Learn? SM Barnett · 2002 · Cited by 3474 — Despite a century's worth of research, arguments surrounding the question of whether far tran... This explains why transfer often fails even when learners understand the original lesson perfectly. The obstacle is frequently not lack of knowledge but failure to recognise that previous knowledge is relevant. Researchers distinguish between: Near transfer , where the new problem closely resembles the original learning situation. Far transfer , where the new situation looks different on the surface even though the same reasoning principle applies. [rapunselshair.pbworks.com] rapunselshair.pbworks.com When and Where Do We Apply What We Learn? SM Barnett · 2002 · Cited by 3474 — Despite a century's worth of research, arguments surrounding the question of whether far tran... For example: Comparing two newspaper articles and comparing two historical eyewitness accounts both require evaluating evidence quality. Diagnosing a fault in an engineering system and identifying the cause of declining business sales both require testing alternative explanations before settling on a conclusion. Although the surface details differ, the underlying thinking move is identical. Barnett and Ceci argued that transfer cannot simply be described as either present or absent because new situations differ along many dimensions simultaneously, including subject area, physical setting, time elapsed, social context and task purpose. The greater these differences become, the more difficult successful transfer usually becomes. [rapunselshair.pbworks.com] rapunselshair.pbworks.com When and Where Do We Apply What We Learn? SM Barnett · 2002 · Cited by 3474 — Despite a century's worth of research, arguments surrounding the question of whether far tran... Teaching for Transfer by Dr Nicholas Lee Channel: Sunway Centre for English Language Studies Open on YouTube Using varied examples to teach underlying principles One of the strongest ways to improve transfer is to teach the same thinking principle repeatedly across deliberately varied examples. If students only evaluate evidence in science lessons, they may unconsciously conclude that \"evaluating evidence\" is something people do only in science. If they encounter the same reasoning process in history, geography, economics, media studies and everyday decision making, they begin to recognise the principle itself rather than merely remembering the original lesson. Variation helps learners separate: superficial features of a problem, from the deeper structure that determines how it should be solved. This distinction has been central to transfer research for decades. Learners often notice obvious contextual details while overlooking the deeper relationships that actually determine successful reasoning. Teaching should therefore deliberately draw attention to those hidden common patterns. [rapunselshair.pbworks.com] rapunselshair.pbworks.com When and Where Do We Apply What We Learn? SM Barnett · 2002 · Cited by 3474 — Despite a century's worth of research, arguments surrounding the question of whether far tran... For example, a teacher introducing causal reasoning might use: Surface contextShared thinking principleWhy did a bridge fail?Evaluate competing causal explanations using evidence.Why did a business lose customers?Evaluate competing causal explanations using evidence.Why did an ancient civilisation collapse?Evaluate competing causal explanations using evidence.Why did a medical treatment succeed?Evaluate competing causal explanations using evidence. The learner is repeatedly practising one cognitive operation rather than memorising isolated subject specific routines. Designing transfer tasks without generic drills Transfer improves less from generic \"critical thinking exercises\" than from carefully designed tasks that preserve an authentic problem while changing the surrounding context. Instead of asking learners to solve dozens of nearly identical questions, teachers can vary only one important dimension at a time. For example, after introducing source evaluation, successive tasks might change: the subject matter, the type of evidence, the intended audience, the amount of uncertainty, or the consequences of making a poor judgement. Because the reasoning principle remains constant while contextual details change, learners gradually learn to recognise when the principle should be applied. Research also suggests that learners benefit from being explicitly told that transfer is expected. Simply asking questions such as: \"Where else could this reasoning help?\" \"What makes this problem similar to one you solved last week?\" \"What is different here, and what remains the same?\" encourages learners to search for deeper structural similarities rather than stopping at obvious differences. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICA Review of Transfer Theories and Effective Instructional...by S Hajian · 2019 · Cited by 292 — One of the primary goals of educatio... Transfer of Learning Taking Learning Seriously Channel: Bill Cerbin Open on YouTube Helping learners recognise deep structure One reason transfer fails is that experts and novices notice different features of problems. Experts tend to classify problems according to their underlying structure. Novices often classify them according to surface appearance. For instance, experienced scientists may immediately recognise that two experiments both test competing hypotheses, even if one concerns climate change and the other medicine. A beginner may instead see only that one involves weather and the other hospitals. Teaching can narrow this gap by making deep structure explicit through techniques such as: comparing multiple cases side by side, asking learners to explain why two unrelated problems require the same reasoning, requiring students to justify which thinking strategy they selected, discussing why an apparently similar problem actually requires different reasoning. These activities help learners build abstract mental representations that are more likely to survive changes in context. Balancing subject knowledge and general thinking skills Transfer research also cautions against assuming that general thinking skills can be taught independently of subject knowledge. Critical thinking depends on understanding the content of the problem as well as knowing appropriate reasoning strategies. A learner cannot evaluate medical evidence effectively without some understanding of medicine, nor analyse economic claims without basic economic concepts. Successful transfer therefore combines two elements: explicit teaching of transferable thinking strategies, and repeated opportunities to apply those strategies within rich subject knowledge. Rather than treating thinking as separate from disciplines, effective instruction helps learners discover that"},{"title":"Mistakes","url":"/mistakes/","description":"Biases and reasoning errors become more manageable when people practice spotting them in real decisions.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Mistakes Which Thinking Mistakes Keep Repeating? Mistakes Mistakes Which Thinking Mistakes Keep Repeating? Biases and reasoning errors become more manageable when people practice spotting them in real decisions. Biases and reasoning errors become more manageable when people practice spotting them in real decisions. mechanism Common reasoning errors Practice examples Prevention routines improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes Introduction Common thinking mistakes are not character flaws; they are repeatable patterns in how people search for evidence, estimate risk, explain outcomes and protect previous choices. They matter because the same error can look different in each decision: confirmation bias in a political argument, anchoring in a salary negotiation, base rate neglect in a medical judgement, or sunk cost thinking in a failing project. The practical aim is not to memorise a long catalogue of biases, but to practise recognising the few mistakes that keep returning when decisions are uncertain, emotional, socially exposed or hard to reverse. The most useful practice is concrete: write down the claim, name the tempting shortcut, ask what evidence would change your mind, compare the case with similar past cases, and review the decision before the outcome is known. Research on debiasing suggests that warnings alone are usually weak, while structured training, feedback , “consider the opposite” prompts and decision routines can improve judgement in real settings. [PubMed+2Sage Journals] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Debiasing Training Improves Decision Making in the Fieldby AL Sellier · 2019 · Cited by 181 — The results provide promising evidenc... Why the Same Mistakes Keep Repeating Thinking errors persist because they often start as useful shortcuts. It is sensible to use memory when judging risk, first estimates when planning, and vivid examples when learning from experience. The trouble begins when a shortcut is used in the wrong setting: a vivid anecdote replaces a base rate, a first number becomes a hidden anchor, or a previous investment becomes a reason to keep investing even when the future case has collapsed. Biases are also hard to notice from the inside. The “bias blind spot” describes people’s tendency to see cognitive and motivational biases more readily in others than in themselves; Pronin, Lin and Ross found this pattern across several surveys, including comparisons with “average” others and peers. [Princeton University] collaborate.princeton.edu the bias blind spot perceptions of bias in self versus others Princeton UniversityThe bias blind spot: Perceptions of bias in self versus othersby E Pronin · 2002 · Cited by 2042 — Three studies sugg... That is why “be less biased” is a poor instruction. A better goal is to build routines that make the mistake visible before it quietly shapes the conclusion. A useful practice frame is: What is my first answer, and what kind of mistake would make that answer feel too easy? If the answer came quickly, the likely risks are anchoring, availability or overconfidence. If the answer protects a prior belief, the likely risks are confirmation bias and motivated reasoning. If the answer excuses continuing a failing choice, sunk cost thinking and escalation of commitment are likely suspects. Common Reasoning Errors Confirmation bias: looking for evidence that lets you stop thinking Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, interpret or remember information in ways that support what you already believe. Raymond Nickerson’s influential review describes it as a broad and persistent phenomenon, not merely a deliberate attempt to distort evidence; people can be selective without realising they are being selective. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many A common everyday version is the “one sided search”. Someone asks, “Why is this proposal a good idea?” rather than “What would make this proposal fail?” The wording quietly turns analysis into advocacy. In hiring, it might mean treating a strong first impression as the hypothesis and then interpreting later answers through it. In health or finance, it might mean searching only for evidence that supports the action one already wants to take. The practice move is to make disconfirmation explicit. Before gathering more evidence, write: “The main reason I might be wrong is…” Then look for the strongest contrary case, not the easiest objection to defeat. “Consider the opposite” has experimental support as a corrective strategy: Lord, Lepper and Preston found that prompting people to consider alternative possibilities reduced biased social judgement more effectively than simply telling them to be unbiased. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov In two separate but conceptually parallel Considering the opposite: a corrective strategy for social...by CG Lord · 1984 · Cited by 1170 — Individuals who are induced to co... Anchoring: letting the first number set the room temperature Anchoring happens when an initial value, example or framing point pulls later estimates towards it, even when the anchor is arbitrary or weakly relevant. The classic account comes from work by Tversky and Kahneman, and later research has examined why adjustments away from anchors are often insufficient. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This mistake is worth practising against because anchors often enter decisions innocently. A project’s first budget, a seller’s first price, a colleague’s first diagnosis, a forecast in a slide deck or an artificial intelligence output can become the mental reference point. Even when people know the first estimate is rough, later discussion may revolve around “adjusting” it rather than independently rebuilding the estimate. The practice move is independent estimation. Before seeing other people’s numbers, produce your own estimate and state the basis for it. In group settings, ask each person to write an initial judgement privately before discussion. When using a model, calculator or AI system, first define what evidence would make its answer too high, too low or irrelevant. Research on AI assisted decision making has specifically examined de anchoring strategies because people may over weight machine suggestions when the system is uncertain or wrong. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. Availability: mistaking memorable for likely The availability heuristic is the tendency to judge frequency, probability or importance by how easily examples come to mind. It can be useful: recent experience often contains real information. But it misleads when memory is shaped by vividness, recency, media exposure, personal experience or emotional intensity rather than by representative evidence. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision Lab Availability Heuristic The Decision Lab Availability Heuristic A manager may overestimate a rare operational risk because a recent failure was painful, while underestimating a dull but common source of loss. A traveller may fear a dramatic accident more than a routine danger. A voter may treat the most shareable story as typical. The problem is not that anecdotes are useless; it is that they need to be placed beside base rates. The practice move is to ask: “What would I believe if this example had not come easily to mind?” Then look for frequencies, comparison classes and boring denominators. If one case dominates your attention, deliberately collect three ordinary cases from the same category. Availability loses some of its grip when the memorable example is no longer the only mental evidence on the table. Confirmation Bias: The Reason Smart People Are Usually the Most Wrong Channel: ThinkLessPsy Open on YouTube Base rate neglect: ignoring how common something is before the new clue arrives Base rate neglect occurs when people underuse general statistical information about how common something is and overuse vivid case specific clues. This matters in domains such as medicine, law, hiring, forecasting and data interpretation, where a striking signal can still be misleading if the underlying condition is rare. Research on base rates is nuanced: some studies show neglect, while other work finds that experts can use base rates in real world sequential decisions when the information is presented in usable ways. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment+2PMC] cambridge.org Open source on cambridge.org. The classic practical trap is confusing “this looks like X” with “this is probably X”. A candidate may resemble a successful employee, a symptom may resemble a serious condition, or a start up may resemble a famous winner. Without asking how common the category is, the judgement can become a story matching exercise. The practice move is to start with the outside number. Ask: “In a relevant comparison group, how often does this happen?” Then update from there. This does not mean ignoring"},{"title":"Availability","url":"/availability/","description":"Availability errors happen when a memorable story crowds out ordinary frequencies, base rates and dull comparison cases.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Availability Memorable is not the same as likely Availability Availability Memorable is not the same as likely Availability errors happen when a memorable story crowds out ordinary frequencies, base rates and dull comparison cases. Availability errors happen when a memorable story crowds out ordinary frequencies, base rates and dull comparison cases. mechanism Vivid stories versus boring denominators Risk examples in work, travel and politics How to collect ordinary comparison cases improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes availability Introduction When people judge risk, they rarely calculate probabilities from scratch. Instead, they often ask themselves a simpler question: How easily can I think of an example? If a dramatic event comes to mind immediately, it feels common. If ordinary events leave little impression, they feel rare or unimportant. This mental shortcut, known as the availability heuristic , is often useful because frequent events are genuinely easier to remember. However, it also produces predictable errors when memorable stories replace actual frequencies or base rates. The result is that rare but vivid dangers can appear more threatening than common but less newsworthy risks, affecting decisions in everyday life, work, travel and public policy. [UMass People] people.umass.edu Tversky availability UMass PeopleAvailability: A heuristic for judging frequency and probabilityby A Tversky · Cited by 17494 — Availability is an ecologicall... Why memorable stories feel more convincing than statistics The availability heuristic was first described by psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman as a way people estimate frequency and probability by relying on examples that come readily to mind. In many situations this shortcut is efficient because repeated experiences are easier to recall . Problems arise when ease of recall is driven not by frequency but by emotional intensity, novelty, media attention or personal experience. [UMass People] people.umass.edu Tversky availability UMass PeopleAvailability: A heuristic for judging frequency and probabilityby A Tversky · Cited by 17494 — Availability is an ecologicall... A vivid story has several advantages over a table of numbers: It is easier to imagine. It often includes identifiable people rather than anonymous statistics. It evokes emotion, making the memory stronger. It is frequently repeated by news media and social networks. It fits naturally into conversations, allowing it to spread further. Because memory retrieval feels effortless, the brain mistakenly treats that ease as evidence that the event itself is common. In other words, the accessibility of an example is confused with its probability . [UMass People] people.umass.edu Tversky availability UMass PeopleAvailability: A heuristic for judging frequency and probabilityby A Tversky · Cited by 17494 — Availability is an ecologicall... Why boring denominators disappear Risk is always a comparison between a numerator and a denominator. Hearing that ten people experienced an unusual side effect sounds alarming until it is compared with ten million people who did not. Unfortunately, denominators are rarely memorable. Human memory stores stories more effectively than proportions. A single dramatic account of an aircraft emergency can overshadow millions of uneventful flights, while thousands of safe daily journeys by car rarely become memorable because nothing unusual happened. This imbalance means that people often remember the exceptional event but forget the population from which it came. The result is systematic neglect of base rates—the ordinary frequencies needed to judge risk accurately. [UMass People] people.umass.edu Tversky availability UMass PeopleAvailability: A heuristic for judging frequency and probabilityby A Tversky · Cited by 17494 — Availability is an ecologicall... Anchoring Bias Explained Psychology Channel: Sociologylearners Open on YouTube How vivid examples distort risk in everyday decisions At work Managers may overestimate the chance of a cyberattack after a highly publicised breach while underinvesting in more common operational failures such as routine human error, poor documentation or supplier disruptions. Recruiters may also remember one spectacular hiring mistake and become excessively cautious about candidates who resemble that case, despite broader hiring evidence suggesting otherwise. During travel Many people report greater fear of flying than driving after seeing extensive television coverage of an aviation accident. Commercial aircraft accidents receive intense international attention because they are unusual and dramatic, whereas road collisions occur individually, receive less coverage and therefore feel less psychologically available despite their far greater frequency. The issue is not that aircraft accidents are unimportant. Rather, the amount of attention they receive is a poor guide to their statistical likelihood. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Perception of riskby P Slovic · 1987 · Cited by 15680 — This research aims to aid risk analysis and policy making by providing a ba... In politics and public debate Political campaigns frequently rely on memorable individual cases rather than representative evidence. One shocking crime, one striking immigration story or one exceptional welfare fraud case can dominate public discussion, even if the broader data show these cases are uncommon. Individual stories are emotionally compelling and important in their own right, but they do not automatically represent the underlying pattern. Sound policy requires asking whether the memorable case is typical or exceptional. Why media coverage amplifies the effect News organisations naturally prioritise events that are unusual, emotionally engaging and visually striking. Those are also precisely the events that become easiest to remember. This creates a gap between the world people experience directly and the world they remember. Repeated exposure to reports of terrorism, violent crime, shark attacks or rare diseases can increase perceived prevalence even when objective risks remain very low. Meanwhile, chronic hazards such as cardiovascular disease, air pollution or everyday traffic collisions often receive less dramatic coverage despite causing far more harm overall. [PubMed+2Lamont Doherty Earth Observatory] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Perception of riskby P Slovic · 1987 · Cited by 15680 — This research aims to aid risk analysis and policy making by providing a ba... Research on risk perception also shows that people respond not only to probabilities but to feelings. Hazards perceived as catastrophic, uncontrollable or unfamiliar produce stronger emotional reactions than equally probable risks that seem familiar or voluntary. Emotion therefore strengthens the influence of memorable examples instead of correcting it. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Affect, Risk, and Decision MakingRisk is perceived and acted on in 2 fundamental ways. Risk as feelings refers to indiv... Anchoring, with Daniel Kahneman (author of Thinking, Fast and Slow) Channel: Farrar Straus & Giroux Open on YouTube Why personal experience can mislead Personal experience often feels like the strongest possible evidence because it is concrete rather than abstract. Yet an individual's experience is an extremely small sample. Someone who has experienced one burglary may conclude crime is rapidly increasing. Someone who has never encountered flooding may underestimate flood risk despite living in a vulnerable area. Neither judgement necessarily reflects the wider pattern. The same applies in organisations. Teams often generalise from one recent success or failure without asking whether it represents a long term trend or merely random variation. This is one reason organisations increasingly rely on dashboards, incident databases and long run performance measures rather than retrospective storytelling alone. The Anchoring Bias: Why The First Offer Matters Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube How to collect ordinary comparison cases The most effective defence is not trying to ignore vivid stories. Instead, place them alongside ordinary comparison cases. Useful habits include: Ask for the denominator. Out of how many total cases did this event occur? Look for the base rate first. What usually happens before examining exceptional cases? Collect several examples, not one. Five representative cases are usually more informative than the single most memorable one. Search deliberately for uneventful outcomes . Safe flights, successful software deployments or ordinary medical recoveries rarely become headlines but often represent the overwhelming majority. Compare with similar risks. Instead of asking whether an event sounds frightening, ask whether it is more or less likely than comparable hazards. Separate emotional impact from frequency. A tragic event deserves empathy without automatically becoming evidence"},{"title":"Base Rates","url":"/base-rates/","description":"Base-rate neglect turns diagnosis, hiring and forecasting into story matching when the first question should be how common the outcome is.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Base Rates Start with how common it is Base Rates Base Rates Start with how common it is Base rate neglect turns diagnosis, hiring and forecasting into story matching when the first question should be how common the outcome is. Base rate neglect turns diagnosis, hiring and forecasting into story matching when the first question should be how common the outcome is. dataset evidence The trap of 'this looks like X' Medical, hiring and forecasting examples Updating from a comparison group improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes base rates Introduction Good judgement often begins with a question that feels almost too simple: how common is this outcome before considering the details of this particular case? Base rates are the background frequencies of events in a relevant comparison group. Ignoring them leads people to match stories rather than estimate probabilities . A candidate \"looks like\" a successful executive, a patient \"sounds like\" they have a rare disease, or a new project \"feels different\" from past failures. These impressions may contain useful information, but they should usually be adjustments to the starting probability, not replacements for it. Research on judgement and decision making consistently shows that people often overweight vivid, case specific information and underweight statistical prevalence, particularly when a compelling narrative is available. Yet the same body of research also shows that the problem is more nuanced than early laboratory experiments suggested: people can use base rates effectively when information is presented clearly, when they have relevant experience, or when decisions unfold over repeated real world situations. [PMC+2Branden Fitelson] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCExperts use base rates in real world sequential decisions by D Link · 2021 · Cited by 15 — We argue here that while naïve subjects demonstrate base rate neglect in laboratory conditions, exper... The trap of \"this looks like X\" The classic mistake is to confuse resemblance with probability. If someone matches our mental image of a profession, illness or personality type, we instinctively treat that resemblance as strong evidence. Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky demonstrated that people frequently judge likelihood by representativeness: they ask whether a case resembles a stereotype rather than how frequently the outcome actually occurs. The result is base rate neglect—giving too little weight to prior probabilities when making predictions. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net 209410239 On the psychology of prediction On the psychology of prediction27 Sept 2025 — Considers that intuitive predictions follow a judgmental heuristic representati... This shortcut is appealing because stories are concrete while base rates are abstract. A detailed personal description feels informative, whereas \"2% of applicants succeed\" or \"1 in 1,000 people has this disease\" can seem impersonal or irrelevant. Research suggests that people often classify specific information as more relevant than general statistical information, even when the statistics are objectively more informative for estimating probability. [The Decision Lab] thedecisionlab.com The Decision Lab Base Rate Fallacy The Decision LabBase Rate Fallacy The Decision...Base Rate Fallacy is our tendency to give more weight to the event specific informati... The consequence is not that individual details should be ignored. Instead, they should update an initial estimate derived from the appropriate comparison group. The sequence matters: 1. Start with the outside frequency. 2. Ask how diagnostic the case specific evidence really is. 3. Adjust the probability accordingly. Skipping the first step often produces overconfident judgements. The Anchoring Bias: Why The First Offer Matters Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Medical, hiring and forecasting examples Medical diagnosis Medicine provides one of the clearest demonstrations of why base rates matter. Imagine a disease affecting only 1% of the population. Even if a diagnostic test correctly identifies affected patients 99% of the time, false positives can outnumber true positives because healthy people vastly outnumber patients. Many people—including trained physicians in some studies—substantially overestimate the probability that a positive result means a patient actually has the disease. The prevalence of the disease must be combined with the test's accuracy before interpreting the result. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Tversky and Kahneman's Cognitive Illusions: Who Can Solve...by G Bruckmaier · 2021 · Cited by 47 — The mathematical reason for the co... This explains why clinicians often distinguish between screening tests used in low risk populations and confirmatory tests used after other evidence has increased the prior probability. The same laboratory result can have very different meanings depending on how common the condition is in the population being tested. Hiring decisions Recruitment often shifts rapidly from evidence to storytelling. A confident interviewee who resembles previous successful managers may feel like an obvious choice. However, interviews typically provide limited predictive information compared with structured assessments and historical hiring data. Suppose only one in ten applicants for a demanding role ultimately performs exceptionally. An impressive interview should increase the estimated probability of success, but it rarely justifies acting as though excellence has suddenly become the most likely outcome. Beginning with historical success rates encourages more calibrated expectations and reduces the influence of charisma or stereotype. The practical question is not, \"Does this person remind me of a successful employee?\" but, \"Among applicants who looked similar in measurable ways, how many actually succeeded?\" Forecasting projects Forecasting research has repeatedly found that planners favour the \"inside view\": concentrating on the unique features of the current project while neglecting how comparable projects actually turned out. The recommended alternative is an \"outside view\" or reference class forecast. Instead of asking why the current project will succeed, decision makers identify a relevant class of similar completed projects, examine the distribution of outcomes , and then adjust only if there is strong evidence that the current case genuinely differs. This approach has become influential in project planning because it counteracts optimism driven by compelling project narratives. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting Updating from a comparison group Using base rates does not mean treating every individual as average. It means establishing a sensible starting point before incorporating additional evidence. A practical sequence looks like this: Choose the right comparison group. The base rate must come from genuinely similar cases. The average success rate of all businesses is less informative than the success rate of businesses in the same industry, scale and market. Estimate the prior probability. Ask, \"Before I knew anything special about this case, what would I expect?\" Evaluate the strength of the new evidence. Does it genuinely distinguish this case from others, or is it merely vivid? Update gradually. Strong evidence should move the estimate substantially; weak evidence should move it only slightly. This process reflects the logic behind Bayesian reasoning, even when no formal calculations are performed. The important habit is to begin with frequencies rather than anecdotes . Anchoring, with Daniel Kahneman (author of Thinking, Fast and Slow) Channel: Farrar Straus & Giroux Open on YouTube Why experts sometimes do better—and sometimes do not Early laboratory studies gave the impression that people almost automatically ignore base rates. Later work has complicated that picture. Research examining expert decisions in real world settings has found that experienced decision makers can make effective use of base rate information, particularly when decisions are repeated over time and feedback is available. For example, analyses of elite beach volleyball serving decisions suggest that players adapt their choices according to opponents' underlying success rates rather than relying solely on recent memorable plays. This indicates that repeated experience can encourage attention to statistical regularities. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCExperts use base rates in real world sequential decisions by D Link · 2021 · Cited by 15 — We argue here that while naïve subjects demonstrate base rate neglect in laboratory conditions, exper... However, expertise is not a guarantee. Medical research has documented situations in which physicians still underweight disease prevalence, especially when presented with compelling case descriptions or when statistical information is communicated poorly. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University"},{"title":"Confirmation Bias","url":"/confirmation-bias-af1e34/","description":"Confirmation bias often begins with one-sided questions that make a preferred answer feel better supported than it really is.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Confirmation Bias When evidence search becomes self defense Confirmation Bias Confirmation Bias When evidence search becomes self defense Confirmation bias often begins with one sided questions that make a preferred answer feel better supported than it really is. Confirmation bias often begins with one sided questions that make a preferred answer feel better supported than it really is. mechanism One sided questions and selective evidence Hiring, health and finance examples How to look for disconfirming evidence improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes confirmation bias Introduction Confirmation bias turns evidence gathering into advocacy when the purpose of a search quietly shifts from discovering what is true to defending what already feels true. The change is often subtle. Instead of asking, “What does the evidence show?”, people ask, “Why am I right?” or “Where can I find support for the conclusion I already prefer?” The result is not simply an incomplete search. It is a search strategy designed—often unconsciously—to make one answer appear stronger than it really is. This matters because many important decisions in hiring, health and finance are made under uncertainty, where selective evidence can create unwarranted confidence rather than better judgement. Research consistently shows that confirmation bias affects what people look for, how they interpret ambiguous information and what they remember afterwards. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UCSD PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation... Published: October 6, 2004 How a search becomes an act of self defence Confirmation bias is commonly described as the tendency to seek, interpret and recall information in ways that support an existing belief. However, the more revealing mechanism is that the search itself changes purpose. Instead of treating evidence as something that could overturn a hypothesis, people begin treating evidence as something that should justify it. Raymond Nickerson's influential review argues that this process is usually not deliberate deception. Rather, people unknowingly become selective in how they acquire and use evidence. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UCSD PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation... Published: October 6, 2004 The shift often follows a simple sequence: 1. A person forms an early preference or impression. 2. That preference becomes the working hypothesis. 3. Questions are framed to confirm rather than challenge it. 4. Supporting evidence feels persuasive because contradictory evidence was never actively sought. 5. Confidence increases, even if the overall quality of evidence has not improved. The critical point is that confidence grows because the search was one sided, not because the evidence genuinely became stronger. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UCSD PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation... Published: October 6, 2004 One sided questions create one sided evidence The wording of a question determines the evidence that can be found. A neutral investigator asks: What explanations best fit the evidence? What evidence would distinguish between competing explanations? An advocate tends to ask: Why is this idea correct? What evidence supports this conclusion? Who agrees with me? Those questions almost guarantee supportive answers because they exclude competing possibilities before the search begins. Psychological research on hypothesis testing has repeatedly shown that people often favour \"positive tests\"—looking for evidence expected if their hypothesis is true—rather than seeking observations that would prove it wrong. This approach is efficient in many situations but becomes misleading when several competing explanations could also fit the available evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias The internet amplifies this tendency. Search engines can return excellent information, but if someone searches for \"benefits of investment X\" rather than \"evidence against investment X\" or \"risks of investment X\", the initial query has already narrowed the information landscape. How to \"See\" Your Bias Blind Spot? Channel: Psychology of Everything Open on YouTube Why supportive evidence feels more convincing than it is Confirmation bias is not simply collecting favourable information. It also changes how evidence is interpreted. When people encounter: evidence supporting their belief, they often treat it as reliable and representative; ambiguous evidence, they interpret it in the direction they already favour; contradictory evidence, they scrutinise its weaknesses more aggressively than they scrutinised supporting evidence. This creates an uneven standard of proof. Evidence that agrees with existing beliefs receives relatively little scepticism, while opposing evidence is expected to overcome a much higher bar. Because these different standards are applied unconsciously, people often believe they are evaluating both sides equally, even when they are not. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UCSD PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation... Published: October 6, 2004 How it appears in hiring, health and finance Hiring A manager may form a positive impression within the first few minutes of an interview. From that point onward, later questions subtly become confirmation exercises: asking about achievements rather than failures; interpreting confident behaviour as evidence of competence; overlooking inconsistent answers; remembering information that fits the favourable first impression. The interview gradually becomes an attempt to justify the initial judgement instead of testing whether it survives further evidence. This is one reason structured interviews, standardised questions and independent scoring generally produce better hiring decisions than informal conversations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Confirmation bias Confirmation bias Health Someone who wants a particular treatment may search almost exclusively for success stories, testimonials or articles supporting it while avoiding systematic reviews or clinical guidelines that discuss limitations. Conversely, someone convinced a treatment is dangerous may seek only reports of adverse effects while overlooking evidence about effectiveness and risk magnitude. In both cases, the search protects the preferred conclusion rather than evaluating the overall balance of evidence. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UCSD PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation... Published: October 6, 2004 Finance Investors frequently become emotionally attached to a company or investment thesis. Once committed, searches often change from: \"Should I own this investment?\" to \"Why will this investment succeed?\" Negative earnings reports, changing market conditions or competing analyses may receive much less attention than optimistic forecasts that reinforce the existing position. The danger is not simply missing information. It is gradually constructing an increasingly persuasive narrative from a selectively chosen evidence base. [UCSD Pages] pages.ucsd.edu Pages Confirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many UCSD PagesConfirmation Bias: A Ubiquitous Phenomenon in Many...October 6, 2004 — by RS Nickerson · 1998 · Cited by 12455 — Confirmation... Published: October 6, 2004 Confirmation Bias: The Reason Smart People Are Usually the Most Wrong Channel: ThinkLessPsy Open on YouTube Looking for disconfirming evidence The most effective safeguard is usually not trying to suppress bias but deliberately changing the search process. A practical routine is to identify the strongest reason you might be wrong before collecting further evidence. Useful questions include: What evidence would genuinely change my mind ? Which explanation competes most seriously with mine? What would someone who disagrees point to first? If I wanted to prove my current conclusion false, where would I start? This shifts the search from defending one explanation to comparing several plausible alternatives. Experimental work by Lord, Lepper and Preston found that prompting people to \"consider the opposite\" reduced biased social judgement more effectively than simply instructing them to avoid bias. The intervention worked because it required participants to actively generate alternative explanations rather than merely"},{"title":"Anchors","url":"/anchors/","description":"Anchoring makes rough first estimates, prices or AI outputs pull later judgements even when they should be rebuilt from scratch.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Anchors Why the first number sticks Anchors Anchors Why the first number sticks Anchoring makes rough first estimates, prices or AI outputs pull later judgements even when they should be rebuilt from scratch. Anchoring makes rough first estimates, prices or AI outputs pull later judgements even when they should be rebuilt from scratch. mechanism Where anchors enter ordinary decisions Why adjustment is often not enough Private estimates before group discussion improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes anchors Introduction The first number you hear often becomes far more influential than it deserves. Whether it is an opening salary offer, an initial budget estimate, a property asking price, a medical probability or even the first answer generated by an AI system, that number can quietly become the reference point against which later judgements are made. Psychologists call this anchoring : a tendency to begin with an available value and adjust away from it, but usually not by enough. The result is that decisions appear thoughtful while still being pulled towards an arbitrary starting point. Understanding how anchors take hold is a practical thinking skill because the problem is rarely the first number itself; it is failing to notice that it has become the baseline for every later judgement. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biasesby A Tversky · 1974 · Cited by 59019 — This article described three heuristics tha... Where anchors enter ordinary decisions Anchors rarely announce themselves. They usually arrive disguised as ordinary information. In negotiations, the opening offer often shapes what later feels \"reasonable\", even if both parties know it is a strategic starting position. In shopping, a product advertised as reduced from £300 to £180 makes £180 feel attractive because attention stays fixed on the original figure rather than the item's independent value. During project planning, the first time estimate discussed in a meeting often becomes the unofficial schedule, even when everyone admits it was only a rough guess. [PON Harvard Law] pon.harvard.edu PON Harvard LawWhat is the Anchoring Effect?The anchoring effect is a cognitive bias that describes the tendency to rely too heavily on t... The same mechanism appears in professional settings: An initial diagnosis influences later interpretation of symptoms . A preliminary budget frames discussions about what is \"too expensive\". An early sales forecast becomes the benchmark for revisions. The first AI generated estimate or draft can shape later editing, even when users intend to evaluate it critically. The common feature is that the first number changes the question being asked. Instead of asking, \"What is the correct value?\", people unconsciously ask, \"How far should I move from this starting point?\" That shift is subtle but important because the anchor now defines the search space. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biasesby A Tversky · 1974 · Cited by 59019 — This article described three heuristics tha... Anchoring, with Daniel Kahneman (author of Thinking, Fast and Slow) Channel: Farrar Straus & Giroux Open on YouTube Why adjustment is often not enough The original explanation proposed by psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman is known as anchoring and adjustment . When estimating an unknown quantity, people begin from an available number and mentally adjust until the answer feels plausible. The difficulty is that the adjustment process typically stops too early. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biasesby A Tversky · 1974 · Cited by 59019 — This article described three heuristics tha... Classic experiments illustrate this clearly. In one study, participants estimated the percentage of African countries in the United Nations after seeing a roulette wheel stop at either 10 or 65. Although the wheel was rigged and obviously unrelated to the question, people exposed to the higher number produced substantially higher estimates than those exposed to the lower number. Another experiment asked participants to estimate the product of the numbers one to eight after seeing either an ascending or descending sequence. Early exposure to large intermediate values produced much higher final estimates than starting with small values, despite everyone solving the same mathematical problem. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biasesby A Tversky · 1974 · Cited by 59019 — This article described three heuristics tha... Later research suggests that insufficient adjustment is only part of the story. Another influential explanation, known as selective accessibility , argues that an anchor changes which facts come most easily to mind. A high property price, for example, encourages people to think about premium features, while a low price encourages thoughts about defects. The anchor therefore influences not only the final calculation but also the evidence retrieved during reasoning. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anchoring effect Anchoring effect This helps explain why even obviously irrelevant numbers can influence judgement. They do not merely distort arithmetic; they subtly redirect attention. Why arbitrary numbers can feel meaningful People often assume they would ignore a completely random number. Research shows otherwise. An anchor does not have to be accurate to become influential. It only has to be available before judgement begins. Once present, it becomes the easiest reference point for the mind to use under uncertainty. Even recognising that a number is arbitrary does not reliably eliminate its effect. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biasesby A Tversky · 1974 · Cited by 59019 — This article described three heuristics tha... This is particularly likely when: there is no clearly known correct answer; the decision must be made quickly; information is incomplete; several plausible answers exist; confidence exceeds available evidence. These conditions are common in forecasting, hiring, budgeting and strategic planning, making anchoring an everyday risk rather than a laboratory curiosity. Private estimates before group discussion Groups often believe they will correct individual bias through discussion, but anchoring can spread socially. When one person states a numerical estimate first, later discussion frequently revolves around modifying that estimate instead of generating independent alternatives. The first speaker unintentionally establishes the reference point for the whole group. Subsequent estimates become adjustments rather than fresh judgements, and confidence in the emerging consensus can make the original anchor even harder to detect. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anchoring effect Anchoring effect A practical safeguard is therefore simple: Anchoring Bias Explained Psychology Channel: Sociologylearners Open on YouTube 1. Ask each participant to write down an independent estimate before discussion begins. 2. Reveal the estimates simultaneously. 3. Discuss reasons before averaging or converging on a final figure. This process preserves genuinely independent information instead of allowing the first contribution to dominate the conversation. The same principle applies when consulting AI systems. Recording your own estimate before requesting an AI generated answer reduces the risk that the model's first number becomes the unquestioned starting point for your thinking. The Anchoring Bias: Why The First Offer Matters Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Making anchors visible before they shape the decision Anchoring cannot be eliminated completely, but it can be made easier to detect. Useful habits include: Generate your own estimate first. Produce a private answer before seeing market prices, expert opinions or AI outputs. Ask where the number came from. Distinguish measured evidence from negotiation tactics, historical defaults or rough guesses. Restart instead of adjusting. If the initial value may be arbitrary, rebuild the estimate from first principles rather than editing the original figure. Use multiple independent reference points. Compare historical data, comparable cases and objective benchmarks instead of relying on a single starting value. Separate exploration from commitment. Treat early numbers explicitly as placeholders until better evidence becomes available. These practices do not prevent anchors from appearing, but they reduce the chance that an unnoticed first number quietly defines the entire decision. The practical lesson Anchoring is powerful because it changes the structure of reasoning before conscious analysis begins. The first number often becomes the hidden centre around which later thinking revolves, even when everyone believes they are evaluating evidence objectively. Better judgement therefore depends less on resisting persuasive numbers than on"},{"title":"Planning Fallacy","url":"/planning-fallacy/","description":"Reference-class forecasting checks ambitious plans against what similar past tasks actually took, not what the current plan hopes will happen.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Planning Fallacy Plans need a past case reality check Planning Fallacy Planning Fallacy Plans need a past case reality check Reference class forecasting checks ambitious plans against what similar past tasks actually took, not what the current plan hopes will happen. Reference class forecasting checks ambitious plans against what similar past tasks actually took, not what the current plan hopes will happen. policy intervention Why detailed plans create false confidence Inside view versus outside view Choosing a useful reference class improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes planning fallacy Introduction One of the most reliable ways to reduce the planning fallacy is to stop treating a project as if it were unique and instead ask what happened to similar projects in the past. This approach, known as reference class forecasting , replaces optimistic guesses with evidence from comparable cases. Rather than asking, \"How long should this take if everything goes well?\", it asks, \"How long did similar work actually take?\" Research by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky showed that people systematically underestimate time, costs and risks because they focus on the details of the current plan instead of the statistical record of comparable projects. Later work by Bent Flyvbjerg translated this insight into a practical forecasting method used in public planning and large infrastructure projects. [Wikipedia+2IDEAS/RePEc] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting Within the broader goal of improving analytical thinking, reference class forecasting is valuable because it provides a disciplined reality check. It does not replace detailed planning; it tests whether the plan is plausible when judged against experience rather than hope. Why detailed plans create false confidence Detailed plans often feel convincing because they contain many concrete steps, milestones and dependencies. Ironically, this detail can make forecasts less reliable if it encourages people to imagine an ideal sequence of events while overlooking the delays that routinely arise in real work. Common sources of error include: Unexpected technical problems. Coordination between multiple people or teams. Waiting for approvals or external dependencies. Rework after mistakes or changing requirements. Interruptions from unrelated priorities. Most planners recognise these risks in principle, yet still assume that this particular project will avoid them. Kahneman and Tversky argued that this happens because people rely on an inside view : they mentally simulate the current project instead of considering the distribution of outcomes from previous, similar projects. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting This explains why experience alone does not eliminate the planning fallacy. Someone may have repeatedly missed deadlines yet still believe that the next project will be different because its individual features appear more favourable. Anchoring, with Daniel Kahneman (author of Thinking, Fast and Slow) Channel: Farrar Straus & Giroux Open on YouTube Inside view versus outside view The distinction between the inside and outside view is the central practical idea behind reference class forecasting. The inside view asks questions such as: What tasks must be completed? Which obstacles can we already identify? What is our current schedule? These questions are necessary for execution but tend to encourage optimism because unknown risks receive little weight. The outside view begins somewhere else. It asks: What projects genuinely resemble this one? How long did they actually take? How often did they exceed their budgets? Where did they typically encounter delays? Instead of building an estimate from imagined future events, the outside view starts with observed historical outcomes and adjusts only when there is convincing evidence that the current case is genuinely unusual. This shifts attention from stories to statistics, making forecasts more resistant to wishful thinking. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting A classic illustration comes from Kahneman's account of a team writing a curriculum. Team members initially predicted completion within roughly two years. When one member was asked how long similar curriculum projects had taken, he recalled that most required seven to ten years and many were never completed. Despite recognising this, the group ignored the outside view and continued with the optimistic estimate. The project ultimately took around eight years. The episode has become a well known demonstration of the planning fallacy in practice . [Conversable Economist] conversableeconomist.com reference class forecasting an origin story from daniel kahneman Conversable EconomistReference Class Forecasting: An Origin Story from Daniel...Apr 17, 2023 — And one technique used by the superior fo... Choosing a useful reference class Reference class forecasting is only as good as the comparison group. Choosing the wrong reference class can produce misleading forecasts. A useful reference class should be:( endnote 1 \"Endnote 1\") [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting Similar enough to share important characteristics with the current project. Large enough to reveal meaningful patterns rather than isolated anecdotes . Based on completed projects , using actual rather than planned outcomes. Relevant to the decision , matching the type, scale and organisational context where possible. For example, estimating the completion time for a software migration by comparing it with previous software migrations is generally more informative than comparing it with all technology projects. Conversely, choosing only projects that resemble the current one in every detail can make the sample so small that it loses statistical value. Researchers sometimes describe this balancing act as the \"reference class problem\": the comparison group must be neither too broad nor too narrow. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting The Anchoring Bias: Why The First Offer Matters Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Applying reference class forecasting in everyday decisions The same reasoning works well beyond large engineering projects. A student estimating the time needed for a dissertation should examine how long previous dissertations of similar scope actually took, rather than relying solely on a personal work schedule. Someone renovating a house should compare expected costs with completed renovations of similar size, including delays caused by permits, supply shortages and unexpected repairs. A business launching a new product should review historical launch schedules across comparable products before accepting ambitious internal deadlines. A practical process is: 1. Define the decision clearly. 2. Identify a genuinely comparable group of past cases. 3. Gather actual outcomes rather than original estimates. 4. Use the average or probability distribution as the starting forecast. 5. Adjust only when there is strong evidence that the current case differs in ways that matter. The important shift is psychological as much as statistical. Instead of asking, \"Why will we succeed?\", the decision maker asks, \"What normally happens?\" What organisations have learned Reference class forecasting has had its greatest impact in large public investments, where cost overruns and schedule delays can have substantial financial consequences. Bent Flyvbjerg developed practical procedures that adapted Kahneman and Tversky's research for transport planning. The approach has influenced forecasting guidance for major infrastructure projects, including work commissioned by the UK government, and has been applied in countries including Denmark, the Netherlands and Hong Kong. Rather than relying solely on engineering estimates, planners compare proposed projects with large databases of completed projects and adjust budgets or schedules according to observed historical risk. [arXiv+3Wikipedia+3IDEAS/RePEc] Wikipedia Reference class forecasting Reference class forecasting Research consistently shows that projects judged exceptional are often less exceptional than their planners believe. Recent work has highlighted uniqueness bias —the tendency to treat one's own project as fundamentally different from previous ones—as another reason forecasts become overly optimistic. Reference class forecasting works partly because it counters this instinct by forcing planners to search for similarities before emphasising differences. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Uniqueness Bias: Why It Matters, How to Curb It Uniqueness Bias: Why It Matters, How to Curb ItAugust 13, 2024... Published: August 13, 2024 Limits and good practice Reference class forecasting is not a substitute for detailed planning. It cannot predict unique events that have no historical precedent, nor can it identify"},{"title":"Sunk Costs","url":"/sunk-costs/","description":"Sunk-cost thinking lets past time, money and reputation influence choices that should depend on future costs and benefits.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Sunk Costs Do not let the past vote Sunk Costs Sunk Costs Do not let the past vote Sunk cost thinking lets past time, money and reputation influence choices that should depend on future costs and benefits. Sunk cost thinking lets past time, money and reputation influence choices that should depend on future costs and benefits. mechanism Why unrecoverable investments feel relevant Escalation after negative feedback Future only questions for quitting or continuing improving your thinking and analytical skills mistakes sunk costs Introduction Sunk cost thinking is the mistake of allowing past investments of time, money, effort or reputation to influence a decision that should depend only on expected future costs and benefits. Once those earlier investments cannot be recovered, they are economically irrelevant. Yet they often become psychologically dominant. This is why organisations continue funding failing projects, managers double down after disappointing results, and individuals remain committed to careers, businesses or relationships that no longer justify further investment. The danger is not simply wasting what has already been spent; it is allowing yesterday's losses to create tomorrow's losses as well. Research on the sunk cost effect and escalation of commitment shows that this pattern is widespread across laboratory experiments, business decisions and public policy. [ResearchGate+2PMC] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The psychology of sunk costThe sunk cost effect is manifested in a greater tendency to continue an endeavor once an inv... Why unrecoverable investments feel relevant A sunk cost is any cost that cannot be recovered regardless of what happens next. Rationally, whether a project has already consumed £10,000 or £10 million should not affect the decision to continue if those costs are gone either way. The only relevant question is whether investing additional resources is expected to create more value than stopping. People rarely experience the decision this way. Instead, abandoning a project often feels like making the previous investment \"count for nothing\". This creates an emotional pressure to continue, even when the objective evidence has turned negative. Arkes and Blumer's classic experiments demonstrated that people become more willing to continue an activity after investing more money, effort or time, even though those previous investments cannot be recovered. They argued that much of this behaviour reflects a desire to avoid appearing wasteful rather than careful evaluation of future outcomes . [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The psychology of sunk costThe sunk cost effect is manifested in a greater tendency to continue an endeavor once an inv... Several psychological forces reinforce this tendency: Loss aversion. Accepting a definite loss often feels worse than risking a larger future loss in the hope of recovery. Need for consistency. People prefer decisions that appear consistent with earlier choices rather than admitting that circumstances have changed. Self justification. Leaders who approved the original project may feel that cancelling it implies they made a poor decision. Identity and reputation. The more public the commitment, the harder it becomes to reverse course without feeling that credibility has been damaged. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A New Inaction Effect Perspective on the Sunk Cost Fallacyby G Feldman · 2018 · Cited by 66 — Escalation of commitment situations invo... The result is that the mind quietly substitutes the wrong question—\"How can we avoid wasting what we've already spent?\"—for the right one: \"What is the best decision from today onwards?\" Escalation of Commitment: Why We Struggle to Walk Away (90 Seconds) Channel: The Business Learning Hub Open on YouTube Why negative feedback often increases commitment The most surprising feature of escalation is that evidence against a project can strengthen commitment instead of weakening it. Research on escalation of commitment examines situations where decision makers receive negative performance information after making an initial commitment. Rather than treating disappointing results as evidence to reconsider, many participants invest even more resources in an attempt to recover earlier losses. Meta analyses show that escalation becomes more likely when decision makers feel personally responsible for the original decision, when sunk costs are high, and when they believe success remains just possible. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A New Inaction Effect Perspective on the Sunk Cost Fallacyby G Feldman · 2018 · Cited by 66 — Escalation of commitment situations invo... This creates a repeating cycle: 1. A project begins with reasonable expectations. 2. Results disappoint. 3. More resources are committed to \"protect\" the original investment. 4. New disappointments appear. 5. The increased investment makes quitting feel even harder. Each round raises the psychological cost of stopping while lowering the objective chance of success. Importantly, this is not simply stubbornness. Decision makers often reinterpret new evidence to support continued investment, convince themselves that recovery is just around the corner, or emphasise how close the project seems to completion. These reactions can feel rational from inside the decision even while producing systematically worse outcomes. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A New Inaction Effect Perspective on the Sunk Cost Fallacyby G Feldman · 2018 · Cited by 66 — Escalation of commitment situations invo... Why organisations are especially vulnerable Projects rarely survive on one person's judgement alone. Organisations create additional pressures that can keep failing initiatives alive. Managers may worry that cancelling a project signals poor leadership or wastes previous budget allocations. Teams that have spent years developing specialist knowledge naturally become advocates for continuing. Political incentives can also reward avoiding visible failure today even if doing so creates larger costs later. Large infrastructure and technology projects provide well known illustrations. The supersonic Concorde programme became so closely associated with continued investment despite deteriorating economic prospects that the phrase \"Concorde fallacy\" entered discussions of decision making. Other large public projects have similarly shown how reputational concerns, political commitments and previous spending can combine to delay termination long after warning signs emerge. These examples are complex and involve strategic, political and social considerations in addition to pure sunk cost reasoning, but they illustrate how irrecoverable investments can distort future choices. [LuissThesis] tesi.luiss.it ThesisThe Sunk Cost Fallacy in Decision Making: Psychological...These examples demonstrate how decisions influenced by sunk costs a... Modern project management research therefore treats escalation as more than an individual cognitive bias. Organisational structure, incentives and accountability can either amplify or reduce the tendency to continue failing projects. [Project Management Institute] pmi.org psychology project termination decision maker 5914 Project Management InstituteThe psychology of project termination28 Oct 2013 — Arkes and Blumer (1985) present a number of experiments to... Daniel Kahneman and the sunk cost fallacy The Night Science Podcast Ep 21 Channel: Night Science Podcast Open on YouTube Future only questions for quitting or continuing The most effective defence is to deliberately remove the past from the decision. Useful questions include: If we had not already started, would we begin this project today? Ignoring what has already been spent, is the next pound, hour or employee likely to generate enough future benefit? What evidence would convince us to stop? If another team owned this project, what advice would we give them? What alternative use of these resources offers the highest expected return? These questions shift attention away from recovering historical investments and towards comparing realistic future opportunities. Another valuable practice is to define exit criteria before a project begins. Predetermined milestones, performance thresholds and review dates reduce the temptation to invent new reasons for continuing after disappointing results. Independent reviews by people who were not responsible for the original decision can also reduce self justification pressures. Research on de escalation suggests that changing how people frame future choices can make withdrawal psychologically easier without reducing accountability. [ResearchGate+2Communication Cache] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Promoting De Escalation of CommitmentStudy 2 thus provided direct evidence that promotion focused motivations can reduc... A common misunderstanding Avoiding sunk cost thinking does not mean abandoning every difficult project after early setbacks. Many worthwhile projects"},{"title":"Three Parts","url":"/three-parts/","description":"Strong reasoning combines ability, self-monitoring, and the willingness to seek reasons and revise views.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment?","level":2,"text":"Three Parts What Makes a Strong Thinker? Three Parts Three Parts What Makes a Strong Thinker? Strong reasoning combines ability, self monitoring, and the willingness to seek reasons and revise views. Strong reasoning combines ability, self monitoring, and the willingness to seek reasons and revise views. mechanism Cognitive skill Metacognitive control Intellectual disposition improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts Introduction A strong thinker is not simply someone with a high stock of facts or a quick mind. Strong reasoning combines three working parts: cognitive skill , the ability to analyse and infer; metacognitive control , the ability to monitor and correct one’s own thinking; and intellectual disposition , the willingness to seek reasons, face uncertainty and revise a view. This matters because many poor judgements do not come from not knowing any reasoning rule. They come from using the rule too late, applying it to other people but not oneself, or resisting the evidence when it becomes inconvenient. Within the wider goal of improving thinking and analytical skills, this three part model is useful because it explains why “be more rational” is too vague. Better thinking needs machinery, a dashboard and a motive. The machinery is reasoning skill. The dashboard is self monitoring. The motive is the disposition to care about truth, accuracy and fair evaluation more than comfort, speed or winning. Why strong thinking needs three parts The American Philosophical Association’s Delphi report described critical thinking as purposeful, self regulatory judgement that produces interpretation, analysis, evaluation and inference, together with an explanation of the evidence, concepts, methods, criteria and context behind that judgement. It also described the ideal critical thinker as inquisitive, well informed, open minded, fair minded, honest about personal bias, willing to reconsider and persistent in seeking precise results where precision is possible. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICed315423.tif.pdf... That definition is important because it refuses to reduce thinking to one thing. A person may have the cognitive skill to detect a weak argument yet lack the metacognitive control to notice when they are rationalising their own position. Another person may be reflective and cautious yet lack the analytic tools needed to distinguish a correlation from a causal explanation. A third may have both ability and self awareness but lack the intellectual courage to change their mind publicly. The Delphi report made this split explicit by treating critical thinking as having both a cognitive skill dimension and a dispositional dimension. It also recorded a real dispute: experts largely agreed that good critical thinkers have certain dispositions, but they did not fully agree on whether those dispositions are part of the meaning of critical thinking itself or qualities of the person who uses critical thinking skills well. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICed315423.tif.pdf... For practical improvement, the dispute matters less than the lesson: ability alone is not enough. The useful question is not “Can this person reason?” but “Will this person reason carefully, at the moment when it counts, about a claim they care about?” Cognitive skill: doing the reasoning work Cognitive skill is the visible work of thinking: interpreting a problem, analysing its parts, evaluating claims , drawing inferences and explaining why a conclusion follows. The Delphi report’s consensus list included interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, explanation and self regulation , with sub skills such as clarifying meaning, identifying arguments, assessing claims, querying evidence, conjecturing alternatives and drawing conclusions. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICed315423.tif.pdf... A practical example is a workplace decision about why a project is late. A weak analysis jumps to a single cause: “The team is disorganised.” A stronger analysis separates possibilities: unclear requirements, underestimated complexity, dependency delays, poor prioritisation, insufficient staffing or unrealistic deadlines. Cognitive skill is what lets someone break the issue into testable claims rather than treat the first plausible explanation as the answer. These skills also include the ability to explain reasoning. The Delphi report describes explanation as stating the results of one’s reasoning, justifying that reasoning in terms of evidence and context, and presenting it as a cogent argument. [ERIC] files.eric.ed.gov ERICed315423.tif.pdf... This is why strong thinkers often write things down. A conclusion that feels solid in the mind may look incomplete when forced into sentences: the premise is vague, the comparison is unfair, or the evidence does not quite support the claim. Cognitive skill improves when reasoning is made visible and practised deliberately. A meta analysis of critical thinking instruction found 341 effect sizes from quasi experimental or true experimental studies using standardised critical thinking measures; the average effect was positive, and dialogue, authentic problems and mentoring were associated with better outcomes . [ERIC] eric.ed.gov Open source on ed.gov. This supports a practical principle: people learn to think better when they practise on real arguments and decisions, not only when they memorise definitions of fallacies. Argument mapping is one concrete method because it turns reasoning into a structure that can be inspected. Tim van Gelder’s review argues that high intensity argument mapping instruction has strong evidence as a way to accelerate critical thinking gains in higher education, partly because maps sit between messy natural language and overly formal logic, helping learners see claims, reasons and objections. [Reasoninglab] reasoninglab.com TvG Using argument mapping to improve critical thinking skills 2015 TvG Using argument mapping to improve critical thinking skills 2015 The deeper mechanism is simple: once reasoning is externalised, it becomes easier to test. Thinking About Thinking: How to Challenge & Change Metacognitive Beliefs Katy O'Brien TEDxUGA Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube Metacognitive control: knowing when your thinking needs help Metacognition means monitoring and regulating one’s own thinking. In the classic Nelson and Narens framework, metacognitive monitoring informs control: people judge what they know, how difficult something is, whether to continue searching memory, how much study time to allocate, and whether to revise or stop. Their work also warned that inaccurate monitoring can mislead control, such as when people allocate effort poorly because they misjudge what they do and do not know. [UC Irvine Social Sciences] sites.socsci.uci.edu UC Irvine Social Sciences Untitled.PD F UC Irvine Social Sciences Untitled.PD F In everyday thinking, metacognitive control sounds like a set of internal checks: “Do I understand the question?” “What evidence would change my mind?” “Am I more confident than my evidence permits?” “Have I considered the strongest alternative?” These questions do not replace reasoning skill. They decide when to deploy it. The Education Endowment Foundation’s updated guidance on metacognition and self regulated learning draws on a detailed review of 355 studies and emphasises that successful approaches develop knowledge of oneself as a learner, of strategies and of tasks. It recommends explicitly teaching learners to plan, monitor and evaluate their learning, and stresses that these strategies are best applied to specific content and tasks rather than treated as free floating slogans. [Cloudfront] d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning This specificity matters outside school as well. A doctor reviewing a diagnosis, a manager assessing a hiring decision, or a reader checking a news claim does not need generic “self awareness” in the abstract. They need task sensitive monitoring: What are the usual failure points in this kind of judgement? What would a competent reviewer check? Where might my confidence be inflated? Good metacognitive control also depends on feedback. The EEF guidance notes that learners need timely feedback and strategies to judge accurately how effectively they are learning. [Cloudfront] d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Without feedback, reflection can become theatre: a person may feel thoughtful while simply reinforcing a preferred story. Feedback connects self monitoring to reality. Intellectual disposition: the will to use the skill Intellectual disposition is the habit of caring enough about good reasons to use one’s skills even when it is uncomfortable. The Delphi report’s portrait of the ideal critical thinker includes being inquisitive, well informed, trustful of reason, open minded, fair minded, honest about"},{"title":"Open Mindedness","url":"/open-mindedness/","description":"Open-minded thinking means letting strong contrary evidence count without treating every claim as equally credible.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Three Parts","level":3,"text":"Open Mindedness How open minded should good reasoning be? Open Mindedness Open Mindedness How open minded should good reasoning be? Open minded thinking means letting strong contrary evidence count without treating every claim as equally credible. Open minded thinking means letting strong contrary evidence count without treating every claim as equally credible. mechanism What actively open minded thinking requires How to weigh alternatives without false balance Why identity and pride make revision harder improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts open mindedness Introduction Open minded reasoning is often misunderstood as treating every opinion as equally valid or remaining permanently undecided. In reality, good reasoning is more demanding. It requires allowing strong evidence to change your view while still judging claims by their quality. Actively open minded thinking is therefore not passive tolerance but an active search for reasons that could prove you wrong, combined with a willingness to revise conclusions when the evidence genuinely warrants it. Research over several decades suggests that this disposition predicts better judgement independently of general intelligence, because it reduces the tendency to defend existing beliefs regardless of the facts. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement by KE Stanovich · 2023 · Cited by 133 — Actively open minded thinking (AOT) is measured by items that tap the willingness to consider... Within the broader goal of improving thinking and analytical skills, actively open minded thinking is especially important when evidence conflicts with expectations, identity or prior commitments. Those are precisely the situations in which reasoning is most likely to become biased. What actively open minded thinking requires The defining feature of actively open minded thinking (often abbreviated as AOT) is not simply listening to opposing views. It is deliberately testing whether your current conclusion deserves to survive serious criticism. Researchers such as Jonathan Baron, Keith Stanovich and Richard West describe AOT as involving several connected habits: Seeking evidence that could disconfirm your current belief. Considering alternative explanations before settling on one. Distinguishing confidence from certainty. Updating beliefs when stronger evidence appears. Remaining aware that even well supported conclusions remain open to revision if new evidence emerges. [PMC+2Helda] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCActively Open Minded Thinking and Its Measurement by KE Stanovich · 2023 · Cited by 133 — Actively open minded thinking (AOT) is measured by items that tap the willingness to consider... These habits differ from ordinary curiosity. Someone may enjoy learning new information yet become defensive whenever evidence challenges a deeply held opinion. AOT becomes visible precisely when revision is psychologically uncomfortable. An important implication follows from this definition: changing your mind is not a failure of reasoning but often evidence that reasoning has worked. If beliefs never change regardless of what is learned, they are no longer functioning as evidence based conclusions but as fixed commitments. Research also distinguishes AOT from raw intellectual ability. Studies have found that people with stronger actively open minded thinking evaluate arguments more according to their logical quality and less according to whether those arguments support their existing beliefs, even after differences in cognitive ability are taken into account. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Reasoning independently of prior belief and individual...Further, actively open minded thinking predicted variance in AET pe... Actively open minded thinking (AOT) Channel: Madeline Sherry Barr Open on YouTube How to weigh alternatives without false balance One of the biggest misunderstandings about open mindedness is the idea that every disagreement deserves equal weight. Good reasoning does not require splitting the difference between all competing claims. Instead, evidence should be weighted according to factors such as: Quality of evidence. Systematic reviews, well designed experiments and reliable observations normally deserve more weight than anecdotes or speculation. Consistency. Claims supported by multiple independent lines of evidence deserve greater confidence than isolated findings. Expert agreement. Consensus is not proof, but widespread agreement among qualified experts generally reflects accumulated evidence rather than mere opinion. Ability to survive criticism. Claims that continue to withstand serious attempts at refutation become more credible over time. Actively open minded thinkers therefore remain open to minority views without assuming that minority status itself makes a claim equally plausible. A new hypothesis deserves investigation, not automatic acceptance. This distinction helps avoid false balance. Giving equal credibility to unsupported claims in the name of fairness is not open mindedness. It is a failure to discriminate between stronger and weaker evidence. The same principle applies in everyday decisions. Imagine receiving conflicting advice about a medical treatment. An actively open minded response is neither to believe the first expert nor to average every opinion. It is to examine why experts disagree, what evidence supports each position and whether one explanation better fits the available data. Why identity and pride make revision harder The greatest obstacle to changing one's mind is often not lack of information but attachment to identity. People naturally connect beliefs with personal values, social groups, professional status and public reputation. Once a belief becomes part of \"who I am\", contradictory evidence can feel like a personal attack rather than new information. Several psychological mechanisms reinforce this tendency: Confirmation bias , the tendency to seek supporting evidence while overlooking contradictory information. Myside bias , evaluating arguments more favourably when they support one's existing position. Motivated reasoning , using reasoning skills primarily to defend preferred conclusions rather than discover what is true. [Helda+2keithstanovich.com] helda.helsinki.fi Helda Actively open minded thinking Baron, one central principle is avoiding myside bias (also known as confirmation bias), that is, looking at issues from a...Read more... These mechanisms explain why intelligent people are not immune to poor judgement. Greater intelligence can sometimes provide more sophisticated arguments for defending an incorrect conclusion if the motivation is protecting identity rather than evaluating evidence. Actively open minded thinking attempts to interrupt this process by separating self worth from correctness. Instead of asking, \"How do I defend my position?\" the question becomes, \"What would convince me that I am mistaken?\" That small shift changes the function of reasoning from advocacy to investigation. The Intelligence Trap: Why Smart People Make Bad Decisions David Robson 133 Channel: The Inner Entrepreneur Open on YouTube What revision looks like in practice Changing your mind rarely means abandoning one extreme for another overnight. More often, evidence leads to gradual refinement. For example, someone might move through stages such as: 1. \"I am certain this explanation is correct.\" 2. \"Some evidence creates reasonable doubt.\" 3. \"Several explanations remain plausible.\" 4. \"The alternative now fits the evidence better.\" 5. \"I should update my conclusion.\" Notice that each step reflects changing confidence rather than changing identity. The person is not required to become permanently sceptical about everything. Instead, confidence tracks the strength of available evidence. This process also allows partial revision. Sometimes evidence changes only one part of a belief while leaving the broader conclusion intact. Good reasoning permits nuanced updates instead of demanding complete reversals. Practical habits that strengthen actively open minded thinking Because AOT is a thinking disposition rather than a single skill, improvement comes from repeated habits. Useful practices include: Ask what evidence would change your mind before evaluating new information. Defining this in advance reduces the temptation to move the goalposts later. Generate competing explanations. Before deciding, deliberately produce at least two plausible alternatives. Separate facts from confidence. Strong confidence should reflect strong evidence, not personal investment. Seek informed disagreement. Exposure to knowledgeable critics is usually more valuable than exposure to random opposing opinions. Treat belief revision as progress. Recording how your views have changed over time can reinforce the idea that updating beliefs reflects learning rather than inconsistency. Research also suggests that people higher in actively open minded"},{"title":"Argument Maps","url":"/argument-maps-c50bfa/","description":"Argument maps make hidden assumptions, weak links and unanswered objections easier to see before a conclusion hardens.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Three Parts","level":3,"text":"Argument Maps Can mapping an argument expose bad reasoning? Argument Maps Argument Maps Can mapping an argument expose bad reasoning? Argument maps make hidden assumptions, weak links and unanswered objections easier to see before a conclusion hardens. Argument maps make hidden assumptions, weak links and unanswered objections easier to see before a conclusion hardens. policy intervention What an argument map makes visible How to map claims, reasons and objections Common mistakes when maps feel cleaner than the thinking improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts argument maps Introduction Argument mapping is a practical way to test your own conclusions before you become committed to them. Instead of asking only, “What do I believe?”, it asks, “Exactly what reasons support this conclusion, what assumptions connect those reasons to it, and what would count against it?” By forcing reasoning into a visible structure, an argument map exposes hidden assumptions, unsupported leaps and neglected objections that are easy to miss when reasoning remains informal. Research on argument mapping suggests that learning to represent arguments visually can improve critical thinking performance more effectively than many traditional discussion based approaches, particularly because it makes reasoning explicit and open to evaluation. [Wikipedia+2eCampusOntario Pressbooks] Wikipedia Argument map Argument map Within metacognition —the ability to monitor and regulate your own thinking—argument mapping is valuable because it shifts attention from defending a conclusion to inspecting the reasoning that produced it. The goal is not to prove yourself wrong, but to discover whether your conclusion survives careful examination. Unit 2.2: Argument Diagrams Channel: Michael FitzGerald · Views: 41.5K · Uploaded: January 2019 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube What an argument map makes visible Many reasoning errors survive because they remain hidden inside vague language. A statement such as \"This policy will improve productivity\" sounds complete until someone asks why, what evidence supports it, and what assumptions connect the evidence to the conclusion. An argument map separates these pieces into distinct claims and shows how they relate. A typical map makes visible: The main conclusion you are trying to justify. Supporting reasons that argue for that conclusion. Evidence supporting each reason. Intermediate conclusions , where one claim supports another before reaching the final conclusion. Objections that weaken either the conclusion or one of its supporting reasons. Rebuttals that answer those objections. Hidden assumptions , sometimes called warrants , that explain why particular evidence should support a conclusion. [Wikipedia+2CIRIS] Wikipedia Argument map Argument map Once these elements appear separately, weaknesses become easier to identify. A conclusion may rest on only one weak reason. Several reasons may actually depend on the same unsupported assumption. An objection may undermine the entire chain instead of merely attacking a minor point. This visibility is the main advantage of mapping. The map does not improve the reasoning automatically; it simply makes the quality of the reasoning easier to judge. Argument Mapping 1 What it is and Why it's Great Channel: UW Stevens Point, Critical Thinking Center Open on YouTube Argument Mapping: Understanding and Making Arguments w/ Nate Otey Channel: The Stoa · Views: 3.5K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube How to map claims, reasons and objections Argument mapping works best when it starts from a concrete conclusion rather than a general topic. A useful sequence is: 1. Write one precise conclusion. Make it a claim that could be true or false rather than a broad subject. 2. Add the direct reasons that support the conclusion. 3. Attach evidence beneath each reason instead of treating opinion as evidence. 4. Identify the hidden assumption connecting each reason to the conclusion. Ask, \"Why does this evidence actually support my claim?\" 5. Add the strongest objections , not merely weak criticisms that are easy to dismiss. 6. Evaluate whether the objections defeat the argument or whether stronger evidence or qualifications are needed. CIRIS+2Purdue OWL For example: Conclusion: I should accept this new job. Reasons: It offers higher pay. It provides better career development. The organisation appears financially stable. Each reason then needs evidence. \"Higher pay\" becomes an employment contract rather than a rumour. \"Career development\" becomes promotion history or training opportunities. \"Financial stability\" becomes audited reports or other reliable indicators. The map should also include objections: The commute is significantly longer. The role has greater uncertainty. The salary increase may not compensate for higher living costs. Rather than weakening the exercise, these objections improve it. A conclusion that survives serious objections is generally more reliable than one tested only against friendly evidence. Hidden assumptions are often the weakest links One of the most valuable features of argument mapping is exposing warrants—the assumptions that connect reasons to conclusions. Suppose someone argues: Exercise improves memory. Therefore everyone should exercise before every examination. The hidden assumption is that findings about memory improvement automatically justify a universal recommendation for every individual and situation. Once the assumption is written down, it becomes easier to evaluate. Questions immediately arise: How large is the effect? Does it apply equally to everyone? Does timing matter? Are there exceptions? The reasoning has not necessarily failed, but the map reveals where scrutiny belongs. The Toulmin model of argumentation explicitly distinguishes between evidence (grounds), the claim being defended, and the warrant that authorises the move from one to the other. It also encourages adding qualifiers, which acknowledge uncertainty, and rebuttals, which identify circumstances where the conclusion may not hold. Purdue OWL Why objections strengthen rather than weaken your thinking People often avoid writing objections because they fear it will undermine their own position. In practice , the opposite is usually true. A well tested conclusion has already faced the strongest competing explanations before action is taken. This mirrors practices used in law, intelligence analysis and scientific reasoning, where competing hypotheses are deliberately examined rather than ignored. Argument visualisation tools are designed to represent both supporting and opposing arguments because evaluation depends on understanding both sides of the reasoning network. Sage Journals+2OUP Academic A useful discipline is to ask: What would someone intelligent who disagrees with me say? Which objection would genuinely make me reconsider? What evidence, if discovered tomorrow, would change my mind ? If these questions cannot be answered, the conclusion may be more emotionally committed than analytically justified. Common mistakes when maps feel cleaner than the thinking A tidy looking map does not guarantee sound reasoning. Several common mistakes can make a weak argument appear stronger than it is. Confusing evidence with reasons. Saying \"Experts agree\" is not the same as presenting the evidence on which the experts rely. Leaving assumptions invisible. A map that omits warrants may appear complete while hiding its weakest step. Using multiple versions of the same reason. Several boxes may repeat essentially identical evidence, creating an illusion of independent support. Ignoring uncertainty. Conclusions are often stronger when qualified appropriately. Absolute claims frequently require stronger evidence than available. Adding token objections. Including only weak counterarguments gives a false impression of fairness. The strongest opposing case is usually the most informative test. Treating maps as advocacy rather than diagnosis. The purpose is not to produce the most persuasive picture but the most accurate representation of the reasoning. Recent analyses also note that argument maps work better for some forms of reasoning than others. Straightforward evidential arguments often map clearly, whereas analogy, equivocation and some mathematical or reductio arguments can require additional interpretation beyond simple box and arrow structures. Springer Turning argument mapping into a metacognitive habit The greatest value of argument mapping comes before important decisions, not afterwards. ... A simple routine is: State your conclusion before collecting more reasons. Map only the claims that actually support it. Search deliberately for evidence that weakens your preferred conclusion. Ask whether every arrow in the map represents a justified inference. Revise the conclusion if the structure changes. This transforms mapping from a note taking technique into a"},{"title":"Humility","url":"/humility-f006b2/","description":"Intellectual humility helps prevent reasoning skill from becoming a tool for defending whatever you already wanted to believe.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Three Parts","level":3,"text":"Humility Can smart people reason themselves into error? Humility Humility Can smart people reason themselves into error? Intellectual humility helps prevent reasoning skill from becoming a tool for defending whatever you already wanted to believe. Intellectual humility helps prevent reasoning skill from becoming a tool for defending whatever you already wanted to believe. mechanism How skill can serve rationalisation What intellectual humility changes in practice Ways to make belief revision less threatening improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts humility Introduction Reasoning ability is often treated as a safeguard against error. In practice , it can become a remarkably effective tool for defending beliefs we already hold. The better someone is at constructing arguments, spotting weaknesses and generating explanations, the more capable they may be of rationalising a preferred conclusion rather than impartially testing it. This phenomenon is known as motivated reasoning: reasoning directed towards protecting a desired belief, identity or outcome rather than discovering what is true. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Motivated reasoning Motivated reasoning Intellectual humility is one of the most important counterweights to this tendency. It is not about lacking confidence or refusing to form opinions. Instead, it is the willingness to recognise that one's beliefs may be incomplete or mistaken, and that changing one's mind in response to better evidence is a sign of good judgement rather than personal failure. Within metacognition , intellectual humility acts as a protective disposition that keeps analytical skill aligned with truth seeking instead of self defence. Can smart people reason themselves into error? One of the most counterintuitive findings in psychology is that greater reasoning ability does not automatically reduce bias. In situations where beliefs are tied to personal identity, politics or group membership, highly capable reasoners can become especially effective at constructing sophisticated justifications for conclusions they already favour. Rather than eliminating motivated reasoning, intelligence sometimes supplies it with better tools. [ndg.asc.upenn.edu] ndg.asc.upenn.edu Ideology motivated reasoning Ideology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2098 — When in dividuals display identity protectiv... This helps explain why intelligent people can passionately disagree over questions where they have access to much the same evidence. The disagreement is not always caused by differences in logical ability. Instead, people may unconsciously search for evidence selectively, interpret ambiguous findings differently, or apply stricter standards to information that threatens their existing commitments than to information that supports them. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Motivated reasoning Motivated reasoning The result is not deliberate dishonesty. Most people genuinely experience their reasoning as objective because the filtering occurs largely outside conscious awareness. How skill can serve rationalisation Motivated reasoning does not usually involve inventing facts from nothing. It operates through ordinary cognitive processes that become systematically biased when important beliefs are at stake. Common mechanisms include: Selective information search: seeking confirming evidence while avoiding conflicting information. Biased evaluation: demanding stronger proof from unwelcome evidence than from supportive evidence. Alternative explanation generation: rapidly producing reasons why inconvenient evidence should not count. Selective trust: treating experts as credible when they agree and unreliable when they disagree. Memory bias: recalling supporting examples more easily than contradictory ones. These mechanisms often reinforce one another. Because each step appears individually reasonable, people rarely notice that the overall process consistently favours one conclusion. [The Cultural Cognition Project+2Wikipedia] culturalcognition.net The Cultural Cognition Project Motivated reasoning & its cognates The Cultural Cognition ProjectMotivated reasoning & its cognates Cultural Cognition of...15 May 2013 — Identity protective cognition... Published: May 2013 Research on identity protective cognition illustrates this pattern clearly. When factual questions become associated with social identities, people are motivated to preserve their standing within valued groups. Evidence is then evaluated partly according to its implications for belonging, reputation or loyalty rather than solely for its accuracy. [ndg.asc.upenn.edu+2informalscience.org] ndg.asc.upenn.edu Ideology motivated reasoning Ideology, motivated reasoning, and cognitive reflectionby DM Kahan · 2013 · Cited by 2098 — When in dividuals display identity protectiv... Why Most People’s Opinions Are Worthless — Arthur Schopenhauer Channel: Mindplicit Open on YouTube What intellectual humility changes in practice Intellectual humility interrupts motivated reasoning at several points rather than attempting to eliminate bias completely. First, it changes a person's relationship with certainty. Instead of treating confidence as proof of correctness, intellectually humble thinkers recognise that confidence and accuracy are only loosely connected. Second, it changes the goal of reasoning. Rather than asking, \"How can I defend my position?\" the guiding question becomes, \"What evidence would genuinely change my view?\" Third, it separates personal identity from individual beliefs. If being wrong is no longer experienced as a threat to one's competence or character, evidence becomes easier to evaluate fairly. In practice, intellectually humble people are more likely to: distinguish confidence from certainty; acknowledge uncertainty explicitly; revise conclusions when evidence changes; recognise the limits of their own expertise; treat disagreement as potentially informative rather than automatically hostile. These habits do not guarantee correct conclusions. Instead, they reduce the chance that analytical ability becomes a servant of prior commitments. Ways to make belief revision less threatening Changing one's mind is psychologically difficult because beliefs often become connected to reputation, relationships and self image. Intellectual humility helps by reducing the emotional cost of revision. Several practical habits make this easier. Treat beliefs as working hypotheses. Instead of viewing opinions as permanent possessions, regard them as models that improve through testing . Scientists routinely replace successful theories with better ones without treating revision as failure. Separate identity from conclusions. It is safer to think \"I currently believe this\" than \"This belief defines who I am.\" The weaker the identity attachment, the easier honest evaluation becomes. Actively search for disconfirming evidence . Looking only for supporting arguments feels objective but usually strengthens confirmation bias. Deliberately asking what could prove a favourite idea wrong creates a more balanced evidence search. Keep a record of changed views. Many people remember being correct more vividly than remembering occasions when they learned something important by changing their minds. Recording belief revisions reframes them as intellectual successes rather than embarrassments. Reward good updating, not just being right. In organisations and discussions, recognising careful revision encourages truth seeking more effectively than rewarding consistency alone. These practices shift the emotional incentives surrounding reasoning. Instead of protecting existing beliefs at all costs, they make accuracy itself the valued outcome. Why Smart People Believe the Most Ridiculous Things Channel: PhiloNautica Open on YouTube Intellectual humility is not endless self doubt Humility is sometimes confused with indecisiveness or excessive scepticism. That is a mistake. An intellectually humble person can hold strong views while recognising that any conclusion remains open to revision if sufficiently strong evidence emerges. The defining feature is not low confidence but appropriately calibrated confidence. Likewise, humility does not require treating every opinion as equally credible. Evidence still matters. Experts remain more reliable than uninformed speculation. Intellectual humility simply recognises that expertise, including one's own, is never infallible. Recent research also suggests that intellectual humility is associated with better discrimination between reliable and unreliable information rather than indiscriminate scepticism. People higher in intellectual humility appear better able to distinguish true from false claims while maintaining greater awareness of the possibility of error in their own judgements. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Intellectual humility Intellectual humility Can You Be a Good Person in 2026? 'Most Influential Living Philosopher'"},{"title":"Overconfidence","url":"/overconfidence/","description":"The right self-checks can reveal when confidence is running ahead of evidence, alternatives or feedback.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Three Parts","level":3,"text":"Overconfidence When should you distrust your confidence? Overconfidence Overconfidence When should you distrust your confidence? The right self checks can reveal when confidence is running ahead of evidence, alternatives or feedback. The right self checks can reveal when confidence is running ahead of evidence, alternatives or feedback. mechanism Signs that confidence is outrunning evidence Questions that trigger better self monitoring Using feedback to recalibrate judgement improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts overconfidence Introduction Confidence is useful because it allows decisions to be made without endless hesitation. The problem is that confidence and accuracy are only loosely connected. People often feel most certain precisely when they have overlooked key evidence, failed to consider alternatives, or mistaken familiarity for understanding. Research on judgment calibration consistently finds that subjective certainty frequently exceeds objective accuracy, particularly on difficult questions and when people are asked to justify answers from memory. [APA+2Wikipedia] apa.org This bias is thought to be robust...Read more... Metacognitive checks are practical ways of asking whether your confidence deserves to be trusted. Rather than trying to eliminate confidence, they aim to calibrate it—bringing certainty into closer alignment with the strength of the available evidence. Good thinkers therefore treat confidence as information to evaluate rather than proof that they are right. Signs that confidence is outrunning the evidence Overconfidence is not simply \"feeling confident\". It is confidence that exceeds what the available evidence justifies. Because people rarely notice this directly, useful metacognitive checks focus on observable warning signs. Common indicators include: The answer arrived unusually quickly. Fast judgments are often appropriate, especially in areas of genuine expertise, but speed alone is not evidence that a conclusion is correct. Automatic responses deserve additional scrutiny when the decision is unfamiliar or consequential. Only one explanation comes to mind. The first plausible explanation is often treated as the only explanation. If no serious alternatives have been considered, confidence may reflect a narrow search rather than a strong conclusion. The evidence feels familiar rather than convincing. Repeated exposure to an idea can create an illusion of truth or understanding. Recognising an argument is not the same as having critically evaluated it. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia May 8, 2026 — Metacognition is an awareness of one's thought processes and an understanding of the patterns behind them. It is \"thinking... Published: May 8, 2026 Disconfirming evidence is ignored. Confidence becomes unreliable when attention shifts towards supporting information while contradictory evidence is dismissed as exceptional. The conclusion feels clearer than the reasoning. If you can state your answer but cannot explain why competing answers are wrong, your certainty may exceed your understanding. These warning signs matter because overconfidence is often a calibration problem rather than an intelligence problem. Highly capable people can become overconfident when working outside their expertise, relying on incomplete evidence, or becoming emotionally invested in a conclusion. [IDEAS/RePEc] ideas.repec.org IDEAS/RePEcJudgmental overconfidence: Three measures, one bias?by G Fellner · 2012 · Cited by 102 — Overconfidence is used to explain var... Why Overconfident People Miss Their Own Mistakes Dunning Kruger Effect Channel: Neuri Psychology Open on YouTube Questions that trigger better self monitoring The most effective metacognitive checks interrupt automatic certainty before a decision becomes fixed. Instead of asking, \"Am I right?\", ask questions that test the quality of the reasoning process. Useful prompts include: What evidence would change my mind ? If no conceivable evidence could alter your conclusion, the problem is often commitment rather than reasoning. What is the strongest alternative explanation? Generating at least one credible rival explanation reduces the tendency to mistake the first answer for the best answer. How confident should I be, numerically? Assigning an explicit probability—for example, 70% rather than \"I'm sure\"—encourages calibration and makes later comparison with outcomes possible. What assumptions am I making? Many confident judgments rest on unstated assumptions about missing facts, intentions or future events. If someone I respected disagreed, what argument would I expect them to make? This encourages genuine perspective taking rather than constructing a weak \"straw man\" objection. How much of my confidence comes from evidence and how much from familiarity, experience or reputation? Separating these sources often reveals that certainty has been borrowed from context rather than earned by the current evidence. These questions work because they redirect attention from defending a conclusion to evaluating the process that produced it. Educational research on metacognition consistently finds that explicitly teaching learners to plan, monitor and evaluate their thinking improves the quality of self regulation, particularly when these questions are embedded in real tasks rather than taught as abstract rules. [EEF] educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk EEFMetacognition and self regulation EEFMetacognition and self regulation approaches to teaching support pupils to think about their ow n learning Use feedback to recalibrate judgement The most reliable way to reduce overconfidence is to compare predictions with reality repeatedly. Calibration improves when people keep track of: predictions they made before knowing the outcome; how confident they were at the time; what actually happened; and why any errors occurred. This matters because memory is selective. Once outcomes are known, hindsight makes earlier uncertainty difficult to remember, creating the impression that \"I knew it all along\". Recording forecasts before receiving feedback exposes this illusion and provides a more accurate picture of judgment quality. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Overconfidence effect Overconfidence effect A simple calibration habit is to attach confidence ratings to important decisions: ConfidenceWhat it should roughly mean60%More likely correct than not, but substantial uncertainty remains.80%Strong evidence, though meaningful doubt is still possible.95%Only appropriate when the chance of being wrong is genuinely very small. After enough decisions, compare confidence with outcomes. If statements made with 80% confidence prove correct only 60% of the time, confidence is inflated. If they are correct almost every time, confidence may actually be too low. Research on calibration shows that people frequently provide confidence intervals that are far too narrow, revealing greater certainty than their knowledge supports. Learning from repeated feedback gradually widens uncertainty estimates to more realistic levels. [Wikipedia+2ResearchGate] Wikipedia Overconfidence effect Overconfidence effect Talk: Systematic review and meta analysis of the calibration of confidence judgments in individuals… Channel: Neuromatch Conference Open on YouTube Why overconfidence survives even when we know about it Knowing that overconfidence exists does not eliminate it. Several mechanisms make it persistent: Commitment strengthens belief. Once a decision has been made publicly, people often interpret subsequent evidence in ways that justify the existing choice, increasing confidence even without improving accuracy. Recent experimental work suggests that the act of deciding itself can inflate confidence by biasing how later information is interpreted. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentWhen deciding creates overconfidence Judgment and...by PJ Boyle · 2025 · Cited by 6 — We demons... Small amounts of knowledge create an illusion of mastery. Brief exposure to a topic may produce enough familiarity to feel knowledgeable while leaving major gaps unnoticed. Recent research on health information illustrates how limited knowledge can produce unwarranted confidence in self diagnosis. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Metacognition and diagnostic decision making: short \"blips\" of...by S Hong · 2026 — This study demonstrates that brief exposure to me... Confidence feels like evidence. People often mistake the subjective feeling of certainty for objective support, even though confidence is generated by the mind rather than directly by external reality. Success provides uneven feedback. Correct outcomes do not necessarily prove good reasoning. A poor process can occasionally produce the right answer by luck, reinforcing excessive confidence. These mechanisms explain why simply warning people about overconfidence has limited effects. Better calibration requires structured reflection and"},{"title":"Real Problems","url":"/real-problems/","description":"Authentic problems teach reasoning better than memorised fallacy lists because they force evidence, judgement and revision together.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Three Parts","level":3,"text":"Real Problems Why practice thinking on messy problems? Real Problems Real Problems Why practice thinking on messy problems? Authentic problems teach reasoning better than memorised fallacy lists because they force evidence, judgement and revision together. Authentic problems teach reasoning better than memorised fallacy lists because they force evidence, judgement and revision together. policy intervention Why real problems improve transfer Dialogue and mentoring in reasoning practice Turning workplace decisions into thinking drills improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts real problems Introduction Critical thinking improves most when it is practised on the kinds of messy, uncertain problems people actually face. Memorising lists of logical fallacies or completing tidy textbook exercises can build useful knowledge, but it often fails to transfer into everyday decisions because real situations rarely arrive with clear premises, complete information or a single correct answer. Authentic problems require people to gather evidence, weigh competing explanations, justify judgements, recognise uncertainty and revise conclusions as new information emerges. Research on problem based, contextual and work based learning consistently finds that these features help learners develop reasoning that is more likely to transfer beyond the classroom into professional, civic and personal life. [BIBB+2IJELS] bibb.de Bzb B Bahl Dietzen Work based learning Work based Learning as a Pathway to Competence ...by A Bahl · 2019 · Cited by 41 — Work based learning (WBL) is increasingly recogni... Within the broader goal of improving thinking and analytical skills, practising on real problems strengthens not only cognitive skills but also metacognitive habits. Learners become more aware of how they reached a conclusion, where their confidence exceeds their evidence, and when they need to rethink an initial judgement rather than defend it. Why real problems improve transfer The central advantage of authentic problems is that they combine several reasoning skills at once. In real life, analysing evidence, recognising assumptions , communicating reasons and adapting to new information happen together rather than separately. Traditional exercises often isolate one skill. A student may identify a faulty argument in a worksheet yet struggle to evaluate conflicting reports about a workplace project, a public policy or a medical decision. Real world problems demand coordination of multiple abilities under uncertainty, making practice resemble the situations where those abilities will eventually be used. Educational research on problem based learning shows that authentic cases encourage learners to: distinguish facts from assumptions; identify missing information rather than ignoring it; compare alternative explanations instead of accepting the first plausible answer; justify conclusions with evidence rather than intuition alone; and revise decisions when contradictory evidence appears. [IJELS] ijels.com 5IJELS 10320236 Strategies Strategies for Promoting Critical Thinking in the Classroom17 Mar 2023 — Problem based learning (PBL) is an instructional method tha... This matters because transfer—the ability to apply learning in a new context—is notoriously difficult. People often perform well on exercises that closely resemble what they have practised yet fail when the context changes. Authentic problems increase the similarity between learning and later decision making, making transfer more likely. Work based learning research similarly argues that competence develops most effectively when knowledge is continually applied within realistic settings instead of remaining detached from practice. [BIBB] bibb.de Bzb B Bahl Dietzen Work based learning Work based Learning as a Pathway to Competence ...by A Bahl · 2019 · Cited by 41 — Work based learning (WBL) is increasingly recogni... 5 tips to improve your critical thinking Samantha Agoos Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Why messy problems reveal how people actually think Real decisions contain ambiguity. Evidence is incomplete, stakeholders disagree, objectives conflict and time is limited. Those conditions expose reasoning habits that simplified exercises can hide. Consider a workplace team investigating why a project missed its deadline. A classroom version may provide all relevant facts. The real version does not. Team members must decide: Which evidence is reliable? What information is still missing? Could several causes be operating simultaneously? Which explanation best fits the available evidence? How confident should the team be before acting? These questions force metacognitive monitoring. Instead of asking only \"What is the answer?\", participants also ask \"Why do I believe this?\" and \"What evidence would change my mind ?\" Authentic intellectual work frameworks in education were developed around precisely this insight: valuable learning requires constructing knowledge, carrying out disciplined inquiry and producing work with value beyond the classroom rather than simply recalling information. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Framework for authentic intellectual work Framework for authentic intellectual work Problem Based Learning: How to solve real world issues in today’s classroom – Part 1 Feb 21, 2018 Channel: Ontario Teachers' Federation OTF Open on YouTube Dialogue and mentoring strengthen reasoning Real world reasoning is rarely a solitary activity. Many important decisions emerge through discussion with colleagues, mentors, clients or community members. This makes dialogue an important part of critical thinking practice rather than merely a way of communicating conclusions. Effective mentors rarely provide immediate answers. Instead, they ask questions that require learners to expose their assumptions: What evidence supports this conclusion? What alternative explanation have you considered? Which facts would contradict your current view? How confident are you, and why? What additional information would reduce uncertainty? These questions slow intuitive judgement and encourage reflective thinking. They also make reasoning visible, allowing weaknesses to be identified before important decisions are made. Research on collaborative and problem based learning suggests that discussion centred on authentic problems improves both reasoning quality and the ability to explain one's thinking because participants must defend claims with evidence rather than simply repeat memorised content. [IJELS] ijels.com 5IJELS 10320236 Strategies Strategies for Promoting Critical Thinking in the Classroom17 Mar 2023 — Problem based learning (PBL) is an instructional method tha... The quality of dialogue matters. Productive discussion is not debate for its own sake. Its purpose is to improve the decision, not to reward whoever argues most forcefully. Turning workplace decisions into thinking drills Everyday work contains numerous opportunities to practise critical thinking without creating artificial exercises. Instead of asking only whether a decision succeeded, organisations can examine how it was reached. For example: After a project delay Rather than assigning blame immediately, reconstruct the chain of reasoning. Which assumptions proved incorrect? Which warning signs were ignored? Which evidence was unavailable at the time? After a customer complaint Separate observations from interpretations. Which facts are established? Which explanations remain speculative? What additional evidence should be gathered before changing policy? Before approving a proposal Require competing explanations or alternative options. This reduces the tendency to stop searching once an acceptable answer appears. Following an unexpected success Ask whether the outcome resulted from good judgement, favourable circumstances or both. Successful outcomes do not necessarily validate the reasoning that produced them. These routines transform ordinary organisational events into repeated opportunities for evidence evaluation, judgement calibration and reflective improvement. 10 Real Life Examples of Critical Thinking in Action! Channel: Critical Thinking Secrets Open on YouTube Common mistakes when using real problems Authentic problems are valuable only when designed to encourage reasoning rather than guesswork. Several mistakes reduce their educational value: Focusing only on outcomes. A correct decision reached through poor reasoning teaches little. Choosing problems that are too simple. If one obvious answer exists immediately, learners do not need to evaluate competing explanations. Making tasks overwhelmingly complex. Excessive ambiguity can encourage random speculation rather than disciplined inquiry. Skipping reflection. Experience alone does not guarantee learning. Reflection connects actions to underlying reasoning. Rewarding speed over quality. Fast answers often discourage evidence gathering and revision. The objective is not to make problems"},{"title":"Self Regulation","url":"/self-regulation/","description":"Planning, monitoring and evaluating learning helps thinkers choose better strategies for specific tasks instead of relying on effort alone.","breadcrumb":"Can You Train Better Judgment? / Three Parts","level":3,"text":"Self Regulation How do better thinkers manage their learning? Self Regulation Self Regulation How do better thinkers manage their learning? Planning, monitoring and evaluating learning helps thinkers choose better strategies for specific tasks instead of relying on effort alone. Planning, monitoring and evaluating learning helps thinkers choose better strategies for specific tasks instead of relying on effort alone. policy intervention Planning before reasoning begins Monitoring progress during a task Evaluating what worked after feedback improving your thinking and analytical skills three parts self regulation Introduction Self regulated learning is the practical side of metacognition : it is the process of deliberately planning, monitoring and evaluating your own learning so that your thinking improves over time. For analytical work, this matters because better reasoning rarely comes from trying harder alone. It comes from choosing appropriate strategies, noticing when those strategies are failing, and adapting before errors become entrenched. Research over several decades consistently shows that successful learners actively regulate their goals, attention, motivation and methods rather than relying on repetition or intuition . [PMC+2ResearchGate] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... Within the broader goal of improving analytical thinking, self regulated learning connects abstract metacognitive awareness with everyday practice . It turns questions such as \"Am I thinking clearly?\" into concrete habits: defining the task before starting, checking progress while working, and reviewing both successes and mistakes afterwards. These habits help people become more accurate, adaptable and independent thinkers rather than simply more knowledgeable. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... Planning before reasoning begins Strong analysis starts before the first argument is evaluated. Self regulated learners spend time understanding the problem they are trying to solve instead of immediately generating answers. Barry Zimmerman and other researchers describe the first phase of self regulated learning as one of forethought. Before beginning a task, learners establish goals, consider what the task requires, estimate possible difficulties and select strategies appropriate to those demands. This planning stage influences everything that follows because poor task definitions often lead to poor reasoning, even when later thinking is careful. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Self regulated learning and academic achievement: TheoryFrom a self regulation perspective, learner autonomy is closely assoc... For analytical work, effective planning typically includes asking questions such as: What exactly is the problem? What evidence will count as convincing? What assumptions am I making? Which analytical tools are appropriate? How will I know whether my conclusion is reliable? Notice that none of these questions concern the answer itself. Instead, they define the process by which an answer will be judged. Research on self regulated learning also emphasises that planning depends on realistic self assessment. Learners consider not only external task demands, such as deadlines or available evidence, but also internal conditions including prior knowledge, confidence and motivation. Choosing a strategy therefore becomes an informed decision rather than a habit. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Self‐Regulated LearningMoreover, self regulation is students' ability to monitor, evaluate, and engage their learning b... For example, someone analysing conflicting scientific studies might decide in advance to compare research methods before examining reported conclusions. Someone evaluating a business proposal may first identify measurable success criteria rather than becoming persuaded by presentation quality. Self Regulated Learning Explained: How to Become Your Own Teacher Channel: Powerful Learning · Views: 1.5K · Uploaded: July 2025 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Monitoring progress during a task Planning is valuable only if thinking continues to be monitored while work is underway. Self monitoring means continuously comparing current thinking against the original goal. Instead of assuming that reasoning remains on track, self regulated learners actively look for signs that their understanding or strategy needs adjustment. During complex analytical tasks, monitoring often involves questions such as: Am I still answering the original question? Does this evidence actually support my conclusion? Have I overlooked contradictory information? Am I becoming overconfident because one explanation feels familiar? Should I slow down or change strategy? These questions interrupt automatic thinking before mistakes become expensive. Research distinguishes between simply noticing progress and actively controlling learning. Monitoring provides information; regulation uses that information to change behaviour. A learner who realises they are misunderstanding a dataset but continues regardless is monitoring without regulating. Effective self regulated learning requires both recognising the problem and adapting accordingly. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org FrontiersClassic and modern models of self regulated learningby C Tinajero · 2024 · Cited by 66 — The model subdivides the performance ph... This adaptive element explains why self regulated learners often outperform equally intelligent peers. Rather than persisting with ineffective approaches, they revise hypotheses, consult additional sources, reorganise notes or seek clarification when necessary. These adjustments improve analytical quality long before the final answer is produced. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... Monitoring also extends beyond purely cognitive processes. Attention, effort and emotional state influence reasoning quality. Becoming aware that frustration is encouraging premature conclusions or that fatigue is reducing careful evaluation allows learners to modify their approach rather than unknowingly allowing these factors to shape judgement. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Self regulated learning (SRL) includes the cognitive, metacognitive, behavioral, motivational, and emotional/affective aspects of learnin... Evaluating what worked after feedback The final stage of self regulated learning is reflection. [d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net] d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net Metacognition and Self Regulated Learning Evidence reviewApproaches that promoted self directed learning often mirrored Zimme... After completing a task, strong thinkers do not ask only whether they reached the correct conclusion. They also ask why they succeeded or failed and whether their methods should be reused. Zimmerman's model describes this as self reflection, in which learners evaluate outcomes against their original goals and consider how their strategies contributed to performance. This reflection creates the feedback loop that allows future improvement. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Self regulated learning and academic achievement: TheoryFrom a self regulation perspective, learner autonomy is closely assoc... Useful post task evaluation includes questions such as: Which strategy produced the strongest evidence? Where did my reasoning become weakest? Which assumptions proved incorrect? What surprised me? What should I change next time? These reflections are especially valuable after receiving external feedback. Instead of treating criticism simply as confirmation of success or failure, self regulated learners compare external evaluations with their own judgements. Differences between the two often reveal calibration problems—for example, consistently overestimating understanding or underestimating uncertainty. Importantly, reflection should focus on controllable processes rather than fixed ability. Research consistently associates adaptive learning with attributing outcomes to strategy use, preparation and effort directed towards appropriate methods rather than believing success or failure reflects permanent intelligence alone. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Self‐Regulated LearningMoreover, self regulation is students' ability to monitor, evaluate, and engage their learning b... Multimodal Learning Analytics for Self Regulated Learning: Challenges and opportunities Channel: IFE Living Lab & Data Hub Open on YouTube Why self regulated learning improves analytical thinking Self regulated learning strengthens analysis because it reduces common reasoning failures before they become embedded. Instead of treating reasoning as"}]};
